(window.LH_SEARCH = window.LH_SEARCH || {})["python"] = {"s":"python","t":[["py-start","What a program is and your first Python program","1.1"],["py-values","Variables, values, types and numeral systems","1.2"],["py-operators","Operators, expressions and the order Python works them out","1.3"],["py-io","Input, output and formatting text","1.4"],["py-conditions","Making decisions: if, elif, else and match","2.1"],["py-loops","Loops: while, for, range, break and continue","2.2"],["py-strings","Strings: text, slicing, methods and Unicode","3.1"],["py-lists","Lists: storing, changing, sorting and copying","3.2"],["py-tuples-sets","Tuples and sets: fixed records and unique items","3.3"],["py-dicts","Dictionaries: keys, values and hash tables","3.4"],["py-functions","Functions: def, return and every kind of argument","4.1"],["py-scope-recursion","Scope and recursion: LEGB, closures and the call stack","4.2"],["py-functional","lambda, map, filter and comprehensions","4.3"],["py-exceptions","Exceptions: try, except, else, finally and raise","5.1"],["py-modules","Modules and packages: import, the standard library and pip","5.2"],["py-files","Files: reading, writing, modes, paths and streams","5.3"],["py-classes","Classes and objects: attributes, methods, self and encapsulation","6.1"],["py-inheritance","Inheritance: super(), overriding, polymorphism and the MRO","6.2"],["py-core-exam","Exam practice: PCEP and PCAP style papers","7.1"],["py-dunder","Special methods and operator overloading","1.1"],["py-decorators","Extended arguments, closures and decorators","1.2"],["py-class-design","Class design: methods, properties, abstract classes and dataclasses","1.3"],["py-errors-copy","Exceptions in depth, copying and serialisation","1.4"],["py-metaclasses","Metaprogramming: type, class hooks and metaclasses","1.5"],["py-generators","Iterators and generators","2.1"],["py-itertools","itertools, functools and operator","2.2"],["py-context","Context managers and the with statement","2.3"],["py-style","Clean code: PEP 8, docstrings and type hints","3.1"],["py-testing","Testing: unittest, pytest and mocks","3.2"],["py-logging","Logging and debugging","3.3"],["py-complexity","Complexity, timing and profiling","4.1"],["py-collections","collections, heapq and bisect","4.2"],["py-concurrency","Threads and processes","5.1"],["py-asyncio","asyncio: async and await","5.2"],["py-regex","Regular expressions","6.1"],["py-formats","CSV, JSON, XML and settings files","6.2"],["py-sqlite","Databases with sqlite3 and SQL","6.3"],["py-tkinter","Desktop windows with tkinter","6.4"],["py-how-python-runs","How Python Runs: From Source Code to Output","7.1"],["py-adv-exam","Exam practice: PCPP1 style papers","8.1"],["py-http","HTTP and REST: how the web talks","1.1"],["py-sockets","Sockets, TCP and UDP: under HTTP","1.2"],["py-requests","Calling web APIs with requests","2.1"],["py-api-data","Real API work: pages, retries, limits and validation","2.2"],["py-flask","Flask basics: routes, templates, forms and JSON","3.1"],["py-flask-apps","Real Flask apps: structure, database, login and tests","3.2"],["py-django","Django basics: project, URLs, views, templates, models and admin","4.1"],["py-django-orm","Django in depth: ORM, migrations, forms, users and tests","4.2"],["py-drf","Django REST framework: JSON APIs on top of Django","5.1"],["py-fastapi","FastAPI: fast, typed APIs with automatic docs","5.2"],["py-api-security","API security: passwords, JWT, OAuth 2.0, CORS and OWASP","6.1"],["py-web-exam","Web revision exam: HTTP, Flask, Django, FastAPI and security","6.2"],["cheatsheet","Cheatsheet","📄"],["interview-questions","Interview Questions","🎤"]],"c":[[0,"","What a program is and your first Python program","Programs, compiler and interpreter, CPython, REPL and scripts, print() with sep and end, keywords, indentation, error kinds, PEP 8. · first program · hello world · print · sep · end · interpreter · compiler · keywords · indentation · syntax error · PEP 8 · PCEP Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Core Python›Step 1: Getting started›First program Step 1 · Topic 1 of 4 · Beginner Start from zero. Learn what a program is, how a compiler and an interpreter differ and what CPython does with your file, install Python, write your first program, master print() with sep and end, and learn to tell syntax, runtime and logic errors apart. About 40 min11 lessons2 animations4 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions2 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1What a program is 2Compiler, interpreter and how Python runs your file 3Install Python and run your first program 4print(): showing results on the screen 5Keywords, statements, comments and indentation 6Three kinds of errors 7Writing code other people can read: PEP 8 and PEP 20 8The maths: counting what print() writes 9From beginner to expert 10LeetCode practice 11Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 11"],[0,"idea","What a program is","Source: Python 3.14 tutorial, chapters 1 to 3, and the PCEP-30-02 syllabus, section 1.1. A computer is very fast but it has no common sense. It does exactly what it is told, one small step at a time. A program is a list of those steps, written down so that the computer can follow them. Real world scenario: a recipe in the kitchen A recipe says: boil water, add rice, wait 15 minutes, switch off the stove. A cook follows the steps in order. If a step is missing (\"add rice\" is not written), the rice never goes in. If a step is wrong (\"wait 150 minutes\"), the cook still follows it and the rice burns. A computer is a cook who follows every line exactly, even a silly one. What it is A program is a set of instructions written in a programming language. Python is one such language. Its instructions look close to plain English, for example print(\"Hello\"). Why we need it The chip inside a computer only understands machine code: long rows of 0s and 1s. People cannot write large programs in 0s and 1s without mistakes, so we write in a language people can read, and let a tool translate it. How it works You write the steps in a text file that ends in .py. A program called the Python interpreter reads the file, checks it, translates it, and runs it line by line from the top."],[0,"idea","Three words every programmer uses","Word Meaning In everyday language Lexis The words and symbols a language allows (print, if, +, \"text\") The vocabulary of English Syntax The grammar rules: how words may be put together \"I rice eat\" breaks English grammar Semantics What a correct sentence means \"The stone ate the rice\" has good grammar but makes no sense Python is popular because its lexis is small, its syntax is clean, and the same code runs on Windows, macOS, Linux, phones and even inside a web browser. It is used for websites (Instagram is built on Django), data science, artificial intelligence, test automation and small scripts that save people hours of boring work. Lesson 2 of 11"],[0,"translate","Compiler, interpreter and how Python runs your file","Code written by people must be translated into something the machine can run. There are two classic ways to translate. Two ways to translate a program Compiler Interpreter How it works Translates the whole program first into a separate machine code file, then you run that file Reads the program and runs it straight away, statement by statement Like Translating a full book into Hindi, printing it, then reading it A live interpreter at a meeting who translates each sentence as it is spoken When errors show Most mistakes are found before anything runs A mistake is found when Python reaches that line (except grammar mistakes, see below) Speed Usually faster to run Usually slower, but you see results at once Examples C, C++, Go, Rust Python, JavaScript in old browsers, shell scripts Bottom line: CPython, the standard Python, does a bit of both. It first compiles your file into bytecode (simple instructions for a virtual machine), then its interpreter loop runs that bytecode. Because the compile step comes first, a grammar mistake anywhere in the file stops the program before line 1 runs. What happens when you type python3 hello.py Python opens hello.py and reads the text. It splits the text into tokens (words, numbers, symbols) and checks the grammar. A grammar mistake here gives a SyntaxError and nothing runs. It compiles the grammar tree into bytecode, small instructions such as LOAD_CONST and CALL. The Python virtual machine runs the bytecode from top to bottom. print() sends text to the screen. source code (.py) → tokens → syntax tree → bytecode → run → output Remember Python is often called an interpreted language, but CPython does compile your code to bytecode first. Both words are partly true. The Advanced Python step 7 chapter \"How Python runs\" opens every one of these stages with an animation. Python implementation Written in Where it is used CPython C The standard one from python.org. This course uses CPython 3.14 PyPy Python (RPython) Has a just in time compiler, often several times faster on long loops MicroPython C Small boards like the Raspberry Pi Pico and ESP32 Pyodide CPython compiled to WebAssembly Runs Python inside your web browser. The Run buttons on this page use it Lesson 3 of 11"],[0,"setup","Install Python and run your first program","You can try every example on this page with the Run button, which runs real Python in your browser. To work on your own computer, install Python once. Install Python Go to python.org/downloads and download the newest Python 3 for your system (Windows, macOS or Linux). On Linux it is often already installed. On Windows, tick \"Add python.exe to PATH\" in the installer. This lets you type python in any terminal. Open a terminal (Command Prompt, PowerShell or Terminal) and type python3 --version (on Windows: python --version or py --version). If you see a version such as Python 3.14.0, Python is ready Two ways to run Pythonshell $ python3 --version Python 3.14.6 $ python3 >>> 2 + 3 5 >>> print(\"Hello\") Hello >>> exit() $ python3 hello.py Hello, world! Way to run What it is Good for Interactive mode (REPL) Type python3 with no file. The prompt >>> waits for one line, runs it and shows the result at once Quick tests, like a calculator Script mode Save lines in a .py file and run python3 file.py Real programs you want to keep and run again An editor or IDE IDLE (comes with Python), VS Code, PyCharm, Thonny Writing, running and debugging bigger programs A notebook Jupyter or Google Colab: code cells and notes in one page Data science and learning Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 hello.py. Common mistake In the REPL, typing 2 + 3 shows 5 without print(). In a .py file, nothing appears unless you call print(). Beginners often expect the REPL behaviour inside a file. Lesson 4 of 11"],[0,"print","print(): showing results on the screen","What it is print() is a built in function. A function is a named action. You give it values inside the round brackets (called arguments) and it writes them on the screen. Why we need it A program that shows nothing is useless to a person. print() is how a program talks back: results, messages, and while learning, what is happening inside. How it works print() turns each argument into text with str(), joins the texts with sep (a space unless you say otherwise), adds end (a new line unless you say otherwise), and writes the result. The full form of print()Exam favourite print(*values, sep=\" \", end=\"\\n\", file=sys.stdout, flush=False) values: any number of values. sep: text between them. end: text after the last one. file: where to write. flush: write at once. Characters written by one print()Exam favourite N = ∑ len(str(vi)) + (n − 1) · len(sep) + len(end) n values v1 to vn. There are n − 1 gaps between n values. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 sepend.py. You write The screen shows print(\"A\", \"B\") A B print(\"A\", \"B\", sep=\"\") AB print(\"A\", end=\"\"), then print(\"B\") AB print(\"A\", \"B\", sep=\"-\", end=\"!\") A-B! print() an empty line print(3 + 4, \"3 + 4\") 7 3 + 4 In the exam sep and end must be given by name (keyword arguments). print(\"A\", \"B\", \"-\") prints A B - with the dash as a third value, it does not change sep. Lesson 5 of 11"],[0,"structure","Keywords, statements, comments and indentation","A Python program is made of statements, normally one per line. Some words have a fixed meaning in the language. They are called keywords and you cannot use them as names for your own things. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 keywords.py. Part What it is Example Keyword A reserved word with a fixed job. Python 3.14 has 35 of them if, else, for, while, def, class, return, True, None Soft keyword A word that is a keyword only in one place, and a normal name everywhere else match, case, type, _ Statement One complete instruction price = 120 Comment Text after # that Python ignores. It is a note for people # add the tax Indentation Spaces at the start of a line. They show which lines belong to a block 4 spaces under an if Docstring A text in triple quotes at the top of a file or function that explains it \"\"\"Works out the bill.\"\"\" Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 indent.py. Common mistake Python uses indentation where many languages use curly brackets. Mixing tabs and spaces, or using 3 spaces on one line and 4 on the next, gives IndentationError. PEP 8 says: always 4 spaces. Going deeper: why indentation instead of brackets Guido van Rossum took the idea from the ABC language. People already indent code to read it, so making the indentation itself the rule removes a whole class of bugs where the brackets say one thing and the indentation says another. The tokenizer turns a deeper indent into an INDENT token and a shallower one into DEDENT tokens, so the parser sees blocks just as clearly as with brackets. Lesson 6 of 11"],[0,"errors","Three kinds of errors","Everyone makes mistakes in code, even experts, every day. What matters is knowing which kind of mistake you made, because each kind is found in a different way. Kind When Python finds it Example What you see Syntax error Before anything runs, while checking the grammar of the whole file print(\"Hi\" SyntaxError, and not one line has run Runtime error (exception) Only when the bad line runs 10 / 0, a misspelt name A traceback. The lines before it have already run Logic error Never. Python cannot know what you meant price + 2 instead of price * 2 A wrong answer and no message at all Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 errors.py. How to read a traceback Read the last line first. It names the error and gives a short reason, for example ZeroDivisionError: division by zero. Look at the line just above it. It shows the file name, the line number and the code that failed. Go to that line in your editor and ask: which value was not what I expected? last line = what went wrong, line number = where Lesson 7 of 11"],[0,"style","Writing code other people can read: PEP 8 and PEP 20","Code is read many more times than it is written. A PEP (Python Enhancement Proposal) is a design document for Python. Two of them are about style. PEP 8 rule Good Bad Indent with 4 spaces four spaces before print a tab, or 2 spaces Variable and function names: lower case with underscores (snake_case) total_price TotalPrice, totalprice Class names: CapWords BankAccount bank_account Constants: all capitals MAX_SIZE = 100 maxsize = 100 One space around = and + in normal code x = a + b x=a+b Lines no longer than 79 characters short lines one very long line Two blank lines between top level functions a gap you can see everything packed together Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 zen.py. In the exam PCEP asks which names follow PEP 8 conventions. snake_case for variables and functions, CapWords for classes, UPPER_CASE for constants. Lesson 8 of 11"],[0,"maths","The maths: counting what print() writes","Being exact about small things is the habit that makes a good programmer. Here we count exactly how many characters a print() writes. Characters from one print()Exam favourite N = L + (n − 1) · s + e L = total length of all values as text, n = number of values, s = len(sep), e = len(end). Bytes in a plain English text file bytes = characters Each English letter, digit, space and new line is 1 byte in UTF-8. Letters from other scripts can take 2 to 4 bytes (Core 3.1). Worked example: print(\"Total\", 3, \"apples\") The values as text are \"Total\" (5), \"3\" (1) and \"apples\" (6). L = 5 + 1 + 6 = 12. There are n = 3 values, so n − 1 = 2 gaps. sep is one space, s = 1, so the gaps add 2. end is one new line, e = 1. N = 12 + 2 × 1 + 1 = 15 characters Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 count.py. Worked example: print(\"2026\", \"11\", \"07\", sep=\"-\", end=\"\") L = 4 + 2 + 2 = 8. Two gaps of one dash: 2. end is empty: 0. N = 8 + 2 + 0 = 10, the text 2026-11-07 Lesson 9 of 11"],[0,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths A program is a recipe for the computer. Python reads your recipe from top to bottom and does each step. print() is how it shows you the result. CPython compiles a .py file to bytecode and runs it on a stack based virtual machine. Grammar is checked for the whole file first (SyntaxError), while exceptions happen only when a line runs. print() calls str() on each argument and writes sep.join(texts) + end to sys.stdout. sys.stdout is a buffered text stream. When it is a terminal it is line buffered, so print(\"x\", end=\"\") may not appear until a new line is written or flush=True is passed. When output goes to a file or a pipe it is block buffered, which is why progress messages sometimes appear late in logs. The grammar of Python is a PEG grammar (PEP 617, since 3.9), and the tokenizer produces INDENT and DEDENT tokens from leading white space. Error messages improved a lot in 3.10 to 3.14 (\"Did you mean: price?\"), based on research on helping novices read errors. Beginner Write and run a .py file, use print() with sep and end, and read the last line of a traceback. Intermediate Know the difference between compile time and run time, follow PEP 8 names, and use the REPL to test small ideas. Expert Explain bytecode, output buffering and flush, and why a SyntaxError on line 50 stops line 1 from running. Real world scenario: a shop bill printed at the counter The billing machine at a shop runs a small program. It prints the shop name, then each item and price with a fixed gap (that is sep), then a line of dashes and the total (each print ends with a new line, that is end). If the programmer types + instead of * for quantity times price, the machine prints a wrong bill with no error. That is a logic error, and it is why shops test the machine with a known bill before using it. Lesson 10 of 11"],[0,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","These problems use exactly what this chapter teaches. The page gives only a hint. Write your own answer on LeetCode first, then compare with the downloadable solution. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 2235 Add Two Integers Easy Arithmetic Return num1 + num2. The point is to get used to a function that returns a value instead of printing it. 2469 Convert the Temperature Easy Formula Kelvin = Celsius + 273.15 and Fahrenheit = Celsius × 1.80 + 32.00. Return both in a list. 2413 Smallest Even Multiple Easy Arithmetic If n is already even, n itself works. If n is odd, the answer is 2 × n. 1108 Defanging an IP Address Easy String replace Replace every dot with [.] using the string method replace(). .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 11 of 11"],[0,"recap","Chapter summary","A program is a list of exact instructions. Python is a programming language with a small, readable vocabulary (lexis) and clear grammar (syntax). A compiler translates the whole program first. An interpreter runs it statement by statement. CPython compiles to bytecode, then interprets the bytecode. Run Python in the REPL (>>>) for quick tests, or save a .py file and run python3 file.py. print() calls str() on each value, puts sep (a space) between them and end (a new line) after them. N = L + (n − 1)s + e characters. Python 3.14 has 35 keywords and 4 soft keywords. Comments start with #. Blocks are shown by indentation of 4 spaces. Syntax errors stop the program before it starts. Runtime errors stop it at the bad line. Logic errors give wrong answers silently. PEP 8 is the style guide (snake_case, 4 spaces, 79 characters). PEP 20 is the Zen of Python (import this). Next: Core 1.2 looks at variables, values and types: what x = 5 really does inside the computer. .py Practice files for this chapterTwo tasks: build the exact text print() writes, and fix a greeting function.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[1,"","Variables, values, types and numeral systems","Names and objects, the basic types and literals, numeral systems, type conversion, naming rules, multiple assignment. · variables · data types · int · float · bool · str · None · binary · hexadecimal · octal · type conversion · casting · naming rules Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Core Python›Step 1: Getting started›Variables and types Step 1 · Topic 2 of 4 · Beginner Learn what x = 5 really does: names stuck on objects. Meet int, float, complex, bool, str and None, write numbers in binary, octal and hexadecimal, convert between types safely and pick good names. About 45 min11 lessons2 animations8 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1What a variable really is 2The basic data types and their literals 3Numeral systems: binary, octal and hexadecimal 4Changing one type into another 5Naming rules and good names 6More ways to assign 7How Python variables compare with other languages 8The maths: place value and conversions 9From beginner to expert 10LeetCode practice 11Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 11"],[1,"idea","What a variable really is","Source: Python 3.14 Language Reference, section 3.1 (objects, values and types), and PCEP-30-02 section 1.3. In maths class, x = 5 means \"x is equal to 5\". In Python, x = 5 is an action: \"make the value 5, and stick the label x on it\". What it is A variable is a name that points to an object. Every object has three things: an identity (where it lives, id()), a type (what kind of thing it is, type()) and a value. Why we need it Programs need to remember things: a price, a name, a score. Names let us refer to values again later without typing them again, and let the value change while the name stays the same. How it works The assignment name = expression first works out the right side, which gives an object. Then it binds (sticks) the name on the left to that object. The old object is not changed. Real world scenario: name tags at a party Think of objects as people at a party and names as sticky name tags. age = 25 puts the tag \"age\" on the person 25. my_age = age puts a second tag on the same person. age = age + 1 does not change that person into 26. It finds the person 26 and moves the \"age\" tag over. my_age is still stuck on 25. Variables are names stuck on objectsType a few assignments, one per line. Each step shows the object Python makes and where each name points. Step 0 of 6 Assignments (name = value, name = other name, or a + b) Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this Watch line 3. age moves to a new object 26, but my_age still points to 25. Assignment never changes an object, it moves a name. x = 5, then y = x, then x = 7. y still points to 5. Changing what x points to never changes y. Use a name before giving it a value: total = total + 1. Python stops with NameError, because a name only exists after its first assignment. Python age = 25# the name age now points to the int object 25my_age = age # a second name for the SAME objectprint(age, my_age)print(id(age) == id(my_age)) # True: one object, two namesage = age + 1# a NEW object 26 is made, and age moves to itprint(age, my_age) # my_age still points to 25print(id(age) == id(my_age)) # False now Output 25 25 True 26 25 False Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 names.py. Remember In Python a variable has no type of its own. The object has the type. The same name can point to an int now and to a str later. This is called dynamic typing. Lesson 2 of 11"],[1,"types","The basic data types and their literals","A literal is a value written straight into the code, such as 42 or \"Asha\". Each literal makes an object of a certain type. Type What it holds Literals Can it change? int Whole numbers of any size 7, -3, 1_000_000, 0b101, 0o17, 0xFF No (immutable) float Numbers with a decimal point, about 15 to 17 correct digits 3.14, -0.5, 2.0, 1.5e3, 1e-9 No complex Numbers with a real and an imaginary part 2 + 3j, 1j No bool True or False True, False No str Text, any number of characters \"hi\", 'hi', \"\"\"many lines\"\"\" No NoneType The single value meaning \"nothing here\" None No Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 value_types.py. Scientific notationExam favourite a e b = a × 10b 1.5e3 = 1.5 × 103 = 1500.0. The result is always a float. Underscores in numbers 1_000_000 == 1000000 Since Python 3.6 (PEP 515) underscores can group digits. Python ignores them. In the exam bool is a kind of int: True == 1 and False == 0, so True + True is 2. This surprises many people in exams. Going deeper: why int has no size limit In C or Java an int has a fixed number of bits (32 or 64), so very big numbers overflow. CPython stores an int as a list of 30 bit digits and adds more digits when needed, so 2 ** 1000 is exact. The cost: big numbers are slower and use more memory. Since 3.11 CPython limits int to str conversion to 4,300 digits by default (sys.set_int_max_str_digits) to stop denial of service attacks. The code below shows this. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 bigint.py. Lesson 3 of 11"],[1,"bases","Numeral systems: binary, octal and hexadecimal","People count in base 10 because we have ten fingers. Computers store everything as 0s and 1s, so programmers often use base 2 and its friends base 8 and base 16, which are short ways to write long rows of bits. Base Digits Python literal Turn into text Example 2 (binary) 0, 1 0b1010 bin(10) 0b1010 == 10 8 (octal) 0 to 7 0o17 oct(15) 0o17 == 15 10 (decimal) 0 to 9 42 str(42) 42 16 (hexadecimal) 0 to 9, A to F 0xFF hex(255) 0xFF == 255 Value of a number written in base bExam favourite value = dkbk + ... + d1b1 + d0b0 Each digit is multiplied by the base to the power of its position, counting from 0 on the right. Digits needed in base 2 bits(n) = ⌊log2 n⌋ + 1 For n > 0. Python gives it as n.bit_length(). 156 needs 8 bits. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 bases.py. Common mistake A leading zero is not allowed on a decimal literal in Python 3: 017 is a SyntaxError. Write 0o17 for octal. Lesson 4 of 11"],[1,"casting","Changing one type into another","What it is Type conversion (casting) makes a new object of another type from a value: int(\"42\") gives the int 42. Why we need it Data often arrives as text: from input(), a file, a web form. To do maths with it you must turn it into a number first. To join a number into a message you may need str(). How it works Call the type like a function: int(), float(), str(), bool(), complex(). Each one has rules about what it accepts. When the value does not fit the rules you get ValueError. Call Result Rule int(\"42\") 42 Text with only digits (and a sign, spaces around) int(3.99) 3 Cuts towards zero, it does not round int(\"3.5\") ValueError A decimal point in the text is not allowed int(\"ff\", 16) 255 A second argument gives the base float(\"3.5\") 3.5 Also accepts \"1e3\", \"inf\", \"nan\" str(7) \"7\" Anything can become text bool(0), bool(\"\") False Zero and empty things are False bool(\"0\"), bool(\" \") True Any non empty text is True, even \"0\" round(2.5), round(3.5) 2, 4 Ties go to the even number (bankers rounding) Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 casting.py. In the exam int(-3.99) is -3, not -4. int() cuts towards zero. math.floor(-3.99) is -4 because floor goes down. Lesson 5 of 11"],[1,"naming","Naming rules and good names","Rule Valid Not valid Letters, digits and underscores only total_2 total-2, total 2 Must not start with a digit price2 2price Must not be a keyword klass, class_ class, for, None Case matters Total and total are two different names Letters from any language are allowed año, naam Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 naming.py. Names that help and names that hurt Good Poor Says what it holds total_price, student_count x, tp, data2 Follows PEP 8 snake_case for variables TotalPrice for a variable (that style is for classes) Avoids built in names items, values list, str, sum (they hide the built in functions) Bottom line: a good name saves a reader from having to guess, so it is worth a few more letters. Common mistake Naming a variable list or str hides the built in. After list = [1, 2], calling list(\"abc\") fails with TypeError: 'list' object is not callable. Lesson 6 of 11"],[1,"assigning","More ways to assign","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 swap.py. Form Meaning a, b = 1, 2 Two names at once. The right side is a tuple that is unpacked a, b = b, a Swap. The right side is worked out fully before any name changes x = y = 0 Both names point to the same object 0 n += 1 Augmented assignment, n = n + 1. Also -=, *=, /=, //=, %=, **= total: int = 0 A type hint. Python does not check it, tools like mypy do (Advanced 3.1) Going deeper: what swap does inside a, b = b, a first evaluates b and a and packs them into a temporary tuple, then unpacks the tuple into a and b. CPython optimises the two name case with a SWAP bytecode instruction instead of building a real tuple, but the meaning is the same. Lesson 7 of 11"],[1,"compare","How Python variables compare with other languages","Variables in three languages Python C Java Type belongs to The object (dynamic typing) The variable, fixed when declared The variable, fixed when declared Declare first? No, the first assignment creates the name Yes: int age; Yes: int age; What a variable holds A reference to an object The value itself, in a box of fixed size A value (primitives) or a reference (objects) Int size Unlimited 32 or 64 bits, can overflow 32 bits (int) or 64 bits (long) Type errors found When the line runs, or earlier with mypy When compiling When compiling Bottom line: Python is quicker to write and change, C and Java catch more mistakes before running. Type hints with mypy give Python some of that safety when a project grows. Lesson 8 of 11"],[1,"maths","The maths: place value and conversions","Base b to decimalExam favourite N = ∑i=0k di · bi di is the digit at position i from the right. Decimal to base bExam favourite d0 = N mod b, N ← N // b, repeat The remainders in reverse order are the digits. Largest number in k bits 2k − 1 8 bits hold 0 to 255. 16 bits hold 0 to 65,535. One hex digit = 4 bits, one octal digit = 3 bits So 0xFF is 1111 1111 and 0o7 is 111. Worked example: 0x2F to decimal 2F has digits 2 and F. F means 15. Position 1: 2 × 161 = 32. Position 0: 15 × 160 = 15. 32 + 15 = 47 Worked example: 45 to binary 45 // 2 = 22 remainder 1. 22 // 2 = 11 remainder 0. 11 // 2 = 5 remainder 1. 5 // 2 = 2 remainder 1. 2 // 2 = 1 remainder 0. 1 // 2 = 0 remainder 1. Read upwards: 101101, so bin(45) is \"0b101101\" Lesson 9 of 11"],[1,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths A variable is a name tag on a value. You can move the tag to another value at any time. Every value is an object with an id, a type and a value. Assignment binds a name to an object and never copies it. int, float, str, bool and None are immutable, so \"changing\" them really makes a new object. Names live in namespaces (dictionaries such as globals()). CPython caches the small ints from -5 to 256 and some strings, so id() can be equal for equal small values, but code must never rely on that. Use == for value, is only for None. CPython objects start with a reference count and a type pointer (PyObject header). An int is a PyLongObject with an array of 30 bit digits. Since 3.12 some objects are immortal (their count never changes), which matters for free threaded builds. Beginner Use clear names, know the six basic types and convert text with int() and float(). Intermediate Explain names and objects, use bin/oct/hex and int(text, base), know why int(3.99) is 3 and round(2.5) is 2. Expert Explain small int caching, immortal objects, int digit storage and why float has about 15.95 decimal digits of precision. Real world scenario: a bank app reading an amount A banking app reads \"1,500.75\" from a text box. It is text, so the app first removes the comma, then calls float() or better decimal.Decimal() for money, checks it is above zero, and only then adds it to the balance. Forgetting the conversion would join texts (\"1500.75\" + \"100\" gives \"1500.75100\") instead of adding numbers. Lesson 10 of 11"],[1,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","These problems use exactly what this chapter teaches. The page gives only a hint. Write your own answer on LeetCode first, then compare with the downloadable solution. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1281 Subtract the Product and Sum of Digits of an Integer Easy Digits with % and // Take the last digit with n % 10 and drop it with n // 10 until n is 0. Keep a product and a sum. 191 Number of 1 Bits Easy Binary bin(n) gives the binary text. Count the \"1\" characters in it. 1290 Convert Binary Number in a Linked List to Integer Easy Place value Start with 0. For each bit, value = value * 2 + bit. Here the list is given as a Python list of bits. 405 Convert a Number to Hexadecimal Easy Base conversion For negative numbers add 2 ** 32 first (two's complement). Then repeat: digit = num % 16, num = num // 16. 504 Base 7 Easy Base conversion Same as base 2 but divide by 7. Handle the sign separately. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 11 of 11"],[1,"recap","Chapter summary","A variable is a name bound to an object. Assignment works out the right side, then sticks the name on the result. It never copies. Every object has an id, a type and a value. The type belongs to the object, not the name (dynamic typing). Basic types: int (any size), float (about 15 to 17 digits), complex, bool (True == 1), str and None. All of them are immutable. Literals: 1_000_000, 1.5e3, 0b1010, 0o17, 0xFF. bin(), oct(), hex() give text, int(text, base) reads it back. Conversions: int() cuts towards zero, round() rounds ties to even, bool() is False only for zero and empty values. Names: letters, digits, underscores, not starting with a digit, not a keyword, case sensitive. Use snake_case and never name a variable list or str. Next: Core 1.3 combines values with operators: + - * / // % **, comparisons, logic and bits. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: describe a value, convert between bases by hand, and clean a typed amount.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[2,"","Operators, expressions and the order Python works them out","Arithmetic, // and %, precedence, comparisons, float precision, logic and short circuit, bitwise operators. · operators · floor division · modulo · precedence · comparison · float precision · and or not · short circuit · bitwise · shift Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Core Python›Step 1: Getting started›Operators Step 1 · Topic 3 of 4 · Beginner Calculate, compare and decide. Learn the seven arithmetic operators, why -7 // 2 is -4, the full priority list, chained comparisons, short circuit logic, bitwise tricks, and why 0.1 + 0.2 is not 0.3. About 50 min11 lessons5 animations13 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Operators: the verbs of a program 2Arithmetic: the seven number operators 3Which operation goes first 4Comparisons and the trouble with floats 5Logical operators and short circuit 6Bitwise operators 7Text operators and assignment shortcuts 8The maths behind the operators 9From beginner to expert 10LeetCode practice 11Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 11"],[2,"idea","Operators: the verbs of a program","Source: Python 3.14 Language Reference, chapter 6 (Expressions), and PCEP-30-02 section 1.4. What it is An operator is a symbol that does something with values, called operands. In 7 + 2 the operator is + and the operands are 7 and 2. A combination of values and operators is an expression, and it always produces a value. Why we need it Programs exist to work things out: a bill total, an average, whether a user may log in. Operators are how we tell Python to calculate, compare and decide. How it works Python reads an expression, decides the order of the operations with fixed priority rules, and works them out one at a time until one value is left. Group Operators Example Result Arithmetic + - * / // % ** 17 // 5 3 Comparison == != < <= > >= 3 < 5 True Logical and or not True and False False Bitwise & | ^ ~ << >> 12 & 10 8 Assignment = += -= *= /= //= %= **= n += 1 n grows by 1 Membership in, not in \"a\" in \"cat\" True Identity is, is not x is None True or False In the exam A unary operator has one operand (-x, not x, ~x). A binary operator has two (a + b). The same symbol - can be either. Lesson 2 of 11"],[2,"arith","Arithmetic: the seven number operators","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 arith.py. True division a / b Always gives a float: 6 / 3 = 2.0. Floor divisionExam favourite a // b = ⌊a ÷ b⌋ Divide, then round down towards minus infinity. 7 // 2 = 3, -7 // 2 = -4. Remainder (modulo)Exam favourite a % b = a − b · (a // b) Has the same sign as b. -7 % 2 = 1. The division identityExam favourite a == (a // b) * b + a % b Always true in Python for b ≠ 0. Use it to check your answers. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 floor.py. Operand types + - * // % ** / int and int int (but int ** negative int is float) float int and float float float bool and int int (True counts as 1) float In the exam C and Java cut integer division towards zero, so -7 / 2 is -3 there. Python floors, so -7 // 2 is -4. Exams love this difference. Lesson 3 of 11"],[2,"precedence","Which operation goes first","When an expression has several operators, Python does not just go left to right. It follows a priority list, the same idea as BODMAS or PEMDAS in school maths, with a few Python twists. Priority (high to low) Operators Direction 1 ( ) brackets innermost first 2 ** right to left 3 +x, -x, ~x (unary) 4 * / // % left to right 5 + - left to right 6 << >> left to right 7 &, then ^, then | left to right 8 comparisons, in, not in, is, is not chained 9 not 10 and 11 or 12 if else (conditional expression), lambda, := Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 precedence.py. Common mistake -2 ** 2 is -4, not 4. The power is done first, then the minus. Write (-2) ** 2 when you mean minus two squared. Lesson 4 of 11"],[2,"compare","Comparisons and the trouble with floats","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 compare.py. Safe float comparisonExam favourite |a − b| ≤ max(rel · max(|a|, |b|), abs_tol) This is math.isclose(a, b). The default rel is 1e-09 and abs_tol is 0. Machine epsilon ε = 2−52 ≈ 2.22 × 10−16 The gap between 1.0 and the next float. sys.float_info.epsilon. Three ways to hold decimal numbers float decimal.Decimal fractions.Fraction Stores base 2, 53 bits base 10, as many digits as you set an exact top and bottom number 0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3 False True (with Decimal(\"0.1\")) True Speed Fastest, done by the chip Slower Slowest Use for Science, graphics, machine learning Money and bills Exact maths with ratios Bottom line: use float by default, Decimal for money, and never test floats with == when the values come from calculations. Lesson 5 of 11"],[2,"logic","Logical operators and short circuit","a b a and b a or b not a True True True True False True False False True False False True False True True False False False False True Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 logic.py. How and and or really work a and b: look at a. If a is falsy (False, 0, \"\", [], None), the answer is a and b is never looked at. Otherwise the answer is b. a or b: look at a. If a is truthy, the answer is a and b is skipped. Otherwise the answer is b. Skipping the right side is called short circuit evaluation In the exam not has a higher priority than and, and and is higher than or. So True or False and False is True or (False and False) = True. Lesson 6 of 11"],[2,"bits","Bitwise operators","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 bitwise.py. Bitwise notExam favourite ~x = −x − 1 Python ints act like two's complement numbers with endless bits. ShiftsExam favourite x << k = x · 2k, x >> k = x // 2k Shifting is a fast way to multiply or divide by powers of 2. Useful tricks x & 1 (odd?), x & (x − 1) == 0 (power of 2?), x ^ x == 0 Used in LeetCode problems such as Single Number and Power of Two. Real world scenario: file permissions on Linux Read, write and run permissions are three bits: 4, 2 and 1. 7 (111) means all three, 5 (101) means read and run. A program checks \"can this user write?\" with perm & 2 != 0. Python's os and stat modules use exactly these bitwise operators. Lesson 7 of 11"],[2,"more","Text operators and assignment shortcuts","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 strops.py. Operator With text Example + Joins two texts \"ab\" + \"cd\" is \"abcd\" * Repeats a text a whole number of times \"ha\" * 3 is \"hahaha\" in Is one text inside another? \"py\" in \"python\" is True == < > Compare letter by letter by Unicode number \"Zebra\" < \"apple\" is True Common mistake \"5\" + 5 is a TypeError. Python never turns a number into text by itself. Use str(5) or an f-string. Lesson 8 of 11"],[2,"maths","The maths behind the operators","Euclidean style division in PythonExam favourite a = q · b + r, q = ⌊a/b⌋, 0 ≤ r < b (b > 0) For b < 0, b < r ≤ 0. Last digit and dropping itExam favourite n % 10, n // 10 Used to loop over the digits of a number. Sum of digits test for 9 n % 9 == digit_sum(n) % 9 Because 10 % 9 = 1, every place value leaves remainder 1. Float spacing near x ulp(x) ≈ |x| · 2−52 math.ulp(x). Bigger numbers have bigger gaps between floats. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 floats.py. Worked example: -17 // 5 and -17 % 5 -17 / 5 = -3.4. Floor (round down) of -3.4 is -4, so -17 // 5 = -4. r = a − b · q = -17 − 5 × (-4) = -17 + 20 = 3. -17 // 5 = -4 and -17 % 5 = 3. Check: -4 × 5 + 3 = -17 Lesson 9 of 11"],[2,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths Operators are like buttons on a calculator. Python presses them in a fixed order: brackets, powers, times and divide, then plus and minus. // floors and % follows the sign of the divisor, so a == (a // b) * b + a % b always holds. and and or short circuit and return an operand. Floats are binary fractions, so compare them with math.isclose(). Every operator calls a special method: a + b tries a.__add__(b), then b.__radd__(a). Comparisons can be chained because a < b < c compiles to a < b and b < c with b evaluated once. Advanced 1.1 builds your own operators. IEEE 754 binary64 gives correctly rounded + - * / and sqrt. Python's repr uses the shortest round trip algorithm (David Gay), and its int ** int uses binary exponentiation in O(log n) multiplications. Errors in long float sums can be cut with math.fsum (Shewchuk's exact summation). Beginner Use + - * / // % ** and comparisons, and add brackets when unsure. Intermediate Know floor division with negatives, short circuit, chained comparisons, bit tricks and float pitfalls. Expert Explain operator dispatch to special methods, IEEE 754 rounding, math.fsum and exponentiation by squaring. Real world scenario: splitting a restaurant bill Six friends share a bill of 1,000 rupees. 1000 // 6 = 166 rupees each, and 1000 % 6 = 4 rupees are left over, so four friends pay 167. With floats, 1000 / 6 = 166.666... cannot be paid exactly. Apps that split bills use // and % exactly like this, and Decimal for the money itself. Lesson 10 of 11"],[2,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","These problems use exactly what this chapter teaches. The page gives only a hint. Write your own answer on LeetCode first, then compare with the downloadable solution. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1009 Complement of Base 10 Integer Easy Bitwise xor with a mask Make a mask of 1s as long as n in binary, (1 << n.bit_length()) - 1, then n ^ mask flips every bit. n = 0 gives 1. 231 Power of Two Easy Bit trick A power of two has exactly one 1 bit, so n & (n - 1) removes it and leaves 0. n must be above 0. 258 Add Digits Easy Maths (digital root) Without a loop: 0 stays 0, otherwise the answer is 1 + (num - 1) % 9. 461 Hamming Distance Easy xor then count bits x ^ y has a 1 exactly where the bits differ. Count the 1s. 29 Divide Two Integers Medium Shifts and truncation Work with absolute values. Subtract the biggest divisor << k that fits, add 1 << k to the answer. LeetCode truncates towards zero and clamps to 32 bits. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 11 of 11"],[2,"recap","Chapter summary","Arithmetic: + - * / // % **. / always gives a float. // rounds down. % takes the sign of the divisor. a == (a // b) * b + a % b. Priority: brackets, **, unary -, * / // %, + -, shifts, & ^ |, comparisons, not, and, or. ** is right to left, the rest left to right. -2 ** 2 = -4. Comparisons give True or False and can be chained: 1 < x < 10. Never compare calculated floats with ==, use math.isclose(). and, or short circuit and return one of their operands: name or \"Guest\". Bitwise: & | ^ ~ << >>. ~x = -x - 1, x << k = x · 2k. Text: + joins, * repeats, in tests membership. \"5\" + 5 is a TypeError. Next: Core 1.4 reads input from the user and formats output with f-strings. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: split a bill with // and %, check a leap year with logical operators, and count set bits.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[3,"","Input, output and formatting text","input() and conversion, split and map, f-strings, the format spec, tables, older formatting, safe input. · input · output · f-string · format spec · str.format · percent formatting · split · map · stdin · stdout · PCEP Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Core Python›Step 1: Getting started›Input and output Step 1 · Topic 4 of 4 · Beginner Make programs that talk with people. Read input and convert it safely, then format output like a pro with f-strings and the format spec: widths, alignment, decimals, separators, percentages and tables. About 45 min11 lessons2 animations6 formulas10 worked problems12 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Input and output: how a program talks to people 2input() always returns text 3f-strings: the modern way to build text 4The format spec: width, alignment, decimals and more 5Older ways: % formatting and str.format() 6Reading input safely 7Where input and output really go 8The maths: widths and rounding 9From beginner to expert 10LeetCode practice 11Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 11"],[3,"idea","Input and output: how a program talks to people","Source: Python 3.14 tutorial chapter 7 (Input and Output), the Format Specification Mini-Language, and PCEP-30-02 section 1.5. What it is Output is what a program shows (print). Input is what it reads from the user while it runs (input). Together they make the program interactive. Why we need it A program that always uses the same numbers is not very useful. Reading input lets one program work for any user, any shop bill, any set of marks. How it works input(prompt) shows the prompt, waits for the user to press Enter and returns what was typed as text. You convert it with int() or float() before doing maths. print() and f-strings turn values back into neat text. Python name = input(\"What is your name? \")age = int(input(\"How old are you? \"))print(\"Hello,\", name)print(\"Next year you will be\", age + 1) Output with the sample input \"Asha\\n25\" What is your name? How old are you? Hello, Asha Next year you will be 26 Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 greet.py. Remember The Run button above feeds the typed answers (Asha and 25) for you. On your own computer, Python waits for you to type them. Real world scenario: a ticket machine at a metro station The machine shows \"Choose your station\" (output), you press a station (input), it shows the fare (output), you insert money (input) and it prints a ticket (output). Every interactive program, from an ATM to a quiz app, is this loop of asking, reading, working out and showing. Lesson 2 of 11"],[3,"inputs","input() always returns text","You want Write User types You get Text name = input(\"Name: \") Asha \"Asha\" A whole number n = int(input(\"N: \")) 42 42 A decimal number x = float(input(\"X: \")) 3.5 3.5 Several numbers on one line a, b = map(int, input().split()) 4 9 4 and 9 Yes or no ok = input(\"y/n? \").strip().lower() == \"y\" Y True Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 many.py. Common mistake input() returns text even when the user types digits. \"5\" + \"5\" is \"55\". Convert first, then calculate. Lesson 3 of 11"],[3,"fstrings","f-strings: the modern way to build text","What it is An f-string is a text with an f before the quotes: f\"Total: {total}\". Anything inside curly brackets is worked out and put into the text. Why we need it Joining texts with + and str() is long and easy to get wrong (\"Total: \" + str(total)). f-strings are shorter, easier to read and also the fastest way. How it works Python finds each {...} field, works out the expression, formats it with the optional spec after a colon, and joins all the pieces. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 fstr.py. Inside the brackets Meaning Example Gives {x} The value as str(x) f\"{3 * 4}\" 12 {x!r} repr(x), shows quotes on text f\"{'hi'!r}\" 'hi' {x = } The expression text, then the value (Python 3.8+) f\"{price = }\" price = 45.5 {x:spec} Formatted with a format spec f\"{3.14159:.2f}\" 3.14 {{ and }} A real curly bracket f\"{{x}}\" {x} Remember Since Python 3.12 (PEP 701) you may reuse the same quotes inside an f-string expression, for example f\"{\", \".join(names)}\". Older versions need the other kind of quote inside. Lesson 4 of 11"],[3,"spec","The format spec: width, alignment, decimals and more","Format spec grammarExam favourite [[fill]align][sign][#][0][width][grouping][.precision][type] Every part is optional, but the order is fixed. align is < > ^ or =. Common types d (int), f (fixed), e (scientific), % (percent), b o x X (bases), s (text) No type means the same as str() for most values. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 spec.py. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 table.py. In the exam .2f rounds the stored binary value, and exact halves go to the even digit: f\"{2.5:.0f}\" gives 2 and f\"{2.675:.2f}\" gives 2.67. Lesson 5 of 11"],[3,"older","Older ways: % formatting and str.format()","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 old.py. Three ways to format text % formatting str.format() f-string Looks like \"%s is %d\" % (name, age) \"{} is {}\".format(name, age) f\"{name} is {age}\" Since Python 1 (copied from C printf) Python 2.6 Python 3.6 Readability Values far from their places Better, numbered or named fields Best, values sit in place Speed Medium Slowest Fastest Still useful for logging messages: log.info(\"%s\", x) templates stored as data, built before the values exist almost everything else Bottom line: use f-strings in new code. Recognise the other two because you will meet them in older code and in exams. Lesson 6 of 11"],[3,"robust","Reading input safely","Users make typing mistakes. A good program checks the input and asks again instead of crashing with a ValueError. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 robust.py. The ask again pattern Loop for ever with while True. Read the text and try to convert it inside try. On ValueError, explain the problem and continue (go back to the top of the loop). If the number is out of range, explain and continue. Only a good value reaches break, which leaves the loop Remember Loops arrive in Core 2.2 and try/except in Core 5.1. You can use this pattern now and understand every line later. Lesson 7 of 11"],[3,"streams","Where input and output really go","Stream Python name Normally Used by Standard input sys.stdin The keyboard input() Standard output sys.stdout The screen print() Standard error sys.stderr The screen, often in red Tracebacks, print(..., file=sys.stderr) Redirecting the streams in a terminalshell $ python3 bill.py < answers.txt > result.txt $ python3 bill.py | sort $ python3 bill.py 2> errors.txt Going deeper: why input() is slow in competitive programming input() reads one line at a time and does extra work for the prompt. For a million lines, sys.stdin.readline() or sys.stdin.read().split() is several times faster. Competitive programmers often write input = sys.stdin.readline at the top of their file. Lesson 8 of 11"],[3,"maths","The maths: widths and rounding","Padding added by a widthExam favourite pad = max(0, width − len(text)) Width is a minimum. It never cuts the text. Centre alignmentExam favourite left = ⌊pad / 2⌋, right = pad − left With an odd pad the extra character goes on the right. Rounding to p places round half to even on the exact binary value f\"{0.125:.2f}\" is 0.12 because 0.125 is exact and 2 is even. Digits of a number len(str(n)) = ⌊log10 n⌋ + 1 For a whole number n > 0. Worked example: f\"{'Menu':*^11}\" The text Menu has 4 characters. pad = 11 − 4 = 7. left = 7 // 2 = 3, right = 7 − 3 = 4. ***Menu**** Lesson 9 of 11"],[3,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths input() asks a question and gives you the answer as text. f-strings let you put values into a sentence. Convert input with int() or float(), split lines with split(). Use the format spec for width, alignment, decimals and separators. Check input with try and a loop. f-strings compile to fast bytecode (FORMAT_VALUE, BUILD_STRING). The spec is passed to the value's __format__ method, so dates, Decimal and your own classes can define their own codes, for example f\"{today:%d %B %Y}\". Python 3.14 adds template strings (t-strings, PEP 750): t\"...\" gives a Template object with the static parts and the values kept apart, so a library can escape values safely for HTML or SQL before joining them. Beginner Read a name and a number, and print a sentence with an f-string. Intermediate Format a bill as a neat table and keep asking until the input is valid. Expert Write __format__ for your own class and explain why t-strings help against injection. Real world scenario: printing a pay slip A payroll script reads hours and rate for each worker, works out the pay and prints a pay slip where every column lines up: names left aligned in 15 characters, amounts right aligned with two decimals and thousands separators. This is exactly f\"{name:<15}{pay:>12,.2f}\". Without widths, columns zigzag and the slip is hard to check. Lesson 10 of 11"],[3,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","These problems use exactly what this chapter teaches. The page gives only a hint. Write your own answer on LeetCode first, then compare with the downloadable solution. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 412 Fizz Buzz Easy Modulo and building text Check divisible by 15 first, then 3, then 5. Build a list of texts. 709 To Lower Case Easy Character codes Without lower(): for \"A\" to \"Z\" add 32 to the character code with ord() and chr(). 1678 Goal Parser Interpretation Easy Text replace Replace \"()\" with \"o\" and then \"(al)\" with \"al\". 2864 Maximum Odd Binary Number Easy Counting and building text Keep one \"1\" for the end (odd). Put all other 1s at the front, then the 0s. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 11 of 11"],[3,"recap","Chapter summary","input(prompt) shows the prompt, waits for Enter and returns text. Use int() or float() before doing maths. Several values on one line: a, b = map(int, input().split()). f-strings put values into text: f\"{name} is {age}\". {x!r} uses repr, {x = } shows the expression too. Format spec: [[fill]align][sign][#][0][width][grouping][.precision][type], for example {amount:>12,.2f}. Width is a minimum, centre puts the extra pad on the right, rounding goes half to even on the stored value. % formatting and str.format() are older. Know them, but use f-strings in new code. Check input in a loop with try and except ValueError, so typing mistakes do not crash the program. Next: Core 2.1 makes decisions with if, elif, else and match. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: format a price line, centre a title by hand, and parse a line of numbers.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[4,"","Making decisions: if, elif, else and match","if, elif, else, truthiness, combining conditions, conditional expression, walrus, match and case, De Morgan. · if · elif · else · conditions · truthy · falsy · ternary · walrus · match · case · pattern matching · De Morgan · PCEP Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Core Python›Step 2: Control flow›Conditions Step 2 · Topic 1 of 2 · Beginner Teach your program to choose. Write if, elif and else chains in the right order, understand truthy and falsy values, combine conditions cleanly, use one line conditional expressions, and match data by its shape with match and case. About 45 min10 lessons2 animations5 formulas10 worked problems12 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Programs that choose 2if, elif and else chains 3Truthy and falsy values 4Combining conditions 5One line choices: the conditional expression and the walrus 6match and case: pattern matching 7The maths: boolean algebra 8From beginner to expert 9LeetCode practice 10Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 10"],[4,"idea","Programs that choose","Source: Python 3.14 tutorial sections 4.1 and 4.7, Language Reference 8.1 and 8.6, PCEP-30-02 section 2.1. What it is A conditional statement runs a block of code only when a condition is True. The condition is any expression, and Python asks \"is it truthy?\". Why we need it Real decisions depend on data: pass or fail, discount or no discount, access allowed or denied. Without conditions a program does the same thing every time. How it works Python works out the condition. If it is truthy, the indented block under it runs. If not, the block is skipped and Python moves to the next elif, the else, or the code after the statement. Real world scenario: a traffic signal A driver checks the signal: if it is red, stop. Otherwise if it is yellow, slow down. Otherwise (green), go. The driver checks in that order and does exactly one thing. That is an if, elif, else chain. Checking \"green\" first and then also checking \"red\" separately would be dangerous, and in code it gives the wrong answer too. Python temperature = 31if temperature > 30:print(\"It is hot. Drink water.\")print(\"Have a nice day.\") # always runsmarks = 38if marks >= 40:print(\"Pass\")else:print(\"Fail, try again\") Output It is hot. Drink water. Have a nice day. Fail, try again Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 basic.py. Lesson 2 of 10"],[4,"chain","if, elif and else chains","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 grade.py. Rules for a chain A chain starts with one if, may have any number of elif, and may end with one else. Python checks the conditions from the top. The first truthy one wins and only its block runs. After a block runs, every remaining elif and else of the chain is skipped without being checked. Put the strictest condition first, for example score >= 90 before score >= 75 elif chain, separate ifs, or nesting if / elif / else separate if statements nested if How many blocks can run Exactly one (or none without else) Any number Depends on the tree Use when The cases exclude each other (a grade) Several things can be true together (flags) A second question only makes sense after the first Risk Wrong order catches cases too early Several outputs when you wanted one Deep indentation that is hard to read Bottom line: an elif chain is the usual choice. Use separate ifs for independent checks and keep nesting to two levels. Lesson 3 of 10"],[4,"truthy","Truthy and falsy values","A condition does not have to be True or False. Python accepts any value and decides if it counts as true. Falsy (counts as False) Truthy (counts as True) False, None True 0, 0.0, 0j any other number, even -2 and 0.001 \"\" (empty text) \"0\", \" \", \"False\" (non empty text) [], (), {}, set(), range(0) [0], (None,), {\"a\": 1} (non empty collections) Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 truthy.py. Remember Write if items: instead of if len(items) > 0:, and if not name: instead of if name == \"\":. PEP 8 recommends this short form. But write if x is None: when you really mean None, because 0 and \"\" are falsy too. Lesson 4 of 10"],[4,"combine","Combining conditions","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 combine.py. Need Write Instead of Both must hold a and b a nested if inside if Either is enough a or b two ifs with the same block A range 13 <= age <= 19 age >= 13 and age <= 19 One of several values day in (\"Sat\", \"Sun\") day == \"Sat\" or day == \"Sun\" The opposite not done done == False Common mistake if day == \"Sat\" or \"Sun\": is always True. Python reads it as (day == \"Sat\") or \"Sun\", and \"Sun\" is truthy. Write day in (\"Sat\", \"Sun\"). Lesson 5 of 10"],[4,"ternary","One line choices: the conditional expression and the walrus","Conditional expressionExam favourite value_if_true if condition else value_if_false An expression, so it gives a value: kind = \"even\" if n % 2 == 0 else \"odd\". Assignment expression (walrus) (name := expression) Python 3.8+. Assigns and gives the value in one go, useful inside if and while conditions. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 ternary.py. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 walrus.py. Remember Do not chain many conditional expressions in one line. a if x else b if y else c is legal but hard to read. Use an if, elif, else statement instead. Lesson 6 of 10"],[4,"match","match and case: pattern matching","What it is match compares a value (the subject) with a list of patterns. The first pattern that fits runs its block. Patterns can check the shape of the data and pull values out of it at the same time. Why we need it Long elif chains that look inside lists, tuples and dicts are hard to read. match describes the shape you expect directly, like a picture of the data. How it works Python tries each case from the top. A literal must be equal, a name captures the value, [a, b] needs a sequence of length 2, *rest collects the remaining items, {\"key\": v} looks inside a dict, | means or, if adds a guard, and _ matches anything. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 match.py. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 matchmore.py. Pattern Fits Example Literal A value equal to it case 404: Capture Anything, and binds it to the name case code: Wildcard Anything, binds nothing case _: Or Any of the alternatives case 200 | 201: Sequence A list or tuple of that length case [\"go\", direction]: Star Collects the rest of a sequence case [\"drop\", *things]: Mapping A dict with those keys (others allowed) case {\"x\": x, \"y\": y}: Class An instance of the class, with attributes case Point(x=0, y=y): Guard The pattern plus a condition case int(n) if n > 0: Common mistake A plain name in a case is a capture, not a comparison. case RED: catches everything and binds it to RED. To compare with a constant, use a dotted name such as case Color.RED:. Lesson 7 of 10"],[4,"maths","The maths: boolean algebra","De Morgan lawsExam favourite not (a and b) == (not a) or (not b), not (a or b) == (not a) and (not b) Use them to simplify or flip a condition. Number of pathsExam favourite k independent if statements give up to 2k combinations An elif chain with k conditions and an else has exactly k + 1 paths. Distributive law a and (b or c) == (a and b) or (a and c) Like a × (b + c) = ab + ac in school algebra. Worked example: flip \"is a teenager\" Teenager: 13 <= age and age <= 19. Not a teenager: not (13 <= age and age <= 19). De Morgan: not (13 <= age) or not (age <= 19). age < 13 or age > 19 a b not (a and b) not a or not b True True False False True False True True False True True True False False True True Lesson 8 of 10"],[4,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths if is a fork in the road. Python checks a question and takes one path. elif adds more questions, else is the path when every answer was no. Conditions use truthiness. A chain runs exactly one block. Combine with and, or, not, chained comparisons and in. Use the conditional expression for simple one line choices and match for data shaped choices. match compiles to a sequence of type checks, length checks and lookups. Sequence patterns do not match str, bytes or bytearray, so case [a, b]: never fits \"ab\". Class patterns use __match_args__ for positional parts. Branches cost time on real chips when they are hard to predict (branch misprediction), which is why sorted data can make an if inside a loop faster in compiled languages. In testing, branch coverage (every if both ways) and MC/DC coverage (each condition shown to matter, required for avionics software) measure how well conditions are tested. Beginner Write if, elif, else chains in the right order and test every branch. Intermediate Use truthiness, De Morgan, conditional expressions and match with captures and guards. Expert Explain how match is compiled, why sequence patterns skip strings, and design tests for branch and MC/DC coverage. Real world scenario: an online shop checkout At checkout, the shop decides delivery: if the order is above 499 rupees, delivery is free. Otherwise, if the customer is a member, it is 20 rupees. Otherwise it is 40. A coupon code is then matched: \"WELCOME10\", \"FESTIVE\", or anything else is \"invalid code\". The first part is an elif chain, the second is a natural match statement. Lesson 9 of 10"],[4,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","These problems use exactly what this chapter teaches. The page gives only a hint. Write your own answer on LeetCode first, then compare with the downloadable solution. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1455 Check If a Word Occurs As a Prefix of Any Word in a Sentence Easy Condition inside a loop Split the sentence. Return the 1 based position of the first word that starts with searchWord, else -1. 2129 Capitalize the Title Easy if else on length Words of length 1 or 2 become all lower case. Longer words get a capital first letter and the rest lower. 1672 Richest Customer Wealth Easy Keep the biggest with if Add up each row. Keep the biggest sum seen so far with an if. 2678 Number of Senior Citizens Easy Condition on a slice Characters 11 and 12 of each passenger text are the age. Count ages above 60. 1344 Angle Between Hands of a Clock Medium Formula and min Minute hand at 6 × minutes degrees, hour hand at 30 × (hour % 12) + 0.5 × minutes. Return the smaller of the gap and 360 minus the gap. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 10 of 10"],[4,"recap","Chapter summary","if runs its block only when the condition is truthy. else runs when it is not. elif adds more checks in between. In a chain Python checks from the top, runs the first truthy branch only, and skips the rest. Put the strictest test first. Falsy values: False, None, 0, 0.0, \"\", and empty collections. Everything else is truthy, even \"0\" and [0]. Combine with and, or, not, 13 <= age <= 19 and day in (\"Sat\", \"Sun\"). day == \"Sat\" or \"Sun\" is always True. One line choices: x if cond else y. The walrus (n := len(data)) assigns inside a condition. match tries patterns from the top: literals, captures, _, |, [a, *rest], {\"k\": v}, Class(...), and guards with if. De Morgan: not (a and b) == not a or not b. k independent ifs give up to 2k paths to test. Next: Core 2.2 repeats work with while and for loops, range(), break, continue and else. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: a ticket price chain, a leap year with match, and a triangle classifier.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[5,"","Loops: while, for, range, break and continue","while, for, range, break, continue, pass, loop else, loop patterns, nested loops, enumerate and zip, loop maths. · loops · while · for · range · break · continue · pass · for else · nested loops · enumerate · zip · iteration · PCEP Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Core Python›Step 2: Control flow›Loops Step 2 · Topic 2 of 2 · Beginner Make the computer do repeated work. Learn while and for loops, every detail of range(), break, continue, pass and the loop else, the patterns every programmer uses, nested loops, enumerate and zip, and how to count exactly how many times a loop runs. About 50 min11 lessons1 animation9 formulas10 worked problems12 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Why we need loops 2for loops and range() 3break, continue, pass and else 4Loop patterns every programmer uses 5Loops inside loops 6enumerate, zip, reversed and sorted 7When to use while 8The maths of loops 9From beginner to expert 10LeetCode practice 11Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 11"],[5,"idea","Why we need loops","Source: Python 3.14 tutorial sections 4.2 to 4.6 and PCEP-30-02 section 2.2. What it is A loop runs the same block of code again and again. Python has two loops: while repeats as long as a condition is True, and for repeats once for each item of a sequence. Why we need it Computers are good at boring, repeated work: printing 1,000 pay slips, checking every row of a file, trying every password on a list. Writing the same line 1,000 times is impossible to maintain. A loop writes it once. How it works Each repetition is called an iteration or a turn. Before every turn, Python checks if it should go on: is the condition still True (while), or is there another item (for)? Real world scenario: washing a pile of plates \"While there are dirty plates, take one and wash it\" is a while loop: you do not know how many plates there are, you just check before each one. \"For each of the 12 plates on the table, wash it\" is a for loop: you walk through a known collection. If you forget to take a plate off the pile, the while loop never ends. In code that is an infinite loop. Python count = 1while count <= 5:print(\"Turn\", count) count += 1# without this line the loop never endsprint(\"Done, count is now\", count) Output Turn 1 Turn 2 Turn 3 Turn 4 Turn 5 Done, count is now 6 Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 while.py. Python for fruit in [\"apple\", \"mango\", \"banana\"]:print(\"I like\", fruit)for letter in\"Hi!\":print(letter)for i inrange(3): # 0, 1, 2print(\"range gives\", i) Output I like apple I like mango I like banana H i ! range gives 0 range gives 1 range gives 2 Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 for.py. Lesson 2 of 11"],[5,"range","for loops and range()","range(start, stop, step)Exam favourite start, start + step, start + 2·step, ... while still before stop stop is never included. start defaults to 0, step to 1. step must not be 0. How many numbers range givesExam favourite len = max(0, ⌈(stop − start) / step⌉) range(1, 10, 3) has ⌈9/3⌉ = 3 numbers: 1, 4, 7. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 range.py. In the exam range(5) is 0, 1, 2, 3, 4: five numbers, but 5 itself is not included. This \"stop is excluded\" rule matches list indexes, so range(len(items)) gives every valid index. Going deeper: range is lazy range(10**12) does not build a trillion numbers. It is a small object that knows start, stop and step and works out each number when asked. len(), indexing and the in test are all O(1) maths, for example 999 in range(0, 10**12, 3) is answered instantly. Lesson 3 of 11"],[5,"control","break, continue, pass and else","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 breakcont.py. Statement What it does Typical use break Leaves the innermost loop at once Stop searching once you found what you wanted continue Skips the rest of this turn and starts the next Ignore bad rows and carry on pass Does nothing. A placeholder where a statement is needed An empty block you will write later else (on a loop) Runs once after the loop if it was not stopped by break Say \"not found\" after a search In the exam The loop else is not \"else if the loop did not run\". It means \"no break happened\". A for loop over an empty list runs its else block. Lesson 4 of 11"],[5,"patterns","Loop patterns every programmer uses","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 patterns.py. Pattern Start with Each turn Built in shortcut Accumulator (sum) total = 0 total += x sum(items) Counter count = 0 if test: count += 1 sum(1 for x in items if test) Running maximum best = first item if x > best: best = x max(items) Search found = None if test: found = x and break next((x for x in items if test), None) Build a new list result = [] result.append(f(x)) [f(x) for x in items] (Core 4.3) Sentinel read a value while value != \"stop\": ... read again Remember Learn the loops first, then the shortcuts. In an interview you may be asked to write max() yourself, and the running maximum pattern is the answer. Lesson 5 of 11"],[5,"nested","Loops inside loops","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 nested.py. Turns of a nested loopExam favourite outer × inner 3 rows × 5 columns = 15 prints. Three nested loops of n each run n3 times. Triangle loopExam favourite 1 + 2 + ... + n = n(n + 1)/2 When the inner loop runs i times on turn i, the total is a triangular number. Common mistake break only leaves the inner loop. To leave two loops at once, put them in a function and use return, or set a flag and check it in the outer loop. Lesson 6 of 11"],[5,"pythonic","enumerate, zip, reversed and sorted","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 enumzip.py. Looping over a list with positions Index loop enumerate Code for i in range(len(names)): print(i, names[i]) for i, name in enumerate(names): print(i, name) Reads Two steps: index, then look up Says what you mean Mistakes Off by one, wrong list in names[i] Fewer When you still need indexes Changing items in place, comparing names[i] with names[i + 1] Bottom line: loop over the items directly, add enumerate when you need the position and zip when you walk two lists together. Common mistake zip stops at the shortest list. zip([1, 2, 3], \"ab\") gives only two pairs. Python 3.10 added zip(..., strict=True) to raise an error when the lengths differ. Lesson 7 of 11"],[5,"whileuse","When to use while","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 digits.py. for or while for while Number of turns Known: one per item Unknown: until a condition changes Stops by itself Yes, when items run out Only if your code makes the condition False Examples Each student, each line of a file, range(10) Digits of a number, asking until the input is valid, a game loop, Collatz Bottom line: use for whenever you walk over a collection or a range. Use while when you wait for something to happen. Remember An infinite loop is not always a bug: servers and games run while True: with a break or return inside. In the REPL or a terminal, press Ctrl+C to stop a runaway loop. Lesson 8 of 11"],[5,"maths","The maths of loops","Length of a rangeExam favourite len(range(a, b, s)) = max(0, ⌈(b − a)/s⌉) Works for negative steps too: range(10, 0, -3) has ⌈-10/-3⌉ = 4 numbers. Last number of a range a + (len − 1) · s range(0, 100, 7)[-1] = 0 + 14 × 7 = 98. Sum of an arithmetic sequenceExam favourite S = n(first + last)/2 sum(range(1, 101)) = 100 × 101 / 2 = 5050 (the young Gauss story). Turns to halve n down to 1 ⌊log2 n⌋ while n > 1: n //= 2 runs about log2 n times. This is why binary search is fast. Digits of n (turns of the digit loop) ⌊log10 n⌋ + 1 The digit loop above ran 5 times for 90731. Worked example: how many times does the inner print run? for i in range(1, 5): for j in range(i): print(j) On turn i, the inner loop runs i times: 1, 2, 3, 4. Total = 1 + 2 + 3 + 4. 4 × 5 / 2 = 10 Lesson 9 of 11"],[5,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths A loop repeats a job. for does it once per item, while keeps going until a condition says stop. range(start, stop, step) excludes stop. break leaves, continue skips, else runs when no break happened. Use accumulator, counter, maximum and search patterns, and enumerate and zip instead of index loops. for works with any iterable: it calls iter() once and next() each turn until StopIteration (Advanced 2.1). Changing a list while looping over it skips items, so loop over a copy (items[:]) or build a new list. Loop analysis is the start of algorithm analysis: counting turns gives the running time, O(n) for one loop, O(n2) for two nested loops. Loop invariants (a statement true before and after every turn) are how computer scientists prove that a loop is correct, as in Hoare logic. Beginner Write while and for loops, use range() correctly, and stop with break. Intermediate Use the standard patterns, nested loops, enumerate and zip, and count how many times a loop runs. Expert Explain the iterator protocol behind for, avoid changing a list while looping, and prove a loop correct with an invariant. Real world scenario: an ATM PIN check An ATM gives you three tries for your PIN. That is a loop of at most 3 turns: if the PIN is right, break and show the menu. If all three turns end without a break, the else block keeps the card. The for loop with else says this exactly: for attempt in range(3): ... break, else: keep the card. Lesson 10 of 11"],[5,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","These problems use exactly what this chapter teaches. The page gives only a hint. Write your own answer on LeetCode first, then compare with the downloadable solution. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1480 Running Sum of 1d Array Easy Accumulator Keep a total. Each turn add the current number and append the total. 9 Palindrome Number Easy Digit loop with % and // Negative numbers are not palindromes. Build the reversed number digit by digit and compare. 1295 Find Numbers with Even Number of Digits Easy Counter Count the digits of each number with len(str(n)) and count the even ones. 7 Reverse Integer Medium Digit loop and range check Reverse the absolute value with a digit loop, put the sign back, and return 0 if it leaves the 32 bit range. 202 Happy Number Easy while loop with a seen set Replace n by the sum of the squares of its digits. Stop at 1 (happy) or when a number repeats (a cycle). 1137 N-th Tribonacci Number Easy Loop with three running values Keep the last three numbers a, b, c and move them forward n times. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 11 of 11"],[5,"recap","Chapter summary","while repeats while a condition is True. Something in the body must change the condition, or the loop never ends. for repeats once per item of a list, text, range or any other iterable. range(start, stop, step) excludes stop. Its length is max(0, ⌈(stop − start)/step⌉). break leaves the loop, continue skips to the next turn, pass does nothing, and a loop else runs when no break happened. Patterns: accumulator, counter, running maximum, search with break, building a list, sentinel loop. Nested loops run outer × inner times. A triangle loop runs n(n + 1)/2 times. Use enumerate for positions, zip for walking lists together, reversed and sorted for order. Next: Core 3.1 opens up strings: indexing, slicing, methods and how text is stored as Unicode. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: FizzBuzz as text, prime check with a loop else, and a digit sum loop.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[6,"","Strings: text, slicing, methods and Unicode","str, quotes, escapes, indexing, slicing, immutability, methods, split and join, Unicode, UTF-8, string maths. · strings · str · slicing · indexing · string methods · split · join · Unicode · UTF-8 · ord · chr · immutable · PCEP · PCAP Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Core Python›Step 3: Data collections›Strings Step 3 · Topic 1 of 4 · Beginner Work with text the way real programs do. Make strings, use escape characters, index and slice them, learn why they never change, use the most important methods, split and join text, and see how Unicode and UTF-8 store every language. About 60 min14 lessons2 animations8 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1What a string is 2Escape characters and raw strings 3Indexing and slicing 4Strings never change 5Joining, repeating, searching and comparing 6The most used string methods 7split and join 8Working through text with loops 9Unicode, code points and UTF-8 10str, bytes and lists of characters 11The maths of strings 12From beginner to expert 13LeetCode practice 14Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 14"],[6,"idea","What a string is","Source: Python 3.14 library reference, Text Sequence Type str. PCEP-30-02 section 3.4 and PCAP-31-03 section 2. What it is A string (type str) is a piece of text: a sequence of characters such as letters, digits, spaces and symbols. You write it between quotes: single, double, or three of either for many lines. Why we need it Almost every program works with text: names, messages, file contents, web pages, passwords. Numbers alone cannot hold \"Hello, Asha\". The str type stores any text in any language and gives you more than 40 ready made tools (methods) to work with it. How it works Python keeps the characters in order, so each one has a position number (an index) starting at 0. You can read one character, cut out a part (a slice), join strings and search inside them. A string never changes after it is made. Every change builds a new string. Real world scenario: a name on a train ticket A train ticket prints \"RAVI KUMAR\". The booking app stored what you typed, \" ravi kumar \", then removed the extra spaces with strip(), made it upper case with upper(), and printed it. It checked the PNR number with isdigit() and cut the coach number out of \"B2-34\" with a slice. Every one of these steps is a string method from this chapter. Python a = 'single quotes'b = \"double quotes\"c = \"It's easy\"# an apostrophe inside double quotesd = 'She said \"hi\"'# double quotes inside single quotese = \"\"\"Three quoteslet a string run overmany lines\"\"\"print(a, b, c, d, sep=\" | \")print(e)print(len(\"hello\"), len(\"\"), len(\" \"))print(str(42) + \"!\", str(3.5), str(True)) Output single quotes | double quotes | It's easy | She said \"hi\" Three quotes let a string run over many lines 5 0 2 42! 3.5 True Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 make.py. Remember Single and double quotes make exactly the same string. Pick one style and keep it. Use the other kind when the text itself contains a quote. Lesson 2 of 14"],[6,"escape","Escape characters and raw strings","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 escape.py. You write You get Name \\n a new line newline \\t a tab tab \\\\ one backslash backslash \\' and \\\" a quote inside quotes of the same kind quote \\u20b9 the character with code point 20B9 (the rupee sign) Unicode escape In the exam A backslash starts an escape sequence: the backslash and the next character together stand for one special character. That is why len of the new line escape is 1. In a raw string (r before the quote) Python keeps every backslash, which is handy for Windows paths and regular expressions (Advanced 6.1). Lesson 3 of 14"],[6,"index","Indexing and slicing","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 index.py. Index of a characterExam favourite 0, 1, ..., len(s) − 1 from the left, and −1, −2, ..., −len(s) from the right s[-k] is the same as s[len(s) − k]. SliceExam favourite s[start:stop:step] start is included, stop is not. Leave out start for \"from the beginning\" and stop for \"to the end\". Length of a simple slice len(s[a:b]) = b − a when 0 ≤ a ≤ b ≤ len(s). \"PYTHON\"[1:4] is \"YTH\", 3 characters. In the exam A single index outside the string raises IndexError. A slice outside the string never fails: it just gives what is there, or an empty string. Lesson 4 of 14"],[6,"immut","Strings never change","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 immut.py. Changing text str (immutable) list of characters (mutable) Change one character Not allowed: build a new string chars[0] = \"R\" works Safe to share Yes, nobody can change it behind your back No, every name sees the change Can be a dict key or set item Yes No Best for Storing and passing text around Many small edits, then \"\".join(chars) at the end Bottom line: keep text as str. If you need hundreds of edits in place, turn it into a list, edit, and join it back. Going deeper: Why Python made strings immutable Because a string cannot change, Python can work out its hash once and keep it, which makes strings fast dict keys. Two names can share the same string object without risk, and Python reuses (interns) many short strings. Use == to compare text. Never use is, because whether two equal strings are the same object depends on the interpreter. Lesson 5 of 14"],[6,"ops","Joining, repeating, searching and comparing","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 ops.py. Operation Example Result + joins \"ab\" + \"cd\" \"abcd\" * repeats \"ha\" * 3 \"hahaha\" in searches \"cat\" in \"concatenate\" True < > compare \"apple\" < \"banana\" True ord() ord(\"A\") 65 chr() chr(97) \"a\" Common mistake Strings are compared letter by letter by code point, not by dictionary order. All upper case letters (65 to 90) come before all lower case letters (97 to 122), so \"Zebra\" < \"apple\" is True. \"10\" < \"9\" is True too, because \"1\" comes before \"9\". Turn text into numbers before comparing numbers. Lesson 6 of 14"],[6,"methods","The most used string methods","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 methods.py. Group Methods Note Clean strip(), lstrip(), rstrip(), removeprefix(), removesuffix() strip() with no argument removes spaces, tabs and new lines at both ends Case upper(), lower(), title(), capitalize(), swapcase(), casefold() Use casefold() to compare text without caring about case Search find(), rfind(), index(), rindex(), count(), startswith(), endswith() find() gives −1 when not found, index() raises ValueError Test isdigit(), isalpha(), isalnum(), isspace(), isupper(), islower() Return True or False. An empty string gives False Change replace(old, new, count) Returns a new string Pad zfill(), center(), ljust(), rjust() Make columns of the same width Common mistake Every method returns a new string. s.upper() alone on a line does nothing useful. Write s = s.upper() to keep the result. Lesson 7 of 14"],[6,"split","split and join","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 split.py. Building one string from many pieces Adding in a loop (s += piece) \"sep\".join(pieces) Code result = \"\" then result += w + \", \" each turn \", \".join(words) Extra separator at the end Yes, you must remove it No Speed Each += may copy the whole text so far: up to O(n2) work Works out the size once and copies each piece once: O(n) Pieces must be Anything you convert with str() All strings already Bottom line: collect the pieces in a list and join them once at the end. In the exam split() with no argument and split(\" \") are different. \"a b\".split() gives [\"a\", \"b\"], but \"a b\".split(\" \") gives [\"a\", \"\", \"b\"] because there are two spaces. Lesson 8 of 14"],[6,"build","Working through text with loops","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 build.py. Remember Most text problems follow one plan: walk through the characters with for ch in text, test each one (isalpha, isdigit, a comparison), and collect or count the ones you want. The Caesar cipher above uses ord() and chr() to do arithmetic on letters. Lesson 9 of 14"],[6,"unicode","Unicode, code points and UTF-8","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 unicode.py. What it is Unicode gives every character of every language a number called a code point. \"A\" is 65, \"₹\" is 8377 (U+20B9). UTF-8 is the most common way to store those numbers as bytes. Why we need it Old ASCII had only 128 characters, enough for English but not for Hindi, Tamil, Chinese or emojis. Unicode covers more than 150,000 characters, so one program can handle every language. How it works A Python str holds code points, so len() counts characters. When you save to a file or send over a network, encode() turns the text into bytes and decode() turns bytes back into text. UTF-8 uses 1 byte for ASCII and 2, 3 or 4 bytes for other characters. Code point range UTF-8 bytes Examples 0 to 127 1 A, z, 5, space 128 to 2,047 2 é, ñ, Greek and Arabic letters 2,048 to 65,535 3 ₹, Hindi and most Indian scripts, Chinese 65,536 to 1,114,111 4 emojis, old scripts Common mistake Always say the encoding when you open a text file: open(name, encoding=\"utf-8\"). Otherwise Python uses the system default, which is not UTF-8 on every Windows computer (Core 5.3). Lesson 10 of 14"],[6,"versus","str, bytes and lists of characters","Three ways to hold text str bytes list of str Holds Unicode characters Numbers 0 to 255 Any items, here single characters Written as \"café\" b\"caf\\xc3\\xa9\" [\"c\", \"a\", \"f\", \"é\"] Changeable No No (bytearray is) Yes len(\"café\") 4 characters 5 bytes in UTF-8 4 items Used for Text you read and show Files, images, network data Editing text one character at a time Bottom line: use str for text inside your program, bytes only at the edges where data goes to a file or the network. Going deeper: How CPython stores a str (PEP 393) CPython looks at the largest code point in a string and picks 1, 2 or 4 bytes per character for the whole string. Pure ASCII text uses 1 byte each, a string with a rupee sign uses 2, and one emoji pushes it to 4. That is why sys.getsizeof(\"a\") is 42 but sys.getsizeof of the emoji chr(128512) is 64 on Python 3.14. Indexing stays O(1) because every character in one string has the same width. Lesson 11 of 14"],[6,"maths","The maths of strings","Number of substrings of a text of length nExam favourite n(n + 1) / 2 Pick a start and an end. \"abc\" has 6: a, b, c, ab, bc, abc. Number of subsequences (keep or drop each letter) 2n Including the empty one. \"abc\" has 8. Number of different arrangements (anagrams)Exam favourite n! / (c1! · c2! · ...) ci is how often each letter repeats. \"banana\": 6! / (3! · 2! · 1!) = 60. Caesar shift of a lower case letter new = (ord(ch) − 97 + k) mod 26 + 97 mod 26 wraps z round to a. Polynomial hash of a text (used in Rabin Karp search) h = (c0·bn−1 + c1·bn−2 + ... + cn−1) mod m Lets a program compare a window of text with a pattern in O(1) by sliding the hash one letter at a time. Worked example: how many slices s[a:b] with a < b does \"PYTHON\" have? n = 6. A slice is fixed by a start a and an end b with 0 ≤ a < b ≤ 6. That is choosing 2 different numbers from the 7 numbers 0 to 6. C(7, 2) = 7 × 6 / 2 = 21 This equals n(n + 1)/2 = 6 × 7 / 2 = 21, the number of substrings by position. Lesson 12 of 14"],[6,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths A string is text in quotes. You can read one character with s[i], cut a part with s[a:b] and join strings with +. Strings are immutable, so methods return new strings. Use strip, split, join, replace, find and the is tests. Compare with ==, and know that comparison goes by code point. A str is a sequence of Unicode code points. encode and decode move between text and bytes. Build large text with join, not +=. Use casefold() for case blind matching and say encoding=\"utf-8\" for files. CPython stores each string with a fixed width of 1, 2 or 4 bytes per character (PEP 393), caches its hash, and interns many identifiers. Real text processing must also think about Unicode normalisation (unicodedata.normalize, because \"é\" can be one or two code points) and grapheme clusters that look like one letter but are several code points. Beginner Make strings, index and slice them, and use upper, lower, strip and len. Intermediate Use split, join, find, replace and the is tests, loop over characters and compare strings correctly. Expert Explain code points, UTF-8 and the cost of +=, use slicing with negative steps without thinking, and handle text from any language. Lesson 13 of 14"],[6,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","These problems use exactly what this chapter teaches. The page gives only a hint. Write your own answer on LeetCode first, then compare with the downloadable solution. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 344 Reverse String Easy Two pointers The input is a list of characters. Swap the first and last, then move both ends inward. 125 Valid Palindrome Easy Clean then compare Keep only letters and digits in lower case, then compare the text with its reverse. 58 Length of Last Word Easy split split() with no argument ignores extra spaces. Take the last word. 1768 Merge Strings Alternately Easy Index loop over the longer length Loop i up to the longer length and add word1[i] and word2[i] when they exist. 14 Longest Common Prefix Easy Compare column by column Take the first word. For each position, check that every other word has the same character there. 28 Find the Index of the First Occurrence in a String Easy Sliding window of slices Try every start i and compare haystack[i:i + len(needle)] with needle. str.find does this for you. 13 Roman to Integer Easy Look ahead one character Add each value, but subtract it when the next symbol is bigger (IV, IX, XL). 151 Reverse Words in a String Medium split and join split() removes the extra spaces. Reverse the list of words and join with one space. 6 Zigzag Conversion Medium Simulate rows Keep one string per row. Walk down and up the rows, adding each character to the current row. 5 Longest Palindromic Substring Medium Expand around the centre Every palindrome has a centre: a letter or the gap between two letters. Grow outwards from each of the 2n − 1 centres. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 14 of 14"],[6,"recap","Chapter summary","A str is an ordered, immutable sequence of Unicode characters, written in single, double or triple quotes. A backslash starts an escape such as new line or tab. A raw string r\"...\" keeps backslashes. Indexing s[i] counts from 0 on the left and from −1 on the right. Slicing s[a:b:c] includes a, excludes b, and never fails. Strings cannot change. Methods such as upper, strip and replace return a new string. + joins, * repeats, in searches, and < compares by code point, so \"Z\" < \"a\" and \"10\" < \"9\". split cuts text into a list and \"sep\".join(list) glues it back. join is the fast way to build big text. ord and chr move between characters and code points. encode and decode move between str and UTF-8 bytes. Next: Core 3.2 moves to lists: a sequence you can change, with append, sort, slicing, copying and how CPython grows a list. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: a palindrome sentence check, a run length text compressor and a word capitaliser without title().practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[7,"","Lists: storing, changing, sorting and copying","list methods, sort vs sorted, aliases and copies, matrices, bubble sort, unpacking, list growth and costs. · lists · list methods · append · sort · sorted · copy · deepcopy · matrix · bubble sort · mutable · amortised · PCEP Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Core Python›Step 3: Data collections›Lists Step 3 · Topic 2 of 4 · Beginner Keep many values in one place and change them. Learn to make lists, add, remove and find items, sort them two ways, understand names and copies, build matrices, write bubble sort by hand and see how CPython makes append fast. About 65 min16 lessons2 animations10 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1What a list is 2Adding and changing items 3Removing items 4Finding items 5Sorting: sort() and sorted() 6Names, aliases and copies 7Shallow copy, deep copy and lists of lists 8Lists in lists: matrices 9Bubble sort, step by step 10Unpacking, swapping and a first look at comprehensions 11How CPython makes append fast 12Lists compared with other collections 13The maths of lists 14From beginner to expert 15LeetCode practice 16Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 16"],[7,"idea","What a list is","Source: Python 3.14 tutorial section 5.1 and PCEP-30-02 section 3.1. What it is A list is an ordered collection of items in square brackets, such as [72, 45, 91]. The items can be of any type and a list can change: you can add, remove and replace items. Why we need it With one variable per value, 500 student marks would need 500 names. A list keeps all of them under one name, keeps their order, and lets a loop visit each one. It is the most used collection in Python. How it works Like a string, each item has an index from 0, and you can slice. Unlike a string, a list is mutable: methods such as append and sort change the list itself. In CPython a list is an array of references to objects, with some spare room at the end so adding is fast. Real world scenario: a shopping list on the fridge Your family keeps one shopping list on the fridge. Anyone can add \"milk\" at the bottom (append), squeeze \"eggs\" in at the top (insert), cross out \"sugar\" (remove) or put it in shop order (sort). Everyone looks at the same paper, so a change by one person is seen by all. That last point is exactly how two names for one Python list behave. Python marks = [72, 45, 91, 38]mixed = [\"Asha\", 21, 5.8, True] # a list can hold any typesempty = []letters = list(\"abc\") # list() turns any iterable into a listnumbers = list(range(1, 6))print(marks, mixed, empty, letters, numbers)print(len(marks), marks[0], marks[-1], marks[1:3])print(91in marks, 50notin marks)print(min(marks), max(marks), sum(marks), sum(marks) / len(marks)) Output [72, 45, 91, 38] ['Asha', 21, 5.8, True] [] ['a', 'b', 'c'] [1, 2, 3, 4, 5] 4 72 38 [45, 91] True True 38 91 246 61.5 Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 make.py. Lesson 2 of 16"],[7,"change","Adding and changing items","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 change.py. Method What it does Cost append(x) Add x at the end O(1) on average insert(i, x) Add x before index i, the rest move right O(n) extend(items) Add every item of another iterable at the end O(k) for k new items items[i] = x Replace one item O(1) items[a:b] = other Replace a slice with any number of items O(n) In the exam append adds one item, even if that item is a list. [1, 2].append([3, 4]) gives [1, 2, [3, 4]]. extend adds the items one by one and gives [1, 2, 3, 4]. Lesson 3 of 16"],[7,"remove","Removing items","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 remove.py. Way Removes Gives back If missing remove(x) The first item equal to x None ValueError pop() The last item The item IndexError on an empty list pop(i) The item at index i The item IndexError del items[i] or del items[a:b] By index or slice Nothing (it is a statement) IndexError for a single index clear() Everything None Never fails Common mistake Do not remove items from a list while a for loop is walking over it. The loop skips the item after each removal. Loop over a copy (for x in items[:]) or build a new list instead. Lesson 4 of 16"],[7,"search","Finding items","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 search.py. Remember x in items checks each item from the left until it finds a match, so it takes O(n) time. For many lookups in a big collection use a set or a dict (Core 3.3 and 3.4), which answer in O(1) on average. Lesson 5 of 16"],[7,"sort","Sorting: sort() and sorted()","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 sort.py. Two ways to sort items.sort() sorted(items) Changes the original Yes No Returns None A new list Works on Lists only Any iterable: tuples, strings, sets, dict keys Options key=..., reverse=True key=..., reverse=True Bottom line: use sorted() when you want to keep the original, sort() when you do not need it and want to save memory. Going deeper: Timsort and stability Python sorts with Timsort (Powersort since Python 3.11), which finds runs that are already in order and merges them. It takes O(n log n) time in the worst case and O(n) when the data is already sorted. It is stable: items with equal keys keep their old order. That is why sorted(words, key=len) keeps \"banana\" before \"cherry\": both have 6 letters and \"banana\" came first. Lesson 6 of 16"],[7,"alias","Names, aliases and copies","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 alias.py. What it is A variable does not hold the list. It holds a reference to the list. b = a gives the same list a second name (an alias). Why we need it Copying a big list each time you pass it around would waste time and memory. Sharing is fast and lets a function change a list for its caller. How it works Use a[:], a.copy() or list(a) when you really want a second, separate list. Use is to check if two names point to the same object, and == to check if two lists have equal items. Lesson 7 of 16"],[7,"deep","Shallow copy, deep copy and lists of lists","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 deep.py. Way New outer list New inner lists b = a No No a[:], a.copy(), list(a) Yes No (shallow copy) copy.deepcopy(a) Yes Yes, at every level In the exam [[0] * 3] * 3 makes one inner list and three references to it, so changing one row changes all three. Build a grid with a comprehension: [[0] * 3 for _ in range(3)]. Lesson 8 of 16"],[7,"matrix","Lists in lists: matrices","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 matrix.py. Item of a matrixExam favourite matrix[row][col] The first index picks the row, the second the column. Transpose T[c][r] = M[r][c] An r × c matrix becomes c × r. Number of items rows × cols A 3D list (a cube) has rows × cols × depth items. Lesson 9 of 16"],[7,"bubble","Bubble sort, step by step","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 bubble.py. What it is Bubble sort walks through the list and swaps any two neighbours that are in the wrong order. After each pass the largest item left \"bubbles\" to the end. Why we need it It is the easiest sort to understand, which is why the PCEP exam asks about it. It shows the ideas behind every sort: compare, swap, and repeat until nothing is out of order. How it works Pass k compares n − 1 − k pairs. If a whole pass makes no swap, the list is sorted and we stop. Comparisons in the worst caseExam favourite (n − 1) + (n − 2) + ... + 1 = n(n − 1)/2 5 items need at most 10 comparisons, 1,000 items about 500,000. That is O(n2). Best case with the early stop n − 1 comparisons An already sorted list needs one pass. Remember Never use bubble sort in real code. sorted() is far faster. Learn it to understand sorting and to answer exam questions. Lesson 10 of 16"],[7,"unpack","Unpacking, swapping and a first look at comprehensions","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 unpack.py. In the exam The number of names must match the number of items, unless one name has a star. a, b = [1, 2, 3] raises ValueError: too many values to unpack. Lesson 11 of 16"],[7,"growth","How CPython makes append fast","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 growth.py. What it is A CPython list keeps a block of memory with more slots than it needs right now. len() is the number of slots in use, and the capacity is the number of slots it has. Why we need it If the list grew by exactly one slot on every append, each append would copy every item into new memory: n appends would cost about n2/2 copies. How it works When the list is full, CPython grows the capacity to about 1.125 times the new length plus a little (the rule in listobject.c is new = (n + n // 8 + 6) rounded down to a multiple of 4). Copies happen rarely, so the average cost of one append is O(1). This is called amortised O(1). Lesson 12 of 16"],[7,"versus","Lists compared with other collections","Which collection to choose list tuple set dict Order kept Yes Yes No Yes (insertion order) Can change Yes No Yes Yes Repeats allowed Yes Yes No Keys no, values yes Find an item O(n) O(n) O(1) average O(1) average by key Written as [1, 2] (1, 2) {1, 2} {\"a\": 1} Best for A changing sequence A fixed record Unique items and membership Looking up a value by a key Bottom line: start with a list. Switch to a tuple when the data must not change, a set when you test membership often, and a dict when you look things up by name. Operation Time items[i], items[i] = x, len, append, pop() O(1) insert(0, x), pop(0), remove(x), x in items, index(x) O(n) sort(), sorted() O(n log n) items[a:b] (copy of k items) O(k) Remember pop(0) and insert(0, x) move every other item. For a queue that adds at one end and removes at the other, use collections.deque, which does both in O(1) (Advanced 4.2). Lesson 13 of 16"],[7,"maths","The maths of lists","Average cost of an append (amortised)Exam favourite (n + total copies) / n < a small constant With growth by a factor g > 1 the copies form a geometric series n/g + n/g2 + ... < n/(g − 1). Bubble sort comparisonsExam favourite n(n − 1)/2 The same sum as the triangle loop of Core 2.2. Number of slices items[a:b] with a < b C(n + 1, 2) = n(n + 1)/2 Choose 2 of the n + 1 cut points. Mean of a list sum(items) / len(items) Undefined for an empty list: guard against ZeroDivisionError. Binary search steps on a sorted list ⌊log2 n⌋ + 1 A sorted list of 1,000,000 items needs at most 20 checks with bisect (Advanced 4.2). Worked example: total copies for 1,000 appends with doubling With doubling, copies happen when the list has 1, 2, 4, 8, ..., 512 items. The copies add up to 1 + 2 + 4 + ... + 512. 210 − 1 = 1,023 copies, about 1 per append CPython grows by about 1.125 instead of 2, which uses less memory and may copy up to about 8 items per append on average. Still a constant, so still O(1). Lesson 14 of 16"],[7,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths A list holds many items in order under one name. Use index and slice to read, append to add, and a for loop to visit every item. Lists change in place. Know append vs extend, remove vs pop vs del, sort vs sorted, and that b = a shares one list. Copy with a[:]. Copies are shallow unless you use deepcopy. Never build a grid with [[0] * n] * n. Know the cost of each method and choose deque, set or dict when a list is slow. A CPython list is a dynamic array of PyObject pointers with geometric over allocation, giving amortised O(1) append. Sorting uses an adaptive, stable merge sort (Timsort, with the Powersort merge policy since 3.11). Comparison sorts cannot beat Ω(n log n) comparisons in the worst case, because n! orders need log2(n!) yes or no answers to tell apart. Beginner Make lists, read items with index and slice, add with append and loop over them. Intermediate Use every list method correctly, copy lists safely, work with 2D lists and write bubble sort by hand. Expert Explain references, shallow and deep copies, the cost of each operation and the amortised analysis of append. Lesson 15 of 16"],[7,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","These problems use exactly what this chapter teaches. The page gives only a hint. Write your own answer on LeetCode first, then compare with the downloadable solution. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1929 Concatenation of Array Easy List repetition The answer is the list followed by itself. 66 Plus One Easy Walk from the right with a carry Start at the last digit. A 9 becomes 0 and the carry moves left. If every digit was 9, put a 1 in front. 27 Remove Element Easy Two pointers (read and write) Keep a write position. Copy every item that is not val to the write position. Return how many you kept. 26 Remove Duplicates from Sorted Array Easy Two pointers The list is sorted, so repeats sit next to each other. Write an item only when it differs from the last one written. 283 Move Zeroes Easy Two pointers Move every non zero item forward to a write position, then fill the rest with zeros. 121 Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock Easy Running minimum Keep the lowest price so far. Each day, check the profit if you sold today. 88 Merge Sorted Array Easy Three pointers from the back Fill nums1 from its end. Each time place the bigger of the two last items. 118 Pascal's Triangle Easy Build each row from the one above Each row starts and ends with 1. Every inner number is the sum of the two numbers above it. 189 Rotate Array Medium Three reversals Reverse the whole list, then reverse the first k items and the rest. Use k % n first. 75 Sort Colors Medium Dutch national flag (three pointers) Keep low, mid and high. A 0 swaps to low, a 2 swaps to high, a 1 just moves mid on. 48 Rotate Image Medium Transpose, then reverse each row Swap matrix[r][c] with matrix[c][r] above the diagonal, then reverse every row. 54 Spiral Matrix Medium Shrinking borders Keep top, bottom, left and right borders. Walk right, down, left and up, moving a border in after each side. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 16 of 16"],[7,"recap","Chapter summary","A list is an ordered, changeable collection in square brackets. Items can be of any type. Add with append, insert and extend. Remove with remove (by value), pop and del (by index) and clear. Find with in, index and count. All three walk the list, so they take O(n). sort() changes the list and returns None. sorted() returns a new list. Both take key and reverse and are stable. b = a shares one list. a[:], a.copy() and list(a) make a shallow copy, copy.deepcopy makes a full copy. Build grids with [[0] * n for _ in range(m)], never [[0] * n] * m. Bubble sort makes at most n(n − 1)/2 comparisons. append is amortised O(1) because CPython keeps spare room. Next: Core 3.3 covers tuples (lists that cannot change) and sets (collections of unique items with fast membership). .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: second largest without sorting, rotate a list by k, and flatten a list of lists.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[8,"","Tuples and sets: fixed records and unique items","tuples, packing and unpacking, hashable keys, namedtuple, sets, set algebra, deduplication, O(1) membership, set maths. · tuple · set · frozenset · unpacking · namedtuple · hashable · union · intersection · difference · Venn diagram · PCEP Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Core Python›Step 3: Data collections›Tuples and sets Step 3 · Topic 3 of 4 · Beginner Learn the two other everyday collections. Tuples hold fixed records, unpack into names and work as dict keys. Sets keep each item once, answer \"is it here?\" in one step, and do union, intersection and difference like a Venn diagram. About 55 min15 lessons1 animation5 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Two new collections: tuples and sets 2Making and using tuples 3What \"cannot change\" means 4Packing, unpacking and returning many values 5Tuples as keys, and named tuples 6Tuple or list 7Making sets and changing them 8Union, intersection, difference 9Removing duplicates 10Why sets are fast 11frozenset and set comprehensions 12The maths of sets 13From beginner to expert 14LeetCode practice 15Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 15"],[8,"idea","Two new collections: tuples and sets","Source: Python 3.14 tutorial sections 5.3 and 5.4, the library reference section on set and frozenset, and PCEP-30-02 section 3.2. What it is A tuple is an ordered sequence like a list, written with round brackets, that can never change. A set is a collection with no order and no repeats, written with curly brackets. Why we need it A list is great for data that changes. But some data should not change (a date, a point on a map, a row from a database), and sometimes you only care whether something is present, not where. Tuples protect fixed data. Sets remove repeats and answer \"is it in here?\" almost instantly. How it works A tuple is a fixed array of references, so it is a little smaller and faster than a list and can be used as a dict key. A set is a hash table (like a dict without values), so adding, removing and the in test take O(1) on average. Real world scenario: a train seat and a guest list Your train seat is (coach \"B2\", seat 34). That pair must not change after booking: it is a tuple. A wedding guest list written by two families has many names twice. Turn both lists into sets, take the union, and every guest appears once. To find the guests both families invited, take the intersection. Lesson 2 of 15"],[8,"tuple","Making and using tuples","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 tuple.py. In the exam The comma makes a tuple, not the brackets. (5) is the number 5. (5,) is a tuple with one item. This is a favourite exam trick. You can Example Index and slice t[0], t[-1], t[1:] Join and repeat (1, 2) + (3,), (0,) * 4 Search x in t, t.index(x), t.count(x) Loop and unpack for a, b in pairs, x, y = point Not: append, remove, sort, item assignment Use sorted(t) to get a sorted list Lesson 3 of 15"],[8,"tupimm","What \"cannot change\" means","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 tupimm.py. Common mistake A tuple holds references. You cannot change which objects it holds, but if one of them is a list, that list can still change. Such a tuple is not hashable, so it cannot be a dict key. Lesson 4 of 15"],[8,"unpack","Packing, unpacking and returning many values","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 unpack.py. What it is Packing puts several values into one tuple: t = 1, 2, 3. Unpacking takes them out into names: a, b, c = t. Why we need it A function can return only one object. Returning a tuple lets it give back several results at once, and unpacking gives each result a clear name. How it works Python evaluates the right side fully first, packs it into a tuple, then assigns each item to the names on the left in order. That is why a, b = b, a swaps safely. Lesson 5 of 15"],[8,"tupkey","Tuples as keys, and named tuples","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 tupkey.py. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 named.py. Remember A named tuple gives each position a name, so p.x is clearer than p[0]. For records with many fields or that need to change, use a dataclass (Advanced 1.3). Lesson 6 of 15"],[8,"versus1","Tuple or list","Tuple compared with list tuple list Brackets ( ) [ ] Can change No Yes Hashable (dict key, set item) Yes, if every item is hashable No Memory for (1, 2, 3) 72 bytes on CPython 3.14 88 bytes Methods count, index about 11, including append and sort Meaning A fixed record: (name, age), (x, y) Many items of the same kind that grows or shrinks Bottom line: use a tuple when the size and the items should stay the same, and a list when you will add, remove or sort. Lesson 7 of 15"],[8,"set","Making sets and changing them","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 set.py. Method Does If the item is missing add(x) Adds x (nothing happens if it is already there) remove(x) Removes x KeyError discard(x) Removes x Nothing happens pop() Removes and returns some item (you cannot choose which) KeyError on an empty set update(items) Adds every item of an iterable clear() Empties the set In the exam {} is an empty dict, not an empty set. Write set() for an empty set. Also, a set can only hold hashable items: numbers, strings, tuples of them, but not lists or other sets. Lesson 8 of 15"],[8,"setops","Union, intersection, difference","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 setops.py. Operator Method Means A | B A.union(B) In A or B or both A & B A.intersection(B) In both A − B A.difference(B) In A but not in B A ^ B A.symmetric_difference(B) In exactly one of them A <= B A.issubset(B) Every item of A is in B A < B Subset and not equal A.isdisjoint(B) No item in common In the exam The operators need two sets. The methods accept any iterable, so A.union([1, 2]) works but A | [1, 2] raises TypeError. Lesson 9 of 15"],[8,"dedupe","Removing duplicates","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 dedupe.py. Remember set(items) is the fastest way to remove repeats, but the order is lost. If order matters, use list(dict.fromkeys(items)), which keeps the first time each item appears. Lesson 10 of 15"],[8,"speed","Why sets are fast","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 speed.py. What it is A set stores each item in a table at a place worked out from the item hash, a number made from its value. Why we need it To check x in a list, Python may have to look at every item. With a million items that is a million checks. How it works To check x in a set, Python works out hash(x), jumps straight to that place in the table and looks at one or a few slots. The number of checks stays about the same for 10 items or 10 million items: O(1) on average. Core 3.4 shows the hash table step by step with the dict animation. Checking membership x in list x in set Average time O(n) O(1) Keeps order and repeats Yes No Items must be Anything Hashable Extra memory Less More (the table has empty slots) Bottom line: turn a list into a set once if you will check membership many times. Lesson 11 of 15"],[8,"frozen","frozenset and set comprehensions","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 frozen.py. Remember A frozenset is a set that cannot change. Because it never changes it is hashable, so it can be a dict key or an item of another set. Lesson 12 of 15"],[8,"maths","The maths of sets","Size of a union (inclusion and exclusion)Exam favourite |A ∪ B| = |A| + |B| − |A ∩ B| The middle part was counted twice, so take it away once. Three sets |A ∪ B ∪ C| = |A| + |B| + |C| − |A ∩ B| − |A ∩ C| − |B ∩ C| + |A ∩ B ∩ C| Add singles, take away pairs, add back the triple. Symmetric difference |A Δ B| = |A| + |B| − 2|A ∩ B| A ^ B in Python. Number of subsets (the power set)Exam favourite 2n Each item is in or out. {1, 2, 3} has 8 subsets. De Morgan's laws not (A or B) = (not A) and (not B) For sets inside a universe U: U − (A ∪ B) = (U − A) ∩ (U − B). Worked example: students in at least one class 40 students take Python, 30 take Java and 12 take both. Use |A ∪ B| = |A| + |B| − |A ∩ B|. 40 + 30 − 12 = 58 students Students in exactly one class: 58 − 12 = 46, which is |A ^ B|. Lesson 13 of 15"],[8,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths A tuple is a list that cannot change. A set is a bag of unique items with no order. (5,) is a tuple, (5) is not. Tuples unpack and can be dict keys. Sets give union, intersection and difference, and set() is the empty set. Hashable means \"has a hash that never changes\", which needs an immutable value. Sets and dict keys need hashable items. Use a set to turn O(n) membership tests into O(1), and dict.fromkeys to remove repeats while keeping order. CPython sets use open addressing with a mix of linear and random probing and keep the table at most about 60 percent full. Set algebra is Boolean algebra: union is OR, intersection is AND, and the power set of an n item set has 2n members, which is why \"try every subset\" algorithms take exponential time. Beginner Make tuples and sets, unpack a tuple, and remove duplicates with set(). Intermediate Return several values, use tuples as keys, and solve problems with |, &, − and ^. Expert Explain hashability, choose between list, tuple and set by their costs, and use inclusion and exclusion to count. Lesson 14 of 15"],[8,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","These problems use exactly what this chapter teaches. The page gives only a hint. Write your own answer on LeetCode first, then compare with the downloadable solution. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 217 Contains Duplicate Easy Seen set Add each number to a set. If it is already there, you found a duplicate. 349 Intersection of Two Arrays Easy Set intersection Turn both lists into sets and take the intersection. 771 Jewels and Stones Easy Set membership Put the jewel letters in a set, then count the stones that are in it. 1832 Check if the Sentence Is Pangram Easy Set size A pangram uses every letter a to z. The set of its letters has 26 items. 268 Missing Number Easy Gauss sum The numbers 0 to n add up to n(n + 1)/2. The missing one is that total minus the sum of the list. 2215 Find the Difference of Two Arrays Easy Set difference A − B and B − A answer the two parts. 1436 Destination City Easy Set difference The destination is a city that appears as an end but never as a start. 645 Set Mismatch Easy Set and Gauss sum The repeated number is sum(nums) − sum(set(nums)). The missing number is n(n + 1)/2 − sum(set(nums)). 3 Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters Medium Sliding window with a set Grow the window on the right. While the new letter is already in the window, drop letters from the left. 128 Longest Consecutive Sequence Medium Set, start only at the beginning of a run Put all numbers in a set. Start counting only from x where x − 1 is not in the set, so each run is walked once: O(n). .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 15 of 15"],[8,"recap","Chapter summary","A tuple is an ordered sequence that cannot change. The comma makes it: (5,) is a tuple, (5) is an int. Tuples pack and unpack: return a, b gives a tuple, and x, y = point takes it apart. Tuples of hashable items can be dict keys and set items. Lists cannot. A set holds unique hashable items with no order. set() is empty, {} is a dict. add, remove (KeyError), discard (no error), pop (any item). | union, & both, − only in the first, ^ in exactly one. x in a set is O(1) on average, x in a list is O(n). |A ∪ B| = |A| + |B| − |A ∩ B|, and a set of n items has 2n subsets. Next: Core 3.4 covers dictionaries: key and value pairs, every method, counting and grouping, and the hash table behind them. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: common friends of two people, the first repeated item, and all subsets of a small set.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[9,"","Dictionaries: keys, values and hash tables","dict basics, get and setdefault, keys values items, counting, grouping, nesting and JSON, merging, hash table internals. · dictionary · dict · key value · get · items · Counter · defaultdict · JSON · hash table · hashing · collision · PCEP Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Core Python›Step 3: Data collections›Dictionaries Step 3 · Topic 4 of 4 · Beginner Look things up by name instead of by position. Learn to make and change dicts, read keys safely, loop over keys, values and items, count and group, nest dicts like JSON, merge them, and see the hash table that finds any key in one step. About 65 min16 lessons1 animation5 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1What a dictionary is 2Adding, changing and removing 3Safe lookups: get and setdefault 4Looping over a dict 5Views, and changing a dict while looping 6Counting with a dict 7Grouping with a dict of lists 8Nested dicts and JSON 9Merging, comprehensions and other ways to build 10What can be a key 11How a dict finds a key in one step 12dict compared with list and other mappings 13The maths of hashing 14From beginner to expert 15LeetCode practice 16Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 16"],[9,"idea","What a dictionary is","Source: Python 3.14 tutorial section 5.5, the library reference section on dict, and PCEP-30-02 section 3.3. What it is A dictionary (type dict) stores key and value pairs. You look a value up by its key, not by a position: phone[\"Asha\"] gives Asha's number. Why we need it With a list you must remember that Asha is at index 0 and Ravi at 1, and finding someone means checking every item. A dict lets you use a meaningful name as the index and finds it in one step, however many entries there are. How it works Each key is hashed to a number, and that number picks a slot in a table where the pair is stored. Keys must be unique and hashable. Values can be anything. Since Python 3.7 a dict also remembers the order in which keys were added. Real world scenario: the contacts app on your phone Your phone does not ask \"which position is Ravi in?\". You type the name and it shows the number. Names are keys and numbers are values. Two contacts cannot have exactly the same name, adding a new number for an old name replaces it, and the search is instant even with 2,000 contacts. That is a Python dict. Python phone = {\"Asha\": \"98450 11111\", \"Ravi\": \"99001 22222\"}ages = dict(asha=21, ravi=23) # keyword style, keys become stringspairs = dict([(\"x\", 1), (\"y\", 2)]) # from a list of pairsempty = {}print(phone)print(ages, pairs, empty)print(phone[\"Asha\"]) # look up by keyprint(len(phone), \"Ravi\"in phone, \"98450 11111\"in phone) Output {'Asha': '98450 11111', 'Ravi': '99001 22222'} {'asha': 21, 'ravi': 23} {'x': 1, 'y': 2} {} 98450 11111 2 True False Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 make.py. Lesson 2 of 16"],[9,"change","Adding, changing and removing","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 change.py. Code Does If the key is missing d[k] = v Adds k, or replaces its value Adds it del d[k] Removes k KeyError d.pop(k) Removes k and returns its value KeyError d.pop(k, default) Same, with a default Returns default d.popitem() Removes and returns the last added pair KeyError on an empty dict d.update(other) Adds or replaces every pair of other Adds them d.clear() Empties the dict Lesson 3 of 16"],[9,"get","Safe lookups: get and setdefault","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 get.py. Reading a key that may be missing d[k] d.get(k, default) k in d first Missing key KeyError Returns default (None if not given) You decide in an if Use when The key must be there and a missing key is a bug A missing key is normal You need different code for the two cases Bottom line: use d[k] when the key must exist, so mistakes show up as errors, and get() when a missing key is a normal case. Lesson 4 of 16"],[9,"loop","Looping over a dict","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 loop.py. In the exam for x in d gives the keys, not the values and not the pairs. Use d.values() for values and d.items() for (key, value) pairs. x in d also checks keys only. Lesson 5 of 16"],[9,"views","Views, and changing a dict while looping","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 views.py. Common mistake keys(), values() and items() return views: windows onto the dict that update by themselves. Adding or removing keys while you loop over a dict raises RuntimeError. Loop over list(d) instead. Lesson 6 of 16"],[9,"count","Counting with a dict","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 count.py. Remember Counting is the most common use of a dict. Learn the get(word, 0) + 1 line by heart, then use collections.Counter in real code. Counter also adds, subtracts and finds the most common items. Lesson 7 of 16"],[9,"group","Grouping with a dict of lists","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 group.py. What it is Grouping puts items into buckets by some property: words by first letter, students by class, orders by date. Why we need it Reports and summaries are built from groups: total sales per city, average mark per subject. How it works Use the property as the key and a list as the value. Create the empty list the first time you see a key (or let defaultdict(list) do it), then append. Lesson 8 of 16"],[9,"nested","Nested dicts and JSON","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 nested.py. Remember A dict of dicts and lists looks exactly like JSON, the text format that web APIs use. json.dumps turns a dict into JSON text and json.loads turns it back. The web part of this subject uses this all the time. Lesson 9 of 16"],[9,"merge","Merging, comprehensions and other ways to build","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 merge.py. Way Result a | b A new dict. For equal keys the value from b wins a |= b or a.update(b) Changes a in place {**a, **b} A new dict, works before Python 3.9 {k: v for ...} A dict comprehension dict.fromkeys(keys, value) Every key gets the same value dict(zip(keys, values)) Pairs keys and values from two lists Common mistake dict.fromkeys(keys, []) gives every key the same list object. Appending to one changes all of them. Use a comprehension {k: [] for k in keys} instead. Lesson 10 of 16"],[9,"keys","What can be a key","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 keys.py. In the exam Keys are compared with ==. Because 1 == 1.0 == True and they have the same hash, they are the same key: the dict keeps the first key object and the last value. Use one type for your keys. Lesson 11 of 16"],[9,"hash","How a dict finds a key in one step","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 internals.py. What it is A dict is a hash table. hash(key) gives a whole number, and that number decides where in the table the pair lives. Why we need it Looking up a name in a list of a million names may need a million checks. A hash table needs about one or two, so dict lookup, insert and delete take O(1) time on average. How it works CPython keeps two arrays. A small index table of size 8, 16, 32 ... holds entry numbers. An entries array holds (hash, key, value) in the order you added them, which is why dicts keep insertion order. The slot is hash mod size. If it is taken (a collision), Python probes other slots. When two thirds of the index table is used, the dict grows and every key is placed again. Going deeper: Why hash(-1) is -2 Inside CPython, the C function that computes a hash returns −1 to say \"an error happened\". So no object may have the hash −1, and Python quietly uses −2 for the number −1. String hashes change every time Python starts (hash randomisation, PEP 456), to stop attackers from sending many keys that collide and make a web server slow. Lesson 12 of 16"],[9,"versus","dict compared with list and other mappings","Storing marks for 30 students two lists (names, marks) list of pairs dict Get Ravi's mark Find the index in names, then marks[i]: O(n) Loop until the name matches: O(n) marks[\"Ravi\"]: O(1) Add a student Append to both lists and keep them in step Append a pair marks[\"Zara\"] = 80 Repeated names Allowed (can be a bug) Allowed Not possible, the key is unique Keeps order Yes Yes Yes (insertion order) Bottom line: whenever you find yourself searching a list for a name to get a value, use a dict. Tool Use it for dict Any key to value lookup collections.Counter Counting things collections.defaultdict Grouping, when missing keys need a starting value collections.OrderedDict Moving keys to the end (move_to_end), an LRU cache types.MappingProxyType A read only view of a dict Lesson 13 of 16"],[9,"maths","The maths of hashing","Slot of a keyExam favourite slot = hash(key) mod table size Table sizes are powers of 2, so this is just the last few bits of the hash. Load factorExam favourite α = used slots / table size ≤ 2/3 A CPython dict with 8 slots holds at most 5 keys. The 6th key makes it grow. New table size smallest power of 2 ≥ 3 × (number of keys) When the 6th key arrives, 5 keys are stored: 3 × 5 = 15, so the new size is 16. Expected probes for a lookup that hits (uniform hashing) about (1/α) ln(1/(1 − α)) At α = 2/3 this is about 1.6 probes, so lookups stay fast. Chance that two of k keys share a slot of m (birthday problem) 1 − e−k(k − 1)/(2m) With 23 people and 365 days the chance of a shared birthday is above 50 percent. Collisions are normal, so tables must handle them. Worked example: when does a dict grow? Start with 8 slots. The limit is 2/3 of 8 = 5.33, so 5 keys fit. The 6th key finds the table full and it grows to 16 slots (limit 10). The 11th key grows it to 32 slots (limit 21), and the 22nd key to 64. Growth at keys 6, 11 and 22, which matches the sizes printed by the program above Lesson 14 of 16"],[9,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths A dict maps keys to values. d[key] reads, d[key] = value writes, and for key in d loops over the keys. Use get with a default, pop, update and items(). Count with get(k, 0) + 1 and group with a dict of lists. Keys must be hashable and unique. Use Counter and defaultdict, merge with |, build with comprehensions, never change the size while looping, and know that views are live. A nested dict is JSON. CPython dicts are compact, ordered hash tables with a separate sparse index and a dense entry array (Raymond Hettinger, Python 3.6), open addressing with perturbed probing, and key sharing between instances of the same class. Hash flooding attacks are why string hashes use SipHash with a random key. Beginner Make a dict, read, add and change values, and loop over keys and items. Intermediate Count and group with a dict, use get and setdefault, and work with nested dicts and JSON. Expert Explain hashing, collisions, load factor and growth, choose Counter, defaultdict or OrderedDict, and reason about the cost of every operation. Lesson 15 of 16"],[9,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","These problems use exactly what this chapter teaches. The page gives only a hint. Write your own answer on LeetCode first, then compare with the downloadable solution. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1 Two Sum Easy Dict of seen values For each number x, the partner is target − x. Keep a dict from value to index of the numbers seen so far. 242 Valid Anagram Easy Count letters Two words are anagrams when every letter appears the same number of times. 387 First Unique Character in a String Easy Count, then scan again First count every letter. Then walk the text again and return the first index whose letter count is 1. 383 Ransom Note Easy Count and spend Count the magazine letters. Each letter of the note uses one up. 1512 Number of Good Pairs Easy Count as you go Each new copy of a value makes a pair with every earlier copy, so add the count so far before increasing it. 169 Majority Element Easy Count, or Boyer Moore voting Count each value and return the one seen more than n/2 times. The O(1) memory trick is the Boyer Moore vote. 205 Isomorphic Strings Easy Two dicts, one per direction Each letter of s must always map to the same letter of t, and no two letters may map to the same one. 290 Word Pattern Easy Two dicts, one per direction Split the text into words. Then it is Isomorphic Strings with letters on one side and words on the other. 49 Group Anagrams Medium Group by a sorted key Anagrams have the same letters in sorted order. Use that sorted text as the dict key and a list of words as the value. 347 Top K Frequent Elements Medium Count, then bucket by count Count each number. Put numbers into buckets by their count, then read the buckets from the highest count down. 560 Subarray Sum Equals K Medium Prefix sums in a dict A part from i to j sums to k when prefix[j] − prefix[i] = k. Count how often each prefix sum has appeared. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 16 of 16"],[9,"recap","Chapter summary","A dict maps unique, hashable keys to any values. Write it as {key: value, ...}. d[k] reads (KeyError if missing), d.get(k, default) reads safely, d[k] = v adds or replaces. Remove with del, pop and popitem. Merge with update, | and |=. for k in d gives keys. Use values() and items() for the rest. Views are live, and changing the size while looping is an error. Count with d[k] = d.get(k, 0) + 1 or Counter. Group with a dict of lists or defaultdict(list). A dict is a hash table: lookup, insert and delete are O(1) on average. It grows when two thirds full and keeps insertion order. 1, 1.0 and True are the same key. Lists cannot be keys. Next: Step 4 starts with functions: def, parameters, return values and every way to pass arguments. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: a word frequency table, invert a dict that has repeated values, and a tiny inventory with orders.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[10,"","Functions: def, return and every kind of argument","def, return, None, argument kinds, *args, **kwargs, / and *, unpacking, mutable defaults, docstrings, first-class functions. · function · def · return · None · arguments · parameters · *args · **kwargs · keyword-only · positional-only · default argument · PCEP · PCAP Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Core Python›Step 4: Functions›Functions Step 4 · Topic 1 of 3 · Intermediate Write a job once and use it everywhere. Learn def and return, the difference between print and return, positional, keyword and default arguments, *args and **kwargs, keyword-only and positional-only parameters, the mutable default trap, docstrings, type hints, and functions as objects. About 65 min13 lessons1 animation5 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1What a function is 2return, print and None 3Positional, keyword and default arguments 4*args, **kwargs, keyword-only and positional-only 5Spreading lists and dicts into a call 6The mutable default trap 7Docstrings and type hints 8Functions are objects too 9Kinds of reusable code compared 10Functions in maths and in code 11From beginner to expert 12LeetCode practice 13Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 13"],[10,"idea","What a function is","Source: Python 3.14 tutorial section 4.8, language reference section 8.7, PCEP-30-02 sections 4.1 and 4.2 and PCAP-31-03. What it is A function is a named block of code that does one job. You define it once with def and then call it by name as often as you like, giving it different inputs each time. Why we need it Without functions you copy the same lines again and again. When there is a bug, you must fix every copy, and the program becomes long and hard to read. A function lets you write the job once, give it a clear name, test it alone and reuse it everywhere. This idea is called DRY: do not repeat yourself. How it works def name(parameters): followed by an indented body creates a function object and binds it to the name. A call name(arguments) gives each parameter a value, runs the body, and the call itself becomes the value after return. Real world scenario: a recipe card A recipe card for \"masala tea\" says: take water, milk, tea leaves and sugar, then boil, add, strain. The card is the function definition. Making tea for 2 people or 10 people with less sugar is a call with different arguments. You do not rewrite the card each time, and if you improve the recipe, every future cup gets better. Python def greet(name): # name is a parameterprint(\"Hello,\", name)greet(\"Asha\") # \"Asha\" is an argumentgreet(\"Ravi\")def area(width, height):return width * height # send a value back to the callerroom = area(4, 5)print(\"room:\", room, \"m2\")print(\"two rooms:\", area(4, 5) + area(3, 3), \"m2\") Output Hello, Asha Hello, Ravi room: 20 m2 two rooms: 29 m2 Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 basic.py. In the exam A parameter is the name in the def line (name, width). An argument is the value you pass in the call (\"Asha\", 4). Exams use both words, so learn the difference. Lesson 2 of 13"],[10,"ret","return, print and None","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 ret.py. print inside a function or return print(x) return x What it does Shows x on the screen Gives x back to the code that called the function Can the caller use the value No, it is gone Yes: store it, add it, pass it on The call evaluates to None x Stops the function No Yes, at once Bottom line: functions should usually return results and let the caller decide whether to print them. That makes them reusable and testable. In the exam A function with no return, or with a bare return, gives back None. That is why a = show_double(5) printed 10 but left a as None. Lesson 3 of 13"],[10,"args","Positional, keyword and default arguments","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 args.py. Kind of argument Example call Rule Positional describe(\"Ravi\", 23) Matched by position, left to right Keyword describe(age=30, name=\"Meera\") Matched by name, any order Mixed describe(\"John\", city=\"Goa\", age=40) All positional arguments must come before any keyword argument Default value def describe(..., city=\"Pune\") Used when the caller gives nothing for that parameter In the exam describe(name=\"Sara\", 25) is a SyntaxError: a positional argument may not follow a keyword argument. Python refuses to even start the program. Lesson 4 of 13"],[10,"star","*args, **kwargs, keyword-only and positional-only","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 star.py. In the def line Meaning Inside the function it is a Positional or keyword The value a=1 Optional, with a default The value or the default *args Collects extra positional arguments A tuple **kwargs Collects extra keyword arguments A dict * alone Every parameter after it is keyword-only / Every parameter before it is positional-only (Python 3.8+) Remember The full order is: positional-only, /, normal, *args (or a bare *), keyword-only, **kwargs. The names args and kwargs are only a habit. What matters is the * and **. Lesson 5 of 13"],[10,"unpackcall","Spreading lists and dicts into a call","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 unpackcall.py. In the exam In a def line, * and ** collect arguments. In a call, * and ** spread a list or dict out into separate arguments. Same symbols, opposite jobs. Lesson 6 of 13"],[10,"mutdef","The mutable default trap","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 mutdef.py. What it is A default value is worked out once, when the def line runs, and kept in the function object (func.__defaults__). Why we need it For numbers, strings and None this never matters, because they cannot change. For a list or dict it does: every call that uses the default shares the same object, so changes pile up from one call to the next. How it works Use None as the default and create a new list inside the function. This is one of the most common Python bugs, and a favourite interview question. Lesson 7 of 13"],[10,"doc","Docstrings and type hints","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 doc.py. Remember A docstring is a string on the first line of the body. help(bmi) and editors show it. Type hints such as height_m: float and -> float tell readers and tools like mypy what you expect, but Python itself does not check them when the program runs (Advanced 3.1). Lesson 8 of 13"],[10,"objects","Functions are objects too","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 objects.py. Remember A function name is just a variable that holds a function object. You can give it another name, store it in a list or dict, and pass it to another function. Functions that take or return functions are called higher-order functions. Core 4.3 uses this with sorted, map, filter and closures. Lesson 9 of 13"],[10,"versus","Kinds of reusable code compared","Ways to reuse code Copy and paste Function Method (Core 6.1) Module (Core 5.2) Defined with Nothing def def inside a class a .py file Fix a bug in Every copy One place One place One place Called as name(args) obj.name(args) module.name(args) Best for Never One job A job tied to an object Sharing many functions between programs Bottom line: as soon as you write the same few lines twice, turn them into a function. Lesson 10 of 13"],[10,"maths","Functions in maths and in code","A function maps each input to one outputExam favourite f: X → Y, x ↦ f(x) area(width, height) maps each pair of numbers to one number. CompositionExam favourite (f ∘ g)(x) = f(g(x)) apply_twice(f, x) is f(f(x)). len(str(n)) is len ∘ str. Idempotent function f(f(x)) = f(x) abs, str.strip and sorted are idempotent: doing them twice changes nothing more. How many functions from a set of m inputs to n outputs nm Each of the m inputs can go to any of the n outputs. From {1, 2, 3} to {a, b}: 23 = 8. Arguments a call may give with p positional parameters, d of them with defaults and no *args between p − d and p positional arguments describe has p = 3, d = 1, so 2 or 3 positional arguments. Worked example: what does apply_twice(lambda n: n * 3, 2) return? apply_twice(f, v) returns f(f(v)). Inner call: f(2) = 6. Outer call: f(6) = 18. (f ∘ f)(2) = 18 Going deeper: Pure functions A pure function gives the same output for the same input and changes nothing outside itself: no printing, no changing global variables or arguments. area and double are pure, add_bad is not. Pure functions are easy to test, to cache (Core 4.2) and to run in parallel (Advanced 5.1), which is why functional programming prefers them. Lesson 11 of 13"],[10,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths def makes a function, a call runs it, and return gives a value back. A function without return gives None. Arguments can be positional or keyword, and parameters can have defaults. *args collects extra positional arguments into a tuple and **kwargs collects extra keyword arguments into a dict. Use / and * to make parameters positional-only or keyword-only, spread arguments with * and **, avoid mutable defaults, and write docstrings and type hints. Functions are objects you can pass around. Calling a function creates a frame object with its local variables. CPython binds arguments in C (the rules shown in the animation), stores defaults in __defaults__ and __kwdefaults__, and inspect.signature(f).bind(*args, **kwargs) reproduces the same binding in Python. Since 3.11 calls between Python functions avoid creating C stack frames, which made calls much cheaper. Beginner Write a function with parameters, call it and return a value. Intermediate Use keyword and default arguments, *args and **kwargs, and return several values as a tuple. Expert Design clean signatures with / and *, explain the mutable default trap, and use functions as objects. Real world scenario: the API of a payment app A payment function might be pay(amount, /, *, to, note=\"\", notify=True). amount must be passed by position, the receiver must be named (to=\"Asha\") so nobody swaps the two numbers by mistake, and note and notify are optional. Good signatures prevent costly mistakes before the code even runs. Lesson 12 of 13"],[10,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","These problems use exactly what this chapter teaches. The page gives only a hint. Write your own answer on LeetCode first, then compare with the downloadable solution. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1342 Number of Steps to Reduce a Number to Zero Easy Loop inside a function While num > 0: halve it if even, else take away 1. Count the steps and return the count. 1920 Build Array from Permutation Easy Index into a list ans[i] is nums[nums[i]]. Build a new list and return it. 1470 Shuffle the Array Easy Two halves side by side Walk i from 0 to n − 1 and add nums[i] then nums[i + n]. 2011 Final Value of Variable After Performing Operations Easy A helper decides plus or minus Every operation contains either \"++\" or \"--\". Add 1 or take away 1. 1365 How Many Numbers Are Smaller Than the Current Number Easy Helper function per item Write a small helper that counts the numbers smaller than x, then call it for every item. 1431 Kids With the Greatest Number of Candies Easy Find the max once Work out max(candies) once. Kid i can be the greatest if candies[i] + extra reaches it. 2652 Sum Multiples Easy Small test function Add every number from 1 to n that divides by 3, 5 or 7. 1323 Maximum 69 Number Easy Change the first 6 Turn the number into text, replace the first 6 with a 9, and turn it back. 2160 Minimum Sum of Four Digit Number After Splitting Digits Easy Sort the digits Sort the 4 digits a ≤ b ≤ c ≤ d. The best split is (10a + c) + (10b + d). 1641 Count Sorted Vowel Strings Medium Maths in a function A sorted string of length n over 5 vowels is a choice of n items from 5 with repeats: C(n + 4, 4). .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 13 of 13"],[10,"recap","Chapter summary","def name(parameters): defines a function. name(arguments) calls it. return sends a value back and leaves the function. No return means the call gives None. Arguments are matched by position or by name. Positional arguments come first. Defaults make a parameter optional. *args collects extra positional arguments into a tuple, **kwargs extra keyword arguments into a dict. After * parameters are keyword-only. Before / they are positional-only. In a call, *list and **dict spread values out into arguments. Default values are made once. Use None instead of [] or {} as a default. Functions are objects: name them, store them and pass them to other functions. Next: Core 4.2 explains where names live (scope), global and nonlocal, and functions that call themselves (recursion). .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: a flexible average with *args, a receipt builder with keyword-only options, and a safe append with a None default.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[11,"","Scope and recursion: LEGB, closures and the call stack","scope, LEGB, global, nonlocal, closures, recursion, call stack, recursion limit, memoisation, recurrences. · scope · LEGB · global · nonlocal · closure · recursion · base case · call stack · RecursionError · lru_cache · memoization · PCEP · PCAP Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Core Python›Step 4: Functions›Scope and recursion Step 4 · Topic 2 of 3 · Intermediate Find out where every name lives and how functions call themselves. Learn local and global names, shadowing, the LEGB rule, global and nonlocal, UnboundLocalError, closures, recursion with base cases, the call stack and its limit, classic recursive functions, memoisation and the maths of recurrences. About 65 min15 lessons2 animations6 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Where names live, and functions that call themselves 2Local names hide global names 3The LEGB rule 4The global statement 5UnboundLocalError 6nonlocal and closures 7Recursion: base case and recursive case 8The recursion limit 9Classic recursive functions 10Memoisation: remember answers 11Recursion or a loop 12The maths of recursion 13From beginner to expert 14LeetCode practice 15Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 15"],[11,"idea","Where names live, and functions that call themselves","Source: Python 3.14 tutorial section 9.2, language reference section 4.2, PCEP-30-02 sections 4.1 and 4.2. What it is The scope of a name is the part of the program where you can use it. A name made inside a function is local to that function. A name made at the top of a file is global. Recursion is when a function calls itself to solve a smaller copy of the same problem. Why we need it Scopes keep functions independent: a helper can use the name i or total without breaking the same name somewhere else. Recursion makes problems that are made of smaller copies of themselves (folders inside folders, family trees, maths like n!) short and clear. How it works Each call gets a fresh set of local names (a frame). Python looks names up in the order Local, Enclosing, Global, Built-in. A recursive function has a base case that answers directly and a recursive case that calls itself on a smaller input, so it always reaches the base case. Real world scenario: Russian nesting dolls To count the dolls in a matryoshka set, open the outer doll. If it is empty, the answer is 1 (the base case). Otherwise the answer is 1 plus the number of dolls inside, and you count those the same way (the recursive case). Each opened doll waits on the table until the inner count comes back. Those waiting dolls are the call stack. Python tax_rate = 0.18# global: made at the top level of the filedef price_with_tax(price): tax = price * tax_rate # tax is local: it exists only during this callreturn price + taxprint(price_with_tax(100))print(tax_rate) # the global is visible everywheretry:print(tax) # the local is gone after the callexceptNameErroras err:print(\"NameError:\", err) Output 118.0 0.18 NameError: name 'tax' is not defined Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 scope1.py. Lesson 2 of 15"],[11,"shadow","Local names hide global names","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 shadow.py. In the exam Assigning to a name inside a function makes a new local name, even if a global with the same name exists. This is called shadowing. Changing an object (items.append) is different from rebinding a name (items = [...]): the first is seen by the caller, the second is not. Lesson 3 of 15"],[11,"legb","The LEGB rule","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 legb.py. Letter Scope Made by L Local Assignments and parameters inside the current function call E Enclosing The local names of any outer function, for a function inside a function G Global Assignments at the top level of the module (the .py file) B Built-in Names Python gives you: print, len, range, int, ValueError ... In the exam Python decides which names are local when it compiles the function, not while it runs. If a name is assigned anywhere in the function, it is local in the whole function. Lesson 4 of 15"],[11,"globalkw","The global statement","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 globalkw.py. Sharing a changing value between functions global variable pass in and return Code global visits then visits += 1 total = next_count(total) Who can change it Any function, from anywhere Only code that gets the returned value Testing Hard: the result depends on hidden state Easy: same input, same output Good for Settings read in many places, small scripts Almost everything else Bottom line: pass values in and return results. Keep global for constants and the rare value that the whole program really shares. Lesson 5 of 15"],[11,"unbound","UnboundLocalError","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 unbound.py. Remember The fix is either to rename the local variable, to pass count in as a parameter, or, if you really mean the global, to add global count at the top of the function. Lesson 6 of 15"],[11,"closure","nonlocal and closures","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 closure.py. What it is A closure is an inner function that remembers names from the function around it, even after that outer function has finished. Why we need it It lets you make functions with their own private memory: counters, multipliers, settings, without a global variable or a class. How it works Python stores each remembered name in a cell attached to the inner function (func.__closure__). Reading an enclosing name needs nothing special. Assigning to it needs nonlocal, or Python would make a new local name. In the exam global reaches the module scope. nonlocal reaches the nearest enclosing function scope and is a SyntaxError at the top level. Decorators (Advanced 1.2) are built from closures. Lesson 7 of 15"],[11,"fact","Recursion: base case and recursive case","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 fact.py. Part factorial Question to ask Base case if n <= 1: return 1 What is the smallest input I can answer at once? Recursive case return n * factorial(n - 1) How does the answer use the answer for a smaller input? Progress n − 1 is smaller than n Does every call get closer to the base case? Lesson 8 of 15"],[11,"limit","The recursion limit","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 limit.py. Common mistake Each call uses a frame on the call stack. To protect the program, CPython stops at about 1,000 frames deep with RecursionError. sys.setrecursionlimit can raise the limit, but a recursion that deep is usually better written as a loop. Lesson 9 of 15"],[11,"examples","Classic recursive functions","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 examples.py. Remember total(items[1:]) is clear but copies the list each time, which makes it O(n2). Real code uses sum(). Fast power is the opposite: halving the exponent makes it O(log n), which is how pow(a, b, m) handles huge numbers in cryptography. Lesson 10 of 15"],[11,"memo","Memoisation: remember answers","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 memo.py. What it is Memoisation means storing the answer of each call so the same call never has to be worked out twice. Why we need it Naive fib(n) calls fib(n − 2) twice, fib(n − 3) three times, and so on: the number of calls grows like 1.6n. fib(25) needs 242,785 calls. How it works functools.lru_cache keeps a dict from arguments to results. With it each fib(k) is computed once, so fib(n) takes n + 1 calls. A plain loop with two names does the same in O(n) time and O(1) memory. Lesson 11 of 15"],[11,"versus","Recursion or a loop","Recursion compared with iteration Recursion Loop Fits best Nested data: trees, folders, JSON, divide and conquer Walking a list, counting, simple repeats Memory One frame per pending call: O(depth) Usually O(1) Depth limit in Python About 1,000 None Speed in CPython Slower (each call has a cost) Faster Readability Very clear when the problem is recursive Very clear when it is a sequence Bottom line: use recursion when the data or the problem is naturally nested, and a loop for everything else. Python does not remove tail calls, so a deep \"tail recursive\" function still hits the limit. Lesson 12 of 15"],[11,"maths","The maths of recursion","Recurrence for a simple recursionExam favourite T(n) = T(n − 1) + c gives T(n) = O(n) factorial and countdown do constant work and then one smaller call. Calls made by naive fib(n)Exam favourite C(n) = C(n − 1) + C(n − 2) + 1 = 2F(n + 1) − 1 fib(5) makes 2 × 8 − 1 = 15 calls, as the animation counts. Golden ratio growth F(n) ≈ φn / √5, φ = (1 + √5)/2 ≈ 1.618 So naive fib is exponential: about 1.6n calls. Tower of Hanoi movesExam favourite M(n) = 2M(n − 1) + 1 = 2n − 1 3 discs need 7 moves, 64 discs need about 1.8 × 1019. Fast power T(n) = T(n/2) + c gives T(n) = O(log n) power(2, 1000) needs only about 10 levels of calls. Master theorem (divide and conquer) T(n) = a·T(n/b) + O(nd) If d > logb a: O(nd). If equal: O(nd log n). If less: O(nlogb a). Merge sort: a = 2, b = 2, d = 1 gives O(n log n). Worked example: prove factorial is correct by induction Claim: factorial(n) returns n! for every n ≥ 0. Base: for n = 0 and n = 1 it returns 1, which is 0! and 1!. Step: assume factorial(n − 1) returns (n − 1)!. Then factorial(n) returns n × (n − 1)! = n!. By induction the claim holds for every n ≥ 0 This is why a correct base case and a smaller recursive call are all you need. Lesson 13 of 15"],[11,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths Names made in a function are local and vanish after the call. A function can call itself if it has a base case. Python finds names with LEGB. global and nonlocal let a function assign to an outer name. Assigning anywhere makes a name local. Recursion needs a base case and a smaller step, and stops near 1,000 frames. Closures keep enclosing names alive in cells. Memoise repeated calls with lru_cache. Write recurrences to find the cost of a recursive function, and prove it correct with induction. The compiler classifies every name as local, cell, free or global and emits different bytecodes (LOAD_FAST, LOAD_DEREF, LOAD_GLOBAL). Since Python 3.11 pure Python calls do not use the C stack, but the recursion limit still guards against runaway recursion. Recursion with memoisation is top down dynamic programming, the loop version is bottom up. Beginner Tell local from global names and write a factorial with a base case. Intermediate Use LEGB, global and nonlocal correctly, trace the call stack, and avoid UnboundLocalError. Expert Write closures, memoise with lru_cache, analyse recursion with recurrences and prove it correct by induction. Lesson 14 of 15"],[11,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","These problems use exactly what this chapter teaches. The page gives only a hint. Write your own answer on LeetCode first, then compare with the downloadable solution. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 509 Fibonacci Number Easy Recursion with memo or a loop F(0) = 0, F(1) = 1. Use two names a and b and move them forward n times. 70 Climbing Stairs Easy Recurrence ways(n) = ways(n − 1) + ways(n − 2) The last step was 1 or 2 stairs. It is Fibonacci shifted by one. 326 Power of Three Easy Divide while you can Keep dividing by 3 while n % 3 == 0. A power of three ends at 1. 342 Power of Four Easy Recursive halving Base cases 1 (True) and anything not divisible by 4 (False). Otherwise ask the same question for n // 4. 1688 Count of Matches in Tournament Easy Recursion, then a maths shortcut Each round plays n // 2 matches and n − n // 2 teams go on. In the end every team but one loses once: n − 1 matches. 50 Pow(x, n) Medium Fast power by halving xn = (xn/2)2, times x when n is odd. For negative n use 1 / x−n. 22 Generate Parentheses Medium Backtracking Build the text one bracket at a time. Add \"(\" while you have some left, and \")\" only when it closes an open one. 46 Permutations Medium Backtracking Pick each unused number for the next place, recurse, then undo the choice. 78 Subsets Medium Take it or leave it For each number, make two recursive calls: one that takes it and one that skips it. 779 K-th Symbol in Grammar Medium Recursion on the parent Symbol k of row n comes from symbol (k + 1) // 2 of row n − 1. An odd k copies the parent, an even k flips it. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 15 of 15"],[11,"recap","Chapter summary","Names assigned in a function are local. Names at the top of a file are global. A local name hides a global one with the same name. Python looks names up in the order Local, Enclosing, Global, Built-in. If a name is assigned anywhere in a function, it is local in the whole function. Reading it first gives UnboundLocalError. global name lets a function assign to a module name. nonlocal name does the same for an enclosing function. A closure is an inner function that remembers enclosing names after the outer function returns. Recursion needs a base case and a recursive case that gets smaller. Each pending call is a frame on the call stack. CPython stops at about 1,000 frames with RecursionError. Memoise repeated calls with lru_cache, or use a loop. Naive fib makes 2F(n + 1) − 1 calls. Hanoi needs 2n − 1 moves. Fast power needs O(log n) calls. Next: Core 4.3 covers lambda, map, filter, sorted with key, and list, dict and set comprehensions. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: a running total closure, a recursive digit sum, and a recursive flatten for nested lists of any depth.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[12,"","lambda, map, filter and comprehensions","lambda, key functions, map, filter, reduce, list, dict and set comprehensions, generator expressions, higher-order functions. · lambda · map · filter · reduce · list comprehension · dict comprehension · set comprehension · generator expression · sorted key · functools · PCAP Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Core Python›Step 4: Functions›Lambda and comprehensions Step 4 · Topic 3 of 3 · Intermediate Write the common jobs on collections in one clear line. Learn lambda, sorting with key, map, filter and reduce, list comprehensions with filters and if-else, nested comprehensions, dict and set comprehensions, generator expressions, and functions that make functions. About 60 min14 lessons1 animation7 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Functions as values: the functional style 2lambda: a function in one expression 3Sorting and choosing with key 4map, filter and reduce 5List comprehensions 6Nested comprehensions 7Dict and set comprehensions 8Generator expressions: comprehensions without the list 9Higher-order functions, compose and partial 10Loop, comprehension or map 11The maths behind comprehensions 12From beginner to expert 13LeetCode practice 14Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 14"],[12,"idea","Functions as values: the functional style","Source: Python 3.14 tutorial sections 4.9.6 and 5.1.3 to 5.1.4, the Functional Programming HOWTO, and PCAP-31-03 section 4. What it is A lambda is a small function written in one expression. map and filter apply a function to every item of a collection. A comprehension builds a new list, dict or set from an old one in a single readable line. Why we need it Many jobs are \"do something to every item\" or \"keep the items that pass a test\". Writing a full loop with an empty list and append each time is long and hides the idea. These tools say the idea directly: squares of all numbers, prices under 100, names in upper case. How it works Because functions are objects (Core 4.1), you can hand a small function to sorted, max, map or filter. A comprehension is a loop and an optional if folded into brackets. Python runs it like the loop, but you read it like a sentence. Real world scenario: a mark sheet in a spreadsheet A teacher has a column of marks. \"Add 5 grace marks to everyone\" is map. \"Show only students below 40\" is filter. \"Sort by mark, highest first\" is sorted with a key. \"Make a new column that says Pass or Fail\" is a comprehension with if-else. One line each, and the original column is not changed. Python square = lambda x: x * x # a tiny function without a defprint(square(7))add = lambda a, b=10: a + b # parameters and defaults work as usualprint(add(1), add(1, 2))def square_def(x): # the same thing with defreturn x * xprint(square_def(7), square.__name__, square_def.__name__)print((lambda word: word[::-1])(\"level\")) # called at once Output 49 11 3 49 <lambda> square_def level Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 lambda.py. Lesson 2 of 14"],[12,"lambda","lambda: a function in one expression","lambda or def lambda def Body One expression, its value is returned Any statements, return when you want Name Anonymous (shown as <lambda>) Has a real name for errors and tracebacks Docstring and type hints No Yes Best for A tiny throw away function passed to sorted, max, map Anything you name, reuse or test Bottom line: use a lambda only as an argument. If you write name = lambda ..., a def is clearer (PEP 8 says so too). In the exam A lambda cannot contain statements such as if blocks, for loops, return or assignment with =. It can contain a conditional expression: lambda n: \"even\" if n % 2 == 0 else \"odd\". Lesson 3 of 14"],[12,"key","Sorting and choosing with key","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 key.py. Remember key is a function that is called once per item. Python sorts by what it returns. To sort by two things, return a tuple: tuples compare item by item. To reverse just one of two numeric keys, make it negative. Lesson 4 of 14"],[12,"mapfilter","map, filter and reduce","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 mapfilter.py. Tool Takes Gives Comprehension version map(f, items) A function and one or more iterables A lazy iterator of f(x) [f(x) for x in items] filter(test, items) A test function (or None) and an iterable A lazy iterator of the x where test(x) is true [x for x in items if test(x)] reduce(f, items, start) A function of two values One value A loop with an accumulator, or sum, max, min Common mistake map and filter return iterators, not lists. They give each value once, only when asked. Wrap them in list() to see all the values, and do not loop over the same map object twice. Lesson 5 of 14"],[12,"listcomp","List comprehensions","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 listcomp.py. List comprehensionExam favourite [expression for item in iterable if condition] Read it as: the expression, for each item, but only if the condition holds. With if-elseExam favourite [a if test else b for item in iterable] The if-else is part of the expression, so it comes before for. A filter if comes after. In the exam [n for n in nums if n % 2 == 0 else 0] is a SyntaxError. An if after for only filters and has no else. Lesson 6 of 14"],[12,"nestedcomp","Nested comprehensions","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 nestedcomp.py. Remember In [v for row in matrix for v in row] the for clauses are in the same order as nested loops: outer first. A comprehension inside the expression ([[... for c] for r]) builds a list of lists. If it needs more than two for clauses, a normal loop is easier to read. Lesson 7 of 14"],[12,"dictset","Dict and set comprehensions","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 dictset.py. Brackets Builds Example [ ] list [w.upper() for w in words] { } with key: value dict {w: len(w) for w in words} { } with one value set {w[0] for w in words} ( ) generator (lazy, not a tuple) (len(w) for w in words) Lesson 8 of 14"],[12,"genexp","Generator expressions: comprehensions without the list","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 genexp.py. List comprehension or generator expression [... for ...] (... for ...) Makes The whole list now One value at a time, when asked Memory for a million items Megabytes A couple of hundred bytes Use again Yes, as often as you like No, once only Index, len, slicing Yes No Best for Results you keep or reuse Feeding sum, max, any, all, join, or a for loop once Bottom line: when the values go straight into sum, max, any, all or \"\".join, use a generator expression. Advanced 2.1 shows how generators work. Lesson 9 of 14"],[12,"higher","Higher-order functions, compose and partial","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 higher.py. Remember A higher-order function takes a function or returns one: sorted, map, filter, make_greeter, compose and partial are all examples. functools.partial is often clearer than a lambda that only fixes some arguments. Lesson 10 of 14"],[12,"versus","Loop, comprehension or map","Three ways to build squares of the even numbers for loop comprehension map and filter Code result = [] then for n in nums: if n % 2 == 0: result.append(n * n) [n * n for n in nums if n % 2 == 0] list(map(lambda n: n * n, filter(lambda n: n % 2 == 0, nums))) Lines 4 1 1 Reads like Instructions A sentence Nested calls, inside out Speed in CPython Slowest Fast Fast with built-in functions, slower with lambdas Good when Several steps, prints, try and except in the body One expression and an optional filter You already have a named function: map(str.upper, words) Bottom line: use a comprehension for most transformations, map with an existing function, and a loop when the body needs statements. Lesson 11 of 14"],[12,"maths","The maths behind comprehensions","Set builder notationExam favourite { f(x) | x ∈ S, p(x) } Maths wrote \"the set of f(x) for x in S where p(x)\" long before Python. {f(x) for x in S if p(x)} is the same idea. map is f applied to each item map(f, [a1, ..., an]) = [f(a1), ..., f(an)] The length does not change. reduce is a foldExam favourite reduce(⊕, [a1, a2, a3], s) = ((s ⊕ a1) ⊕ a2) ⊕ a3 With + this is the sum, with max it is the maximum. Size of a comprehension with two for clauses |A| × |B| [(x, y) for x in range(1, 4) for y in \"ab\"] has 3 × 2 = 6 pairs, the Cartesian product A × B. Sum of squares 12 + 22 + ... + n2 = n(n + 1)(2n + 1)/6 sum(n * n for n in range(1, 11)) = 10 × 11 × 21 / 6 = 385. Worked example: what is reduce(lambda a, b: a * 10 + b, [4, 0, 7], 0)? Start with 0. 0 × 10 + 4 = 4. 4 × 10 + 0 = 40. 40 × 10 + 7 = 407 This fold turns a list of digits into a number. Lesson 12 of 14"],[12,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths A lambda is a one line function. [x * 2 for x in items] builds a new list from an old one. Use key= with sorted, min and max. Filter in a comprehension with if after for, and choose between two values with if-else before for. Build dicts and sets the same way. map and filter are lazy iterators, reduce folds a list into one value, and generator expressions save memory. Use partial and compose to build functions from functions. Comprehensions run in their own scope (since Python 3.12 they are inlined for speed, PEP 709, but the loop name still does not leak). Lambdas capture names, not values, so callbacks made in a loop all see the last value unless you bind it with a default argument. These ideas come from the lambda calculus of Alonzo Church (1936) and from languages such as Haskell. Beginner Write a lambda for sorted and a simple list comprehension. Intermediate Use map, filter, reduce, dict and set comprehensions and nested comprehensions. Expert Choose between loops, comprehensions and generator expressions by memory and speed, and build pipelines with higher-order functions. Lesson 13 of 14"],[12,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","These problems use exactly what this chapter teaches. The page gives only a hint. Write your own answer on LeetCode first, then compare with the downloadable solution. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 977 Squares of a Sorted Array Easy Comprehension and sorted Square every number with a comprehension, then sort. (A two pointer version does it in O(n).) 867 Transpose Matrix Easy Nested comprehension Row c of the answer is column c of the matrix. 1572 Matrix Diagonal Sum Easy Generator expression with sum Add mat[i][i] and mat[i][n − 1 − i]. When n is odd the middle cell was added twice. 2942 Find Words Containing Character Easy Comprehension with enumerate Keep the index i of every word that contains x. 1748 Sum of Unique Elements Easy Count, then sum with a filter Count each value, then add the values whose count is 1. 1913 Maximum Product Difference Between Two Pairs Easy sorted Biggest two times each other, minus smallest two times each other. 1636 Sort Array by Increasing Frequency Easy sorted with a tuple key Sort by (count, −value): rarer numbers first, and for equal counts the bigger number first. 451 Sort Characters By Frequency Medium Count, sort by count, join Count each letter, sort the letters by count from high to low, and repeat each one count times. 791 Custom Sort String Medium sorted with a dict key Give each letter of order its position. Letters not in order can go anywhere, here at the end. 179 Largest Number Medium Custom comparison with cmp_to_key Put a before b when a + b is bigger than b + a as text. Join, and turn \"000\" into \"0\". .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 14 of 14"],[12,"recap","Chapter summary","lambda args: expression makes a small anonymous function. Use it as an argument, use def for anything you name. sorted, min and max take key=, a function called once per item. Return a tuple to sort by several things. map(f, items) applies f to every item, filter(test, items) keeps the items that pass. Both are lazy. reduce folds a list into one value. List comprehension: [expr for x in items if test]. if-else goes before for, a filter if after it. Several for clauses run like nested loops, outer first. Comprehensions also build dicts and sets. A generator expression (expr for x in items) makes values one at a time and saves memory. It can be used once. Higher-order functions take or return functions: make_greeter, compose, functools.partial. Next: Step 5 is about things going wrong and working with the outside world: exceptions, modules and packages, and files. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: grade labels with a comprehension, top scorers with sorted and key, and a word length index with a dict comprehension.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[13,"","Exceptions: try, except, else, finally and raise","try, except, else, finally, exception hierarchy, propagation, raise, custom exceptions, assert, EAFP, with. · exceptions · try · except · finally · else · raise · traceback · ValueError · ZeroDivisionError · custom exception · assert · PCEP · PCAP Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Core Python›Step 5: Errors, modules and files›Exceptions Step 5 · Topic 1 of 3 · Intermediate Keep a program running when something goes wrong. Read tracebacks, catch errors with try and except, order handlers along the exception hierarchy, use else and finally, let errors travel to the right level, raise and re-raise your own errors, write custom exception classes, and know when to use assert, EAFP and with. About 65 min15 lessons1 animation4 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1When things go wrong: exceptions 2try and except 3Several handlers, and the exception object 4The exception hierarchy and the order of handlers 5else and finally 6Exceptions travel up through function calls 7Raising exceptions yourself 8Your own exception classes 9assert: checks for programmers 10Two styles: EAFP and LBYL 11Cleaning up: finally and with 12Counting and reasoning about failures 13From beginner to expert 14LeetCode practice 15Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 15"],[13,"idea","When things go wrong: exceptions","Source: Python 3.14 tutorial chapter 8, library reference Built-in Exceptions, PCEP-30-02 section 4.4 and PCAP-31-03 section 3. What it is An exception is an object Python raises when something goes wrong while the program runs: text that is not a number, a missing key, a file that is not there, dividing by zero. If nobody handles it, the program stops and prints a traceback. Why we need it Real programs meet bad input, broken networks and missing files every day. A program that crashes on the first typo is useless. Exceptions separate the normal path from the error path, so you can say \"try this, and if this kind of problem happens, do that instead\", and keep running. How it works You put risky code in a try block. When an exception is raised, Python stops that block at once and looks for an except clause whose class matches. If it finds one, that handler runs and the program carries on after the try statement. If not, the exception travels up to the caller, then its caller, until something handles it or the program ends. Real world scenario: a payment that fails You pay for a train ticket and your bank declines the card. A good app does not freeze or close. It catches the \"payment declined\" error, shows \"Please try another card\", keeps your booking details, and lets you try again. That is an except clause. Closing the payment window in every case, success or failure, is the finally clause. An unhandled exception (real Python 3.14 output)shell $ python3 crash.py Traceback (most recent call last): File \"/tmp/crash.py\", line 3, in <module> print(total / len(marks)) ~~~~~~^~~~~~~~~~~~ ZeroDivisionError: division by zero Remember Read a traceback from the bottom up. The last line names the exception and the reason. The lines above show where it happened, with the newest call last. Python 3.11 and later mark the exact part of the line with ^ and ~ characters. Lesson 2 of 15"],[13,"basic","try and except","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 basic.py. Syntax errors and exceptions SyntaxError Exception at run time When Before the program starts, while Python reads the file While the program runs Example print(\"hi\" (a missing bracket) int(\"seven\"), 10 / 0, {}[\"x\"] Can try and except catch it Not in the same file, which never starts Yes Fix Correct the code Handle it, or prevent the cause Bottom line: syntax errors are mistakes in writing. Exceptions are problems that happen while running, often caused by data you cannot control. Lesson 3 of 15"],[13,"multi","Several handlers, and the exception object","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 multi.py. Exception Typical cause ValueError Right type, wrong value: int(\"seven\") TypeError Wrong type: \"2\" + 3, len(5) ZeroDivisionError Dividing by zero with / // or % IndexError List index out of range: [1, 2][5] KeyError Missing dict key NameError A name that was never defined (UnboundLocalError is its child) AttributeError A missing attribute or method: (5).append(1) FileNotFoundError Opening a file that does not exist (a child of OSError) ImportError and ModuleNotFoundError A module that cannot be found or loaded RecursionError Too deep recursion (a child of RuntimeError) KeyboardInterrupt Ctrl+C. A child of BaseException, not of Exception Lesson 4 of 15"],[13,"order","The exception hierarchy and the order of handlers","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 order.py. What it is Exceptions are classes arranged in a family tree. ZeroDivisionError is a child of ArithmeticError, which is a child of Exception, which is a child of BaseException. Why we need it Sometimes you want to handle one exact problem, sometimes a whole family of related problems. The tree lets one except clause do either. How it works except SomeClass catches that class and all its children. Python tries the handlers from top to bottom and runs the first match only. So put the most specific classes first. A handler for a parent placed first would catch everything and the specific handlers below it would never run. Common mistake Avoid a bare except: or except BaseException:. They also catch KeyboardInterrupt and SystemExit, so Ctrl+C stops working. Catch Exception at most, and only where you log or report the error. Lesson 5 of 15"],[13,"elsefin","else and finally","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 elsefin.py. Clause Runs when try Always first except X An exception of class X (or a child) was raised in try else try finished with no exception finally Always last: after try, except or else, even after return, break or an unhandled exception In the exam The order is fixed: try, then except clauses, then else, then finally. You need at least one except or a finally. else keeps code out of the try block, so you do not accidentally catch errors from it. Lesson 6 of 15"],[13,"propagate","Exceptions travel up through function calls","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 propagate.py. The same call without a handler: every level is listedshell $ python3 crash2.py Traceback (most recent call last): File \"/tmp/crash2.py\", line 7, in <module> print(report([])) ~~~~~~^^^^ File \"/tmp/crash2.py\", line 5, in report return \"Average: \" + str(average(marks)) ~~~~~~~^^^^^^^ File \"/tmp/crash2.py\", line 2, in average return sum(marks) / len(marks) ~~~~~~~~~~~^~~~~~~~~~~~ ZeroDivisionError: division by zero Remember A low level function often cannot know what the right reaction is. average should not print \"No marks yet\" because it may be used in a web page or a report. Let it raise, and handle the exception at the level that knows what to tell the user. This is called delegating responsibility. Lesson 7 of 15"],[13,"raise","Raising exceptions yourself","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 raise.py. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 reraise.py. Form Meaning raise ValueError(\"message\") Raise a new exception with a message raise ValueError Python makes the object for you, with no message raise (alone, inside except) Raise the same exception again, after logging or cleaning up raise NewError(...) from err Raise a new exception and record err as its cause (__cause__) raise NewError(...) from None Hide the original exception from the traceback Lesson 8 of 15"],[13,"custom","Your own exception classes","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 custom.py. Remember Make your own exceptions by subclassing Exception (never BaseException). Give your program one base class, such as BankError, and make specific errors its children. Callers can then catch one exact problem or every problem of your program. Classes are explained in Core 6.1. Lesson 9 of 15"],[13,"assert","assert: checks for programmers","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 assert.py. assert or raise assert condition, message if not condition: raise ... Meant for Things that can only fail if the code itself has a bug Bad input, missing files, anything the outside world causes Turned off by python -O Yes No Exception AssertionError Any class you choose Bottom line: use assert to document and check your own assumptions while developing, and raise for every check that must always happen. Lesson 10 of 15"],[13,"eafp","Two styles: EAFP and LBYL","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 eafp.py. Checking first or catching the error LBYL (look before you leap) EAFP (ask forgiveness) Code if key in d: use d[key] try: use d[key] then except KeyError Fast when Errors are common Errors are rare (no extra check on the normal path) Race conditions Possible: a file can disappear between the check and the open None: the action itself is the check Python style Fine for simple checks Preferred by most Python code Bottom line: use EAFP for files, network calls and conversions, and simple if checks or get() when they read more clearly. Lesson 11 of 15"],[13,"with","Cleaning up: finally and with","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 with.py. Remember A with statement uses a context manager that closes the file (or releases a lock, or ends a database transaction) even when an exception happens. It is the short, safe form of try and finally. Advanced 2.2 shows how to write your own. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 group.py. Going deeper: Exception groups Since Python 3.11 a program can raise several unrelated exceptions at once in an ExceptionGroup, for example when ten network tasks run together and three fail (Advanced 5.1). except* picks out the parts of the group it can handle and lets the rest travel on. Lesson 12 of 15"],[13,"maths","Counting and reasoning about failures","Handlers checked before a matchExam favourite position of the first matching except clause Python stops at the first match, so order decides the cost and the meaning. Chance that at least one of n independent steps failsExam favourite 1 − (1 − p)n With p = 1 percent per network call, 50 calls fail at least once with chance 1 − 0.9950 ≈ 39 percent. Errors are normal at scale, so handle them. Expected tries until success (retry loop) 1 / q If each try succeeds with chance q = 0.8, you need 1.25 tries on average (a geometric distribution). Exponential back off before retry k wait = base × 2k 1, 2, 4, 8 seconds. Used by web clients so a busy server is not flooded (Web 2.2). Worked example: a 3 step booking with failure chances 2, 5 and 1 percent Chance that every step works: 0.98 × 0.95 × 0.99. 0.98 × 0.95 = 0.931, and 0.931 × 0.99 = 0.92169. Chance of at least one failure: 1 − 0.92169 ≈ 7.8 percent About 1 booking in 13 meets an error, so every step needs a clear except path. Lesson 13 of 15"],[13,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths An exception is an error while the program runs. try runs risky code, except handles a problem, and the program goes on. Catch specific classes, most specific first. Use as err to read the message. else runs when nothing failed, finally always runs. Exceptions travel up through function calls until something handles them. Raise your own errors with raise, re-raise with a bare raise, chain causes with from, write custom exception classes, use assert only for programmer checks, prefer EAFP, and close resources with with. CPython 3.11 made try blocks zero cost: entering a try does nothing at run time, and only raising looks up an exception table. Tracebacks are linked frame objects (__traceback__), which can keep large objects alive if you store an exception. Exception groups and except* support structured concurrency in asyncio TaskGroup. Beginner Wrap a risky line in try and except ValueError and keep the program running. Intermediate Order several handlers, use else and finally, and let errors propagate to the right level. Expert Design an exception hierarchy for a program, chain causes, and choose between EAFP and LBYL. Lesson 14 of 15"],[13,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","Error handling is mostly about checking input carefully. These problems are all about validating and parsing text, where a missed case means a wrong answer, exactly like a missed except clause in real code. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 20 Valid Parentheses Easy Stack Push every opening bracket. A closing bracket must match the top of the stack. At the end the stack must be empty. 1614 Maximum Nesting Depth of the Parentheses Easy Counter Add 1 for \"(\" and take 1 for \")\". The answer is the largest value the counter reaches. 1021 Remove Outermost Parentheses Easy Depth counter Keep a bracket only when it is not at depth 0 before an opening or after a closing. 682 Baseball Game Easy Stack of scores \"+\" adds the last two, \"D\" doubles the last, \"C\" removes the last, anything else is a number. 8 String to Integer (atoi) Medium Careful parsing Skip leading spaces, read one optional sign, read digits until something else, then clamp to the 32 bit range. 150 Evaluate Reverse Polish Notation Medium Stack Numbers go on a stack. An operator pops two numbers, applies itself and pushes the result. Division truncates toward zero. 468 Validate IP Address Medium Validate every part IPv4: 4 parts of digits, 0 to 255, no leading zero. IPv6: 8 parts of 1 to 4 hex digits. Check characters yourself, because int() also accepts signs and spaces. 71 Simplify Path Medium Stack of folder names Split on \"/\". Skip \"\" and \".\", pop for \"..\", push anything else. Join with \"/\". 227 Basic Calculator II Medium Stack with the previous operator Read a number, then apply the operator before it: + and − push, * and / combine with the top of the stack. Sum the stack. 65 Valid Number Hard Scan with a few flags Track whether you saw a digit, a dot and an exponent. A sign is allowed only at the start or right after e. A dot is not allowed after e. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 15 of 15"],[13,"recap","Chapter summary","An exception is raised when something goes wrong while the program runs. Unhandled, it stops the program with a traceback. Read tracebacks from the bottom. try holds risky code. except X handles X and its child classes. as err gives the exception object. Handlers are checked top to bottom and only the first match runs, so put specific classes first. else runs when try raised nothing. finally always runs, even after return. Exceptions travel up the call stack until handled. Handle them where you know what to do. raise X(\"message\") raises, a bare raise re-raises, and raise ... from err records the cause. Write your own exceptions as subclasses of Exception. Use assert only for programmer checks. Prefer EAFP for files and conversions, and with for anything that must be closed. Next: Core 5.2 shows how Python finds and loads modules and packages, and how pip installs new ones. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: a safe integer reader, a validator that raises the right exceptions, and a retry helper.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[14,"","Modules and packages: import, the standard library and pip","import forms, sys.modules and sys.path, math, random, platform, own modules and packages, __all__, main guard, pip and venv. · module · package · import · math · random · platform · sys.path · __init__.py · __name__ · pip · venv · PyPI · PCAP Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Core Python›Step 5: Errors, modules and files›Modules and packages Step 5 · Topic 2 of 3 · Intermediate Split code into files and use code others wrote. Learn every form of import and what import really does, the math, random and platform modules, writing your own modules and packages, private names and __all__, the main guard, pip, PyPI and virtual environments, and a tour of the standard library. About 60 min15 lessons1 animation5 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Modules: code in many files 2Every way to write import 3The math module 4The random module 5platform and sys: where am I running 6Writing your own module 7Packages: folders of modules 8Public and private names, and __all__ 9if __name__ == \"__main__\" 10pip, PyPI and virtual environments 11A short tour of the standard library 12Maths you get from math and random 13From beginner to expert 14LeetCode practice 15Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 15"],[14,"idea","Modules: code in many files","Source: Python 3.14 tutorial chapter 6, language reference chapter 5 \"The import system\", PCAP-31-03 section 1. What it is A module is a .py file whose functions, classes and variables other programs can use. A package is a folder of modules. import loads a module and gives you a name to reach what is inside. Why we need it One huge file is hard to read, test and share. Modules split a program into parts with clear jobs, let many programs reuse the same code, and give you the standard library: more than 200 modules for maths, dates, files, the web and much more, already installed. pip adds over half a million more from PyPI. How it works import name checks a cache (sys.modules). If the module is not loaded yet, Python searches the folders in sys.path, runs the file once from top to bottom in a new module object, stores it in the cache and binds the name. Later imports reuse the cached module. Real world scenario: a toolbox with labelled drawers A carpenter does not carry every tool in one heap. There is a drawer for measuring tools, one for saws, one for drills. \"import math\" opens the measuring drawer. \"from math import sqrt\" takes out just the one tool you need. The standard library is the toolbox that comes with Python, and pip is the shop where you buy new drawers. Python import math # the whole module, used as math.nameprint(math.sqrt(16), math.pi)from math import factorial, tau # just some names, used without math.print(factorial(5), tau)import statistics as st # a short aliasprint(st.mean([2, 4, 9]))from math import sqrt as root # an alias for one nameprint(root(81))names = [n for n in dir(math) ifnot n.startswith(\"_\")]print(len(names), \"public names in math, for example\", names[:6]) Output 4.0 3.141592653589793 120 6.283185307179586 5 9.0 62 public names in math, for example ['acos', 'acosh', 'asin', 'asinh', 'atan', 'atan2'] Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 imports.py. Lesson 2 of 15"],[14,"forms","Every way to write import","Form Binds Use as import math math math.sqrt(2) import math as m m m.sqrt(2) from math import sqrt, pi sqrt and pi sqrt(2) from math import sqrt as root root root(2) from math import * every public name (or those in __all__) sqrt(2), pi, ... (avoid in real code) import os.path os (and loads os.path) os.path.join(...) from . import circle circle, from the same package circle.area(1) (relative import, inside packages only) import module or from module import name import math from math import sqrt Where a name comes from Clear: math.sqrt You must look at the top of the file Name clashes None: math.log and your own log can live together Possible: a later def log replaces the imported one Typing Longer Shorter Bottom line: import the module for most code. Import single names when you use them very often and the name is clear, and never use import * outside the interactive prompt. Lesson 3 of 15"],[14,"mathmod","The math module","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 mathmod.py. Function Does Example ceil(x), floor(x) Round up, round down to a whole number ceil(4.2) = 5, floor(-4.2) = −5 trunc(x) Cut off the fraction, toward zero trunc(−4.8) = −4 sqrt(x), isqrt(n) Square root as float, exact whole number square root isqrt(20) = 4 hypot(x, y) Length of the hypotenuse, √(x2 + y2) hypot(3, 4) = 5.0 factorial, comb, perm n!, C(n, k), P(n, k) comb(5, 2) = 10 gcd, lcm Greatest common divisor, least common multiple gcd(84, 36) = 12 log, log2, log10, exp Logarithms and ex log(8, 2) = 3.0 isclose(a, b) Compare floats safely isclose(0.1 + 0.2, 0.3) is True In the exam floor goes down and trunc goes toward zero. They agree for positive numbers and differ for negative ones: floor(−4.8) is −5, trunc(−4.8) is −4. round() is a built-in, not in math, and rounds halves to the even number. Lesson 4 of 15"],[14,"randmod","The random module","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 randmod.py. Function Gives random() A float with 0.0 ≤ x < 1.0 randint(a, b) A whole number with a ≤ n ≤ b (b included!) randrange(start, stop, step) Like range: stop is not included choice(seq) One item sample(seq, k) k different items, as a new list shuffle(list) Mixes the list in place and returns None seed(n) Restart the sequence, so results repeat (useful for tests) Common mistake random gives pseudo-random numbers from the Mersenne Twister algorithm: good for games and simulations, but predictable. For passwords, tokens and anything secret use the secrets module (secrets.token_hex(16)). Lesson 5 of 15"],[14,"platmod","platform and sys: where am I running","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 platmod.py. Remember The output above came from the build machine. Press Run on this page and platform.system() says Emscripten, because the Python on this page runs inside your browser (Pyodide). The same code gives Windows or Darwin on other computers. Lesson 6 of 15"],[14,"makemod","Writing your own module","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 makemod.py. Common mistake Any file name.py that Python can find is a module called name. Use lower case names with underscores, and never name your file after a standard module: a file called random.py in your folder hides the real random module, and import random gives you your own file. Going deeper: __pycache__ When a module is imported, CPython compiles it to bytecode and saves it as __pycache__/name.cpython-314.pyc. The next import skips compiling if the .py file has not changed. The main script you run is compiled every time and never cached. You can delete __pycache__ folders safely, and they usually go in .gitignore. Lesson 7 of 15"],[14,"package","Packages: folders of modules","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 package.py. geometry/ (the package built by the program above)text 1geometry/2 __init__.py runs on \"import geometry\"3 circle.py geometry.circle4 square.py geometry.square Line 1 A folder with an __init__.py file is a regular package. Line 2 __init__.py can be empty. Here it prints a line and re-exports area from circle. Line 3 Inside the package, from .circle import area is a relative import: the dot means \"this package\". Remember Since Python 3.3 a folder without __init__.py can still be imported as a namespace package, but an __init__.py makes the intent clear and lets you run setup code and choose what the package exports. Lesson 8 of 15"],[14,"private","Public and private names, and __all__","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 private.py. Name Meaning by convention slug Public: part of what the module offers _clean Internal: may change without warning. from module import * skips it __all__ The list of names that from module import * takes __name__, __file__, __doc__ Set by Python for every module Lesson 9 of 15"],[14,"mainguard","if __name__ == \"__main__\"","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 mainguard.py. What it is Every module has a name in __name__. When you run a file directly, its __name__ is \"__main__\". When it is imported, __name__ is the module name. Why we need it You often want one file to be both a library (functions others import) and a program (code that runs when you start it). Without the check, importing the file would also run the program part. How it works Put the program part in a main() function and call it under if __name__ == \"__main__\":. Importing the file then only defines the functions. Lesson 10 of 15"],[14,"pip","pip, PyPI and virtual environments","Installing packages (a typical session)shell $ python3 -m venv .venv # make a private environment for this project $ source .venv/bin/activate # Windows: .venv\\Scripts\\activate (.venv) $ pip install requests # download from PyPI and install (.venv) $ pip list # what is installed (.venv) $ pip show requests # version, location, dependencies (.venv) $ pip install \"flask>=3.0\" # a version rule (.venv) $ pip freeze > requirements.txt (.venv) $ pip install -r requirements.txt (.venv) $ pip uninstall requests (.venv) $ deactivate What it is PyPI (the Python Package Index, pypi.org) hosts hundreds of thousands of packages. pip downloads and installs them. A virtual environment (venv) is a private folder of packages for one project. Why we need it Different projects need different versions: one app needs Django 4, another Django 5. Installing everything into the one system Python causes conflicts and can even break tools of your operating system. How it works python -m venv .venv makes the folder. Activating it puts its python and pip first on your PATH, so pip install goes into that project only. requirements.txt records exact versions so others can rebuild the same environment. Remember Run pip as python -m pip install ... when you are not sure which Python a plain pip belongs to. Modern projects describe their dependencies in a pyproject.toml file, and tools such as uv and poetry manage environments for you. Lesson 11 of 15"],[14,"stdlib","A short tour of the standard library","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 stdlib.py. Module For Chapter math, statistics, random, decimal, fractions Numbers Core 1.3 and here datetime, time, calendar Dates and times Here collections, itertools, functools, heapq, bisect Data structures and iteration Core 3.4, 4.3, Advanced 2.1 and 4.2 os, os.path, pathlib, shutil, glob Files and folders Core 5.3 json, csv, sqlite3, xml.etree, configparser, logging Data formats and logs Advanced 6.2 re Regular expressions Advanced 6.1 threading, multiprocessing, asyncio Doing several things at once Advanced 5.1 socket, http, urllib Networks and the web Web 1.1 to 1.2 unittest, doctest Testing Advanced 3.2 tkinter Desktop windows Advanced 6.3 Lesson 12 of 15"],[14,"maths","Maths you get from math and random","Combinations and permutationsExam favourite C(n, k) = n! / (k!(n − k)!), P(n, k) = n! / (n − k)! math.comb(5, 2) = 10, math.perm(5, 2) = 20. Euclid: greatest common divisorExam favourite gcd(a, b) = gcd(b, a mod b), gcd(a, 0) = a gcd(84, 36) = gcd(36, 12) = gcd(12, 0) = 12. lcm(a, b) = a × b / gcd(a, b). Change of base for logarithms logb x = ln x / ln b math.log(8, 2) = 3.0. Floats can make this 2.9999999999999996 for other numbers, so use log2 for base 2. Expected value of a fair die (randint(1, 6)) E = (1 + 2 + ... + 6)/6 = 3.5 The mean of many rolls gets close to 3.5 (law of large numbers). Monte Carlo estimate of π π ≈ 4 × (points with x2 + y2 ≤ 1) / (all points) Random points in a square, counted inside a quarter circle. The error shrinks like 1/√n. Worked example: Euclid for gcd(1071, 462) 1071 mod 462 = 147, so gcd(1071, 462) = gcd(462, 147). 462 mod 147 = 21, so it equals gcd(147, 21). 147 mod 21 = 0, so it equals gcd(21, 0). gcd(1071, 462) = 21, which is what math.gcd(1071, 462) returns Lesson 13 of 15"],[14,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths import math gives you math.sqrt and friends. A .py file you write is a module you can import too. Know every import form, the math, random and platform modules, packages with __init__.py, private _names, __all__, and if __name__ == \"__main__\". Install packages with pip inside a venv. Imports are cached in sys.modules and searched along sys.path. Modules run once. Relative imports work inside packages. Avoid circular imports and file names that hide standard modules. Use secrets, not random, for security. The import system is built from finders and loaders on sys.meta_path (importlib.machinery), which is how zip files, frozen modules and namespace packages are imported. Python 3.12 removed distutils, and packaging is now described by pyproject.toml (PEP 517, 518, 621) with build back ends such as setuptools and hatchling. Beginner Import a module three ways and use math and random. Intermediate Write your own module and package, use __name__ and __all__, and manage packages with pip and venv. Expert Explain sys.modules, sys.path, finders and loaders, and package a project with pyproject.toml. Lesson 14 of 15"],[14,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","These problems are easy once you know the right standard module: math for gcd and square roots, datetime for calendars, random and bisect for random picks. Knowing the library is a skill interviewers value. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1979 Find Greatest Common Divisor of Array Easy math.gcd Take the smallest and the largest number and return math.gcd of the two. 1071 Greatest Common Divisor of Strings Easy math.gcd on lengths If str1 + str2 differs from str2 + str1 there is no answer. Otherwise the answer is the first gcd(len1, len2) characters. 69 Sqrt(x) Easy Binary search (or math.isqrt) Find the largest m with m × m ≤ x by halving the search range. 367 Valid Perfect Square Easy math.isqrt math.isqrt(n) is the exact whole square root rounded down. Square it back and compare. 1185 Day of the Week Easy datetime datetime.date(year, month, day).weekday() gives 0 for Monday. Map it to the names. 1360 Number of Days Between Two Dates Easy datetime subtraction Turn both texts into dates with date.fromisoformat and take abs of the difference in days. 1154 Day of the Year Easy datetime Subtract 1 January of the same year and add 1. 172 Factorial Trailing Zeroes Medium Count factors of 5 Each trailing zero needs a 10 = 2 × 5, and 5s are rarer. Add n // 5 + n // 25 + n // 125 + ... 384 Shuffle an Array Medium random and the Fisher Yates shuffle Keep a copy of the original. To shuffle, swap item i with a random item from i to the end. 528 Random Pick with Weight Medium Prefix sums, random and bisect Build running totals. Pick a random number up to the total and find its slot with bisect. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 15 of 15"],[14,"recap","Chapter summary","A module is a .py file, a package is a folder of modules (usually with __init__.py). import math, import math as m, from math import sqrt, from math import sqrt as root. Avoid import *. import checks sys.modules, searches sys.path, runs the file once and caches it. importlib.reload runs it again. math: ceil, floor, trunc, sqrt, isqrt, hypot, factorial, comb, gcd, log, isclose. random: random, randint (both ends), randrange, choice, sample, shuffle, seed. A name starting with _ is private by convention. __all__ lists what import * takes. __name__ is \"__main__\" when a file is run directly. Guard the program part with if __name__ == \"__main__\":. pip installs packages from PyPI. Use a venv per project and record versions in requirements.txt. Next: Core 5.3 reads and writes files: open modes, reading line by line, with, paths, and text vs binary data. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: a dice simulation with a fixed seed, importing a module by its name, and gcd and lcm of a whole list.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[15,"","Files: reading, writing, modes, paths and streams","open modes, with, read and write methods, binary files, encodings, errno, pathlib, os and shutil, streams, csv and json. · files · open · with · read · write · readline · binary · bytearray · encoding · errno · pathlib · os · stdout · csv · PCAP Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Core Python›Step 5: Errors, modules and files›Files Step 5 · Topic 3 of 3 · Intermediate Make data last after the program ends. Learn open and with, every open mode, reading line by line, writing, binary files with bytes and bytearray, encodings, file errors and errno, paths with pathlib, folders with os and shutil, the standard streams, and CSV and JSON files. About 60 min15 lessons1 animation5 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Files: data that lasts 2Reading a text file 3Open modes 4Always use with 5Binary files, bytes and bytearray 6Encodings: always say utf-8 7File errors and errno 8Paths with pathlib 9Folders with os and shutil 10stdin, stdout and stderr 11CSV and JSON files 12Sizes, bytes and cursors 13From beginner to expert 14LeetCode practice 15Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 15"],[15,"idea","Files: data that lasts","Source: Python 3.14 tutorial section 7.2, the io, os, pathlib, errno, csv and json documentation, and PCAP-31-03 section 4. What it is A file is named data stored on a disk. Python reaches it through a file object (a stream) that open() gives you. Text files hold characters, binary files hold raw bytes. Why we need it Variables vanish when a program ends. Files keep data between runs: settings, saved games, reports, logs, photos. They are also how programs exchange data, as CSV, JSON or plain text. How it works open(name, mode) connects to the file and returns a file object with a cursor. read and write work at the cursor and move it on. close() (or the end of a with block) saves everything and frees the file. In text mode Python decodes bytes to str with an encoding such as UTF-8. Real world scenario: a notebook and a bookmark Opening a notebook to read puts a bookmark at page 1. Each page you read moves the bookmark forward. Opening it to write a fresh story (\"w\") tears out every old page first. Opening it to add notes (\"a\") turns to the last page. Closing the notebook puts it back on the shelf. The bookmark is the file cursor. Python import os, tempfileos.chdir(tempfile.mkdtemp()) # work in an empty folder made for this demof = open(\"shopping.txt\", \"w\", encoding=\"utf-8\")f.write(\"milk\\n\") # write() does not add a new line by itselff.write(\"eggs\\n\")f.writelines([\"rice\\n\", \"tea\\n\"]) # nor does writelines()print(\"bread\", file=f) # print() adds the new linef.close()print(open(\"shopping.txt\", encoding=\"utf-8\").read())print(os.path.getsize(\"shopping.txt\"), \"bytes\") Output milk eggs rice tea bread 25 bytes Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 write.py. Remember All the programs in this chapter first move into a new empty temporary folder with tempfile.mkdtemp(), so they never touch your own files. Drop that line when you work in your project folder. Lesson 2 of 15"],[15,"read","Reading a text file","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 read.py. Method Returns At the end of the file read() The rest of the file as one str \"\" read(n) Up to n characters \"\" readline() One line, including its new line character \"\" readlines() A list of all remaining lines [] for line in f One line per turn, without loading the whole file The loop ends In the exam Lines keep their new line character. Use line.strip() or line.rstrip(\"\\n\") before you compare or split them. For big files loop with for line in f, which reads one line at a time instead of loading gigabytes into memory. Lesson 3 of 15"],[15,"modes","Open modes","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 modes.py. Mode Read Write File must exist Empties the file Cursor starts r (default) Yes No Yes (else FileNotFoundError) No Start w No Yes No, it is created Yes Start a No Yes, always at the end No, it is created No End x No Yes Must NOT exist (else FileExistsError) New file Start r+ Yes Yes Yes No Start w+ Yes Yes No Yes Start a+ Yes Yes, at the end No No End Common mistake Add t (text, the default) or b (binary) to any mode: \"rb\", \"wb\", \"ab\", \"r+b\". The most common accident is opening a file with \"w\" when you meant \"a\": the old contents are gone at once. Lesson 4 of 15"],[15,"with","Always use with","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 with.py. Closing files f = open(...) then f.close() with open(...) as f: If an exception happens in between close() is skipped, the file may stay open and data may not be saved The file is closed anyway If you forget close() Data may sit in a buffer and never reach the disk Not possible Lines of code More, plus try and finally to be safe Fewer Bottom line: open files with with every time. It is try and finally in one line. Lesson 5 of 15"],[15,"binary","Binary files, bytes and bytearray","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 binary.py. Text mode or binary mode text (\"r\", \"w\") binary (\"rb\", \"wb\") read() returns str bytes write() takes str bytes or bytearray Encoding Decodes and encodes (say encoding=\"utf-8\") None, raw bytes New line characters Changed to and from the system style Never changed Use for Text, CSV, JSON, code Images, audio, zip, PDF, anything not text Bottom line: use text mode for human readable text, and binary mode for everything else, or when you must copy a file byte for byte. Lesson 6 of 15"],[15,"encoding","Encodings: always say utf-8","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 encoding.py. Common mistake Without encoding=..., open() uses a default that depends on the computer. It is UTF-8 on Linux and macOS but can be an old code page on Windows, so a file written on one computer may not read on another. Since Python 3.15 the default becomes UTF-8 everywhere (PEP 686), but writing encoding=\"utf-8\" is still the safe habit. Lesson 7 of 15"],[15,"errors","File errors and errno","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 errors.py. Exception (all are OSError) errno name When FileNotFoundError ENOENT The file or folder does not exist FileExistsError EEXIST Mode \"x\" or mkdir on something that exists PermissionError EACCES or EPERM Not allowed to read or write it IsADirectoryError EISDIR open() on a folder NotADirectoryError ENOTDIR A path part is a file, not a folder OSError with ENOSPC ENOSPC The disk is full Lesson 8 of 15"],[15,"paths","Paths with pathlib","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 paths.py. Two ways to work with paths os.path (strings) pathlib.Path (objects) Join os.path.join(\"a\", \"b.txt\") Path(\"a\") / \"b.txt\" Parts os.path.basename, splitext p.name, p.stem, p.suffix, p.parent Read and write open(...) then read() p.read_text(), p.write_text() Find files glob.glob(\"*.csv\") p.glob(\"*.csv\"), p.rglob(\"*.csv\") Style Older, everywhere in old code Modern, clearer Bottom line: use pathlib in new code. Both work on Windows, macOS and Linux, which use different separators. Lesson 9 of 15"],[15,"osmod","Folders with os and shutil","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 osmod.py. Common mistake os.remove, os.rename and shutil.rmtree do not use a recycle bin: deleted files are gone. Print the paths and check them before you delete anything in a loop. Lesson 10 of 15"],[15,"streams","stdin, stdout and stderr","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 streams.py. Stream Python name Used by Standard input sys.stdin input() reads from it Standard output sys.stdout print() writes to it Standard error sys.stderr Tracebacks and warnings, print(..., file=sys.stderr) Remember The page shows only stdout, so the stderr line above is not in the output. In a terminal both appear, but they are separate streams: python3 app.py > out.txt saves only stdout and still shows errors on the screen. Lesson 11 of 15"],[15,"csvjson","CSV and JSON files","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 csvjson.py. Common mistake Never split CSV lines with line.split(\",\"). A value like \"Ravi, Jr\" contains a comma and is stored in quotes. The csv module handles quotes, commas and new lines inside values correctly. Advanced 6.2 covers csv, json, xml and sqlite3 in depth. Lesson 12 of 15"],[15,"maths","Sizes, bytes and cursors","File size in bytes of an ASCII textExam favourite sum of len(line) for all lines, new lines included \"milk\\n\" is 5 bytes. On Windows a text file stores each new line as 2 bytes (\\r\\n). UTF-8 sizeExam favourite 1 byte for code points 0 to 127, 2 up to 2,047, 3 up to 65,535, 4 above \"नमस्ते ₹100 café\\n\" has 16 characters but 31 bytes. Units 1 KB = 1,000 bytes, 1 KiB = 1,024 bytes Disks are sold in KB, MB, GB (powers of 10). Operating systems often show KiB, MiB, GiB (powers of 2). Cursor after a read in text mode (ASCII) new position = old position + characters read In binary mode tell() is always a byte offset. In text mode it is an opaque number, so only seek() to values that tell() gave you. Lines in memory readlines(): O(file size). for line in f: O(longest line) A 10 GB log can be processed with a for loop on a laptop with 8 GB of memory. Worked example: size of the shopping file Lines: \"milk\\n\", \"eggs\\n\", \"rice\\n\", \"tea\\n\", \"bread\\n\". Lengths: 5, 5, 5, 4, 6. 5 + 5 + 5 + 4 + 6 = 25 bytes, which is what os.path.getsize printed Lesson 13 of 15"],[15,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths open a file with \"w\" to write and \"r\" to read, and use with so it closes itself. Know every mode, read with read, readline, readlines and a for loop, write with write and writelines, say encoding=\"utf-8\", and catch FileNotFoundError. Use binary mode for non text data, bytearray and readinto for buffers, pathlib for paths, os and shutil for folders, errno to tell OSErrors apart, and csv and json for structured data. open() builds a layered stack: a raw FileIO (system calls), a BufferedReader or BufferedWriter (fewer system calls), and a TextIOWrapper (decoding and new line translation). flush() pushes the buffer to the operating system, and os.fsync() asks it to write to the disk. Writing to a temporary file and then os.replace() makes saving atomic, so a crash never leaves a half written file. Beginner Write and read a text file with with and a for loop. Intermediate Choose the right mode, handle file errors, use encodings, binary files, pathlib and csv. Expert Explain the io layers and buffering, write files atomically, and process files bigger than memory. Lesson 14 of 15"],[15,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","Files are mostly text, folders and paths. These problems practise exactly that: cleaning and counting words, parsing tokens, grouping files by content and working with folder paths. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1816 Truncate Sentence Easy split and join Split on spaces, keep the first k words, join them again. 819 Most Common Word Easy Clean text, count words Turn every non letter into a space, lower case everything, count the words that are not banned and take the most common. 884 Uncommon Words from Two Sentences Easy Count words of both texts A word is uncommon if it appears exactly once in the two sentences together. 1592 Rearrange Spaces Between Words Easy Count spaces, divide Count the spaces and words. Put spaces // (words − 1) between words and the rest at the end. 2042 Check if Numbers Are Ascending in a Sentence Easy Parse tokens Split into tokens. Every token made of digits must be bigger than the previous number. 609 Find Duplicate File in System Medium Group paths by content Each entry is \"folder name(content) ...\". Group full paths by content in a dict and keep groups with more than one path. 388 Longest Absolute File Path Medium Depth by tabs, a stack of lengths Split into lines. The number of tabs is the depth. Keep the path length at each depth. A name with a dot is a file. 1233 Remove Sub-Folders from the Filesystem Medium Sort, then compare with the last kept folder After sorting, a sub folder comes right after its parent. Keep a folder unless it starts with the last kept folder plus \"/\". 1047 Remove All Adjacent Duplicates In String Easy Stack Push each character. If it equals the top of the stack, pop instead. 68 Text Justification Hard Greedy lines, spread spaces Fill each line with as many words as fit. Spread the spaces, giving the left gaps one extra when they do not divide evenly. The last line is left aligned. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 15 of 15"],[15,"recap","Chapter summary","open(name, mode, encoding=\"utf-8\") returns a file object with a cursor. Always use with so it closes. Modes: r read (must exist), w write (empties), a append, x create new only, + adds the other direction, b for binary. read(), read(n), readline(), readlines(), and for line in f, which is best for big files. Lines keep their new line. write() and writelines() do not add new lines. print(..., file=f) does. Binary mode reads and writes bytes. bytearray and readinto fill a buffer. seek and tell move and report the cursor. File errors are OSErrors: FileNotFoundError, FileExistsError, PermissionError. err.errno matches errno.ENOENT and friends. pathlib.Path joins with /, and has name, suffix, glob, read_text and write_text. os and shutil handle folders. sys.stdin, sys.stdout, sys.stderr are the standard streams. Use csv and json for structured files. Next: Step 6 starts object oriented programming: classes, objects, attributes and methods. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: count words in a file, append a log line with a time stamp, and copy a file in fixed size chunks.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[16,"","Classes and objects: attributes, methods, self and encapsulation","classes, objects, __init__, self, instance and class attributes, __str__ and __repr__, introspection, name mangling, is and ==. · class · object · instance · __init__ · self · method · attribute · __dict__ · __str__ · __repr__ · hasattr · name mangling · encapsulation · OOP · PCAP Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Core Python›Step 6: Object oriented programming›Classes Step 6 · Topic 1 of 2 · Intermediate Model real things as objects. Learn to write a class with __init__ and methods, what self is, instance attributes and class attributes, the shared list trap, __str__ and __repr__, introspection with hasattr and getattr, private names and name mangling, and identity and equality of objects. About 70 min15 lessons1 animation6 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Classes and objects 2Methods, self and __init__ 3Instance attributes and class attributes 4The shared list trap 5Class attributes as counters 6__str__ and __repr__ 7Introspection: looking inside objects 8Encapsulation, _private and name mangling 9Identity and equality of objects 10Putting it together: a bank account 11Classes compared with other ways to group data 12The maths inside a class 13From beginner to expert 14LeetCode practice 15Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 15"],[16,"idea","Classes and objects","Source: Python 3.14 tutorial chapter 9 \"Classes\", the data model reference section 3.3, and PCAP-31-03 section 3. What it is A class is a blueprint that describes a kind of thing: what data it holds (its attributes) and what it can do (its methods). An object (also called an instance) is one real thing made from that blueprint. Why we need it Programs model the real world: students, bank accounts, shapes, orders. Keeping each thing as loose variables and separate functions gets messy fast. A class keeps the data and the code that works on it together in one place, so each object looks after itself. How it works class Dog: makes a class object. Calling Dog(\"Rex\", 3) makes a new empty object and runs __init__ on it to fill in its attributes. Every object has its own attributes but shares the methods of its class. When you write rex.bark(), Python finds bark on the class and passes rex in as self. Real world scenario: a cake recipe and the cakes A recipe says a cake has a flavour and a size and can be cut into slices. The recipe itself is not a cake. Each cake you bake from it is a separate cake with its own flavour and size. Cutting one cake does not change the others. The recipe is the class and each cake is an object. Python classDog: species = \"dog\"# class attribute: shared by every dogdef __init__(self, name, age): # runs when a new dog is made self.name = name # instance attributes: each dog has its own self.age = agedef bark(self): # a method: a function that belongs to the classreturn self.name + \" says woof\"def birthday(self): self.age += 1rex = Dog(\"Rex\", 3) # make an object (an instance) of the classmoti = Dog(\"Moti\", 5)print(rex.name, rex.age, \"|\", moti.name, moti.age)print(rex.bark())rex.birthday()print(rex.age, moti.age) # only Rex got olderprint(type(rex).__name__, isinstance(rex, Dog), rex.species) Output Rex 3 | Moti 5 Rex says woof 4 5 Dog True dog Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 first.py. Word Meaning In the program class The blueprint Dog object or instance One thing made from the class rex, moti attribute A value stored on an object or a class name, age, species method A function defined inside the class bark, birthday constructor The code that sets up a new object __init__ self The object a method is working on self.name Lesson 2 of 15"],[16,"self","Methods, self and __init__","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 self.py. In the exam self is not a keyword. It is only the name every Python programmer uses for the first parameter of a method. Python fills it in for you: rex.bark() becomes Dog.bark(rex). Forget self in the def line and the call fails with \"takes 0 positional arguments but 1 was given\". You write Python does rex = Dog(\"Rex\") Makes an empty object, then calls Dog.__init__(that object, \"Rex\"), then names it rex rex.bark() Finds bark on the class and calls Dog.bark(rex) Dog.bark A plain function rex.bark A bound method: the function plus the object, ready to call Going deeper: Where does the new object come from Making an object has two steps. First the special method __new__ creates the empty object. Then __init__ fills it in. __init__ only sets things up and must return None. Returning anything else raises TypeError. You almost never write __new__ yourself (Advanced 1.4 shows when you do). Lesson 3 of 15"],[16,"attrs","Instance attributes and class attributes","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 attrs.py. Two places an attribute can live instance attribute class attribute Made by self.name = ... (usually in __init__) name = ... in the class body Stored in obj.__dict__ Class.__dict__ How many copies One per object One for the whole class Good for Data that differs between objects: name, age, balance Constants and data every object shares: species, bank name, a counter Bottom line: put data that changes from object to object on the instance, and shared constants on the class. How Python reads obj.attr Look in obj.__dict__, the attributes of the object itself. If it is not there, look in the class of the object (and later in its parent classes, see 6.2). If it is still not found, raise AttributeError. In the exam Writing obj.attr = value never goes to the class. It always creates or changes the attribute on that one object. That is why a.species = \"wolf\" left b.species and Dog.species alone. Lesson 4 of 15"],[16,"shared","The shared list trap","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 shared.py. Common mistake A class attribute that holds a list, dict or set is ONE object for all instances. Changing it with append or update through any object changes it for every object. Create such data in __init__ with self.items = [] instead. This is the same trap as a mutable default argument (4.1). Lesson 5 of 15"],[16,"counter","Class attributes as counters","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 counter.py. In the exam Inside a method, change a class attribute through the class name: Student.count += 1. Writing self.count += 1 reads the class value but then stores the result on the object, so the class counter would never change. Lesson 6 of 15"],[16,"strrepr","__str__ and __repr__","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 strrepr.py. Two ways to turn an object into text __str__ __repr__ Used by print(obj), str(obj), f\"{obj}\" repr(obj), f\"{obj!r}\", the REPL, items inside a list Written for Users Developers Good style Short and friendly: (3, 4) Clear and exact, ideally valid code: Point(3, 4) If missing Python uses __repr__ Python shows the class name and memory address Bottom line: always write __repr__. Add __str__ only when users need a friendlier form. Lesson 7 of 15"],[16,"introspect","Introspection: looking inside objects","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 introspect.py. Tool Answers type(obj), obj.__class__ Which class made this object isinstance(obj, C) Is obj a C (or a child class of C, see 6.2) hasattr(obj, \"x\") Can obj.x be read without an error getattr(obj, \"x\", default) Read an attribute by its name as a string setattr(obj, \"x\", v), delattr(obj, \"x\") Set or delete an attribute by name vars(obj), obj.__dict__ The instance attributes as a dict C.__name__, C.__module__, C.__bases__, C.__doc__ The class name, its module, its parent classes and its docstring dir(obj) Every attribute name, including the inherited ones Remember getattr with a name in a variable is how frameworks such as Django read a field whose name comes from a settings file or a URL. Lesson 8 of 15"],[16,"private","Encapsulation, _private and name mangling","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 private.py. Name Meaning Enforced balance Public: anyone may use it Not needed _balance Internal: please use it only inside the class No, a convention only __balance Name mangled to _Account__balance so child classes cannot clash with it by accident Partly: the short name fails outside the class __init__ Special (dunder) name used by Python itself Do not invent your own In the exam Encapsulation means an object guards its own data and offers methods to work with it, so outside code cannot put it into a bad state (like a negative balance). Python trusts programmers: it has no real private keyword like Java. Use one underscore for internal things, and properties (Advanced 1.3) when you need checks on reading or writing. Lesson 9 of 15"],[16,"identity","Identity and equality of objects","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 identity.py. In the exam a is b asks \"are these the same object\". a == b asks \"are these equal\". For your own classes == means the same as is until you write __eq__. Writing __eq__ sets __hash__ to None, so the objects can no longer be set items or dict keys unless you also write __hash__ (Advanced 1.1). Lesson 10 of 15"],[16,"bank","Putting it together: a bank account","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 bank.py. Remember Each method keeps the object valid: deposit refuses amounts that are not positive and withdraw refuses to go below zero. Code outside the class never has to repeat these checks. That is the main gain of object oriented design. Lesson 11 of 15"],[16,"compare","Classes compared with other ways to group data","A student record three ways separate variables a dict a class Example name1, mark1, name2, mark2 {\"name\": \"Asha\", \"mark\": 88} Student(\"Asha\", 88) Typing mistakes Easy to mix up s[\"nmae\"] fails only when run s.nmae fails, and editors warn early Behaviour Separate functions Separate functions Methods next to the data Rules (no negative marks) Checked everywhere by hand Checked everywhere by hand Checked once, in the class Many of them Impossible A list of dicts A list of objects Best for A few unrelated values Data from JSON, flexible keys Things with data AND behaviour Bottom line: use a dict for loose data, and a class when the data has rules and actions. For pure data with no behaviour, dataclasses (Advanced 1.3) give you a class in two lines. Object oriented and procedural style procedural (functions and data apart) object oriented (classes) Organised around Steps and functions Things and their behaviour Data protection Any function can change any data Methods guard their own object Reuse Copy or call functions Inheritance and composition (6.2) Good for Short scripts, data pipelines Bigger programs, games, GUIs, web frameworks Bottom line: Python lets you mix both. Start with functions and move to a class when several functions keep passing the same data around. Lesson 12 of 15"],[16,"maths","The maths inside a class","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 vector.py. Length of a vector (Pythagoras)Exam favourite |v| = √(x2 + y2) Vector(3, 4).length() is √(9 + 16) = 5. Dot productExam favourite a · b = axbx + ayby (3, 4) · (1, 2) = 3 + 8 = 11. Angle between two vectors cos θ = (a · b) / (|a| |b|) cos θ = 11 / (5 × 2.236) = 0.984, so θ = 10.3 degrees. Counting objects with a class attribute count after n objects = n One class attribute is shared, so 3 students give Student.count = 3. Name mangling __name inside class C becomes _C__name __balance in Account becomes _Account__balance (17 characters). Memory n objects store n copies of each instance attribute but 1 copy of each class attribute and method 1,000 dogs: 1,000 names, 1 species, 1 bark function. Worked example: the angle between a = (3, 4) and b = (1, 2) a · b = 3 × 1 + 4 × 2 = 11. |a| = √25 = 5 and |b| = √5 ≈ 2.236. cos θ = 11 / 11.18 ≈ 0.9839. θ = acos(0.9839) ≈ 10.30 degrees, which the program printed Lesson 13 of 15"],[16,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths A class is a blueprint and objects are things made from it. Write __init__ to set attributes with self, and call methods with dot notation. Tell instance attributes from class attributes, avoid the shared list trap, write __str__ and __repr__, use isinstance, hasattr and getattr, and protect data with methods and the underscore convention. Know how attribute lookup works (instance dict, then class), what a bound method is, what name mangling does and why it exists, and how __eq__ and __hash__ go together. Choose between a dict, a class and a dataclass for a design. Classes are objects too: their type is type, the class body runs once and fills a namespace that type() turns into a class. Attribute access goes through object.__getattribute__, which checks data descriptors on the class, then the instance __dict__, then other class attributes. Methods are functions that are descriptors: their __get__ makes the bound method. __slots__ replaces the per object dict with fixed slots to save memory (Advanced 1.3 and 1.4). Beginner Write a class with __init__, attributes and methods, and make objects from it. Intermediate Use class attributes correctly, write __str__ and __repr__, use introspection and encapsulation. Expert Explain attribute lookup, bound methods, name mangling and the __eq__ and __hash__ contract. Lesson 14 of 15"],[16,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","LeetCode \"design\" problems ask you to write a class: you choose the attributes in __init__ and write the methods. They are the best practice for this chapter because each one is about keeping an object in a correct state. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 155 Min Stack Medium Keep a second stack of minimums Next to the normal stack, keep a stack whose top is always the smallest value so far. Push to it when the new value is not bigger. 232 Implement Queue using Stacks Easy Two stacks Push onto an inbox stack. To pop, pour the inbox into an outbox stack (which reverses it) only when the outbox is empty. 225 Implement Stack using Queues Easy Rotate one queue After adding x to the back, move every older item from the front to the back, so x is at the front. 705 Design HashSet Easy Buckets of lists Keep 1,000 small lists. A key goes in bucket key % 1000, so each search looks at only one short list. 706 Design HashMap Easy Buckets of key and value pairs Like the hash set, but each bucket holds [key, value] pairs. put updates a pair or adds one. 1603 Design Parking System Easy One counter per car type Store the free spaces in a list indexed by car type. A car parks only if its counter is above zero. 1472 Design Browser History Medium A list and a position Keep the pages in a list and the index of the current page. visit cuts off the forward pages, back and forward move the index within limits. 622 Design Circular Queue Medium Fixed list, head index and count The back of the queue is at (head + count) % k. Moving the head with % k makes the list wrap around. 2043 Simple Bank System Medium Check, then change Every method first checks that the account numbers exist and the money is there, and only then changes the balances. 146 LRU Cache Medium A dict keeps insertion order Python dicts remember the order keys were added. Move a key to the end when it is used, and remove the first key when the cache is full. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 15 of 15"],[16,"recap","Chapter summary","A class is a blueprint, an object (instance) is one thing made from it. Calling the class makes an object. __init__(self, ...) sets up a new object. self is the object a method works on: obj.m() means Class.m(obj). Instance attributes (self.x) belong to one object. Class attributes (in the class body) are shared by all objects. Reading obj.x looks in the object first, then the class. Writing obj.x = v always writes to the object. A list or dict as a class attribute is shared by every object. Create it in __init__ instead. __str__ is for print, __repr__ is for developers. Write __repr__ at least. type, isinstance, hasattr, getattr, setattr, vars and __name__, __module__, __bases__ let code look inside objects. _x means internal by convention. __x is name mangled to _Class__x. is checks identity, == checks equality (identity until you write __eq__). Next: 6.2 builds on this with inheritance, super(), overriding, multiple inheritance and the MRO. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: a Rectangle class, a bank Account that refuses bad amounts, and a Stack class that keeps its own list.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[17,"","Inheritance: super(), overriding, polymorphism and the MRO","inheritance, overriding, super(), isinstance, polymorphism, duck typing, abstract methods, mixins, MRO, composition. · inheritance · subclass · override · super · isinstance · issubclass · polymorphism · duck typing · abstract · mixin · MRO · C3 · diamond · composition · PCAP Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Core Python›Step 6: Object oriented programming›Inheritance Step 6 · Topic 2 of 2 · Intermediate Reuse classes. Learn single inheritance and overriding, super() for the parent setup and for extending methods, isinstance and issubclass, polymorphism and duck typing, abstract methods, multiple inheritance and mixins, the method resolution order with C3 and the diamond problem, and when composition is the better choice. About 70 min13 lessons1 animation7 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Inheritance: reuse a class 2Overriding methods 3super(): use the parent version 4isinstance, issubclass and __bases__ 5Polymorphism and duck typing 6Abstract methods and a shape family 7Multiple inheritance and mixins 8The MRO and the diamond problem 9Composition compared with inheritance 10The maths: areas, MROs and lookup cost 11From beginner to expert 12LeetCode practice 13Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 13"],[17,"idea","Inheritance: reuse a class","Source: Python 3.14 tutorial section 9.5 \"Inheritance\", the built-in functions super, isinstance and issubclass, the abc module documentation, M. Simionato \"The Python 2.3 Method Resolution Order\", and PCAP-31-03 section 3. What it is Inheritance lets a new class (the child, subclass or derived class) take all the attributes and methods of an existing class (the parent, superclass or base class) and then add to them or change them. Why we need it Many classes share most of their code: dogs and cats both have a name and can describe themselves. Writing that code again in each class means copies to keep in step. Inheritance writes it once in the parent, and each child only adds what is different. How it works class Dog(Animal): makes Dog a child of Animal. When you use rex.describe(), Python looks for describe in Dog, does not find it, and moves on to Animal. A method the child defines with the same name overrides the parent one. Real world scenario: a family recipe A family has a basic dal recipe. One cousin uses it exactly as it is. Another follows it but adds more chilli. A third keeps every step but swaps the last one for a coconut finish. Nobody rewrites the whole recipe: they inherit it and change only what they need. Python classAnimal: # the parent class (superclass, base class)def __init__(self, name): self.name = namedef speak(self):return\"...\"def describe(self):return self.name + \" says \" + self.speak()classDog(Animal): # Dog inherits everything from Animaldef speak(self): # and overrides speakreturn\"woof\"classCat(Animal):def speak(self):return\"meow\"classFish(Animal): # overrides nothingpassfor pet in [Dog(\"Rex\"), Cat(\"Tom\"), Fish(\"Nemo\")]:print(pet.describe()) # describe is written once, in Animal Output Rex says woof Tom says meow Nemo says ... Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 basic.py. Remember The child is a special kind of the parent: a Dog is an Animal. If you cannot say \"a Child is a Parent\" in plain words, inheritance is probably the wrong tool (see composition below). Lesson 2 of 13"],[17,"override","Overriding methods","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 override.py. How Python finds rex.speak() Look in rex.__dict__ (the object itself). Look in Dog, the class of rex. speak is there, so use it. Only if Dog had no speak would Python look in Animal, then in object. Not found anywhere: AttributeError. In the exam An overriding method normally keeps the same parameters as the parent one, so code that works with an Animal also works with a Dog. This rule is called the Liskov substitution principle. Lesson 3 of 13"],[17,"super","super(): use the parent version","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 super.py. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 nosuper.py. Two ways to call the parent super().method(...) Parent.method(self, ...) self Passed for you You pass it by hand If the parent class is renamed Nothing to change Every call must be edited Multiple inheritance Follows the MRO, so every class runs once Can run a class twice or skip one Seen in Modern code Old code Bottom line: use super(). It is shorter and correct with multiple inheritance. Common mistake A child __init__ replaces the parent one completely. If it does not call super().__init__(...), the attributes the parent sets are never created. Lesson 4 of 13"],[17,"checks","isinstance, issubclass and __bases__","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 checks.py. Check True when isinstance(obj, C) obj was made by C or by any child of C type(obj) is C obj was made by exactly C (children do not count) issubclass(A, B) A is B or a child (at any depth) of B C.__bases__ A tuple of the direct parents of C C.__mro__ The full search order, ending with object In the exam Prefer isinstance over type(x) == C, because a child object is also a valid parent object. Every class inherits from object in the end, and bool is a child of int, which is why True + True is 2. Lesson 5 of 13"],[17,"poly","Polymorphism and duck typing","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 poly.py. What it is Polymorphism means one piece of code works with many types: make_noise(thing) calls thing.speak() without caring what thing is. Why we need it Without it you would write if type is Dog ... elif type is Cat ... everywhere, and add a branch for every new type. How it works Python does not check the type. It only looks up the method by name when the code runs. If the object has speak, it works. This is duck typing: if it walks like a duck and quacks like a duck, treat it as a duck. Two kinds of polymorphism through inheritance duck typing Needs a shared parent Yes No Checked When the code runs When the code runs Error if a method is missing NotImplementedError from the parent, or a TypeError from abc AttributeError at the call Seen in Frameworks: Django models, views Everywhere: len, for loops, files, + Bottom line: Python mostly relies on duck typing. Use a common parent when the classes really share code. Lesson 6 of 13"],[17,"shapes","Abstract methods and a shape family","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 shapes.py. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 abstract.py. Making children write a method raise NotImplementedError abc.ABC with @abstractmethod When the mistake shows Only when the missing method is called As soon as you try to make the object Parent can be made Yes No Import needed No from abc import ABC, abstractmethod Bottom line: abc finds the mistake earlier. Advanced 1.3 covers abstract base classes in depth. Lesson 7 of 13"],[17,"multi","Multiple inheritance and mixins","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 multi.py. Remember A mixin is a small class that adds one ability (like to_json) and is not meant to be used alone. Django uses mixins a lot, for example LoginRequiredMixin on views. Put mixins first or last consistently and keep them free of __init__ when you can. Lesson 8 of 13"],[17,"mro","The MRO and the diamond problem","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 diamond.py. C3 linearisationExam favourite L[C] = C + merge(L[B1], ..., L[Bn], [B1, ..., Bn]) B1 ... Bn are the parents of C in the order written. The merge rule take the first head that is not in the tail of any list, remove it everywhere, repeat If every head is in some tail, there is no valid order and Python raises TypeError. Rules every MRO keeps A class comes before its parents. Parents keep the order you wrote them in: in D(B, C), B comes before C. Each class appears exactly once, so in the diamond A runs only once. object is always last. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 mroerror.py. In the exam super() does not mean \"my parent\". It means \"the next class in the MRO of the object I am working on\". Inside B, super().hello() went to C for a D object but to A for a B object. That is what lets every class in a diamond run exactly once. Lesson 9 of 13"],[17,"compose","Composition compared with inheritance","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 compose.py. Two ways to reuse code inheritance (is a) composition (has a) Example class Dog(Animal) self.engine = Engine(...) Link Fixed when the class is written Can be swapped while running Child gets Every parent method, wanted or not Only what you pass on Risk A change in the parent can break children More small methods that pass work on Use when The child really is a kind of the parent The object uses another object to do a job Bottom line: the well known rule is \"favour composition over inheritance\". Inherit for real \"is a\" families, compose for everything else. Lesson 10 of 13"],[17,"maths","The maths: areas, MROs and lookup cost","CircleExam favourite A = πr2 Circle(2): 3.14159 × 4 = 12.57. Heron for a triangleExam favourite s = (a + b + c) / 2, A = √(s(s − a)(s − b)(s − c)) Triangle(3, 4, 5): s = 6, A = √(6 × 3 × 2 × 1) = 6. Total area by polymorphism total = sum of shape.area() for every shape 12.57 + 12 + 6 = 30.57, one loop for every kind of shape. Length of an MRO len(C.__mro__) = number of distinct ancestors + 1 (C itself), object included Diamond D(B, C): D, B, C, A, object is 5. Lookup cost worst case one dict check per class in the MRO: O(len(MRO)) CPython caches the result per type, so repeated lookups are close to O(1). Worked example: the MRO of the diamond with C3 L[A] = [A, object]. L[B] = [B, A, object]. L[C] = [C, A, object]. L[D] = D + merge([B, A, object], [C, A, object], [B, C]). Take B (in no tail). Next head A is in the tail of [C, A, object], so skip it and take C. Now A is free, then object. L[D] = [D, B, C, A, object] Lesson 11 of 13"],[17,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths Write class Child(Parent): to reuse the parent, override a method by writing it again, and call super().__init__ in the child __init__. Use isinstance and issubclass, extend methods with super(), use polymorphism and duck typing, write abstract methods, and choose composition when \"is a\" does not fit. Explain multiple inheritance, mixins, the MRO and the C3 rules, why super() follows the MRO of the object, and why some class orders raise TypeError. C3 gives a linearisation that is monotonic (a class keeps the order of its parents in every child) and respects local precedence. super() is a proxy built from the type and the instance that searches the MRO after the given class. __init_subclass__ and metaclasses hook into class creation, and abc uses __subclasshook__ and register for virtual subclasses (Advanced 1.4). Beginner Make a child class, override a method and call super(). Intermediate Use isinstance, polymorphism, abstract methods, mixins and composition. Expert Work out an MRO with C3 by hand and explain cooperative super() in a diamond. Lesson 12 of 13"],[17,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","These design problems build classes that hold other objects: an iterator inside a wrapper, node objects inside a linked list or a trie, and queues and stacks inside counters. That is composition, the partner of inheritance. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 284 Peeking Iterator Medium Wrap an object and look one step ahead Keep the next value in an attribute. peek returns it, next returns it and fetches a new one. This is composition: the class holds another object. 341 Flatten Nested List Iterator Medium Flatten once with recursion Walk the nested list with a recursive helper and store every integer in a plain list. Then next and hasNext only move an index. 707 Design Linked List Medium Node objects and a dummy head Each node object holds a value and a link to the next node. A dummy node before the first one means adding at index 0 needs no special case. 208 Implement Trie (Prefix Tree) Medium A tree of node objects Each node has a dict of children, one per next letter, and a flag that marks the end of a word. 641 Design Circular Deque Medium Fixed list with a moving front Like the circular queue, but the front can also move back by one: (front − 1) % k. 933 Number of Recent Calls Easy A queue of times Add each new time to the back. Remove times from the front that are older than t − 3000. The length is the answer. 901 Online Stock Span Medium Stack of price and span pairs Keep a stack of [price, span]. While the top price is not bigger than today, pop it and add its span to today. 1352 Product of the Last K Numbers Medium Prefix products, reset at zero Keep running products. The product of the last k is last / products[−k − 1]. A zero clears the list, and asking past it gives 0. 2241 Design an ATM Machine Medium Greedy from the biggest note Store how many of each note there are. To withdraw, take as many big notes as possible, then smaller ones. If money is left over, refuse and change nothing. 1396 Design Underground System Medium Two dicts One dict remembers where and when each card checked in. Another adds up total time and trip count for each pair of stations. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 13 of 13"],[17,"recap","Chapter summary","class Child(Parent): gives Child every attribute and method of Parent. Every class inherits from object in the end. Writing a method again in the child overrides it. Python looks in the object, then the class, then the parents in MRO order. super().__init__(...) runs the parent setup. A child __init__ without it skips the parent attributes. super().m() extends a method. isinstance(obj, C) counts children, type(obj) is C does not. issubclass, __bases__ and __mro__ show the family. Polymorphism: one call, many types. Duck typing needs only the right method name, not a shared parent. Abstract methods: raise NotImplementedError, or abc.ABC with @abstractmethod which blocks making the object. Multiple inheritance uses the C3 MRO: children before parents, parents in written order, each class once, object last. Impossible orders raise TypeError. Prefer composition (has a) to inheritance (is a) unless the child really is a kind of the parent. Next: Step 7 is a full practice exam in the style of PCEP and PCAP, covering everything in the core part. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: a shape family with an abstract area, an Employee and Manager pair that uses super(), and a ReprMixin that any class can use.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[18,"","Exam practice: PCEP and PCAP style papers","Four practice papers, a bug hunt, the traps table, exam technique and a mock quiz. · PCEP · PCAP · exam practice · mock quiz · predict output · find the bug · traps · certification · revision Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Core Python›Step 7: Exam practice›Core exam practice Step 7 · Topic 1 of 1 · All levels Revise the whole core part in the shape of the Python Institute exams: four predict the output papers with real answers, a bug hunt, the traps in one table, exam technique, a mock quiz of 30 questions and timed problems. About 90 min10 lessons3 formulas10 worked problems30 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1The exams and how to use this chapter 2Paper A: fundamentals and control flow 3Paper B: data collections 4Paper C: functions and exceptions 5Paper D: objects, strings and modules 6Find the bug 7The traps in one table 8Exam technique and a study plan 9LeetCode review set 10Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 10"],[18,"map","The exams and how to use this chapter","Source: pythoninstitute.org pages for PCEP-30-02, PCAP-31-03 and their syllabi, plus the pages for the newer PCEP-30-03 and PCAP-31-04, read on 7 November 2026. This chapter teaches nothing new. It revises the whole core part in the shape of the two Python Institute exams, with four practice papers, a bug hunt, the traps examiners love, a mock quiz and timed problems. Every question was written for this course, and every answer below was printed by a real run of Python 3.14. PCEP-30-02 (entry) PCAP-31-03 (associate) Questions 30 40 Time 40 minutes, plus 5 for the agreement and tutorial 65 minutes, plus 10 for the agreement and tutorial Pass mark 70 % overall 70 % overall Question types Single and multiple choice, drag and drop, gap fill, sort, code fill, code insertion Single and multiple choice, plus interactive and scenario items Blocks and weights Fundamentals 18 %, control flow 29 %, data collections 25 %, functions and exceptions 28 % Modules and packages 12 %, exceptions 14 %, strings 18 %, object oriented programming 34 %, miscellaneous (comprehensions, lambdas, closures, files) 22 % Chapters here 1.1 to 4.3 and 5.1 3.1, 4.1 to 6.2 Common mistake The Python Institute has announced newer versions, PCEP-30-03 (35 questions, 50 minutes, 70 %) and PCAP-31-04 (42 questions, 65 minutes, 72 %), released on 31 October 2026. The skills tested are the same core Python, but check which version your voucher is for before you book. Paper Covers Lesson A Operators, numbers, loops, booleans, print 2 B Lists, tuples, slices, dicts, sets, strings 3 C Arguments, defaults, scope, closures, exceptions 4 D Classes, inheritance, strings, modules, generators 5 Remember Cover the answers, write down what you think each line prints, then open the answers. A wrong guess tells you exactly which chapter to reread. Lesson 2 of 10"],[18,"paper_a","Paper A: fundamentals and control flow","Paper A: what does each line printpython 1print(\"Q1\", -2 ** 2, 2 ** 3 ** 2)2print(\"Q2\", 7 // -2, -7 % 3, 7 % -3)3print(\"Q3\", 0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3, round(2.5), round(3.5))4print(\"Q4\", 1 < 2 < 3, 3 > 2 > 1 == 1)56x = 07while x < 10:8 x += 39else:10 print(\"Q5 done\", x)1112for i in range(3):13 if i == 1:14 break15else:16 print(\"no break\")17print(\"Q6 i is\", i)1819print(\"Q7\", int(\"1F\", 16), 0o17, 0b101, 1_000)20print(\"Q8\", bool(\"False\"), bool(\"\"), bool([0]), bool(0.0))21print(\"Q9\", \"ab\" * 3, \"5\" + \"5\", 5 + 5)22print(\"Q10\", end=\" \")23print(\"a\", \"b\", sep=\"-\", end=\"!\\n\") Open the answers for paper A Q Output Why (chapter) 1 -4 512 ** binds tighter than the minus sign, so -2 ** 2 is -(2 ** 2). ** groups from the right: 2 ** 9 (1.3) 2 -4 2 -2 // rounds down (towards minus infinity). The result of % takes the sign of the divisor (1.3) 3 False 2 4 0.1 and 0.2 are not exact in binary. round() goes to the even neighbour on a tie (1.2) 4 True True Chained comparisons mean 3 > 2 and 2 > 1 and 1 == 1 (1.3) 5 done 12 The loop ends normally when x reaches 12, so the else block runs (2.2) 6 i is 1 break skips the else block. The loop variable keeps its last value (2.2) 7 31 15 5 1000 int(text, 16) reads hexadecimal. 0o is octal, 0b is binary, _ is only for reading (1.2) 8 True False True False Any non empty string is true, even \"False\". [0] is a non empty list (1.2) 9 ababab 55 10 * repeats a string, + joins strings, + adds numbers (1.2) 10 a-b! sep goes between the values, end replaces the new line (1.4) Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 paper_a.py. Lesson 3 of 10"],[18,"paper_b","Paper B: data collections","Paper B: what does each line printpython 1a = [1, 2, 3]2b = a3b += [4]4print(\"Q1\", a)56t = (1, 2, 3)7u = t8u += (4,)9print(\"Q2\", t, u)1011grid = [[0] * 3] * 312grid[0][0] = 113print(\"Q3\", grid)1415nums = [1, 2, 3, 4]16print(\"Q4\", nums[::-2], nums[-1:0:-1], nums[10:])1718d = {\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2}19d[\"a\"] = 320print(\"Q5\", list(d), d.get(\"z\", 0), len(d))2122s = \"python\"23print(\"Q6\", s[1:4], s[-3:], s.find(\"z\"), s.upper()[0])2425one = (1,)26two = (1)27print(\"Q7\", type(one).__name__, type(two).__name__)28print(\"Q8\", {1, 2, 2, 3} | {3, 4}, {1, 2} & {2, 3})2930x = [3, 1, 2]31y = x.sort()32print(\"Q9\", x, y)3334keys = {True: \"a\", 1: \"b\", 1.0: \"c\"}35print(\"Q10\", len(keys), keys) Open the answers for paper B Q Output Why (chapter) 1 [1, 2, 3, 4] b is the same list as a, and += changes a list in place (3.2) 2 (1, 2, 3) (1, 2, 3, 4) A tuple cannot change, so += builds a new tuple and only u points to it (3.3) 3 [[1, 0, 0], [1, 0, 0], [1, 0, 0]] [row] * 3 repeats the SAME inner list three times. Use a comprehension (3.2) 4 [4, 2] [4, 3, 2] [] A negative step walks backwards. The stop index is never included. Slices never raise IndexError (3.2) 5 ['a', 'b'] 0 2 Changing a value keeps the key in its place. get gives the default for a missing key (3.4) 6 yth hon -1 P Slices stop before the end index. find gives -1 when nothing is found (3.1) 7 tuple int The comma makes a tuple, not the brackets (3.3) 8 {1, 2, 3, 4} {2} | is union, & is intersection, and a set keeps one copy of each value (3.3) 9 [1, 2, 3] None sort changes the list and returns None. sorted would return a new list (3.2) 10 1 {True: 'c'} True == 1 == 1.0 with equal hashes, so they are one key. The first key stays, the last value wins (3.4) Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 paper_b.py. Lesson 4 of 10"],[18,"paper_c","Paper C: functions and exceptions","Paper C: what does each line printpython 1def f(a, b=2, *args, c, **kw):2 return a, b, args, c, kw3print(\"Q1\", f(1, 3, 4, 5, c=6, d=7))45def add(item, items=[]):6 items.append(item)7 return items8add(1)9print(\"Q2\", add(2))1011x = 1012def g():13 print(x)14 x = 515try:16 g()17except NameError as err:18 print(\"Q3\", type(err).__name__)1920funcs = [lambda: i for i in range(3)]21print(\"Q4\", [fn() for fn in funcs])2223def h():24 try:25 return \"try\"26 finally:27 print(\"Q5 finally runs\")28print(\"Q5\", h())2930try:31 int(\"x\")32except ValueError:33 print(\"Q6 V\")34except Exception:35 print(\"Q6 E\")36else:37 print(\"Q6 else\")38finally:39 print(\"Q6 fin\")4041def nothing():42 pass43print(\"Q7\", nothing())4445try:46 raise KeyError(\"k\")47except LookupError as err:48 print(\"Q8\", type(err).__name__, err)4950print(\"Q9\", list(map(lambda n: n * 2, filter(lambda n: n % 2, range(6)))))5152def outer():53 n = 054 def inner():55 nonlocal n56 n += 157 return n58 return inner59counter = outer()60counter()61print(\"Q10\", counter()) Open the answers for paper C Q Output Why (chapter) 1 (1, 3, (4, 5), 6, {'d': 7}) Positional values fill a and b, the rest go to *args. c is keyword only. Unknown keywords go to **kw (4.1) 2 [1, 2] The default list is made once, when def runs, and shared by every call (4.1) 3 UnboundLocalError x = 5 anywhere in g makes x local in all of g, so print(x) reads it too early (4.2) 4 [2, 2, 2] Each lambda looks up i when it is called, and by then i is 2 (4.3) 5 finally runs, then try finally runs before the function really returns (5.1) 6 V, then fin The first matching except wins. else runs only when no error happened. finally always runs (5.1) 7 None A function without return gives None (4.1) 8 KeyError 'k' KeyError is a child of LookupError. str of a KeyError shows the key with quotes (5.1) 9 [2, 6, 10] filter keeps 1, 3 and 5 (n % 2 is 1, which is true), map doubles them (4.3) 10 2 nonlocal lets inner change n in outer. The closure keeps n between calls (4.2) Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 paper_c.py. Lesson 5 of 10"],[18,"paper_d","Paper D: objects, strings and modules","Paper D: what does each line printpython 1class A:2 x = 13a = A()4a.x = 25print(\"Q1\", A.x, a.x, \"x\" in a.__dict__)67class B:8 def __init__(self):9 self.__secret = 110b = B()11print(\"Q2\", hasattr(b, \"__secret\"), hasattr(b, \"_B__secret\"))1213class P:14 def f(self):15 return \"P\"16class Q(P):17 def f(self):18 return \"Q\" + super().f()19print(\"Q3\", Q().f(), isinstance(Q(), P), issubclass(P, Q))2021print(\"Q4\", \"abc\" < \"abd\", \"Z\" < \"a\", \"10\" < \"9\", ord(\"A\"), chr(97))22print(\"Q5\", \"a,b,,c\".split(\",\"), \" x y \".split(), \"-\".join(\"abc\"))23print(\"Q6\", \"Hello\".center(9, \"*\"), \"abc def\".title(), \"ab\".isalpha(), \"12\".isdigit())2425import math26print(\"Q7\", math.floor(-2.5), math.ceil(-2.5), math.trunc(-2.5), math.sqrt(16))27print(\"Q8\", __name__)2829class C:30 def __str__(self):31 return \"C!\"32print(\"Q9\", C(), str(C()) + \"?\", f\"{C()}\")3334gen = (n * n for n in range(4))35print(\"Q10\", next(gen), sum(gen))36print(\"Q11\", [n for n in range(10) if n % 3 == 0], {n: n * n for n in range(3)})3738def make(k):39 return lambda v: v * k40triple = make(3)41print(\"Q12\", triple(4), make(2)(\"ab\"))4243class Base:44 def __init__(self):45 self.name = \"base\"46class Child(Base):47 def __init__(self):48 pass49try:50 print(Child().name)51except AttributeError as err:52 print(\"Q13\", type(err).__name__) Open the answers for paper D Q Output Why (chapter) 1 1 2 True Assigning through an instance adds an instance attribute and leaves the class alone (6.1) 2 False True __secret is name mangled to _B__secret (6.1) 3 QP True False super() calls the parent version. A child is an instance of its parent, not the other way round (6.2) 4 True True True 65 a Strings compare code point by code point. \"Z\" is 90, \"a\" is 97, and \"1\" comes before \"9\" (3.1) 5 ['a', 'b', '', 'c'] ['x', 'y'] a-b-c split(\",\") keeps empty parts. split() with no argument splits on runs of spaces and drops the ends (3.1) 6 **Hello** Abc Def True True center pads both sides. title capitalises each word (3.1) 7 -3 -2 -2 4.0 floor goes down, ceil goes up, trunc cuts towards zero. sqrt always returns a float (5.2) 8 __main__ A file run directly gets the name __main__. Imported, it would get its module name (5.2) 9 C! C!? C! print, str and f-strings all use __str__ (6.1) 10 0 14 next takes 0. sum uses what is left: 1 + 4 + 9 (4.3) 11 [0, 3, 6, 9] {0: 0, 1: 1, 2: 4} A list comprehension with if, and a dict comprehension (4.3) 12 12 abab make returns a closure that remembers k. * works on strings too (4.2) 13 AttributeError Child.__init__ never calls super().__init__(), so name is never set (6.2) Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 paper_d.py. Lesson 6 of 10"],[18,"bugs","Find the bug","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 bugs.py. Six bugs examiners and interviewers love Bug 1. Removing items from a list while a for loop walks it skips the item after each removal, so 4 survives. Build a new list instead (3.2). Bug 2. Strings never change. upper() returns a new string, which was thrown away. Assign the result (3.1). Bug 3. The function computes w * h but never returns it, so the caller gets None (4.1). Bug 4. except Exception catches ValueError first, so the narrow handler can never run. Put specific handlers before general ones (5.1). Bug 5. input() returns text, and \"7\" * 2 is \"77\". Convert with int() first (1.4). Bug 6. list = [...] hides the built in list, so list(\"abc\") calls a list object and fails. Never use built in names as variables (4.2). Lesson 7 of 10"],[18,"traps","The traps in one table","Trap Looks like Really Chapter Power and minus -2 ** 2 is 4 -4 1.3 Floor division -7 // 2 is -3 -4 1.3 Rounding round(2.5) is 3 2 (round half to even) 1.2 Integer from text int(\"3.5\") is 3 ValueError (use int(float(\"3.5\"))) 1.2 Boolean of text bool(\"False\") is False True 1.2 is and == a is b means equal is means the same object 1.3 Copy b = a copies a list b is another name for the same list 3.2 Nested repeat [[0] * 3] * 3 makes 3 rows It makes 3 names for one row 3.2 One item tuple (5) is a tuple It is the int 5, write (5,) 3.3 Empty set {} is an empty set It is an empty dict, write set() 3.3 sort x = x.sort() sorts x becomes None 3.2 Default argument def f(x=[]) gets a new list each call One list for all calls 4.1 Scope Reading a global inside a function that also assigns it works UnboundLocalError 4.2 Lambda in a loop Each lambda keeps its own i They all see the last i 4.3 else on loops Runs when the loop runs Runs when the loop was not stopped by break 2.2 finally return skips finally finally always runs 5.1 Class attribute obj.x = 1 changes the class It adds an instance attribute 6.1 Forgotten super() Parent attributes exist anyway AttributeError 6.2 Lesson 8 of 10"],[18,"plan","Exam technique and a study plan","Time per questionExam favourite minutes × 60 / questions PCEP: 40 × 60 / 30 = 80 seconds. PCAP: 65 × 60 / 40 = 97.5 seconds. Questions to pass (if every item counts the same)Exam favourite correct needed = ceil(pass mark × questions) PCEP: 0.7 × 30 = 21. PCAP: 0.7 × 40 = 28. Points by block block share of the exam = block weight Object oriented programming is 34 % of PCAP, more than any other block, so it deserves the most practice. A four week plan Week 1: chapters 1.1 to 2.2. Do paper A without looking, then reread the chapter behind every wrong answer. Week 2: chapters 3.1 to 3.4 and paper B. Practise slicing and dict methods until you can predict them instantly. Week 3: chapters 4.1 to 5.3 and paper C. Write small programs for args, scope, closures and exceptions. Week 4: chapters 6.1 and 6.2 and paper D, then the mock quiz in the Quiz tab with a timer. Aim for 85 % to leave a safety margin. In the exam In the exam: read the whole snippet before the options, trace it line by line on scratch paper, watch for indentation and for a missing comma or bracket that turns the answer into \"the code raises an error\". Mark hard questions and come back, every question is worth the same. One idea, four depths PCEP level: predict the output of short programs with operators, loops, lists, dicts, strings, functions and simple try and except. PCAP level: add modules and packages, the exception tree, string methods, classes, inheritance, comprehensions, lambdas, closures and files. PCPP level (Advanced part): decorators, the data model, generators, testing, concurrency, design patterns and the standard library in depth. Expert: explain why each trap happens from the language reference (evaluation order, the data model, the C3 MRO, name binding rules) and spot them in code review before they reach production. Lesson 9 of 10"],[18,"leetcode","LeetCode review set","Ten problems that mix everything in the core part: loops, two pointers, sets, dicts, strings and comprehensions. Try to solve each in under 25 minutes, as you would in a coding interview. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 11 Container With Most Water Medium Two pointers Start at both ends. The area is width times the shorter wall. Move the shorter wall inwards, because only that can make the area bigger. 12 Integer to Roman Medium Greedy table Walk a table from 1000 (M) down to 1 (I), including 900, 400, 90, 40, 9 and 4. Take each value as many times as it fits. 15 3Sum Medium Sort, then two pointers Sort the list. For each first number, look for two more with two pointers. Skip equal neighbours so no triple repeats. 17 Letter Combinations of a Phone Number Medium Grow the list digit by digit Start with [\"\"] and, for every digit, add each of its letters to every string made so far. 36 Valid Sudoku Medium Sets for rows, columns and boxes Keep a set for each row, column and 3 by 3 box. A digit seen twice in any of them makes the board invalid. The box number is (r // 3) * 3 + c // 3. 38 Count and Say Medium Describe the previous term Read the previous string in runs of equal digits and write the run length followed by the digit. 56 Merge Intervals Medium Sort by start Sort the intervals by start. If the next one starts before the last merged one ends, stretch the end, otherwise start a new interval. 67 Add Binary Easy Add from the right with a carry Walk both strings from the end, add the digits and the carry, write total % 2 and carry total // 2. 238 Product of Array Except Self Medium Products from the left and from the right answer[i] is the product of everything left of i times everything right of i. Two passes, no division. 448 Find All Numbers Disappeared in an Array Easy Set difference The numbers should be 1 to n. Put the list in a set and keep the numbers from 1 to n that are not in it. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 10 of 10"],[18,"recap","Chapter summary","PCEP-30-02: 30 questions, 40 minutes, 70 %. PCAP-31-03: 40 questions, 65 minutes, 70 %. Newer versions started on 31 October 2026. Operators: ** before unary minus and right to left, // rounds down, % takes the sign of the divisor, round goes to even on ties. Loops: else runs only without break. Never change a list while looping over it. Collections: assignment makes aliases, [[0] * 3] * 3 shares one row, (1,) is a tuple, sort returns None, True and 1 are the same dict key. Functions: mutable defaults are shared, assigning a name makes it local, lambdas in loops bind late, nonlocal changes an enclosing name. Exceptions: first matching except wins, specific before general, else when no error, finally always. Objects: instance assignment never changes the class, __x is mangled, call super().__init__(). Technique: about 80 to 98 seconds per question, trace on paper, aim for 85 % in practice. Next: The Advanced part goes on to the PCPP level: the data model, decorators, generators, testing, concurrency and the standard library. .py Practice files for this chapterThree exam style tasks: a marks report from a dict, a Caesar cipher that keeps case and punctuation, and a settings parser that raises ValueError on bad lines.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[19,"","Special methods and operator overloading","special methods, operator overloading, reflected operators, total_ordering, the hash contract, container protocol, __call__, __format__. · special methods · magic methods · dunder · operator overloading · __add__ · __radd__ · NotImplemented · total_ordering · __hash__ · __getitem__ · __call__ · PCPP Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Advanced Python›Step 1: Advanced object oriented programming›Special methods Step 1 · Topic 1 of 5 · Advanced Make your own classes work with +, ==, len, in, for, print and f-strings. Learn how Python turns operators into special method calls, reflected and in place operators, comparisons and total_ordering, the hash contract, the container protocol, callable objects and attribute hooks. About 75 min12 lessons1 animation6 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Special methods: make your class feel built in 2The main special methods 3Arithmetic, reflected and unary operators 4Comparisons and total_ordering 5The hash contract 6Emulating containers 7Callable objects, text formats and in place operators 8Attribute hooks: __getattr__ and __setattr__ 9The maths: fractions and vectors with operators 10From beginner to expert 11LeetCode practice 12Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 12"],[19,"idea","Special methods: make your class feel built in","Source: Python 3.14 data model reference section 3.3 \"Special method names\", the functools and fractions documentation, and PCPP-32-101 block 1. What it is Special methods (also called magic methods or dunder methods, for \"double underscore\") are methods with names like __add__ or __len__ that Python calls for you when you use an operator or a built in function on your object. Why we need it Built in types work with +, ==, len, in, for and print. Your own classes do not, unless you tell Python how. Without special methods you would write v.add(w) and v.equals(w), which is harder to read and does not work with sorted, sum, sets or dicts. How it works Python turns every operator into a method call on the type of the object: a + b becomes type(a).__add__(a, b), len(x) becomes type(x).__len__(x). Write those methods and your objects join in with every part of Python that uses them. Changing the meaning of an operator for your class is called operator overloading. Real world scenario: a universal socket A phone charger works in any socket because it has the standard plug shape. Python operators are the sockets. A special method is your class giving itself the standard plug, so + and len and for can use it like any built in type. Operators become special method callsPick an expression. Each step shows which special (dunder) method Python looks for, what it finds, and what it does when a method is missing or says NotImplemented. Step 0 of 4 Expression Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this a + b calls type(a).__add__(a, b). The method returns a new object. When the left side returns NotImplemented, Python tries the reflected method of the right side. in and for fall back to older protocols when the newer special method is missing. Python import mathclassVector:def __init__(self, x, y): self.x = x self.y = ydef __repr__(self):return f\"Vector({self.x}, {self.y})\"def __add__(self, other): # v + wreturnVector(self.x + other.x, self.y + other.y)def __sub__(self, other): # v - wreturnVector(self.x - other.x, self.y - other.y)def __mul__(self, k): # v * 3returnVector(self.x * k, self.y * k)def __rmul__(self, k): # 3 * vreturn self * kdef __neg__(self): # -vreturnVector(-self.x, -self.y)def __abs__(self): # abs(v)return math.hypot(self.x, self.y)def __eq__(self, other): # v == wreturn isinstance(other, Vector) and (self.x, self.y) == (other.x, other.y)def __bool__(self): # if v:return self.x != 0or self.y != 0a = Vector(3, 4)b = Vector(1, 2)print(a + b, a - b, a * 2, 2 * a, -a)print(abs(a), a == Vector(3, 4), bool(Vector(0, 0)))print(a.__add__(b)) # what a + b really runs Output Vector(4, 6) Vector(2, 2) Vector(6, 8) Vector(6, 8) Vector(-3, -4) 5.0 True False Vector(4, 6) Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 vector.py. Lesson 2 of 12"],[19,"table","The main special methods","You write Python calls Group repr(x), str(x), format(x, s) __repr__, __str__, __format__ Text x + y, x - y, x * y, x / y, x // y, x % y, x ** y, x @ y __add__, __sub__, __mul__, __truediv__, __floordiv__, __mod__, __pow__, __matmul__ Arithmetic y + x when y does not know x __radd__ and the other r methods on x Reflected x += y __iadd__ (or __add__ then assign) In place -x, +x, abs(x), ~x __neg__, __pos__, __abs__, __invert__ Unary x == y, x != y, x < y, x <= y, x > y, x >= y __eq__, __ne__, __lt__, __le__, __gt__, __ge__ Comparison hash(x), set and dict keys __hash__ Hashing bool(x), if x __bool__, else __len__ Truth len(x), x[i], x[i] = v, del x[i], v in x __len__, __getitem__, __setitem__, __delitem__, __contains__ Container for v in x, iter(x), next(it) __iter__, __next__ Iteration (2.1) x(...) __call__ Callable with x: __enter__, __exit__ Context manager (2.3) x.name when it is missing, x.name = v __getattr__, __setattr__ Attributes int(x), float(x), round(x) __int__, __float__, __round__ Conversion In the exam Special methods are looked up on the class, not on the object. Setting obj.__len__ = ... on one object does nothing for len(obj). Also, never invent your own __names__: they are reserved for Python. Lesson 3 of 12"],[19,"arith","Arithmetic, reflected and unary operators","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 vector.py. What Python does for a + b Call type(a).__add__(a, b). If that method is missing or returns NotImplemented, call type(b).__radd__(b, a). If that is missing or returns NotImplemented too, raise TypeError: unsupported operand type(s). Special case: if b is an instance of a child class of type(a) that overrides __radd__, Python tries b first. In the exam Return the special value NotImplemented (not raise NotImplementedError) when your method cannot handle the other type. That gives the other object a chance to answer. Raising stops Python from trying. Lesson 4 of 12"],[19,"compare","Comparisons and total_ordering","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 compare.py. Writing comparisons all six methods by hand @total_ordering with __eq__ and __lt__ Lines of code Many, easy to make inconsistent Two methods Speed Fastest Slightly slower for the generated ones Needed for sorted, min, max __lt__ is enough __lt__ is enough Good for Hot loops Almost everything else Bottom line: write __eq__ and __lt__ and let functools.total_ordering write the rest. Or use a dataclass with order=True (1.3). Remember If you write __eq__ but not __ne__, Python gives != for free by inverting __eq__. Lesson 5 of 12"],[19,"hashing","The hash contract","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 hashing.py. The rules for __eq__ and __hash__ If a == b, then hash(a) must equal hash(b). Otherwise sets and dicts will hold duplicates or lose items. Writing __eq__ sets __hash__ to None, so the class becomes unhashable until you write __hash__ too. Build the hash from the same fields that __eq__ compares, for example hash((self.x, self.y)). Only hash fields that never change. Changing a field after the object is in a set or dict hides it, as the last line shows. Lesson 6 of 12"],[19,"container","Emulating containers","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 container.py. Remember A class with only __len__ and __getitem__ already works with for, in, reversed and slicing. That is why Python calls this the sequence protocol. Inheriting from collections.abc.Sequence (or MutableSequence) also gives you index, count and more for free. Going deeper: Slices are objects p[1:3] passes a slice object slice(1, 3, None) to __getitem__. Its start, stop and step attributes, and its indices(len) method, let you handle any slice correctly. That is how NumPy supports a[1:3, ::2]. Lesson 7 of 12"],[19,"call","Callable objects, text formats and in place operators","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 call.py. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 format.py. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 inplace.py. In the exam x += y first tries x.__iadd__(y) and then ASSIGNS the result to x. That is why __iadd__ must return self. A tuple has no __iadd__, so += makes a new tuple and other names still see the old one. Lesson 8 of 12"],[19,"attrs","Attribute hooks: __getattr__ and __setattr__","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 attrs.py. Three attribute hooks __getattr__ __getattribute__ __setattr__ Called when Normal lookup failed EVERY attribute read EVERY attribute assignment Typical use Defaults, proxies, lazy loading Rare: logging every access Validation, read only objects Danger Small Infinite recursion if you read self.x inside it Infinite recursion if you write self.x inside it Bottom line: prefer properties (1.3) for one or two checked attributes, and keep these hooks for whole groups of names. Lesson 9 of 12"],[19,"maths","The maths: fractions and vectors with operators","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 fraction.py. Adding fractionsExam favourite a/b + c/d = (ad + cb) / bd 1/2 + 1/3 = (3 + 2) / 6 = 5/6. Lowest termsExam favourite divide top and bottom by g = gcd(top, bottom) 2/4: gcd(2, 4) = 2, so 1/2. Euclid gcd(a, b) = gcd(b, a mod b), gcd(a, 0) = a gcd(48, 18) = gcd(18, 12) = gcd(12, 6) = gcd(6, 0) = 6. Vector length |v| = √(x2 + y2) abs(Vector(3, 4)) = 5.0, using math.hypot. Scaling k(x, y) = (kx, ky) 2 * Vector(3, 4) = Vector(6, 8), through __rmul__. Dot product with @ a @ b = axbx + ayby Python 3.5 added @ (__matmul__) for exactly this kind of product. Worked example: 2/3 × 9/4 Multiply tops and bottoms: 18 / 12. gcd(18, 12) = 6. 18/12 = 3/2, which the program printed Remember 0.1 + 0.2 is 0.30000000000000004 with floats, but Fraction(1, 10) + Fraction(2, 10) is exactly 3/10. Use Fraction or Decimal when exact answers matter, such as money. Lesson 10 of 12"],[19,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths Write __repr__, __str__ and __eq__ so objects print nicely and compare by value. Overload arithmetic and comparison operators, use total_ordering, keep __eq__ and __hash__ consistent, and make containers with __len__, __getitem__ and __contains__. Return NotImplemented correctly, handle reflected and in place operators, accept slice objects, write __call__ and __format__, and use __getattr__ and __setattr__ without recursion. Special methods are looked up on the type through slots (tp_as_number, tp_as_sequence in CPython), which is why setting them on an instance has no effect and why they are fast. Implicit lookups skip __getattribute__. The numbers and collections.abc hierarchies define which methods make a type a Number, a Sequence or a Mapping. Beginner Write __repr__, __str__ and __eq__. Intermediate Overload operators, comparisons, hashing and the container protocol. Expert Explain NotImplemented, reflected operands, slot lookup and the abc protocols. Lesson 11 of 12"],[19,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","Two problems are pure fraction arithmetic, like the Frac class in this chapter. The others ask for classes that behave like little built in types: streams, iterators, queues and heaps with a clean method interface. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 592 Fraction Addition and Subtraction Medium Parse, add, reduce with gcd Read each signed fraction, add it to a running a/b with (ad + cb) / bd, then divide by the gcd. 166 Fraction to Recurring Decimal Medium Long division, remember remainders Do long division. When a remainder comes back, the digits since its first appearance repeat, so wrap them in brackets. 1656 Design an Ordered Stream Easy A list and a pointer Store each value at its id. After each insert, return the run of filled slots starting at the pointer and move the pointer past them. 1845 Seat Reservation Manager Medium A min heap of free seats Keep the free seats in a heap so the smallest one is always on top. 2336 Smallest Number in Infinite Set Medium A counter plus a set of returned numbers Numbers from next_new upwards are all present. Numbers added back below it go into a heap and a set. 1381 Design a Stack With Increment Operation Medium A list with a size limit push only when there is room. increment adds val to the bottom k items. 1286 Iterator for Combination Medium Make the combinations up front itertools.combinations gives the combinations in order. Store them and walk through them with an index. 900 RLE Iterator Medium Use up counts Walk the pairs (count, value). Take n from the current count, moving to the next pair when one runs out. 1670 Design Front Middle Back Queue Medium A plain list A list with insert and pop at the right index is simple and fast enough for the limits. The middle index is len // 2 for push and (len - 1) // 2 for pop. 2349 Design a Number Container System Medium Dict plus a lazy heap per number Remember the number at each index. For each number keep a heap of indexes and drop stale ones when you look at the top. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 12 of 12"],[19,"recap","Chapter summary","Operators and built in functions call special methods on the TYPE: a + b is type(a).__add__(a, b), len(x) is type(x).__len__(x). Return NotImplemented when you cannot handle the other operand. Python then tries the reflected method (__radd__ and friends) and finally raises TypeError. __repr__ for developers, __str__ for users, __format__ for format specs in f-strings. Comparisons: __eq__ and __lt__ plus functools.total_ordering. != comes free from __eq__. If a == b then hash(a) == hash(b). Writing __eq__ removes __hash__. Hash only fields that never change. Containers: __len__, __getitem__ (which may get a slice), __setitem__, __contains__, __iter__. __call__ makes objects callable. __iadd__ must return self. __getattr__ runs only when lookup fails, __setattr__ runs on every assignment. Next: 1.2 moves on to extended function arguments, closures and decorators. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: a Money class with safe addition and ordering, a 2 by 2 Matrix with @ and ==, and a Sentence container.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[20,"","Extended arguments, closures and decorators","parameters, closures, decorators, wraps, decorator factories, stacking, class decorators, cache. · decorator · closure · *args · **kwargs · positional only · keyword only · functools.wraps · lru_cache · cache · decorator factory · class decorator · PCPP Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Advanced Python›Step 1: Advanced object oriented programming›Decorators Step 1 · Topic 2 of 5 · Advanced Add behaviour to functions without touching their code. Learn positional only and keyword only parameters, *args and **kwargs for forwarding, functions as objects and closures, decorators with functools.wraps, decorators that keep state, decorators with arguments, stacking, class decorators and caching with @cache. About 75 min12 lessons1 animation5 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Decorators: add behaviour without changing the code 2Extended argument syntax 3Functions are objects, closures remember 4functools.wraps: keep the name and docstring 5Decorators that keep state 6Decorators with arguments 7Stacking decorators 8Built in decorators and caching 9The maths: memoisation and backoff 10From beginner to expert 11LeetCode practice 12Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 12"],[20,"idea","Decorators: add behaviour without changing the code","Source: Python 3.14 reference section 8.7 (function definitions), PEP 318, PEP 570, PEP 3102, the functools documentation and PCPP-32-101 block 1. What it is A decorator is a function that takes a function (or a class) and returns a replacement for it, usually a wrapper that does something extra before or after calling the original. Writing @decorator above a def applies it. Why we need it Many functions need the same extra jobs: logging, timing, checking arguments, retrying, caching, checking a login. Copying that code into every function is repetitive and easy to get wrong. A decorator writes the job once and adds it to any function with one line. How it works Functions are objects, so they can be passed to other functions and returned from them. @shout above def add runs add = shout(add) right after the def. shout returns a wrapper, a closure that remembers the original function and calls it inside. Real world scenario: gift wrapping A gift wrapped in paper is still the same gift, but now it comes with a ribbon and a card. Anyone who receives it opens the paper first, then reaches the gift. A decorator wraps a function the same way: callers reach the wrapper first, and the wrapper hands them on to the original. How a decorator wraps a functionPick a program. The Names panel shows which function object each name points to and what each closure remembers. The Call stack shows who is running. Step 0 of 9 Program Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this @shout above def add means add = shout(add). The name ends up pointing to the wrapper. Stacked decorators are applied from the bottom up, and the top one runs first at call time. @repeat(3) calls repeat(3) first, and what it returns is the real decorator. Python def shout(func):def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):print(\"calling\", func.__name__) result = func(*args, **kwargs)return resultreturn wrapper@shout# the same as add = shout(add)def add(a, b):return a + bprint(add(2, 3))print(add.__name__) # the name is now wrapper: see the next program Output calling add 5 wrapper Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 simple.py. Lesson 2 of 12"],[20,"args","Extended argument syntax","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 args.py. Part of def Meaning Example call name, / Positional only: cannot be passed by keyword (PEP 570) describe(\"Asha\", ...) age Positional or keyword describe(\"Asha\", 30) or age=30 *hobbies Collects extra positional values into a tuple \"chess\", \"music\" city=\"Pune\" after * Keyword only: must be passed by name (PEP 3102) city=\"Delhi\" **extra Collects extra keyword values into a dict lang=\"hi\" f(*seq, **mapping) At a CALL, * and ** unpack instead of collecting describe(*values, **options) In the exam The order in a def is always: positional only, /, normal, *args (or a bare *), keyword only, **kwargs. *args, **kwargs in a wrapper accept any call and pass it on unchanged, which is why every general decorator uses them. Notice the fourth line of output: name=\"Asha\" did not fill name. Because describe has **extra, it went into extra, so Python reports name as missing. Lesson 3 of 12"],[20,"first","Functions are objects, closures remember","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 first.py. Remember A closure is a function that remembers the variables of the function it was made in, even after that function has returned. greet remembers greeting. That memory, stored in __closure__, is what lets a wrapper keep hold of the original function. Lesson 4 of 12"],[20,"wraps","functools.wraps: keep the name and docstring","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 wraps.py. Common mistake Without @wraps every decorated function is called \"wrapper\" and loses its docstring, which breaks help(), debuggers, logging and frameworks that look up functions by name (Flask uses the name for its routes). Always put @wraps(func) on the wrapper. Lesson 5 of 12"],[20,"count","Decorators that keep state","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 count.py. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 classdeco.py. Where a decorator keeps its state attribute on the wrapper function a class with __call__ a closure variable with nonlocal Example wrapper.calls self.calls nonlocal calls Readable from outside Yes: greet.calls Yes: square.calls No Works on methods Yes Needs extra work (__get__) Yes Best for Simple counters and flags Complex state Private state Bottom line: a function decorator with wraps is the usual choice. A class decorator object shines when the state is big. Lesson 6 of 12"],[20,"factory","Decorators with arguments","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 factory.py. Reading @repeat(3) repeat(3) runs first and returns decorate, which remembers times = 3. decorate(wave) runs and returns wrapper, which remembers times and func. The name wave now points to wrapper. Every call wave(...) runs wrapper(...), which calls the original three times. Common mistake A decorator with arguments has three nested functions: the factory (settings), the decorator (the function) and the wrapper (the call). Forgetting the brackets, @retry instead of @retry(3), passes the function as attempts and fails in a confusing way. Lesson 7 of 12"],[20,"stack","Stacking decorators","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 stack.py. In the exam Decorators are applied from the bottom up (nearest the def first) and run from the top down at call time. @a then @b above def f means f = a(b(f)). Lesson 8 of 12"],[20,"cache","Built in decorators and caching","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 cache.py. Decorator Module What it does Chapter @functools.cache, @lru_cache(maxsize) functools Remembers results for arguments seen before 1.2 @functools.wraps(func) functools Copies name and docstring onto a wrapper 1.2 @functools.total_ordering functools Fills in comparison methods 1.1 @property, @x.setter built in Methods that look like attributes 1.3 @classmethod, @staticmethod built in Methods that get the class, or nothing 1.3 @abc.abstractmethod abc Methods every child must write 1.3 @dataclasses.dataclass dataclasses Writes __init__, __repr__ and __eq__ for you 1.3 @contextlib.contextmanager contextlib Turns a generator into a with statement helper 2.3 @app.route(\"/\") Flask Registers a function as a web page Web 3.1 Common mistake Only cache pure functions, whose result depends only on the arguments. Arguments must be hashable. @cache never forgets, so for many different arguments use lru_cache with a maxsize. Lesson 9 of 12"],[20,"maths","The maths: memoisation and backoff","Calls of plain recursive FibonacciExam favourite C(n) = 1 + C(n − 1) + C(n − 2), C(0) = C(1) = 1, so C(n) = 2F(n + 1) − 1 fib(25): 2 × 121,393 − 1 = 242,785 calls, as the program counted. Calls with @cacheExam favourite each n from 0 to N is computed once: N + 1 misses fib(25): 26 misses and 23 hits. Growth F(n) ≈ φn / √5, φ = (1 + √5) / 2 ≈ 1.618 Plain recursion is exponential, O(φn). The cached version is linear, O(n). Exponential backoff wait before retry k = base × 2k, total for n retries = base(2n − 1) base 1 s, 4 retries: 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 = 15 s. LRU hit rate hit rate = hits / (hits + misses) square with maxsize 2 on 1, 2, 1, 3, 2: 1 hit, 4 misses, 20 %. Worked example: why fib(25) with @cache has 23 hits fib(25) misses and calls fib(24), which misses and calls fib(23), and so on down to fib(1): 25 misses on the way down. Back in fib(2), the second call fib(0) has never been seen: one more miss, 26 in total. Back in fib(3) up to fib(25), each second call fib(n − 2) finds its answer already cached: 23 hits. cache_info() reports hits=23, misses=26 Lesson 10 of 12"],[20,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths Know that @decorator above a def means f = decorator(f), and use built in decorators such as @cache. Write decorators with *args and **kwargs and @wraps, decorators that keep state, decorators with arguments, and know the order of stacked decorators. Write class decorators and classes used as decorators, decorate methods safely, combine positional only and keyword only parameters, and use caching with a clear idea of when results may go stale. Decorators are just expressions evaluated at definition time (any expression since PEP 614). To decorate methods with a class based decorator, implement the descriptor protocol (__get__) so it binds self. Type checkers keep the signature of a wrapper with ParamSpec and Concatenate (PEP 612). Beginner Use @decorators and write a simple wrapper with @wraps. Intermediate Write decorators with arguments, with state, and stack them correctly. Expert Write class decorators, descriptor aware decorators and typed decorators with ParamSpec. Lesson 11 of 12"],[20,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","Every solution here is plain recursion plus one line, @cache. That is memoisation by decorator: the recursion states the idea clearly, and the decorator removes the repeated work. Compare the call counts in the maths lesson. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 746 Min Cost Climbing Stairs Easy Top down with @cache best(i) is the cheapest way to stand past the top from step i: cost[i] + min(best(i + 1), best(i + 2)). Start from step 0 or 1. 62 Unique Paths Medium Paths from a cell, cached paths(r, c) = paths(r + 1, c) + paths(r, c + 1), and 1 on the last row or column. 64 Minimum Path Sum Medium Cheapest from a cell, cached cost(r, c) is the grid value plus the cheaper of moving down or right. 91 Decode Ways Medium Ways from a position, cached From position i, take one digit (if not 0) or two digits (if 10 to 26) and add the ways for the rest. 139 Word Break Medium Can the rest be split, cached From position i, try every dictionary word that matches there and ask the same question for the rest. 198 House Robber Medium Rob or skip, cached best(i) = max(rob house i and jump two, or skip to i + 1). 322 Coin Change Medium Fewest coins for an amount, cached fewest(a) = 1 + the smallest fewest(a - coin). Use infinity for impossible amounts. 343 Integer Break Medium Best split, cached For each first piece k, either keep the rest whole or break it further: max(k * (n - k), k * best(n - k)). 96 Unique Binary Search Trees Medium Catalan numbers, cached Choose each value as the root. The trees on the left and right are independent, so multiply their counts. 494 Target Sum Medium Ways from (index, total), cached At each number add it or subtract it. Count the ways that land exactly on target. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 12 of 12"],[20,"recap","Chapter summary","Parameters: positional only before /, keyword only after * or *args, *args is a tuple, **kwargs is a dict. At a call, * and ** unpack. Functions are objects: name them, store them, pass them, return them. A closure remembers the variables of the function that made it. @d above def f means f = d(f). A decorator usually returns a wrapper(*args, **kwargs) that calls the original. Always use @functools.wraps(func) on the wrapper to keep the name, docstring and __wrapped__. Decorators with arguments have three levels: factory(settings), decorator(func), wrapper(*args). Stacked decorators are applied bottom up and run top down: @a @b def f is a(b(f)). Decorators can keep state, take classes, or be classes with __call__. @cache and @lru_cache memoise pure functions. Next: 1.3 covers class design: class and static methods, properties, abstract classes, dataclasses and __slots__. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: a call counting decorator, a retry decorator with a number of attempts, and a decorator that checks arguments are positive.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[21,"","Class design: methods, properties, abstract classes and dataclasses","class and static methods, properties, cached_property, ABCs, dataclasses, __slots__, descriptors. · classmethod · staticmethod · property · setter · cached_property · abc · abstractmethod · dataclass · frozen · __slots__ · descriptor · PCPP Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Advanced Python›Step 1: Advanced object oriented programming›Class design Step 1 · Topic 3 of 5 · Advanced Say exactly what each part of a class is. Learn instance, class and static methods, alternative constructors, properties with setters and read only values, cached_property, abstract base classes, dataclasses with all their options, __slots__ and descriptors. About 80 min10 lessons1 animation6 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Designing classes well 2Instance, class and static methods 3Properties: attributes with rules 4Abstract base classes 5Dataclasses: classes for data 6__slots__ and descriptors 7The maths: conversions, ordering and memory 8From beginner to expert 9LeetCode practice 10Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 10"],[21,"idea","Designing classes well","Source: Python 3.14 documentation of classmethod, staticmethod, property, functools.cached_property, abc and dataclasses, the data model sections on __slots__ and descriptors, the Descriptor HowTo Guide, and PCPP-32-101 block 1. What it is Python gives you tools to say exactly what each part of a class is: methods that work on an object, on the class, or on neither, attributes that check their values (properties), classes that only describe a shape (abstract classes), and classes that are mostly data (dataclasses). Why we need it A class written with only plain methods and attributes lets callers store bad values, repeats boilerplate like __init__ and __repr__, and gives no clear contract for child classes. These tools fix each of those problems with one decorator. How it works @classmethod passes the class, @staticmethod passes nothing, @property turns attribute access into method calls, @abstractmethod blocks objects until children fill the gaps, and @dataclass writes __init__, __repr__ and __eq__ from type annotated fields. Method kinds and propertiesPick a program. Each step shows the call Python really makes, what it passes as the first argument, and what is stored in the object. Step 0 of 7 Program Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this self is the object, cls is the class, and a static method gets neither. A property turns attribute reads and writes into method calls, so checks always run. Python from datetime import dateclassPerson: count = 0# class attributedef __init__(self, name, born): self.name = name self.born = bornPerson.count += 1def age(self, today): # instance method: gets selfreturn today.year - self.born@classmethoddef from_text(cls, text): # class method: gets cls name, born = text.split(\",\")return cls(name.strip(), int(born)) # an alternative constructor@staticmethoddef is_adult(age): # static method: gets nothing extrareturn age >= 18classStudent(Person):passasha = Person(\"Asha\", 1996)ravi = Person.from_text(\"Ravi, 2010\")meera = Student.from_text(\"Meera, 2001\") # cls is Student heretoday = date(2026, 11, 7)print(asha.age(today), ravi.age(today), type(meera).__name__)print(Person.is_adult(ravi.age(today)), Person.count)print(type(Person.__dict__[\"from_text\"]).__name__, type(Person.__dict__[\"is_adult\"]).__name__) Output 30 16 Student False 3 classmethod staticmethod Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 kinds.py. Lesson 2 of 10"],[21,"kinds","Instance, class and static methods","Three kinds of method instance method @classmethod @staticmethod First argument self (the object) cls (the class) none Can read or change The object and its class The class only Only its arguments Called as obj.m() Cls.m() or obj.m() Cls.m() or obj.m() Typical use Almost everything Alternative constructors like from_text, dict.fromkeys, datetime.now A helper that belongs to the topic, like is_adult With inheritance self is the child object cls is the child class Nothing changes Bottom line: use a class method when you need the class (above all to build objects), a static method for a helper that needs neither, and an instance method otherwise. In the exam Student.from_text returned a Student because the method calls cls(...), not Person(...). That is why alternative constructors should be class methods. Lesson 3 of 10"],[21,"property","Properties: attributes with rules","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 property.py. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 cached.py. Three ways to give access to data a public attribute get_x() and set_x() methods @property Caller writes t.celsius = 5 t.set_celsius(5) t.celsius = 5 Checks values No Yes Yes Can add checks later without changing callers No No Yes Feels like Python Yes No (Java style) Yes Bottom line: start with plain attributes. When a rule appears, turn the attribute into a property: no calling code has to change. Common mistake Store the real value under another name (self._celsius). If the setter wrote self.celsius = value it would call itself forever and raise RecursionError. Lesson 4 of 10"],[21,"abc","Abstract base classes","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 abstract.py. Remember An abstract base class is a contract: \"every Payment can pay and has a name\". Python refuses to create an object until every abstract method is filled in, so a forgotten method fails at once instead of in front of a customer. collections.abc gives ready made contracts like Sized, Iterable, Sequence and Mapping. Going deeper: Virtual subclasses Payment.register(SomeClass) makes isinstance(SomeClass(), Payment) True without inheritance and without any checks. collections.abc uses __subclasshook__ instead, which is why Box counts as Sized just because it has __len__. Lesson 5 of 10"],[21,"dataclass","Dataclasses: classes for data","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 dataclass.py. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 dcmore.py. Option What it adds @dataclass __init__, __repr__ and __eq__ from the annotated fields field(default_factory=list) A new default object for every instance (a plain [] default is refused) frozen=True Assignment raises FrozenInstanceError, and __hash__ is generated order=True <, <=, >, >= comparing the fields as a tuple, in order field(init=False) and __post_init__ Computed fields and checks after __init__ kw_only=True Every field must be passed by name slots=True Generates __slots__ for less memory and faster access asdict, astuple, replace Convert to a dict or tuple, or copy with changes Ways to bundle data tuple namedtuple dict @dataclass Fields by name No Yes Yes (keys) Yes Can change No No Yes Yes (or frozen) Methods and properties No Some No Yes Type hints and defaults No Defaults only No Yes Best for Small fixed groups Light records JSON style data Most records in an app Bottom line: reach for @dataclass first when a class is mostly data. Lesson 6 of 10"],[21,"slots","__slots__ and descriptors","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 slots.py. Remember __slots__ removes the per object __dict__, so objects are smaller and adding a misspelt attribute fails at once. In Python 3.14 a normal object already keeps its attributes compactly until you touch __dict__, so the saving is smaller than older guides say: about a third in this run. Use slots for classes with very many objects. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 descriptor.py. In the exam A descriptor is an object with __get__, __set__ or __delete__, stored as a class attribute. property, classmethod, staticmethod and every normal method are descriptors. Write your own when the same rule (like \"must be positive\") applies to many attributes in many classes. Lesson 7 of 10"],[21,"maths","The maths: conversions, ordering and memory","Celsius to Fahrenheit (a computed property)Exam favourite F = C × 9 / 5 + 32 25 °C is 25 × 1.8 + 32 = 77 °F. The other way C = (F − 32) × 5 / 9 212 °F is 180 × 5 / 9 = 100 °C. Absolute zero C ≥ −273.15, so F ≥ −459.67 The setter rejects anything colder. order=True compares as tuplesExam favourite (a1, a2, ...) < (b1, b2, ...) at the first position where they differ Version(3, 9, 7) < Version(3, 14, 0) because 9 < 14, even though \"9\" > \"14\" as text. Memory for n objects total ≈ n × bytes per object 1,000,000 objects at 40 bytes less each save about 40 MB. Age from a birth year (ignoring the birthday) age = current year − birth year 2026 − 1996 = 30. Worked example: sorting Version(3, 14), Version(3, 9, 7), Version(3, 10, 2) As tuples: (3, 14, 0), (3, 9, 7), (3, 10, 2). All start with 3, so compare the second numbers: 14, 9, 10. 9 < 10 < 14, so the order is 3.9.7, 3.10.2, 3.14.0 Lesson 8 of 10"],[21,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths Use @classmethod for alternative constructors, @staticmethod for helpers, and @property for computed or checked attributes. Write setters and read only properties, abstract base classes with @abstractmethod, and dataclasses with defaults, default_factory, frozen and order. Use cached_property, __post_init__, kw_only and slots, collections.abc contracts, and write descriptors with __set_name__ for rules shared by many attributes. Attribute lookup: data descriptors on the class (property, slots) beat the instance __dict__, which beats non data descriptors (functions, cached_property) and plain class attributes. That order explains why a property cannot be hidden by vars(obj) while cached_property can store its answer in the instance dict. dataclass builds its methods with exec at class creation. Beginner Use class methods, static methods and properties. Intermediate Design with abstract classes and dataclasses. Expert Explain the descriptor protocol and the attribute lookup order. Lesson 9 of 10"],[21,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","Each problem is a small class design exercise: choose the attributes in __init__ so that every method stays simple and fast. Several of them pair a dict with a heap or with bisect, a pattern you will meet again in Advanced 4.2. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1146 Snapshot Array Medium Per index history plus binary search For each index keep a list of (snap id, value) changes. get looks for the last change at or before the snap id with bisect. 981 Time Based Key-Value Store Medium Lists of times per key, bisect Timestamps arrive in increasing order, so each key keeps a sorted list. get finds the last time not after the asked time. 1797 Design Authentication Manager Medium A dict of expiry times Store when each token expires. renew only works while the token is alive. Count the tokens that expire after the current time. 1500 Design a File Sharing System Medium Reuse the smallest free id with a heap Hand out ids from a heap of freed ids first, then new ids. Each user has a set of chunks. 1244 Design A Leaderboard Medium A dict of scores addScore adds to a player, top sorts the scores and adds the K biggest, reset sets a player back to 0. 2526 Find Consecutive Integers from a Data Stream Medium Count the current run Keep how many values in a row have equalled value. consec returns whether that run has reached k. 2296 Design a Text Editor Hard Two stacks around the cursor Keep the text left of the cursor and the text right of it (reversed) as two lists. Every operation moves characters between them. 1825 Finding MK Average Hard Keep the last m values, sort when asked A deque keeps the last m values. To answer, sort them, drop k from each end and average the middle. Simple, and fine for small m. 2034 Stock Price Fluctuation Medium Dict plus two lazy heaps Store the price at each time. Push into a max heap and a min heap, and skip heap tops whose price was later corrected. 1600 Throne Inheritance Medium A family tree and a preorder walk Keep each person's children in birth order and a set of the dead. The order of succession is a preorder walk that skips the dead. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 10 of 10"],[21,"recap","Chapter summary","Instance methods get self, @classmethod gets cls (use it for alternative constructors), @staticmethod gets nothing. @property turns reads and writes into method calls: computed values, checks in a setter, read only attributes. Store the real value under _name. @cached_property computes once and stores the answer in the instance dict. Abstract base classes (ABC with @abstractmethod) are contracts: objects cannot be made until every abstract method exists. @dataclass writes __init__, __repr__ and __eq__. Use field(default_factory=list), frozen, order, __post_init__, kw_only and slots as needed. __slots__ removes the instance dict to save memory and stop new attributes. Descriptors (__get__, __set__, __set_name__) are the machinery behind property and methods, and let one rule serve many attributes. Next: 1.4 covers exceptions in depth, copying objects, and saving objects with pickle and shelve. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: a BankAccount with a checked balance property and a from_text class method, a Shape abstract class with two children, and an Order dataclass.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[22,"","Exceptions in depth, copying and serialisation","exception hierarchies, chaining, tracebacks, notes, exception groups, warnings, shallow and deep copy, pickle and shelve. · exception chaining · __cause__ · __context__ · traceback · add_note · ExceptionGroup · except* · warnings · copy · deepcopy · pickle · shelve · PCPP Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Advanced Python›Step 1: Advanced object oriented programming›Exceptions, copy, pickle Step 1 · Topic 4 of 5 · Advanced Give errors their full story and keep data safe. Learn exception hierarchies, chaining with raise from, __cause__ and __context__, tracebacks, add_note, exception groups with except*, warnings, shallow and deep copies, custom copying, and saving objects with pickle and shelve. About 80 min10 lessons1 animation4 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Exceptions, copies and saved objects 2Chaining: __cause__ and __context__ 3Tracebacks, notes and exception groups 4Warnings and assertions 5Shallow and deep copies 6pickle and shelve: save objects 7The maths: retries, copies and sizes 8From beginner to expert 9LeetCode practice 10Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 10"],[22,"idea","Exceptions, copies and saved objects","Source: Python 3.14 tutorial chapter 8, PEP 3134, PEP 654, PEP 678, and the traceback, warnings, copy, pickle and shelve documentation, plus PCPP-32-101 block 1. What it is This chapter goes deeper into three things every real program needs: exceptions that carry their full story (their own types, causes, notes and groups), copies of objects that do or do not share their insides, and serialisation, turning objects into bytes so they can be saved and loaded later with pickle and shelve. Why we need it In a big program a bare \"ValueError\" says little. You need to know what went wrong, where, and what caused it. Copying is a classic source of bugs: changing a \"copy\" that secretly shares data with the original. And objects vanish when the program ends unless you save them. How it works Custom exception classes form a family you can catch at any level. raise ... from ... links a new error to its cause. add_note and ExceptionGroup add context and report many errors at once. copy.copy and copy.deepcopy decide how deep a copy goes. pickle.dumps turns almost any object into bytes and pickle.loads rebuilds it. Python classShopError(Exception):\"\"\"The parent of every error this shop raises.\"\"\"classOutOfStock(ShopError):def __init__(self, item, wanted, left): super().__init__(f\"{item}: wanted {wanted}, only {left} left\") self.item = item self.left = leftclassPaymentFailed(ShopError):passdef buy(item, wanted): stock = {\"pen\": 3, \"book\": 0}if stock[item] < wanted:raiseOutOfStock(item, wanted, stock[item])return\"bought\"for item, n in [(\"pen\", 2), (\"pen\", 5), (\"book\", 1)]:try:print(buy(item, n))exceptOutOfStockas err:print(\"OutOfStock:\", err, \"| left:\", err.left, \"| args:\", err.args)exceptShopError:print(\"some other shop problem\") Output bought OutOfStock: pen: wanted 5, only 3 left | left: 3 | args: ('pen: wanted 5, only 3 left',) OutOfStock: book: wanted 1, only 0 left | left: 0 | args: ('book: wanted 1, only 0 left',) Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 hierarchy.py. Remember Give your package one base exception (ShopError) and children for each problem. Callers can then catch one specific error, or everything from your package with one except. Lesson 2 of 10"],[22,"chain","Chaining: __cause__ and __context__","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 chain.py. You write Attribute set on the new error Traceback says raise New() from old __cause__ = old \"The above exception was the direct cause of the following exception\" raise New() inside an except block __context__ = the error being handled \"During handling of the above exception, another exception occurred\" raise New() from None __cause__ = None, __suppress_context__ = True Only the new error In the exam Use raise ... from err when you turn a low level error into a meaningful one, so the original is never lost. Use from None only when the original adds nothing for the reader. Lesson 3 of 10"],[22,"trace","Tracebacks, notes and exception groups","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 tracebacks.py. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 notes.py. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 groups.py. Reporting problems raise one exception add_note ExceptionGroup and except* Since Always Python 3.11 Python 3.11 Reports The first problem One problem with extra context Many problems at once Caught with except except (notes in err.__notes__) except*, each matching part separately Typical use Most errors Adding the row, file or user while an error passes up Form checks, many parallel tasks (asyncio TaskGroup) Bottom line: raise a single exception normally, add notes as it travels up, and group errors when several independent things fail together. Lesson 4 of 10"],[22,"warn","Warnings and assertions","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 warn.py. Signals that something is wrong exception warning assert Stops the program Yes, unless caught No, by default it prints once Yes (AssertionError) For Errors the caller must handle Old or risky use that still works Checks of your own logic while developing Turned off by Never warnings filters python -O removes every assert Example raise ValueError warnings.warn(..., DeprecationWarning) assert total >= 0 Bottom line: never use assert to check user input, because -O deletes it. Lesson 5 of 10"],[22,"copying","Shallow and deep copies","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 copying.py. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 customcopy.py. Three ways to \"copy\" b = a shallow copy deep copy Written as b = a a.copy(), a[:], list(a), dict(a), copy.copy(a) copy.deepcopy(a) New outer object No Yes Yes New inner objects No No, shared Yes, all the way down Cost Free O(length) O(every object inside) Safe to change inner items No No Yes Bottom line: a shallow copy is enough when the items are immutable (numbers, strings, tuples of those). Use deepcopy for nested mutable data. Lesson 6 of 10"],[22,"pickling","pickle and shelve: save objects","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 pickling.py. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 shelf.py. Common mistake Never unpickle data from a source you do not trust. pickle.loads can run any code the data asks for, so a crafted file can take over your computer. For data from users or the internet use JSON. Saving data json pickle shelve Format Text, readable by any language Python only bytes A pickle per key in a small database Saves dict, list, str, numbers, bool, None Almost any Python object Almost any object, by string key Safe with untrusted data Yes NO NO Good for APIs, config, data exchange Caches, saving program state, sending objects between Python processes A simple persistent dict Bottom line: use JSON to talk to the world, pickle for trusted Python to Python data, and shelve when you want a dict that survives restarts. In the exam A shelf returns copies. db[\"ravi\"][\"tags\"].append(\"work\") changes a copy that is thrown away. Read the value, change it and assign it back (or open with writeback=True). Lesson 7 of 10"],[22,"maths","The maths: retries, copies and sizes","Chance that k independent attempts all failExam favourite P(all fail) = pk, P(success) = 1 − pk A call fails 20 % of the time. With 3 attempts: 1 − 0.23 = 99.2 % success. Objects created by copies of an n by m nested listExam favourite shallow: 1 list. deep: 1 + n lists For [[1, 2], [3, 4]]: shallow makes 1 new list, deep makes 3. The ints are immutable and shared either way. Attempts needed for a target k ≥ log(1 − target) / log(p) p = 0.2, target 99.99 %: k ≥ log(0.0001) / log(0.2) ≈ 5.7, so 6 attempts. Expected attempts until success E = 1 / (1 − p) p = 0.2: 1.25 attempts on average. Worked example: success chance with 3 attempts at p = 0.2 All three fail: 0.2 × 0.2 × 0.2 = 0.008. So at least one succeeds with 1 − 0.008. 0.992, which is 99.2 % Lesson 8 of 10"],[22,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths Write your own exception class, and know that b = a does not copy while a.copy() and copy.deepcopy(a) do. Build an exception hierarchy, chain with raise from, read tracebacks, choose shallow or deep copies, and save objects with pickle and shelve. Use add_note, ExceptionGroup and except*, warnings filters, __copy__ and __deepcopy__, and know the pickle security risk and when JSON is the right tool. pickle stores a program for a small stack machine: classes are saved by import path and rebuilt with __reduce__, __getstate__ and __setstate__, which is both why pickle can rebuild almost anything and why loading it can run arbitrary code. Protocol 5 (PEP 574) adds out of band buffers for large data. Beginner Raise and catch your own exceptions, copy lists safely. Intermediate Chain exceptions, choose the right copy, pickle and shelve objects. Expert Use exception groups and notes, customise copying and pickling, explain the pickle VM. Lesson 9 of 10"],[22,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","Two problems are deep copies done by hand with a memo dict, which is exactly how copy.deepcopy avoids loops. Five are serialisation: turning trees and URLs into strings and back, like pickle. Two rebuild nested structure from a flat string with a stack, which is what any loader does. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 138 Copy List with Random Pointer Medium Deep copy with a memo dict Map every old node to its new copy in a dict, exactly like copy.deepcopy does with its memo. Then set next and random from the map. 133 Clone Graph Medium DFS with a memo, like deepcopy Copy a node, remember the copy, then copy its neighbours. The memo stops the copy from looping forever around cycles. 297 Serialize and Deserialize Binary Tree Hard Preorder with markers Write the tree in preorder with # for an empty child. Read it back with an iterator, building the left subtree before the right. This is pickling by hand. 449 Serialize and Deserialize BST Medium Preorder values and bounds A BST is fixed by its preorder alone. Rebuild it by giving each value a range (low, high) it must fit in. 535 Encode and Decode TinyURL Medium A counter and two dicts Give each new long URL the next number as its short code, and remember both directions. 606 Construct String from Binary Tree Easy Preorder with brackets Write the value, then the left child in brackets, then the right child in brackets. Keep () for a missing left child only when a right child exists. 331 Verify Preorder Serialization of a Binary Tree Medium Count open slots Start with one slot. Every token fills a slot, and a number opens two more. The string is valid if slots never go below zero and end at zero. 652 Find Duplicate Subtrees Medium Serialise every subtree Turn each subtree into a string (its own little pickle). Count the strings and report a root the second time its string appears. 394 Decode String Medium A stack of unfinished parts Read the text once. A number before [ says how many times to repeat. On [ save the text so far and the count on a stack. On ] repeat the inner text and glue it to the saved text. This is how a loader rebuilds nested data from a flat string. 1106 Parsing A Boolean Expression Hard A stack of values and operators Push every character except commas. On ) pop the values back to the (, take the operator in front of it, and push the single result. Nested expressions are solved from the inside out. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 10 of 10"],[22,"recap","Chapter summary","Make one base exception per package and a child per problem. Pass a clear message to super().__init__ and store useful fields. raise New() from err sets __cause__. Raising inside except sets __context__. from None hides the original. err.__traceback__ and the traceback module show where an error happened. add_note adds context. ExceptionGroup with except* reports many errors. Warnings flag old or risky use without stopping. assert checks your own logic and disappears with python -O. b = a shares everything, a shallow copy shares the inner objects, copy.deepcopy shares nothing and handles loops with a memo. pickle.dumps and loads turn objects into bytes and back. Never unpickle untrusted data. shelve is a dict on disk that returns copies. Next: 1.5 finishes Step 1 with metaprogramming: type(), metaclasses, __init_subclass__ and class decorators. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: an exception hierarchy with chaining, a safe deep copy of a nested config, and saving and loading a high score table with pickle.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[23,"","Metaprogramming: type, class hooks and metaclasses","classes as objects, type(), introspection, __init_subclass__, class decorators, metaclasses, creation order. · metaclass · type · metaprogramming · getattr · setattr · introspection · __init_subclass__ · class decorator · __new__ · __call__ · __prepare__ · singleton · PCPP Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Advanced Python›Step 1: Advanced object oriented programming›Metaclasses Step 1 · Topic 5 of 5 · Advanced Write code that builds and checks classes. Learn that classes are objects, how type() makes classes, introspection with getattr and friends, __init_subclass__, class decorators, metaclasses with __new__ and __call__, and the exact order in which Python creates a class. About 75 min10 lessons1 animation6 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Classes are objects too 2Building classes with type() 3Introspection: looking inside while the program runs 4__init_subclass__ and class decorators 5Metaclasses 6The full order of class creation 7The maths: counting classes, lookups and steps 8From beginner to expert 9LeetCode practice 10Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 10"],[23,"idea","Classes are objects too","Source: Python 3.14 data model chapter 3.3.3 (customizing class creation), the documentation of type, getattr, setattr, vars, dir and inspect, PEP 3115, PEP 3129, PEP 487, and PCPP-32-101 block 1. What it is Metaprogramming means writing code that makes or changes other code, mostly classes, while the program runs. In Python a class is an ordinary object. You can store it in a variable, pass it to a function, make one with type(), look inside it, and run your own code at the moment it is created. A metaclass is the class of a class: the thing that builds class objects. Why we need it Some rules apply to many classes at once: \"every plugin must register itself\", \"every model needs a table name\", \"every command must have a run method\". Writing that rule by hand in each class repeats code and is easy to forget. Metaprogramming puts the rule in one place and applies it to every class automatically. How it works When Python runs a class statement it collects the class body into a dict and then calls the metaclass (normally type) with the name, the base classes and that dict. Hooks let you join in: __init_subclass__ on a parent class, a class decorator after the class is built, or a custom metaclass that controls the whole process. How a class statement becomes a classEach step shows what Python does now, the namespace dict the class body fills, the class object once it exists, and the registry. Step 0 of 13 Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this Watch steps 6 to 9: type.__new__, __init_subclass__, Meta.__init__ and the decorator each get their own moment. Python classDog: sound = \"woof\"def speak(self):return self.soundrex = Dog()print(type(rex).__name__, type(Dog).__name__, type(type).__name__)print(isinstance(Dog, type), isinstance(rex, object), isinstance(type, object))animals = {\"dog\": Dog} # a class can live in a dictmake = animals[\"dog\"]print(make().speak())def build(cls, times): # and can be passed to a functionreturn [cls() for i inrange(times)]print(len(build(Dog, 3)), Dog.__name__, Dog.__bases__)print(sorted(k for k in vars(Dog) ifnot k.startswith(\"__\"))) Output Dog type type True True True woof 3 Dog (<class 'object'>,) ['sound', 'speak'] Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 objects.py. In the exam type has two jobs. With one argument, type(x) tells you the class of x. With three arguments, type(name, bases, namespace) builds a new class. And type(type) is type: type is its own class, which is where the chain ends. Lesson 2 of 10"],[23,"dynamic","Building classes with type()","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 maketype.py. Remember A class statement and a type() call give exactly the same kind of class. Python itself turns every class statement into a call like type(\"Cat\", (object,), namespace). You only need type() directly when the name or the fields are known only while the program runs, for example from a config file. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 classbody.py. Common mistake The class body runs once, at the moment Python reaches the class statement, not each time you make an object. Names it creates (tax, price, total) end up in the namespace dict, which becomes the class __dict__. That is why a class body can hold any normal code, even print. Ways to make a class class statement type(name, bases, dict) collections.namedtuple or dataclasses.make_dataclass Readable Best Hard for big classes Good Name and fields known only at run time No Yes Yes Methods Written normally Functions in the dict Generated for you, add more by hand Typical use Almost always Factories, plugins, tests Record types built from data Bottom line: write a class statement whenever you can. Use type() or make_dataclass when the shape of the class comes from data. Lesson 3 of 10"],[23,"introspect","Introspection: looking inside while the program runs","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 introspect.py. Tool What it gives you getattr(obj, \"name\", default) The attribute with that name, or the default instead of AttributeError setattr(obj, \"name\", value), delattr(obj, \"name\") Set or remove an attribute when the name is in a string hasattr(obj, \"name\") True if getattr would work callable(x) True if x can be called: functions, methods, classes, objects with __call__ vars(obj) or obj.__dict__ The attributes stored in the object itself dir(obj) Every name you can reach from obj, including inherited ones, sorted obj.__class__, C.__name__, C.__bases__, C.__mro__ The class of an object, its name, its parents and its lookup order C.__subclasses__() The direct children that exist right now inspect.signature(f) The parameters of a function, with defaults Choosing a method by its name if and elif chain getattr(obj, name) a dict of name to function Code to add a new action A new branch every time Only the new method One new dict entry Speed Checks branches one by one One lookup One lookup Safety with user input Safe, only listed names Can reach any attribute, so check the name first Safe, only listed names Bottom line: use getattr for names you control (like LeetCode style command lists) and a dict for names that come from users. Lesson 4 of 10"],[23,"hooks","__init_subclass__ and class decorators","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 initsub.py. In the exam __init_subclass__ (PEP 487, Python 3.6) runs on the PARENT every time a child class is created. It is a class method automatically. Keyword arguments in the class line, like name=\"csv\", are passed to it. Always call super().__init_subclass__(**kwargs) so other parents in a multiple inheritance chain get their turn. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 classdeco.py. Remember A class decorator is a function that takes a finished class and returns a class, usually the same one after changing it. @dataclass, @functools.total_ordering and @enum.unique are class decorators from the standard library. Stacked decorators run from the bottom up, so auto_repr ran before route. Three ways to run code when a class is made class decorator __init_subclass__ metaclass Applies to Only the decorated class Every child (and grandchild) of the parent Every class it builds, and their children When it runs After the class exists While the class is being built Before, during and after building, and on every call of the class Can change the namespace before the class exists No No Yes, in __new__ or __prepare__ Can change how objects are made Only by replacing methods Only by replacing methods Yes, with __call__ Mixes with other metaclasses Always Always Can clash (metaclass conflict) How hard to read Easy Easy Hard Bottom line: reach for a class decorator or __init_subclass__ first. They cover most real needs. Use a metaclass only when you must control the namespace or object creation. Lesson 5 of 10"],[23,"metaclass","Metaclasses","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 metaclass.py. In the exam A metaclass is a subclass of type. Its __new__(mcs, name, bases, namespace) gets everything the class statement collected and must return the new class, normally from super().__new__. Children of Job use CheckMeta too, because Python uses the metaclass of the base class when none is given. The check fires when the class is DEFINED, long before anyone uses it. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 singleton.py. Common mistake Calling a class, Config(...), really calls type(Config).__call__. The normal type.__call__ runs __new__ and then __init__. Overriding __call__ in a metaclass changes what calling the class does: here it returns the saved object. Notice that the second call did not even run __init__, so mode stayed \"prod\". Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 stdmeta.py. Remember You already use metaclasses: ABC is built by ABCMeta (that is how abstract methods block objects) and every Enum by EnumType (that is why len(Colour) and for c in Colour work on the class itself). A class whose bases have two unrelated metaclasses cannot be made, because Python cannot pick one metaclass that is a child of both. Ways to allow only one object a module level object a metaclass __call__ __new__ that returns a saved object Code needed One line: config = Config() A metaclass class A few lines in the class Easy to understand Yes No Medium __init__ runs again on each call Not called again No Yes, a common surprise Easy to test and replace Yes Hard Hard Bottom line: in Python a module is already created only once, so a module level object is the usual answer. Know the metaclass version for exams and old code. Going deeper: Tim Peters on metaclasses \"Metaclasses are deeper magic than 99% of users should ever worry about. If you wonder whether you need them, you don't.\" Most frameworks that once needed a metaclass (Django models, SQLAlchemy declarative classes) can now be written with __init_subclass__ and __set_name__. Lesson 6 of 10"],[23,"order","The full order of class creation","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 order.py. What Python does for a class statement, in order Pick the metaclass: the one given with metaclass=, otherwise the metaclass of the bases (the most derived one), otherwise type. Call Meta.__prepare__(name, bases) to get the namespace. type gives a plain dict. Run the class body inside that namespace. It stores __module__, __qualname__ and every name the body defines. Call Meta(name, bases, namespace). That runs Meta.__new__, which calls type.__new__ to build the class object. Inside type.__new__: call __set_name__ on every attribute that has it, then __init_subclass__ on the parent. Run Meta.__init__ on the new class. Apply class decorators from the bottom up. Store the result under the class name. Later, calling the class runs Meta.__call__, which runs __new__ and __init__. Going deeper: Going further: __prepare__, __mro_entries__ and types.new_class __prepare__ may return any mapping, for example one that refuses duplicate names or records the order of definitions. __mro_entries__ lets an object that is not a class appear in a base list (typing.Generic uses it). types.new_class(name, bases, kwds, exec_body) is the safe way to build a class with a metaclass and keywords from code, because it follows every step above exactly like a class statement. Lesson 7 of 10"],[23,"maths","The maths: counting classes, lookups and steps","Classes made by a factory classes = n1 × n2 × ... × nk make_record for 3 colours and 4 sizes builds 3 × 4 = 12 classes. Classes in a full hierarchy below one baseExam favourite b + b2 + ... + bd = b(bd − 1) / (b − 1) With b = 3 children each and d = 2 levels: 3 + 9 = 12 subclasses, each one seen by __init_subclass__. Average checks in an if and elif chainExam favourite average = (n + 1) / 2 for n equally likely names A chain of 9 actions needs 5 checks on average. A dict or getattr needs about 1 lookup. Worst case attribute lookup dicts checked ≤ 1 + len(C.__mro__) The instance dict, then each class in the MRO. For Kitten, Cat, object: at most 1 + 3 = 4. Objects made by a singleton objects = 1 for any n ≥ 1 calls, against n for a normal class 50 calls of Config() make 1 object. Steps from an object up to type type(rex) = Dog, type(Dog) = Meta, type(Meta) = type, type(type) = type Three steps reach type, and then the chain repeats itself. Worked example: how many registry entries does Plugin collect? Plugin has 3 direct children: b = 3. Each child has 3 children of its own: d = 2 levels. Level 1 gives 3 classes and level 2 gives 3 × 3 = 9. 3 + 9 = 3(32 − 1) / (3 − 1) = 12 entries Lesson 8 of 10"],[23,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths Know that classes are objects: type(x), isinstance, store and pass classes, and read __name__, __bases__ and __dict__. Build classes with type(name, bases, dict), use getattr, setattr, hasattr and vars to work with names in strings, and write class decorators and __init_subclass__ registries. Write metaclasses with __new__ and __call__, explain inherited metaclasses and metaclass conflicts, and recognise ABCMeta and EnumType in the standard library. Explain the whole class creation protocol: metaclass selection, __prepare__, the body, __new__, __set_name__, __init_subclass__, __init__, decorators. object is an instance of type and type is a subclass of object, a loop CPython sets up by hand at start up. Know when __mro_entries__ and types.new_class are the right tools, and why most designs should not need a metaclass at all. Beginner Treat classes as objects and look inside them. Intermediate Make classes from data and react when classes are created. Expert Control class creation completely, and choose the lightest tool. Lesson 9 of 10"],[23,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","Every problem here is a \"design\" problem, and LeetCode tests them with metaprogramming: it reads a list of method names, makes the object, and calls each method by its name with getattr. The downloadable file includes a small play() helper that does exactly that, so you can run the official examples as they appear on the site. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 211 Design Add and Search Words Data Structure Medium A trie of dicts, search with recursion Store each word letter by letter in nested dicts. A dot must try every child, so search with a small recursive helper. 380 Insert Delete GetRandom O(1) Medium A list plus a dict of positions The list allows a random pick. The dict maps each value to its index. To remove, move the last value into the hole and pop. 2502 Design Memory Allocator Medium Scan a list of owners Keep one owner id per memory unit (0 means free). allocate looks for the first run of free units that is long enough. 2353 Design a Food Rating System Medium A heap per cuisine with lazy deletion Push (-rating, name) into the heap of the cuisine. When asked, pop entries whose rating is out of date before reading the top. 2671 Frequency Tracker Medium Two counters Count each number, and also count how many numbers have each frequency. Then hasFrequency is one dict lookup. 1993 Operations on Tree Medium Parents list, children lists, a lock dict lock and unlock check one node. upgrade checks that no ancestor is locked and at least one descendant is, then unlocks every descendant. 1357 Apply Discount Every n Orders Medium A counter and a price dict Count customers. Every n-th bill is multiplied by (100 − discount) / 100. 1476 Subrectangle Queries Medium Keep a list of updates Instead of painting every cell, remember each update. getValue checks the updates from newest to oldest and falls back to the original grid. 2166 Design Bitset Medium A list of bits plus a flipped flag flip only toggles a flag, so it is O(1). Keep a count of ones and fix it up when flip happens. 2424 Longest Uploaded Prefix Medium A set and a pointer that only moves forward Remember uploaded videos in a set. The prefix pointer moves right while the next video is there. Each video is passed once in total. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 10 of 10"],[23,"recap","Chapter summary","Classes are objects. type(x) gives the class of x, and the class of a class is its metaclass, normally type. type(name, bases, namespace) builds a class without a class statement. Use it, or make_dataclass, when the class shape comes from data. A class body runs once and fills a namespace dict that becomes the class __dict__. getattr, setattr, hasattr, vars, dir and inspect let code look at and change objects by name while it runs. __init_subclass__ runs on the parent for every new child (great for registries). A class decorator gets the finished class. A metaclass is a subclass of type. __new__ controls building the class, __call__ controls making objects. Children inherit the metaclass. Order: pick metaclass, __prepare__, body, Meta.__new__, __set_name__, __init_subclass__, Meta.__init__, decorators, bind the name. Next: Step 2 starts with iterators and generators, the protocol behind every for loop. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: a plugin registry with __init_subclass__, a class factory built with type(), and a metaclass that demands docstrings and lists public methods.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[24,"","Iterators and generators","iterator protocol, iterator classes, yield, pipelines, yield from, send, throw, close, lazy built ins. · iterator · iterable · generator · yield · next · StopIteration · yield from · send · pipeline · lazy · PCAP Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Advanced Python›Step 2: Iterators, generators and context managers›Generators Step 2 · Topic 1 of 3 · Advanced Make values only when they are needed. Learn the iterator protocol behind every for loop, iterable and iterator classes, generator functions with yield, endless generators, generator pipelines, yield from, send, throw and close, and the lazy tools already built into Python. About 75 min10 lessons1 animation6 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Iterators and generators: one value at a time 2Writing iterators as classes 3Generator functions and yield 4Generator pipelines 5yield from, send, throw and close 6Lazy tools you already use 7The maths: memory, early stopping and counting 8From beginner to expert 9LeetCode practice 10Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 10"],[24,"idea","Iterators and generators: one value at a time","Source: Python 3.14 glossary (iterable, iterator, generator), the iterator types and yield expressions documentation, inspect.getgeneratorstate, PEP 255, PEP 342, PEP 380, PCAP-31-03 block 5 and PCPP-32-101. What it is An iterable is anything you can loop over: a list, a string, a dict, a file, a range. An iterator is the object that walks through it and hands out the next value each time you call next(). A generator is the easiest way to write your own iterator: a normal function that uses yield instead of return. Why we need it Building a full list before you use it costs memory and time. A million lines from a log file, an endless stream of sensor readings or every possible password cannot all sit in memory. Often you also only need the first few results. Iterators make values only when asked, so memory stays small and work stops as soon as you stop asking. How it works Every for loop calls iter() once to get an iterator and then next() again and again until StopIteration is raised. A generator function returns a generator object. Each next() runs its body until the next yield, hands that value out and pauses, keeping all local variables. The next call continues from exactly that spot. A generator pauses at yieldThe caller on the right asks for values with next(). Each step shows which line runs, the state of the generator, its local variable n and the value it hands over. Step 0 of 11 Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this Compare steps 4 and 5: after a pause the generator goes on from the line after yield, with n still in memory. Python nums = [10, 20, 30]it = iter(nums) # ask the list for an iteratorprint(type(it).__name__)print(next(it), next(it), next(it))try: next(it) # nothing is leftexceptStopIteration:print(\"StopIteration: the iterator is used up\")# what a for loop really does with \"hi\"it = iter(\"hi\")whileTrue:try: ch = next(it)exceptStopIteration:breakprint(\"got\", ch)print(iter(nums) is iter(nums), iter(it) is it)print(next(iter([]), \"empty\")) # a default instead of StopIteration Output list_iterator 10 20 30 StopIteration: the iterator is used up got h got i False True empty Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 protocol.py. In the exam iter(nums) gave a NEW iterator each time, so the first answer was False. An iterator returns itself from iter(), so the second was True. That is the whole difference: an iterable can make many iterators, an iterator is one walk that can be used once. Lesson 2 of 10"],[24,"classes","Writing iterators as classes","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 iterclass.py. Iterable or iterator iterable (like Shelf, list) iterator (like Countdown) Has __iter__ Yes, returns a NEW iterator Yes, returns itself Has __next__ No Yes Loop over it twice Works both times Second loop gets nothing Remembers a position No Yes Examples list, str, dict, range, set iter(list), zip, map, enumerate, files, generators Bottom line: give your own containers an __iter__ that returns a fresh iterator (or is a generator), so people can loop over them as often as they like. Common mistake Raising StopIteration ends the loop. Do not raise it inside a generator body: since Python 3.7 (PEP 479) it is turned into RuntimeError. In a generator, simply return. Lesson 3 of 10"],[24,"generators","Generator functions and yield","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 yieldfn.py. In the exam A function that contains yield anywhere is a generator function. Calling it runs nothing: the first line, print(\"start\"), only ran at the first next(). After the last value the generator is closed, and list() on a used generator gives an empty list. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 infinite.py. Three ways to produce a sequence return a list iterator class generator function Lines of code Few Many (__init__, __iter__, __next__) Few Memory for n items Grows with n Constant Constant Can be endless No Yes Yes Stops work early No, everything is made first Yes Yes Use twice, index, len Yes No No Bottom line: use a generator function whenever you want to produce values one by one. Return a list only when the caller really needs all the values at once or needs to index them. Lesson 4 of 10"],[24,"pipelines","Generator pipelines","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 pipeline.py. Remember Building the pipeline read nothing. Asking for 2 errors read only 4 lines and never touched \"WARN slow\" or the last line. Each value travels through all three stages before the next line is read. Unix pipes such as grep | cut | head work the same way. Processing a big file read everything into lists a generator pipeline Memory The whole file, maybe gigabytes One line at a time First result appears After the whole file is read Right away Stopping early Too late, the work is done No extra work Code One big loop with many ifs Small reusable stages Bottom line: chain small generators. Each stage does one job, can be tested alone and reused in another pipeline. Lesson 5 of 10"],[24,"delegation","yield from, send, throw and close","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 yieldfrom.py. Remember yield from other passes every value of other straight to whoever is asking, and when other finishes, its return value becomes the value of the yield from expression. It is the clean way to split a big generator into smaller ones, and it handles send and throw for you too. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 send.py. Method What it does next(g) Run to the next yield and return the value it yields g.send(v) Resume, and make the paused yield expression evaluate to v. The first call must be next(g) or g.send(None) g.throw(exc) Raise exc at the paused yield. The generator may catch it and go on g.close() Raise GeneratorExit at the paused yield so finally blocks run, then mark it closed Going deeper: Generators as coroutines PEP 342 turned yield into an expression so generators could receive values, which made them simple coroutines. Early asyncio was built on yield from. Modern code uses async def and await (Advanced 5.2), which are coroutines designed for this job, but they follow the same idea of pausing and resuming a function. Lesson 6 of 10"],[24,"lazy","Lazy tools you already use","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 lazy.py. Built in Lazy? Use more than once? range Yes, works out each value from start, step and index Yes, it is a sequence dict.keys(), values(), items() Yes, live views of the dict Yes zip, map, filter, enumerate, reversed Yes, iterators No open(...) file objects Yes, one line per next() No, unless you seek(0) iter(function, sentinel) Calls function until it returns sentinel No sorted, list, tuple, sum No, they use up the whole input They return a value Common mistake A common bug: saving map(...) or zip(...) in a variable, looping over it once for a check, and then finding it empty the second time. Wrap it in list(...) if you need it twice. Lesson 7 of 10"],[24,"maths","The maths: memory, early stopping and counting","Memory of a list against a generatorExam favourite list: about 8n bytes of pointers plus the items, generator: a constant (about 200 bytes) 100,000 squares: the list needs about 800 KB of pointers, the generator stays near 200 bytes. Work done by a lazy searchExam favourite items read = position of the first match (k), not n Looking for the first even square above 50 in 1, 2, 3, ... reads 8 numbers and stops (8 × 8 = 64). Running average after k values averagek = (x1 + x2 + ... + xk) / k After 10, 20 and 60: (10 + 20 + 60) / 3 = 30. Number of next() calls to see StopIteration values + 1 countdown(4) gives 4 values, and the 5th call raises StopIteration. Subsets and combinations (LeetCode 77 and 90) subsets = 2n, combinations of k from n = n! / (k! (n − k)!) From 5 numbers choose 2: 5! / (2! × 3!) = 10. A pipeline of s stages over n items time grows with n × s, memory with s only 106 lines through 4 stages: 4 × 106 stage steps, but only 4 paused generators in memory. Worked example: how many lines does the error pipeline read for the first 2 errors? Line 1 is INFO: read, dropped. Line 2 is ERROR: read, passed on, first result. Line 3 is INFO: read, dropped. Line 4 is ERROR: read, second result, and islice stops asking. 4 lines read out of 6 Lesson 8 of 10"],[24,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths Loop over lists, strings, dicts and files. Use iter() and next() by hand, and know that StopIteration ends a for loop. Write generator functions with yield, use them in for loops and with list(), sum() and islice(), and write iterator classes with __iter__ and __next__. Build generator pipelines, use yield from to delegate and collect return values, and control generators with send, throw and close. A generator object keeps a suspended frame: its local variables, its position (the last instruction) and its block stack. yield from forwards send and throw to the inner generator. PEP 479 turns StopIteration inside a generator into RuntimeError. These same suspended frames are what async def coroutines are built on. Beginner Use the iterator protocol. Intermediate Write generators and pipelines. Expert Control and combine generators, and explain suspended frames. Lesson 9 of 10"],[24,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","Every solution here is written with generators: tree walks with yield from, sorted streams joined lazily with heapq.merge, backtracking that yields each answer, and searches that stop at the k-th value with islice. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 94 Binary Tree Inorder Traversal Easy A recursive generator with yield from Yield everything from the left subtree, then the node, then everything from the right subtree. 173 Binary Search Tree Iterator Medium Keep one generator inside the class An inorder generator of a BST gives the values in sorted order. next takes the next value. To answer hasNext, look one value ahead and remember it. 1305 All Elements in Two Binary Search Trees Medium Merge two sorted generators Each BST gives a sorted stream with an inorder generator. heapq.merge joins sorted streams lazily, like the merge step of merge sort. 23 Merge k Sorted Lists Hard Turn each list into a generator, then heapq.merge A small generator walks one linked list. heapq.merge takes k sorted streams and always hands out the smallest next value, using a heap of size k. 77 Combinations Medium A backtracking generator Choose the next number from start to n, yield when k numbers are chosen. itertools.combinations does the same in C. 90 Subsets II Medium Sort, then skip equal neighbours Sort first so equal numbers sit together. At each depth, do not start a branch with the same number twice. 216 Combination Sum III Medium Filter a combinations stream Every answer is a set of k different digits from 1 to 9. Generate the combinations lazily and keep the ones whose sum is n. 401 Binary Watch Easy Loop over every time, count the lit bits There are only 12 × 60 times. For each one count the 1 bits of the hour and the minute with bin(x).count(\"1\"). 1415 The k-th Lexicographical String of All Happy Strings of Length n Medium Generate in order, stop at k A generator makes happy strings in alphabetical order. islice jumps to the k-th one without building the rest. 119 Pascal's Triangle II Easy A generator of rows Each row is made from the one before: add neighbours and put 1 at both ends. Yield rows one by one and keep the one you need. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 10 of 10"],[24,"recap","Chapter summary","An iterable can give an iterator with iter(). An iterator gives values with next() and raises StopIteration at the end. A for loop is iter() once plus next() until StopIteration. An iterator class has __iter__ returning self and __next__. An iterable class returns a fresh iterator, so it can be looped over many times. A function with yield returns a generator. Each next() runs to the next yield and pauses, keeping its local variables. Generators use constant memory, can be endless and stop work early. Chain them into pipelines. yield from delegates to another generator and returns its return value. send, throw and close control a paused generator. range and dict views are lazy and reusable. zip, map, filter, enumerate and files are one shot iterators. Next: 2.2 shows itertools and functools, the standard toolbox for working with iterators. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: a chunked generator, a reusable FloatRange iterable, and a running average coroutine driven with send.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[25,"","itertools, functools and operator","endless iterators, slicing, joining, groupby, combinatorics with formulas, reduce, partial, singledispatch, operator. · itertools · functools · operator · groupby · product · permutations · combinations · accumulate · partial · reduce · singledispatch · itemgetter Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Advanced Python›Step 2: Iterators, generators and context managers›itertools and functools Step 2 · Topic 2 of 3 · Advanced Use the standard toolbox for iterators and functions. Learn endless counters, taking and dropping items, chain, zip_longest, pairwise, batched, accumulate and tee, groupby, product, permutations and combinations with their formulas, reduce, partial, singledispatch, lru_cache and the operator module. About 75 min12 lessons1 animation7 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1The iterator toolbox 2Taking and dropping items 3Joining, pairing and running totals 4groupby: runs of equal keys 5Combinations and permutations 6functools: reduce, partial, singledispatch and caches 7operator: itemgetter, attrgetter, methodcaller 8Recipes built from the tools 9The maths: counting, prefix sums and folds 10From beginner to expert 11LeetCode practice 12Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 12"],[25,"idea","The iterator toolbox","Source: Python 3.14 documentation of itertools (with its recipes), functools, operator, math.comb and math.perm. What it is itertools is a module of fast building blocks that take iterators and give back new iterators: endless counters, slicing, joining, grouping and every kind of combination. functools has tools that work on functions: reduce, partial, singledispatch and caches. operator gives the + , [ ] and . operators as ready made functions. Why we need it Loops that join lists, number items, group rows, try every pair or keep a running total are written again and again, and each hand written loop is a chance for an off by one bug. These modules are tested, written in C, lazy (they keep memory small) and they make the intention clear: product(sizes, colours) says more than two nested loops. How it works Each itertools function returns an iterator, so the tools snap together: chain them, slice them with islice, feed them to sum or list. functools.partial fixes some arguments of a function, reduce folds a list into one value, and singledispatch picks a version of a function by the type of its argument. itertools: product, permutations and combinationsPick a function, how many items (n) and how long each tuple is (r). Each step makes the next tuple in the exact order Python gives them. Step 0 of 7 Function Items (n) Tuple length (r) Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this combinations of 4 items, 2 at a time: 6 tuples. AB and BA count once. permutations of the same items: 12 tuples, because AB and BA are different. product of 3 items with repeat=2: 9 tuples, AA and BB included. Python from itertools import count, cycle, repeat, isliceticket = count(start=100, step=5) # 100, 105, 110, ... for everprint(next(ticket), next(ticket), next(ticket))players = cycle([\"Asha\", \"Ravi\", \"Meera\"]) # go round and roundturns = []for turn inrange(5): turns.append(next(players))print(turns)print(list(repeat(\"-\", 3)))print(list(map(pow, [2, 3, 4], repeat(2)))) # repeat feeds the same value to mapprint(list(zip(count(1), \"abc\"))) # numbering without enumerateprint(list(islice(count(), 2, 12, 3))) # start 2, stop before 12, step 3 Output 100 105 110 ['Asha', 'Ravi', 'Meera', 'Asha', 'Ravi'] ['-', '-', '-'] [4, 9, 16] [(1, 'a'), (2, 'b'), (3, 'c')] [2, 5, 8, 11] Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 endless.py. Common mistake count, cycle and repeat never end on their own. Always stop them: with islice, zip with something finite, takewhile, or a break. Lesson 2 of 12"],[25,"filtering","Taking and dropping items","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 filtering.py. Picking items from a sequence filter takewhile dropwhile compress Looks at Every item Items until the test first fails Items until the test first fails Each item and its mask value Keeps Items that pass The front part that passes Everything from the first failure on Items where the mask is true Works on endless input Yes, but never finishes Yes, it stops by itself Yes Yes Typical use Clean a list Read until a blank line Skip a header Apply a yes or no column Bottom line: use takewhile when the data is sorted or a stop signal exists. It can stop early, which filter never does. Lesson 3 of 12"],[25,"combining","Joining, pairing and running totals","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 combining.py. Tool What it gives Instead of chain(a, b, c) All items of a, then b, then c a + b + c, which builds new lists chain.from_iterable(lists) Flattens one level A nested loop zip_longest(a, b, fillvalue=x) Pairs until the LONGEST input ends zip, which stops at the shortest pairwise(xs) Each item with the next one zip(xs, xs[1:]) batched(xs, n) Tuples of n items, the last may be shorter Slicing by hand starmap(f, pairs) f(*pair) for each pair A lambda that unpacks accumulate(xs, f, initial=) Running results of f (sum by default) A loop with a total variable tee(it, n) n independent iterators from one list(it), when the data is small Remember tee keeps every value that one copy has seen and the other has not, so if one copy runs far ahead it can use as much memory as a list. Use it when both copies move at a similar pace. Lesson 4 of 12"],[25,"grouping","groupby: runs of equal keys","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 grouping.py. Common mistake groupby only joins items that are NEXT to each other. On unsorted data tea appeared twice. Sort by the same key first, or use a dict (collections.defaultdict(list)) when you cannot sort. Grouping rows sorted then groupby a dict of lists collections.Counter Needs sorted data Yes No No Memory One group at a time All groups at once One count per key Keeps runs (aaa then b then aa) Yes, on unsorted data No No Best for Run length encoding and data that is already sorted General grouping Counting only Bottom line: groupby is perfect for runs and for data that already comes sorted (from a database ORDER BY). Otherwise a dict is simpler. Lesson 5 of 12"],[25,"combos","Combinations and permutations","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 combos.py. Trying every choice nested for loops itertools math.comb and math.perm Number of choices fixed in the code Yes, one loop each No, repeat=r or r is a variable Not needed Gives the actual tuples Yes Yes, lazily No, only the count Speed Python speed C speed Instant Use when Two or three simple loops You need the tuples You only need how many Bottom line: count with math.comb or math.perm before you generate. 10 items in every order is 3,628,800 tuples, and 20 items is far too many to ever list. Lesson 6 of 12"],[25,"functools","functools: reduce, partial, singledispatch and caches","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 functools_demo.py. Making a simpler version of a function partial(power, exponent=2) lambda b: power(b, 2) def square(b) Code Short Short Longest Shows what it wraps Yes: .func, .args, .keywords No No Can be pickled and sent to another process Yes No Yes Has its own docstring and name No No Yes Bottom line: use partial for quick \"fix this argument\" versions, above all for callbacks and multiprocessing. Write a def when it deserves a name and a docstring. In the exam singledispatch chose the int version for 21, the list version for [1, 2] and the base version for 2.5. It looks at the type of the FIRST argument only. For methods use functools.singledispatchmethod. Remember lru_cache(maxsize=2) keeps the 2 most recently used answers. Calling 1, 2, 1, 3, 1 gave 2 hits: when 3 arrived, 2 was the least recently used and was thrown away. Lesson 7 of 12"],[25,"operators","operator: itemgetter, attrgetter, methodcaller","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 operators.py. A key function for sorting lambda r: (r[1], r[0]) itemgetter(1, 0) Readable Yes Yes, once you know it Speed Python function call Faster, written in C Works with attributes Yes Use attrgetter(\"pages\") Can be pickled No Yes Bottom line: both are fine. itemgetter and attrgetter are a little faster and say clearly \"sort by these fields\". Lesson 8 of 12"],[25,"recipes","Recipes built from the tools","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 recipes.py. Remember The itertools documentation ends with a long list of recipes like these: sliding_window, unique_everseen, first_true, roundrobin, partition and more. The third party package more-itertools has all of them ready to install with pip. Lesson 9 of 12"],[25,"maths","The maths: counting, prefix sums and folds","product(items, repeat=r)Exam favourite nr 3 items, r = 2: 32 = 9 tuples. permutations(items, r)Exam favourite P(n, r) = n! / (n − r)! 4 items, r = 2: 24 / 2 = 12. combinations(items, r)Exam favourite C(n, r) = n! / (r! (n − r)!) 4 items, r = 2: 24 / (2 × 2) = 6. combinations_with_replacement(items, r) C(n + r − 1, r) 3 items, r = 2: C(4, 2) = 6. Pascal's rule C(n, r) = C(n − 1, r − 1) + C(n − 1, r) C(4, 2) = C(3, 1) + C(3, 2) = 3 + 3 = 6: tuples with the first item, plus tuples without it. Prefix sums with accumulateExam favourite P = accumulate(a, initial=0), sum of a[i] to a[j] = P[j + 1] − P[i] a = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5], P = [0, 3, 4, 8, 9, 14], a[1] + a[2] + a[3] = 9 − 3 = 6. reduce is a left fold reduce(f, [a, b, c]) = f(f(a, b), c) reduce(operator.sub, [10, 3, 2]) = (10 − 3) − 2 = 5. Worked example: 4 digit PINs with no digit used twice The first digit has 10 choices. The second has 9 left, the third 8 and the fourth 7. This is P(10, 4) = 10! / 6!. 10 × 9 × 8 × 7 = 5040, the same as the brute force count Lesson 10 of 12"],[25,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths Use count, cycle, repeat and islice, chain lists together and keep running totals with accumulate. Use product, permutations and combinations, group with sorted then groupby, and sort with itemgetter and attrgetter. Use partial, reduce, singledispatch and lru_cache, and build your own tools such as sliding_window from the recipes. Know the costs: combinatoric tools grow like nr or n!, tee buffers the gap between its copies, groupby shares one underlying iterator so each group must be used before the next. Choose between generating and counting with math.comb, and use prefix sums to answer range queries in O(1). Beginner Use the endless and joining tools. Intermediate Group, combine and sort with key functions. Expert Build recipes and reason about their cost. Lesson 11 of 12"],[25,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","Four problems are prefix sums with accumulate, three are runs found with groupby, two are generated with product and permutations, and one uses batched, repeat and chain together. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 303 Range Sum Query - Immutable Easy Prefix sums with accumulate Build P = accumulate(nums, initial=0) once. Then the sum from left to right is P[right + 1] − P[left], one subtraction per query. 1732 Find the Highest Altitude Easy Running totals, then max The altitudes are the running totals of gain starting from 0, which is accumulate(gain, initial=0). 1310 XOR Queries of a Subarray Medium Prefix XOR with accumulate XOR works like a sum that undoes itself: P[r + 1] ^ P[l] is the XOR of arr[l] to arr[r]. 1313 Decompress Run-Length Encoded List Easy batched, repeat and chain Read the list two numbers at a time with batched. Each pair (freq, val) becomes repeat(val, freq). chain joins them. 443 String Compression Medium groupby gives the runs Each run of the same letter becomes the letter plus its count (only if the count is above 1). Write the result back into chars and return its length. 1446 Consecutive Characters Easy Longest run with groupby groupby splits the text into runs of one letter. The answer is the length of the longest run. 830 Positions of Large Groups Easy groupby over enumerate Group (index, letter) pairs by the letter. The first and last index of each group give its interval. Keep groups of 3 or more. 784 Letter Case Permutation Medium product of choices per character A letter has two choices (lower, upper), a digit has one. product of all the choices gives every string. 47 Permutations II Medium permutations, then remove repeats permutations treats equal numbers as different positions, so it repeats some orders. A set removes the repeats. Fine for small inputs, backtracking with a counter is faster. 1588 Sum of All Odd Length Subarrays Easy Prefix sums, then every odd window With prefix sums each subarray sum is one subtraction. Try every start and every odd length. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 12 of 12"],[25,"recap","Chapter summary","count, cycle and repeat are endless: stop them with islice, zip or takewhile. takewhile and dropwhile cut a sequence at the first failure. filter and compress check every item. chain joins, zip_longest pairs to the longest, pairwise gives neighbours, batched gives groups, accumulate gives running results. groupby joins only neighbours with equal keys, so sort by the same key first. product nr, permutations n!/(n − r)!, combinations C(n, r), with replacement C(n + r − 1, r). Count first with math.comb and math.perm. functools: reduce folds left, partial fixes arguments, singledispatch picks by type, lru_cache keeps recent answers. operator: itemgetter, attrgetter and methodcaller are fast, clear key functions. Next: 2.3 covers context managers and the with statement. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: run length encoding with groupby, a moving average over any iterable, and a value formatter with singledispatch.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[26,"","Context managers and the with statement","with, __enter__ and __exit__, suppressing errors, @contextmanager, standard managers, exit order, ExitStack. · context manager · with · __enter__ · __exit__ · contextmanager · suppress · ExitStack · redirect_stdout · Lock · TemporaryDirectory · PCPP Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Advanced Python›Step 2: Iterators, generators and context managers›Context managers Step 2 · Topic 3 of 3 · Advanced Never leak a file, lock or setting again. Learn what with really does, how to write context managers as classes and with @contextmanager, how to suppress exactly the right errors, the managers in the standard library, several managers at once and ExitStack. About 70 min10 lessons1 animation5 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Context managers and the with statement 2Writing a context manager class 3Suppressing exceptions 4contextmanager: a context manager from a generator 5Context managers in the standard library 6Several managers and ExitStack 7The maths: leaks, failures and exit order 8From beginner to expert 9LeetCode practice 10Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 10"],[26,"idea","","Source: Python 3.14 language reference (the with statement), the data model section on context managers, the contextlib documentation, PEP 343 and PCPP-32-101 block 1. What it is A context manager is an object that sets something up at the start of a block and cleans it up at the end, whatever happens in between. The with statement uses it: with open(path) as f: opens a file and promises to close it. Why we need it Programs borrow things that must be given back: files, network connections, locks, temporary folders, changed settings. If an error happens halfway, or a return leaves early, the clean up line is easy to skip. A leaked file or a lock that is never released can crash a server hours later. Writing try and finally everywhere works but is long and easy to get wrong. How it works with X as v: calls X.__enter__() and stores its return value in v. The body runs. When the body ends, normally, by return, or by an exception, Python calls X.__exit__(type, error, traceback). If __exit__ returns a true value the exception is suppressed, otherwise it continues. What a with statement doesPick a case. Each step shows the line that runs, what as stores, the arguments __exit__ gets, and whether an error is on its way. Step 0 of 9 Case Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this An error in the body still runs __exit__, so close is printed before the program stops. A true return value from __exit__ swallows the error. Python import osimport tempfilepath = os.path.join(tempfile.mkdtemp(), \"notes.txt\")with open(path, \"w\") as f: # the file is closed when the block ends f.write(\"line one\\n\")print(\"closed after with?\", f.closed)try:with open(path) as f:print(f.readline().strip())raiseValueError(\"something went wrong\")exceptValueErroras err:print(\"error:\", err, \"| closed anyway?\", f.closed)# what with saves you from writing by handf = open(path)try:print(len(f.read()), \"characters\")finally: f.close()print(f.closed) Output closed after with? True line one error: something went wrong | closed anyway? True 9 characters True Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 files.py. Remember A file object is its own context manager: its __exit__ calls close(). Always open files with with. In CPython an unclosed file is usually closed by garbage collection, but other Pythons (PyPy) and long running programs can run out of file handles first. Lesson 2 of 10"],[26,"protocol","Writing a context manager class","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 protocol.py. Method Called Gets Returns __enter__(self) When the with line runs Nothing else The value stored by as (often self, a file, or a connection) __exit__(self, kind, err, tb) When the block ends, for ANY reason None, None, None, or the exception type, object and traceback True to hide the exception, False or None to let it continue Making sure clean up runs clean up as the last line try and finally with and a context manager Runs after an error No Yes Yes Runs after return or break No Yes Yes Code at each use One line, often forgotten Four or more lines One line Set up and clean up kept together No No, they are far apart Yes, in one class or function Reusable No No Yes Bottom line: write the set up and clean up once as a context manager, then every user gets safe clean up with one with line. Lesson 3 of 10"],[26,"suppress","Suppressing exceptions","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 suppress.py. Common mistake Only return True from __exit__ on purpose and only for the exact errors you expect. Hiding every error makes bugs invisible. contextlib.suppress(SomeError) is the clear, ready made way to say \"this error is fine here\". Ignoring an expected error try / except / pass with suppress(Error) Lines Four Two Says what it means Not at a glance Yes Can run code on the error Yes No Other errors Pass through Pass through Bottom line: use suppress when you only want to ignore one kind of error. Use try and except when you need to do something about it. Lesson 4 of 10"],[26,"generator","contextmanager: a context manager from a generator","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 generator.py. In the exam Everything before yield is the set up, the yielded value goes into as, and everything after yield is the clean up. The try and finally around the yield is what makes the clean up run after an error: without it, an exception in the body would be raised at the yield and skip the rest. Two ways to write a context manager a class with __enter__ and __exit__ @contextmanager on a generator Code Longer Short Keeps state between uses Yes, in attributes Only for one with Can decide to hide errors Return True from __exit__ Catch the error around yield and do not re raise Can be used as a decorator With ContextDecorator Yes, automatically Best for Reusable objects like connections Quick set up and clean up pairs Bottom line: start with @contextmanager. Write a class when the manager is an object that does more than set up and clean up. Lesson 5 of 10"],[26,"stdlib","Context managers in the standard library","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 stdlib.py. Context manager Set up Clean up open(...) Open a file Close it threading.Lock() Acquire the lock Release it tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() Make a folder Delete it with everything inside contextlib.chdir(path) Change the working folder Change back contextlib.redirect_stdout(f) Send print to f Send it back to the screen decimal.localcontext() A private copy of the decimal settings Restore the old settings contextlib.closing(obj) Nothing Call obj.close() contextlib.nullcontext(v) Nothing, gives v Nothing sqlite3 connection Start a transaction Commit, or roll back on error (it does not close) unittest.mock.patch(...) Replace an object for a test Put the original back Remember nullcontext is handy when a with is sometimes needed and sometimes not: lock if threaded else nullcontext(). Lesson 6 of 10"],[26,"many","Several managers and ExitStack","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 many.py. In the exam Managers are entered left to right and exited in the reverse order, like a stack: the last one opened is the first one closed. That is what you want: an inner resource that depends on an outer one is cleaned up first. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 exitstack.py. Remember ExitStack is for when the number of managers is only known while the program runs. enter_context adds a manager, callback adds any function to call at the end, and everything is cleaned up in reverse order, even if opening the third file fails. Going deeper: async with Async code uses async with and the methods __aenter__ and __aexit__ (or @contextlib.asynccontextmanager), so the set up and clean up can await network calls. Advanced 5.2 uses it with asyncio. Lesson 7 of 10"],[26,"maths","The maths: leaks, failures and exit order","Exit order of n nested managersExam favourite the k-th one entered is the (n − k + 1)-th one exited with A, B, C: A is entered 1st and exited 3rd. Chance that at least one of n steps failsExam favourite 1 − (1 − p)n Each of 10 steps fails with p = 0.01: 1 − 0.9910 ≈ 9.6 %. Errors are common, so clean up must not depend on success. Expected leaks without with leaks = calls × p (chance a path forgets to close) 1000 calls with p = 0.02 leak about 20 files. With with: 0. Time until a leaking server runs out of file handles requests = free handles / leaks per request 1024 handles and 2 leaks per request: 512 requests. Elapsed time with a timer context manager elapsed = texit − tenter Entered at 1.50 s, exited at 2.75 s: 1.25 s. Worked example: a server that leaks file handles 2000 requests per minute, and 5 % of them forget to close a file: 2000 × 0.05 = 100 leaks per minute. The limit is 1024 open handles and 24 are in use: 1000 are free. Minutes until the limit: 1000 / 100. 10 minutes, then every open() fails. A with statement makes the leak rate 0. Lesson 8 of 10"],[26,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths Open files with with and know the file is closed afterwards, even after an error. Write context manager classes with __enter__ and __exit__, and generator based ones with @contextmanager and try and finally. Suppress exact errors on purpose, use several managers in one with, ExitStack for a dynamic number, and the standard ones: locks, temporary folders, chdir, redirect_stdout, localcontext. with is defined by PEP 343: Python looks up __enter__ and __exit__ on the TYPE (not the object), calls __exit__ with the exception triple, and treats a true result as suppression. @contextmanager wraps the generator and throws the body exception in at the yield. ExitStack unwinds callbacks in LIFO order and chains exceptions raised during clean up. Beginner Use with for files. Intermediate Write context managers both ways. Expert Combine managers and explain the protocol exactly. Lesson 9 of 10"],[26,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","with blocks nest like brackets: the last one opened is the first one closed. Every problem here uses a stack for that same reason, from timing nested function calls to matching brackets and replaying pushes and pops. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 636 Exclusive Time of Functions Medium A stack of running functions Logs open and close like nested with blocks. Keep a stack of running ids. When something starts or ends, give the time since the last event to the function on top. 946 Validate Stack Sequences Medium Replay the pushes and pops on a real stack Push each value. After every push, pop while the top is the next value that should come out. It works if the stack ends empty, just as every with block must close in reverse order. 921 Minimum Add to Make Parentheses Valid Medium Count open brackets Track how many \"(\" are still open. A \")\" with nothing open needs an added \"(\". At the end every open one needs a \")\". 1249 Minimum Remove to Make Valid Parentheses Medium A stack of open positions Push the index of each \"(\". A \")\" with an empty stack is removed. Any \"(\" left on the stack at the end is removed too. 856 Score of Parentheses Medium A stack of scores per level Opening starts a new level with score 0. Closing turns the level into 1 if it was empty, or double its score otherwise, and adds it to the level outside. 1190 Reverse Substrings Between Each Pair of Parentheses Medium A stack of unfinished text On \"(\" save the text so far and start fresh. On \")\" reverse the inner text and glue it to the saved text. 32 Longest Valid Parentheses Hard A stack of indexes with a base Keep the index of the last unmatched position at the bottom of the stack. After each match, the length is the current index minus the index on top. 735 Asteroid Collision Medium A stack of survivors Only a left moving asteroid can hit the right moving ones on the stack. Pop the smaller ones, stop if it is destroyed. 844 Backspace String Compare Easy Build each text with a stack Push letters, pop on \"#\". Compare the two results. 2390 Removing Stars From a String Medium A stack again Each star removes the closest letter on its left: push letters and pop on \"*\". .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 10 of 10"],[26,"recap","Chapter summary","with X as v: runs X.__enter__() (its return value goes into v), then the body, then X.__exit__(type, error, traceback), whatever happens. Clean up in __exit__ runs after errors, return and break, so resources are never leaked. __exit__ returning True hides the exception. Use contextlib.suppress for the exact errors you expect. @contextmanager: code before yield is set up, after yield is clean up. Wrap the yield in try and finally. Standard managers: open, locks, TemporaryDirectory, chdir, redirect_stdout, localcontext, closing, nullcontext, mock.patch. Several managers enter left to right and exit right to left. ExitStack handles a number known only at run time. Next: Step 3 is about writing professional code: style, type hints, tests, logging and debugging. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: a Setting manager that always restores a value, a log_block generator manager, and a Transaction that rolls back and hides only ValueError.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[27,"","Clean code: PEP 8, docstrings and type hints","Zen of Python, PEP 8 layout and names, bug preventing habits, docstrings, doctest, type hints, ruff, black, mypy. · PEP 8 · PEP 257 · PEP 20 · type hints · mypy · ruff · black · docstring · doctest · Protocol · clean code · PCPP Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Advanced Python›Step 3: Professional code: style, testing and logging›Style and type hints Step 3 · Topic 1 of 3 · Advanced Write code that is easy to read and hard to break. Learn the Zen of Python, the PEP 8 layout and naming rules, the habits that prevent bugs, PEP 257 docstrings with doctest, type hints from simple to generic, and the tools that check all of it: ruff, black and mypy. About 70 min9 lessons1 animation5 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Clean code: style, docstrings and type hints 2PEP 8: layout and names 3Rules that prevent bugs 4Docstrings and doctest (PEP 257) 5Type hints 6The maths: complexity, paths and line budgets 7From beginner to expert 8LeetCode practice 9Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 9"],[27,"idea","Clean code: style, docstrings and type hints","Source: PEP 8, PEP 257, PEP 20, PEP 484, PEP 544, PEP 589, PEP 604, PEP 695, the typing and doctest documentation, and PCPP-32-101 block 2. Tool outputs are real runs of ruff 0.17.4, black 26.10.1 and mypy 2.4.1. What it is Clean code is code that other people (and you, in six months) can read quickly and change safely. Python has written rules for it: PEP 8 for layout and names, PEP 257 for docstrings, PEP 20 (the Zen of Python) for the spirit, and type hints (PEP 484) that say what kind of value each name holds. Why we need it Code is read far more often than it is written. When every file looks different, readers waste time on layout instead of logic, and small mistakes hide in the mess. Some style rules also prevent real bugs: is None cannot be fooled, a None default avoids a shared list, and a type checker finds a wrong argument before any user does. How it works You follow the rules by habit and let tools check them: a formatter (black or ruff format) fixes layout automatically, a linter (ruff or flake8) reports problems like unused imports and bad names, and a type checker (mypy or pyright) reads the hints and reports type mistakes without running the code. Cleaning code one rule at a timeEach step fixes one style rule. Changed lines are marked with a tick. The behaviour stays the same, the code gets easier to read. Step 0 of 10 Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this Step 8 is the only rule that can change what the program does: is None cannot be fooled. Python import this Output The Zen of Python, by Tim Peters Beautiful is better than ugly. Explicit is better than implicit. Simple is better than complex. Complex is better than complicated. Flat is better than nested. Sparse is better than dense. Readability counts. Special cases aren't special enough to break the rules. Although practicality beats purity. Errors should never pass silently. Unless explicitly silenced. In the face of ambiguity, refuse the temptation to guess. There should be one-- and preferably only one --obvious way to do it. Although that way may not be obvious at first unless you're Dutch. Now is better than never. Although never is often better than *right* now. If the implementation is hard to explain, it's a bad idea. If the implementation is easy to explain, it may be a good idea. Namespaces are one honking great idea -- let's do more of those! Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 zen.py. Remember The Zen is a set of values, not rules. Lines like \"Readability counts\", \"Explicit is better than implicit\" and \"Errors should never pass silently\" explain WHY PEP 8 and good habits exist. Lesson 2 of 9"],[27,"pep8","PEP 8: layout and names","Rule Do Do not Indentation 4 spaces per level Tabs, 2 spaces, or a mix Line length At most 79 characters (72 for comments and docstrings) Lines that need side scrolling Blank lines 2 around top level functions and classes, 1 between methods None at all, or many Imports One per line, at the top: standard library, then third party, then your own, each group sorted import sys, os in the middle of the file Spaces x = 1, a == b, f(a, b), d[\"k\"] x=1, f( a ,b ), d [\"k\"] Functions and variables snake_case: circle_area, total_price CircleArea, totalPrice, A Classes CapWords: ShoppingCart shopping_cart Constants UPPER_CASE: MAX_USERS maxUsers Private by convention _helper, _cache __everything (name mangling, only when needed) Long expressions Break inside brackets, operators at the start of the new line Backslashes at line ends This is the messy code from the animation, checked by the ruff linter: messy.pypython 1import sys, math2def CircleArea( R ):3 if R==None : return 04 A=math.pi*R**25 return A Linting with ruffshell $ ruff check --select E,F,W,N,I --output-format concise messy.py messy.py:1:1: E401 [*] Multiple imports on one line messy.py:1:1: I001 [*] Import block is un-sorted or un-formatted messy.py:1:8: F401 [*] `sys` imported but unused messy.py:2:5: N802 Function name `CircleArea` should be lowercase messy.py:2:17: N803 Argument name `R` should be lowercase messy.py:3:9: E711 Comparison to `None` should be `cond is None` messy.py:3:14: E701 Multiple statements on one line (colon) messy.py:4:3: N806 Variable `A` in function should be lowercase Found 8 errors. [*] 3 fixable with `--fix` (1 unsafe fix available with `--fix --unsafe-fixes`). Formatting with blackshell $ black --diff messy.py @@ -1,5 +1,8 @@ import sys, math -def CircleArea( R ): - if R==None : return 0 - A=math.pi*R**2 - return A + + +def CircleArea(R): + if R == None: + return 0 + A = math.pi * R**2 + return A In the exam black fixed the layout but not the names, the unused import or == None: a formatter only moves spaces and line breaks, it never changes meaning. That is why teams use a formatter AND a linter. ruff can do both (ruff format and ruff check). Lesson 3 of 9"],[27,"recommend","Rules that prevent bugs","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 recommend.py. Checking a value x == None x is None if not x Asks Does x say it equals None (__eq__)? Is x the None object? Is x empty, zero, False or None? Can a class change the answer Yes No Yes, with __bool__ or __len__ Right for Never for None Checking for a missing value Checking for \"nothing in it\" Bottom line: use is None for missing values and plain truthiness (if items:) for empty containers. They mean different things: 0 and [] are falsy but not None. Remember PEP 8 also says: use isinstance instead of comparing types, use startswith and endswith instead of slicing, do not compare booleans to True with ==, and keep try blocks small so you only catch the error you expect. Lesson 4 of 9"],[27,"docstrings","Docstrings and doctest (PEP 257)","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 docstrings.py. PEP 257 rule Example Always triple double quotes \"\"\"Return the mean.\"\"\" First line: a short summary that ends with a full stop, written as a command \"\"\"Return the mean of a list.\"\"\", not \"\"\"Returns the mean\"\"\" Longer docstring: summary, blank line, then details Arguments, return value, errors raised, examples Every public module, function, class and method gets one Private helpers may skip it A class docstring says what the class is, __init__ says how to make one See Wallet above Remember doctest finds >>> lines in docstrings, runs them and compares the output with the next line. TestResults(failed=0, attempted=2) means both examples still work. It keeps documentation honest. Real test suites come in 3.2. Lesson 5 of 9"],[27,"hints","Type hints","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 hints.py. In the exam Hints are stored in __annotations__ but Python never checks them while running: first(\"hello\") worked although the hint says list. In Python 3.14 annotations are evaluated lazily (PEP 649 and 749), so a hint can even name a class defined later in the file. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 type_hints.py. Hint Means int, str, float, bool, None That exact kind of value list[int], dict[str, int], tuple[int, str], set[str] Containers and what is inside int | None (old: Optional[int]) An int or None Callable[[int], str] A function taking an int and returning a str Literal[\"up\", \"down\"] Only these values TypedDict A dict with known keys and value types Protocol Any class that has these methods (structural, \"duck\" typing) Final Must not be changed after it is set def f[T](x: list[T]) -> T and type Alias = ... Generics and aliases with the Python 3.12 syntax shop.pypython 1def total(prices: list[float], discount: float = 0.0) -> float:2 return sum(prices) * (1 - discount)345def label(name: str) -> str:6 return name.upper()789print(total([10.0, 20.0], \"10%\"))10print(label(42))11count: int = \"three\" Type checking with mypyshell $ mypy shop.py shop.py:9: error: Argument 2 to \"total\" has incompatible type \"str\"; expected \"float\" [arg-type] shop.py:10: error: Argument 1 to \"label\" has incompatible type \"int\"; expected \"str\" [arg-type] shop.py:11: error: Incompatible types in assignment (expression has type \"str\", variable has type \"int\") [assignment] Found 3 errors in 1 file (checked 1 source file) Finding a type mistake no hints hints and mypy checks with isinstance at run time When it is found When a user hits that line Before the program runs When that line runs Cost while running None None A little on every call Documents the code No Yes Partly Catches every mistake No Most, if hints are complete Only the ones you check Bottom line: add hints to public functions and run mypy (or let your editor do it). Keep isinstance checks for data from outside, like user input or JSON. Lesson 6 of 9"],[27,"maths","The maths: complexity, paths and line budgets","Cyclomatic complexity (McCabe)Exam favourite M = E − N + 2 (edges and nodes of the control flow graph) = decision points + 1 circle_area has one if, so M = 1 + 1 = 2. ruff rule C901 warns when M is above a limit, often 10. Paths through k independent if statementsExam favourite paths = 2k 4 separate ifs give 16 paths. Smaller functions keep this testable. Minimum tests to cover every independent path tests ≥ M A function with M = 6 needs at least 6 tests for basis path coverage. Characters left on a line room = 79 − indent Code nested 2 levels deep (8 spaces) has 71 characters left. Spaces of indentation spaces = 4 × depth 3 levels deep: 12 spaces. Deep nesting eats the line budget, a hint to split the function. Worked example: the complexity of a grading function It has if, elif, elif and else: 3 decisions (else adds none). It also has a for loop: 1 more decision. Decisions = 3 + 1 = 4. M = 4 + 1 = 5, so at least 5 tests to cover its paths Lesson 7 of 9"],[27,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths Follow the PEP 8 basics: 4 space indents, snake_case names, spaces around operators, one statement per line. Write PEP 257 docstrings with doctest examples, use is None and truthiness correctly, and run a formatter and a linter. Add type hints with generics, unions, Callable, Protocol and TypedDict, and run mypy in strict mode. Configure ruff, black and mypy in pyproject.toml and run them in pre commit hooks and CI. Know the limits: gradual typing, Any, variance of generics (list is invariant, Sequence is covariant), and PEP 649 lazy annotations read through annotationlib. Beginner Write PEP 8 style code. Intermediate Document and lint it. Expert Type it fully and automate every check. Lesson 8 of 9"],[27,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","Every problem is about following written rules for text exactly, which is what a style guide asks of code. The downloadable solutions are written the way this chapter teaches: PEP 8 layout, clear names, a docstring and type hints on every function. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 520 Detect Capital Easy Three allowed shapes Like a naming rule: all capitals, all small, or only the first letter capital. 482 License Key Formatting Easy Clean, then group from the right Remove dashes, make it upper case, then cut groups of k from the end so only the first group may be shorter. 273 Integer to English Words Hard Groups of three digits with clear helper names Split the number into groups of three from the right (ones, Thousand, Million, Billion). Turn each group under 1000 into words with a small helper, and skip groups that are zero. 824 Goat Latin Easy Follow the rules word by word Vowel start: add \"ma\". Otherwise move the first letter to the end and add \"ma\". Then add one \"a\" per word position. 1859 Sorting the Sentence Easy Sort by the last character Each word ends with its position. Sort by that digit and remove it. 2047 Number of Valid Words in a Sentence Easy Check each rule, like a linter No digits, at most one hyphen with letters on both sides, at most one punctuation mark and only at the end. 929 Unique Email Addresses Easy Normalise, then count with a set In the local part drop everything after \"+\" and remove dots. Keep the domain as it is. 1556 Thousand Separator Easy Format with a separator Python can group digits with format(n, \",\"). LeetCode wants dots, so swap the commas. 1694 Reformat Phone Number Easy Blocks of 3, end with 2 and 2 if 4 are left Keep only digits. Take blocks of 3 while more than 4 remain. Then 4 digits become 2 and 2, otherwise one last block. 2299 Strong Password Checker II Easy One check per rule At least 8 characters, a lower case letter, an upper case letter, a digit, a special character, and no two equal characters side by side. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 9 of 9"],[27,"recap","Chapter summary","PEP 8: 4 spaces, lines up to 79 characters, 2 blank lines around top level definitions, sorted imports one per line. Names: snake_case for functions and variables, CapWords for classes, UPPER_CASE for constants. Use is None, truthiness for empty containers, isinstance, and None instead of a mutable default. PEP 257: triple double quoted docstrings with a one line summary. doctest runs the examples in them. Type hints document types and let mypy find mistakes before running. Python itself ignores them. A formatter fixes layout, a linter finds problems, a type checker finds type mistakes: use all three. Next: 3.2 is about testing: unittest, pytest, fixtures and mocks. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: check that a name is a valid PEP 8 function name, read the type hints of a function, and convert CamelCase names to snake_case.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[28,"","Testing: unittest, pytest and mocks","unittest, fixtures, failures and errors, mocks, pytest, coverage, property based testing, test strategy. · testing · unittest · pytest · mock · patch · fixture · parametrize · coverage · Hypothesis · TDD · PCPP Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Advanced Python›Step 3: Professional code: style, testing and logging›Testing Step 3 · Topic 2 of 3 · Advanced Prove that your code works, and keep it working. Learn unittest test cases, fixtures and subTest, reading failures, mocks and patch, pytest with fixtures, parametrize and approx, coverage, property based testing with Hypothesis, and what to test. About 80 min11 lessons1 animation5 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Why we test, and a first test suite 2setUp, subTest and skip 3Reading failures and errors 4Mocks: replacing what you cannot test 5pytest: the most used test runner 6Property based testing 7What to test and how much 8The maths: coverage, boundaries and binary search 9From beginner to expert 10LeetCode practice 11Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 11"],[28,"idea","Why we test, and a first test suite","Source: Python 3.14 documentation of unittest and unittest.mock, and the documentation of pytest 9.2, coverage.py and Hypothesis. Terminal outputs are real runs. What it is A test is a small piece of code that calls your code with known inputs and checks the result. A test suite is all of them together, and a test runner (unittest or pytest) finds them, runs them and reports what passed and what failed. Why we need it Without tests you check by hand, once, and every later change can silently break something that used to work. Tests check the same things in a second, every time, so you can change and improve code without fear. They also show how the code is meant to be used, and a failing test points straight at the bug. How it works Each test follows Arrange, Act, Assert: set up the inputs, call the code, and assert what must be true. The runner records PASSED, FAILED (an assert was false) or ERROR (the test crashed). In test driven development you write the failing test first (red), make it pass (green), then clean up the code (refactor) with the tests watching. A test run: red, green, refactorThe code under test is on the left, the tests on the right. Each step runs one test or changes the code. Step 0 of 9 Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this Step 4 shows why tests matter: the bug only appears for prices whose discount is not a whole number. Python import sysimport unittestdef discount(price, percent):if percent < 0or percent > 100:raiseValueError(\"percent must be 0 to 100\")return price * (100 - percent) / 100classTestDiscount(unittest.TestCase):def test_no_discount(self): self.assertEqual(discount(100, 0), 100)def test_half_price(self): self.assertEqual(discount(80, 50), 40)def test_float_rounding(self): self.assertAlmostEqual(discount(0.3, 0), 0.1 + 0.2)def test_bad_percent(self):with self.assertRaises(ValueError): discount(10, 150)runner = unittest.TextTestRunner(stream=sys.stdout, verbosity=2)unittest.main(argv=[\"tests\"], testRunner=runner, exit=False) Output test_bad_percent (__main__.TestDiscount.test_bad_percent) ... ok test_float_rounding (__main__.TestDiscount.test_float_rounding) ... ok test_half_price (__main__.TestDiscount.test_half_price) ... ok test_no_discount (__main__.TestDiscount.test_no_discount) ... ok ---------------------------------------------------------------------- Ran 4 tests in 0.000s OK Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 unit.py. In the exam unittest is built into Python. A test is a method whose name starts with test_ inside a class that inherits from unittest.TestCase. assertEqual, assertTrue, assertIn, assertRaises and assertAlmostEqual (for floats) check results and give a clear message when they fail. Lesson 2 of 11"],[28,"fixtures","setUp, subTest and skip","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 fixtures.py. Tool What it does setUp(self) and tearDown(self) Run before and after EVERY test, so each test starts fresh setUpClass(cls) and tearDownClass(cls) Run once for the whole class (for slow set up like a database) self.subTest(...) Inside a loop, report each case separately and keep going after a failure @unittest.skip(reason), skipIf(cond, reason) Do not run this test, and say why @unittest.expectedFailure A known bug: the test is expected to fail for now python -m unittest discover Find every test_*.py file in the folder and run it Common mistake Tests must not depend on each other or on their order. test_add_more added a book, but test_total still saw only the pen, because setUp made a new cart for each test. Lesson 3 of 11"],[28,"failures","Reading failures and errors","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 failures.py. Three outcomes of a test PASSED FAILED ERROR Means Every assert was true An assert was false The test crashed with another exception Usually points to Nothing to do Wrong result: a bug in the code or in the test A crash: bad input handling, a typo, a missing file First thing to read The two values in the message The last line of the traceback Bottom line: read the last line first. When a test fails, check whether the code or the test is wrong: here test_wrong_expectation expected 2, but the average of 1 and 2 really is 1.5. Lesson 4 of 11"],[28,"mocking","Mocks: replacing what you cannot test","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 mocking.py. Mock feature Use Mock().method.return_value = x The fake method returns x side_effect = [a, b] or an exception A different answer on each call, or raise an error to test failure handling assert_called_once_with(...), call_count, call_args Check HOW your code used the dependency patch(\"module.name\") and patch.object(Class, \"attr\") Replace a real object for the length of a with block or a test, then put it back MagicMock A Mock that also supports special methods like len() and with Remember advice() receives the client as an argument (dependency injection), so a test can hand it a fake. Code that creates its own network client deep inside is much harder to test. Designing for testability means passing dependencies in. Testing code that calls a web service call the real service a Mock a fake local server Speed Slow Instant Fast Works offline and in CI No Yes Yes Can test timeouts and errors Hard Easy with side_effect Yes Catches changes in the real service Yes No No Bottom line: use mocks for unit tests, and keep a few slow integration tests that call the real thing. Lesson 5 of 11"],[28,"pytest","pytest: the most used test runner","pytest needs plain functions and plain assert, and gives much more detail when something fails. Install it with pip install pytest. Here is a small module and its tests: shop.pypython 1import json2import os345def discount(price, percent):6 if percent < 0 or percent > 100:7 raise ValueError(\"percent must be 0 to 100\")8 return price - price * percent / 10091011def price_with_tax(price):12 rate = float(os.environ.get(\"TAX_RATE\", \"0.18\"))13 return round(price * (1 + rate), 2)141516class Cart:17 def __init__(self):18 self.items = {}1920 def add(self, name, price, qty=1):21 self.items[name] = self.items.get(name, 0) + price * qty2223 def total(self):24 return sum(self.items.values())2526 def save(self, path):27 with open(path, \"w\") as f:28 json.dump(self.items, f) test_shop.pypython 1import json23import pytest45from shop import Cart, discount, price_with_tax678@pytest.fixture9def cart():10 c = Cart()11 c.add(\"pen\", 10, qty=3)12 c.add(\"book\", 250)13 return c141516def test_total(cart):17 assert cart.total() == 280181920@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"price, percent, expected\", [21 (100, 0, 100),22 (80, 50, 40),23 (99, 10, 89.1),24 (0.3, 0, 0.1 + 0.2),25])26def test_discount(price, percent, expected):27 assert discount(price, percent) == pytest.approx(expected)282930def test_bad_percent():31 with pytest.raises(ValueError, match=\"0 to 100\"):32 discount(10, 150)333435def test_save(cart, tmp_path):36 path = tmp_path / \"cart.json\"37 cart.save(path)38 assert json.loads(path.read_text()) == {\"pen\": 30, \"book\": 250}394041def test_tax_rate_from_environment(monkeypatch):42 monkeypatch.setenv(\"TAX_RATE\", \"0.05\")43 assert price_with_tax(200) == 210.0 Line 8 A fixture: any test that names cart as a parameter gets a fresh one. Line 20parametrize runs the same test once per row. Line 27pytest.approx compares floats with a small tolerance, so 0.1 + 0.2 equals 0.3. Line 31pytest.raises passes only if the error is raised, and match checks its message. Line 35tmp_path is a built in fixture: a fresh temporary folder for this test. Line 41monkeypatch changes an environment variable just for this test. Running the testsshell $ pytest -v collecting ... collected 8 items test_shop.py::test_total PASSED [ 12%] test_shop.py::test_discount[100-0-100] PASSED [ 25%] test_shop.py::test_discount[80-50-40] PASSED [ 37%] test_shop.py::test_discount[99-10-89.1] PASSED [ 50%] test_shop.py::test_discount[0.3-0-0.30000000000000004] PASSED [ 62%] test_shop.py::test_bad_percent PASSED [ 75%] test_shop.py::test_save PASSED [ 87%] test_shop.py::test_tax_rate_from_environment PASSED [100%] ============================== 8 passed in 0.11s =============================== Change / to // in discount and run only the discount tests. pytest shows the values on both sides of the failing assert: A failing testshell $ pytest -q -k discount ..F. [100%] =================================== FAILURES =================================== __________________________ test_discount[99-10-89.1] ___________________________ price = 99, percent = 10, expected = 89.1 > assert discount(price, percent) == pytest.approx(expected) E assert 90 == 89.1 &plusmn; 8.9e-05 E comparison failed E Obtained: 90 E Expected: 89.1 &plusmn; 8.9e-05 test_shop.py:27: AssertionError =========================== short test summary info ============================ FAILED test_shop.py::test_discount[99-10-89.1] - assert 90 == 89.1 &plusmn; 8.9e-05 1 failed, 3 passed, 4 deselected in 0.11s Measuring coverageshell $ coverage run -m pytest -q $ coverage report -m Name Stmts Miss Cover Missing --------------------------------------- shop.py 19 0 100% --------------------------------------- TOTAL 19 0 100% Test runners unittest pytest doctest Install Built in pip install pytest Built in A test is A method in a TestCase class Any test_ function A >>> example in a docstring Checks with self.assertEqual and friends Plain assert, with detailed messages Printed output Set up setUp and tearDown Fixtures, shared and combined by name None"],[28,"pytest","pytest: the most used test runner","Many inputs subTest parametrize More examples Runs the others No Yes, unittest and doctest tests too No Bottom line: use pytest for projects. It runs old unittest suites too. Keep doctest for examples in documentation. Common mistake 100 % coverage means every line ran during the tests, NOT that every case was checked. The next lesson shows a bug that survives full coverage. Lesson 6 of 11"],[28,"property","Property based testing","Instead of picking examples yourself, you state a rule that must hold for EVERY input, and the Hypothesis library (pip install hypothesis) searches for an input that breaks it. Rule: a 100 % discount makes any price free. test_props.pypython 1from hypothesis import given, strategies as st23from shop import discount456@given(st.floats(min_value=0, max_value=1e6))7def test_full_discount_is_free(price):8 assert discount(price, 100) == 0 Hypothesis finds a real bugshell $ pytest -q test_props.py F [100%] =================================== FAILURES =================================== __________________________ test_full_discount_is_free __________________________ price = 41943.17912896944 > assert discount(price, 100) == 0 E assert -7.275957614183426e-12 == 0 E + where -7.275957614183426e-12 = discount(41943.17912896944, 100) E Failing test case: test_full_discount_is_free( E price=41943.17912896944, E ) =========================== short test summary info ============================ FAILED test_props.py::test_full_discount_is_free - assert -7.275957614183426e... 1 failed in 0.26s In the exam All 8 example tests passed with 100 % coverage, yet price − price × 100 / 100 can come out a tiny bit below zero because of float rounding. The fix is price × (100 − percent) / 100, which gives exactly 0 at 100 %. The failing price changes from run to run, because Hypothesis tries random inputs. Lesson 7 of 11"],[28,"strategy","What to test and how much","Kind of test Checks Speed How many Unit test One function or class, dependencies mocked Milliseconds Most of them Integration test Several parts together: code plus a real database or file Seconds Some End to end test The whole program as a user uses it (for web apps: Selenium or Playwright) Slow A few for the main journeys Always test Example for discount(price, percent) A normal case discount(80, 50) is 40 The edges of every range percent 0 and 100, price 0 Just outside the range percent −1 and 101 raise ValueError Awkward values floats like 0.3, very large prices Every bug you fixed A regression test that would have caught it Remember This shape, many fast unit tests at the bottom and few slow end to end tests at the top, is called the test pyramid. Run the fast tests on every save and all tests in CI (see the Git and CI/CD subject). Lesson 8 of 11"],[28,"maths","The maths: coverage, boundaries and binary search","Line coverageExam favourite coverage = lines run / total lines × 100 % 17 of 19 lines run: 17 / 19 ≈ 89.5 %. Boundary value tests for a range a to bExam favourite test a − 1, a, b, b + 1 (and one value inside) percent from 0 to 100: test −1, 0, 100, 101 and 50, five tests. Combinations of k options with v values each all combinations = vk 3 settings with 4 values each: 64 tests. Pairwise testing covers every pair of values with far fewer, about v2 = 16. Binary search steps (the LeetCode set)Exam favourite steps ≤ ⌈log2(n + 1)⌉ n = 1000 sorted items: at most 10 steps. This is also how git bisect finds the commit that broke a test. Chance a random test hits a bug that needs one value out of N 1 − (1 − 1/N)t after t tests N = 1000 and t = 100: about 9.5 %. Picking edge cases on purpose beats random luck. Worked example: how many commits does git bisect test? A test passed 1000 commits ago and fails now. Each check halves the commits that could contain the bug. 210 = 1024 is the first power of 2 that is at least 1000. at most 10 test runs to find the commit that broke it Lesson 9 of 11"],[28,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths Write unittest test cases with assertEqual and assertRaises and run them. Use setUp, subTest and skip, read failures and errors, and write pytest tests with fixtures, parametrize, approx and raises. Mock dependencies with Mock, side_effect and patch, measure coverage, test edge cases on purpose, and work test first. Design for testability (dependency injection, pure functions), write property based tests with Hypothesis, use mutation testing to check that the tests themselves catch bugs, and build a pyramid of unit, integration and end to end tests that runs in CI. Beginner Write and run tests. Intermediate Use fixtures, parametrize and mocks. Expert Design testable code and test the tests. Lesson 10 of 11"],[28,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","Binary search is the classic place for off by one bugs, so it is the perfect place to practise testing edge cases: empty lists, one item, the first and the last position. Two problems call an outside API (isBadVersion and guess), and the downloadable solutions inject a fake one, the same idea as a Mock. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 704 Binary Search Easy Halve the range each step Keep low and high. Look at the middle. The famous bug is an off by one in the loop condition, so test arrays of length 0, 1 and 2. 35 Search Insert Position Easy Binary search for the first place not smaller When the loop ends, low is where target belongs. Test before the first item and after the last. 278 First Bad Version Easy Binary search on a yes or no question Exactly what git bisect does with a failing test. The is_bad check is passed in, so a test can give a fake one. 374 Guess Number Higher or Lower Easy Binary search against an API The guess API answers -1, 1 or 0. In tests we inject a fake API, like a Mock with a side effect. 34 Find First and Last Position of Element in Sorted Array Medium Two binary searches Find the first index not smaller than target and the first index bigger than target. bisect_left and bisect_right do exactly this. 162 Find Peak Element Medium Walk uphill with binary search If the middle is lower than its right neighbour, a peak lies to the right. Otherwise it lies at the middle or to the left. 153 Find Minimum in Rotated Sorted Array Medium Compare the middle with the right end If the middle is bigger than the last item, the drop (and the minimum) is to the right. 33 Search in Rotated Sorted Array Medium One half is always sorted Check which half is in order, then whether target lies inside it. Many edge cases: test a rotation at every position. 875 Koko Eating Bananas Medium Binary search on the answer Try a speed in the middle and count the hours needed. Too slow: go faster. Fast enough: try slower. 4 Median of Two Sorted Arrays Hard Binary search the cut in the shorter array Cut both arrays so the left parts hold half of all items and every left item is at most every right item. Then the median comes from the four items around the cuts. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 11 of 11"],[28,"recap","Chapter summary","A test arranges inputs, acts by calling the code and asserts the result. A runner reports PASSED, FAILED or ERROR. unittest: TestCase classes, assertEqual, assertRaises, assertAlmostEqual, setUp for a fresh start, subTest for loops, skip. Mocks replace slow or outside dependencies: return_value, side_effect, assert_called_once_with, patch and patch.object. pytest: plain assert, fixtures, parametrize, approx, raises, tmp_path, monkeypatch, and detailed failure messages. Coverage shows which lines ran, not whether the checks are good. Hypothesis searches for inputs that break a rule. Test normal cases, edges, just outside the edges and every fixed bug. Many unit tests, fewer integration and end to end tests. Next: 3.3 shows logging and debugging: finding out what a running program is doing. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: a tiny test runner, a currency converter tested with Mock, and a TestCase that must catch three buggy versions of a Stack.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[29,"","Logging and debugging","log levels, loggers, handlers, formatters, habits, dictConfig, rotation, print, assert and pdb debugging. · logging · logger · handler · formatter · basicConfig · dictConfig · RotatingFileHandler · pdb · breakpoint · assert · debugging Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Advanced Python›Step 3: Professional code: style, testing and logging›Logging and debugging Step 3 · Topic 3 of 3 · Advanced Know what your program is doing and find out why it went wrong. Learn the five log levels, basicConfig, loggers, handlers and formatters, the logger tree, good logging habits, dictConfig and rotating files, and debugging with print, assert and the pdb debugger. About 70 min9 lessons1 animation6 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Logging: a diary for your program 2Loggers, handlers and formatters 3Good logging habits 4Configuring logging for a whole app 5Debugging: print, assert and pdb 6The maths: log volume, rotation and bisecting 7From beginner to expert 8LeetCode practice 9Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 9"],[29,"idea","Logging: a diary for your program","Source: Python 3.14 documentation of logging, the Logging HOWTO and Cookbook, logging.config, logging.handlers, pdb, breakpoint() (PEP 553) and the assert statement. The pdb session is a real run. What it is Logging means your program writes short messages about what it is doing, each with a level that says how important it is: DEBUG, INFO, WARNING, ERROR or CRITICAL. Debugging is finding out why a program does the wrong thing, with tools such as print, logging, assert and the pdb debugger. Why we need it When a program runs on a server at 3 am, nobody is watching the screen. print messages vanish, cannot be switched off without editing code, and say nothing about when or where they came from. Logs are kept in files, can be turned up or down without changing code, and carry the time, module and line, so you can find out later what happened. How it works You ask for a logger by name and call logger.info(...) and friends. The logger drops messages below its level. Passing messages become LogRecords that go to handlers (screen, file, email, network), each with its own level and a formatter that decides the text layout. The journey of a log recordPick the level of the message, the level of the logger and the level of the console handler. The file handler takes everything from DEBUG up. Step 0 of 6 Message level Logger level Console handler level Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this An INFO message reaches the file but not the console, whose level is WARNING. With the logger at WARNING a DEBUG message stops at once: no record is even made. An ERROR passes everywhere. Python import loggingimport syslogging.basicConfig(stream=sys.stdout, level=logging.INFO, force=True, format=\"%(levelname)s %(name)s: %(message)s\")logging.debug(\"hidden: DEBUG is below INFO\")logging.info(\"app started\")logging.warning(\"disk is 91% full\")logging.error(\"could not save the file\")logging.critical(\"database is down\")print(logging.getLevelName(logging.WARNING), logging.WARNING, logging.getLogger().level) Output INFO root: app started WARNING root: disk is 91% full ERROR root: could not save the file CRITICAL root: database is down WARNING 30 20 Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 basics.py. Level Number Use it for DEBUG 10 Details for developers: values, steps INFO 20 Normal events: started, saved, user logged in WARNING 30 Something odd that the program can handle (the default level) ERROR 40 An operation failed CRITICAL 50 The program may not be able to go on In the exam Without basicConfig the root logger shows only WARNING and above, on stderr. basicConfig sets the level, the format and the stream in one call, but only the first time, unless you pass force=True. Lesson 2 of 9"],[29,"loggers","Loggers, handlers and formatters","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 loggers.py. Part Job Example Logger Named entry point, first level check, part of a tree split by dots logging.getLogger(\"shop.cart\") Handler Sends records somewhere, with its own level StreamHandler, FileHandler, RotatingFileHandler, SMTPHandler Formatter Turns a record into text \"%(asctime)s %(levelname)s %(name)s: %(message)s\" Filter Extra yes or no rule for a logger or handler Drop records that contain a password Remember Loggers form a tree by name: shop.cart is a child of shop, which is a child of root. A record goes to the handlers of its logger and then, unless propagate is False, up to the parents. Add handlers in one place (often the root or the top package logger) and just call getLogger(__name__) in every module. Format field Gives %(asctime)s Date and time %(levelname)s DEBUG, INFO, ... %(name)s Logger name %(message)s Your message with its arguments filled in %(funcName)s, %(lineno)d, %(filename)s Where the call was made %(process)d, %(threadName)s Which process and thread Lesson 3 of 9"],[29,"practice","Good logging habits","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 habits.py. In the exam Pass values as arguments: logger.debug(\"total %s\", total). The text is only built if the record passes the level checks. An f-string is always built, even when the message is thrown away, as the first slow __str__ line shows. Common mistake Inside except, logger.exception(\"...\") logs at ERROR level and adds the full traceback. Never log passwords, card numbers or personal data: logs are read by many people and kept for a long time. Reporting what a program does print logging Turn off without editing code No Yes, change the level Time, module and line Only if you add them by hand Yes, from the format Where it goes The screen Screen, files, servers, email, all at once Importance All the same Five levels Good for Quick checks while learning and the normal output of a script Everything in programs that run for real Bottom line: print is for the output your user asked for. Messages about how the program is doing belong in logging. Lesson 4 of 9"],[29,"config","Configuring logging for a whole app","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 config.py. Handler (logging.handlers) Keeps logs under control by RotatingFileHandler(path, maxBytes, backupCount) Starting a new file at maxBytes and keeping backupCount old ones TimedRotatingFileHandler(path, when=\"midnight\", backupCount=7) Starting a new file every day (or hour) and keeping a week QueueHandler and QueueListener Writing from a background thread, so slow disks do not slow the program SysLogHandler, HTTPHandler Sending logs to a central server Remember Configure logging once, at program start, with dictConfig (or a file read into a dict, often YAML or TOML). Libraries must never call basicConfig: they only call getLogger(__name__), and the application decides what to show. dictConfig switches off loggers that already exist unless you set \"disable_existing_loggers\": False. Lesson 5 of 9"],[29,"debugging","Debugging: print, assert and pdb","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 debugging.py. The same bug in a function, found with the pdb debugger. Put breakpoint() in the code, or start the program with python -m pdb, and you can stop on any line and look at the variables: buggy.pypython 1def average(scores):2 total = 03 for s in scores:4 total = s5 return total / len(scores)678print(average([70, 80, 90])) A pdb sessionshell $ python -m pdb buggy.py > /tmp/demo/buggy.py(1)<module>() -> def average(scores): (Pdb) b 4 Breakpoint 1 at /tmp/demo/buggy.py:4 (Pdb) c > /tmp/demo/buggy.py(4)average() -> total = s (Pdb) p s, total (70, 0) (Pdb) c > /tmp/demo/buggy.py(4)average() -> total = s (Pdb) p s, total (80, 70) (Pdb) cl 1 Deleted breakpoint 1 at /tmp/demo/buggy.py:4 (Pdb) n > /tmp/demo/buggy.py(3)average() -> for s in scores: (Pdb) n > /tmp/demo/buggy.py(4)average() -> total = s (Pdb) p total 80 (Pdb) q Remember On the second stop total is 70, not 70 + 0: the line replaces total instead of adding to it. The fix is total += s. pdb command Does b 4 (break) Stop at line 4. b alone lists breakpoints c (continue) Run until the next breakpoint n (next) Run the current line, stepping over calls s (step) Step into a function call p expr, pp expr Print (pretty print) a value l, ll List the code around here, or the whole function w (where), u, d Show the call stack, move up or down in it r (return) Run until the current function returns cl 1 (clear) Remove breakpoint 1 q (quit) Stop debugging Ways to find a bug print / f\"{x=}\" logging pdb or an IDE debugger Set up None Once per project One breakpoint() line See values at any line Only where you printed Only where you logged Yes, and change them Works on a server with nobody watching No Yes No Best for Quick checks Problems in running programs Puzzling bugs you can reproduce Bottom line: reproduce the bug first, then use a debugger to watch it happen. Keep logging in place for the bugs you cannot reproduce. Common mistake assert checks a condition you believe is always true and raises AssertionError if not. Python started with -O skips every assert, so never use assert to check user input. Raise ValueError for that. Lesson 6 of 9"],[29,"maths","The maths: log volume, rotation and bisecting","Log volumeExam favourite bytes = lines per second × bytes per line × seconds 50 lines a second of 200 bytes for a day (86,400 s) is 864,000,000 bytes, about 864 MB. Disk used by a rotating logExam favourite max size = (backupCount + 1) × maxBytes maxBytes 1 MB with backupCount 4: at most 5 MB, for ever. Levels shown for a given level L shown = levels with number ≥ L With WARNING (30): WARNING, ERROR and CRITICAL, 3 of 5. Sampling lines kept = events / N when keeping 1 in N 1 in 100 of 2,000,000 requests: 20,000 lines. Bisecting a bug (halving the suspect code) checks = ⌈log2 n⌉ 64 suspect lines: 6 checks with well placed prints or breakpoints. Error budget allowed failures = requests × (1 − target) 1,000,000 requests at 99.99 % success allow 100 failures. 120 ERROR lines use 120 % of the budget. Worked example: how much disk does a month of logs need? 50 lines per second × 200 bytes = 10,000 bytes per second. One day: 10,000 × 86,400 = 864,000,000 bytes = 864 MB. Thirty days: 864 MB × 30. 25,920 MB, about 25.9 GB, unless you rotate and delete old files Lesson 7 of 9"],[29,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths Use logging.basicConfig and the five levels, and debug with print and f\"{x=}\". Use named loggers with getLogger(__name__), handlers with their own levels and formatters, %s arguments and logger.exception. Configure a whole app with dictConfig and rotating files, use pdb and breakpoint() with breakpoints, stepping and the call stack, and use assert correctly. Design logs for machines: structured JSON lines with request ids, central collection (ELK, Loki, CloudWatch), sampling and error budgets. Use QueueHandler for slow sinks, post mortem debugging with pdb.pm(), and remote or conditional breakpoints. Beginner Log with levels and print debug. Intermediate Build loggers, handlers and formatters. Expert Configure, debug and run logging in production. Lesson 8 of 9"],[29,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","These problems are log analysis: sorting log lines, finding three key card swipes inside an hour, active minutes per user, servers with no requests in a time window, the closest two log times, and turning order records into a report table. It is exactly the code you write to find problems in real logs. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 937 Reorder Data in Log Files Medium A sort key that splits two kinds of logs Letter logs come first, sorted by content then identifier. Digit logs keep their order: sorted is stable, so give them all the same key. 1598 Crawler Log Folder Easy Track the depth \"../\" goes up one level (never above the top), \"./\" stays, anything else goes one level down. 2933 High-Access Employees Medium Group times per person, then a window of 3 Turn \"hhmm\" into minutes, sort each person's times, and check whether any 3 in a row fit within 59 minutes. 1604 Alert Using Same Key-Card Three or More Times in a One Hour Period Medium The same window idea on access logs Group by name, sort the minutes, and alert if times[i] − times[i − 2] is at most 60. 1817 Finding the Users Active Minutes Medium A set of minutes per user Collect each user's different minutes in a set. The size of the set is that user's active minutes. Count users per size. 2747 Count Zero Request Servers Medium Sort logs and queries, slide a window Sort logs by time. Answer queries in time order with two pointers that keep only logs inside [q − x, q] and a count per server. 1169 Invalid Transactions Medium Parse, then compare every pair A transaction is invalid above 1000, or when the same name has another one in a different city within 60 minutes. 539 Minimum Time Difference Medium Turn log times into minutes and sort Change each \"HH:MM\" into minutes, sort, and compare neighbours. Do not forget the gap that wraps around midnight: first + 1440 − last. 1418 Display Table of Food Orders in a Restaurant Medium Turn order records into a report table Count orders in a dict of table number to a dict of food to count. The header is \"Table\" plus the sorted food names. Rows go in number order of the tables. 165 Compare Version Numbers Medium Compare number by number Split on dots, turn each part into an int (so 01 equals 1) and treat missing parts as 0. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 9 of 9"],[29,"recap","Chapter summary","Five levels: DEBUG 10, INFO 20, WARNING 30 (the default), ERROR 40, CRITICAL 50. basicConfig for small scripts. Named loggers with getLogger(__name__), handlers with their own levels and formatters for real programs. Records pass the logger level, go to its handlers (each filters again), then propagate to parent loggers. Pass values as %s arguments so text is only built when needed. Use logger.exception inside except. Never log secrets. Configure once with dictConfig. Rotate files so logs do not fill the disk. Libraries only call getLogger. Debug with f\"{x=}\", assert for things that must always be true (off with -O), and pdb or breakpoint() to stop and look. Next: Step 4 is about speed: complexity and the data structures in collections, heapq and bisect. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: decide whether a level is shown, capture the log lines of a function, and summarise a log file.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[30,"","Complexity, timing and profiling","Big O, operation costs, timeit, cProfile, memory, amortized cost and caching. · complexity · Big O · timeit · cProfile · profiling · tracemalloc · amortized · lru_cache · master theorem · performance Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Advanced Python›Step 4: Speed: complexity and smart data structures›Complexity and profiling Step 4 · Topic 1 of 2 · Advanced Find out how your code behaves when the data grows. Learn Big O, the cost of list, dict and set operations, measuring with timeit, finding the slow part with cProfile, memory with tracemalloc, amortized cost and caching. About 75 min10 lessons1 animation7 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Big O: how the work grows 2Finding the Big O of code 3Measuring with timeit 4Finding the slow part with cProfile 5Memory: getsizeof, tracemalloc and generators 6Amortized cost and trading memory for time 7The maths behind Big O 8From beginner to expert 9LeetCode practice 10Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 10"],[30,"idea","Big O: how the work grows","Source: Python 3.14 documentation of timeit, cProfile, pstats, tracemalloc, sys.getsizeof and functools.lru_cache, and the TimeComplexity page of the Python wiki. Terminal outputs are real runs with CPython 3.14. What it is Time complexity says how the number of steps of an algorithm grows when the input gets bigger. We write it in Big O notation: O(1), O(log n), O(n), O(n log n), O(n²). Space complexity says the same about memory. Why we need it A program that is quick with 100 items can take hours with 1,000,000. Timing it on your laptop does not tell you that, because the time depends on the computer and the data you tried. Big O tells you, before you run anything, how the program will behave when the data grows. How it works Count the basic steps (a comparison, an addition, a lookup) as a function of n, the size of the input. Keep only the part that grows fastest and drop constant factors: 3n² + 5n + 7 becomes O(n²). Then check with real measurements (timeit) and find the slow part with a profiler (cProfile). How the work grows with nPick the size of the data. Each step adds one Big O class to the chart and to the table of steps. Step 0 of 7 Size of the data n Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this With n = 16 the classes look close together. With n = 1000, n² is 1,000,000 steps but log n is only 10. Python def linear_search(items, target): steps = 0for item in items: steps += 1if item == target:breakreturn stepsdef binary_search(items, target): steps = 0 low = 0 high = len(items) - 1while low <= high: steps += 1 middle = (low + high) // 2if items[middle] == target:breakif items[middle] < target: low = middle + 1else: high = middle - 1return stepsdef count_pairs(items): steps = 0for a in items:for b in items: steps += 1return stepsprint(\"n linear binary pairs\")for n in [10, 100, 1000]: data = list(range(n)) target = n - 1# the last item: the worst case for linear searchprint(f\"{n:<6} {linear_search(data, target):<7} {binary_search(data, target):<7} {count_pairs(data)}\") Output n linear binary pairs 10 10 4 100 100 100 7 10000 1000 1000 10 1000000 Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 counting.py. Remember Look at the table: for 1000 items linear search needs 1000 steps, binary search only 10, and comparing every pair needs 1,000,000. The same computer, the same data size, and a 100,000 times difference. Big O Name n = 1000 needs about Typical code O(1) constant 1 step d[key], x in a set, list[i], append O(log n) logarithmic 10 steps binary search, bisect, heap push O(n) linear 1,000 steps one loop, x in a list, sum, max, copy O(n log n) linearithmic 10,000 steps sorted(), list.sort() O(n²) quadratic 1,000,000 steps a loop inside a loop over the same data O(2n) exponential more than the atoms in the universe trying every subset, naive recursion Lesson 2 of 10"],[30,"rules","Finding the Big O of code","Four rules Drop constants. 2n + 10 steps is O(n). Big O is about the shape of the growth, not the exact count. Keep the biggest term. n² + n is O(n²), because for large n the n² part is almost all the work. Loops one after another add. A loop over n, then another loop over n: O(n + n) = O(n). Loops inside loops multiply. A loop over n inside a loop over m: O(n × m). Over the same list: O(n²). Common mistake Hidden loops count too. x in my_list, my_list.index(x), my_list.remove(x), my_list.insert(0, x), \"\".join and slicing a[i:j] each walk through items. Putting one of them inside a loop is the most common way Python code becomes quadratic by accident. Operation list dict / set collections.deque Get or set by index or key O(1) O(1) on average O(n) in the middle, O(1) at the ends x in container O(n) O(1) on average O(n) Add at the end O(1) amortized (append) O(1) on average (d[k] = v, add) O(1) (append) Add or remove at the front O(n) (insert(0, x), pop(0)) not ordered by position O(1) (appendleft, popleft) Remove a given value O(n) (remove) O(1) on average (del d[k], discard) O(n) Sort O(n log n) sorted(d) O(n log n) not supported in place Copy, len O(n), O(1) O(n), O(1) O(n), O(1) In the exam Average case versus worst case: a dict lookup is O(1) on average, but if many keys had the same hash it could take O(n). Python adds random salt to str hashes so attackers cannot cause this on purpose. For interviews and exams, dict and set operations are O(1). Checking whether a value is in a collection list set x in c O(n): looks at items one by one O(1): jumps to the right place with the hash Memory Small About 3 to 4 times more Keeps order and duplicates Yes No Items must be Anything Hashable (no lists or dicts) Best for Data you go through in order Many \"is it there\" questions Bottom line: when you ask \"is x there\" inside a loop, turn the list into a set once before the loop. That one line often changes O(n²) into O(n). Lesson 3 of 10"],[30,"timing","Measuring with timeit","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 timing.py. timeit from the terminal (real run, CPython 3.14)shell $ python -m timeit -s \"nums = list(range(10_000))\" \"9_999 in nums\" 5000 loops, best of 5: 60.9 usec per loop $ python -m timeit -s \"nums = set(range(10_000))\" \"9_999 in nums\" 10000000 loops, best of 5: 19.4 nsec per loop Remember 60.9 microseconds against 19.4 nanoseconds: the set is about 3,000 times faster for 10,000 numbers. -s is the setup, which runs once and is not timed. timeit chooses how many loops to run and reports the best of 5 rounds. Rule for fair timing Why Use timeit or time.perf_counter, not time.time perf_counter is the most precise clock, and timeit repeats the code many times Take the minimum of several runs Other programs only ever slow a run down, so the fastest run is closest to the truth Time with realistic sizes With 10 items everything is fast. Differences show up with large n Keep setup out of the timed part Building test data is not what you want to measure Compare on the same computer Times are not comparable across machines, ratios are Ways to measure time time.time() time.perf_counter() timeit Precision Can be coarse, and jumps if the clock is changed The best clock available Uses perf_counter Repeats the code No No Yes, many loops, several rounds Best for Dates and times of day Timing one block in a real program Comparing two small pieces of code Bottom line: timeit for small comparisons, perf_counter around a step of a real program, and time.time only for the time of day. Lesson 4 of 10"],[30,"profile","Finding the slow part with cProfile","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 profiling.py. The same idea from the terminal. This is the file and a real run of python -m cProfile -s cumulative dedupe.py: dedupe.pypython 1def slow_unique(items):2 result = []3 for item in items:4 if item not in result:5 result.append(item)6 return result789def fast_unique(items):10 return list(dict.fromkeys(items))111213def main():14 data = [i % 2000 for i in range(20_000)]15 slow = slow_unique(data)16 fast = fast_unique(data)17 print(len(slow), len(fast), slow == fast)181920main() cProfile (real run, first lines)shell $ python -m cProfile -s cumulative dedupe.py 2000 2000 True 2010 function calls in 0.138 seconds Ordered by: cumulative time ncalls tottime percall cumtime percall filename:lineno(function) 1 0.000 0.000 0.138 0.138 {built-in method builtins.exec} 1 0.000 0.000 0.138 0.138 dedupe.py:1(<module>) 1 0.001 0.001 0.138 0.138 dedupe.py:13(main) 1 0.136 0.136 0.136 0.136 dedupe.py:1(slow_unique) 1 0.000 0.000 0.000 0.000 dedupe.py:9(fast_unique) 1 0.000 0.000 0.000 0.000 {built-in method fromkeys} 2000 0.000 0.000 0.000 0.000 {method 'append' of 'list' objects} Column Means ncalls How many times the function was called tottime Time spent in the function itself, without the functions it calls cumtime Time in the function including everything it calls percall tottime / ncalls and cumtime / ncalls Remember slow_unique takes 0.136 of the 0.138 seconds, so that is the only function worth fixing. Speeding up anything else could save at most 0.002 seconds. Always profile before you optimise: the slow part is often not where you expect. Profilers cProfile (built in) line_profiler py-spy, Scalene Shows Time per function Time per line A live sampling of a running program, Scalene also memory Install Nothing pip install line_profiler pip install py-spy or scalene Slows the program Somewhat A lot, only on chosen functions Hardly at all (sampling) Best for Finding the slow function Finding the slow line inside it Programs already running in production Bottom line: start with cProfile to find the function, then line_profiler if you need the exact line. Lesson 5 of 10"],[30,"memory","Memory: getsizeof, tracemalloc and generators","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 memory.py. In the exam sys.getsizeof is shallow. For a list it counts the list and its slots, not the objects inside, which is why a list holding a huge number has the same size as one holding a small number. tracemalloc counts every allocation made while it runs, so it shows the real memory a piece of code needed. Way to save memory How Generators instead of lists sum(x * x for x in data) makes one value at a time __slots__ in classes with many objects No per object __dict__, often half the memory array.array or NumPy for numbers Raw numbers in one block instead of separate int objects Read files line by line for line in f instead of f.read() or f.readlines() del large objects you no longer need The memory can be reused as soon as nothing refers to it Lesson 6 of 10"],[30,"amortized","Amortized cost and trading memory for time","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 amortized.py. Remember Most appends cost one step. Now and then the array is full and must copy everything, which costs n steps. But because the space doubles each time, the copies add up to less than n in total, so the average (amortized) cost per append stays about 1. That is why list.append is called O(1) amortized. Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 memo.py. Remember fib_slow(20) makes 21,891 calls because it works out the same values again and again. With lru_cache each value from 0 to 20 is computed once: 21 calls. We spend a little memory (21 stored answers) to save a huge amount of time, which is the idea behind dynamic programming. Making code faster A better algorithm or data structure Caching (lru_cache, a dict) Small tweaks (local variables, fewer function calls) Typical gain 10 to 1,000,000 times Big when the same inputs repeat 10 % to 2 times Cost Thinking Memory, and care with changing data Harder to read code When First When profiling shows repeated work Last, and only in the hot spot Bottom line: fix the Big O first. A better algorithm beats any amount of tuning. Lesson 7 of 10"],[30,"maths","The maths behind Big O","Definition of Big OExam favourite f(n) = O(g(n)) if there are numbers c > 0 and n0 with f(n) ≤ c · g(n) for every n ≥ n0 3n + 5 = O(n): take c = 4 and n0 = 5, since 3n + 5 ≤ 4n when n ≥ 5. Big Omega and Big Theta Ω(g) is a lower bound, Θ(g) means both O(g) and Ω(g) Comparison sorting is Ω(n log n): no comparison sort can do better. Sum of a loop inside a loopExam favourite 1 + 2 + ... + n = n(n + 1) / 2 = O(n²) Comparing each item with the ones after it: 1000 items give 499,500 comparisons. Geometric sum (doubling)Exam favourite 1 + 2 + 4 + ... + n = 2n − 1 < 2n Copies for 1024 appends with doubling: 1 + 2 + ... + 512 = 1023, about 1 per append. Halving steps = ⌊log2 n⌋ + 1 Binary search in 1,000,000 sorted items: at most 20 steps. Master theoremExam favourite T(n) = a·T(n/b) + O(nd) gives O(nd) if d > logb a, O(nd log n) if d = logb a, O(nlogb a) if d < logb a Merge sort: a = 2, b = 2, d = 1. log2 2 = 1 = d, so O(n log n). Time estimate seconds ≈ steps / (about 107 simple Python steps per second) n = 105 with O(n²) is 1010 steps: about 1000 s. With O(n log n) it is 1.7 × 106: about 0.17 s. Worked example: is this fast enough for 100,000 items? The code compares every pair: n(n − 1) / 2 comparisons. n = 100,000 gives 100,000 × 99,999 / 2 = 4,999,950,000, about 5 × 109. At about 107 simple steps per second in Python: 5 × 109 / 107 = 500 seconds. Sorting first (n log2 n ≈ 100,000 × 17 = 1.7 × 106) and then one scan takes well under a second. O(n²): about 8 minutes. O(n log n): under 1 second Going deeper: Why log n has no base in Big O log2 n = log10 n / log10 2, so logs with different bases differ only by a constant factor, and Big O drops constant factors. O(log2 n), O(ln n) and O(log10 n) are the same class. Lesson 8 of 10"],[30,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths Know O(1), O(n) and O(n²), see that a loop inside a loop is slow, and use a set for \"is it there\" questions. Work out the Big O of functions with the four rules, know the costs of list, dict, set and deque operations, measure with timeit and find the slow function with cProfile. Use amortized analysis, the master theorem and recurrences, measure memory with tracemalloc, and trade memory for time with caching and dynamic programming. Reason about lower bounds (Ω(n log n) for comparison sorting), cache misses and memory layout, hash collision attacks, sampling profilers in production, and when C extensions, NumPy or PyPy change the constants. Beginner Spot slow loops and pick the right container. Intermediate Analyse, measure and profile. Expert Prove bounds and tune real systems. Lesson 9 of 10"],[30,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","Every problem here has an easy O(n²) answer that is too slow for the real limits (often n up to 100,000). The downloadable solutions use the ideas of this chapter: a dict or set for O(1) lookups, prefix sums, sliding windows, meet in the middle, a sieve and in place tricks for O(1) memory. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 219 Contains Duplicate II Easy Remember the last index of each value A dict from value to its last index answers \"where did I see this before\" in O(1), so one pass is enough: O(n) instead of comparing all pairs. 2006 Count Number of Pairs With Absolute Difference K Easy Count what you have seen For each number, the partners seen so far are x − k and x + k. A dict of counts finds them in O(1), so the whole list takes O(n). 1010 Pairs of Songs With Total Durations Divisible by 60 Medium Group by remainder Two songs fit when their remainders mod 60 add up to 0 or 60. Count remainders seen so far: O(n) instead of O(n²). 454 4Sum II Medium Meet in the middle Trying all four lists is O(n4). Count every a + b in a dict (O(n²)), then look up −(c + d) for every pair of the other two lists (O(n²)). 974 Subarray Sums Divisible by K Medium Prefix sums and remainders A subarray sum is prefix[j] − prefix[i]. It is divisible by k when both prefixes have the same remainder. Count remainders: O(n) instead of trying all O(n²) subarrays. 209 Minimum Size Subarray Sum Medium A sliding window Grow the window on the right, shrink it from the left while the sum is big enough. Each index enters and leaves once, so it is O(n). 1004 Max Consecutive Ones III Medium A window with at most k zeros Move the right end one step at a time. When the window holds more than k zeros, move the left end until it does not. O(n) time, O(1) memory. 204 Count Primes Medium Sieve of Eratosthenes Testing each number by division is about O(n √n). The sieve crosses out multiples of each prime starting at p², which is O(n log log n). 287 Find the Duplicate Number Medium Treat the list as a linked list Without changing the list and with O(1) memory: i points to nums[i]. The repeated value is where a cycle starts. Floyd's slow and fast pointers find it in O(n). 41 First Missing Positive Hard Use the list itself as the table O(n) time and O(1) extra memory: swap each value v into place v − 1. Then the first place i that does not hold i + 1 gives the answer. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 10 of 10"],[30,"recap","Chapter summary","Big O says how steps grow with n. Drop constants, keep the biggest term. Loops in a row add, loops inside loops multiply. O(1) dict and set lookups, O(log n) binary search, O(n) one pass, O(n log n) sorting, O(n²) nested loops. Watch for hidden loops: x in list, remove, insert(0, x) and slicing inside a loop make code quadratic. Measure with timeit (best of several runs, realistic n). Find the slow function with cProfile before optimising. getsizeof is shallow. tracemalloc shows real memory. Generators save memory. list.append is O(1) amortized because space doubles. lru_cache trades memory for time. Next: Next you meet the containers that make code fast: collections, heapq and bisect. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: find a pair with a given sum in one pass, guess the Big O from step counts, and write a caching decorator that counts hits and misses.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[31,"","collections, heapq and bisect","Counter, deque, namedtuple, OrderedDict, ChainMap, heapq priority queues and bisect. · collections · Counter · deque · namedtuple · OrderedDict · ChainMap · defaultdict · heapq · priority queue · bisect · binary search Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Advanced Python›Step 4: Speed: complexity and smart data structures›collections, heapq, bisect Step 4 · Topic 2 of 2 · Advanced Use the containers that make common jobs fast and short. Learn Counter, deque, namedtuple, OrderedDict, ChainMap and defaultdict, priority queues with heapq, and binary search in sorted lists with bisect. About 75 min10 lessons1 animation6 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1The right container for the job 2Counter: counting made easy 3deque: a fast queue with two ends 4namedtuple, OrderedDict, ChainMap and defaultdict 5heapq: a priority queue in a list 6bisect: binary search in a sorted list 7The maths: heap indexes, heights and costs 8From beginner to expert 9LeetCode practice 10Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 10"],[31,"idea","The right container for the job","Source: Python 3.14 documentation of collections, heapq and bisect. What it is The collections module adds containers that do one job better than a plain list or dict: Counter for counting, deque for queues, namedtuple for small records, OrderedDict and ChainMap for special dicts, and defaultdict for missing keys. heapq turns a list into a priority queue and bisect searches and inserts in a sorted list. Why we need it Chapter 4.1 showed that the wrong container makes code slow: list.pop(0) is O(n), finding the smallest of a changing list again and again is O(n) each time, and searching a sorted list item by item wastes its order. These tools make those jobs O(1) or O(log n), and the code shorter and clearer too. How it works Each one is written in C and tested by millions of programs. You import it and use it like a normal list or dict: deque has append and pop at both ends, Counter is a dict of counts, heapq is a set of functions that keep any list in heap order, and bisect uses binary search. You need to Use Speed of the key operation Count things collections.Counter O(1) per item Group things by a key collections.defaultdict(list) O(1) per item Add and remove at both ends (a queue) collections.deque O(1) at each end Keep only the last n items deque(maxlen=n) O(1), old items drop off A small read only record with names namedtuple or a frozen dataclass Same as a tuple Always get the smallest (or largest) item heapq O(log n) push and pop, O(1) peek The k largest or smallest of many heapq.nlargest, heapq.nsmallest O(n log k) Search or insert in a sorted list bisect O(log n) search, O(n) insert Look through several dicts in order collections.ChainMap O(number of dicts) Lesson 2 of 10"],[31,"counter","Counter: counting made easy","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 counting.py. Remember Counter is a dict subclass, so everything a dict can do works too. Unlike a dict, a missing key gives 0 instead of KeyError. most_common(k) returns the k most common items with their counts, and items with the same count keep the order in which they were first seen. Counter operation Result c + d Adds counts, keeps only positive ones c - d Subtracts counts, keeps only positive ones c & d The smaller count of each item (intersection) c | d The bigger count of each item (union) c.update(items), c.subtract(items) Change counts in place (subtract can go below 0) c.elements() Each item repeated count times c.total() Sum of all counts (3.10+) Lesson 3 of 10"],[31,"deque","deque: a fast queue with two ends","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 queues.py. In the exam A deque (say \"deck\") is a double ended queue. Adding and removing at either end is O(1). A list does the same at the end, but list.insert(0, x) and list.pop(0) must shift every other item, which is O(n). Remember Breadth first search (BFS) always takes the oldest path from the front of the queue and adds new paths at the back. That is why it finds the path with the fewest steps first, and why it needs a deque, not a list. A queue (first in, first out) list with pop(0) collections.deque queue.Queue Remove from the front O(n) O(1) O(1) Index in the middle O(1) O(n) Not supported Safe between threads No append and popleft are thread safe Yes, with blocking get and put Best for Stacks and arrays Queues, BFS, sliding windows, last n items Passing work between threads (step 5) Bottom line: use deque for queues inside one program and queue.Queue to pass work between threads. Lesson 4 of 10"],[31,"records","namedtuple, OrderedDict, ChainMap and defaultdict","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 records.py. Small records tuple namedtuple dataclass dict Fields by name No, only p[0] Yes, p.x Yes, p.x Yes, p[\"x\"] Can change No No (use _replace) Yes, unless frozen=True Yes Memory Smallest Same as a tuple More, unless slots=True Most Methods and defaults No defaults=, few methods Yes, any No Best for Two or three values that go together Read only rows, like CSV lines Objects with behaviour Data whose keys are not known in advance Bottom line: namedtuple for light read only records, dataclass when you need methods, defaults or changes. In the exam Since Python 3.7 every dict keeps insertion order. OrderedDict still has extras: move_to_end(key), popitem(last=False) for the oldest item, and == compares order too. That makes it the classic tool for an LRU cache. Remember ChainMap does not copy anything. It searches the dicts one after another, so a change to user is seen at once. Writes go to the first dict only. It is how Python looks up names: locals, then globals, then builtins. Lesson 5 of 10"],[31,"heap","heapq: a priority queue in a list","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 heaps.py. In the exam A heap is a list where every item is smaller than or equal to its two children: heap[i] ≤ heap[2i + 1] and heap[i] ≤ heap[2i + 2]. So heap[0] is always the smallest. The rest is NOT sorted. heappush and heappop are O(log n), heapify is O(n), and looking at heap[0] is O(1). Remember Put tuples like (priority, count, task) in a heap. Tuples compare item by item, so the priority decides first. The count (from itertools.count()) breaks ties, so two tasks with the same priority never have to be compared, which would fail for objects that cannot be compared. Getting the smallest item many times min(list) each time Keep the list sorted heapq Add an item O(1) O(n) (insort shifts items) O(log n) Take the smallest O(n) O(1) from the end of a reverse sorted list O(log n) Good when You ask only once or twice You rarely add, often read in order You keep adding and taking, as in a scheduler or Dijkstra Bottom line: a heap is the best all round choice when items keep arriving and you keep taking the most urgent one. Lesson 6 of 10"],[31,"bisect","bisect: binary search in a sorted list","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 sorted_lists.py. Function Returns bisect_left(a, x) The first index where x could go: before any equal items bisect_right(a, x) (also bisect) The index after the last item equal to x insort(a, x) Puts x into a, keeping it sorted (search O(log n), insert O(n)) key=func (3.10+) Compare func(item) instead of the item, without building a new list Remember bisect_right(a, x) − bisect_left(a, x) is how many times x appears. Counting values in a range or looking up which band a number falls into (grades, tax brackets, prices) are classic uses. Going deeper: Sorted containers for big data insort is O(n) because a list must shift items. For millions of inserts the third party package sortedcontainers (SortedList) keeps O(log n) inserts. In the standard library, a heap is the tool when you only ever need the smallest item. Lesson 7 of 10"],[31,"maths","The maths: heap indexes, heights and costs","Heap indexesExam favourite parent(i) = (i − 1) // 2, children 2i + 1 and 2i + 2 Index 4 has parent 1 and children 9 and 10. Height of a heapExam favourite h = ⌊log2 n⌋ 1,000,000 items: 19 levels below the top, so a push makes at most 19 swaps. heapify is O(n) work ≤ ∑ (n / 2h+1) · h < n Most items are near the bottom and move only a little, so the total is less than n swaps. Top k with a heapExam favourite O(n log k) instead of O(n log n) for a full sort k = 10 of n = 106: about 3.3 × 106 steps against 2 × 107. Binary search comparisons ≤ ⌈log2(n + 1)⌉ 1000 sorted items: at most 10 comparisons. Front removals from a list shifts = (n − 1) + (n − 2) + ... + 1 = n(n − 1) / 2 Emptying 10,000 items with pop(0): about 5 × 107 shifts. With deque.popleft: 0. Worked example: the top 3 of 1,000,000 scores Sorting: n log2 n ≈ 106 × 20 = 2 × 107 comparisons. heapq.nlargest(3, scores) keeps a heap of only 3 items while it reads all the scores. Each score costs at most log2 3 ≈ 1.6 steps: about 1.6 × 106 in total. Over 10 times less work, and only 3 items in memory Lesson 8 of 10"],[31,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths Count with Counter, group with defaultdict(list), and use deque for a queue. Use Counter arithmetic, deque(maxlen), namedtuple, OrderedDict for LRU, heapq for priority queues and top k, and bisect for sorted lookups. Explain heap order and its index maths, heapify in O(n), BFS with a deque, two heap medians, monotonic deques for sliding windows and patience sorting with bisect. Choose between heaps, balanced trees and skip lists, build indexed priority queues with lazy deletion, use heapq.merge for external sorting of data bigger than memory, and know where the C code of these modules gives its speed. Beginner Count, group and queue. Intermediate Prioritise, search and keep records. Expert Design data structure algorithms. Lesson 9 of 10"],[31,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","Each problem is solved by one tool of this chapter: heaps for the k largest, closest or heaviest items and for a running median, bisect for sorted lookups and the longest increasing subsequence, Counter for frequencies, and a deque for the sliding window maximum. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1046 Last Stone Weight Easy A max-heap with negative numbers Always smash the two heaviest stones. heapq is a min-heap, so store −weight. (Python 3.14 also has heappop_max, but LeetCode may run an older Python.) 1351 Count Negative Numbers in a Sorted Matrix Easy bisect on each row Each row goes down from left to right. With key=lambda v: −v it goes up, so bisect finds where the negatives start in O(log n) per row. 215 Kth Largest Element in an Array Medium A min-heap of size k Keep the k largest numbers seen so far in a min-heap. Its top is the kth largest. O(n log k) time and O(k) memory. 973 K Closest Points to Origin Medium nsmallest with a key Compare squared distances x² + y² (no square root needed, the order is the same). heapq.nsmallest does it in O(n log k). 692 Top K Frequent Words Medium Counter, then sort by two keys Count with Counter. Sort by count going down, then by the word going up for ties: key=(-count, word). 2300 Successful Pairs of Spells and Potions Medium Sort once, then bisect for each spell A spell s needs a potion of at least ⌈success / s⌉. Sort the potions and bisect_left gives how many are too weak. O((n + m) log m). 300 Longest Increasing Subsequence Medium Patience sorting with bisect tails[i] is the smallest last number of an increasing run of length i + 1. Each number replaces the first tail that is not smaller, found with bisect_left. O(n log n). 621 Task Scheduler Medium Count the most common task The most common task (count m) needs m − 1 gaps of n slots after it. Tasks that share that top count fill the last row. The answer is never less than the number of tasks. 239 Sliding Window Maximum Hard A monotonic deque of indexes Keep indexes whose values go down from front to back. Drop smaller values from the back before adding, and the front once it leaves the window. Each index enters and leaves once: O(n). 295 Find Median from Data Stream Hard Two heaps A max-heap (negative numbers) holds the smaller half, a min-heap the bigger half. Keep their sizes equal or the low half one bigger. The median is at the tops. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 10 of 10"],[31,"recap","Chapter summary","Counter counts in one line, gives 0 for missing keys, and supports most_common, total and + - & |. deque adds and removes at both ends in O(1). Use it for queues, BFS and (with maxlen) the last n items. namedtuple gives read only records with names. OrderedDict has move_to_end and popitem(last=False) for LRU caches. ChainMap layers dicts. heapq keeps a list in heap order: heap[0] is the smallest, push and pop are O(log n), heapify is O(n). Tuples set priorities. bisect finds positions in a sorted list in O(log n): bisect_left, bisect_right, insort and key=. Next: Step 5 is about doing many things at once: threads, processes and asyncio. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: the most common words with Counter, the hot potato game with a deque, and the fewest meeting rooms with a heap.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[32,"","Threads and processes","threads, races and locks, worker queues, futures, the GIL, processes and free-threaded Python. · concurrency · parallelism · threading · Lock · race condition · deadlock · queue · concurrent.futures · GIL · multiprocessing · free-threaded Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Advanced Python›Step 5: Doing many things at once: threads, processes and asyncio›Threads and processes Step 5 · Topic 1 of 2 · Advanced Make programs do several things at once. Learn concurrency and parallelism, threads and their races, locks and the other sync tools, worker queues, concurrent.futures, the GIL, multiprocessing, and the free-threaded build of Python 3.14. About 80 min9 lessons1 animation7 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Doing many things at once 2Threads: start, join and share 3Race conditions and locks 4Worker threads with queue and futures 5The GIL and processes 6The maths: speedup, Amdahl and pool sizes 7From beginner to expert 8LeetCode practice 9Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 9"],[32,"idea","Doing many things at once","Source: Python 3.14 documentation of threading, queue, concurrent.futures and multiprocessing, the GIL, and the free-threaded build (PEP 703 and PEP 779). Terminal outputs are real runs on a 4 core machine. What it is Concurrency means a program deals with several jobs during the same period, switching between them. Parallelism means jobs really run at the same moment on different CPU cores. A thread is a path of execution inside one process that shares its memory. A process is a separate running program with its own memory. Why we need it Most programs spend time waiting: for the network, the disk or a database. While one job waits, the computer could do another. Other programs do heavy calculations, and a modern computer has 4 to 16 cores that sit idle if the program uses only one. Doing jobs one after another wastes both. How it works Threads let other work run while one job waits for I/O. Processes run Python code on several cores at once. concurrent.futures gives both the same simple interface: a pool of workers, submit or map, and futures that hold results. Three workers on a timelineEach column is one unit of time. Pick the kind of work and see what the GIL (global interpreter lock) allows to run at the same time. Step 0 of 10 Kind of work Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this While one thread waits for the network, another can run. Threads help I/O bound work. CPU work with threads: only one runs at a time, so it takes as long as doing the jobs one by one. Processes each have their own GIL and run on different cores at the same time. Python import timefrom concurrent.futures importThreadPoolExecutordef download(page): time.sleep(0.5) # stands for waiting on the networkreturn f\"{page}: done\"pages = [\"home\", \"shop\", \"cart\", \"help\"]start = time.perf_counter()results = [download(p) for p in pages]one_by_one = time.perf_counter() - startstart = time.perf_counter()withThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=4) as pool: results = list(pool.map(download, pages)) # map keeps the order of the inputswith_threads = time.perf_counter() - startprint(results)print(f\"one by one: about {round(one_by_one, 1)} s\")print(f\"4 threads: about {round(with_threads, 1)} s\") Output ['home: done', 'shop: done', 'cart: done', 'help: done'] one by one: about 2.0 s 4 threads: about 0.5 s Run this one on your own computer. Browsers cannot start threads or processes, so the in page Python cannot run it. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 io_threads.py. Remember Four downloads of 0.5 seconds take 2 seconds one by one but only 0.5 seconds with four threads, because the waiting happens at the same time. While a thread waits for I/O (sleep, network, files) it lets go of the GIL, so others can run. Kind of work Example Best tool I/O bound: mostly waiting Downloading pages, calling APIs, reading many files, database queries Threads (or asyncio, chapter 5.2) CPU bound: mostly calculating Image processing, prime numbers, simulations, parsing huge files Processes (or the free-threaded build, or NumPy which releases the GIL) A mix A web crawler that also parses pages Threads or asyncio for I/O, a process pool for the heavy parts Lesson 2 of 9"],[32,"threads","Threads: start, join and share","Run this one on your own computer. Browsers cannot start threads or processes, so the in page Python cannot run it. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 thread_basics.py. threading Does Thread(target=f, args=(...), name=...) Makes a thread that will run f(*args) t.start() Starts it. It runs next to the main thread t.join(timeout=None) Waits until t has finished Thread(..., daemon=True) A background thread that does not keep the program alive threading.current_thread(), active_count() Which thread is running this line, how many threads exist Common mistake Every thread in a process shares the same global variables and objects. That makes it easy to pass data, and also easy to break data: two threads changing the same object at the same time is a race condition. Lesson 3 of 9"],[32,"locks","Race conditions and locks","Run this one on your own computer. Browsers cannot start threads or processes, so the in page Python cannot run it. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 race.py. Common mistake counter = counter + 1 is really three steps: read, add, write. If another thread writes between your read and your write, your write wipes out its update. time.sleep(0) here forces a switch at the worst moment, but real programs hit the same bug by chance, often only under heavy load. Tool Use it to Lock Let only one thread at a time run a block (use with lock:) RLock A lock the same thread may take again, for recursive code Semaphore(n) Let at most n threads in at once, for example 5 connections Event Let threads wait until one sets a flag: event.set(), event.wait() Condition Wait until some state is true, with wait_for and notify_all Barrier(n) Wait until n threads have all arrived queue.Queue Pass data between threads safely, with no lock code of your own In the exam A deadlock happens when thread 1 holds lock A and waits for lock B while thread 2 holds B and waits for A. Neither can go on. Prevent it by always taking locks in the same order, holding them briefly, or using a timeout. Lesson 4 of 9"],[32,"queues","Worker threads with queue and futures","Run this one on your own computer. Browsers cannot start threads or processes, so the in page Python cannot run it. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 workers.py. Remember queue.Queue does the locking for you. Producers put jobs in, worker threads get them out. get() waits when the queue is empty. Putting one None per worker is a common way to tell workers to stop. Run this one on your own computer. Browsers cannot start threads or processes, so the in page Python cannot run it. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 futures.py. Ways to run work in threads threading.Thread queue.Queue + workers ThreadPoolExecutor Get results back By hand (shared dict or list) A results queue Futures: result(), map, as_completed Errors in the thread Printed and lost You must catch them Raised again by result() Limit how many run at once By hand Number of workers max_workers Best for One long background job Long running pipelines Most everyday jobs Bottom line: start with ThreadPoolExecutor. Use queues for pipelines, and bare Thread objects only for one background job. Lesson 5 of 9"],[32,"gil","The GIL and processes","The GIL (global interpreter lock) lets only one thread run Python bytecode at a time in the normal CPython build. It keeps the interpreter simple and fast for one thread, but it means threads cannot use several cores for pure Python work. This file does the same CPU heavy work one by one, with threads and with processes: cpu_bound.pypython 1import time2from concurrent.futures import ProcessPoolExecutor, ThreadPoolExecutor345def count_primes(limit):6 count = 07 for n in range(2, limit):8 is_prime = True9 d = 210 while d * d <= n:11 if n % d == 0:12 is_prime = False13 break14 d += 115 if is_prime:16 count += 117 return count181920def timed(label, run):21 start = time.perf_counter()22 results = run()23 print(f\"{label:<12} {time.perf_counter() - start:5.2f} s {results}\")242526if __name__ == \"__main__\":27 jobs = [200_000] * 428 timed(\"one by one\", lambda: [count_primes(n) for n in jobs])29 with ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=4) as pool:30 timed(\"4 threads\", lambda: list(pool.map(count_primes, jobs)))31 with ProcessPoolExecutor(max_workers=4) as pool:32 timed(\"4 processes\", lambda: list(pool.map(count_primes, jobs))) Line 26 Child processes import this file again, so the start code must sit under the if __name__ == \"__main__\" guard. Line 31 A ProcessPoolExecutor has the same interface as the thread pool. The function and its arguments are pickled and sent to the child processes. Real runs: normal CPython and the free-threaded buildshell $ python3.14 cpu_bound.py one by one 1.30 s [17984, 17984, 17984, 17984] 4 threads 1.37 s [17984, 17984, 17984, 17984] 4 processes 0.40 s [17984, 17984, 17984, 17984] $ python3.14t -c \"import sys; print(sys._is_gil_enabled())\" False $ python3.14t cpu_bound.py one by one 1.16 s [17984, 17984, 17984, 17984] 4 threads 0.35 s [17984, 17984, 17984, 17984] 4 processes 0.41 s [17984, 17984, 17984, 17984] In the exam With the GIL, 4 threads are no faster than one (1.37 s against 1.30 s). 4 processes are 3.3 times faster. The free-threaded build python3.14t (officially supported since 3.14) has no GIL, so there 4 threads are fast too. shared.pypython 1import multiprocessing as mp2import os345def square(n):6 return n * n789def report(q, name):10 q.put(f\"{name} ran in its own process: {os.getpid() != os.getppid()}\")111213if __name__ == \"__main__\": # needed: child processes import this file again14 with mp.Pool(processes=4) as pool:15 print(pool.map(square, range(10)))1617 q = mp.Queue()18 workers = [mp.Process(target=report, args=(q, f\"worker {i}\")) for i in range(2)]19 for w in workers:20 w.start()21 for w in workers:22 w.join()23 print(sorted(q.get() for _ in workers))24 print(\"start method:\", mp.get_start_method()) multiprocessing (real run)shell $ python3.14 shared.py [0, 1, 4, 9, 16, 25, 36, 49, 64, 81] ['worker 0 ran in its own process: True', 'worker 1 ran in its own process: True'] start method: forkserver Threads or processes Threads Processes Memory Shared: easy to pass data, easy to break it Separate: data is pickled and copied Pure Python CPU work on many cores No, with the GIL (yes in python3.14t) Yes Start up cost Small Bigger (a new interpreter) A crash Can take the whole program down Stays inside one process Best for I/O bound work CPU bound work Bottom line: threads for waiting, processes for calculating. Measure, because the overhead of processes can be bigger than the gain for small jobs. Going deeper: Start methods and new options in 3.14 multiprocessing can start children by fork (copy the parent, Unix only), spawn (start a fresh Python, the default on Windows and macOS) or forkserver (ask a small clean server process to fork, the default on Linux since 3.14). With spawn and forkserver the child imports your main file, which is why the __main__ guard is required. Python 3.14 also added concurrent.interpreters and InterpreterPoolExecutor: several interpreters in one process, each with its own GIL (PEP 734). Lesson 6 of 9"],[32,"maths","The maths: speedup, Amdahl and pool sizes","Speedup and efficiencyExam favourite S = T1 / Tn, E = S / n 1.30 s alone, 0.40 s with 4 processes: S = 3.25, E = 0.8125 (about 81 %). Amdahl's lawExam favourite S(n) = 1 / ((1 − p) + p / n) p = 0.9 of the work can run in parallel, n = 4: S = 1 / (0.1 + 0.225) ≈ 3.08. The limit of Amdahl's law S(∞) = 1 / (1 − p) With p = 0.9, even a million cores give at most 10 times. Gustafson's law S(n) = n − (1 − p)(n − 1) If the problem grows with the cores: n = 4, p = 0.9 gives 3.7. Threads for I/O workExam favourite threads ≈ cores × (1 + wait time / compute time) 4 cores, 90 ms waiting and 10 ms computing per job: 4 × 10 = 40 threads. Little's law L = λ × W 200 requests per second that each take 0.25 s: 50 requests in progress, so about 50 workers. Lost updates final = expected − lost 4 threads × 100 additions should give 400. Every overwrite loses one. Worked example: is it worth buying 8 more cores? A job takes 100 s. Profiling shows 80 s can run in parallel, so p = 0.8. With 4 cores: S = 1 / (0.2 + 0.8 / 4) = 1 / 0.4 = 2.5, so 40 s. With 12 cores: S = 1 / (0.2 + 0.8 / 12) = 1 / 0.2667 ≈ 3.75, so about 26.7 s. The 20 s serial part never shrinks. Making it faster helps more than more cores. 8 more cores: from 40 s to about 26.7 s, never below 20 s Lesson 7 of 9"],[32,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths Run I/O jobs in a ThreadPoolExecutor and know that threads help with waiting, not with calculating. Start and join threads, protect shared data with a Lock, pass work through queue.Queue, use futures with as_completed, and use a ProcessPoolExecutor with the __main__ guard for CPU work. Explain the GIL, race conditions and deadlocks, choose between Event, Condition, Semaphore and Barrier, and size pools with Amdahl and Little. Use the free-threaded build and know which C extensions are ready for it, use subinterpreters, shared memory (multiprocessing.shared_memory) and lock free designs, and reason about memory ordering and contention. Beginner Use a thread pool for I/O. Intermediate Write correct threaded and multiprocess code. Expert Design and tune parallel systems. Lesson 8 of 9"],[32,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","The first six are LeetCode's concurrency problems: they are solved with exactly the tools of this chapter (Event, Semaphore, Barrier, Condition and Lock), and each download has a small runner that starts the threads in different orders to test them. The last four simulate how a CPU or a pool of servers schedules jobs, using heaps. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1114 Print in Order Easy Two Events second waits for an Event that first sets, and third waits for one that second sets. The threads may start in any order. 1115 Print FooBar Alternately Medium Two Semaphores take turns foo starts with one permit and bar with none. Each thread waits on its own semaphore and then gives a permit to the other one. 1116 Print Zero Even Odd Medium Three Semaphores zero prints 0 and then wakes odd or even depending on the next number. odd and even print and wake zero again. 1117 Building H2O Medium Semaphores for places, a Barrier for the group A Semaphore(2) lets two hydrogen in, a Semaphore(1) one oxygen. A Barrier(3) makes the three wait for each other before they bond. 1195 Fizz Buzz Multithreaded Medium One Condition and a shared counter Every thread waits until the current number is its kind (or the end is reached), prints, moves the counter on and wakes the others with notify_all. 1226 The Dining Philosophers Medium Always take the lower numbered fork first A deadlock needs a cycle of waiting. If every philosopher takes the lower numbered fork first, no cycle can form, so somebody can always eat. 1834 Single-Threaded CPU Medium A heap of ready tasks Sort tasks by arrival. Push every task that has arrived into a heap of (time needed, index), run the shortest, and jump ahead in time when nothing is ready. 1882 Process Tasks Using Servers Medium A heap of free servers and a heap of busy ones Free servers by (weight, index), busy servers by (free time, weight, index). Before each task, move servers that are free again. If none is free, jump to the time the first one is. 2402 Meeting Rooms III Hard Free rooms and busy rooms in two heaps Take meetings by start time. Free every room whose meeting has ended. Use the lowest free room, or delay the meeting until the room that frees first is free. 1094 Car Pooling Medium Changes at each point, then a running total At each pickup the load goes up and at each drop it goes down. Sort the points and add up the changes. Too many people at any point means False. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 9 of 9"],[32,"recap","Chapter summary","Threads share memory inside one process. Processes have their own memory and their own interpreter. I/O bound work: threads (they let go of the GIL while waiting). CPU bound work: processes, or python3.14t without the GIL. ThreadPoolExecutor and ProcessPoolExecutor share one interface: submit, map, as_completed and futures whose result() raises errors. Shared data needs a Lock (use with lock:). queue.Queue passes work safely. Take locks in one order to avoid deadlocks. Process code needs the if __name__ == \"__main__\" guard, and its data must be picklable. Amdahl's law: the serial part limits the speedup, S(n) = 1 / ((1 − p) + p / n). Next: Next, asyncio: thousands of waiting jobs in one thread with async and await. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: the speedup from Amdahl's law, a counter that always releases its lock, and finding threads stuck in a deadlock.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[33,"","asyncio: async and await","coroutines, the event loop, gather and TaskGroup, timeouts, limits, queues and httpx. · asyncio · async · await · coroutine · event loop · gather · TaskGroup · Semaphore · asyncio.Queue · httpx · to_thread Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Advanced Python›Step 5: Doing many things at once: threads, processes and asyncio›asyncio Step 5 · Topic 2 of 2 · Advanced Handle thousands of waiting jobs in one thread. Learn coroutines, async and await, the event loop, gather, tasks and TaskGroup, timeouts and cancelling, Semaphore limits, asyncio.Queue pipelines, async generators, real requests with httpx, and how to avoid blocking the loop. About 80 min9 lessons1 animation6 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1asyncio: many waits in one thread 2Tasks, TaskGroup and as_completed 3Timeouts, cancelling and limits 4Pipelines with asyncio.Queue 5Real network code, and blocking calls 6The maths: waits that overlap 7From beginner to expert 8LeetCode practice 9Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 9"],[33,"idea","asyncio: many waits in one thread","Source: Python 3.14 documentation of asyncio, PEP 492 (async and await), PEP 525 (async generators) and httpx. The httpx run is real, against a local server that answers after 0.2 s. What it is asyncio runs many tasks in one thread. A function written with async def is a coroutine: it can pause at every await while it waits for something, and an event loop runs another coroutine in the meantime. Why we need it A web server or a crawler may have thousands of network connections open, each waiting most of the time. One thread per connection costs memory and needs locks. asyncio handles them all in one thread, and because a task can only be paused at an await, shared data cannot change in the middle of a line. How it works You write normal looking code with await in front of anything that waits: await asyncio.sleep(1), await client.get(url). The event loop keeps a queue of tasks that are ready to run, and a list of tasks that wait for a time or for network data. asyncio.run(main()) starts the loop. asyncio.gather and TaskGroup run coroutines at the same time. Inside the asyncio event loopOne thread, one loop. Watch tasks move between running, ready and waiting while the clock advances. Step 0 of 10 How main runs the two teas Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this gather: both teas wait at the same time, 0.3 s in total. Awaiting one coroutine after another: 0.5 s. One time.sleep blocks the whole loop. Python import asyncioimport timeasync def make_tea(name, seconds):print(f\"{name}: start\") await asyncio.sleep(seconds) # waiting: the loop may run other code nowprint(f\"{name}: ready\")return nameasync def main(): start = time.perf_counter() await make_tea(\"green\", 0.3) # one after the other await make_tea(\"black\", 0.2)print(f\"in a row: {round(time.perf_counter() - start, 1)} s\") start = time.perf_counter() results = await asyncio.gather(make_tea(\"green\", 0.3), make_tea(\"black\", 0.2))print(results, f\"together: {round(time.perf_counter() - start, 1)} s\")coro = make_tea(\"test\", 0)print(type(coro).__name__) # calling an async def does not run it yetcoro.close()asyncio.run(main()) Output coroutine green: start green: ready black: start black: ready in a row: 0.5 s green: start black: start black: ready green: ready ['green', 'black'] together: 0.3 s Run this one on your own computer. The Python in this page lives inside the browser's own event loop, so asyncio.run cannot start a loop of its own here. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 async_basics.py. Common mistake Calling make_tea(...) only creates a coroutine object, it runs nothing. The code runs when it is awaited, or when it is wrapped in a task (gather, create_task, TaskGroup). Forgetting await is the most common asyncio bug: Python warns \"coroutine ... was never awaited\". Word Means async def f() Defines a coroutine function. Calling it gives a coroutine object await x Pause this coroutine until x is done, and let the loop run others. Only allowed inside async def Task A coroutine scheduled on the loop. It runs on its own, next to the others Event loop The scheduler: runs ready tasks one at a time and wakes waiting ones asyncio.run(main()) Start a loop, run main until it finishes, close the loop. Call it once, at the top Lesson 2 of 9"],[33,"tasks","Tasks, TaskGroup and as_completed","Run this one on your own computer. The Python in this page lives inside the browser's own event loop, so asyncio.run cannot start a loop of its own here. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 tasks_demo.py. Tool Use it to asyncio.create_task(coro) Start a coroutine in the background and get a Task you can await later await asyncio.gather(a, b, ...) Run several and get their results as a list, in the order given async with asyncio.TaskGroup() as g Start tasks with g.create_task. If one fails, the others are cancelled and errors come out as an ExceptionGroup asyncio.as_completed(aws) Get results in the order they finish asyncio.wait(aws, return_when=...) Wait for the first one, the first error, or all, and get done and pending sets Common mistake Keep a reference to every task you create with create_task (in a list or a set), or use a TaskGroup. The loop holds tasks only weakly, so a task nobody refers to can disappear before it finishes. Running coroutines together gather TaskGroup (3.11+) Results A list in order task.result() for each task When one fails The error is raised, the others keep running (unless you cancel them) The others are cancelled and all errors arrive in an ExceptionGroup Catching errors try / except try / except* Best for A fixed list of jobs, return_exceptions=True to collect errors Structured code where no task may be left running Bottom line: TaskGroup for new code, because no task can be forgotten. gather is still fine for a simple list of results. Lesson 3 of 9"],[33,"control","Timeouts, cancelling and limits","Run this one on your own computer. The Python in this page lives inside the browser's own event loop, so asyncio.run cannot start a loop of its own here. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 timeouts.py. In the exam Cancelling a task raises CancelledError inside it at its current await. Clean up in finally blocks, and do not swallow CancelledError without raising it again, or the task cannot be stopped. Remember A Semaphore limits how many tasks do something at once. Without it, gathering 10,000 downloads opens 10,000 connections at the same moment, which servers often punish by refusing or blocking you. Lesson 4 of 9"],[33,"queues","Pipelines with asyncio.Queue","Run this one on your own computer. The Python in this page lives inside the browser's own event loop, so asyncio.run cannot start a loop of its own here. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 pipeline.py. Remember asyncio.Queue is like queue.Queue, but put and get are awaited. With maxsize=2 the producer waits when two items are waiting. This back pressure stops a fast producer from filling memory while a slow consumer catches up. Going deeper: Async generators and async for An async def with yield is an async generator: async for x in agen() awaits each value, and [x async for x in agen()] is an async comprehension. async with calls the awaitable methods __aenter__ and __aexit__, which is how httpx.AsyncClient and database connections clean up. Lesson 5 of 9"],[33,"real","Real network code, and blocking calls","A small test server that answers every request after 0.2 seconds, and a client that fetches 20 pages one by one and then with asyncio and httpx (pip install httpx): slow_server.pypython 1import time2from http.server import BaseHTTPRequestHandler, ThreadingHTTPServer345class SlowHandler(BaseHTTPRequestHandler):6 def do_GET(self):7 time.sleep(0.2) # pretend the server needs 0.2 s8 body = f\"page {self.path}\".encode()9 self.send_response(200)10 self.send_header(\"Content-Length\", str(len(body)))11 self.end_headers()12 self.wfile.write(body)1314 def log_message(self, *args): # keep the terminal quiet15 pass161718class Server(ThreadingHTTPServer):19 request_queue_size = 64 # accept many connections at once202122Server((\"127.0.0.1\", 8001), SlowHandler).serve_forever() fetch_all.pypython 1import asyncio2import time34import httpx56URLS = [f\"http://127.0.0.1:8001/{n}\" for n in range(20)]789def fetch_one_by_one():10 with httpx.Client() as client:11 return [client.get(url).text for url in URLS]121314async def fetch_together():15 async with httpx.AsyncClient() as client:16 responses = await asyncio.gather(*(client.get(url) for url in URLS))17 return [r.text for r in responses]181920start = time.perf_counter()21pages = fetch_one_by_one()22print(f\"one by one: {time.perf_counter() - start:.2f} s for {len(pages)} pages\")2324start = time.perf_counter()25pages = asyncio.run(fetch_together())26print(f\"asyncio: {time.perf_counter() - start:.2f} s for {len(pages)} pages\")27print(pages[:3]) Line 14 One AsyncClient shares connections between all the requests. Line 15 client.get(url) gives coroutines. gather runs all 20 at the same time and keeps the results in order. Real run (CPython 3.14, httpx 0.28)shell $ python3 slow_server.py & $ python3 fetch_all.py one by one: 4.09 s for 20 pages asyncio: 0.26 s for 20 pages ['page /0', 'page /1', 'page /2'] Remember 20 × 0.2 s = 4 s one by one. With asyncio all 20 waits overlap, so it takes about one wait: 0.26 s, 16 times faster, in one thread. Run this one on your own computer. asyncio.to_thread starts a thread, which browsers do not allow. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 mixing.py. Common mistake A normal blocking call (time.sleep, requests.get, a slow calculation) inside a coroutine freezes the whole loop. Use the async version (asyncio.sleep, httpx.AsyncClient, aiofiles), or push the blocking call to a thread with asyncio.to_thread, or to a process pool with loop.run_in_executor. Waiting for many things at once Threads asyncio Switches between jobs Any time (the OS decides) Only at await Shared data Needs locks Safe between awaits Cost of one job A thread: about 8 MB of address space reserved for its stack A task: a few KB Thousands of connections Heavy Easy Libraries Any normal library Needs async libraries (httpx, aiohttp, asyncpg) CPU heavy work Not with the GIL Never: it blocks the loop Bottom line: asyncio for many network connections with async libraries, threads to speed up existing blocking code, processes for CPU work. Lesson 6 of 9"],[33,"maths","The maths: waits that overlap","Time one by oneExam favourite T = n × t 20 requests of 0.2 s: 4 s. Time with all at onceExam favourite T ≈ max(ti) + overhead 20 requests of 0.2 s: about 0.2 s (measured 0.26 s). With a limit of k at onceExam favourite T ≈ ⌈n / k⌉ × t 20 requests, at most 5 at once, 0.2 s each: 4 rounds, 0.8 s. Throughput requests per second = k / t 5 at once, 0.2 s each: 25 requests per second. Little's law for open connections L = λ × W 1000 requests per second with 0.5 s waits: 500 tasks waiting at any moment. Memory for many waiters memory ≈ n × size of one waiter 10,000 tasks of about 2 KB is about 20 MB. 10,000 threads reserve about 80 GB of address space for stacks. Worked example: how long do 1000 API calls take? Each call waits 0.5 s for the server. One by one: 1000 × 0.5 = 500 s. The API allows at most 50 calls at a time, so use asyncio.Semaphore(50). ⌈1000 / 50⌉ = 20 rounds of 0.5 s. 20 × 0.5 = 10 s, fifty times faster, in one thread Lesson 7 of 9"],[33,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths Write async def and await, start with asyncio.run, and run several coroutines with gather. Use create_task, TaskGroup and except*, timeouts, cancellation, Semaphore limits, asyncio.Queue pipelines, and httpx.AsyncClient for real requests. Explain how the event loop schedules tasks, find blocking calls, move them to to_thread or executors, write async generators and async context managers. Build servers with asyncio streams or protocols, tune with uvloop, design structured concurrency and cancellation safe code, and understand how await works on top of generators (send, throw and yield from). Beginner Await and gather. Intermediate Control tasks, limits and timeouts. Expert Design async systems. Lesson 8 of 9"],[33,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","LeetCode has no asyncio problems in Python. Its async problems are in JavaScript (promises, sleep, time limits, debounce, pools and generators), and each one maps straight onto asyncio. The downloads solve them in Python with gather, wait_for, call_later, a shared iterator and a hand driven generator, and say so at the top of each problem. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 2621 Sleep Easy await asyncio.sleep LeetCode asks this one in JavaScript. In Python asyncio the same idea is a coroutine that awaits asyncio.sleep(millis / 1000). 2723 Add Two Promises Easy Await both at the same time A JavaScript problem. In Python, gather awaits both coroutines at the same time and returns their values, so the total wait is the longer one, not the sum. 2648 Generate Fibonacci Sequence Easy An endless generator A JavaScript generator problem. In Python, a while True loop with yield gives the numbers one at a time, and itertools.islice takes as many as you need. 2649 Nested Array Generator Medium yield from for the nested lists A JavaScript generator problem. Walk the list, and for a nested list hand over to a recursive generator with yield from. 2637 Promise Time Limit Medium asyncio.wait_for A JavaScript problem. Wrap the coroutine in asyncio.wait_for with the limit in seconds, and turn the TimeoutError into the message the problem asks for. 2721 Execute Asynchronous Functions in Parallel Medium gather keeps the order A JavaScript problem (write Promise.all without Promise.all). In Python, gather starts all of them, keeps the results in the order given and raises the first error. 2622 Cache With Time Limit Medium Store the value with its end time A JavaScript design problem. Keep key: (value, expires_at). A key counts only while the clock is before its end time. The clock is passed in so the tests do not have to wait. 2627 Debounce Medium Cancel the waiting call and schedule a new one A JavaScript problem. With asyncio, loop.call_later schedules the call. A new call cancels the handle of the one still waiting. 2636 Promise Pool Medium n workers share one iterator A JavaScript problem. Start n worker coroutines that all take the next function from the same iterator. At most n functions run at any moment. 2650 Design Cancellable Function Hard Drive a generator by hand, race each wait against cancel A JavaScript problem, and exactly how await works inside: send each result into the generator, throw errors into it, and throw a Cancelled error if cancel() wins the race. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 9 of 9"],[33,"recap","Chapter summary","async def makes a coroutine. It runs only when awaited or wrapped in a task. await pauses it and lets the loop run others. asyncio.run(main()) once at the top. gather or TaskGroup to run coroutines at the same time. create_task for background work. Waits overlap: n waits of t seconds take about t, not n × t. A Semaphore limits how many run at once. asyncio.timeout and wait_for stop slow work. task.cancel() raises CancelledError at the current await. asyncio.Queue builds pipelines with back pressure. Async generators and async with exist too. Never block the loop: use async libraries such as httpx, or asyncio.to_thread for blocking calls. Next: Step 6 is the standard library toolbox: regular expressions, files and formats, sqlite3 and tkinter. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: fix a missing await, write an async generator with an async comprehension, and build a tiny round robin scheduler like the one inside an event loop.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[34,"","Regular expressions","regex syntax, the re functions, groups, sub and split, flags, greedy and lazy, lookarounds. · regex · regular expression · re · search · findall · groups · sub · split · lookahead · greedy · lazy Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Advanced Python›Step 6: The standard library toolbox: text, data, databases and GUIs›Regular expressions Step 6 · Topic 1 of 4 · Advanced Find, check and change text with patterns. Learn the regex syntax, search, match, fullmatch, findall and finditer, groups and named groups, sub and split, flags and compile, greedy and lazy repeats, lookarounds, and how the engine backtracks. About 75 min10 lessons1 animation6 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Regular expressions: patterns for text 2search, match, fullmatch, findall, finditer 3Groups: pulling parts out 4sub, split, flags and compile 5Greedy, lazy, anchors and lookarounds 6Parsing real text 7The maths: languages and automata 8From beginner to expert 9LeetCode practice 10Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 10"],[34,"idea","Regular expressions: patterns for text","Source: Python 3.14 documentation of the re module and the Regular Expression HOWTO. What it is A regular expression (regex) is a small pattern that describes text, such as \"three digits, a dash, four digits\". Python's re module uses patterns to find, check, split and replace text. Why we need it Finding a phone number, checking an email address, pulling the date out of a log line or cleaning extra spaces would take many lines of loops and if statements. A pattern does it in one line, and the same pattern works in Python, JavaScript, SQL databases, editors and command line tools. How it works Write the pattern as a raw string, r\"...\", so backslashes reach re unchanged. Special characters stand for groups of characters (\\d is any digit) or for repetition ({3} means exactly three times). The engine tries the pattern at each place in the text, from left to right, and returns a match object with the text and its position. How the regex engine walks through the textPick a pattern. The engine tries each start, matches token by token, and backs off when the rest does not fit. Step 0 of 16 Pattern and text Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this Greedy .+ runs to the end of the text and then backs off to the LAST >. Lazy .+? stops at the FIRST > that lets the match finish. \\b refuses the \"cat\" inside \"concat\". Python import retext = \"Call 555-1234 or 555-9876 before 6 pm.\"m = re.search(r\"\\d{3}-\\d{4}\", text) # the first match anywhereprint(m.group(), m.start(), m.end(), m.span())print(re.match(r\"\\d\", text)) # match only looks at the very startprint(re.fullmatch(r\"\\d{3}-\\d{4}\", \"555-1234\") isnotNone) # the WHOLE string must matchprint(re.findall(r\"\\d{3}-\\d{4}\", text)) # every match, as stringsfor m in re.finditer(r\"\\d+\", text): # every match, as match objectsprint(m.group(), m.span())print(len(\"\\n\"), len(r\"\\n\")) # a raw string keeps the backslash Output 555-1234 5 13 (5, 13) None True ['555-1234', '555-9876'] 555 (5, 8) 1234 (9, 13) 555 (17, 20) 9876 (21, 25) 6 (33, 34) 1 2 Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 basics.py. Pattern Matches Pattern Matches . Any character except a newline \\d, \\D A digit, not a digit \\w, \\W A word character (letter, digit, _), not one \\s, \\S Whitespace, not whitespace [abc], [a-z] One of these characters, a range [^0-9] Any character NOT in the set ^, $ Start, end of the text (or line with MULTILINE) \\b A word edge *, +, ? 0 or more, 1 or more, 0 or 1 {3}, {2,5} Exactly 3, from 2 to 5 a|b a or b (...) A group, also captured \\. \\$ \\( A real dot, dollar, bracket *?, +? The lazy versions Finding things in text str methods (in, find, split, replace) re module Fixed text Best: simple and fast Works, but more than needed Patterns (any digits, optional parts, either or) Many lines of code One pattern Readability Very clear Can be cryptic: use re.VERBOSE and comments Nested structures (HTML, JSON, brackets) No No: use a real parser Bottom line: use str methods for fixed text, regex for patterns, and a parser (html.parser, json, xml) for nested formats. Lesson 2 of 10"],[34,"functions","search, match, fullmatch, findall, finditer","Function Looks Returns re.search(p, s) Anywhere, first match A match object or None re.match(p, s) Only at the start A match object or None re.fullmatch(p, s) The whole string A match object or None re.findall(p, s) Everywhere A list of strings (or tuples of groups) re.finditer(p, s) Everywhere An iterator of match objects m.group(), m.start(), m.end(), m.span() On a match object The text and where it is In the exam Use fullmatch to check input such as a postcode or a username. search would also accept \"xx12345yy\" because it finds 12345 inside it. Lesson 3 of 10"],[34,"groups","Groups: pulling parts out","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 groups.py. Syntax Meaning (...) Capture this part as group 1, 2, ... (?P<name>...) A named group, read with m[\"name\"] or m.group(\"name\") (?:...) Group without capturing, for | or repeats \\1, (?P=name) The same text that group 1 or name matched m.groups(), m.groupdict() All groups as a tuple, named groups as a dict Common mistake findall changes what it returns when the pattern has groups: with one group it returns that group's text, with several it returns tuples. Use (?:...) for groups you only need for | or repeats. Lesson 4 of 10"],[34,"subsplit","sub, split, flags and compile","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 subsplit.py. Flag Effect re.IGNORECASE (re.I) Upper and lower case match each other re.MULTILINE (re.M) ^ and $ match at the start and end of every line re.DOTALL (re.S) . matches newlines too re.VERBOSE (re.X) Spaces and # comments in the pattern are ignored re.ASCII (re.A) \\w, \\d and \\s match only ASCII characters Remember re.compile(pattern) gives a pattern object with the same methods. re caches recent patterns, so compiling is mostly about clarity: give a long pattern a name and write it once. Lesson 5 of 10"],[34,"greedy","Greedy, lazy, anchors and lookarounds","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 greedy.py. Syntax Meaning .*, .+ Greedy: take as much as possible, then back off .*?, .+? Lazy: take as little as possible, then take more (?=...), (?!...) Lookahead: what follows must (not) match. Uses up no characters (?<=...), (?<!...) Lookbehind: what comes before must (not) match *+, ++, (?>...) Possessive and atomic (3.11+): never give characters back Common mistake Some patterns make the engine try a huge number of ways before failing, for example (a+)+$ on \"aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa!\". This is catastrophic backtracking, and attackers use it to freeze servers (ReDoS). Avoid nested repeats of the same thing, or use possessive quantifiers. Lesson 6 of 10"],[34,"parsing","Parsing real text","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 parsing.py. Remember A named, verbose pattern reads like documentation. fullmatch makes sure the line has exactly the expected shape, and broken lines are skipped instead of giving wrong data. Going deeper: How the engine works Python's re is a backtracking engine: it tries one way, and when it gets stuck, goes back to the last choice and tries another. That makes features like backreferences possible, but can be slow on bad patterns. Engines such as RE2 (Google) turn the pattern into an automaton that never backtracks and always runs in time proportional to the text, but they cannot support backreferences. Lesson 7 of 10"],[34,"maths","The maths: languages and automata","Choices for a character classExam favourite patterns of length n over a class of size k match kn strings \\d{4} matches 104 = 10,000 strings, 0000 to 9999. A range of repeats [ab]{1,3} matches 2 + 4 + 8 = 14 strings ∑ ki for i from min to max. Positions tried by search up to n + 1 starting places Text of length 17: starts 0 to 17. A regular languageExam favourite built from single characters with union (|), concatenation and star (*) Every such pattern can be matched by a finite automaton with no memory. Catastrophic backtrackingExam favourite ways to split n letters among nested repeats grows like 2n (a+)+$ on 30 a's followed by \"!\": about 230 ≈ 109 tries. Linear time engines time = O(m × n) for pattern size m and text size n RE2 and Rust's regex guarantee this, Python's re does not. Worked example: how many PIN codes does r\"\\d{4}\" accept, and how many have no repeated digit? Each of the 4 places can be any of 10 digits: 104 = 10,000 codes. No repeated digit: 10 choices, then 9, then 8, then 7. 10 × 9 × 8 × 7 = 5040. 10,000 codes, of which 5040 have four different digits Lesson 8 of 10"],[34,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths Use \\d, \\w, \\s, ., [ ], *, + and ? with re.search, findall and sub, always in raw strings. Use groups and named groups, fullmatch for validation, split, flags, re.VERBOSE and re.compile, and lazy quantifiers. Use lookarounds and backreferences, understand backtracking, avoid catastrophic patterns, and know when a parser is the right tool. Know regular languages, NFAs and DFAs, Thompson's construction, why backreferences make matching NP-hard, and linear time engines such as RE2. Beginner Find and replace with patterns. Intermediate Validate and parse with groups. Expert Reason about engines and their cost. Lesson 9 of 10"],[34,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","The easy ones use findall, split and re.escape. The medium ones build a pattern from the input or use a lazy block comment pattern. The hard ones are classics: write your own matcher for . and * (how a regex engine works inside), wildcard matching, and a tag validator that shows where regex stops and a stack must take over. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1805 Number of Different Integers in a String Easy findall the digit runs r\"\\d+\" finds every run of digits. int() removes leading zeros, and a set keeps the different values. 2114 Maximum Number of Words Found in Sentences Easy Count words with split split() with no argument splits on any run of whitespace, which is the same as re.split(r\"\\s+\", s.strip()). 2788 Split Strings by Separator Easy re.split with an escaped separator The separator may be a character that is special in regex, such as \".\" or \"|\", so pass it through re.escape. Drop empty pieces. 1910 Remove All Occurrences of a Substring Medium A stack of characters Removing one copy can create a new one, so a single re.sub is not enough. Build the result character by character and cut the part off whenever it ends with it. 722 Remove Comments Medium Join the lines, then one regex Join the source with newlines. r\"//.*|/\\*(.|\\n)*?\\*/\" matches a line comment or a block comment (lazy, across lines). Remove them, split again and drop empty lines. 1023 Camelcase Matching Medium Build a regex from the pattern Between and around the pattern letters, only lower case letters may be added: \"FB\" becomes ^[a-z]*F[a-z]*B[a-z]*$. 1451 Rearrange Words in a Sentence Medium A stable sort by length Lower case the words, sort by length (sorted is stable, so equal lengths keep their order), then capitalise the first letter of the result. 10 Regular Expression Matching Hard Dynamic programming over the two strings match(i, j) asks whether s[i:] matches p[j:]. With a * after p[j], either skip \"x*\" or use it once if the first characters match. Memoise with lru_cache. 44 Wildcard Matching Hard The same dynamic programming with ? and * ? matches one character, * any run (also empty). A * can either match nothing (move in the pattern) or one more character (move in the text). 591 Tag Validator Hard Regex for the pieces, a stack for the nesting Regex cannot check nesting, so use it only for the pieces: remove CDATA blocks, then find tags with a regex and match open and close tags with a stack. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 10 of 10"],[34,"recap","Chapter summary","Write patterns as raw strings. \\d \\w \\s . [ ] are characters, * + ? {m,n} are repeats, ^ $ \\b are positions. search finds the first match, match only at the start, fullmatch the whole string. findall gives strings, finditer match objects. Groups ( ) and named groups (?P<name>...) pull out parts. (?:...) groups without capturing. sub replaces (with \\1 or a function), split cuts. Flags: IGNORECASE, MULTILINE, DOTALL, VERBOSE. Repeats are greedy unless you add ?. Lookarounds check without using characters. Avoid nested repeats (ReDoS). Regex is for patterns, not nested formats: use a parser for HTML, JSON and XML. Next: Next: reading and writing CSV, JSON, XML, configuration files and pickles. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: find email addresses, turn dates around with named groups, and write a small tokenizer for arithmetic.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[35,"","CSV, JSON, XML and settings files","CSV, JSON with custom types, XML with ElementTree, INI and TOML settings, pickle safety. · csv · json · xml · ElementTree · configparser · toml · tomllib · pickle · serialization · data format Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Advanced Python›Step 6: The standard library toolbox: text, data, databases and GUIs›Data formats Step 6 · Topic 2 of 4 · Advanced Save data and share it with other programs. Learn CSV with DictReader and DictWriter, JSON with custom types, XML with ElementTree, settings with configparser and tomllib, and why pickle is only for trusted data. About 75 min10 lessons1 animation5 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Data formats: saving and sharing data 2CSV: tables as text 3JSON: nested data for the web 4XML with ElementTree 5Settings: INI and TOML 6pickle: Python objects as bytes 7The maths: sizes and overheads 8From beginner to expert 9LeetCode practice 10Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 10"],[35,"idea","Data formats: saving and sharing data","Source: Python 3.14 documentation of csv, json, xml.etree.ElementTree, configparser, tomllib and pickle. What it is A data format is an agreed way to write data as text or bytes, so that another program (or the same one tomorrow) can read it back. Python's standard library reads and writes the common ones: CSV for tables, JSON for nested data, XML for documents with tags, INI and TOML for settings, and pickle for Python objects. Why we need it Objects live only while the program runs. To save them, send them over the network or share them with a spreadsheet, a web browser or a program in another language, they must become text or bytes in a format the other side understands. How it works Each module turns Python objects into text (writing, also called serializing or dumping) and text back into objects (reading, parsing or loading). The conversion is not always perfect, as the animation shows: types can change on the way. From Python objects to text and backEach step writes one piece. The last step reads the text back and shows what changed on the way. Step 0 of 8 Format Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this JSON: watch the tuple and the int key change. CSV: a comma inside a value needs quotes, and numbers come back as strings. Choosing a format CSV JSON XML TOML / INI pickle Shape Flat table Nested lists and dicts Nested tags with attributes Settings in sections Any Python object Types kept None, all strings str, numbers, bool, null, list, dict All strings TOML: numbers, bools, dates, lists All of them Read by people Yes, and spreadsheets Yes Yes, wordy Yes, made for it No (bytes) Other languages Every one Every one Every one Many Python only Safe with untrusted data Yes Yes With care (defusedxml) Yes NO: loading can run code Typical use Exports, reports, data science Web APIs, settings, logs Documents, older APIs, office files pyproject.toml, app config Caches inside one trusted program Bottom line: JSON for most data exchange, CSV for tables, TOML for configuration, XML when a system requires it, and pickle only for your own trusted data. Lesson 2 of 10"],[35,"csv","CSV: tables as text","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 csv_files.py. In the exam Always open CSV files with newline=\"\". The csv module handles line endings itself (rows end with \\r\\n by default), and quotes inside values. Without newline=\"\" you can get blank lines between rows on Windows. csv tool Does csv.reader(f), csv.writer(f) Rows as lists csv.DictReader(f), csv.DictWriter(f, fieldnames) Rows as dicts keyed by the header delimiter=\";\", quotechar='\"' Other separators and quote characters quoting=csv.QUOTE_ALL, QUOTE_MINIMAL, QUOTE_NONNUMERIC When values get quotes csv.Sniffer().sniff(sample) Guess the separator of an unknown file Lesson 3 of 10"],[35,"json","JSON: nested data for the web","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 json_data.py. Python JSON Back to Python dict object {...} dict (keys always str) list, tuple array [...] list str string \"...\" str int, float number int or float True, False true, false True, False None null None set, date, Decimal, your classes TypeError unless default= handles them Remember dump and load work with files, dumps and loads (s for string) with strings. indent=2 makes it readable, sort_keys=True makes the output stable for tests and diffs, and ensure_ascii=False keeps letters like ü instead of \\u00fc. Lesson 4 of 10"],[35,"xml","XML with ElementTree","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 xml_data.py. ElementTree Does ET.parse(path), ET.fromstring(text) Read a file or a string into a tree el.tag, el.attrib, el.get(name), el.text The tag name, attributes and text of an element el.find(path), el.findall(path), el.findtext(path) Children by a simple path, such as \"book/title\" el.iter(tag) Every element with that tag, at any depth .//book[@lang=\"de\"] The XPath subset: any depth, attribute tests, positions ET.SubElement, el.set, ET.indent, ET.tostring, tree.write Build, change and write XML Common mistake Never parse XML from strangers with the standard library alone: specially made files can use huge amounts of memory (the \"billion laughs\" attack). Use the defusedxml package for untrusted XML. Lesson 5 of 10"],[35,"config","Settings: INI and TOML","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 config_files.py. Settings files INI (configparser) TOML (tomllib) JSON Environment variables Types Strings, with getint, getboolean Real numbers, bools, dates, lists, tables Numbers, bools, lists, dicts Strings Comments Yes Yes No No Write with the standard library Yes No, read only (tomli-w writes) Yes os.environ Typical use Older tools, setup.cfg pyproject.toml, new apps Web app settings Secrets and per server values (12 factor apps) Bottom line: TOML for new configuration files, environment variables for secrets such as passwords and API keys. Lesson 6 of 10"],[35,"pickle","pickle: Python objects as bytes","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 pickle_data.py. Common mistake pickle can save almost any Python object, but pickle.loads can run any code the creator of the data chose, as the Evil class shows with a harmless print. Never unpickle data from the internet, uploads or anyone you do not fully trust. Use JSON for data you exchange. Going deeper: Other binary formats For big tables, columnar formats such as Parquet (with pandas or pyarrow) are much smaller and faster than CSV. For messages between services, Protocol Buffers or MessagePack are compact and typed. The struct module packs numbers into exact byte layouts for files and network protocols. Lesson 7 of 10"],[35,"maths","The maths: sizes and overheads","CSV file sizeExam favourite bytes ≈ rows × (sum of value lengths + separators + 2 for \\r\\n) 1000 rows of 3 values of 6 characters: 1000 × (18 + 2 + 2) = 22,000 bytes. JSON overhead for a list of dicts every row repeats every key with quotes, a colon and a comma Keys \"name\", \"city\", \"age\" add about 25 bytes to each row, often more than the data itself. Base64 for binary data in JSON or XMLExam favourite size out = 4 × ⌈n / 3⌉ A 300 KB image becomes 400 KB of text. Unicode escapes ensure_ascii=True writes a non ASCII letter as 6 characters \"Zürich\" grows from 7 bytes (UTF-8) to 11 characters with \\u00fc. Sniffing a separator pick the character that appears the same number of times on every line \"id;price\" and \"1;2,50\": the ; appears once on each line, the comma does not. Worked example: CSV or JSON for 10,000 orders with id, city and total? A CSV row like \"1042,Paris,29.90\\r\\n\" is 16 characters plus 2 line ending bytes, so 18 bytes: 10,000 rows is about 180 KB. A JSON row like {\"id\": 1042, \"city\": \"Paris\", \"total\": 29.9}, is about 46 bytes: about 460 KB. The keys repeat in every JSON row, so JSON is about 2.5 times bigger here. CSV ≈ 180 KB, JSON ≈ 460 KB, but JSON keeps types and nesting Lesson 8 of 10"],[35,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths Read and write CSV with DictReader and DictWriter (newline=\"\"), and JSON with dump, load, dumps and loads. Handle quoting and separators, write default= and object_hook for dates and sets, read XML with find, findall and iter, and settings with configparser and tomllib. Choose a format by shape, types, size and safety, avoid pickle and raw XML with untrusted data, and validate JSON with a schema (jsonschema, pydantic). Stream huge files (csv row by row, ET.iterparse, ijson), use columnar and binary formats (Parquet, Arrow, Protocol Buffers), and design versioned formats that stay readable for years. Beginner Save and load CSV and JSON. Intermediate Handle XML, settings and custom types. Expert Choose, validate and stream formats at scale. Lesson 9 of 10"],[35,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","These problems read and write small formats: spreadsheet column names and ranges, dates, HTML entities, a nested list parser that works like a tiny JSON reader, masking personal data before it is logged, equations, chemical formulas and brace patterns. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 171 Excel Sheet Column Number Easy Base 26 with letters Spreadsheet columns count A to Z, then AA. Each letter is a digit from 1 to 26: result = result × 26 + value. 168 Excel Sheet Column Title Easy Base 26 the other way, with a shift There is no zero digit, so subtract 1 before each divmod by 26. 1507 Reformat Date Easy Split and look up the month Split \"20th Oct 2052\" into three parts. Strip the two letter ending from the day, look the month up in a list, and pad with zfill(2). 2194 Cells in a Range on an Excel Sheet Easy Two nested loops over letters and rows The range \"K1:L2\" gives column letters K to L and rows 1 to 2. Columns go in the outer loop. 1410 HTML Entity Parser Medium Replace the entities, the ampersand one last Replace each named entity with its character. Do the ampersand entity last, or the text you just made could be read as a new entity. 385 Mini Parser Medium A stack of lists while reading characters LeetCode uses a NestedInteger class. Here plain ints and lists stand for it. Push a new list at [, finish a number at , or ], and pop at ]. 831 Masking Personal Information Medium Two formats, two rules An email keeps the first and last letter of the name and lower cases everything. A phone keeps only digits, shows the last 4, and stars for a country code. 640 Solve the Equation Medium A regex for the terms on each side The pattern [+-]?\\d*x|[+-]?\\d+ finds terms like +2x, -x or 5. Add up x coefficients and numbers on each side, then move everything to one side. 726 Number of Atoms Hard A stack of Counters Read an atom name (capital letter and lower case letters) and its count. At ( push a new Counter, at ) multiply the top one by the number after it and add it to the one below. 1096 Brace Expansion II Hard A recursive parser for union and product A comma means union of sets, writing things next to each other means every combination (product). Parse with a function that reads until } and calls itself at {. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 10 of 10"],[35,"recap","Chapter summary","CSV: csv.DictReader and DictWriter, open with newline=\"\", values come back as strings, commas inside values get quotes. JSON: dumps and loads (strings), dump and load (files). Tuples become lists, keys become strings. default= and object_hook for other types. XML: ET.fromstring or parse, find, findall, iter and simple XPath. defusedxml for untrusted files. Settings: configparser for INI (getint, getboolean, fallback), tomllib to read TOML. Secrets in environment variables. pickle saves any object but loading can run code: only for trusted data. Next: Next: storing data in a real database with sqlite3 and SQL. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: add up a CSV by city, write JSON with dates and sets, and pick cheap books out of XML.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[36,"","Databases with sqlite3 and SQL","sqlite3, SQL queries and joins, placeholders against injection, transactions, constraints and indexes. · sqlite3 · SQLite · SQL · database · JOIN · GROUP BY · SQL injection · transaction · index · IntegrityError Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Advanced Python›Step 6: The standard library toolbox: text, data, databases and GUIs›sqlite3 and SQL Step 6 · Topic 3 of 4 · Advanced Store data in a real database that ships with Python. Learn SQL step by step, keep it safe from SQL injection with placeholders, make changes all or nothing with transactions, and find rows fast with indexes. About 90 min10 lessons1 animation6 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Databases and SQL from Python 2Connect, execute, commit, fetch 3SQL in one lesson: SELECT, WHERE, JOIN, GROUP BY 4Placeholders and SQL injection 5Transactions and rules on the data 6Friendlier rows and fast lookups with indexes 7The maths: indexes, joins and estimates 8From beginner to expert 9LeetCode practice 10Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 10"],[36,"idea","Databases and SQL from Python","Source: Python 3.14 documentation of sqlite3 and the SQLite documentation. What it is A database stores data in tables: rows of records with named columns, like a strict spreadsheet. You ask it questions in SQL (Structured Query Language). SQLite is a complete SQL database in one file, and Python ships with the sqlite3 module to use it, so there is nothing to install. Why we need it A JSON or CSV file must be read completely, changed in memory and written back completely. Two programs writing at once can destroy it, a crash halfway leaves half a file, and finding one record among millions means reading them all. How it works The database reads and writes only the pages it needs, finds rows fast with indexes, checks rules such as \"balance may not be negative\", and groups changes into transactions that are saved completely or not at all. SQL says WHAT you want and the database works out HOW to get it. Inside a database queryPick a case. Each step runs one part and shows the rows the database holds at that moment. Step 0 of 7 Case Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this Order: WHERE runs before GROUP BY, and SELECT runs almost last. Injection: one quote in the input changes the meaning of the SQL. Transaction: a failed second update undoes the first one. Where to keep data JSON or CSV file SQLite (sqlite3) PostgreSQL or MySQL pandas DataFrame Setup None None, one file A server to install and run pip install pandas Find one record in a million Read everything Milliseconds with an index Milliseconds with an index Fast, but all data in memory Safe when a program crashes No Yes, transactions Yes, transactions No, memory only Many writers at once No One writer at a time Yes, many No Rules on the data None Types, NOT NULL, UNIQUE, CHECK, foreign keys The same and more Only in your code Typical use Small settings and exports Apps, phones, browsers, tests, prototypes Web sites with many users Analysis and reports Bottom line: SQLite for one program or a small site, PostgreSQL for a web app with many users at once, pandas for analysis. The SQL you learn here works in all of them with small changes. Lesson 2 of 10"],[36,"first","Connect, execute, commit, fetch","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 first_db.py. Step Code Meaning Open conn = sqlite3.connect(\"shop.db\") Opens the file, or makes it. \":memory:\" gives a database in memory only. Cursor cur = conn.cursor() An object that runs SQL and walks through results. conn.execute() makes one for you. Run SQL cur.execute(sql, values) Runs one statement. Values go in a tuple, matching the ? marks. Save conn.commit() Makes INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE permanent. Without it they are lost on close. Read fetchone(), fetchall(), fetchmany(n), for row in cur Rows come back as tuples. Close conn.close() Frees the file. Uncommitted changes are thrown away. In the exam The most common beginner bug: INSERT works, the program ends, and the data is gone. You forgot conn.commit(). Using \"with conn:\" commits for you. Remember SQL types in SQLite are flexible: INTEGER, REAL, TEXT, BLOB and NULL. Python int, float, str, bytes and None map to them. Store dates as ISO text such as \"2026-11-07\", because the automatic date converters are deprecated since Python 3.12. Lesson 3 of 10"],[36,"sql","SQL in one lesson: SELECT, WHERE, JOIN, GROUP BY","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 queries.py. SQL Does Runs in step FROM a JOIN b ON ... Takes rows from tables and pairs them up 1 WHERE condition Keeps rows where the condition is true 2 GROUP BY column Puts rows with the same value in one group 3 HAVING condition Keeps groups (it can use COUNT, SUM, AVG) 4 SELECT columns Picks and names the output columns 5 ORDER BY column DESC Sorts the result 6 LIMIT n OFFSET k Keeps n rows after skipping k 7 Change data Example Make a table CREATE TABLE t (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE) Add rows INSERT INTO t (name) VALUES (?) Change rows UPDATE t SET name = ? WHERE id = ? Remove rows DELETE FROM t WHERE id = ? Remove the table DROP TABLE IF EXISTS t Kinds of JOIN INNER JOIN (JOIN) LEFT JOIN CROSS JOIN Keeps Only pairs that match Every left row, with NULL when nothing matches Every left row with every right row Rows for 4 customers, 3 with orders One per matching order Matching orders plus 1 row for the customer without orders 4 × number of orders Use it for Orders with their customer Customers and their orders, even with none All combinations (rarely) Bottom line: Use LEFT JOIN with COALESCE(SUM(...), 0) when rows without a partner must still appear with 0. Common mistake An UPDATE or DELETE without WHERE changes EVERY row. Write the WHERE first, and try it as a SELECT before you run it. Lesson 4 of 10"],[36,"params","Placeholders and SQL injection","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 params.py. Style SQL Values qmark WHERE name = ? AND age > ? a tuple: (\"ann\", 30) named WHERE name = :name AND age > :age a dict: {\"name\": \"ann\", \"age\": 30} Many rows executemany(\"INSERT INTO t VALUES (?, ?)\", rows) a list of tuples A script executescript(\"CREATE ...; INSERT ...\") no values: only for your own fixed SQL Common mistake Never put values into SQL with f-strings, +, % or .format(). Use ? or :name every time. Placeholders stop SQL injection, handle quotes in names like O'Neil, and let the database reuse the prepared statement. In the exam Placeholders are for VALUES only. A table or column name cannot be a ?. If a user picks the column to sort by, check it against a fixed list such as {\"name\", \"price\"} before you put it into the SQL. Lesson 5 of 10"],[36,"txn","Transactions and rules on the data","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 transactions.py. ACID: what a transaction promises Letter Means In the example A, atomic All the changes happen, or none The failed transfer did not give Ann 500 C, consistent Rules such as CHECK and UNIQUE always hold No balance is ever below 0 I, isolated Others do not see half done changes Another connection sees the old balances until commit D, durable After commit the data survives a crash or power cut SQLite writes a journal before it changes the file Bottom line: Put every group of changes that must happen together inside one \"with conn:\" block. Rule in CREATE TABLE Stops Error PRIMARY KEY, UNIQUE Two rows with the same value IntegrityError: UNIQUE constraint failed NOT NULL A missing value IntegrityError: NOT NULL constraint failed CHECK (balance >= 0) Values that break your rule IntegrityError: CHECK constraint failed REFERENCES other(col) A link to a row that does not exist IntegrityError: FOREIGN KEY constraint failed (after PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON) Remember \"with conn:\" commits or rolls back, but it does NOT close the connection. Since Python 3.12 you can also pass autocommit=False to sqlite3.connect for the transaction rules of the database standard (PEP 249). Lesson 6 of 10"],[36,"rows","Friendlier rows and fast lookups with indexes","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 rows_index.py. Remember An index is a sorted copy of one column with a pointer to each row, kept in a B-tree. It turns a scan of every row into a few page reads. It costs disk space and makes INSERT and UPDATE a little slower, so index the columns you search, join or sort by often. Going deeper: ORMs: databases without writing SQL An ORM (object relational mapper) turns tables into classes and rows into objects. SQLAlchemy is the most used one, and Django has its own ORM, which the web part of this course uses. ORMs still create SQL for you, so knowing SQL helps you find slow queries and the \"N+1 queries\" problem, where a loop runs one query per row instead of one JOIN. Lesson 7 of 10"],[36,"maths","The maths: indexes, joins and estimates","Full scan cost ≈ N rows read Finding one user among 1,000,000 without an index reads 1,000,000 rows. B-tree heightExam favourite h = ⌈logf N⌉, f = keys per page (fan out) N = 1,000,000 and f = 100: h = 3 page reads instead of a million rows. Binary search in a sorted index comparisons ≈ ⌈log2 N⌉ log2 1,000,000 ≈ 20. Selectivity of WHERE a = vExam favourite rows ≈ N / V(a), V(a) = number of different values 1,000,000 rows and 100 cities: about 10,000 rows per city. Equi join size estimate |R ⋈ S| ≈ |R| × |S| / max(V(R, a), V(S, a)) 1000 customers, 5000 orders, joined on 1000 customer ids: 1000 × 5000 / 1000 = 5000 rows. Cost of commits time ≈ commits × time per disk sync 1000 inserts with 1000 commits at 5 ms each: 5 s. One commit: about 5 ms. Worked example: how many page reads does an index save? A table has 2,000,000 rows. Each 4096 byte page holds 40 rows, so the table has 2,000,000 / 40 = 50,000 pages. A full scan reads all 50,000 pages. The index on the column holds about 200 keys per page, so its height is ⌈log200 2,000,000⌉ = 3, because 2002 = 40,000 is too small and 2003 = 8,000,000 is enough. Reading one row costs 3 index pages plus 1 table page. 4 page reads instead of 50,000: about 12,500 times fewer Lesson 8 of 10"],[36,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths Connect, create a table, INSERT with ? placeholders, commit, and read rows with SELECT and fetchall. Write WHERE, ORDER BY, GROUP BY, HAVING and JOINs, use \"with conn:\" for transactions, and catch IntegrityError from UNIQUE, NOT NULL and CHECK rules. Design tables with primary and foreign keys (normal forms), add indexes after reading EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN, and use window functions such as ROW_NUMBER and DENSE_RANK. Understand B-trees, the write ahead log (WAL), isolation levels and locking, query planners and cost estimates, and move from SQLite to PostgreSQL with an ORM and migrations. Beginner Store and read rows safely. Intermediate Query, join and protect data with transactions. Expert Design schemas and tune queries with indexes. Lesson 9 of 10"],[36,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","LeetCode has a whole set of SQL problems. Here each one is solved with sqlite3 from Python: the function builds a small database in memory from the example rows, runs the SQL and returns the rows. You practise LEFT JOIN, GROUP BY with HAVING, self joins on dates, DELETE with a subquery, LIMIT with OFFSET, and the window functions DENSE_RANK and PARTITION BY. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 175 Combine Two Tables Easy LEFT JOIN keeps people without an address Every person must appear, even without an address, so use LEFT JOIN. Missing columns come back as None. 182 Duplicate Emails Easy GROUP BY with HAVING COUNT Group the rows by email and keep the groups that have more than one row. 183 Customers Who Never Order Easy NOT IN a subquery, or LEFT JOIN ... IS NULL Find customers whose id is not in the list of customer ids of the orders. 197 Rising Temperature Easy Join the table with itself on yesterday Pair each day with the day before using DATE(day, '-1 day'), then keep pairs where the temperature went up. 196 Delete Duplicate Emails Easy DELETE rows that are not the smallest id Keep MIN(id) of each email and delete every other row. 176 Second Highest Salary Medium DISTINCT, ORDER BY, LIMIT 1 OFFSET 1 inside a subquery The outer SELECT turns \"no row\" into NULL, which is None in Python. 178 Rank Scores Medium The DENSE_RANK window function DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY score DESC) gives equal scores the same rank and leaves no gaps after ties. 184 Department Highest Salary Medium Join with the maximum of each department A subquery finds MAX(salary) per department. Join it back to keep every employee who earns that much, ties included. 185 Department Top Three Salaries Hard DENSE_RANK per department with PARTITION BY PARTITION BY departmentId restarts the ranking in each department. Keep rank 3 or better. Ties share a rank. 262 Trips and Users Hard Join twice to the users table, then average a condition Join Users once for the client and once for the driver and keep trips where neither is banned. AVG(status != 'completed') is the share of cancelled trips, because a true test counts as 1. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 10 of 10"],[36,"recap","Chapter summary","sqlite3 is a full SQL database in one file. connect, execute, commit, fetchone or fetchall, close. SQL runs in the order FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING, SELECT, ORDER BY, LIMIT. LEFT JOIN keeps rows without a partner. Always use ? or :name placeholders. f-strings in SQL cause SQL injection and break on quotes. \"with conn:\" makes a transaction: commit at the end, rollback on an exception. PRIMARY KEY, UNIQUE, NOT NULL, CHECK and foreign keys raise IntegrityError. sqlite3.Row gives rows by column name. Indexes turn a full scan into a few B-tree page reads, and EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN shows which one is used. Next: Next: building desktop windows with tkinter. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: save names that contain quotes safely, make a bank transfer all or nothing, and write a report with LEFT JOIN, GROUP BY and HAVING.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[37,"","Desktop windows with tkinter","tkinter widgets, layouts, variables, events, Canvas animation with after() and responsive apps. · tkinter · GUI · ttk · mainloop · grid · pack · Canvas · bind · after · event loop Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Advanced Python›Step 6: The standard library toolbox: text, data, databases and GUIs›tkinter GUIs Step 6 · Topic 4 of 4 · Advanced Build real desktop windows with the toolkit that comes with Python. Learn widgets, layouts with grid and pack, control variables, events, drawing and animation on a Canvas, and how to keep a window alive during long tasks. About 90 min11 lessons1 animation6 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1Windows, buttons and the event loop 2Your first window 3Placing widgets: pack, grid and place 4Widgets and control variables 5Events, the Canvas and animation with after() 6A real app: classes, scrollbars and saving 7Long tasks without freezing the window 8The maths: pixels, frames and how fast people click 9From beginner to expert 10LeetCode practice 11Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 11"],[37,"idea","Windows, buttons and the event loop","Source: Python 3.14 documentation of tkinter and tkinter.ttk, and the PCPP-32-101 GUI programming section. What it is A GUI (graphical user interface) is a program with windows, buttons, text boxes and menus instead of typed commands. tkinter is the GUI toolkit that comes with Python. It wraps Tk, a toolkit that runs on Windows, macOS and Linux. Why we need it Many users never open a terminal. A small window lets them pick a file, fill in a form or press a button, and it can show pictures, charts and live data. Office tools, installers and lab instruments often need exactly this. How it works You build a tree of widgets (the window, frames, labels, buttons), place them with a geometry manager (pack, grid or place), connect events such as clicks to your functions, and hand control to mainloop(). From then on your code runs only when an event calls it. How a tkinter window worksPick a case. Each step shows the window, the waiting events and what mainloop is doing. Step 0 of 8 Case Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this mainloop: your function runs only when an event arrives. Freeze: time.sleep blocks the loop, after() does not. grid: sticky and weight decide where widgets sit and what grows. Ways to build a user interface in Python tkinter PySide6 or PyQt6 Kivy Web app (Flask, Django) Textual Install Comes with Python pip, about 100 MB pip pip, plus a browser pip Look Simple, native with ttk Modern, very rich Its own touch style Anything HTML and CSS can do Text in the terminal Runs on Desktop Desktop Desktop, Android, iOS Any device with a browser Any terminal, also over SSH Licence Python licence LGPL (PySide6) or GPL / paid (PyQt6) MIT Your choice MIT Best for Small tools, teaching, the PCPP exam Large desktop applications Touch and mobile apps Apps for many users Developer tools Bottom line: tkinter to learn how every GUI works and for small tools, Qt for big desktop programs, and a web app when many people must use it. Lesson 2 of 11"],[37,"first","Your first window","hello_window.pypython 1import tkinter as tk23clicks = 0456def say_hello():7 global clicks8 clicks = clicks + 19 label.config(text=f\"Hello! You clicked {clicks} times.\")101112root = tk.Tk()13root.title(\"My first window\")14root.geometry(\"360x140\")1516label = tk.Label(root, text=\"Press the button\", font=(\"Arial\", 14))17label.pack(pady=15)1819button = tk.Button(root, text=\"Say hello\", command=say_hello)20button.pack()2122root.mainloop()23print(\"The window is closed, so mainloop returned.\") Line 1 Import tkinter under the short name tk. This is the usual style. Line 6 The function the button will call. It changes the global counter, so it needs the global statement. Line 12 tk.Tk() makes the main window. A program has exactly one Tk object. Line 14 geometry(\"WIDTHxHEIGHT\") sets the size in pixels. Line 16 Every widget gets its parent as the first argument. Options such as text and font are keyword arguments. Line 17 A widget is invisible until a geometry manager places it. pack() stacks widgets from the top. Line 19 command=say_hello hands over the function itself, without brackets. Tk calls it on every click. Line 22 mainloop() shows the window and handles events until the window is closed. Line 23 This line runs only after the window is closed. The window after two clicks on \"Say hello\". Run this one on your own computer. A window needs a real screen, so it cannot run inside this page. The picture is a real screenshot of this program, taken with Python 3.14 and Tk 9.0 on Linux. On Windows and macOS the window looks a little different. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 hello_window.py. Close the window to end the program. Step Code What it does 1 root = tk.Tk() Make the main window 2 w = tk.Label(root, text=...) Make a widget inside a parent 3 w.pack() or w.grid(...) Place it, or it stays invisible 4 command=function, w.bind(event, function) Connect events to your functions 5 w.config(text=...) or w[\"text\"] = ... Change a widget later 6 root.mainloop() Start the event loop mainloop() is a loop that waits for events and calls the right function for each one. This small model shows the idea without a window, so it runs in the page: Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 event_loop_model.py. In the exam command=say_hello() (with brackets) is the classic beginner bug: it calls the function ONCE while the button is made and stores its result, None, as the command. Then clicks do nothing. To pass arguments, use command=lambda: say(name). Lesson 3 of 11"],[37,"layout","Placing widgets: pack, grid and place","form_grid.pypython 1import tkinter as tk2from tkinter import messagebox, ttk345def submit():6 name = name_entry.get().strip()7 age = age_entry.get().strip()8 if not name or not age.isdigit():9 messagebox.showerror(\"Sign up\", \"Please type a name and an age in numbers.\")10 return11 result.config(text=f\"Welcome, {name} ({age})!\")121314root = tk.Tk()15root.title(\"Sign up\")16root.geometry(\"340x170\")17root.columnconfigure(1, weight=1) # column 1 gets any extra width1819ttk.Label(root, text=\"Name:\").grid(row=0, column=0, sticky=\"e\", padx=8, pady=6)20name_entry = ttk.Entry(root)21name_entry.grid(row=0, column=1, sticky=\"ew\", padx=8)2223ttk.Label(root, text=\"Age:\").grid(row=1, column=0, sticky=\"e\", padx=8, pady=6)24age_entry = ttk.Entry(root, width=6)25age_entry.grid(row=1, column=1, sticky=\"w\", padx=8)2627ttk.Button(root, text=\"Submit\", command=submit).grid(row=2, column=0, columnspan=2, pady=8)28result = ttk.Label(root, text=\"\")29result.grid(row=3, column=0, columnspan=2)3031name_entry.focus()32root.bind(\"<Return>\", lambda event: submit())33root.mainloop() Line 6 Entry.get() returns the text the user typed, always as a string. Line 8 Check the input before you use it. isdigit() is True only for whole numbers made of digits. Line 9 messagebox shows a small dialog window and waits until the user closes it. Line 17 weight=1: when the window gets wider, column 1 gets the extra space. Line 19 grid puts the label in row 0, column 0. sticky=\"e\" pushes it to the right side of its cell. Line 21 sticky=\"ew\" stretches the entry from the left to the right edge of its cell. Line 27 columnspan=2 lets the button use both columns. Line 31 focus() puts the text cursor in the name box, so the user can type at once. Line 32 bind connects the Enter key to submit. A bound function gets an event object, so the lambda takes one argument. The sign up form after typing Ann and 31 and submitting. Run this one on your own computer. A window needs a real screen, so it cannot run inside this page. The picture is a real screenshot of this program, taken with Python 3.14 and Tk 9.0 on Linux. On Windows and macOS the window looks a little different. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 form_grid.py. Close the window to end the program. Geometry managers pack grid place Idea Stack widgets from one side (top, left ...) A table of rows and columns Exact x, y position or relative position Good for Toolbars, simple columns of widgets Forms, dialogs, almost every real layout Overlays, games, special cases Grows with the window fill and expand sticky and weight Only with relx, rely, relwidth Main options side, fill, expand, padx, pady, anchor row, column, sticky, columnspan, rowspan, padx, pady x, y, relx, rely, anchor, width, height Bottom line: Use grid for forms and most windows, pack for simple stacks. Inside ONE parent use only one of them. Common mistake Never mix pack and grid for widgets with the same parent: Tk raises TclError (\"cannot use geometry manager grid inside . which already has slaves managed by pack\"). Put each group in its own Frame instead, and each Frame can use its own manager. Lesson 4 of 11"],[37,"vars","Widgets and control variables","pizza_order.pypython 1import tkinter as tk2from tkinter import ttk34PRICES = {\"small\": 6, \"medium\": 8, \"large\": 10}56root = tk.Tk()7root.title(\"Pizza order\")89size = tk.StringVar(value=\"medium\")10cheese = tk.BooleanVar(value=True)11count = tk.IntVar(value=1)12summary = tk.StringVar()131415def update_summary(*args):16 price = PRICES[size.get()]17 if cheese.get():18 price = price + 119 extra = \" with extra cheese\" if cheese.get() else \"\"20 summary.set(f\"{count.get()} x {size.get()}{extra} = {price * count.get()} euros\")212223for value in (\"small\", \"medium\", \"large\"):24 ttk.Radiobutton(root, text=value.title(), value=value, variable=size).pack(anchor=\"w\", padx=12)25ttk.Checkbutton(root, text=\"Extra cheese (+1)\", variable=cheese).pack(anchor=\"w\", padx=12, pady=4)26ttk.Scale(root, from_=1, to=10, variable=count, command=lambda value: count.set(round(float(value)))).pack(fill=\"x\", padx=12)27ttk.Label(root, textvariable=summary, font=(\"Arial\", 12, \"bold\")).pack(padx=12, pady=10)2829for variable in (size, cheese, count):30 variable.trace_add(\"write\", update_summary) # run update_summary whenever the value changes31update_summary()32root.mainloop() Line 9 A StringVar holds a string. Widgets that share it stay in step with it automatically. Line 10 BooleanVar for a check box, IntVar for whole numbers. value= sets the start value. Line 15 A function for trace_add gets three arguments from Tk. *args collects and ignores them. Line 23 All three radio buttons share variable=size, so choosing one sets size to its value and clears the others. Line 26 A Scale gives floats. The command rounds the value, so count stays a whole number. Line 27 textvariable=summary: the label shows whatever summary holds, now and after every change. Line 30 trace_add(\"write\", f) calls f every time the variable gets a new value. The pizza window after choosing Large and moving the scale to 3. Run this one on your own computer. A window needs a real screen, so it cannot run inside this page. The picture is a real screenshot of this program, taken with Python 3.14 and Tk 9.0 on Linux. On Windows and macOS the window looks a little different. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 pizza_order.py. Close the window to end the program. Widget Use it for Read or set ttk.Label, tk.Message Text or an image config(text=...) or textvariable ttk.Button An action command= ttk.Entry One line of text get(), insert(0, s), delete(0, \"end\") tk.Text Many lines of text get(\"1.0\", \"end\"), insert(\"end\", s) ttk.Checkbutton, ttk.Radiobutton Yes or no, one of several variable= BooleanVar or StringVar ttk.Combobox, tk.Listbox Pick from a list get(), curselection() ttk.Scale, ttk.Spinbox A number in a range variable= or get() tk.Canvas Drawing and animation create_line, create_oval, move, coords ttk.Frame, ttk.LabelFrame Group other widgets a parent for others tk.Menu, filedialog, messagebox Menus and standard dialogs askopenfilename(), showinfo() Remember ttk widgets (from tkinter import ttk) use the look of the operating system and are styled with themes. The older tk widgets use options such as bg and fg. Prefer ttk where it has the widget. Listbox, Text, Canvas and Menu exist only in tk. Lesson 5 of 11"],[37,"events","Events, the Canvas and animation with after()","bouncing_ball.pypython 1import tkinter as tk23WIDTH = 4004HEIGHT = 2505SIZE = 3067root = tk.Tk()8root.title(\"Bouncing ball\")9canvas = tk.Canvas(root, width=WIDTH, height=HEIGHT, bg=\"white\")10canvas.pack()1112canvas.create_rectangle(10, 10, 130, 40, fill=\"#dbeafe\", outline=\"#2563eb\")13canvas.create_text(70, 25, text=\"Space: pause\")14canvas.create_line(0, HEIGHT - 20, WIDTH, HEIGHT - 20, fill=\"gray\", dash=(4, 2))15ball = canvas.create_oval(50, 50, 50 + SIZE, 50 + SIZE, fill=\"orange\")1617dx = 418dy = 319running = True202122def move():23 global dx, dy24 if running:25 canvas.move(ball, dx, dy)26 x1, y1, x2, y2 = canvas.coords(ball)27 if x1 <= 0 or x2 >= WIDTH:28 dx = -dx29 if y1 <= 0 or y2 >= HEIGHT - 20:30 dy = -dy31 root.after(20, move) # call move again in 20 ms, about 50 times a second323334def toggle(event):35 global running36 running = not running373839def jump(event):40 canvas.coords(ball, event.x, event.y, event.x + SIZE, event.y + SIZE)414243root.bind(\"<space>\", toggle)44canvas.bind(\"<Button-1>\", jump)45move()46root.mainloop() Line 9 A Canvas is a drawing area. Its point (0, 0) is the TOP left corner, and y grows downwards. Line 12 Every create_ method draws an item and returns its id, a number you use later to move or change it. Line 15 Keep the id of the ball so that move() can find it again. Line 22 move() changes global variables, so it names them in a global statement. Line 25 canvas.move(item, dx, dy) shifts the item by dx pixels right and dy pixels down. Line 26 coords(item) gives the corners x1, y1, x2, y2 of the box around the oval. Line 27 At the left or right wall, turn the horizontal direction round. Line 31 after(20, move) asks mainloop to call move again in 20 ms. This is how tkinter animates without blocking. Line 34 Event handlers get an event object with details: event.x, event.y, event.keysym, event.char. Line 43 \"\" is the space key. \"\" is a click with the left mouse button. The canvas while the ball is bouncing. Run this one on your own computer. A window needs a real screen, so it cannot run inside this page. The picture is a real screenshot of this program, taken with Python 3.14 and Tk 9.0 on Linux. On Windows and macOS the window looks a little different. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 bouncing_ball.py. Close the window to end the program. Event string Happens when Useful event fields <Button-1>, <Button-3> Left or right mouse button pressed event.x, event.y <Double-Button-1> Double click event.x, event.y <B1-Motion>, <Motion> Mouse moved with or without the button down event.x, event.y <Key>, <Return>, <Escape>, a Any key, Enter, Esc, the a key event.keysym, event.char <Control-s> Ctrl and S together event.state <Configure> The widget changed size event.width, event.height <FocusIn>, <FocusOut> The widget got or lost the keyboard event.widget Remember bind on a widget catches events in that widget. root.bind catches them anywhere in the window, and bind_all in every window of the program. For the close button use root.protocol(\"WM_DELETE_WINDOW\", function). Lesson 6 of 11"],[37,"app","A real app: classes, scrollbars and saving","todo_app.pypython 1import json2import tkinter as tk3from pathlib import Path4from tkinter import messagebox, ttk56FILE = Path.home() / \"todo.json\"789class TodoApp(ttk.Frame):10 def __init__(self, master):11 super().__init__(master, padding=10)12 self.grid(row=0, column=0, sticky=\"nsew\")13 master.columnconfigure(0, weight=1)14 master.rowconfigure(0, weight=1)15 self.columnconfigure(0, weight=1)16 self.rowconfigure(1, weight=1)1718 self.entry = ttk.Entry(self)19 self.entry.grid(row=0, column=0, sticky=\"ew\")20 self.entry.bind(\"<Return>\", self.add)21 ttk.Button(self, text=\"Add\", command=self.add).grid(row=0, column=1, padx=(6, 0))2223 self.listbox = tk.Listbox(self, height=8)24 self.listbox.grid(row=1, column=0, sticky=\"nsew\", pady=8)25 scroll = ttk.Scrollbar(self, orient=\"vertical\", command=self.listbox.yview)26 scroll.grid(row=1, column=1, sticky=\"ns\", pady=8)27 self.listbox.config(yscrollcommand=scroll.set)2829 ttk.Button(self, text=\"Done\", command=self.remove).grid(row=2, column=0, sticky=\"w\")30 ttk.Button(self, text=\"Save\", command=self.save).grid(row=2, column=1)31 self.load()3233 def add(self, event=None):34 text = self.entry.get().strip()35 if text:36 self.listbox.insert(\"end\", text)37 self.entry.delete(0, \"end\")3839 def remove(self):40 chosen = self.listbox.curselection()41 if not chosen:42 messagebox.showinfo(\"To do\", \"Click a task first.\")43 return44 self.listbox.delete(chosen[0])4546 def save(self):47 tasks = list(self.listbox.get(0, \"end\"))48 FILE.write_text(json.dumps(tasks, indent=2), encoding=\"utf-8\")49 messagebox.showinfo(\"To do\", f\"Saved {len(tasks)} tasks in {FILE}\")5051 def load(self):52 if FILE.exists():53 for task in json.loads(FILE.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\")):54 self.listbox.insert(\"end\", task)555657root = tk.Tk()58root.title(\"To do list\")59root.geometry(\"320x300\")60app = TodoApp(root)61root.mainloop() Line 6 The tasks are saved as JSON in your home folder. Line 9 The app is a class based on ttk.Frame. Each window part is an attribute, so the methods can reach it. Line 12 sticky=\"nsew\" plus weight=1 on row 0 and column 0 makes the frame fill the whole window. Line 20 Pressing Enter in the entry calls add with an event object. The button calls add without one, so add has event=None. Line 23 Listbox is a tk widget. ttk has no list box. Line 25 A scrollbar and a list box are linked both ways: command= scrolls the list, yscrollcommand moves the bar. Line 31 Load the saved tasks when the app starts. Line 40 curselection() gives a tuple with the indexes of the chosen lines, empty when nothing is chosen. Line 47 get(0, \"end\") gives every line of the list box as a tuple. The to do list with three tasks, one of them chosen. Run this one on your own computer. A window needs a real screen, so it cannot run inside this page. The picture is a real screenshot of this program, taken with Python 3.14 and Tk 9.0 on Linux. On Windows and macOS the window looks a little different. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 todo_app.py. Close the window to end the program. Keep the logic of an app (rules, calculations, saving) outside the window code. Then you can test it without opening a window, and the window code stays short. This is called separating the model from the view: Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 converter_logic.py. Where to put the code Everything in the callbacks Model and view apart Testing Only by clicking by hand Normal unit tests with pytest Changing the look Touches the rules too Only the view changes Moving to another toolkit or the web Rewrite everything Keep the model, write a new view Bottom line: Callbacks should only read the widgets, call the model and show the result. Lesson 7 of 11"],[37,"freeze","Long tasks without freezing the window","frozen_or_not.pypython 1import queue2import threading3import time4import tkinter as tk5from tkinter import ttk67results = queue.Queue()8910def slow_work():11 time.sleep(3) # pretend to download a big file12 return \"file downloaded\"131415def bad_click():16 status.config(text=\"Working... the window is frozen\")17 slow_work() # mainloop cannot run for 3 seconds18 status.config(text=\"Done the bad way\")192021def good_click():22 status.config(text=\"Working in the background...\")23 progress.start(10)24 threading.Thread(target=worker, daemon=True).start()25 root.after(100, check_result)262728def worker(): # runs in another thread: no tkinter calls here29 results.put(slow_work())303132def check_result(): # runs in the main thread, called by mainloop33 try:34 message = results.get_nowait()35 except queue.Empty:36 root.after(100, check_result) # not ready yet: look again in 100 ms37 return38 progress.stop()39 status.config(text=f\"Done the good way: {message}\")404142root = tk.Tk()43root.title(\"Frozen or not\")44ttk.Button(root, text=\"Slow task, bad way\", command=bad_click).pack(padx=20, pady=(15, 5), fill=\"x\")45ttk.Button(root, text=\"Slow task, good way\", command=good_click).pack(padx=20, pady=5, fill=\"x\")46progress = ttk.Progressbar(root, mode=\"indeterminate\", length=280)47progress.pack(padx=20, pady=5)48status = ttk.Label(root, text=\"Press a button\")49status.pack(pady=(5, 15))50root.mainloop() Line 7 A thread safe queue carries results from the worker thread to the main thread. Line 17 bad_click runs inside mainloop. While it sleeps, no event is handled: no redraw, no clicks. Line 23 The progress bar animates because mainloop keeps running. Line 24 The slow work moves to another thread. daemon=True lets the program end even if it still runs. Line 25 Start checking for the result in 100 ms. Line 28 The worker never touches a widget. tkinter calls belong in the main thread only. Line 34 get_nowait() returns at once, or raises queue.Empty when there is nothing yet. Line 36 Not ready: check again later. This keeps every callback short. The window half way through the good way: the progress bar keeps moving. Run this one on your own computer. A window needs a real screen, so it cannot run inside this page. The picture is a real screenshot of this program, taken with Python 3.14 and Tk 9.0 on Linux. On Windows and macOS the window looks a little different. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 frozen_or_not.py. Close the window to end the program. Long task What to use Waiting or a timer root.after(ms, function) Many small steps (a counter, an animation) Do one step, then after(ms, next_step) Slow input and output (download, database) A thread that puts results in a queue.Queue, checked with after() Heavy CPU work A process (concurrent.futures.ProcessPoolExecutor), results checked with after() Never time.sleep() or a long loop inside a callback Common mistake Tk is not thread safe. Create and change widgets only in the thread that runs mainloop. Other threads hand their results over through a queue, and the main thread picks them up with after(). Lesson 8 of 11"],[37,"maths","The maths: pixels, frames and how fast people click","Screen coordinates x grows to the right, y grows DOWN, (0, 0) is the top left corner A point 50 pixels from the top has y = 50, not y = height − 50. Centre a windowExam favourite x = (screen width − window width) // 2, y = (screen height − window height) // 2 A 300 × 200 window on 1920 × 1080: x = 810, y = 440, geometry \"300x200+810+440\". Frames and speed delay = 1000 / fps ms, speed = pixels per step × 1000 / delay after(20, ...) gives 50 frames a second. 4 pixels a step is 200 pixels a second. Sharing extra space extra for column i = extra × weighti / sum of weights 300 extra pixels, weights 1 and 2: 100 and 200 pixels. Fitts's lawExam favourite time = a + b × log2(D / W + 1) D = distance to the target, W = its width. Big, close buttons are faster to hit. Hick's law time = b × log2(n + 1) n equal choices. 7 menu items: log2 8 = 3 times b. Worked example: how long does it take to hit a small button far away? Take a = 0.1 s and b = 0.15 s, typical numbers for a mouse. The button is 20 pixels wide and 300 pixels away: D / W + 1 = 15 + 1 = 16. log2 16 = 4, so the time is 0.1 + 0.15 × 4 = 0.7 s. Make the button 80 pixels wide: D / W + 1 = 4.75, log2 4.75 ≈ 2.25, time ≈ 0.44 s. A 4 times wider button saves about 0.26 s on every click Lesson 9 of 11"],[37,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths Make a window with labels, buttons and entries, place them with pack or grid, and call your function with command=. Use control variables, bind events, draw and animate on a Canvas with after(), and build an app as a class with ttk widgets, menus and dialogs. Keep the model apart from the view and test it, keep the window alive with after(), threads and queues, and style the app with ttk themes. Understand how Tk talks to the window system, the single UI thread rule in every toolkit, accessibility, high DPI screens, and human factors such as Fitts's and Hick's laws. Move to Qt for large apps. Beginner Make a window that reacts to clicks. Intermediate Build forms, canvases and class based apps. Expert Design responsive, tested and usable interfaces. Lesson 10 of 11"],[37,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","Window programs are full of screen geometry. These problems practise it: moving a robot like an item on a canvas, the paint bucket tool (flood fill), boxes that overlap like widgets or game objects, points on a line, squares, and the area covered by many rectangles. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 657 Robot Return to Origin Easy Count the moves in each direction Like moving an item on a canvas: R adds to x, L takes away. Back at the start means x and y are both 0. 733 Flood Fill Easy The paint bucket tool: breadth first search Start at the clicked pixel and paint every neighbour (up, down, left, right) that has the old colour. Stop at once if the new colour equals the old one. 836 Rectangle Overlap Easy Overlap on both axes Two boxes overlap when the left edge of each is before the right edge of the other, on x AND on y. Touching edges do not count. Games use this for collisions. 1232 Check If It Is a Straight Line Easy Compare slopes with a cross product Points are on one line when (y - y0) × (x1 - x0) equals (y1 - y0) × (x - x0) for every point. Multiplying avoids dividing by zero for vertical lines. 223 Rectangle Area Medium Both areas minus the overlap Add the two areas and take away the overlap. The overlap width is max(0, min of right edges minus max of left edges), the same for the height. 593 Valid Square Medium Six distances between four points A square has 4 equal sides and 2 equal, longer diagonals. Use squared distances to stay with whole numbers, and reject a length of 0. 874 Walking Robot Simulation Medium Directions as a list, obstacles as a set Keep the four directions in a list and turn by moving the index. Store obstacles as a set of tuples so each step is checked in O(1). 1041 Robot Bounded In Circle Medium Run the instructions once and look at the result After one run the robot is either back at the start or facing a new direction. In both cases it stays in a circle. Only \"away and still facing north\" escapes. 391 Perfect Rectangle Hard Areas add up and only four corners stay The small areas must add up to the big box. Toggle every corner in a set: inner corners appear an even number of times and cancel out, so exactly the four outer corners must be left. 850 Rectangle Area II Hard Coordinate compression on a grid Collect every x and y edge, sort them and mark which small cells between them are covered. Add up the covered cells and take the result modulo 109 + 7. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 11 of 11"],[37,"recap","Chapter summary","tkinter comes with Python. Make root = tk.Tk(), add widgets with their parent, place them, then call root.mainloop(). mainloop waits for events and calls your functions. command=function, without brackets. bind(event, function) for keys and mouse. grid with sticky and weight for forms, pack for simple stacks, never both in one parent. StringVar, IntVar and BooleanVar keep widgets in step. The Canvas draws items you move with move and coords, animated with after(). Callbacks must return fast: after() for timers and steps, threads with a queue for slow work. Keep the model apart from the view so you can test it. Next: Next: how Python itself runs your code, from source to bytecode. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: place keypad buttons in a grid, read and centre window geometry strings, and write a to do list model with undo.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[38,"","How Python Runs: From Source Code to Output","Compilers and interpreters side by side, then what CPython does with your file, step by step: tokens and indentation, the PEG parser and the syntax tree, the compiler, code objects and bytecode read with dis, __pycache__ and the .pyc header, the evaluation loop on a value stack, frames and tracebacks, the LEGB rule and UnboundLocalError, objects and names, reference counting and the cycle collector, the GIL, free threading and subinterpreters, the specializing interpreter and the JIT, imports and start up, inspection tools, what changed up to 3.14, pytest assertion rewriting and a module shadowing bug. · how Python works · Python execution · CPython internals · interpreter · compiler · bytecode · dis · pyc · __pycache__ · PVM · evaluation loop · AST · tokenizer · frames · GIL · garbage collection · reference counting · JIT · free threading · Python 3.14 · interview Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Step 1: How Python Works›How Python runs Step 1 · Topic 1 of 1 · Beginner to expert"],[38,"top","","See how a compiler and an interpreter differ, then follow one Python file through CPython: the tokenizer, the parser and its syntax tree, the compiler and bytecode, __pycache__, the evaluation loop and its value stack, frames and tracebacks, names and scopes, objects, reference counting and the garbage collector, the GIL and free threading, the specializing interpreter and the JIT, imports and start up, with every change up to Python 3.14. About 50 min21 lessons8 animations25 runnable examples13 line by line5 terminal runs8 worked problems16 quiz questions5 assignments2 QA essential lessonsRun with CPython 3.14.6 For QA engineers Testers who know how Python runs read tracebacks faster, understand why pytest shows the values in a failed assert, and spot module shadowing and stale cache problems that only happen on some machines. 1From source code to output 2Compiler or interpreter: two ways to translate 3Compiled or interpreted: the honest answer 4Try it: follow your own code through CPython 5Step 1: the tokenizer 6Step 2: the parser and the syntax tree 7Step 3: the compiler and code objects 8__pycache__ and .pyc files 9Step 4: the evaluation loop 10Frames, the call stack and tracebacks 11Names and scopes: the LEGB rule 12Everything is an object 13Memory: reference counting and the garbage collector 14The GIL, threads and free-threaded Python 15Faster CPython: specialization and the JIT 16What import really does 17What happens before your first line runs 18Tools to look inside 19What changed inside CPython, release by release 20Python internals in test automation 21Real world scenario: \"it works on my machine\" Lesson 1 of 21Beginner"],[38,"big-picture","From source code to output","When you run python main.py, the computer does not understand Python directly. A program called the interpreter reads your file and does the work for you. The interpreter almost everyone uses is CPython, written in the C language and published by the Python Software Foundation. It is the one you download from python.org, and it is what this chapter describes. CPython works in two stages, just like Java. First it compiles your text into bytecode, a list of small instructions. Then the Python Virtual Machine runs that bytecode one instruction at a time. The big difference from Java is that you never call the compiler yourself. Python does both stages every time you press Run, in a fraction of a second. Press Play in the animation below. Each phase appears on the map only when the journey reaches it, and the panel beside the map shows what that phase does to your own code. Change the program and every phase changes with it. 🎬 Try it"],[38,"big-picture","From main.py to output, one phase at a time","Each step adds the next phase to the map. The panel beside it shows what that phase does to your code. Edit the program to change every phase. Your program Compile time: before your first line runs main.pysource text Tokenizertext to tokens Parsertokens to a tree Compilertree to bytecode .pyc fileimported modules only Code objectbytecode + names + constants Run time: the Python Virtual Machine Evaluation loopone instruction at a time Objects and memorynames point at objects Built-ins and C codeprint, len, max ... Outputwhat you see Not started The map is empty Press Play, or the step arrow, and the first phase appears. Press ▶ Play to start the journey. Each phase appears only when the journey reaches it. 0 / 10 hello.py · ▶ Runs # hello.py: a small bill, the kind of program every learner writes first name = \"Asha\" items = 3 price = 40 total = items * price print(\"Hi\", name + \"!\", \"Your bill is\", total, \"rupees\") Output Hi Asha! Your bill is 120 rupees Here is the same journey for this small bill, in plain words: Read. CPython opens hello.py and reads it as text, using UTF-8 by default. Tokenize. The characters are grouped into tokens such as total, =, items, *. Parse. The tokens are checked against Python's grammar and arranged into a tree. Compile. The tree becomes bytecode inside a code object. Run. The evaluation loop runs the bytecode. print is a built in function written in C, and it writes the text to your screen. Step Java Python (CPython) Source file Hello.java hello.py Compiler javac, run by you Built into python, runs automatically Bytecode file Hello.class, always written .pyc in __pycache__, only for imported modules Virtual machine JVM The CPython evaluation loop, often called the PVM Machine code JIT compiler on by default Interpreted. An experimental JIT exists since 3.13, off by default Memory Tracing garbage collector Reference counting plus a cycle collector Type checks At compile time At run time, when each instruction runs The same .py file runs on Windows, macOS and Linux because each has its own CPython. The bytecode is tied to the Python version, not to the operating system. Lesson 2 of 21Beginner"],[38,"compile-interp","Compiler or interpreter: two ways to translate","A computer's processor only understands machine code, long lists of very simple instructions stored as numbers. Nobody writes that by hand any more. We write in a language people can read, such as Python, C or Java, and a program translates it for the processor. There are two classic ways to do this translation."],[38,"compile-interp","Think of two kinds of human translators","When a famous novel is published in another language, a translator first translates the whole book. Only when every chapter is done is the new book printed, and after that anyone can read it again and again without the translator. That is how a compiler works. At an international cricket press conference, a live interpreter stands next to the captain. The captain says one sentence, the interpreter says it in Hindi at once, then waits for the next sentence. Nothing is written down, so if the same answer is given tomorrow, it is interpreted all over again. That is how an interpreter works. 🎬 Try it"],[38,"compile-interp","Compiler and interpreter, side by side","The same program goes to both. Watch when each one translates and when the first output appears. Pick a mistake to see who finds it first. The program Show CPython as a third lane Compilerlike C, C++ or Go Waiting for step 1 Interpreterlike an old BASIC, or a shell running a script Waiting for step 1 Press ▶ Play. Both lanes start at the same moment, and every box appears only when its step is reached. 0 / 5 Things to try in the animation: With No mistake, look at the step where the first output appears in each lane. The interpreter prints at step 1. The compiler has to read and translate everything first. Pick Syntax mistake on line 3. The compiler prints nothing at all. The interpreter happily runs lines 1 and 2 and only then stops. Pick Unknown name on line 3. A compiled language that declares its names, such as C or Java, catches this before running. An interpreter finds it only when it reaches that line. Tick Show CPython as a third lane. It behaves like the compiler for the syntax mistake and like the interpreter for the unknown name. Question Compiler Interpreter How much does it translate at once The whole program One line or statement at a time What it leaves behind A program file, for example app.exe Nothing, the translation is used and forgotten When the first output appears Only after the whole program is translated Almost at once When mistakes are reported All together, before anything runs One at a time, when the line is reached Speed of running Fast, the translation is already done Slower, every line is translated each time it runs Running on another computer Needs a new translation for that processor and operating system Runs anywhere the interpreter is installed Examples C, C++, Go, Rust Shell scripts, early BASIC"],[38,"compile-interp","So which one is CPython","Both, one after the other. CPython first compiles the whole file, so a syntax error anywhere stops the program before line 1 runs, just like a compiler. But it compiles to bytecode, not to machine code, and it saves no program file for your main script. Then the evaluation loop interprets that bytecode one instruction at a time, so a missing name is found only when its line runs, just like an interpreter: A name error is found only when its line runs · 💥 Raises an error print(\"Line 1 runs\") print(\"Line 2 runs\") print(total) # total was never created print(\"Line 4 never runs\") Output Line 1 runs Line 2 runs Traceback (most recent call last): File \"main.py\", line 3, in <module> print(total) # total was never created ^^^^^ NameError: name 'total' is not defined Lines 1 and 2 printed before the error. If line 3 had a syntax error instead, nothing at all would print. Java works in the same two step way: javac compiles to bytecode and the JVM runs it. The Python prompt you get by typing python with no file is the closest to a pure interpreter: each statement is compiled and run the moment you press Enter. A compiler translates the whole program first and runs it later. An interpreter translates and runs one line at a time. CPython compiles the whole file to bytecode, then interprets the bytecode. Lesson 3 of 21Beginner"],[38,"implementations","Compiled or interpreted: the honest answer","People often say \"Python is interpreted\" and \"Java is compiled\". Both statements are half true. CPython compiles your code to bytecode and then interprets the bytecode. Python the language is a set of rules. An implementation is a program that follows those rules. There are several: Implementation Written in How it runs your code Where you meet it CPython C Bytecode on an interpreter loop, optional JIT python.org, Linux, macOS, most jobs PyPy RPython A tracing JIT that compiles hot loops to machine code Long running pure Python programs MicroPython C A small compiler and VM for chips with little memory Microcontrollers, school robotics kits GraalPy Java Runs on the GraalVM, close to the JVM world Mixing Python with Java Pyodide CPython built for WebAssembly The real CPython inside a web browser The \"Edit and run\" buttons on this page Which Python is running this code · ▶ Runs import sys, platform print(\"Implementation:\", platform.python_implementation()) print(\"Version: \", sys.version.split()[0]) print(\"Cache tag: \", sys.implementation.cache_tag) print(\"Program name: \", sys.executable.split(\"/\")[-1]) Output Implementation: CPython Version: 3.14.6 Cache tag: cpython-314 Program name: python3.14 The cache tagcpython-314 is the name CPython uses for its bytecode files. It contains the implementation and the version, because bytecode from 3.13 cannot be used by 3.14. On this page, every recorded output came from CPython 3.14 on Linux. When you press Edit and run, the code runs in your browser with Pyodide, which is CPython 3.14 compiled to WebAssembly. A few things that need an operating system, such as threads, are marked as not runnable in the browser. Lesson 4 of 21Beginner"],[38,"try-it","Try it: follow your own code through CPython","This animation is a small working model of CPython 3.14. Type a few lines of your own, or pick an example, then open a tab and press Play or step forward. Nothing is shown before its step: the tokens appear one by one, then the nodes of the syntax tree, then each bytecode instruction, and finally the bytecode runs on a value stack, one instruction per step. It was checked against the real CPython 3.14 on more than ten thousand generated programs, so what you see here is what CPython really does. 🎬 Try it"],[38,"try-it","Watch CPython run your code","Type a few lines (names, numbers, strings, + - * / // % **, comparisons, print and a few built ins). Then follow them through the four stages. Your program The tokenizer reads characters and groups them into tokens, the same way python -m tokenize main.py does. Hover a token to see its type. NAMEpriceOP=NUMBER40NEWLINE⏎NAMEqtyOP=NUMBER3NEWLINE⏎NAMEtotalOP=NAMEpriceOP*NAMEqtyOP+NUMBER5NEWLINE⏎NAMEprintOP(STRING\"Total:\"OP,NAMEtotalOP)NEWLINE⏎ENDMARKER⏎ The parser checks the grammar and builds an abstract syntax tree. Brackets and spaces are gone. Only the meaning is left. Module Assignprice = Constant40 Assignqty = Constant3 Assigntotal = BinOp+ BinOp* Nameprice Nameqty Constant5 Exprexpression statement Callcall Nameprint Constant'Total:' Nametotal The same tree as ast.dump(ast.parse(code)) prints it Module(body=[Assign(targets=[Name(id='price', ctx=Store())], value=Constant(value=40)), Assign(targets=[Name(id='qty', ctx=Store())], value=Constant(value=3)), Assign(targets=[Name(id='total', ctx=Store())], value=BinOp(left=BinOp(left=Name(id='price', ctx=Load()), op=Mult(), right=Name(id='qty', ctx=Load())), op=Add(), right=Constant(value=5))), Expr(value=Call(func=Name(id='print', ctx=Load()), args=[Constant(value='Total:'), Name(id='total', ctx=Load())]))]) The compiler walks the tree and writes bytecode. Look for constant folding: arithmetic on plain numbers is done once, here, instead of every time the line runs. 0 RESUME 0 1 LOAD_SMALL_INT 40 STORE_NAME 0 (price) 2 LOAD_SMALL_INT 3 STORE_NAME 1 (qty) 3 LOAD_NAME 0 (price) LOAD_NAME 1 (qty) BINARY_OP 5 (*) LOAD_SMALL_INT 5 BINARY_OP 0 (+) STORE_NAME 2 (total) 4 LOAD_NAME 3 (print) PUSH_NULL LOAD_CONST ('Total:') LOAD_NAME 2 (total) CALL 2 POP_TOP LOAD_CONST (None) RETURN_VALUE Same layout as dis.dis. LOAD_CONST shows its value. The real listing also shows its position in co_consts. Bytecode RESUME L1LOAD_SMALL_INT40 STORE_NAME0(price) L2LOAD_SMALL_INT3 STORE_NAME1(qty) L3LOAD_NAME0(price) LOAD_NAME1(qty) BINARY_OP5(*) LOAD_SMALL_INT5 BINARY_OP0(+) STORE_NAME2(total) L4LOAD_NAME3(print) PUSH_NULL LOAD_CONST('Total:') LOAD_NAME2(total) CALL2 POP_TOP LOAD_CONST(None) RETURN_VALUE Value stack Names (globals) Output Press ▶ Play or step forward. Each step reads one more token. 0 / 24 Things to try: Write x = 2 * 3 and open Bytecode. There is no multiplication. The compiler already worked out 6. Remove a closing bracket. The error appears before any step can run. Use a name before giving it a value. The program runs until the LOAD_NAME that cannot find it, then stops with NameError. Write print(\"5\" + 5). Python only finds out that a string and a number cannot be added when BINARY_OP actually runs. Lesson 5 of 21Intermediate"],[38,"tokens","Step 1: the tokenizer","The tokenizer, also called the lexer, reads characters from left to right and groups them into tokens, the smallest pieces that mean something. It is the same thing you do when you read a sentence: you see words, not single letters. Python has a special job here that most languages do not have. Indentation is part of the grammar, so the tokenizer keeps a stack of indentation levels. When a line is indented more than the previous one, it produces an INDENT token. When the indentation goes back, it produces a DEDENT token. The parser never sees spaces, only these tokens. The tokens Python sees, including INDENT and DEDENT · ▶ Runs import io, tokenize source = \"\"\"if total > 100: discount = 10 print(discount) \"\"\"for tok in tokenize.generate_tokens(io.StringIO(source).readline): kind = tokenize.tok_name[tok.type] print(f\"{kind:<9} {tok.string!r:<14} line {tok.start[0]}\") Output NAME 'if' line 1 NAME 'total' line 1 OP '>' line 1 NUMBER '100' line 1 OP ':' line 1 NEWLINE '\\n' line 1 INDENT ' ' line 2 NAME 'discount' line 2 OP '=' line 2 NUMBER '10' line 2 NEWLINE '\\n' line 2 DEDENT '' line 3 NAME 'print' line 3 OP '(' line 3 NAME 'discount' line 3 OP ')' line 3 NEWLINE '\\n' line 3 ENDMARKER '' line 4 NEWLINE ends a logical line of code. NL is a line break that does not end a statement, such as a blank line or a line inside brackets. OP covers operators and punctuation like >, =, : and brackets. Mixing tabs and spaces so that the meaning is unclear raises TabError right here, before anything runs. You can see the tokens of any file from a terminal with python -m tokenize main.py. Lesson 6 of 21Intermediate"],[38,"parser","Step 2: the parser and the syntax tree","The parser takes the tokens and checks them against Python's grammar. Since Python 3.9, CPython uses a PEG parser (Parsing Expression Grammar), generated from a grammar file that lives in the CPython source. The result is an abstract syntax tree, or AST. \"Abstract\" means that brackets, spaces and commas are gone. Only the meaning is kept. The syntax tree of one line · ▶ Runs import ast tree = ast.parse(\"total = price * qty + 5\") print(ast.dump(tree, indent=2)) Output Module( body=[ Assign( targets=[ Name(id='total', ctx=Store())], value=BinOp( left=BinOp( left=Name(id='price', ctx=Load()), op=Mult(), right=Name(id='qty', ctx=Load())), op=Add(), right=Constant(value=5)))]) Read the tree from the bottom up. price * qty is a BinOp with Mult. That whole node is the left side of another BinOp with Add. The tree already encodes that multiplication happens before addition, so the later stages never think about operator precedence again. The parser reads the whole file before anything runs. That is why one syntax error stops the program completely, even the lines above it that are fine: A syntax error stops the program before line 1 runs · 💥 Raises an error print(\"Step 1: this line never runs\") total = 100if total > 50 print(\"big order\") Output File \"main.py\", line 3 if total > 50 ^ SyntaxError: expected ':' Tools you use every day read this tree: pytest rewrites assert statements in it, linters such as Ruff and Pylint walk it to find bugs, and formatters like Black rebuild your file from it. The ast module lets your own code do the same. Lesson 7 of 21Intermediate"],[38,"compiler","Step 3: the compiler and code objects","The compiler walks the tree and produces a code object. It does this in three small steps: Symbol table. For every function, the compiler decides whether each name is local, global, from an enclosing function or free. This decision is made once, here, and never changes while the program runs. Code generation. Each node of the tree becomes one or more instructions. Optimisation. A peephole optimiser cleans up the instruction list. It removes dead code, shortens jumps and folds constants. Every function has its own code object, stored in __code__. It is a plain Python object you can inspect: Inside a code object · ▶ Runs def add_tax(price, rate=0.18): tax = price * rate return round(price + tax, 2) code = add_tax.__code__ print(\"name: \", code.co_name) print(\"arguments: \", code.co_argcount) print(\"local names: \", code.co_varnames) print(\"constants: \", code.co_consts) print(\"other names: \", code.co_names) print(\"bytecode size:\", len(code.co_code), \"bytes\") print(add_tax(100)) Output name: add_tax arguments: 2 local names: ('price', 'rate', 'tax') constants: (2,) other names: ('round',) bytecode size: 54 bytes 118.0 The dis module (short for disassembler) prints the bytecode in a readable form. The first column is the source line, then the instruction name, its argument, and in brackets what the argument means. Read the bytecode with dis · ▶ Runs import dis def add_tax(price, rate): tax = price * rate return price + tax dis.dis(add_tax) Output 3 RESUME 0 4 LOAD_FAST_BORROW_LOAD_FAST_BORROW 1 (price, rate) BINARY_OP 5 (*) STORE_FAST 2 (tax) 5 LOAD_FAST_BORROW_LOAD_FAST_BORROW 2 (price, tax) BINARY_OP 0 (+) RETURN_VALUE Notice LOAD_FAST_BORROW_LOAD_FAST_BORROW. Local variables live in a small array inside the frame, so reading one is just an index, which is fast. Here the compiler even joined two local loads into one superinstruction. STORE_FAST writes to that array. Global names need slower dictionary lookups. Look at the code object again: the default value 0.18 is not among the constants. Defaults are worked out once, when the def line runs, and kept on the function in add_tax.__defaults__. The 2 in the constants is the second argument of round. Constant folding is the easiest optimisation to see. Arithmetic on plain numbers is done once by the compiler, not every time the line runs: Constant folding · ▶ Runs import dis # The compiler does arithmetic on plain numbers once, before the program runs dis.dis(\"seconds_per_day = 60 * 60 * 24\") Output 0 RESUME 0 1 LOAD_CONST 2 (86400) STORE_NAME 0 (seconds_per_day) LOAD_CONST 1 (None) RETURN_VALUE Bytecode changes in every Python release. LOAD_SMALL_INT and LOAD_FAST_BORROW are new in 3.14, and 3.15 will change more. Bytecode is an internal detail. Use it to understand Python, never as something your programs depend on. Lesson 8 of 21Intermediate"],[38,"pyc","__pycache__ and .pyc files","Compiling is quick, but a big project imports hundreds of modules. To save that work, CPython writes the bytecode of every imported module to a .pyc file in a folder named __pycache__, next to the source. The next run checks that the .pyc is still fresh and loads it directly, skipping the tokenizer, the parser and the compiler. Watch __pycache__ appear, then read a .pyc header · 🧪 Run in a terminal 📄 shop.py GST = 0.18def bill(amount): return round(amount * (1 + GST), 2) 📄 main.py import shop print(\"Bill for 100 rupees:\", shop.bill(100)) 📄 read_pyc.py import glob, importlib.util, struct path = glob.glob(\"__pycache__/shop.*.pyc\")[0] data = open(path, \"rb\").read() magic, flags, mtime, size = struct.unpack(\"<4sIII\", data[:16]) print(\"file: \", path) print(\"magic number: \", magic, \"matches this Python:\", magic == importlib.util.MAGIC_NUMBER) print(\"flags: \", flags, \"(0 means: compare the source timestamp and size)\") print(\"source size: \", size, \"bytes\") print(\"bytecode part: \", len(data) - 16, \"bytes\") Terminal $ python main.py $ ls -R $ python read_pyc.py $ rm -r __pycache__ && python -B main.py && ls Terminal output Bill for 100 rupees: 118.0 --- what is in the folder now .: __pycache__ main.py read_pyc.py shop.py ./__pycache__: shop.cpython-314.pyc --- the .pyc header file: __pycache__/shop.cpython-314.pyc magic number: b'+\\x0e\\r\\n' matches this Python: True flags: 0 (0 means: compare the source timestamp and size) source size: 70 bytes bytecode part: 363 bytes --- run again with -B: nothing new is written Bill for 100 rupees: 118.0 main.py read_pyc.py shop.py What the example shows: Only shop.py got a .pyc. The script you start, main.py, is compiled in memory every time and never cached. The name shop.cpython-314.pyc holds the cache tag, so Python 3.13 and 3.14 can share a folder without breaking each other. The 16 byte header starts with a magic number that changes whenever the bytecode format changes. If it does not match, the file is ignored and rebuilt. By default the header also stores the source file's modification time and size. If you edit shop.py, they no longer match and Python compiles it again. python -B or the environment variable PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 stops Python from writing .pyc files. PYTHONPYCACHEPREFIX moves them all to one separate folder. Tool What it does python -m py_compile shop.py Compile one file to its .pyc python -m compileall src/ Compile a whole folder, used when building Docker images and installers --invalidation-mode checked-hash Store a hash of the source instead of the time, for builds that must be reproducible A .pyc file is not faster to run. It only starts faster, because the compile step is skipped. It also does not hide your code: tools can turn it back into readable Python. Always add __pycache__/ to .gitignore. Lesson 9 of 21Advanced"],[38,"eval-loop","Step 4: the evaluation loop","Now the code object meets the evaluation loop, the heart of CPython. It is a C function that repeats three things for as long as the program runs: read the next instruction, do what it says, move on. Each instruction works with a value stack. Loading a name pushes its value on top. An operator pops its inputs and pushes its result. Storing a name pops the top value. 🎬 Try it"],[38,"eval-loop","The evaluation loop runs total = price * qty","Each step is one bytecode instruction. Watch the value stack grow and shrink. price = 40 qty = 3 total = price * qty print(total) Instruction now Value stack (top on the right) Names in this frame Output Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 9 Every instruction has an offset · ▶ Runs import dis code = compile(\"total = price * qty\", \"<bill>\", \"exec\") for ins in dis.get_instructions(code): print(f\"{ins.offset:>3} {ins.opname:<16} {ins.argrepr}\") Output 0 RESUME 2 LOAD_NAME price 4 LOAD_NAME qty 6 BINARY_OP * 18 STORE_NAME total 20 LOAD_CONST None 22 RETURN_VALUE The offsets go up by 2 because every instruction takes two bytes: one for the instruction and one for its argument. Some instructions also keep hidden cache entries after them, which is why offsets can jump by more. The loop itself is surprisingly simple. Here is the whole idea in a few lines of Python. Press Line by line to watch the stack change. Build your own evaluation loop in 15 lines · ▶ Runs # A tiny stack machine that works the way the CPython evaluation loop does program = [(\"LOAD\", \"price\"), (\"LOAD\", \"qty\"), (\"MULTIPLY\", None), (\"STORE\", \"total\")] names = {\"price\": 40, \"qty\": 3} stack = [] for op, arg in program: if op == \"LOAD\": stack.append(names[arg]) elif op == \"MULTIPLY\": right = stack.pop() left = stack.pop() stack.append(left * right) elif op == \"STORE\": names[arg] = stack.pop() print(f\"{op:<9} {str(arg):<6} stack = {stack}\") print(names) Output LOAD price stack = [40] LOAD qty stack = [40, 3] MULTIPLY None stack = [120] STORE total stack = [] {'price': 40, 'qty': 3, 'total': 120} The real loop knows about 150 instructions (see dis.opmap) plus more than 80 specialized forms of them, and many tricks for speed, but this is honestly how it works. Between instructions, CPython also checks for a few things: a pending signal such as Ctrl + C, a request to switch threads, and the hooks used by debuggers. Lesson 10 of 21Advanced"],[38,"frames","Frames, the call stack and tracebacks","Every time a function is called, CPython creates a frame. A frame holds everything that one call needs: which code object it is running, the current instruction, the local variables and its own value stack. Frames sit on the call stack. The newest one is on top and is the one running. When a function returns, its frame is thrown away and the one below continues from where it paused. 🎬 Try it"],[38,"frames","Frames on the call stack","Every call gets a new frame with its own local names. A return throws the frame away. def tax(amount): return amount * 0.18 def bill(price, qty): subtotal = price * qty return subtotal + tax(subtotal) total = bill(100, 2) print(total) Call stack (newest first) Locals of the running frame Values returned Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 7 A traceback is a print of the call stack at the moment an error happened. Python prints it with the oldest frame first, which is why it says \"most recent call last\". Read it from the bottom up: the last lines show the error and the exact place it happened. A traceback is the call stack, printed · 💥 Raises an error def apply_coupon(total, code): discounts = {\"SAVE10\": 10, \"SAVE20\": 20} return total - discounts[code] def checkout(cart_total, coupon): return apply_coupon(cart_total, coupon) print(checkout(500, \"SAVE10\")) print(checkout(500, \"FREE100\")) Output 490 Traceback (most recent call last): File \"main.py\", line 9, in <module> print(checkout(500, \"FREE100\")) ~~~~~~~~^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ File \"main.py\", line 6, in checkout return apply_coupon(cart_total, coupon) File \"main.py\", line 3, in apply_coupon return total - discounts[code] ~~~~~~~~~^^^^^^ KeyError: 'FREE100' Walk the frames, then hit the recursion limit · ▶ Runs import sys def inner(): frame = sys._getframe() chain = [] while frame: chain.append(frame.f_code.co_name) frame = frame.f_back return chain def middle(): return inner() print(middle()) print(\"recursion limit:\", sys.getrecursionlimit()) def countdown(n): return0if n == 0else countdown(n - 1) try: countdown(5000) exceptRecursionErroras e: print(\"RecursionError:\", e) Output ['inner', 'middle', '<module>'] recursion limit: 1000 RecursionError: maximum recursion depth exceeded Since Python 3.11, frames are small C structures inside the interpreter. A full Python frame object, the one sys._getframe() returns, is only built when someone asks for it. This change made function calls much cheaper. The recursion limit (1000 by default) protects your program from running out of stack memory. You can raise it with sys.setrecursionlimit, but a loop is usually the better fix for deep recursion. Lesson 11 of 21Advanced"],[38,"names","Names and scopes: the LEGB rule","When the evaluation loop needs a name, it searches up to four places in a fixed order: Local, Enclosing, Global and Built-in. The first match wins. 🎬 Try it"],[38,"names","How Python finds a name: L, E, G, B","Python looks in four places, in this order, and stops at the first match. rate = 0.18 def make_bill(price): discount = 5 def final(): total = price - discount return round(total * (1 + rate), 2) return final() Local (final) Enclosing (make_bill) Global (module) Built-in Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 5 Local, Enclosing, Global, Built-in · ▶ Runs rate = 0.18# global namedef make_bill(price): discount = 5# enclosing name, seen by the inner functiondef final(): total = price - discount # local namereturn round(total * (1 + rate), 2) # round is found in builtinsreturn final() print(make_bill(100)) import builtins print(\"round lives in builtins:\", builtins.round is round) Output 112.1 round lives in builtins: True Here is the part that surprises people. The decision \"is this name local\" is made by the compiler, not while the program runs. If a function assigns to a name anywhere in its body, that name is local everywhere in the function, even on lines before the assignment. Why UnboundLocalError happens · ▶ Runs import dis visits = 0def add_visit(): visits = visits + 1# the compiler sees an assignment, so visits is local herereturn visits try: add_visit() exceptUnboundLocalErroras e: print(\"UnboundLocalError:\", e) dis.dis(add_visit) Output UnboundLocalError: cannot access local variable 'visits' where it is not associated with a value 5 RESUME 0 6 LOAD_FAST_CHECK 0 (visits) LOAD_SMALL_INT 1 BINARY_OP 0 (+) STORE_FAST 0 (visits) 7 LOAD_FAST_BORROW 0 (visits) RETURN_VALUE The bytecode shows the reason: the compiler used LOAD_FAST_CHECK for visits, an instruction for local names, so Python never looked at the global. Add global visits at the top of the function to tell the compiler you mean the module level name, or better, pass the value in and return the new one. Where the name lives Instruction How fast Local variable LOAD_FAST, STORE_FAST Array index, fastest Enclosing function LOAD_DEREF Through a cell object Module global or built-in LOAD_GLOBAL Dictionary lookups, cached by the specializing interpreter Module level code LOAD_NAME, STORE_NAME Dictionary lookups Lesson 12 of 21Intermediate"],[38,"objects","Everything is an object","In Python, every value is an object that lives in memory: numbers, strings, lists, functions, classes, even modules. Every object has three things: an identity (its address, shown by id()), a type (shown by type()) and a value. A variable is not a box that holds a value. It is a name that points at an object. Two names can point at the same object. This is the single most useful fact for understanding Python's behaviour. 🎬 Try it"],[38,"objects","Names, objects and reference counts","A name never holds a value. It points at an object, and the object counts how many references it has. a = [1, 2] b = a b.append(3) del a b = None Names Objects in memory What happened Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 5 Names point at objects · ▶ Runs a = [1, 2, 3] b = a # a second name for the same list b.append(4) print(a, a is b, id(a) == id(b)) c = [1, 2, 3, 4] print(a == c, a is c) # equal values, two different objects x = 256 y = int(\"256\") print(\"256 is shared:\", x is y) x = 257 y = int(\"257\") print(\"257 is shared:\", x is y) print(type(5), type(type(5)), isinstance(print, object)) Output [1, 2, 3, 4] True True True False 256 is shared: True 257 is shared: False <class 'int'> <class 'type'> True == asks \"do these have the same value\". is asks \"are these the very same object\". CPython creates the integers from -5 to 256 once at startup and shares them, because they are used everywhere. That is why 256 is 256 is true and the same test with 257 can be false. Never use is to compare numbers or strings. Use it only for None, True and False. Strings that look like names are often interned: kept once and shared. Python does this for its own identifiers to make attribute and dictionary lookups faster. Lesson 13 of 21Advanced"],[38,"memory","Memory: reference counting and the garbage collector","CPython frees memory in two ways. Reference counting. Every object keeps a count of how many references point at it. A new name, a list slot or a function argument adds one. When a reference goes away, the count drops. When it reaches zero, the object is freed at once. This frees the vast majority of objects, and it does so immediately, which is why a file object that nobody uses any more gets closed straight away in CPython. The cycle collector. Reference counting has one weak spot: a reference cycle. If object A points at B and B points back at A, both counts stay above zero even when your program can no longer reach either of them. The gc module finds such groups of unreachable objects and frees them. Reference counts and a reference cycle · ▶ Runs import sys, gc order = {\"id\": 1042} before = sys.getrefcount(order) alias = order print(\"one more name adds one reference:\", sys.getrefcount(order) - before) del alias print(\"del removes it again:\", sys.getrefcount(order) - before) classNode: def __init__(self, name): self.name = name self.partner = Nonedef __del__(self): print(\"freed\", self.name) a, b = Node(\"a\"), Node(\"b\") a.partner, b.partner = b, a # a cycle: each keeps the other alivedel a, b print(\"names deleted, but nothing was freed yet\") found = gc.collect() print(\"the cycle collector found unreachable objects:\", found > 0) print(\"collector thresholds:\", gc.get_threshold()) Output one more name adds one reference: 1 del removes it again: 0 names deleted, but nothing was freed yet freed a freed b the cycle collector found unreachable objects: True collector thresholds: (2000, 10, 10) The collector runs automatically when enough new container objects have been created since the last run. The thresholds above control how often that happens. It sorts objects into generations, because most objects die young: new objects are checked often, and objects that survive several collections are checked rarely. A 3.14 story worth knowing. Python 3.14.0 shipped an incremental collector, which spreads the work over many small steps. Users then reported that some production systems used a lot more memory, so Python 3.14.5 went back to the generational collector from 3.13. Real projects found a problem that tests had not, and the core team chose the safe option. Small objects (up to 512 bytes) do not come straight from the operating system. CPython's own allocator, pymalloc, takes memory in 1 MiB arenas, splits them into 16 KiB pools, and splits each pool into equal sized blocks. Many tiny objects can then be created and freed very quickly. Lesson 14 of 21Advanced"],[38,"gil","The GIL, threads and free-threaded Python","The Global Interpreter Lock (GIL) is a lock inside CPython. A thread must hold it to run Python bytecode, so in a normal build only one thread runs Python code at any moment. Reference counting is the main reason it exists: without the lock, two threads could change the same count at the same time and break it. Four threads share one counter safely · ▶ Runs import sys, threading print(\"GIL enabled:\", sys._is_gil_enabled()) print(\"switch interval:\", sys.getswitchinterval(), \"seconds\") counter = 0 lock = threading.Lock() def work(): global counter for _ in range(100_000): with lock: counter += 1 threads = [threading.Thread(target=work) for _ in range(4)] for t in threads: t.start() for t in threads: t.join() print(\"counter:\", counter) Output GIL enabled: True switch interval: 0.005 seconds counter: 400000 The running thread is asked to give up the GIL every 5 milliseconds, the switch interval, so other threads get a turn. Waiting for a network reply, a file or time.sleep releases the GIL. That is why threads still speed up programs that mostly wait, such as API tests or web scraping. Pure Python number crunching does not get faster with threads in the normal build. Use processes (multiprocessing, concurrent.futures.ProcessPoolExecutor) or libraries like NumPy, which do their heavy work in C without the GIL. The GIL does not make your code thread safe. counter += 1 is several instructions, and a switch can happen between them. The example uses a Lock for that reason. Free-threaded Python. Python 3.13 added an experimental build without the GIL, installed as python3.13t. In Python 3.14 it is officially supported, though still optional. Threads then run Python code truly in parallel on several CPU cores. Single threaded code runs a little slower in that build, and some C extensions are not ready yet, so measure your own program before you switch. Python 3.14 also added the concurrent.interpreters module. It runs several interpreters inside one process, each with its own modules and its own GIL, so they can work in parallel while staying isolated. A second interpreter in the same process (new in 3.14) · ▶ Runs from concurrent import interpreters worker = interpreters.create() worker.exec(\"total = sum(range(1, 101))\\nprint('inside the second interpreter, total =', total)\") print(\"the main interpreter has its own globals:\", \"total\"notin globals()) worker.close() Output inside the second interpreter, total = 5050 the main interpreter has its own globals: True Lesson 15 of 21Expert"],[38,"faster","Faster CPython: specialization and the JIT","Since Python 3.11, CPython watches your code while it runs. This is the specializing adaptive interpreter. When an instruction keeps seeing the same kind of data, CPython swaps it for a specialized version that skips the general checks. A BINARY_OP that always multiplies two small integers becomes BINARY_OP_MULTIPLY_INT. If the data changes later, it goes back to the general form. The specializing interpreter rewrites hot code · ▶ Runs import dis def price_with_tax(price): return price * 118 // 100 print(\"Before warming up:\") dis.dis(price_with_tax, adaptive=True) for p in range(1000): price_with_tax(p) print(\"After 1000 calls:\") dis.dis(price_with_tax, adaptive=True) Output Before warming up: 3 RESUME 0 4 LOAD_FAST_BORROW 0 (price) LOAD_SMALL_INT 118 BINARY_OP 5 (*) LOAD_SMALL_INT 100 BINARY_OP 2 (//) RETURN_VALUE After 1000 calls: 3 RESUME_CHECK 0 4 LOAD_FAST_BORROW 0 (price) LOAD_SMALL_INT 118 BINARY_OP_MULTIPLY_INT 5 (*) LOAD_SMALL_INT 100 BINARY_OP 2 (//) RETURN_VALUE This happens on its own. You do not change anything in your code, and programs got noticeably faster between 3.10 and 3.11 just from this work. The JIT compiler. Python 3.13 added an experimental just in time compiler. It uses a technique called copy and patch: ready made pieces of machine code are copied and filled in for hot sequences of instructions. In 3.14 it is included in the official Windows and macOS installers but stays off by default. You switch it on with the environment variable PYTHON_JIT=1. Whether it is available at all depends on how your Python was built. Is the JIT here, and is it on · 🧪 Run in a terminal 📄 check.py import sys print(\"JIT built in:\", sys._jit.is_available(), \"| switched on:\", sys._jit.is_enabled()) Terminal $ python check.py $ PYTHON_JIT=1 python check.py Terminal output JIT built in: True | switched on: False JIT built in: True | switched on: True Python 3.14 also brought a new way to build the interpreter loop itself, called the tail-call interpreter. When CPython is built with a recent Clang compiler, each instruction becomes its own small C function. The gain is a few percent, and you get it simply by using a Python built that way. The order of what helps speed, from biggest to smallest, is almost always: a better algorithm, a library that does the work in C (NumPy, pandas, polars), a newer Python version, and only then tricks like the JIT. Lesson 16 of 21Intermediate"],[38,"imports","What import really does","import greet looks like one small step, but it is a whole process, and it explains many real bugs. 🎬 Try it"],[38,"imports","What happens on import greet","Follow one import from the name to a ready module object. import greet import greet print(greet.hello(\"Ravi\")) sys.modules Finders and loaders Module code Press ▶ Play to start. 0 / 6 What import really does · ▶ Runs 📄 greet.py print(\"greet.py is running its top level code\") WELCOME = \"Namaste\"def hello(name): returnf\"{WELCOME}, {name}!\"if __name__ == \"__main__\": print(\"this line runs only when greet.py is started directly\") 📄 main.py import sys import greet # first import: find greet.py, compile it, run it onceimport greet # second import: already in sys.modules, nothing runs print(greet.hello(\"Ravi\")) print(\"cached in sys.modules:\", \"greet\"in sys.modules) print(\"loaded from:\", greet.__spec__.origin.split(\"/\")[-1]) print(\"this file's __name__:\", __name__) Terminal $ python main.py Output greet.py is running its top level code Namaste, Ravi! cached in sys.modules: True loaded from: greet.py this file's __name__: __main__ A module's top level code runs once, on the first import. Later imports reuse the same module object from sys.modules. if __name__ == \"__main__\": works because the file you start gets the name \"__main__\", while an imported file gets its own module name. sys.path is the list of folders Python searches. The folder of the script you started comes first, then the standard library, then site-packages, where pip installs packages. The finders and loaders are ordinary Python objects in importlib. Tools such as pytest add their own to change how modules are loaded. Lesson 17 of 21Advanced"],[38,"startup","What happens before your first line runs","Between pressing Enter on python main.py and your first line, CPython does a lot of setup work: Reads the command line options and environment variables such as PYTHONPATH. Creates the main interpreter, its memory allocators and the builtins module with print, len and friends. Imports the modules it needs for itself, mostly from frozen copies built into the program, so they load very fast. Builds sys.path and imports the site module, which adds site-packages and processes .pth files. Compiles main.py as the module __main__ and starts the evaluation loop on it. What is ready before your first line runs · ▶ Runs import sys print(\"folder of this script is first on sys.path:\", sys.path[0] != \"\") print(\"modules loaded before line 1 ran:\", len(sys.modules) > 20) print(\"optimize flag:\", sys.flags.optimize, \"| -B flag:\", sys.flags.dont_write_bytecode) print(\"builtins ready:\", callable(print), callable(len)) Output folder of this script is first on sys.path: True modules loaded before line 1 ran: True optimize flag: 0 | -B flag: 0 builtins ready: True True When a command line tool starts slowly, the cause is almost always its imports. -X importtime prints how long each import took, in microseconds. The last column shows nested imports with extra indentation. Measure startup imports with -X importtime · 🧪 Run in a terminal 📄 app.py import json Terminal $ python -X importtime app.py Terminal output import time: 636 | 4369 | re import time: 58 | 58 | _json import time: 430 | 488 | json.scanner import time: 480 | 5336 | json.decoder import time: 482 | 482 | json.encoder import time: 280 | 6097 | json Other useful options: python -X dev turns on extra warnings and checks while you develop, python -I runs in isolated mode without the script folder and user site-packages on the path, and python -X frozen_modules=off loads standard library modules from their .py files. Lesson 18 of 21Expert"],[38,"tools","Tools to look inside","Everything in this chapter can be seen with tools that come with Python: Module or option What you can see tokenize The tokens of a file ast The syntax tree, and you can change it symtable Which names the compiler made local, global or free dis The bytecode, with adaptive=True for specialized instructions inspect, sys._getframe Frames, signatures and source code of live objects gc, sys.getrefcount, tracemalloc Memory, reference counts and the lines that allocate sys.settrace, sys.monitoring Every line, call and return as it happens python -m pdb -p PID (3.14) Attach the debugger to a program that is already running sys.settrace is the classic hook. Debuggers such as pdb use it, and so does the Line by line viewer on this page. sys.settrace: the hook behind debuggers and the line by line viewer · ▶ Runs import sys def tracer(frame, event, arg): if event == \"line\": print(f\" about to run line {frame.f_lineno} in {frame.f_code.co_name}()\") return tracer def bill(price, qty): total = price * qty total = total + 5return total sys.settrace(tracer) result = bill(40, 3) sys.settrace(None) print(\"result:\", result) Output about to run line 9 in bill() about to run line 10 in bill() about to run line 11 in bill() result: 125 sys.monitoring arrived in Python 3.12. Tools switch on only the events they need, so coverage tools and profilers slow your program down much less than with settrace. sys.monitoring: low cost events (3.12 and later) · ▶ Runs import sys mon = sys.monitoring TOOL = mon.PROFILER_ID calls = {} def on_start(code, offset): calls[code.co_name] = calls.get(code.co_name, 0) + 1 mon.use_tool_id(TOOL, \"call counter\") mon.register_callback(TOOL, mon.events.PY_START, on_start) mon.set_events(TOOL, mon.events.PY_START) def tax(amount): return amount * 0.18def bill(amount): return amount + tax(amount) for price in (100, 200, 300): bill(price) mon.set_events(TOOL, 0) mon.free_tool_id(TOOL) print(calls) Output {'bill': 3, 'tax': 3} tracemalloc: which line used the memory · ▶ Runs import tracemalloc tracemalloc.start() orders = [{\"id\": i, \"items\": [\"pen\", \"book\"]} for i in range(10_000)] current, peak = tracemalloc.get_traced_memory() print(\"memory in use is more than 1 MB:\", current > 1_000_000) top = tracemalloc.take_snapshot().statistics(\"lineno\")[0] print(\"the line that allocated the most:\", top.traceback[0].lineno) tracemalloc.stop() Output memory in use is more than 1 MB: True the line that allocated the most: 4 Python 3.14 added a safe way for a debugger to attach to a running process by its process id, without restarting it. That is what python -m pdb -p 1234 uses. It helps when a long running service hangs and you need to see what it is doing. Lesson 19 of 21Expert"],[38,"versions","What changed inside CPython, release by release","The internals keep improving. This table lists the changes that matter for this chapter. Every entry was checked against the official release notes. Version Change inside the interpreter What you notice 3.9 New PEG parser Clearer syntax error messages over the next releases 3.11 Specializing adaptive interpreter, cheaper frames, zero cost try Often much faster with no code change, error messages point at the exact expression 3.12 sys.monitoring, a GIL per subinterpreter at the C level, comprehensions inlined into the function Faster coverage and profiling tools 3.13 Experimental free-threaded build, experimental JIT, a new interactive shell A colourful REPL with multi line editing 3.14 Free threading officially supported, JIT in the Windows and macOS installers (off by default), tail-call interpreter, concurrent.interpreters, remote debugging, annotations evaluated only when asked for, template strings (t\"...\") New options for parallel code, attaching a debugger to a live process 3.14.5 Back to the generational garbage collector from 3.13 Lower memory use in some long running services Upgrading Python is one of the cheapest speed improvements you can make. Run your test suite on the new version first, because C extensions and tools that read bytecode may need an update. Lesson 20 of 21Intermediate"],[38,"qa","Python internals in test automation","QA essentialpytestSeleniumPlaywrightAssertion messages, import side effects and parallel runs all come straight from how CPython compiles and imports code. Knowing how Python runs explains several things testers meet every week. pytest rewrites your asserts. A plain assert only says \"AssertionError\". pytest installs its own import hook, reads each test module's syntax tree, and rewrites every assert so that it records the values involved. The rewritten bytecode is cached with its own tag, as you can see in the file name. pytest rewrites assert before your test runs · 🧪 Run in a terminal 📄 cart.py def total(prices, discount=0): return sum(prices) - discount 📄 test_cart.py from cart import total def test_total_with_discount(): prices = [120, 80, 50] assert total(prices, discount=20) == 200 Terminal $ python -m pytest -q test_cart.py $ ls __pycache__ Terminal output F [100%] ============================================= FAILURES ============================================= _____________________________________ test_total_with_discount _____________________________________ def test_total_with_discount(): prices = [120, 80, 50] > assert total(prices, discount=20) == 200 E assert 230 == 200 E + where 230 = total([120, 80, 50], discount=20) test_cart.py:5: AssertionError ===================================== short test summary info ====================================== FAILED test_cart.py::test_total_with_discount - assert 230 == 200 1 failed in 0.01s --- the rewritten test module is cached separately cart.cpython-314.pyc test_cart.cpython-314-pytest-9.1.1.pyc Only test modules and conftest.py are rewritten. An assert inside a helper module gives the short message unless you register it with pytest.register_assert_rewrite. python -O removes every assert at compile time. Never use assert for checks your application needs in production. Import side effects run once per process. Code at the top level of conftest.py or a page object module runs during collection, before any test. Put setup in fixtures instead. pytest-xdist runs tests in several worker processes, not threads, so CPU heavy tests really run in parallel even with the GIL. Each worker has its own sys.modules and its own memory, so global state is not shared between workers. A stale __pycache__ copied between machines with a different Python version is ignored safely, because of the magic number and the cache tag. Still, keep it out of Git. Lesson 21 of 21Intermediate"],[38,"scenario","Real world scenario: \"it works on my machine\"","QA essentialShadowed modules and wrong sys.path order cause failures that only happen on some machines, which testers are often first to see. The situationA dice game test suite passes on one laptop and fails in CI with AttributeError: module 'random' has no attribute 'randint'. Nobody changed the code that rolls the dice. Someone had added a small settings file to the project and named it random.py. A file named random.py breaks import random · 🧪 Run in a terminal 📄 random.py # The team kept its dice settings in a file called random.pyFACES = 6 📄 main.py import random roll = random.randint(1, 6) print(\"rolled a number from 1 to 6:\", 1 <= roll <= 6) Terminal $ python main.py $ mv random.py dice_settings.py $ python main.py Terminal output Traceback (most recent call last): File \"main.py\", line 3, in <module> roll = random.randint(1, 6) ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ AttributeError: module 'random' has no attribute 'randint' (consider renaming 'random.py' since it has the same name as the standard library module named 'random' and prevents importing that standard library module) --- after renaming random.py to dice_settings.py rolled a number from 1 to 6: True The import system explains it. The folder of the script comes first on sys.path, so import random found the team's file before the standard library one. Python 3.13 and later even say so in the error message. On the laptop that passed, the tests happened to be started from a different folder, so the standard module won. How the team fixed it for good: Renamed the file to dice_settings.py and deleted the old __pycache__ folders. Moved the code into a package folder (src/dice/), so its modules have unique names such as dice.settings. Added a lint rule that flags module names that hide standard library modules. Added python -c \"import random; print(random.__file__)\" to the CI log, so the next time anything shadows a module, the log shows where it came from."],[38,"scenario","Worked problems","Work out each answer on paper, type it and press Check. The hint and the full working open when you ask for them. 🧮"],[38,"numbers","Number crunch","Work out the answer, type it and press Check. Stuck? Peek at the hint, then the full working. 0/8correct C1 What is the full .pyc file name for the module shop on Python 3.14? Type it without the folder. Module name, cache tag, extension. Answer: shop.cpython-314.pyc The cache tag of CPython 3.14 is cpython-314. Name: shop.cpython-314.pyc C2 How many integers does CPython keep ready made in its small integer cache, from -5 to 256 inclusive? Count both ends. Answer: 262 256 − (−5) + 1 = 262 C1 A .pyc header has 4 fields of 4 bytes each. How many bytes is the header? Magic, flags, time, size. Answer: 16 4 × 4 = 16 C2 What is the value of total after this code runs? price = 40 qty = 3 total = price * qty + 5 Multiplication first. Answer: 125 The tree puts price * qty under the addition. 40 × 3 + 5 = 125 C3 How many values are on the value stack right after the second LOAD_NAME in total = price * qty runs? Two loads, no pop yet. Answer: 2 LOAD_NAME price pushes 1 value. LOAD_NAME qty pushes a second one. BINARY_OP has not run yet, so the stack holds 2. C1 A pymalloc arena is 1 MiB and a pool is 16 KiB. How many pools fit in one arena? Same unit first. Answer: 64 1 MiB = 1024 KiB. 1024 ÷ 16 = 64 C2 The switch interval is 0.005 seconds. How many times per second, at most, is the running thread asked to give up the GIL? One second divided by the interval. Answer: 200 1 ÷ 0.005 = 200 C1 countdown(n) calls itself until n is 0. How many frames are on the stack at the deepest point of countdown(3), counting the module frame? def countdown(n): return0if n == 0else countdown(n - 1) countdown(3) Count calls that have not returned. Answer: 5 The module frame is 1. countdown(3), countdown(2), countdown(1) and countdown(0) add 4. 1 + 4 = 5"],[38,"numbers","Quiz","Pick an answer to see why it is right or wrong. 🎯"],[38,"quiz","Quiz time","Four levels from easy to expert. Every answer comes with the reason behind it. 0/16correct Q1 Which one translates the whole program before any of it runs? Think of translating a whole book before printing it. A compiler translates everything first and saves the result as a program file that runs later. Q2 What does CPython do with your .py file before running it? Two stages. CPython compiles to bytecode, then the evaluation loop runs that bytecode. Q3 Where does Python save the bytecode of imported modules? Look next to the source file. The file name includes the cache tag, for example shop.cpython-314.pyc. Q4 A file has a syntax error on line 10. What happens to the print on line 1? The parser reads the whole file first. Compiling happens before running, and a syntax error stops compiling. Q1 A file prints on line 1 and uses a name that does not exist on line 3. What does CPython show? Names are looked up while the program runs. CPython compiles the whole file first, but a missing name is only found by the evaluation loop when line 3 runs, so line 1 has already printed. Q2 What does b = a do when a is a list? Names point at objects. Use a.copy() or list(a) when you need a separate list. Q3 Why does x = 2 * 3 compile to a single load of 6? It happens before running. The compiler does arithmetic on plain constants once, while it builds the bytecode. Q4 Which file never gets a .pyc in __pycache__? Only imported modules are cached. The main script is compiled in memory on every run. Q5 A traceback says \"most recent call last\". Where is the line that raised the error? Most recent is last. Read a traceback from the bottom up. Q1 A function reads count and later assigns count = count + 1. Why does it raise UnboundLocalError? Who decides what is local. Any assignment in the body makes the name local everywhere in that function. Q2 When is an object freed in CPython in the usual case? Counting references. The cycle collector is only needed for objects that refer to each other. Q3 In the normal build, why do threads speed up API tests but not pure number crunching? One thread runs bytecode at a time. Threads help when they mostly wait. Use processes for CPU heavy work. Q4 What does dis.dis(f, adaptive=True) show after f has run many times? Specializing adaptive interpreter. CPython swaps general instructions for faster ones that fit the data it saw. Q1 What does this print? print(int(\"256\") is256) print(int(\"257\") is int(\"257\")) The small integer cache covers -5 to 256. int(\"256\") returns the shared object. 257 is created fresh. Q2 Which statement about Python 3.14 is correct? Supported, not default. You install the free threaded build on purpose, and the JIT still needs PYTHON_JIT=1. Q3 Why does pytest show the values inside a failing assert? An import hook. pytest changes the AST of test modules, then compiles them, and caches the result with its own tag."],[38,"quiz","Assignments","Write real Python in the box and run the tests. They run in your browser with Pyodide, the same CPython as on a computer. Every model answer passed the same tests when this page was built."],[38,"quiz","Hands-on exercises","Write real Python in the box, then press Run the tests. The tests run in your browser with real Python (Pyodide) and tell you exactly which cases pass. Every model answer was checked against the same tests with CPython when this page was built. 🌱 Easy"],[38,"small-int","Is this integer shared","CPython creates the integers from -5 to 256 once and shares them. Write is_cached(n) that returns True when n is in that range and False otherwise. Python lets you chain comparisons: a <= n <= b. def is_cached(n): return -5 <= n <= 256 The range is a CPython detail, not part of the language, which is one more reason never to compare numbers with is. 🌱 Easy"],[38,"pyc-name","Where will the bytecode go","Write pyc_path(module, major, minor) that returns the path CPython uses for a module's bytecode, for example pyc_path(\"shop\", 3, 14) gives \"__pycache__/shop.cpython-314.pyc\". An f-string with three parts: the folder, the module name and the cache tag. def pyc_path(module, major, minor): return f\"__pycache__/{module}.cpython-{major}{minor}.pyc\" importlib.util.cache_from_source(\"shop.py\") gives the same answer for the Python you are running. 🌿 Medium"],[38,"stack-vm","Your own stack machine","Write run_stack(program). The program is a list of tuples: (\"PUSH\", n) pushes a number, and (\"ADD\",), (\"SUB\",) and (\"MUL\",) pop two values and push the result. For SUB, the value pushed first is on the left. Return the value left on top of the stack. Pop the right value first, then the left one. def run_stack(program): stack = [] for ins in program: op = ins[0] if op == \"PUSH\": stack.append(ins[1]) else: right = stack.pop() left = stack.pop() if op == \"ADD\": stack.append(left + right) elif op == \"SUB\": stack.append(left - right) elif op == \"MUL\": stack.append(left * right) return stack[-1] if stack elseNone This is exactly how BINARY_OP works in CPython: the right operand is on top of the stack. 🔥 Hard"],[38,"count-ops","Count the bytecode instructions","Write count_instructions(source, name) that compiles source and returns how many of its instructions are called name. Use compile(source, \"<x>\", \"exec\") and dis.get_instructions. Notice what constant folding does to the second test. Loop over dis.get_instructions(code) and compare ins.opname. import dis def count_instructions(source, name): code = compile(source, \"<x>\", \"exec\") return sum(1for ins in dis.get_instructions(code) if ins.opname == name) Bytecode differs between versions, so only use counts like this to learn, never in a real test. 🧠 Expert"],[38,"to-stack","Compile an expression yourself","Write compile_expr(source) that turns an arithmetic expression into stack instructions, the way the CPython compiler does. Use ast.parse(source, mode=\"eval\").body and walk the tree: a number gives \"PUSH n\", a name gives \"LOAD name\", and a BinOp gives its left side, its right side, then \"ADD\", \"SUB\", \"MUL\" or \"DIV\". Write a small recursive function. Visit the left child, then the right child, then add the operator. This order is called post order. import ast OPS = {ast.Add: \"ADD\", ast.Sub: \"SUB\", ast.Mult: \"MUL\", ast.Div: \"DIV\"} def compile_expr(source): out = [] def walk(node): if isinstance(node, ast.Constant): out.append(f\"PUSH {node.value}\") elif isinstance(node, ast.Name): out.append(f\"LOAD {node.id}\") elif isinstance(node, ast.BinOp): walk(node.left) walk(node.right) out.append(OPS[type(node.op)]) walk(ast.parse(source, mode=\"eval\").body) return out Post order is the reason a stack machine needs no brackets: by the time an operator runs, both of its inputs are already on the stack."],[38,"to-stack","Summary","The whole chapter as a short comic, then the places you meet these ideas in real life."],[38,"comic","The life of one line of Python","Tap any panel to flip it for the deeper idea, or press Play the comic. 📌 Remember CPython compiles your file to bytecode, then the evaluation loop runs it one instruction at a time on a value stack."],[38,"real-life","Real life, just funnier","The same idea is hiding in things you use every day. 🧾A billing counter at a supermarket Its Python service imports hundreds of modules at start up. Shipping the .pyc files made by compileall inside the Docker image cut its start time, because nothing had to be compiled when the container started. 🛰️A school robotics kit Runs MicroPython: the same compile to bytecode, then run on a small VM idea, squeezed into a chip with a few hundred kilobytes of memory. 🐞A nightly test run Failed only on the build server with \"module random has no attribute randint\". A file called random.py in the tests folder was found first on sys.path. 📈A log analysis script Got faster after moving from Python 3.10 to 3.12 with no code changes, thanks to the specializing interpreter and cheaper function calls. Hi, I am MiraI will read \"How Python Runs: From Source Code to Output\" to you 🐢🐇 Part 1 of 23Welcome This chapter follows one Python file from the first character to the output: tokens, the syntax tree, bytecode, pyc files, the evaluation loop, frames, names, memory, the GIL and the newest changes in Python 3.14. Voice settings Voice In English, read the full notes of each part instead of the short summary Scroll the page along with me ⚠️ No English voice is installed on this device, so your browser may read in another accent or stay silent. Microsoft Edge offers free online English voices, or you can add one in your device's speech settings. The captions above are always there to read along."],[39,"","Exam practice: PCPP1 style papers","Four PCPP1 style papers, a bug hunt, the traps table, exam technique and a mock quiz. · PCPP1 · PCPP-32-101 · exam practice · mock quiz · predict output · find the bug · traps · certification · revision Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Advanced Python›Step 8: Exam practice›Advanced exam practice Step 8 · Topic 1 of 1 · All levels Revise the whole Advanced part in the shape of the PCPP1 exam: four predict the output papers with real answers, a bug hunt, the traps in one table, exam technique, a mock quiz of 30 questions and timed problems. About 100 min10 lessons3 formulas10 worked problems30 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1The PCPP1 exam and how to use this chapter 2Paper A: advanced object oriented programming 3Paper B: generators, context managers and exceptions 4Paper C: the standard library 5Paper D: functions, data classes and modern syntax 6Find the bug 7The traps in one table 8Exam technique and a study plan 9LeetCode review set 10Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 10"],[39,"map","The PCPP1 exam and how to use this chapter","Source: Exam facts from two training providers that agree (Lumify Work and OpenExamPrep, read on 7 November 2026). The official page pythoninstitute.org/pcpp1 could not be opened when this chapter was written. This chapter teaches nothing new. It revises the whole Advanced part in the shape of the Python Institute PCPP1 exam (code PCPP-32-101), with four practice papers, a bug hunt, the traps, a mock quiz and timed problems. Every question was written for this course, and every answer below was printed by a real run of Python 3.14. PCPP1 (PCPP-32-101), as listed by training providers Questions 45 Time 65 minutes Pass mark 70 % Before it PCAP (Certified Associate) is recommended Blocks Advanced object oriented programming. Coding conventions, best practices and standards (PEP 8, PEP 257). GUI programming with tkinter. Network programming (sockets, REST). File processing and the program environment (sqlite3, CSV, XML, logging, configparser). Common mistake Check pythoninstitute.org/pcpp1 before you book. Exam versions, prices and weights change, and this page could not confirm them from the official site. Exam block Chapters in this course Advanced OOP 1.1 to 1.5 (special methods, decorators, class design, exceptions and copies, metaclasses) Conventions and best practices 3.1 (style, PEP 8, PEP 257, type hints), 3.2 testing GUI programming 6.4 tkinter Network programming Web and APIs part (HTTP, sockets, requests, REST) Files and environment 3.3 logging, 6.2 CSV, JSON, XML and settings, 6.3 sqlite3 Extra (not in the exam, used every day) 2.1 to 2.3 generators, itertools, context managers, 4.1 and 4.2 speed, 5.1 and 5.2 concurrency, 6.1 regex Paper Covers Lesson A Special methods, MRO, class and static methods, properties, ABCs, decorators, metaclasses, slots, copies 2 B Generators, send, yield from, iterators, itertools, context managers, exception chaining and groups 3 C Regular expressions, json, csv, collections, heapq, sqlite3 4 D functools, dataclasses, enum, parameter kinds, type hints, sorting, the walrus, match 5 Remember Cover the answers, write down what you think each line prints, then open the answers. A wrong guess tells you exactly which chapter to reread. Lesson 2 of 10"],[39,"paper_a","Paper A: advanced object oriented programming","Paper A: what does each line printpython 1class V:2 def __init__(self, x):3 self.x = x45 def __add__(self, other):6 return V(self.x + other.x)78 def __eq__(self, other):9 return self.x == other.x1011 def __repr__(self):12 return f\"V({self.x})\"131415print(\"Q1\", V(1) + V(2), V(1) == V(1), V(1) is V(1))16try:17 print({V(1)})18except TypeError as err:19 print(\"Q2\", type(err).__name__)202122class A:23 def who(self):24 return \"A\"252627class B(A):28 def who(self):29 return \"B\" + super().who()303132class C(A):33 def who(self):34 return \"C\" + super().who()353637class D(B, C):38 pass394041print(\"Q3\", D().who(), [k.__name__ for k in D.__mro__])424344class Counter:45 count = 04647 def __init__(self):48 Counter.count += 14950 @classmethod51 def made(cls):52 return cls.count5354 @staticmethod55 def hello():56 return \"hi\"575859Counter()60Counter()61print(\"Q4\", Counter.made(), Counter.hello())626364class Temp:65 def __init__(self):66 self._c = 06768 @property69 def c(self):70 return self._c7172 @c.setter73 def c(self, value):74 if value < -273.15:75 raise ValueError(\"too cold\")76 self._c = value777879t = Temp()80t.c = 2581try:82 t.c = -30083except ValueError as err:84 print(\"Q5\", t.c, err)8586from abc import ABC, abstractmethod878889class Shape(ABC):90 @abstractmethod91 def area(self):92 ...939495try:96 Shape()97except TypeError:98 print(\"Q6 cannot make a Shape\")99100101def double(fn):102 def wrapper(*args):103 return fn(*args) * 2104 return wrapper105106107@double108def five():109 return 5110111112print(\"Q7\", five(), five.__name__)113114115def repeat(n):116 def decorator(fn):117 def wrapper():118 return [fn() for _ in range(n)]119 return wrapper120 return decorator121122123@repeat(3)124def letter():125 return \"x\"126127128print(\"Q8\", letter())129130131class Meta(type):132 def __new__(mcs, name, bases, namespace):133 namespace[\"tag\"] = name.lower()134 return super().__new__(mcs, name, bases, namespace)135136137class Thing(metaclass=Meta):138 pass139140141print(\"Q9\", Thing.tag, type(Thing).__name__, type(type).__name__)142143144class Slim:145 __slots__ = (\"a\",)146147148s = Slim()149s.a = 1150try:151 s.b = 2152except AttributeError:153 print(\"Q10\", hasattr(s, \"__dict__\"))154155import copy156157data = [[1, 2], [3]]158shallow = copy.copy(data)159deep = copy.deepcopy(data)160data[0].append(9)161print(\"Q11\", shallow[0], deep[0], shallow is data, shallow[0] is data[0]) Open the answers for paper A Q Output Why (chapter) 1 V(3) True False + calls __add__, == calls __eq__, but is compares identity: two different objects (1.1) 2 TypeError A class that defines __eq__ without __hash__ gets __hash__ = None, so its objects cannot go in a set (1.1) 3 BCA ['D', 'B', 'C', 'A', 'object'] super() follows the MRO of the object, not the parent of the class. In B it calls C, not A (1.3) 4 2 hi The classmethod gets the class, the staticmethod gets nothing. Both can be called on the class (1.3) 5 25 too cold The setter refused -300 before changing _c, so the old value stays (1.3) 6 cannot make a Shape A class with an abstract method that is not overridden cannot be instantiated (1.3) 7 10 wrapper The decorator doubles the result. Without functools.wraps the function now reports the name wrapper (1.2) 8 ['x', 'x', 'x'] repeat(3) runs first and returns the real decorator (1.2) 9 thing Meta type The metaclass __new__ added tag. The type of a class is its metaclass, and the type of type is type (1.5) 10 False __slots__ removes the per object __dict__, so new attributes are refused (1.3) 11 [1, 2, 9] [1, 2] False True A shallow copy is a new outer list that shares the inner lists. deepcopy copies them too (1.4) Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 paper_a.py. Lesson 3 of 10"],[39,"paper_b","Paper B: generators, context managers and exceptions","Paper B: what does each line printpython 1def gen():2 print(\"start\")3 yield 14 print(\"middle\")5 yield 2678g = gen()9print(\"Q1 made\")10print(\"Q1\", next(g))11print(\"Q1\", list(g))121314def count(n):15 x = yield n16 print(\"got\", x)17 yield x * 2181920c = count(5)21print(\"Q2\", next(c), c.send(10))222324def inner():25 yield 126 yield 227 return \"done\"282930def outer():31 result = yield from inner()32 yield result333435print(\"Q3\", list(outer()))3637it = iter([1, 2, 3])38print(\"Q4\", 2 in it, list(it))3940import itertools4142print(\"Q5\", list(itertools.islice(itertools.count(10, 5), 3)), list(itertools.accumulate([1, 2, 3])))43print(\"Q6\", list(itertools.chain(\"ab\", [1])), [k for k, grp in itertools.groupby(\"aabbba\")], len(list(itertools.combinations(range(5), 2))))4445from contextlib import contextmanager464748@contextmanager49def tag(name):50 print(f\"<{name}>\")51 try:52 yield name.upper()53 finally:54 print(f\"</{name}>\")555657with tag(\"b\") as t:58 print(\"Q7\", t)596061class Quiet:62 def __enter__(self):63 return self6465 def __exit__(self, kind, value, tb):66 print(\"Q8 exit saw\", kind.__name__)67 return True686970with Quiet():71 1 / 072print(\"Q8 after the with block\")7374try:75 try:76 {}[\"k\"]77 except KeyError as err:78 raise ValueError(\"bad\") from err79except ValueError as err:80 print(\"Q9\", type(err.__cause__).__name__, err.__suppress_context__)8182try:83 raise ExceptionGroup(\"many\", [ValueError(\"a\"), TypeError(\"b\"), ValueError(\"c\")])84except* ValueError as group:85 print(\"Q10 V\", len(group.exceptions))86except* TypeError as group:87 print(\"Q10 T\", len(group.exceptions)) Open the answers for paper B Q Output Why (chapter) 1 made, start, 1, middle, [2] Calling a generator function runs nothing. Each next runs to the next yield (2.1) 2 got 10, then 5 20 send(10) resumes the paused yield with the value 10. The print inside runs before the Q2 line can print (2.1) 3 [1, 2, 'done'] yield from passes the inner values on and gives back the inner return value (2.1) 4 True [3] in on an iterator uses it up until it finds 2, so only 3 is left (2.1) 5 [10, 15, 20] [1, 3, 6] count is endless, islice takes 3. accumulate gives running totals (2.2) 6 ['a', 'b', 1] ['a', 'b', 'a'] 10 groupby groups only NEIGHBOURS that are equal, so a appears twice. 5 choose 2 is 10 (2.2) 7 <b>, B, </b> The code before yield is the set up, the finally part is the clean up (2.3) 8 exit saw ZeroDivisionError, after the with block __exit__ returning True swallows the exception (2.3) 9 KeyError True raise ... from err sets __cause__ and hides the automatic context (1.4) 10 V 2, T 1 except* splits the group: each handler gets the matching part (1.4) Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 paper_b.py. Lesson 4 of 10"],[39,"paper_c","Paper C: the standard library","Paper C: what does each line printpython 1import csv2import heapq3import io4import json5import re6import sqlite37from collections import Counter, defaultdict, deque, namedtuple89print(\"Q1\", re.findall(r\"\\d+\", \"a1b22c333\"), re.sub(r\"(\\w+)@(\\w+)\", r\"\\2 at \\1\", \"ann@home\"))10print(\"Q2\", re.match(r\"b\", \"abc\"), bool(re.search(r\"b\", \"abc\")), re.fullmatch(r\"a.c\", \"abc\") is not None)11print(\"Q3\", re.findall(r\"<.+>\", \"<a><b>\"), re.findall(r\"<.+?>\", \"<a><b>\"))12print(\"Q4\", json.dumps({\"b\": (1, 2), \"a\": None}, sort_keys=True), json.loads(\"[1, 2.0, true]\"))13rows = list(csv.reader(io.StringIO('x,\"y, z\",3\\n')))14print(\"Q5\", rows, type(rows[0][2]).__name__)15print(\"Q6\", Counter(\"banana\").most_common(2), dict(defaultdict(int, a=1)))16d = deque([1, 2, 3], maxlen=3)17d.append(4)18d.appendleft(0)19print(\"Q7\", list(d))20nums = [5, 1, 4, 2]21heapq.heapify(nums)22print(\"Q8\", nums[0], heapq.nsmallest(2, [5, 1, 4, 2]), heapq.heappop(nums), nums)23Point = namedtuple(\"Point\", \"x y\")24p = Point(1, 2)25print(\"Q9\", p.x + p[1], p._replace(x=9), p._asdict())26db = sqlite3.connect(\":memory:\")27db.execute(\"CREATE TABLE t (n INTEGER)\")28db.executemany(\"INSERT INTO t VALUES (?)\", [(3,), (1,), (2,)])29print(\"Q10\", db.execute(\"SELECT COUNT(*), MAX(n), SUM(n) FROM t\").fetchone()) Open the answers for paper C Q Output Why (chapter) 1 ['1', '22', '333'] home at ann findall returns strings. In the replacement, \\2 and \\1 are the groups (6.1) 2 None True True match only tries at the start, search anywhere, fullmatch the whole text (6.1) 3 ['<a><b>'] ['<a>', '<b>'] .+ is greedy and takes as much as it can, .+? is lazy (6.1) 4 {\"a\": null, \"b\": [1, 2]} [1, 2.0, True] None becomes null, tuples become lists, true becomes True (6.2) 5 [['x', 'y, z', '3']] str The quoted field keeps its comma. csv gives every value as a string (6.2) 6 [('a', 3), ('n', 2)] {'a': 1} most_common sorts by count. A defaultdict is a dict (4.2) 7 [0, 2, 3] maxlen=3: append 4 pushes out 1, appendleft 0 pushes out 4 from the right end (4.2) 8 1 [1, 2] 1 [2, 5, 4] heapify puts the smallest first. After heappop the list is still a heap, not sorted (4.2) 9 3 Point(x=9, y=2) {'x': 1, 'y': 2} A namedtuple works by name and by index. _replace makes a new one (4.2) 10 (3, 3, 6) Aggregates give one row: COUNT, MAX and SUM (6.3) Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 paper_c.py. Lesson 5 of 10"],[39,"paper_d","Paper D: functions, data classes and modern syntax","Paper D: what does each line printpython 1from dataclasses import dataclass, field2from enum import Enum3from functools import lru_cache, partial, reduce, wraps456@lru_cache(maxsize=None)7def fib(n):8 return n if n < 2 else fib(n - 1) + fib(n - 2)91011print(\"Q1\", fib(30), fib.cache_info().misses)1213square = partial(pow, exp=2)14print(\"Q2\", square(5), reduce(lambda a, b: a * b, [1, 2, 3, 4]))151617def logged(fn):18 @wraps(fn)19 def wrapper(*args, **kwargs):20 return fn(*args, **kwargs)21 return wrapper222324@logged25def greet():26 \"\"\"Say hi.\"\"\"272829print(\"Q3\", greet.__name__, greet.__doc__)303132@dataclass(order=True)33class Card:34 rank: int35 suit: str = \"hearts\"36 tags: list = field(default_factory=list)373839print(\"Q4\", Card(2) < Card(3), Card(2), Card(2) == Card(2))404142class Color(Enum):43 RED = 144 GREEN = 2454647print(\"Q5\", Color.RED, Color(2).name, Color[\"RED\"].value, len(Color))484950def f(a, /, b, *, c):51 return a + b + c525354try:55 f(a=1, b=2, c=3)56except TypeError:57 print(\"Q6 a is positional only\")58print(\"Q6\", f(1, b=2, c=3))596061def hinted(x: int) -> str:62 return x * 2636465print(\"Q7\", hinted(3), hinted.__annotations__)66print(\"Q8\", sorted([\"b\", \"C\", \"a\"]), sorted([\"b\", \"C\", \"a\"], key=str.lower), max([], default=0))67x = [1, 2, 3]68print(\"Q9\", (y := len(x)) > 2, y, *x)69match {\"type\": \"point\", \"x\": 1}:70 case {\"type\": \"point\", \"x\": 0}:71 print(\"Q10 origin\")72 case {\"type\": \"point\", \"x\": x0}:73 print(\"Q10 x is\", x0) Open the answers for paper D Q Output Why (chapter) 1 832040 31 With the cache every fib(n) from 0 to 30 is worked out once: 31 misses (1.2, 4.1) 2 25 24 partial fixes exp=2. reduce multiplies 1 × 2 × 3 × 4 (2.2) 3 greet Say hi. functools.wraps copies the name and the docstring to the wrapper (1.2) 4 True Card(rank=2, suit='hearts', tags=[]) True order=True compares the fields in order. The dataclass writes __repr__ and __eq__ (1.3) 5 Color.RED GREEN 1 2 Color(2) finds by value, Color[\"RED\"] by name (1.3) 6 a is positional only, then 6 Parameters before / cannot be passed by name. After * they must be (1.2) 7 6 {'x': <class 'int'>, 'return': <class 'str'>} Type hints are not checked when the code runs. Use mypy for that (3.1) 8 ['C', 'a', 'b'] ['a', 'b', 'C'] 0 Upper case letters sort before lower case ones. key=str.lower ignores case. max needs default= for an empty list 9 True 3 1 2 3 := assigns inside an expression. *x unpacks the list into separate arguments 10 x is 1 The first case needs x == 0. The second captures the value in x0 Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 paper_d.py. Lesson 6 of 10"],[39,"bugs","Find the bug","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 bug_hunt.py. Seven bugs examiners and reviewers love Bug 1. The wrapper calls fn but never returns its result, so every decorated function returns None. Write return fn(*args, **kwargs) (1.2). Bug 2. A generator can be used only once. The second sum sees nothing. Use a list, or make a new generator (2.1). Bug 3. Defining __eq__ removes __hash__. Add __hash__ based on the same fields, or use @dataclass(frozen=True) (1.1). Bug 4. items = [] in the class body is ONE list shared by every Basket. Make it in __init__ with self.items = [] (1.3). Bug 5. except Exception: pass hides the error, and the caller gets None without knowing why. Catch the exact error and log it or raise your own (1.4, 3.3). Bug 6. __exit__ returning True swallows every exception, so the program goes on with total missing. Return True only for the errors you really handle (2.3). Bug 7. In a normal string \\b is the backspace character, so the pattern never matches. Always write patterns as raw strings r\"...\" (6.1). Lesson 7 of 10"],[39,"traps","The traps in one table","Trap Looks like Really Chapter __eq__ only Objects still work in sets They become unhashable 1.1 NotImplemented Return False when the type is wrong Return NotImplemented, so Python can try the other side 1.1 super() Calls the parent class Calls the next class in the MRO of the object 1.3 Decorator without wraps Keeps the name and docstring The function is now called wrapper 1.2 Decorator with arguments @repeat is enough @repeat(3): three levels of functions 1.2 Mutable class attribute Each object has its own list All objects share one list 1.3 Shallow copy copy.copy copies everything Inner objects are shared 1.4 Generator Can be looped over twice It is empty after the first loop 2.1 groupby Groups all equal items Groups only neighbours: sort first 2.2 __exit__ return value Return True to say \"done\" True swallows the exception 2.3 raise from Same as a plain raise Sets __cause__ and shows \"direct cause\" 1.4 Type hints Python checks them Only tools such as mypy check them 3.1 Regex without r \"\\d\" and r\"\\d\" are the same Some escapes, like \\b, change meaning 6.1 pickle Safe like JSON Loading can run code 6.2 sqlite3 Changes are saved at once Call commit() or use with conn 6.3 tkinter callback command=f() is right command=f, without brackets 6.4 pack and grid Can be mixed in one parent TclError: use one per parent 6.4 Lesson 8 of 10"],[39,"plan","Exam technique and a study plan","Time per questionExam favourite minutes × 60 / questions PCPP1 as listed: 65 × 60 / 45 ≈ 87 seconds. Questions to pass (if every item counts the same)Exam favourite correct needed = ceil(pass mark × questions) 0.7 × 45 = 31.5, so 32 correct answers. Safety margin aim = pass mark + 15 points in practice Practise until you score about 85 %, because exam nerves cost a few points. A four week plan Week 1: chapters 1.1 to 1.5 and paper A. Write a small class with __repr__, __eq__, __hash__, a property and a decorator from memory. Week 2: chapters 3.1 to 3.3 (style, testing, logging) and 6.4 tkinter. Build one small window with grid, a variable and an event. Week 3: chapters 6.1 to 6.3 and the network chapters of the web part, with papers B and C. Week 4: paper D, the bug hunt, the traps table, then the mock quiz in the Quiz tab with a timer of 87 seconds per question. In the exam In the exam: read the whole snippet first, then the options. Trace it on paper and watch for the answer \"the code raises an exception\". Mark hard questions and come back, because every question is worth the same. One idea, four depths PCAP level: classes, inheritance, exceptions, modules and strings (the Core part). PCPP1 level: special methods, decorators, metaclasses, PEP 8 and PEP 257, tkinter, sockets and REST, sqlite3, CSV, XML, logging and configparser. PCPP2 and job level: design patterns, testing, concurrency, packaging, and frameworks such as Flask and Django from the Web part. Expert: explain every trap from the language reference and the data model, and spot them in code review before they reach production. Lesson 9 of 10"],[39,"leetcode","LeetCode review set","Ten problems that use the Advanced part: classes that prepare data once, Counters in a sliding window, recursive generators with yield from, functools.cache, dynamic programming tables, two pointers, and a cache built from OrderedDicts. Try each in under 30 minutes, as in a coding interview. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 304 Range Sum Query 2D - Immutable Medium A class with a table of prefix sums Build sums[r + 1][c + 1] = the total of the rectangle from (0, 0) to (r, c) once in __init__. Any rectangle is then four lookups. 438 Find All Anagrams in a String Medium A sliding window of Counters Keep a Counter of the last len(p) letters. Add the new letter, remove the oldest, and compare with the Counter of p. 39 Combination Sum Medium A recursive generator A generator yields each combination as soon as it is found. Pass the start index so a number is never used before a smaller one, which avoids repeats. 131 Palindrome Partitioning Medium yield from for every first piece Try every palindrome as the first piece, then partition the rest the same way. A generator with yield from keeps the code short. 1143 Longest Common Subsequence Medium Recursion with lru_cache best(i, j) compares text1[i:] and text2[j:]. Equal letters add 1, otherwise skip one letter on either side. functools.cache stores every answer. 221 Maximal Square Medium Grow squares from three neighbours size[r][c] is the side of the biggest all \"1\" square ending at (r, c): 1 + the smallest of the sizes above, to the left and diagonal. 72 Edit Distance Medium A table of smaller answers dist[i][j] is the cost to turn the first i letters of word1 into the first j letters of word2. Equal letters cost nothing, otherwise 1 + the best of insert, delete or replace. 42 Trapping Rain Water Hard Two pointers with the highest wall on each side Water above a bar is the lower of the highest walls on its left and right, minus the bar. Move the pointer on the lower side, because that side decides the water level. 76 Minimum Window Substring Hard Grow the window, then shrink it Move the right end until the window holds every letter of t (count with a Counter). Then move the left end while it still does, and remember the shortest window. 460 LFU Cache Hard A dict of OrderedDicts, one per use count Keep the value and the use count of each key, and an OrderedDict of keys for every count. Evict the oldest key in the group with the smallest count. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 10 of 10"],[39,"recap","Chapter summary","PCPP1 (PCPP-32-101) as listed by training providers: 45 questions, 65 minutes, 70 %. Check the official page before you book. Objects: __eq__ needs a matching __hash__, return NotImplemented for unknown types, super() follows the MRO, wraps keeps the name. Iteration: generators run only on next, can be used once, send resumes a yield, yield from passes values and the return value. Context and errors: __exit__ returning True swallows the error, raise from sets __cause__, except* splits exception groups. Library: raw strings for regex, json changes tuples and keys, csv gives strings, deque maxlen drops from the other end, sqlite3 needs commit. Technique: about 87 seconds per question, trace on paper, aim for 85 % in practice. Next: The Web and APIs part goes on with HTTP, sockets, REST, Flask, Django and FastAPI. .py Practice files for this chapterThree exam style tasks: a Vector2 class with the right special methods, a retry decorator, and an LRU cache built on OrderedDict.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[40,"","HTTP and REST: how the web talks","HTTP messages, URLs, methods, status codes, headers, cookies, caching and REST design. · HTTP · REST · URL · status codes · headers · cookies · caching · http.server · urllib · JSON API Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Web and APIs›Step 1: How the web works: HTTP, REST and sockets›HTTP and REST Step 1 · Topic 1 of 2 · Intermediate See exactly what a program and a server send each other. Learn URLs, methods, status codes and headers, build a small JSON API with http.server, and learn the rules of REST design and caching. About 90 min9 lessons1 animation6 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1How the web works: one request, one response 2URLs: the address of a resource 3Inside an HTTP message 4REST: designing an API with resources 5Headers, cookies and caching 6The maths: latency, bandwidth and caching 7From beginner to expert 8LeetCode practice 9Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 9"],[40,"idea","How the web works: one request, one response","Source: RFC 9110 (HTTP semantics), RFC 9111 (caching), RFC 3986 (URLs) and the Python 3.14 documentation of urllib, http.server and http.client. What it is HTTP (HyperText Transfer Protocol) is the language that browsers, apps and servers use to talk. A client sends a request (a method such as GET, a path and some headers) and the server sends back a response (a status code, headers and usually a body). HTTPS is the same thing inside an encrypted TLS connection. Why we need it Programs written in different languages on different computers need one shared, simple set of rules to ask for data and send it. Without a standard every website and app would need its own protocol. How it works HTTP messages are plain text lines sent over a TCP connection: a first line, headers, a blank line, then the body. Because the format is so simple, any language can read and write it, and Python has it in the standard library. One HTTP request and its responsePick a case. Each step adds one line of the real text that travels between the program and the server. Step 0 of 10 Case Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this GET: DNS, handshake, request line, headers, then the answer. POST: a request with a body creates something new. 404: HTTP worked, but the status says it went wrong. 304: the cheapest answer is \"you already have it\". Ways for programs to talk over the network REST over HTTP GraphQL gRPC WebSocket Idea URLs name resources, methods say what to do One URL, the client writes a query for exactly the fields it wants Call functions on a server, messages in Protocol Buffers One connection that stays open, both sides send at any time Data Usually JSON JSON Binary, typed Text or binary frames Good for Public APIs, most web apps Apps that need flexible queries Fast calls between internal services Chat, live prices, games Caching Built into HTTP Harder Do it yourself Not for messages In Python requests, Flask, Django, FastAPI graphene, strawberry grpcio websockets Bottom line: Learn REST over HTTP first: almost every API you will call uses it, and the others build on the same ideas. Lesson 2 of 9"],[40,"urls","URLs: the address of a resource","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 url_parts.py. Part Example Meaning scheme https How to talk: http, https, ftp, mailto ... host api.example.com Which computer. DNS turns it into an IP address port 8443 Which program on that computer. Default 80 for http, 443 for https path /v1/books/42 Which resource on the server query fields=title,year&lang=en Extra options as name=value pairs joined with & fragment reviews A place inside the page. The browser keeps it and never sends it In the exam URLs may only contain a small set of characters. Others, like spaces, é or & inside a value, must be percent encoded: each byte becomes % and two hex digits, so a space is %20 and é (2 bytes in UTF-8) is %C3%A9. Use urlencode or let requests do it with params=, never glue query text together by hand. Lesson 3 of 9"],[40,"message","Inside an HTTP message","Run this one on your own computer. It opens a real network socket, and the Python inside this page has no network sockets. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 raw_http.py. Remember BaseHTTPRequestHandler answers with HTTP/1.0 unless you set protocol_version = \"HTTP/1.1\" in the class. Every line ends with \\r\\n (carriage return and new line), and an empty line separates the headers from the body. Method Means Safe (changes nothing) Idempotent (same result if repeated) Has a body GET Read a resource Yes Yes No HEAD Like GET, but only the headers Yes Yes No POST Create something, or run an action No No Yes PUT Replace the whole resource No Yes Yes PATCH Change part of a resource No Not always Yes DELETE Remove a resource No Yes Usually no OPTIONS Ask what is allowed (used by CORS) Yes Yes No Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 status_codes.py. Range Meaning Common codes 1xx Wait, more is coming 101 Switching Protocols (WebSocket) 2xx Success 200 OK, 201 Created, 204 No Content 3xx Look somewhere else 301 Moved Permanently, 302 Found, 304 Not Modified 4xx The client made a mistake 400 Bad Request, 401 Unauthorized (not logged in), 403 Forbidden (not allowed), 404 Not Found, 409 Conflict, 422 invalid data, 429 Too Many Requests 5xx The server failed 500 Internal Server Error, 502 Bad Gateway, 503 Service Unavailable, 504 Gateway Timeout Lesson 4 of 9"],[40,"rest","REST: designing an API with resources","Run this one on your own computer. It opens a real network socket, and the Python inside this page has no network sockets. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 book_api.py. Action Method and path Success Typical errors List books GET /books 200 with a JSON list Read one book GET /books/2 200 with the book 404 if it does not exist Create a book POST /books with JSON 201 Created and a Location header 400 or 422 for bad data Replace a book PUT /books/2 with JSON 200 or 204 404, 400 Change a book PATCH /books/2 with some fields 200 404, 400 Delete a book DELETE /books/2 204 No Content 404 Rules of a good REST API Paths name things (nouns): /books, /books/2/reviews. The method says the action, so there is no /getBook or /deleteBook. Use the status code that fits: 201 after creating, 404 for missing, 400 or 422 for bad input, never 200 with an error inside. Send and accept JSON with Content-Type: application/json, and give errors a JSON body such as {\"error\": \"...\"}. Keep every request stateless: it carries everything the server needs, such as an auth token, so any server in a group can answer it. Add a version (/v1/books) and paging (?page=2&size=50) before many clients depend on the API. In the exam urllib.request.urlopen raises HTTPError for 4xx and 5xx answers. The requests library in the next chapters does not: it returns the response, and you call raise_for_status() when you want an exception. Lesson 5 of 9"],[40,"headers","Headers, cookies and caching","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 cookies_cache.py. Header Sent by Does Host Client Which site on a server with many sites Accept, Content-Type Both Which format is wanted, which format is sent Authorization Client Proves who you are, for example Bearer and a token User-Agent Client Names the program that sends the request Set-Cookie, Cookie Server, then client The server stores a small value in the browser, which sends it back on every request Cache-Control, ETag, If-None-Match Both How long a copy may be kept, its version, and \"is my version still right?\" Location Server Where a new resource is, or where to go for a redirect Access-Control-Allow-Origin Server Which other websites may call this API from a browser (CORS) Common mistake HTTP itself has no memory between requests. Logins work because the server gives the browser a session cookie or a token, and the client sends it back every time. Mark session cookies HttpOnly, Secure and SameSite, as in the example. Lesson 6 of 9"],[40,"maths","The maths: latency, bandwidth and caching","Time to first byteExam favourite about (TCP 1 + TLS 1 + request 1) round trips × RTT RTT 50 ms: 3 × 50 = 150 ms before the first byte of the answer, however small it is. Transfer time time = size × 8 / bandwidth 2 MB at 40 Mbit/s: 16 Mbit / 40 = 0.4 s. Average time with a cacheExam favourite average = hit rate × hit time + (1 − hit rate) × miss time 80 % hits at 5 ms, misses at 200 ms: 0.8 × 5 + 0.2 × 200 = 44 ms. Little's law requests in progress = arrival rate × time per request 200 requests a second, 50 ms each: 10 requests at the same moment. Bandwidth delay product bits in flight = bandwidth × RTT 100 Mbit/s and 80 ms: 8 Mbit (1 MB) on the wire at once. Percent encoding size a byte outside the allowed set becomes 3 characters \"é\" is 2 bytes in UTF-8, so it becomes 6 characters: %C3%A9. Worked example: why do small requests feel slow far away? A server in another continent has an RTT of 150 ms. A new HTTPS connection needs about 3 round trips: 450 ms. The answer is only 5 KB. At 50 Mbit/s that takes 40,000 / 50,000,000 s, less than 1 ms. So almost all the time is waiting for round trips, not sending data. Keeping the connection open (requests.Session, HTTP keep alive) saves the 2 handshake round trips on later requests: 150 ms instead of 450 ms. Latency, not bandwidth, decides the speed of small requests Lesson 7 of 9"],[40,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths Read a URL, know GET and POST, the main status codes and what JSON in a body looks like. Design REST paths and status codes, use headers for content type, auth and caching, and run a small server with http.server. Understand safe and idempotent methods, conditional requests, cookies and CORS, and measure latency in round trips. Know HTTP/2 multiplexing and HTTP/3 over QUIC, TLS 1.3, CDNs, caching rules from RFC 9111, and how to design APIs that evolve for years. Beginner Read and send simple requests. Intermediate Design REST endpoints and use headers well. Expert Tune latency, caching and protocol versions. Lesson 8 of 9"],[40,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","Web code reads and checks a lot of text: URL paths, host names, IP addresses, query filters, search box suggestions and messages. These problems practise exactly that kind of parsing. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1961 Check If String Is a Prefix of Array Easy Join pieces until the text is long enough Like building a URL path from its parts. Add the words one by one and stop as soon as the joined text is as long as s. 1309 Decrypt String from Alphabet to Integer Mapping Easy Look two places ahead for # This is decoding, like reading %20 in a URL. If the character two places ahead is #, read two digits, otherwise one. 1773 Count Items Matching a Rule Easy Pick the column by its name Like a query string such as ?color=silver filtering records. Map the rule key to a column index with a dict. 2185 Counting Words With a Given Prefix Easy str.startswith Routers match URL paths by prefix in the same way. Count the words that start with the prefix. 811 Subdomain Visit Count Medium Every suffix of a host name A visit to discuss.leetcode.com also counts for leetcode.com and com. Split on dots and add the count to every ending with a Counter. 93 Restore IP Addresses Medium Try every split into four parts Each part is 0 to 255 with no leading zero, like a real IPv4 address. Try lengths 1, 2 and 3 for each of the four parts. 1268 Search Suggestions System Medium Sort once, then filter by the growing prefix This is what a search box API returns while you type. Sort the products, and for each typed prefix keep the first three that start with it. 2284 Sender With Largest Word Count Medium A Counter, then max with a tuple key Count the words each sender wrote. Pick the largest count, and on a tie the name that is largest in alphabetical order: max by (count, name). 140 Word Break II Hard Recursion with a cache of endings All the ways to cut a string into dictionary words. sentences(start) returns every sentence for s[start:], and functools.cache remembers each answer. 301 Remove Invalid Parentheses Hard Breadth first search by number of removals Check all strings with one character removed, then two, and so on. The first level that has valid strings gives the answer with the fewest removals. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 9 of 9"],[40,"recap","Chapter summary","HTTP is a request (method, path, headers, optional body) and a response (status, headers, body) as text lines over TCP. HTTPS adds TLS encryption. A URL has a scheme, host, port, path, query and fragment. Build query strings with urlencode, never by hand. GET reads, POST creates, PUT replaces, PATCH changes, DELETE removes. GET, PUT and DELETE are idempotent, POST is not. Status codes: 2xx success, 3xx redirect, 4xx client mistake, 5xx server failure. REST paths are nouns, methods are verbs. Headers carry the content type, auth tokens, cookies and caching rules. ETag with If-None-Match gives a cheap 304. Next: Next: what is under HTTP, with sockets, TCP and UDP. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: read a query string correctly, describe status codes and decide when to retry, and parse a raw HTTP request into its parts.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[41,"","Sockets, TCP and UDP: under HTTP","TCP and UDP sockets, bytes and struct, message framing, many clients with asyncio, IP addresses and subnets. · socket · TCP · UDP · bytes · struct · framing · asyncio · ipaddress · subnet · port Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Web and APIs›Step 1: How the web works: HTTP, REST and sockets›Sockets Step 1 · Topic 2 of 2 · Intermediate Go one level below HTTP. Write a TCP client and server with the socket module, see why TCP needs message framing, send UDP datagrams, serve many clients with asyncio, and work with IP addresses and subnets. About 90 min11 lessons1 animation6 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1What a socket is 2Bytes: what really travels 3A TCP client and server 4TCP is a stream: framing messages 5UDP: single packets, no promises 6Serving many clients at once 7IP addresses, ports and layers 8The maths: windows, round trips and loss 9From beginner to expert 10LeetCode practice 11Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 11"],[41,"idea","What a socket is","Source: RFC 9293 (TCP), RFC 768 (UDP), RFC 791 and RFC 4632 (IPv4 and CIDR) and the Python 3.14 documentation of socket, struct, ipaddress and asyncio streams. What it is A socket is one end of a network connection inside your program. You give it an address (an IP address and a port), and then you can send and receive bytes through it. Python has the socket module for this, and every web library, from requests to Django, uses sockets underneath. Why we need it Two programs on different computers have no shared memory. They need a way to move bytes across the network to the right program, in the right order, even when the network loses or delays packets. How it works The operating system does the hard part. The IP address finds the computer, the port finds the program on it, and the protocol decides the rules: TCP gives a reliable, ordered stream of bytes, UDP sends single packets fast with no promises. Your program only calls connect, send and recv. Sockets on a timelineTime runs down the page. Each step adds one packet between the client and the server, with the Python call that caused it. Step 0 of 10 Case Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this TCP: the three way handshake, data and ACKs, then FIN. Two sends can arrive as one recv. Framing fixes it. TCP hides a lost packet by sending it again. UDP keeps message borders but never resends. Ways to move bytes between programs TCP socket UDP socket Unix domain socket QUIC Setup Three way handshake None None, a file path on one computer One round trip, with TLS built in Delivery Every byte, in order, no duplicates May lose, repeat or reorder packets Reliable Reliable, per stream Message borders No: a stream of bytes Yes: one datagram is one message Stream or datagram, you choose Streams inside one connection Good for HTTP/1 and HTTP/2, databases, SSH, email DNS, video calls, games, metrics Programs on the same computer, such as Docker or PostgreSQL HTTP/3 In Python socket.SOCK_STREAM, asyncio streams socket.SOCK_DGRAM socket.AF_UNIX aioquic library Bottom line: Use TCP unless you have a clear reason not to. Most of the time a higher level library such as requests is even better, and sockets are what you learn to understand it. Lesson 2 of 11"],[41,"bytes","Bytes: what really travels","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 bytes_text.py. struct code C type Bytes Range B unsigned char 1 0 to 255 H unsigned short 2 0 to 65,535 (a port number fits here) I unsigned int 4 0 to about 4.29 billion (an IPv4 address fits here) Q unsigned long long 8 0 to about 1.8 × 1019 h, i, q signed versions 2, 4, 8 Negative numbers too ! byte order mark Network order: the most important byte first (big endian), no padding In the exam A socket only knows bytes. Turn text into bytes with .encode(\"utf-8\") before sending and back with .decode(\"utf-8\") after receiving. \"é\" is one letter but 2 bytes, so always measure the length of the bytes, never of the text. Real world scenario: a network game sends positions A game sends the x and y position of a player 30 times a second. As text, \"1023,887\" is 8 bytes and the receiver must split and convert it. With struct.pack(\"!HH\", 1023, 887) it is always exactly 4 bytes, and struct.unpack gives the two numbers back at once. Fixed size binary data is smaller and faster to read, which is why real network protocols use it for their headers. Lesson 3 of 11"],[41,"tcp","A TCP client and server","Run this one on your own computer. It opens real network sockets, and the Python inside this page has no network sockets. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 tcp_echo.py. The life of a TCP server and client Server: socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM) makes an IPv4 TCP socket. bind((host, port)) claims a port. listen() starts accepting connections. Server: accept() waits for a client. It returns a NEW socket for that one client plus the client address. The listening socket keeps listening for others. Client: socket.create_connection((host, port), timeout=5) finds the address, makes the socket and connects in one call. Both: sendall(data) sends every byte (plain send() may send only part of it). recv(n) returns between 1 and n bytes, or b\"\" when the other side has closed. Both: close(), or a with block that closes for you. Always set a timeout, so a dead network cannot make your program wait for ever. Run this one on your own computer. It opens real network sockets, and the Python inside this page has no network sockets. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 errors_timeouts.py. Error When What to do ConnectionRefusedError Nothing listens on that port Check the host and port, is the server running TimeoutError (socket.timeout) No answer within the timeout Retry later or give up with a clear message ConnectionResetError The other side crashed or closed hard Reconnect, and send again only if that is safe OSError: Address already in use Another program, or your old one, has the port Pick another port, or wait. SO_REUSEADDR helps after a restart socket.gaierror The host name could not be found (DNS) Check the spelling and the network Common mistake Host \"127.0.0.1\" (localhost) only accepts connections from the same computer. \"0.0.0.0\" accepts them from every network card, so other computers can connect. Use 127.0.0.1 while you learn. Lesson 4 of 11"],[41,"framing","TCP is a stream: framing messages","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 stream_framing.py. Framing method How the receiver finds the end Used by Length prefix Read a fixed size length first, then exactly that many bytes PostgreSQL, Kafka, gRPC, WebSocket frames End marker Read until a special byte sequence, such as a new line SMTP email, Redis, HTTP headers (end with an empty line) Fixed size Every message has the same size Simple sensor data, some games Close the connection The message ends when the sender closes HTTP/1.0 without Content-Length Common mistake A very common bug: the sender calls sendall twice and the receiver expects two recv calls to return the two messages. On a fast local test it often works, and then it fails on a real network. recv only promises \"some bytes\", so always loop until you have a whole message. Going deeper: Why TCP cuts the stream TCP puts your bytes into segments that fit the network. A segment is at most the MSS (usually 1,460 bytes on Ethernet). Nagle's algorithm may join small writes into one segment, and the receiver may read two segments at once. So the borders you see in recv have nothing to do with the borders of your sendall calls. Lesson 5 of 11"],[41,"udp","UDP: single packets, no promises","Run this one on your own computer. It opens real network sockets, and the Python inside this page has no network sockets. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 udp_ping.py. TCP compared with UDP TCP UDP Connection connect and accept first None, just sendto an address Lost packets Sent again automatically Gone, the program must notice Order Always the order they were sent Can arrive in any order Message borders Not kept Kept: one sendto is one recvfrom Header size 20 to 60 bytes 8 bytes Speed of the first message After one round trip At once Python calls connect, sendall, recv sendto, recvfrom Bottom line: TCP is better when every byte matters, such as files, web pages and payments. UDP is better when old data is useless anyway, such as the position of a player a second ago, or when one small question needs one small answer, such as DNS. Remember A UDP datagram larger than the path allows (about 1,472 bytes of data on normal Ethernet) gets split by IP, and if one piece is lost the whole datagram is lost. Keep UDP messages small. Lesson 6 of 11"],[41,"many","Serving many clients at once","Run this one on your own computer. It opens real network sockets, and the Python inside this page has no network sockets. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 many_clients.py. How a server can talk to many clients One at a time A thread per client selectors asyncio Idea accept, serve, then accept the next Each accepted socket gets its own thread One loop asks the OS which sockets are ready Coroutines that wait with await, on one event loop Many slow clients Everyone else waits Fine up to a few thousand Fine, tens of thousands Fine, tens of thousands Code Very simple Simple, but shared data needs locks Hard to write by hand Looks like normal code with async and await In Python socket socketserver.ThreadingTCPServer selectors.DefaultSelector asyncio.start_server Bottom line: For new network code use asyncio, or a framework built on it. Threads are fine for small tools, and the one at a time server is only for learning. What asyncio does in the example asyncio.start_server opens a listening socket and calls handle() for every new client. When handle() reaches await asyncio.sleep(0.1) or await reader.readline(), it pauses and the event loop runs another client. So the three clients wait at the same time, and the whole run takes about 0.1 s instead of 0.3 s. Under the hood the event loop uses selectors (epoll on Linux, kqueue on macOS) to learn which sockets have data. Lesson 7 of 11"],[41,"addresses","IP addresses, ports and layers","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 ip_ports.py. Layer Job Example In Python Application What the program means HTTP, DNS, SMTP, your own protocol requests, http.server, your code Transport Which program, and how reliable TCP, UDP (ports) socket.SOCK_STREAM, socket.SOCK_DGRAM Internet Which computer, and the route there IPv4, IPv6 (IP addresses) socket.AF_INET, socket.AF_INET6, ipaddress Link The next hop on the local network Ethernet, Wi-Fi (MAC addresses) The operating system does it In the exam A /24 network has 232 − 24 = 256 addresses. The first is the network address and the last is the broadcast address, so 254 are left for computers. Ports go from 0 to 65,535, and ports below 1024 usually need admin rights. Lesson 8 of 11"],[41,"maths","The maths: windows, round trips and loss","Addresses in a networkExam favourite addresses = 232 − prefix, usable = addresses − 2 /24: 256 addresses, 254 usable. /20: 4,096 addresses. Data in one packet MSS = MTU − IP header − TCP header 1,500 − 20 − 20 = 1,460 bytes of your data. Window limit on speedExam favourite throughput ≤ window / RTT A 64 KB window with RTT 100 ms: 65,535 × 8 / 0.1 ≈ 5.2 Mbit/s, however fast the line is. Window needed window = bandwidth × RTT (the bandwidth delay product) 100 Mbit/s and 50 ms: 100,000,000 × 0.05 / 8 = 625,000 bytes. TCP with loss (Mathis formula)Exam favourite throughput ≈ (MSS / RTT) × 1.22 / √p MSS 1,460 B, RTT 100 ms, loss p = 0.0001: about 14.2 Mbit/s. Chance of at least one loss P = 1 − (1 − p)n 100 packets with p = 0.01: 1 − 0.99100 ≈ 0.634. Worked example: why a fast line can still be slow A server is 100 ms away (RTT) on a 1 Gbit/s line. The receive window is 64 KB. TCP may only have one window of data on the way before it waits for an ACK, so each RTT moves at most 65,535 bytes. That is 65,535 × 8 / 0.1 ≈ 5.2 Mbit/s, about 0.5 % of the line. To fill the line the window must be bandwidth × RTT = 1,000,000,000 × 0.1 / 8 = 12.5 MB. Modern systems grow the window with window scaling. throughput ≤ window / RTT Lesson 9 of 11"],[41,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths Know that a socket is an IP address plus a port, that text must become bytes, and the difference between TCP and UDP. Write a TCP client and server with timeouts and error handling, use struct for binary data, and frame messages with a length prefix. Serve many clients with asyncio or selectors, compute subnets with ipaddress, and reason about windows, RTT and loss. Tune TCP options (TCP_NODELAY, keep alive, buffer sizes), understand TIME_WAIT, congestion control and QUIC, and design binary protocols that can change over time. Beginner Send and receive bytes. Intermediate Write reliable clients and servers. Expert Tune and design network protocols. Going deeper: TIME_WAIT and SO_REUSEADDR After a TCP connection closes, the side that closed first keeps the address in the TIME_WAIT state for up to a few minutes, so late packets from the old connection cannot be confused with a new one. If you restart a server quickly, bind can fail with \"Address already in use\". Setting SO_REUSEADDR before bind allows the restart. socket.create_server sets it for you on Linux and macOS. Lesson 10 of 11"],[41,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","Networks are graphs: computers are nodes and cables are edges. These problems practise the graph ideas that real routing uses, such as reachability, shortest delay, groups of connected computers and links whose failure splits a network, plus bit work like byte order. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 190 Reverse Bits Easy Take the last bit, push it in from the right Byte order and bit order matter on the wire. Take the lowest bit of n 32 times and add it to the result from the other end. 1971 Find if Path Exists in Graph Easy Breadth first search from the source Can a packet get from one computer to another? Keep a set of visited computers and a queue of computers to visit. 2073 Time Needed to Buy Tickets Easy Count what each person buys before k is done A queue served in turns, like a server that sends one piece to each client in turn. People before k buy at most tickets[k], people after k at most tickets[k] minus 1. 743 Network Delay Time Medium Dijkstra with a heap A signal spreads from computer k over links with delays. Dijkstra finds the fastest time to each computer, and the answer is the slowest of those. 1319 Number of Operations to Make Network Connected Medium Union find counts the groups You need at least n minus 1 cables. If there are enough, the answer is the number of separate groups minus 1. 1514 Path with Maximum Probability Medium Dijkstra on the largest product Each link delivers a packet with some probability. Use a heap of negative probabilities so the most likely path comes out first. 787 Cheapest Flights Within K Stops Medium Bellman Ford for k + 1 rounds Like a packet with a hop limit (TTL). Each round allows one more flight. Copy the prices first so one round uses only one more flight. 1615 Maximal Network Rank Medium Degrees of both cities, minus 1 if they are linked The rank of two cities is the number of roads touching either one. Try every pair and do not count a shared road twice. 1192 Critical Connections in a Network Hard Tarjan: discovery time and lowest reachable time A cable is critical if cutting it splits the network. In a depth first search, the link to a child is critical when the child cannot reach back above itself. 1579 Remove Max Number of Edges to Keep Graph Fully Traversable Hard Two union finds, shared edges first Use the edges both Alice and Bob can use first, then each person's own edges. Every edge that joins nothing new can be removed. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 11 of 11"],[41,"recap","Chapter summary","A socket is one end of a connection: an IP address finds the computer, a port finds the program. Sockets send and receive bytes, so encode text first. TCP server: socket, bind, listen, accept (a new socket per client). Client: create_connection. Use sendall, loop on recv, and always set a timeout. TCP is a reliable stream with no message borders, so frame messages with a length prefix (struct.pack(\"!I\", n)) or an end marker. UDP sends single datagrams with sendto and recvfrom: fast, keeps borders, but may lose or reorder them. Serve many clients with asyncio (start_server, open_connection). Speed is limited by window / RTT and by packet loss. Next: Next: calling real web APIs with the requests library. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: pack and split length prefixed messages, work with subnets using ipaddress, and read exact sizes and lines from a socket that returns small pieces.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[42,"","Calling web APIs with requests","requests: GET and POST, params and json, timeouts, errors, sessions, auth and JSON answers. · requests · web API · GET · POST · JSON · timeout · raise_for_status · Session · Bearer token · cookies Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Web and APIs›Step 2: Calling web APIs›requests Step 2 · Topic 1 of 2 · Intermediate Call web APIs the way real Python programs do. Send GET and POST requests with requests, handle every kind of error and timeout, log in with sessions and tokens, and read JSON answers safely. About 90 min10 lessons1 animation6 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1What a web API is, and why requests 2Your first GET request 3Sending data: POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE 4When a call fails 5Sessions, cookies and authentication 6Working with JSON answers 7The maths: round trips, timeouts and limits 8From beginner to expert 9LeetCode practice 10Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 10"],[42,"idea","What a web API is, and why requests","Source: The requests 2.34 documentation (quickstart and advanced usage), RFC 9110 (HTTP semantics) and the Python 3.14 documentation of json and urllib.request. What it is A web API is a website made for programs instead of people. You send an HTTP request to a URL and get data back, usually as JSON. Weather services, payment companies, GitHub and maps all offer one. requests is the most used Python library for calling them. Why we need it Python can already call APIs with urllib.request, but you must encode query strings, write JSON bodies and headers, and catch errors by hand. That is a lot of code for something every program does. How it works requests does those jobs in one call: requests.get(url, params=..., timeout=...) builds the URL, sends the request, reuses connections and returns a Response object with status_code, headers, text and json(). You only write what is special about your call. What one requests call doesPick a case. Each step runs one line and shows the HTTP text it makes and what you get back in Python. Step 0 of 7 Case Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this GET: params, timeout, the Response object and r.json(). POST: json= writes the body and the Content-Type for you. A 404 is not an exception until raise_for_status(). Always pass a timeout, and catch Timeout and ConnectionError. Python libraries for calling APIs urllib.request requests httpx aiohttp Comes with Python Yes No, pip install requests No, pip install httpx No, pip install aiohttp A JSON call Encode, build a Request, read, json.loads requests.get(url).json() httpx.get(url).json() Inside async with blocks 4xx and 5xx Raises HTTPError Returns the response, raise_for_status() raises Returns, raise_for_status() raises Returns, raise_for_status() raises async and HTTP/2 No No Both async only Best for Scripts that must not install anything Most scripts and apps The same as requests, plus async code Many calls at once in async servers Bottom line: Start with requests: its style is copied by httpx, so what you learn here moves over. Use httpx or aiohttp when you need many calls at the same time, which the next chapter shows. Lesson 2 of 10"],[42,"get","Your first GET request","Run this one on your own computer. It uses the requests library and a small server on your own computer, and the Python inside this page has no network sockets. Install requests once with pip install requests. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 first_get.py. On the Response Gives Example r.status_code, r.reason The status number and its words 200 and OK r.ok True when the status is below 400 False for 404 r.headers A dict of headers that ignores upper and lower case r.headers[\"content-type\"] r.text The body as a str, decoded with r.encoding '[{\"id\": 3 ...' r.content The body as raw bytes, for images and files b'\\x89PNG...' r.json() The body parsed from JSON into Python a list or a dict r.url, r.request The final URL, and the request that was really sent r.request.headers Common mistake Always pass timeout=. requests has no timeout by default, so a server that never answers keeps your program waiting for ever. A number such as timeout=5 is used for connecting and again for each wait while reading. Lesson 3 of 10"],[42,"send","Sending data: POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE","Run this one on your own computer. It uses the requests library and a small server on your own computer, and the Python inside this page has no network sockets. Install requests once with pip install requests. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 sending.py. Argument Sends Content-Type it sets Use it for params=dict The dict in the URL query string None Search words, filters, page numbers json=obj The object as JSON text in the body application/json Almost every modern API data=dict The dict as a web form in the body application/x-www-form-urlencoded HTML forms, OAuth token requests files=dict Files as multipart form data multipart/form-data Uploading pictures and documents headers=dict Extra headers Whatever you set API keys, Accept, a custom User-Agent Remember For a big download use requests.get(url, stream=True, timeout=10) and write for chunk in r.iter_content(64_000) into a file opened with \"wb\". Then the whole file never has to fit in memory. Real world scenario: a shop sends an order to a delivery company When a customer pays, the shop server calls the delivery company API with requests.post(url, json=order, headers=..., timeout=10). The delivery API answers 201 with a tracking number, which the shop stores. If the answer is 422, the address is wrong and the shop asks the customer. If it is 503, the shop tries again a little later. Lesson 4 of 10"],[42,"errors","When a call fails","Run this one on your own computer. It uses the requests library and a small server on your own computer, and the Python inside this page has no network sockets. Install requests once with pip install requests. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 errors.py. Exception Means Parent requests.HTTPError raise_for_status() found a 4xx or 5xx status RequestException requests.ConnectionError No connection: the server is down, the name is wrong, no network RequestException requests.Timeout (ConnectTimeout, ReadTimeout) The connect or a read took longer than timeout RequestException requests.TooManyRedirects Redirects went round in a circle RequestException requests.JSONDecodeError r.json() on a body that is not JSON RequestException and ValueError requests.RequestException The parent of all of them OSError A safe way to call an API Pass a timeout to every call. Call r.raise_for_status() right away, or check r.status_code yourself when some errors are normal (a 404 can mean \"no such user\"). Catch the exact exceptions first and RequestException last, because except blocks are checked from top to bottom. Log the URL, the status and the error message from the body, so you can find the problem later. Retry only errors that may go away (timeouts, 429, 502, 503, 504), and only for requests that are safe to repeat. Lesson 5 of 10"],[42,"session","Sessions, cookies and authentication","Run this one on your own computer. It uses the requests library and a small server on your own computer, and the Python inside this page has no network sockets. Install requests once with pip install requests. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 session_auth.py. Way to log in How requests sends it Notes API key in a header headers={\"X-API-Key\": key} Simple. Keep the key out of your code, read it from an environment variable Bearer token headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {token}\"} Most modern APIs, often an OAuth 2 access token Basic auth auth=(\"user\", \"password\") Name and password in base64, only safe over HTTPS Session cookie A Session keeps Set-Cookie and sends it back Websites that have a login form OAuth 2 Get a token first, then send it as Bearer Libraries such as requests-oauthlib do the steps for you In the exam A Session keeps cookies and default headers, and it reuses the TCP (and TLS) connection for every request to the same host. The example shows 3 requests over 1 connection. That saves one or two round trips each time, so use a Session whenever you make more than a couple of calls. Common mistake Never put a real token or password in code that you share or commit to git. Read it with os.environ[\"API_TOKEN\"] and keep it in a .env file that git ignores. Lesson 6 of 10"],[42,"json","Working with JSON answers","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 json_work.py. JSON Python after json.loads Watch out object {...} dict Keys are always str array [...] list string str Dates stay strings, parse them with datetime.fromisoformat number int or float 1 becomes int, 1.0 becomes float true, false True, False null None A missing key and a null value are different. dict.get covers both Remember Use data[\"key\"] when the key must be there (a missing key is a bug you want to see), and data.get(\"key\", default) when it is optional. The next chapter shows pydantic, which checks the whole shape of an answer at once. Lesson 7 of 10"],[42,"maths","The maths: round trips, timeouts and limits","Pages to fetchExam favourite pages = ⌈items / page size⌉ 95 items, 20 per page: ⌈4.75⌉ = 5 requests. Time without a Session n × (handshake RTTs + 1) × RTT 10 calls, 2 handshake RTTs, RTT 50 ms: 10 × 3 × 50 = 1,500 ms. Time with a SessionExam favourite (handshake RTTs + n) × RTT (2 + 10) × 50 = 600 ms, so 900 ms saved. Longest wait with timeout=(c, r) up to c + r for each silent stretch timeout=(3, 2): up to 3 s to connect, then 2 s with no data before ReadTimeout. Rate limit minimum time = requests / allowed rate 300 calls at 60 per minute: 5 minutes. Chance one of k tries worksExam favourite P = 1 − (1 − p)k p = 0.9, k = 3: 1 − 0.001 = 0.999. Worked example: download 2,000 records The API gives 100 records per page, so ⌈2000 / 100⌉ = 20 requests. RTT is 60 ms and the server needs 40 ms per page. With a Session each request costs about 100 ms. One after another that is 20 × 100 = 2,000 ms, plus 120 ms for the first handshake. Without a Session each call adds 2 handshake round trips: 20 × (100 + 120) = 4,400 ms. Session: about 2.1 s, no Session: about 4.4 s Lesson 8 of 10"],[42,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths Call requests.get with params and a timeout, read status_code and r.json(), and send JSON with requests.post. Handle every kind of error, use a Session with auth headers, send forms and files, and read nested JSON safely. Write a small client class for an API, test it with a fake server, and reason about round trips and limits. Add retries with backoff, pagination, rate limits, validation and async clients (next chapter), and keep secrets out of code. Beginner Make calls and read JSON. Intermediate Handle errors, sessions and auth. Expert Build reliable, testable API clients. Going deeper: What requests is made of requests sits on top of urllib3, which keeps a pool of open connections for each host and does the TLS work with Python's ssl module. Certificates are checked against the certifi bundle by default. That is why verify=False is dangerous: it turns off the check that you really talk to the right server. Lesson 9 of 10"],[42,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","API code spends most of its time on the data that comes back: sorting records, counting by a key, grouping by time, ranking, building feeds and spreading requests over servers. These problems practise exactly those jobs. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 2418 Sort the People Easy Sort pairs by one field Like sorting API records by a field. Zip names with heights and sort by height, largest first. 1854 Maximum Population Year Easy Count people alive in each year Records with a start and an end, like user sessions in logs. Years are only 1950 to 2050, so count each year directly. 1700 Number of Students Unable to Eat Lunch Easy Only the counts matter A queue of requests waiting for a matching resource. Count how many want each kind, then serve the stack until no one wants the top item. 2456 Most Popular Video Creator Medium Group records by creator Typical API analytics: total views per creator, and each creator's best video (most views, then the smallest id). 1366 Rank Teams by Votes Medium Sort by a tuple of vote counts Count how often each team gets each place. Sort by the counts, most first, and by the letter on a tie. 355 Design Twitter Medium Dicts of lists and sets, heapq.nlargest A small social API. Store each user's tweets with a counter as the time. The feed is the 10 newest tweets of the user and the people they follow. 1348 Tweet Counts Per Frequency Medium Put each time into a bucket API dashboards count events per minute, hour or day. The bucket of a time t is (t − start) // size. 1606 Find Servers That Handled Most Number of Requests Hard Two heaps: busy servers and free servers A load balancer. Request i prefers server i % k, or the next free one after it. Store a free server j as i + (j − i) % k so the smallest number in the heap is the right server. 2102 Sequentially Ordinal Rank Tracker Hard Two heaps split at the i-th best Keep the places already returned in one heap with the worst on top, and the rest in another with the best on top. get() moves one place across. 352 Data Stream as Disjoint Intervals Hard A set of numbers, joined into runs when asked Like tracking which parts of a file have arrived. Keep the numbers in a set, and on getIntervals walk them in order and join neighbours into ranges. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 10 of 10"],[42,"recap","Chapter summary","A web API is HTTP for programs. requests.get, post, put, patch and delete call it and return a Response. params= goes into the URL, json= sends a JSON body, data= a form, files= uploads. Always pass timeout=. A 4xx or 5xx is not an exception until raise_for_status(). Catch HTTPError, Timeout and ConnectionError, and RequestException last. A Session keeps cookies and headers and reuses connections. Send tokens in the Authorization header and keep them out of code. r.json() gives dicts and lists. Use [] for required keys and .get() for optional ones, and parse dates with datetime. Next: Next: real API work with pagination, retries, rate limits, validation and async clients. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: pick data out of a list of repositories, read a value from nested JSON by a path, and write a small API client that you can test with a fake server.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[43,"","Real API work: pages, retries, limits and validation","Pagination, retries with backoff and jitter, rate limits, pydantic validation and async calls with httpx. · pagination · cursor · retry · backoff · jitter · rate limit · token bucket · pydantic · httpx · asyncio Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Web and APIs›Step 2: Calling web APIs›API data Step 2 · Topic 2 of 2 · Intermediate Make API code that survives the real world. Read every page, retry the right errors with backoff and jitter, stay inside rate limits, check the data with pydantic, and run many calls at once with httpx. About 90 min10 lessons1 animation6 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1What changes when an API is real 2Pagination: getting every page 3Retries with backoff, jitter and Retry-After 4Rate limits: the token bucket 5Checking the data with pydantic 6Many calls at once with httpx 7The maths: backoff, limits and pages 8From beginner to expert 9LeetCode practice 10Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 10"],[43,"idea","What changes when an API is real","Source: requests, urllib3 Retry, httpx and pydantic 2 documentation, RFC 9110 and RFC 6585 (429 and Retry-After), and the AWS Architecture Blog article on exponential backoff and jitter. What it is Real APIs are bigger and less polite than examples. Answers come in pages, servers sometimes fail for a moment, they limit how many calls you may make, and the data does not always have the shape the documentation promises. Real API work means handling all four. Why we need it A script that reads only the first page misses data. A script that gives up after one 503 fails for no good reason. One that retries too fast makes the outage worse or gets blocked. One that trusts every field crashes deep inside the program on the first odd record. How it works Loop over pages with a generator, retry only temporary errors with exponential backoff and random jitter, respect 429 and Retry-After, check the data at the door with pydantic, and use an async client such as httpx when you need many calls at once. Retrying a failed callTime runs left to right. Each step shows the next tries of three clients. The server can take 2 requests at the same moment. Step 0 of 5 How to retry Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this Fixed waits: all clients retry together and keep failing. Backoff: 1, 2, 4 s. Less load, but still together. Jitter: random waits spread the clients out. Retry-After: the server says how long to wait. Ways to retry a failed call No retry Fixed wait Exponential backoff Backoff with jitter Waits None 1, 1, 1 s 1, 2, 4, 8 s random(0, 1), random(0, 2), random(0, 4) s A short outage Fails Recovers Recovers Recovers Many clients at once Fail together Retry together, keep the server down Less load, still together Spread out, the server recovers Use it for Requests that must not repeat Rarely a good choice A single client Almost everything Bottom line: Use exponential backoff with jitter, a maximum wait, a maximum number of tries, and only for errors that may go away. Lesson 2 of 10"],[43,"paging","Pagination: getting every page","Run this one on your own computer. It talks to a small server on your own computer, and the Python inside this page has no network sockets. Install the libraries once with pip install requests httpx. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 pagination.py. Style The request Good Weak Page number ?page=3&size=50 Simple, can jump to any page Items shift when data changes, so you can see one twice or miss one Offset and limit ?offset=100&limit=50 The same as pages, in rows Slow for big offsets, the database still skips every row Cursor ?after=item3 or ?cursor=eyJpZCI6... Stable while data changes, fast Cannot jump to page 40 Link header Link: <...?page=4>; rel=\"next\" The server gives the next URL You must read the header (requests: r.links[\"next\"][\"url\"]) Remember Write the loop as a generator with yield from. The caller sees one long stream of items and can stop early, and pages are only fetched when they are needed. Lesson 3 of 10"],[43,"retries","Retries with backoff, jitter and Retry-After","Run this one on your own computer. It talks to a small server on your own computer, and the Python inside this page has no network sockets. Install the libraries once with pip install requests httpx. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 retries.py. Retry? Status or error Why Yes ConnectionError, Timeout The network or the server had a short problem Yes 429 Too Many Requests Wait as long as Retry-After says, then try again Yes 502, 503, 504, sometimes 500 A server or a gateway is busy or restarting No 400, 401, 403, 404, 422 The request itself is wrong, it will fail the same way again Careful POST and PATCH Not idempotent: the first try may have worked. Retry only with an idempotency key Rules for good retries Retry only temporary errors, and only for safe or idempotent requests. Wait base × 2attempt, pick a random wait between 0 and that number (full jitter), and never more than a cap such as 30 s. If the server sends Retry-After, wait exactly that long. Stop after a fixed number of tries and raise a clear error, so the caller knows. With requests you can mount HTTPAdapter(max_retries=Retry(...)) on a Session, and urllib3 does all of this for you. Lesson 4 of 10"],[43,"limits","Rate limits: the token bucket","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 token_bucket.py. Header Meaning Retry-After: 30 Wait 30 seconds before the next call (it can also be a date) X-RateLimit-Limit: 5000 How many calls you may make in the current window X-RateLimit-Remaining: 12 How many are left X-RateLimit-Reset: 1793000000 When the window starts again, often as a Unix time RateLimit-Policy, RateLimit Newer standard names for the same ideas (an IETF draft) Real world scenario: a script that syncs orders every night A shop syncs 20,000 orders from a partner API that allows 10 calls per second. The script keeps its own token bucket with rate 10, so it never sends faster than allowed. When the partner is busy and sends 429 with Retry-After: 5, the script waits 5 seconds and goes on. The sync takes about 35 minutes and never gets the API key blocked. In the exam A token bucket holds up to capacity tokens and refills rate tokens every second. Each call takes one token, and with no token the call must wait or gets 429. So it allows short bursts but keeps the average at the rate. Servers use it to protect themselves, and clients can use it to stay polite. Lesson 5 of 10"],[43,"validate","Checking the data with pydantic","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 validate.py. Ways to check an API answer Trust it Checks by hand dataclass pydantic Wrong type, such as \"seven\" for an int Crashes later, far away Found if you wrote the check Not found Found at once with a clear message \"7\" for an int Stays a str Your choice Stays a str Turned into 7 (lax mode) Missing field KeyError somewhere Found if you wrote the check TypeError when you build it Listed with its path, such as author.name Nested objects and lists Plain dicts Much code By hand Built in Cost None Many lines Few lines One class, a fast core written in Rust Bottom line: Check data where it enters your program. pydantic is the usual choice, and FastAPI uses it for every request (step 5). Remember By default pydantic ignores fields it does not know (like \"extra\" above). Set model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"forbid\") to reject them, or strict=True to refuse \"7\" for an int. Lesson 6 of 10"],[43,"async","Many calls at once with httpx","Run this one on your own computer. It talks to a small server on your own computer, and the Python inside this page has no network sockets. Install the libraries once with pip install requests httpx. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 httpx_async.py. What happened One by one, 5 calls of 0.2 s take 5 × 0.2 = 1.0 s, because each call waits for the one before. With httpx.AsyncClient and asyncio.gather, all 5 wait at the same time, so the total is about one call: 0.2 s. An asyncio.Semaphore(2) lets only 2 run at once: ⌈5 / 2⌉ = 3 rounds of 0.2 s = 0.6 s. Always limit how many calls run at once. Without a limit, 10,000 tasks would open 10,000 connections and hit every rate limit. Need Tool A few calls in a script requests or httpx.Client Many calls at once in a script httpx.AsyncClient with asyncio.gather and a Semaphore, or a ThreadPoolExecutor with requests Calls inside an async web app (FastAPI) httpx.AsyncClient, never requests, which would block the event loop HTTP/2 httpx.Client(http2=True) after pip install httpx[http2] Lesson 7 of 10"],[43,"maths","The maths: backoff, limits and pages","Backoff waitExam favourite waitn = min(cap, base × 2n) base 0.5 s: 0.5, 1, 2, 4, 8 s for n = 0 to 4. Total backoff time base × (2k − 1) for k waits base 1 s, 4 waits: 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 = 15 s. Full jitter wait = random(0, base × 2n), mean = base × 2n / 2 n = 3, base 1 s: random between 0 and 8 s, 4 s on average. Token bucket in time TExam favourite at most capacity + rate × T calls rate 5/s, capacity 20, 10 s: 20 + 50 = 70 calls. Rows skipped by an offset skipped = (page − 1) × size page 50, size 20: 980 rows read and thrown away. Calls in flight (Little's law)Exam favourite L = rate × time per call 100 calls/s at 0.25 s each: 25 at the same moment. Worked example: how many tries are enough? A call fails for a short time with probability 0.2, and each try is independent. With 1 try the call fails with probability 0.2. With 4 tries all must fail: 0.24 = 0.0016, that is 16 in 10,000. With backoff base 0.5 s the 3 waits add up to 0.5 + 1 + 2 = 3.5 s at most, which is fine for a nightly job but too long for a web page. P(all k tries fail) = pk Lesson 8 of 10"],[43,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths Loop over pages until there are no more, and know which errors are worth a retry. Write page generators, retries with backoff and jitter, respect 429 and Retry-After, and check answers with pydantic. Run many calls at once with httpx and a Semaphore, keep a client side token bucket, and use cursors for data that changes. Design clients with idempotency keys, circuit breakers and timeouts on every layer, and measure them with logs and metrics. Beginner Read every page safely. Intermediate Retry, limit and validate. Expert Build clients that stay up when the API does not. Going deeper: Circuit breaker If an API keeps failing, every caller still waits for timeouts and retries. A circuit breaker counts failures, and after too many it \"opens\": calls fail at once for a while without touching the network. After a pause it lets one test call through and closes again if that works. Libraries such as pybreaker do this, and service meshes do it for whole systems. Lesson 9 of 10"],[43,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","API work is full of time ranges and streams: merging sorted pages, finding records that two sources share, booking time slots without overlap, keeping the k largest or the median of recent values, and refusing a third overlapping booking. These problems practise those patterns. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 21 Merge Two Sorted Lists Easy Walk both lists, always take the smaller head Like merging two sorted pages of results from two APIs. A dummy first node keeps the code short. 350 Intersection of Two Arrays II Easy Counter and the & operator Which ids do two API answers share, counting repeats? Counter(a) & Counter(b) keeps the smaller count of each value. 1450 Number of Students Doing Homework at a Given Time Easy Check each time range Like counting the sessions that were active at a given moment in API logs. A range counts when start <= time <= end. 703 Kth Largest Element in a Stream Easy A min heap of size k Keep only the k largest values seen so far in a heap. Its smallest value is the k-th largest, like the slowest of the top k response times. 57 Insert Interval Medium Before, overlapping, after Add a time range to a sorted list of ranges. Copy the ranges that end before it, merge the ones that overlap, then copy the rest. 729 My Calendar I Medium Two ranges overlap when each starts before the other ends A booking API must refuse a slot that overlaps an existing one. [s1, e1) and [s2, e2) overlap when s1 < e2 and s2 < e1. 731 My Calendar II Medium Keep the bookings and the double booked parts Now two bookings may overlap but never three. Keep a list of the parts that are already double booked. A new booking that touches one of them must be refused. 435 Non-overlapping Intervals Medium Greedy: keep the range that ends first Sort by end time. Keep a range when it starts after the last kept one ends, otherwise remove it. Ending early leaves the most room. 480 Sliding Window Median Hard Keep the window sorted with bisect Monitoring shows the median response time of the last k calls. Keep the window sorted: insort the new value, remove the old one, and read the middle. 732 My Calendar III Hard Sweep line: +1 at a start, −1 at an end How many bookings overlap at the busiest moment? Add +1 at each start and −1 at each end, then walk the times in order and keep a running sum. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 10 of 10"],[43,"recap","Chapter summary","APIs answer in pages. Walk them with a generator. Cursors stay correct while data changes, page numbers do not. Retry only temporary errors (timeouts, 429, 502, 503, 504) and only safe requests, with exponential backoff, jitter and a cap. Respect 429 and Retry-After. A token bucket allows bursts but keeps the average rate. Check answers at the door with pydantic: types, required fields and nested data, with clear errors. httpx.AsyncClient with asyncio.gather runs many calls at once. Always limit them with a Semaphore. Next: Next: build your own web apps and APIs with Flask. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: collect every page from a cursor API safely, compute backoff waits and read Retry-After, and validate a user record by hand.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[44,"","Flask basics: routes, templates, forms and JSON","Flask routes, request and response, Jinja templates, forms and sessions, a JSON API and running the app. · Flask · route · url_for · request · Jinja · template · session · form · JSON API · test client Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Web and APIs›Step 3: Flask: small and flexible web apps›Flask basics Step 3 · Topic 1 of 2 · Intermediate Build your first web app with Flask. Connect URLs to functions, read what the browser sends, make HTML pages with Jinja templates, handle forms and sessions, and write a JSON API that you test without a browser. About 100 min11 lessons1 animation6 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1What Flask is 2Routes and URLs 3The request and the response 4Templates with Jinja 5Forms, sessions and flash messages 6A JSON API in Flask 7Running a Flask app for real 8The maths: workers, time and limits 9From beginner to expert 10LeetCode practice 11Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 11"],[44,"idea","What Flask is","Source: Flask 3.1 documentation (quickstart, API, testing, deploying), Jinja 3.1 template designer documentation and PEP 3333 (WSGI). What it is Flask is a small web framework for Python. You write normal functions and tell Flask which URL each one answers with @app.route. Flask gives every function the request (the URL, the form, the JSON, the cookies) and turns what it returns into an HTTP response. It comes with Jinja templates for HTML pages and a test client. Why we need it With only http.server you would parse paths, read bodies, set headers, escape HTML and keep sessions yourself, for every page. That is slow to write and easy to get wrong, for example by letting users inject scripts. How it works Flask does the repeated work and stays small: routing, request and response objects, templates with automatic escaping, signed session cookies and error handlers. Everything else (database, login, forms) you add with plain Python or extensions, only when you need it. That is why it is called a microframework. One request through FlaskPick a request. Each step moves it one stage further and shows what Flask knows at that point. Step 0 of 9 Request Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this A page: route, view, template, response. A form: session cookie and redirect. 404: no route matches, an error handler answers. An API: int converter and a dict that becomes JSON. Python web frameworks http.server Flask Django FastAPI Size Standard library, very basic Small core, add what you need Batteries included: ORM, admin, auth, forms Small core focused on APIs Routing Write if statements on self.path @app.route(\"/books/<int:id>\") urls.py with path(\"books/<int:id>/\", view) @app.get(\"/books/{id}\") Data checks By hand By hand or an extension Forms and serializers Automatic from type hints (pydantic) Interface Its own WSGI (sync), async views possible WSGI and ASGI ASGI (async) Good for Learning, tiny tools Small and medium sites, APIs, prototypes Big sites with users, admin and a database Fast JSON APIs with automatic docs Bottom line: Learn Flask first: you see every moving part. Django (step 4) then shows what a full framework adds, and FastAPI (step 5) what type hints add for APIs. Lesson 2 of 11"],[44,"routes","Routes and URLs","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 routes.py. Converter Matches Gives the view <name> or <string:name> Any text without a slash str <int:id> Whole numbers such as 42 int <float:price> Numbers such as 9.5 float <path:rest> Text that may contain slashes str <uuid:key> A UUID uuid.UUID Remember Build links with url_for(\"hello\", name=\"Bob\") and never by hand. If you rename a URL later, every link still works, and url_for escapes special characters for you. Extra arguments become the query string, as with page=3 above. In the exam A route answers only GET (and HEAD, OPTIONS) unless you pass methods=. A wrong method gives 405 Method Not Allowed, a URL that matches no rule gives 404 Not Found. Lesson 3 of 11"],[44,"request","The request and the response","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 request_response.py. On request Holds Example request.args The query string, like a dict request.args.get(\"page\", 1, type=int) request.form Fields of an HTML form request.form[\"user\"] request.get_json() The JSON body silent=True gives None instead of an error request.files Uploaded files request.files[\"photo\"].save(path) request.headers, cookies Headers and cookies request.headers[\"User-Agent\"] request.method, path, url The method and the address \"POST\", \"/login\" A view returns Flask sends A str 200 with Content-Type text/html A dict or a list 200 with JSON (keys sorted by default) (body, status) or (body, status, headers) Your status and extra headers redirect(url) 302 with a Location header abort(code) Stops at once with that error status make_response(...) A Response object you can change: headers, cookies Remember request looks like a global variable, but each request in each thread sees its own. Flask does this with context variables, so two users at the same moment never see each other's data. Lesson 4 of 11"],[44,"templates","Templates with Jinja","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 templates.py. Jinja Does Example {{ value }} Prints a value, HTML escaped {{ user }} {% for ... %} ... {% endfor %} A loop, with loop.index and an else part for empty lists for book in books {% if ... %} A condition if book.year < 1900 value|filter Changes a value upper, length, default(\"none\"), round(2) {% extends %} and {% block %} One base page, other pages fill its blocks extends \"base.html\" {% include %}, macros Reuse a part of a page include \"nav.html\" Common mistake Jinja escapes every value by default in .html templates, so a name such as <script> is shown as text and never runs. This stops cross site scripting (XSS). Only mark text as safe with |safe when you made it yourself. Real world scenario: a shop with 40 pages All 40 pages of a shop share the same header, menu and footer. With template inheritance there is one base.html with a block for the content, and each page only fills that block. Changing the menu means editing one file, not 40. Lesson 5 of 11"],[44,"forms","Forms, sessions and flash messages","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 forms_session.py. How the login works GET /login shows the form. The browser sends it back as POST /login with the fields in the body. The view reads request.form, checks the password and stores the user name in session. Flask puts the session into a cookie, signed with app.secret_key. The user can read it but cannot change it, because the signature would not match. The view answers with a redirect (Post, Redirect, Get), so pressing refresh does not send the form again. flash() stores a short message that the next page shows once. Common mistake The session cookie is signed, NOT encrypted: the example decodes it and reads the user name. Never put secrets in it, keep secret_key secret and long, and read it from an environment variable. In the exam Real forms also need protection against cross site request forgery (CSRF), so another website cannot post forms as your user. The extension Flask-WTF adds a hidden token to every form and checks it. Lesson 6 of 11"],[44,"api","A JSON API in Flask","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 json_api.py. Request View Success Errors GET /api/books list_books 200 and a JSON list GET /api/books/7 get_book 200 and the book 404 with a JSON error POST /api/books create_book 201, the book and Location 422 for bad data DELETE /api/books/1 delete_book 204 with an empty body 404 Remember @app.get, @app.post, @app.put, @app.patch and @app.delete are short forms of @app.route with one method. They make an API easier to read. Common mistake A global dict like BOOKS is fine for learning, but it is lost when the server restarts, and each Gunicorn worker process has its own copy. Real apps keep data in a database, as the next chapter shows. Lesson 7 of 11"],[44,"running","Running a Flask app for real","Run this one on your own computer. It starts a real web server on your computer and calls it with requests. Install both with pip install flask requests. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 real_server.py. From one file to a running site Make a virtual environment and install Flask: python3 -m venv .venv, activate it, then pip install flask. Save the app as app.py and start the development server with flask --app app run --debug. Open http://127.0.0.1:5000 in a browser. --debug reloads the code when you save a file and shows an interactive error page. Never use it on a public server: the error page can run Python code. In production run the app with a real WSGI server such as gunicorn -w 4 \"app:app\", behind a web server such as nginx that handles HTTPS. Keep settings such as SECRET_KEY and the database address in environment variables, not in the code. Lesson 8 of 11"],[44,"maths","The maths: workers, time and limits","Requests in progress (Little's law)Exam favourite L = λ × W 120 requests/s, 0.2 s each: 24 requests at the same moment, so at least 24 workers or threads. Gunicorn starting point workers = 2 × CPU cores + 1 4 cores: 9 worker processes. Throughput of sync workersExam favourite requests/s ≤ workers / time per request 9 workers, 50 ms each: 9 / 0.05 = 180 requests/s. Python time limit with threads (GIL) requests/s ≤ 1 / CPU time per request, per process 10 ms of Python per request: at most 100 requests/s for one process, however many threads. Cookie size base64 makes data 4 / 3 as big A 4,096 byte cookie holds about 3,072 bytes of session data. Error rate rate = errors / requests × 100 % 30 errors out of 600 requests: 5 %. Worked example: how many workers? A page needs 10 ms of Python and 40 ms of waiting for the database: 50 ms in total. You expect 300 requests per second at the busiest time. Little's law: 300 × 0.05 = 15 requests are in progress at once, so you need at least 15 workers or threads. CPU limit: each request needs 10 ms of Python, so one process can do at most 100 per second. You need at least 3 processes, for example 4 workers with 4 threads each. workers × threads ≥ λ × W, and processes ≥ λ × CPU time Lesson 9 of 11"],[44,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths Write routes that return text, read the query string, and render a template with a loop. Handle forms and sessions with redirects and flash messages, build a JSON API with the right status codes, and test it with the test client. Use template inheritance, error handlers and hooks, and run the app with Gunicorn, configured by environment variables. Understand WSGI, the application and request contexts, signed cookies and CSRF, and size a deployment with Little's law. Beginner Make pages and simple APIs. Intermediate Handle forms, sessions and errors. Expert Deploy and scale safely. Going deeper: What WSGI is WSGI (PEP 3333) is the agreement between Python web servers and frameworks. The server calls app(environ, start_response): environ is a dict with the request, and start_response receives the status and headers. A Flask app is such a callable. That is why the same Flask app runs on Werkzeug, Gunicorn, uWSGI or Waitress without changes. ASGI is the async version that FastAPI and Django channels use. Lesson 10 of 11"],[44,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","A web framework is mostly text work: splitting paths into parts, matching prefixes, cleaning input with a stack, finding template parts and evaluating small expressions. These problems practise exactly that. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 434 Number of Segments in a String Easy str.split() with no argument Like counting the words a user typed into a search box. split() with no argument skips any amount of spaces, so empty parts never appear. 1662 Check If Two String Arrays are Equivalent Easy Join and compare A page built from template parts must give the same text however it was cut. Join both lists and compare the results. 1544 Make The String Great Easy A stack of letters Cleaning user input with a stack. Push each letter, but pop instead when it is the same letter in the other case as the top. 2696 Minimum String Length After Removing Substrings Easy A stack removes pairs as they appear Removing \"AB\" and \"CD\" again and again is the same as a stack that pops when the new letter completes a pair with the top. 648 Replace Words Medium Check the shortest roots first Like a router that finds the shortest registered prefix of a path. Put the roots in a set and try prefixes from short to long. 1209 Remove All Adjacent Duplicates in String II Medium A stack of [letter, count] Keep each letter with how many times it repeats on top of the stack. When the count reaches k, pop the whole group. 820 Short Encoding of Words Medium Drop words that are endings of other words A word that is the end of another word needs no space of its own. Remove every ending of every word from a set, then add len(word) + 1 for each word that is left. 916 Word Subsets Medium Merge the needs into one Counter A word is universal when it has every letter of every word in words2, as often as needed. Merge words2 into one Counter of the largest counts, then check each word once. 30 Substring with Concatenation of All Words Hard Cut each window into words and compare Counters Find every place where all the words appear joined in any order, like spotting a set of template parts in a page. Each window has a fixed length, so cut it into word sized pieces and compare counts. 224 Basic Calculator Hard A stack of (result, sign) for each bracket Templates and config files evaluate small expressions. Walk the text, build numbers digit by digit, and push the result so far when a bracket opens. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 11 of 11"],[44,"recap","Chapter summary","Flask maps URLs to view functions with @app.route. Converters such as <int:id> check and convert parts of the path. url_for builds links. request gives args, form, get_json(), files, headers and cookies. A view returns a str, a dict or list (JSON), a (body, status) pair, redirect() or abort(). Jinja templates print values with escaping, loop, test conditions, and share a base page with extends and block. session is a signed cookie: readable, not changeable. Use Post, Redirect, Get after forms and flash() for one time messages. Test with app.test_client(). Develop with flask run --debug, deploy with Gunicorn and keep secrets in environment variables. Next: Next: real Flask apps with blueprints, a database, logins and tests. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: write the URL matching that Flask does, build a small notes API and test it with the test client, and write a tiny template engine with filters and escaping.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[45,"","Real Flask apps: structure, database, login and tests","Application factory, blueprints, sqlite3, login with hashed passwords, pytest, config and deployment. · application factory · blueprint · sqlite3 · login · password hash · pytest · fixture · config · Gunicorn · deployment Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Web and APIs›Step 3: Flask: small and flexible web apps›Flask apps Step 3 · Topic 2 of 2 · Intermediate Turn a one file Flask app into one you can keep. Build it with an application factory and blueprints, store data with sqlite3, add users with hashed passwords, test it with pytest, configure it from the environment and deploy it. About 100 min11 lessons1 animation6 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1From one file to a real app 2The application factory and blueprints 3A database with sqlite3 4Users, passwords and login_required 5Testing a Flask app 6Configuration, errors and logging 7Deploying a Flask app 8The maths: hashing cost, commits and capacity 9From beginner to expert 10LeetCode practice 11Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 11"],[45,"idea","From one file to a real app","Source: Flask 3.1 tutorial (flaskr), Flask configuration, error handling, logging, testing and deployment documentation, and the Werkzeug 3.1 security helpers. What it is A real Flask app is the same Flask, organised so it can grow: an application factory that builds the app, blueprints that split it into parts, a database, logins with hashed passwords, tests, configuration from the environment and a way to deploy it. Why we need it One app.py with global objects is fine for 50 lines. At 2,000 lines it gets hard to find things, tests change each other's data, secrets end up in git, and two people cannot work on it at the same time without conflicts. How it works create_app() builds a fresh app each time, so tests and production get their own settings. Blueprints put each feature in its own file. The database connection lives on g for one request and is closed by teardown. Passwords are stored only as salted hashes. pytest fixtures give every test a clean app and database. Inside a real Flask appPick a view of the app. Each step moves one stage further and shows what the app knows at that point. Step 0 of 6 Show Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this create_app: config layers, database helpers, blueprints. A request: g.user, login_required, g.db, teardown. Login: redirect, password hash, session. A test: fixtures, a fresh app and database, the test client. Ways to organise a Python web app One app.py file Factory and blueprints Django project Where code lives Everything in one file A package: __init__.py, auth.py, books.py, db.py Apps with models.py, views.py, urls.py Tests Share one global app Each test builds its own app A test runner with a test database Settings Often written in the code Defaults, then settings, then environment settings.py, often read from the environment Database and login By hand sqlite3 by hand, or Flask-SQLAlchemy and Flask-Login Built in: ORM, migrations, auth, admin Good for Demos Most Flask apps Big apps that need everything Bottom line: Use the factory and blueprints for anything you will keep. It costs a few lines and saves a lot when the app grows. Lesson 2 of 11"],[45,"layout","The application factory and blueprints","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 factory_blueprints.py. File or folder Holds shop/__init__.py create_app(): config, database setup, register_blueprint for each part shop/db.py get_db(), close_db(), the init-db command shop/auth.py Blueprint auth: register, login, logout, login_required shop/books.py Blueprint books: the pages of the shop shop/api.py Blueprint api with url_prefix=\"/api\": the JSON API shop/templates/, shop/static/ Jinja templates, CSS, JavaScript and images instance/ Files that belong to one installation: the SQLite file, a local config. Not in git tests/conftest.py, tests/test_*.py pytest fixtures and tests pyproject.toml The package name and its dependencies In the exam Inside a blueprint, link with the blueprint name: url_for(\"api.status\") or url_for(\".status\") for the same blueprint. Use current_app instead of a global app variable, because with a factory there is no global app. Lesson 3 of 11"],[45,"database","A database with sqlite3","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 database.py. Ways to use a database in Flask sqlite3 by hand Flask-SQLAlchemy Django ORM You write SQL text with ? placeholders Python classes, queries as method calls Python classes, queries as method calls Changing tables later Write ALTER TABLE yourself Flask-Migrate (Alembic) makes migration files makemigrations and migrate are built in Other databases Change the code for PostgreSQL Change one URL in the config Change the settings Good for Learning SQL, small apps Most Flask apps that grow Django apps Bottom line: Learn it by hand first, as here, so you know what an ORM writes for you. Step 4 shows the Django ORM. The connection rules in the example get_db() opens one connection the first time a request needs it and keeps it on g. g lives for one request only. sqlite3.Row lets you read row[\"title\"] and turn a row into a dict with dict(row). Always pass values as ? placeholders. The SQL injection attempt above is stored as harmless text. Call commit() after changes. teardown_appcontext closes the connection after every request, even after an error. @app.cli.command adds flask init-db, and app.test_cli_runner() runs it from a test. Lesson 4 of 11"],[45,"auth","Users, passwords and login_required","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 auth.py. Rule Why Store generate_password_hash(...), never the password A stolen database must not reveal passwords Use a slow hash with a salt (scrypt or PBKDF2, Werkzeug picks scrypt by default) Slow makes guessing expensive, the salt makes the same password give different hashes Say \"wrong name or password\", not which one Attackers cannot test which names exist session.clear() before storing the user No data from before the login survives (session fixation) Set SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE and SESSION_COOKIE_HTTPONLY in production The cookie only travels over HTTPS and JavaScript cannot read it Limit login attempts, for example 5 a minute per user Stops fast guessing Remember login_required is a normal decorator (Advanced step 2). functools.wraps keeps the view name, which Flask needs, because the endpoint name comes from the function name. In the exam For bigger apps, Flask-Login manages the logged in user and \"remember me\", and Flask-WTF adds CSRF tokens to forms. They do the same jobs as the code here, with more cases covered. Lesson 5 of 11"],[45,"testing","Testing a Flask app","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 testing.py. Remember With pytest the helper becomes a fixture in tests/conftest.py, and pytest passes it to every test that has a parameter called client: import pytest from notes import create_app @pytest.fixture def client(): app = create_app({\"TESTING\": True}) return app.test_client() Then run python3 -m pytest -q. A real run of exactly this printed \"2 passed in 0.18s\". Kind of test Checks Tool Unit test One function, such as bad_book(data) pytest Route test One URL: status code, JSON, redirects app.test_client() Command test flask init-db and other commands app.test_cli_runner() End to end test A real browser clicking through pages Playwright or Selenium (see those subjects) Coverage Which lines the tests ran coverage run -m pytest, then coverage report Lesson 6 of 11"],[45,"config","Configuration, errors and logging","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 config_logging.py. Source Example Use it for Defaults in create_app app.config.from_mapping(PAGE_SIZE=10) Values that are safe everywhere Settings passed to create_app create_app({\"TESTING\": True}) Tests A file in instance/ app.config.from_pyfile(\"config.py\", silent=True) One installation Environment variables FLASK_SECRET_KEY=... with from_prefixed_env() Secrets and production values Remember Flask turns an exception in a view into a 500 answer. An errorhandler(Exception) can log it and send a friendly message, but must pass HTTPExceptions such as 404 through unchanged, as the example does. Use app.logger, never print, so logs get levels and can go to files or a log service. Lesson 7 of 11"],[45,"deploy","Deploying a Flask app","From your computer to a public server List the dependencies in pyproject.toml or requirements.txt (pip freeze > requirements.txt). Never use flask run --debug in public. Run the app with a WSGI server: gunicorn -w 4 -b 0.0.0.0:8000 \"shop:create_app()\". Put nginx (or the hosting platform) in front: it serves HTTPS, static files and passes the rest to Gunicorn. Set SECRET_KEY, the database URL and other secrets as environment variables on the server. Use PostgreSQL or MySQL instead of SQLite when many users write at the same time, and run migrations on each release. Add a health check URL such as /health that returns 200, so the platform can restart the app when it stops answering. Way to host You manage Example A platform (PaaS) Only the code and settings Render, Railway, Fly.io, Google App Engine A container A Dockerfile, the platform runs it Docker image on Cloud Run, AWS ECS, Kubernetes Your own server Everything: Linux, nginx, Gunicorn, updates A virtual machine with systemd Real world scenario: a small team ships every day A team keeps its Flask shop in git. Every push runs the pytest suite in CI (see the Git and CI/CD subject). When the tests pass on the main branch, CI builds a Docker image with Gunicorn inside and the platform swaps it in. SECRET_KEY and DATABASE_URL live in the platform settings, never in the repository. Lesson 8 of 11"],[45,"maths","The maths: hashing cost, commits and capacity","Guesses per second against a slow hashExam favourite guesses/s = raw hashes per second / iterations 10 million SHA-256 per second, 1,000,000 PBKDF2 iterations: 10 guesses per second. Number of passwords N = (letters in the alphabet)length 8 lower case letters: 268 ≈ 2.09 × 1011. Average time to guessExam favourite time ≈ N / 2 / guesses per second 2.09 × 1011 / 2 / 10 per second ≈ 331 years. Commits many commits cost n × commit time, one transaction costs one 200 inserts with a 5 ms commit each: 1,000 ms. In one transaction: about 15 ms. Salt collisions (birthday bound) n ≈ 1.18 × √(number of salts) for a 50 % chance 32 bit salts: 1.18 × 65,536 ≈ 77,000 users. Werkzeug uses 16 random characters, far more. Test suite time time = tests × time per test 120 tests at 15 ms: 1.8 s, fast enough to run on every save. Worked example: why a fast hash is not enough A leaked database stores SHA-256 of each password with no salt. A graphics card computes about 10 billion SHA-256 a second. All 268 ≈ 2.09 × 1011 lower case passwords of 8 letters take about 21 seconds. With PBKDF2 and 1,000,000 iterations each guess costs a million hashes: 10,000 guesses a second. The same search now takes 2.09 × 1011 / 10,000 ≈ 2.1 × 107 s, about 242 days, and a salt stops one search from cracking every user at once. slow, salted hashes turn seconds into months Lesson 9 of 11"],[45,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths Split an app into blueprints, use an application factory, and store data with sqlite3 and ? placeholders. Add register and login with hashed passwords and login_required, and write pytest tests with a fixture for the client. Configure the app from the environment, log errors properly, and deploy it with Gunicorn behind HTTPS. Move to an ORM with migrations, add CSRF protection, rate limits and monitoring, and design the app for many workers and a shared database. Beginner Structure the code. Intermediate Add users and tests. Expert Run it safely in production. Going deeper: Application context and request context current_app and g work only inside an application context, and request and session only inside a request context. Flask pushes both for every request, and the test client does the same. In a script or a test without a request, use with app.app_context(): to get current_app and g. Lesson 10 of 11"],[45,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","Most Flask apps sit on a database, so these are SQL problems from the LeetCode database set, each solved with Python and an in-memory sqlite3 database: filters, NULL handling, self joins, grouping, rates and window functions. The last one is a counting structure for page views. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 595 Big Countries Easy WHERE with OR A country is big when its area is at least 3,000,000 or its population at least 25,000,000. One WHERE with OR does it. 1757 Recyclable and Low Fat Products Easy WHERE with AND Both columns must be \"Y\". The kind of filter a shop page runs for every search. 584 Find Customer Referee Easy NULL needs IS NULL referee_id != 2 is not true for NULL, because NULL compared with anything is unknown. Add OR referee_id IS NULL. 1148 Article Views I Easy DISTINCT with a column compared to another Authors who viewed their own article: author_id = viewer_id. DISTINCT removes repeats. 570 Managers with at Least 5 Direct Reports Medium GROUP BY with HAVING, then join back Count the people per managerId, keep the ones with 5 or more, and join with the table again to get the names. 1934 Confirmation Rate Medium LEFT JOIN, AVG of a CASE, IFNULL Every user who signed up must appear, even with no confirmations, so use a LEFT JOIN. The rate is the average of 1 for confirmed and 0 otherwise. 180 Consecutive Numbers Medium Join the table with itself twice Pair each row with the next two ids. If all three have the same number, that number appears at least three times in a row. 550 Game Play Analysis IV Medium First day per player, then look for the next day Find each player's first login with MIN, check whether they came back the day after, and divide by the number of players. 601 Human Traffic of Stadium Hard Window function: id minus row number groups a run Keep the days with 100 or more people. Within a run of consecutive ids, id minus ROW_NUMBER() stays the same, so it names the run. Keep runs of 3 or more. 432 All O`one Data Structure Hard A dict of counts (simple version) Count page views per key and ask for the most and least viewed key. This simple version keeps a dict and uses max and min. The O(1) version keeps a linked list of buckets, one bucket for each count. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 11 of 11"],[45,"recap","Chapter summary","create_app() builds a fresh app with layered config. Blueprints split features into files, and current_app replaces a global app. get_db() keeps one sqlite3 connection on g per request, teardown_appcontext closes it, and values always go in as ? placeholders. Store only salted slow hashes (generate_password_hash), clear the session at login, and protect views with a login_required decorator. Test with pytest fixtures that build a new app and client for each test. Run commands with test_cli_runner. Configure from the environment, log with app.logger, and deploy with Gunicorn behind HTTPS, never with the debug server. Next: Next: Django, the framework with everything built in. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: write a login_required decorator, write safe database helpers with sqlite3, and hash and check passwords with PBKDF2.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[46,"","Django basics: project, URLs, views, templates, models and admin","Project and apps, urlpatterns, views, templates, models with QuerySets, and the admin site. · Django · startproject · startapp · urlpatterns · views · templates · models · QuerySet · admin · middleware Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Web and APIs›Step 4: Django: the framework with everything built in›Django basics Step 4 · Topic 1 of 2 · Intermediate Build your first Django site. Make a project and an app, send URLs to views, show pages with templates, store data with models and manage it in the admin site. About 110 min10 lessons1 animation6 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1What Django is and why it has so much built in 2A project and its apps 3URLs and views 4Templates 5Models: Python classes that become tables 6The admin site 7The maths: URL matching, pages, queries and hashing 8From beginner to expert 9LeetCode practice 10Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 10"],[46,"idea","What Django is and why it has so much built in","Source: Django 6.1 documentation: tutorial parts 1 to 7, URL dispatcher, views, class based views, templates, models, the admin site, settings, middleware, CSRF protection and the deployment checklist. What it is Django is a Python web framework with everything a website needs in one package: URL routing, views, a template language, an ORM that turns Python classes into database tables, migrations, users and logins, forms, an admin site and strong security defaults. Why we need it With a small framework you choose and connect many libraries yourself: one for the database, one for migrations, one for logins, one for forms, one for an admin page. Each choice takes time, and the parts do not always fit together. Most business websites need all of these parts. How it works Django gives one tested way to do each job, and all parts know each other. A model you write is used by migrations, forms, the admin and the ORM at the same time. You write models.py, views.py, urls.py and templates, and Django does the rest of the work around them. Inside DjangoPick what to follow. Each step moves one stage further and shows what Django knows at that point. Step 0 of 7 Show Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this startproject, startapp, makemigrations, migrate, runserver. One page: middleware, urlpatterns, view, ORM, template. APPEND_SLASH: CommonMiddleware answers with 301. CSRF: the middleware stops a forged form with 403. The admin: staff check, search_fields, list_display. Three popular Python web frameworks Flask Django FastAPI Style Small core, add what you need Everything built in Built for JSON APIs, uses type hints Database Your choice (sqlite3, SQLAlchemy) The Django ORM with migrations Your choice (SQLAlchemy, SQLModel) Users and admin Extensions such as Flask-Login Built in: auth and a full admin site Write them or add libraries HTML pages Jinja templates Django templates Possible, but not its focus Good for Small apps, services, learning Shops, news sites, internal tools, big projects Fast APIs with automatic docs Used by Pinterest (early), many services Instagram, Mozilla, Disqus Many new API services Bottom line: Pick Django when the site has users, data and an admin team. Pick Flask or FastAPI for a small service or an API without pages. Step 5 shows Django REST framework and FastAPI. In the exam Django calls its pattern MTV: the Model holds the data, the Template shows it, and the View decides what to show. It is the same idea as MVC, only the names differ: the Django view does the job of an MVC controller. Lesson 2 of 10"],[46,"project","A project and its apps","Run this one on your own computer. It runs the real django-admin and manage.py commands in a temporary folder. Install Django first with pip install django. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 real_project.py. File What it is for manage.py Runs every command: runserver, startapp, makemigrations, migrate, createsuperuser, shell, test mysite/settings.py All settings: INSTALLED_APPS, MIDDLEWARE, DATABASES, TEMPLATES, DEBUG, SECRET_KEY, ALLOWED_HOSTS mysite/urls.py The first URL list. It sends parts of the site to the apps with include() mysite/wsgi.py, asgi.py The entry point for Gunicorn (WSGI) or Uvicorn (ASGI) in production books/models.py The models of the app, one class for each table books/views.py, books/urls.py The views of the app and their URL patterns (you make urls.py yourself) books/admin.py Which models show up in the admin site, and how books/migrations/ The migration files that makemigrations writes. Keep them in git books/templates/books/ The HTML templates of the app. The second books folder stops name clashes between apps The commands you use every day django-admin startproject mysite makes the project once. python manage.py startapp books makes each app. Add the app to INSTALLED_APPS, write the models, then run python manage.py makemigrations and python manage.py migrate. python manage.py createsuperuser makes an admin user. python manage.py runserver starts the development server at http://127.0.0.1:8000/. python manage.py shell opens Python with your project loaded, which is handy to try ORM queries. python manage.py check --deploy lists settings that are not safe for production. Remember A project is the whole website with its settings. An app is one feature, such as books, accounts or payments. A good app does one thing, so you could even reuse it in another project. Lesson 3 of 10"],[46,"urls","URLs and views","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 urls_views.py. Path converter Matches Gives the view <str:name> (the default) Any text without a slash str <int:book_id> 0 or a positive whole number int <slug:slug> Letters, digits, hyphens and underscores, such as dune-messiah str <uuid:token> A UUID such as 075194d3-6885-417e-a8a8-6c931e272f00 uuid.UUID <path:file> Any text, slashes too str re_path(r\"...\") A regular expression, for special cases str for each group Two ways to write a view Function based view Class based view Looks like def book_list(request): ... class BookList(ListView): model = Book HTTP methods if request.method == \"POST\": ... or @require_http_methods A method for each: get(), post() Reuse Helpers and decorators Inheritance and mixins, many generic views ready made Good for Clear, special logic Standard pages: lists, details, create, update, delete Bottom line: Start with functions, because you can read them from top to bottom. Use generic class based views such as ListView and DetailView when a page is a standard list or detail page. What the example shows Django tries urlpatterns from top to bottom and calls the first view that matches. The names, such as \"book-detail\", let reverse() and the template tag url build links, so a URL can change in one place. request.GET holds the query string and request.POST the form fields. HttpResponse sends text, JsonResponse sends JSON. Raising Http404 gives a 404 answer. @require_http_methods([\"POST\"]) gives 405 to every other method. redirect(\"book-detail\", book_id=3) sends a 302 to the new page. This is the Post, Redirect, Get pattern again. /hello/Ann without the last slash gets a 301 to /hello/Ann/, because CommonMiddleware with APPEND_SLASH adds it. In the exam The test client does not check CSRF tokens unless you ask it to, so the POST in the example works without one. In a real project CsrfViewMiddleware is on, and every POST form needs {% csrf_token %}. The animation above shows what happens without it. Lesson 4 of 10"],[46,"templates","Templates","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 templates.py. Template code What it does {{ book.title }} Shows a value. The dot tries a dict key, then an attribute, then a list index {{ name|default:\"guest\"|title }} Filters change a value, and you can chain them {% for book in books %} ... {% empty %} ... {% endfor %} A loop, with a part for an empty list. forloop.counter counts from 1 {% if book.year < 1900 %} ... {% endif %} A condition {% url \"book-detail\" book.id %} Builds a link from the URL name, like reverse() {% extends \"base.html\" %} and {% block content %} Template inheritance: a page fills the blocks of a base layout {% csrf_token %} The hidden token that every POST form needs {# a comment #} A comment that never reaches the browser Django templates and Jinja Django template language Jinja (used by Flask) Calling functions No arguments allowed, keeps logic out of the page Full expressions such as f(x, y) Speed Fast enough for most sites Faster for heavy templates Escaping On by default On by default in Flask In Django The default Possible, as a second backend in TEMPLATES Bottom line: Both escape HTML by default. The Django language is kept simple on purpose, so designers can edit templates and logic stays in the views. Remember Every value is escaped, so the script tag in the note shows up as harmless text. Only mark text as safe (the safe filter or mark_safe) when you made it yourself, never for text a user typed. Lesson 5 of 10"],[46,"models","Models: Python classes that become tables","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 models_intro.py. Field Column in SQLite Use it for CharField(max_length=200) varchar(200) Short text such as a title TextField() text Long text such as a description IntegerField(), PositiveIntegerField() integer Whole numbers DecimalField(max_digits=6, decimal_places=2) decimal Money. Never use FloatField for money BooleanField(default=True) bool Yes or no DateField(), DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True) date, datetime Dates. auto_now_add fills in the time of creation ForeignKey(Author, on_delete=models.CASCADE) author_id plus an index Many books for one author EmailField(), URLField(), SlugField() varchar Text with a check in forms What the example shows Each model class becomes a table named app label plus model name, here shop_book. Django adds an id column as the primary key. Book.objects is the manager. create() inserts a row, all() and filter() give a QuerySet, get() returns exactly one object. Lookups such as year__gte=1900 or author__name__startswith=\"Frank\" become WHERE clauses. The double underscore can even follow a ForeignKey into the other table. related_name=\"books\" gives the other direction: herbert.books.all(). update() and delete() on a QuerySet change many rows with one SQL statement. str(queryset.query) shows the SQL Django wrote. In the exam In a project you never call schema_editor yourself. You change models.py, run makemigrations to write a migration file, and migrate to run it. Chapter 4.2 goes much deeper into the ORM and migrations. Lesson 6 of 10"],[46,"admin","The admin site","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 admin_site.py. ModelAdmin option What it changes list_display = [\"title\", \"year\"] The columns of the list page list_filter = [\"in_stock\"] A filter box at the side search_fields = [\"title\"] A search box. ?q=dune becomes WHERE title LIKE %dune% ordering = [\"-year\"] The order of the list list_per_page = 50 How many rows on each page readonly_fields, fields, fieldsets What the edit form shows and how inlines = [ReviewInline] Edit related rows, such as reviews, on the same page Real world scenario: a small shop with an office team A bookshop keeps its stock in Django. The developers write the models and three lines of admin.py for each. The office team logs in at /admin/, searches books, fixes prices and adds new titles. Each person gets only the permissions they need, for example \"can change book\" but not \"can delete book\", and the admin keeps a history of every change. Remember The admin is for trusted staff, not for customers. Use a long random password for the superuser, keep DEBUG off in production, and consider a less obvious address than /admin/. Lesson 7 of 10"],[46,"maths","The maths: URL matching, pages, queries and hashing","URL matching checks for a URL ≤ number of patterns Django tries patterns from the top: with 40 patterns the worst case is 40 checks, still well under a millisecond. Number of pagesExam favourite pages = ⌈ n / per page ⌉ 1,234 books at 50 a page: ⌈24.68⌉ = 25 pages. OFFSET of a pageExam favourite OFFSET = (page − 1) × per page Page 3 at 50 a page: LIMIT 50 OFFSET 100. The N + 1 problemExam favourite queries = 1 + n A list of 50 books that reads book.author in the template: 1 + 50 = 51 queries. select_related makes it 1. Hashing cost guesses/s = hashes per second / iterations Django 6.1 uses PBKDF2 with 1,500,000 iterations: 109 hashes per second give about 667 guesses per second. Workers needed (Little's law) busy workers = requests per second × seconds per request 200 requests per second at 0.05 s each keep 10 workers busy. Worked example: why the list page is slow A page shows 50 books with their authors. The view uses Book.objects.all() and the template shows book.author.name. The first query reads the 50 books. Each book.author then runs one more query, 50 in total. At 2 ms a query that is 51 × 2 = 102 ms only for the database. Book.objects.select_related(\"author\") joins the two tables, so one query reads everything: about 3 ms. 51 queries → 1 query, about 34 times faster Lesson 8 of 10"],[46,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths Make a project and an app, write views and URL patterns, and show data with templates. Write models, run migrations, use QuerySets with lookups, and register the models in the admin. Use template inheritance, class based views, include() for each app's URLs, and keep settings safe for production. Know the whole request cycle: the handler, middleware order, URL resolving, the ORM's SQL, and how ASGI views and caching change it. Beginner Pages from URLs, views and templates. Intermediate Data from models and the admin. Expert A project you can deploy and grow. Going deeper: How Django starts django.setup() reads the settings, then fills the apps registry: it imports each app in INSTALLED_APPS, then each app's models module. Only after that can you use models, which is why the programs on this page import models after django.setup(). manage.py and wsgi.py do the same thing for you, using the DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE environment variable to find settings.py. Going deeper: Middleware is an onion Each middleware wraps the next one. A request passes them from the top of MIDDLEWARE to the bottom, and the response passes them from the bottom to the top. A middleware can also answer at once, as CommonMiddleware does with the 301 and CsrfViewMiddleware with the 403. That is why the order in settings.MIDDLEWARE matters: SessionMiddleware must come before AuthenticationMiddleware, which needs the session. Lesson 9 of 10"],[46,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","Problems that match the work of a Django project: paths in a tree of URL files, ranges for page links, joining two lists, writing operations like makemigrations, checking that tags close in order, unique upload names, a small ORM with ordered queries, scoped template variables and slugs made of words. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 257 Binary Tree Paths Easy Depth first search that carries the path so far Like listing every URL of a project whose urls.py files include each other. Walk down the tree, add each value to the path, and save the path at every leaf. 228 Summary Ranges Easy Walk once and close a range when the next number is not one more The admin and paginators show ranges such as 1-3. Keep where the current range starts and close it when the numbers stop following each other. 599 Minimum Index Sum of Two Lists Easy A dict from name to position Like joining two lists of favourites in the ORM. Store the positions of the first list in a dict, then look up each name of the second list once. 1441 Build an Array With Stack Operations Medium Write down the operations, like makemigrations does makemigrations writes a list of operations that turn the old state into the new one. Here: read the numbers 1, 2, 3 ... in order, Push each one, and Pop it again when it is not in target. 1003 Check If Word Is Valid After Substitutions Medium A stack, the same way template tags must close A Django template is only valid when every {% block %} has its {% endblock %} in the right order. Here \"abc\" plays that part: push letters and remove \"abc\" whenever it appears on top. 1487 Making File Names Unique Medium A dict with the next free number for each name When two users upload photo.jpg, Django storage saves the second one under a new name. Keep the next number to try for each name, so no name is checked again and again. 1048 Longest String Chain Medium Sort by length, then dynamic programming on a dict Like a migration history where every migration adds one field. Sort the words by length and, for each word, try removing one letter to find the best chain that ends just before it. 1912 Design Movie Rental System Hard Sorted lists play the part of ORDER BY with an index This is a small ORM: one table of copies, a query \"cheapest 5 shops for a movie\" and a report \"cheapest 5 rented copies\". Keep sorted lists of (price, shop) and (price, shop, movie) so each query only reads the first 5. 736 Parse Lisp Expression Hard A recursive parser with a stack of scopes Like the context of a Django template: {% with %} adds names that live only inside its block, and inner names hide outer ones. Parse the tokens recursively and pass a new dict of names into every let. 472 Concatenated Words Hard Word break with a set and memory Like checking that a URL slug such as \"redbookshop\" is made only of known words. For each word, test every cut point and remember which endings were already solved. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 10 of 10"],[46,"recap","Chapter summary","Django includes routing, views, templates, an ORM with migrations, users and an admin site. Its MTV pattern is MVC with other names. startproject makes the project, startapp makes each app, and manage.py runs every command. Add each app to INSTALLED_APPS. urlpatterns are tried from the top. Converters such as int: change the type, and names let reverse() and the url tag build links. render() fills a template with a context. Values are escaped, filters change them, and extends with blocks shares one layout. A model class is a table, makemigrations writes the changes and migrate runs them. QuerySets with lookups become SQL, and admin.register gives a full admin page. Next: Next: the Django ORM in depth, forms, users and tests. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: write the views and URLs of a book shop, write three Django template filters yourself, and build a tiny QuerySet with lookups and ordering.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[47,"","Django in depth: ORM, migrations, forms, users and tests","Q, F, aggregate, annotate, the N + 1 problem, migrations, forms, users and permissions, transactions and TestCase. · Django ORM · QuerySet · Q · F · annotate · select_related · migrations · ModelForm · permissions · TestCase Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Web and APIs›Step 4: Django: the framework with everything built in›Django ORM Step 4 · Topic 2 of 2 · Intermediate Use the Django ORM well. Write rich queries with Q, F, aggregate and annotate, make them fast, change tables with migrations, check input with forms, protect pages with users and permissions, and test it all. About 120 min11 lessons1 animation6 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1The ORM: asking the database in Python 2QuerySets: filter, Q, F, aggregate and annotate 3Fast queries: the N + 1 problem 4Migrations: changing tables safely 5Forms: checking what users send 6Users, logins and permissions 7Transactions and tests 8The maths: query counts, indexes and transactions 9From beginner to expert 10LeetCode practice 11Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 11"],[47,"idea","The ORM: asking the database in Python","Source: Django 6.1 documentation: making queries, the QuerySet API, aggregation, query expressions, database access optimization, migrations, forms and ModelForms, validation, authentication and permissions, transactions and testing tools. What it is An ORM (object relational mapper) lets you read and change database rows as Python objects. In Django you write Book.objects.filter(year__gte=1900) and Django writes, sends and reads the SQL for you. Why we need it Writing SQL by hand for every page is slow, easy to get wrong and easy to make unsafe. Each database (SQLite, PostgreSQL, MySQL) also speaks a slightly different SQL. And the code that turns rows into objects is the same boring work every time. How it works A QuerySet collects your conditions step by step and runs one SQL query only when you read the results. Values always travel as parameters, so input cannot change the SQL. The same Python works on every supported database, and migrations keep the tables in step with the models. Inside the Django ORMPick what to follow. Each step moves one stage further and shows what Django knows at that point. Step 0 of 7 Show Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this Lazy: chaining runs no SQL until you read. N + 1: 21 queries, then 1 with select_related. Migrations: makemigrations writes, migrate runs. Forms: field checks, clean_isbn, clean. atomic(): an error rolls back every change. Three ways to talk to a database from Python Raw SQL (sqlite3) Django ORM SQLAlchemy You write SQL text with ? placeholders Book.objects.filter(...) select(Book).where(...) or the ORM Changes to tables ALTER TABLE by hand makemigrations and migrate Alembic Joins Written by hand author__name follows the ForeignKey join() or relationships Hard reports Full SQL power annotate, Subquery, Window, or raw() when needed Very close to SQL Used with Small scripts Django Flask, FastAPI, scripts Bottom line: Use the ORM for almost everything in Django. Look at the SQL it writes with str(queryset.query) or the query count in tests, and drop to raw SQL only for the rare report the ORM cannot express. Lesson 2 of 11"],[47,"queries","QuerySets: filter, Q, F, aggregate and annotate","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 orm_queries.py. Tool Example SQL it becomes filter and exclude filter(year__gte=1900).exclude(stock=0) WHERE year >= 1900 AND NOT stock = 0 Lookups title__icontains=\"dune\", year__in=[1965, 1969], author__country=\"UK\" LIKE, IN, a JOIN to author Q objects Q(a) | Q(b), ~Q(c) OR and NOT F expressions filter(price__gt=F(\"stock\") * 2), update(stock=F(\"stock\") + 10) Compare or change columns inside the database aggregate aggregate(Avg(\"price\")) One answer for the whole QuerySet: a dict annotate annotate(n=Count(\"books\")) One extra value per row, with GROUP BY values, values_list values(\"title\", \"year\"), values_list(\"title\", flat=True) Only these columns, as dicts or tuples Relations ForeignKey, ManyToManyField, OneToOneField author_id column, a join table, a unique author_id What the example shows Building the QuerySet ran no SQL. Reading it in the list comprehension ran exactly one query. Q objects combine conditions with | (OR) and ~ (NOT). distinct() removes the copies that a join through tags can make. F(\"stock\") means \"the value in this row\". update(stock=F(\"stock\") + 10) runs inside the database, so two users at the same time cannot overwrite each other. aggregate returned one dict. annotate gave every author an n, and filter(n__gte=2) after it became HAVING. The printed SQL shows the LEFT OUTER JOIN, GROUP BY and HAVING that Django wrote. Remember The QuerySet keeps the rows it read. Looping twice over the same QuerySet runs one query, but calling filter() again builds a new QuerySet and runs new SQL. Save a QuerySet in a variable when you need it twice. Lesson 3 of 11"],[47,"nplus1","Fast queries: the N + 1 problem","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 n_plus_one.py. select_related and prefetch_related select_related(\"author\") prefetch_related(\"tags\") Works for ForeignKey and OneToOne (one related row) ManyToMany and the reverse of a ForeignKey (many rows) How One query with a JOIN One extra query with WHERE id IN (...), joined in Python Queries for 20 books 1 2 Watch out Joining many tables makes wide rows Filtering inside needs a Prefetch object Bottom line: Look at every loop in a template that reads a related object. If it reads one row per item, use select_related. If it reads a list per item, use prefetch_related. More ways to make queries cheaper Why only(\"title\") or defer(\"description\") Read fewer columns values_list(\"title\", flat=True) No model objects at all, only the values exists() instead of len(qs) > 0 The database stops at the first row count() instead of len(qs) SELECT COUNT(*), not every row bulk_create, bulk_update Many rows in one query db_index=True or Meta.indexes An index makes filters and order_by on that column fast iterator() Streams a huge QuerySet without keeping it all in memory In the exam In tests, assertNumQueries(n) fails when a change adds queries, so an N + 1 problem cannot sneak back in. In development, django-debug-toolbar shows every query of a page. Lesson 4 of 11"],[47,"migrations","Migrations: changing tables safely","Run this one on your own computer. It makes a real project in a temporary folder and runs manage.py commands. Install Django first with pip install django. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 migrations_demo.py. Command What it does makemigrations Compares the models with the migration files and writes a new file with the changes makemigrations --empty --name fill_slugs An empty migration for your own code, such as a data migration migrate Runs every migration that is not applied yet, in dependency order migrate books 0001 Goes back: undoes every later migration of books sqlmigrate books 0002 Shows the SQL of a migration without running it showmigrations Lists the migrations with [X] for applied makemigrations --check Fails when a model change has no migration yet, useful in CI Rules that keep migrations safe Commit the migration files to git together with the model change. Never edit a migration that others have already run, write a new one instead. A new column on a table with rows needs a default or null=True, otherwise Django asks what to put into the old rows. Split big changes: add the new column, fill it with a data migration (RunPython), and only then remove the old column in a later release. Inside RunPython use apps.get_model(\"books\", \"Book\"), the model as it was at that migration, never an import of the current model. Give RunPython a reverse function (or RunPython.noop) so the migration can be undone. Remember SQLite cannot change most column types in place, so the SQL above makes a new table, copies the rows and renames it. PostgreSQL runs a short ALTER TABLE instead. Django picks the right way for each database. Lesson 5 of 11"],[47,"forms","Forms: checking what users send","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 forms_demo.py. Step What runs Where your code goes 1. Bind form = BookForm(request.POST) In the view 2. Field checks to_python, required, max_length, min_value, unique (ModelForm) Field options 3. One field clean_isbn() after the field checks pass def clean_<field>(self): return the value 4. Many fields clean() with all cleaned values def clean(self): use self.add_error 5. Result is_valid() is True: cleaned_data, or save() for a ModelForm Redirect after saving 6. Errors form.errors, shown next to each field in the template Render the form again Form and ModelForm forms.Form forms.ModelForm Fields come from You list them The model (Meta.model, Meta.fields) Saves to the database No, you do it form.save() makes or updates the row Model rules Not used max_length, unique and choices are checked for you Good for Search, contact, login Create and edit pages for a model Bottom line: Use a ModelForm whenever the form edits a model, so the rules live in one place. Always list the fields in Meta.fields, so a new model field such as is_admin is never editable by accident. In the exam The usual view: if request.method == \"POST\", bind and check the form, save and redirect when it is valid. Otherwise make an empty form. Either way render the template, which shows the errors. In the template put {% csrf_token %} inside the form tag. Lesson 6 of 11"],[47,"auth","Users, logins and permissions","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 auth_demo.py. Part What Django gives you User model username, email, password (a hash), is_active, is_staff, is_superuser, groups, user_permissions Passwords create_user and set_password store PBKDF2 with 1,500,000 iterations and a random salt, check_password compares Login authenticate(username=..., password=...) then login(request, user), or the ready LoginView In views @login_required, @permission_required, LoginRequiredMixin, PermissionRequiredMixin In templates user.is_authenticated, user.username, perms.books.change_book Permissions add, change, delete and view for every model, grouped with Group Your own user AUTH_USER_MODEL = \"accounts.User\", set before the first migrate Remember Start every new project with a custom user model (class User(AbstractUser): pass) and AUTH_USER_MODEL. Changing it later, when tables already point to auth.User, is a painful migration. In the exam login_required sends a person who is not logged in to LOGIN_URL with ?next=, as the 302 above shows. permission_required with raise_exception=True gives 403 instead, which is right for a logged in user who simply may not do this. Lesson 7 of 11"],[47,"testing","Transactions and tests","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 transactions_tests.py. Tool Use it for transaction.atomic() Changes that must all happen or none: a with block or a decorator F(\"balance\") + amount Change a value inside the database, safe when two requests run at the same time select_for_update() Lock rows until the transaction ends (PostgreSQL, MySQL) TestCase Every test runs in a transaction that is rolled back, so tests never see each other's data setUpTestData Rows made once for the whole class, which is fast self.client The test client inside a TestCase: get, post, login, force_login assertContains, assertRedirects, assertNumQueries Check a page, a redirect, or the number of queries python manage.py test Finds tests.py and test_*.py, makes a test database, runs everything In the exam Look at the query count: inside a test the atomic() of transfer becomes SAVEPOINT and RELEASE, because TestCase already opened a transaction around the test. Outside tests it is BEGIN and COMMIT. Lesson 8 of 11"],[47,"maths","The maths: query counts, indexes and transactions","Queries in a loopExam favourite N + 1 without help, 1 with select_related, 2 with prefetch_related 20 books: 21, 1 and 2 queries. Time of a pageExam favourite time ≈ queries × round trip + work 21 queries at 1.5 ms: 31.5 ms. 1 query: 1.5 ms plus a bigger row. Search without an index rows read = n 1,000,000 books: the database reads all of them. Search with a B-tree indexExam favourite steps ≈ logb(n) With about 100 keys per page: log100(1,000,000) = 3 page reads. Rows from a join table (many to many) pairs = books × average tags 20 books with 2 tags each: 40 rows in book_tags. Weighted average (LeetCode 1251) average = Σ(price × units) / Σunits (5 × 100 + 20 × 15) / 115 = 800 / 115 ≈ 6.96. Worked example: is the index worth it? A shop has 1,000,000 orders and the page filters by customer_id. Without an index the database scans every row: 1,000,000 rows. A B-tree index with about 100 keys per page needs log100(106) = 6 / 2 = 3 page reads to find the first match. The cost: every INSERT also updates the index, and the index takes disk space. For a column you filter on often, that is a good deal. 1,000,000 rows → about 3 page reads Lesson 9 of 11"],[47,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths Write filter, exclude, get and order_by queries, and add relations with ForeignKey and ManyToManyField. Use Q, F, aggregate and annotate, run makemigrations and migrate, and check input with forms and ModelForms. Find N + 1 problems and fix them, write data migrations, protect views with permissions and test with TestCase and assertNumQueries. Read the SQL Django writes, design indexes, use Subquery, Window and transactions with locking, and plan zero downtime migrations. Beginner Queries that are right. Intermediate Queries that are fast. Expert Changes that are safe. Going deeper: Subquery and OuterRef A Subquery puts one query inside another. OuterRef(\"id\") inside it means \"the id of the row in the outer query\". The LeetCode solutions use it to find the unique id of each employee and the price that was valid on each sale date. Django writes it as a correlated subquery in one SQL statement. Going deeper: Zero downtime migrations On a busy site the old code keeps running while the new one starts. So a migration must work with both: first add a nullable column and deploy code that writes it, then fill old rows, then make the column required, and only in a later release remove what the old code needed. This is called expand and contract. Lesson 10 of 11"],[47,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","SQL problems from the LeetCode database set, each solved with the Django ORM in place of SQL: computed columns, a LEFT JOIN with Subquery, following a ForeignKey, conditional aggregation with Case and When, Count with a filter, a weighted average, NOT IN with exclude, grouping by a computed month and the first row per group. Each solution makes its tables in an in-memory SQLite database, so leetcode.py runs on its own. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1683 Invalid Tweets Easy annotate(Length(...)) then filter A computed column: annotate each tweet with the length of its text, then keep the ones longer than 15. 1378 Replace Employee ID With The Unique Identifier Easy Subquery with OuterRef, a LEFT JOIN without a ForeignKey The two tables share an id but have no ForeignKey. A Subquery that looks up the unique id for each employee works like a LEFT JOIN, and gives None when there is no match. 1068 Product Sales Analysis I Easy Follow a ForeignKey inside values() With a ForeignKey, product__product_name in values() makes Django write the JOIN for you. 1661 Average Time of Process per Machine Easy Conditional aggregation with Sum(Case(When(...))) Group by machine. Add the end times and subtract the start times with Case and When, then divide by the number of processes. 1280 Students and Examinations Easy Count per group, then fill in the zeros Every student must appear with every subject, even with 0 exams. Count the exams per (student, subject) with values() and annotate(), then loop over all pairs. 1251 Average Selling Price Easy Subquery for the price on the sale date, then a weighted average Each sale needs the price that was valid on its date. A Subquery with OuterRef and two date lookups finds it, then Sum(price times units) / Sum(units) gives the weighted average. 1211 Queries Quality and Percentage Easy Avg of a computed value and Count with a filter Group by query name. Avg(rating / position) gives the quality, and Count with filter=Q(rating__lt=3) counts the poor results. 607 Sales Person Easy exclude with a subquery of ids Find the sales people with an order for the company RED, as a QuerySet of ids, and exclude them. Django puts the inner query into one SQL statement with NOT IN. 1193 Monthly Transactions I Medium Group by a computed month, Count and Sum with filters Annotate each row with its month (the first 7 characters of the date), group by month and country, and use filter=Q(state=\"approved\") inside Count and Sum. 1174 Immediate Food Delivery II Medium Subquery for the first order of each customer Find each customer's first order date with a Subquery that sorts by date and takes one row, keep only those first orders, then count how many were delivered on the day they were ordered. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 11 of 11"],[47,"recap","Chapter summary","A QuerySet is lazy: filter, exclude and order_by build one SQL query that runs when you read it. Q gives OR and NOT, F uses column values inside the database. aggregate gives one answer, annotate gives one per row with GROUP BY. select_related (a JOIN) and prefetch_related (one more query) fix the N + 1 problem. makemigrations writes the changes into a file for git, migrate runs them, and RunPython moves data. Old rows need a default. Forms turn text into checked Python values: field checks, clean_<field>, clean, then cleaned_data or save(). Always redirect after a save. Users store only password hashes, login_required and permissions protect views, atomic() keeps changes all or nothing, and TestCase rolls back after every test. Next: Next: APIs with Django REST framework and FastAPI. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: write a sign up form with Django forms, plan migrations like migrate does, and build atomic() with savepoints.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[48,"","Django REST framework: JSON APIs on top of Django","Serializers, ViewSets and routers, pagination and filters, authentication, permissions, throttles and API tests. · Django REST framework · serializer · ModelViewSet · router · pagination · TokenAuthentication · permissions · throttling · APITestCase · REST Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Web and APIs›Step 5: Building APIs: Django REST framework and FastAPI›Django REST framework Step 5 · Topic 1 of 2 · Advanced Build JSON APIs with Django REST framework. Turn models into JSON with serializers, get every endpoint from a ViewSet and a router, add pages, search, token logins, permissions and rate limits, and test it all. About 110 min10 lessons1 animation6 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1What Django REST framework adds 2Serializers and API views 3ViewSets, routers, filters and pages 4Authentication, permissions and throttling 5Nested data and API tests 6Designing a good API 7The maths: pages, limits and payload size 8From beginner to expert 9LeetCode practice 10Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 10"],[48,"idea","What Django REST framework adds","Source: Django REST framework 3.18 documentation: tutorial, serializers, views, generic views, viewsets and routers, authentication, permissions, throttling, filtering, pagination, status codes and testing. What it is Django REST framework (DRF) is a library on top of Django for building JSON APIs. It adds serializers that turn models into JSON and check incoming JSON, views and ViewSets made for APIs, routers that write the URLs, and ready parts for logins, permissions, rate limits, filters and pages. Why we need it A mobile app or a JavaScript front end does not want HTML pages, it wants JSON. With plain Django you would parse JSON by hand, check every field, turn objects into dicts, pick status codes and write the same list, create, read, update and delete views for every model. How it works A serializer does for JSON what a form does for HTML: it checks the input and gives clean data, and it turns objects into dicts. A ModelViewSet plus a router gives all the usual endpoints of a model in a few lines, and settings switch on token logins, permissions, throttles and pagination for every view. Inside Django REST frameworkPick a request. Each step moves it one stage further and shows what the API knows at that point. Step 0 of 7 Request Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this The full path: authentication, permissions, throttle, serializer. 401: nobody is logged in. 403: logged in, but not the owner. 429: the throttle stops the 4th request. The router: one ViewSet, six actions. Building a JSON API in Python Plain Django views Django REST framework FastAPI Input checks By hand or with forms Serializers Pydantic models from type hints CRUD endpoints Write each view ModelViewSet and a router Write each function Logins, permissions, limits Django sessions only Token, session, JWT (add on), permissions, throttles Dependencies you write or add Docs None Browsable API, OpenAPI with an add on OpenAPI and Swagger UI built in Good for One or two JSON endpoints APIs on top of a Django project and its models New API only services, async work Bottom line: Pick DRF when you already have Django models and the admin. Pick FastAPI (chapter 5.2) for a new, API only service. Lesson 2 of 10"],[48,"serializers","Serializers and API views","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 serializers_views.py. Serializer part What it does ModelSerializer with Meta.model and Meta.fields Makes the fields from the model, with its rules serializer.data Object to dict, ready to become JSON Serializer(data=...).is_valid() Checks incoming data. errors holds the messages, validated_data the clean values validate_<field>(self, value) A rule for one field validate(self, data) A rule for several fields. Errors go to non_field_errors SerializerMethodField A read only value from a get_<name> method read_only=True, write_only=True Shown but never written, or accepted but never shown (passwords) save() Calls create() or update(), which you can override What the example shows BookSerializer(book).data turned a model object into a dict, with the extra age field. Bad data gave one message per field, before anything touched the database. @api_view reads request.data from JSON, returns Response objects and answers 405 for other methods. is_valid(raise_exception=True) turned the validation error into a 400 answer by itself. The status codes follow REST: 200 read, 201 created, 204 deleted, 400 bad input, 404 missing, 405 wrong method. Remember Response(data) is not JSON yet. The renderer turns it into JSON for programs and into the browsable HTML page for a person with a browser. That page lets you try the API by hand. Lesson 3 of 10"],[48,"viewsets","ViewSets, routers, filters and pages","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 viewsets_routers.py. HTTP request ViewSet action Answer GET /api/books/ list 200, a page of books POST /api/books/ create 201, the new book GET /api/books/7/ retrieve 200 or 404 PUT /api/books/7/ update 200, every field replaced PATCH /api/books/7/ partial_update 200, only the fields sent DELETE /api/books/7/ destroy 204, empty body GET /api/books/oldest/ @action(detail=False) Your own extra endpoint Three levels of DRF views APIView or @api_view Generic views ViewSets You write Each method yourself queryset and serializer_class queryset and serializer_class Example def get(self, request) ListCreateAPIView ModelViewSet URLs path() for each path() for each One router.register Good for Special endpoints A few endpoints Full CRUD of a model Bottom line: Start with a ModelViewSet for each model. Override get_queryset, perform_create or one action when you need something special, and write an APIView only for endpoints that do not fit. In the exam SearchFilter and OrderingFilter come with DRF. For filters such as ?year=1965&author=2, the django-filter add on reads them from a filterset_fields list. Always order the queryset, otherwise pages can show a row twice. Lesson 4 of 10"],[48,"auth","Authentication, permissions and throttling","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 auth_permissions.py. Setting or class Job TokenAuthentication Reads \"Authorization: Token ...\" and finds the user SessionAuthentication Uses the Django login cookie, for a front end on the same site (needs CSRF) JWT (djangorestframework-simplejwt) Signed tokens that expire, no database lookup per request IsAuthenticated, IsAuthenticatedOrReadOnly, IsAdminUser Who may use the view has_object_permission Rules for one object, such as \"only the owner may change it\" AnonRateThrottle, UserRateThrottle Limits such as 100/hour per IP or per user, answered with 429 perform_create(serializer) Adds server side values such as owner=request.user 401 and 403 401 Unauthorized 403 Forbidden Means We do not know who you are We know who you are, and you may not Example No token, or a wrong token Bob deletes ann's note The client should Log in and try again Not try again Bottom line: DRF picks the right one: no credentials give 401, credentials without the right permission give 403. Remember Never trust ids or owners from the request body. The example ignores \"owner\": \"bob\" in the body and sets the owner from the token in perform_create. Mark such fields read only in the serializer. Lesson 5 of 10"],[48,"nested","Nested data and API tests","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 nested_tests.py. What the example shows A serializer can hold another serializer: each author shows its books inside it. DRF does not save nested data by itself. The create() method saves the author and the books inside transaction.atomic(), so a bad book saves nothing. prefetch_related(\"books\") makes the list two queries for any number of authors, and assertNumQueries(2) keeps it that way. An error in book number 0 comes back under its index: {\"books\": {\"0\": {\"title\": [...]}}}. APITestCase gives self.client, an APIClient, and rolls back the database after every test. Way to show a relation Gives Good for PrimaryKeyRelatedField (the default) \"author\": 2 Small answers, the client looks up more if needed StringRelatedField or source=\"author.name\" \"author\": \"Jane Austen\" Showing a name, read only A nested serializer \"author\": {\"id\": 2, \"name\": ...} Fewer requests for the client HyperlinkedRelatedField \"author\": \"http://.../authors/2/\" APIs that link resources together Lesson 6 of 10"],[48,"design","Designing a good API","Rule Good Avoid Nouns in URLs, HTTP methods as verbs POST /api/orders/ POST /api/createOrder Plural names, ids in the path /api/books/7/ /api/book?id=7 Filters, search and order in the query string /api/books/?author=2&ordering=-year A different URL for each filter Always paginate lists count, next, previous, results Returning 100,000 rows Use the right status code 201 with the new object, 204 after delete 200 with {\"error\": ...} Same error shape everywhere {\"field\": [\"message\"]} or {\"detail\": \"...\"} Plain text errors Version when you break things /api/v2/ or an Accept header Changing fields under existing clients Make retries safe PUT and DELETE are idempotent, POST can take an Idempotency-Key Charging a card twice on retry Real world scenario: a mobile app and a shop A shop has a Django site with the admin for staff. The new mobile app needs the same products and orders. The team adds DRF: a ModelViewSet for products (read only for everyone), one for orders (only the owner, token login), pagination of 20, and a throttle of 60 requests a minute per user. The admin and the web pages keep working on the same models, and the tests check every endpoint with APIClient. Lesson 7 of 10"],[48,"maths","The maths: pages, limits and payload size","Number of pagesExam favourite pages = ⌈ count / page size ⌉ 5 books at 2 a page: ⌈2.5⌉ = 3 pages. Requests allowedExam favourite rate = requests / window 3/minute: the 4th request in 60 s gets 429. Wait time in a sliding windowExam favourite Retry-After = oldest + window − now Requests at 0, 10 and 20 s, a 4th at 30 s: 0 + 60 − 30 = 30 s. Requests per day at a limit per day = rate × windows per day 100/hour: 100 × 24 = 2,400 a day. Payload size bytes ≈ rows × bytes per row 20 books of 120 bytes: 2,400 bytes. All 50,000: 6 MB. Requests to read everything requests = ⌈ count / page size ⌉ 50,000 rows at 100 a page: 500 requests. Worked example: choosing a page size A list has 50,000 books of about 120 bytes each in JSON. Without pages one answer is 50,000 × 120 = 6,000,000 bytes, about 6 MB, too much for a phone. At 20 per page an answer is 2,400 bytes, and the first screen appears at once. A client that really needs everything makes ⌈50,000 / 100⌉ = 500 requests at a page size of 100, so allow a larger page_size (with max_page_size) for such clients. small pages for people, larger pages with a limit for programs Lesson 8 of 10"],[48,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths Write a ModelSerializer and @api_view functions, and test them with APIClient. Use ModelViewSet with a router, add pagination, search and ordering, and validate input with validate methods. Add token or JWT logins, permissions per object, throttles, nested serializers and APITestCase suites with query counts. Design versioned APIs, write OpenAPI docs (drf-spectacular), cache answers, and keep queries fast under load. Beginner Endpoints that work. Intermediate Endpoints that are safe. Expert An API others can rely on. Going deeper: What happens inside a DRF view APIView.dispatch wraps the Django request in a DRF Request, then runs initial(): perform_authentication, check_permissions and check_throttles, in this order. Then it calls the handler for the method (get, post, or the ViewSet action). An exception anywhere goes to handle_exception, which turns APIException, Http404 and PermissionDenied into JSON answers. Finally the Response is rendered by the renderer the client asked for. Lesson 9 of 10"],[48,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","Problems that match the work of an API: searching words, validating counts, joining pages of results, answering \"what was true at time t\", range filters, splitting a path into known names, random choice for load balancing, remembering fetched id ranges, counting ids that keys may read, and applying one update lazily to every value. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1408 String Matching in an Array Easy Check each word against the others with in Like the SearchFilter of an API: a word is a match when it appears inside another word. Two loops and the in operator are enough for small lists. 1207 Unique Number of Occurrences Easy Counter, then compare the counts with a set A validation rule in a serializer often counts things. Count each value with Counter, then the counts are unique when a set of them has the same size. 1640 Check Array Formation Through Concatenation Easy A dict from first number to piece Like joining pages of API results back into one list. Each piece starts with a different number, so a dict from first number to piece finds the next piece at once. 911 Online Election Medium Precompute the leader after each vote, then binary search the time Like an API that answers \"what was true at time t\". Work out the leader after every vote once, then each question is a binary search on the times. 2080 Range Frequency Queries Medium Positions per value, then two binary searches Like an API filter ?value=4&from=1&to=2. Keep the sorted positions of each value, then count the positions inside the range with bisect. 2707 Extra Characters in a String Medium Dynamic programming from the end of the string Like splitting a URL into known resource names. best[i] is the fewest extra letters from position i. Either skip the letter at i, or jump over a dictionary word that starts at i. 381 Insert Delete GetRandom O(1) - Duplicates allowed Hard A list plus a dict of index sets, swap with the last item to delete Like a load balancer that picks a random server where some servers count twice. The list gives random choice in O(1), the dict of positions finds an item to remove, and swapping with the last item keeps removal O(1). 715 Range Module Hard A sorted list of disjoint intervals Like a cache that remembers which ranges of ids it already fetched from an API. Keep the ranges sorted and merged, and use bisect to find the ones that touch a new range. 2276 Count Integers in Intervals Hard Merge intervals as they arrive and keep a running total Like counting how many ids a set of API keys may read. Keep disjoint intervals sorted by start, merge every new one with the ones it touches, and update the total on the way. 1622 Fancy Sequence Hard Store one lazy formula a × x + b for every value Like update(price=F(\"price\") * 2 + 1) for every row at once: never touch each value. Keep one global formula and store each new value \"undone\" with the modular inverse, so the formula applies to it correctly later. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 10 of 10"],[48,"recap","Chapter summary","DRF adds serializers, API views, ViewSets, routers, authentication, permissions, throttles, filters and pagination on top of Django. A serializer turns objects into dicts and checks incoming data: field checks, validate_<field>, validate, then save(). A ModelViewSet and router.register give list, create, retrieve, update, partial_update and destroy. get_queryset and perform_create change the details. Authentication finds the user, permissions decide (401 or 403), throttles limit the rate (429). Set the owner on the server, never from the body. Nested serializers need your own create(). Test every endpoint with APITestCase and keep the query count fixed. Next: Next: FastAPI, the type hint based API framework. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: write a serializer for shop orders, write page number and cursor pagination, and build a sliding window rate limiter.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[49,"","FastAPI: fast, typed APIs with automatic docs","Pydantic models, typed parameters, response_model, Depends, async def and the event loop, docs and pytest. · FastAPI · Pydantic · type hints · Depends · response_model · async · event loop · OpenAPI · TestClient · Uvicorn Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Web and APIs›Step 5: Building APIs: Django REST framework and FastAPI›FastAPI Step 5 · Topic 2 of 2 · Advanced Build APIs with FastAPI. Let type hints and Pydantic check every request, get docs at /docs for free, share code with dependencies, use async def the right way and test it all with pytest. About 110 min10 lessons1 animation6 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1What FastAPI is and why type hints matter 2Pydantic: data that checks itself 3Your first API: parameters, bodies and errors 4Dependencies 5async def, background tasks and start up 6Testing a FastAPI app with pytest 7The maths: concurrency, workers and timeouts 8From beginner to expert 9LeetCode practice 10Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 10"],[49,"idea","What FastAPI is and why type hints matter","Source: FastAPI 0.162 documentation (tutorial, dependencies, security, background tasks, testing, async, lifespan, deployment) and Pydantic 2.14 documentation (models, fields, validators, JSON Schema). What it is FastAPI is a Python framework for building APIs. You write normal functions with type hints, such as book_id: int or book: BookIn, and FastAPI uses them to read the request, check it with Pydantic, write the JSON answer and produce live API docs. Why we need it In most frameworks the input rules live in one place (a serializer or form), the function signature in another and the docs in a third. They drift apart, and every endpoint repeats the same checking code. Many APIs also spend most of their time waiting for databases and other services. How it works The type hints are the single source: they check the input (422 when wrong), convert text to numbers and dates, filter the output with response_model and feed the OpenAPI docs at /docs. Depends gives shared parts such as the database and the user. async def endpoints let one process serve many waiting requests at once. Inside FastAPIPick what to follow. Each step moves one stage further and shows what FastAPI knows at that point. Step 0 of 7 Show Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this A request: Depends, Pydantic, response_model. 422: every error listed, the function never runs. The event loop: 0.2 s, 0.2 s, then 2.0 s. OpenAPI, Swagger UI and ReDoc for free. FastAPI, Flask and Django REST framework FastAPI Flask Django REST framework Input checks Pydantic from type hints By hand or with an extension Serializers Docs OpenAPI, Swagger UI and ReDoc built in Extensions Browsable API, OpenAPI with an add on Async Built on ASGI, async def everywhere Mainly sync (WSGI) Mainly sync, async views possible Database and admin Your choice (SQLAlchemy, SQLModel) Your choice Django ORM and admin Good for New API services, many waiting calls Small apps and services APIs next to a Django site Bottom line: Choose FastAPI for a new JSON API, especially one that calls other services. Choose DRF when the data already lives in Django. Lesson 2 of 10"],[49,"pydantic","Pydantic: data that checks itself","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 pydantic_models.py. Tool Example Effect Type hint quantity: int \"3\" becomes 3, \"abc\" is an error Field limits Field(gt=0, le=10), Field(min_length=2), Field(pattern=...) Ranges, lengths, patterns Literal size: Literal[\"S\", \"M\", \"L\"] = \"M\" Only these values, with a default Nested model address: Address A dict is checked as an Address field_validator @field_validator(\"customer\") Your own rule or clean up for one field model_validator @model_validator(mode=\"after\") A rule across several fields Output model_dump(), model_dump_json() A dict or JSON text Schema model_json_schema() The JSON Schema that FastAPI puts in the docs Remember Pydantic converts when it is safe: \"3\" becomes 3, but \"3.5\" for an int is an error, and so is the 13th month. Every problem is listed with its location, such as address.zip_code, which is exactly what FastAPI sends back in a 422 answer. Lesson 3 of 10"],[49,"first","Your first API: parameters, bodies and errors","Run this one on your own computer. FastAPI's test client runs the app in a helper thread, and threads do not work in the browser's Python. Install everything with pip install fastapi httpx. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 first_api.py. Where the value comes from How you write it Example Path A name in {} in the route and a parameter @app.get(\"/books/{book_id}\") def get_book(book_id: int) Query string A simple parameter that is not in the path min_year: int = 0, limit: int = Query(10, le=100) JSON body A parameter with a Pydantic model type def add_book(book: BookIn) Header or cookie Header() or Cookie() authorization: Annotated[str, Header()] Answer shape response_model and status_code @app.post(..., response_model=BookOut, status_code=201) Errors raise HTTPException HTTPException(404, \"Book not found\") What the example shows book_id: int made /books/abc a 422 with the location path.book_id, before the function ran. The body model BookIn listed three problems at once: an empty title, a year after 2026 and a missing price. limit: int = Query(10, ge=1, le=100) refused limit=500. HTTPException(404) gave {\"detail\": \"Book not found\"}, and status_code=204 gave an empty answer to DELETE. /openapi.json, /docs and /redoc existed without one extra line of code. In the exam Run the real server with fastapi dev main.py while you develop (it reloads on every save) and fastapi run main.py or uvicorn main:app --workers 4 in production. Then open http://127.0.0.1:8000/docs. Lesson 4 of 10"],[49,"depends","Dependencies","Run this one on your own computer. FastAPI's test client runs the app in a helper thread, and threads do not work in the browser's Python. Install everything with pip install fastapi httpx. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 dependencies.py. Use of Depends Example A database session per request, closed after def get_db(): db = ... yield db ... close The current user from a token def current_user(authorization: str = Header()) ... Shared query parameters def paging(skip: int = 0, limit: int = 20) A permission check def admin_only(user = Depends(current_user)): if not user.is_admin: raise HTTPException(403) Settings loaded once def get_settings(): return Settings() with lru_cache Fakes in tests app.dependency_overrides[get_db] = fake_db What the example shows my_notes only names what it needs: db, user and page. FastAPI found and called the three dependency functions. get_db uses yield: the code after yield ran after every answer, even the 401. get_db ran before the token check, because it is the first parameter. Put cheap checks first when the order matters. Annotated[str, Depends(current_user)] gives a short name (User) that many endpoints can reuse. dependency_overrides swapped the login for a fake user without touching the endpoint. Remember For real logins FastAPI has OAuth2PasswordBearer, which reads \"Authorization: Bearer ...\" and also adds an Authorize button to /docs. The token is usually a JWT signed with a secret key (chapter 6.1). Lesson 5 of 10"],[49,"async","async def, background tasks and start up","Run this one on your own computer. It uses asyncio.run and a thread pool, which do not work in the browser's Python. Install everything with pip install fastapi httpx. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 async_background.py. Three ways to write a slow endpoint async def with await def (plain) async def with a blocking call Example await asyncio.sleep(0.2), await client.get(...) time.sleep(0.2), requests.get(...) time.sleep(0.2) inside async def Runs on The event loop A thread pool The event loop, which it blocks 10 requests at once 0.2 s 0.2 s 2.0 s Verdict Best with async libraries Fine with normal libraries Never do this Bottom line: Write async def only when everything slow inside it uses await. When you use a normal library such as requests or a sync database driver, write a plain def. Tool Use it for BackgroundTasks Small work after the answer, such as an email A task queue (Celery, RQ, arq) Heavy or important work that must survive a restart lifespan Open pools and load models once at start up, close them at shut down httpx.AsyncClient with ASGITransport Calling the app directly from async tests WebSocket routes (@app.websocket) Two way messages such as chat or live prices Lesson 6 of 10"],[49,"testing","Testing a FastAPI app with pytest","Run this one on your own computer. It writes two files and runs the real pytest. Install everything with pip install fastapi httpx pytest. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 pytest_api.py. The pattern Keep the app in main.py and the tests in test_main.py. A fixture makes TestClient(app) and swaps the real store or database with dependency_overrides, so each test starts empty. Each test sends a request and checks the status code and the JSON with plain assert. pytest.mark.parametrize runs one test with many bodies, here three bad ones that must all give 422. Clear dependency_overrides after the test so it never leaks into the next one. Real world scenario: a team that ships an API every day A team runs a price API. Every pull request runs pytest in CI: about 300 tests with TestClient finish in a few seconds because no server or network is involved. The OpenAPI JSON is saved as a CI artifact, and the mobile team generates its client code from it, so a renamed field breaks the build at once instead of the app in production. Lesson 7 of 10"],[49,"maths","The maths: concurrency, workers and timeouts","Time with awaitExam favourite total ≈ the longest single wait 10 requests that each wait 0.2 s: about 0.2 s. Time with a blocked loopExam favourite total ≈ n × wait 10 × 0.2 = 2.0 s. Requests one process can hold (Little's law)Exam favourite in flight = rate × time per request 500 requests a second at 0.2 s each: 100 in flight at once. Thread pool limit requests per second ≤ threads / time per request 40 threads at 0.2 s: at most 200 plain def requests a second per process. Workers workers ≈ 2 × CPU cores + 1 (a common start for sync servers) 4 cores: 9 workers. Async servers often use one worker per core. Timeout budget total ≤ sum of the calls in a row Three calls of 0.3 s in a row: 0.9 s. In parallel with gather: about 0.3 s. Worked example: one async worker or many threads? An endpoint calls a slow partner API that answers in 0.2 s, and the traffic is 500 requests a second. By Little's law 500 × 0.2 = 100 requests wait at the same time. A plain def endpoint has 40 threads per process, so one process manages at most 40 / 0.2 = 200 requests a second, and 3 processes are needed. An async def endpoint with httpx.AsyncClient keeps all 100 waiting requests on one event loop, so one process is enough, if the CPU work per request is small. waiting is cheap with await, and CPU work still needs more processes Lesson 8 of 10"],[49,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths Write endpoints with path and query parameters, Pydantic body models, response_model and HTTPException. Share code with Depends, close resources with yield, test with TestClient and dependency_overrides. Use async def correctly, background tasks, lifespan, Bearer token logins and a real database with SQLAlchemy. Design versioned APIs from the OpenAPI schema, measure the event loop under load, and deploy with several workers behind a proxy. Beginner Typed endpoints. Intermediate Shared, testable parts. Expert Fast under real load. Going deeper: ASGI, Starlette and Uvicorn FastAPI is built on Starlette, a small ASGI toolkit that does the routing, requests, responses and WebSockets. ASGI is the async successor of WSGI: the server calls an async function with the request and awaits messages. Uvicorn is the ASGI server that speaks HTTP. FastAPI adds the type hint magic, Pydantic, dependencies and the docs on top. Lesson 9 of 10"],[49,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","Problems that match the work of an API service: ordering fields, close values, a quick check in sorted data, splitting a total, calling a hidden function as few times as possible, a state machine that wraps around, an order book with two heaps, a stream checked letter by letter, the cheapest path between services and a seat booking system with a segment tree. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1122 Relative Sort Array Easy Sort with a key: (position in the order, value) Like an API that lists fields in a fixed order and the rest A to Z after them. A dict gives each wanted value its position, and the sort key puts the others at the end. 1200 Minimum Absolute Difference Easy Sort, then only neighbours matter After sorting, the closest pair is always next to each other, so one pass finds the smallest gap and the pairs that have it. 1287 Element Appearing More Than 25% In Sorted Array Easy Compare with the item a quarter further on In a sorted list a value that fills more than a quarter must still be there a quarter of the length later. One loop, no counting. 1013 Partition Array Into Three Parts With Equal Sum Easy Walk once and count parts that reach a third The total must split into three equal parts. Add numbers from the left and count each time the running sum reaches one third, then start again. 1237 Find Positive Integer Solution for a Given Equation Medium Two pointers on a function you can only call You can only call the hidden function f(x, y), like a remote API that you want to call as few times as possible. f grows with x and with y, so start at x = 1 and y = 1000 and move one of them each call. 2069 Walking Robot Simulation II Medium Moves wrap around the border, so use the remainder The robot only walks around the edge of the grid. One full lap is a fixed number of steps, so a huge step count shrinks to steps % lap, like a cursor that wraps around. 1801 Number of Orders in the Backlog Medium Two heaps: highest buy and lowest sell An order book API: a buy matches the cheapest sell, a sell matches the highest buy. A min heap of sells and a max heap of buys (negated prices) give the best match at once. 1032 Stream of Characters Hard A trie of reversed words, checked from the newest letter Like a WebSocket that gets one letter at a time and must say at once if the latest letters end a banned word. Store the words backwards in a trie and walk it from the newest letter back. 2642 Design Graph With Shortest Path Calculator Hard Dijkstra with a heap on every query Like a gateway that finds the cheapest chain of services. Keep the edges in a dict of lists, let addEdge append, and run Dijkstra with a heap for each shortestPath question. 2286 Booking Concert Tickets in Groups Hard A segment tree with the max and the sum of free seats per range A booking API must answer \"first row with k free seats together\" and \"book k scattered seats\" fast. A segment tree keeps the most free seats in a row and the total free seats for every range of rows. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 10 of 10"],[49,"recap","Chapter summary","FastAPI reads type hints: path, query, body and header values are converted and checked by Pydantic, and bad input gets 422 with locations. Pydantic models use Field limits, Literal, nested models and validators, and give model_dump and a JSON Schema for the docs. response_model shapes the answer, HTTPException sends errors, and /openapi.json, /docs and /redoc come for free. Depends shares the database, the user and paging. yield closes resources, and dependency_overrides swaps them in tests. Use async def only with awaitable libraries, plain def otherwise. A blocking call in async def stalls every request. Next: Next: API security, then the web revision exam. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: write a Pydantic model for products, build dependency injection like Depends, and build a router that reads type hints.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[50,"","API security: passwords, JWT, OAuth 2.0, CORS and OWASP","Password hashing, JWTs by hand and with PyJWT, OAuth 2.0 scopes in FastAPI, CORS and CSRF, and the OWASP API Top 10. · API security · JWT · PyJWT · OAuth 2.0 · password hashing · PBKDF2 · CORS · CSRF · OWASP · rate limiting Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Web and APIs›Step 6: Securing APIs and the web revision exam›API security Step 6 · Topic 1 of 2 · Advanced Secure a Python API. Store passwords with slow salted hashes, build and check JWTs by hand and with PyJWT, protect FastAPI endpoints with OAuth 2.0 scopes and owner checks, set CORS and security headers, and close the holes on the OWASP API Top 10. About 120 min12 lessons1 animation6 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1What API security is and why every endpoint needs it 2Storing passwords and secrets 3JSON Web Tokens from scratch 4Real tokens with PyJWT: expiry, refresh and key rotation 5OAuth 2.0 logins, scopes and owner checks in FastAPI 6CORS, CSRF and security headers 7The OWASP API Security Top 10 8Rate limits, HTTPS, logging and secrets in production 9The maths: guessing, entropy, hashing cost and rate limits 10From beginner to expert 11LeetCode practice 12Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 12"],[50,"idea","What API security is and why every endpoint needs it","Source: RFC 7519 (JWT), RFC 6749 (OAuth 2.0), RFC 7636 (PKCE), RFC 6750 (Bearer tokens), the WHATWG Fetch standard (CORS), the OWASP API Security Top 10 2023, the OWASP Password Storage and Secrets Management cheat sheets, the FastAPI security tutorial, PyJWT 2.15 and the Django 6.1 password hashers. What it is API security is the set of checks that decide who is calling (authentication), what they may do (authorization), and how much they may ask for. It also covers how secrets such as passwords and keys are stored, and what a browser is allowed to share between sites. Why we need it An API is open to the whole internet. Anyone can send any request with curl, change an id in the URL, add a field to the JSON or send a million requests. Most real breaches of APIs are not clever cryptography attacks. They are a missing check on one endpoint. How it works Passwords are stored as slow salted hashes. A login gives a signed token (often a JWT) that each request carries in the Authorization header. Every endpoint checks the token, the scope and the owner of the object. CORS tells browsers which other sites may read the answers. Rate limits, input models and secret keys outside the code close the rest. How API security checks workPick a story. Each step moves one stage further and shows what the server knows at that point. Step 0 of 7 Show Nothing is shown yet. Press Step to see the first step, or Play to watch every step. Each step adds one new piece and explains it here. Try this Sign, send, sign again, compare. role: admin without the secret fails. Preflight, allowed origin, blocked origin. A valid token is not enough. Salt, slow hash, constant time compare. Four ways to know who is calling Session cookie JWT bearer token API key OAuth 2.0 What the client holds A random session id in a cookie A signed token with claims A long random string An access token from an authorization server What the server keeps A session table Only the secret key A table of hashed keys Depends on the token type Log out at once Easy: delete the session Hard: wait for exp or keep a deny list Delete the key Revoke at the authorization server Watch out for CSRF, so use SameSite and CSRF tokens Long lifetimes, alg confusion, storing in localStorage Keys leaked in code or logs Using the wrong flow Good for Websites with server pages (Django, Flask) APIs and mobile apps Server to server calls Letting another app act for a user Bottom line: These are not rivals. A typical system uses OAuth 2.0 to log in, gets a JWT as the access token, and uses API keys for partner servers. Lesson 2 of 12"],[50,"passwords","Storing passwords and secrets","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 passwords.py. Way to store a password Verdict Why Plain text Never One leak shows every password, and people reuse them on other sites Encrypted Never Whoever has the key can read them all sha256(password) Never Billions of guesses a second on a graphics card, and the same password gives the same hash sha256(salt + password) Weak The salt stops shared tables, but the hash is still fast PBKDF2, bcrypt, scrypt, Argon2id Yes Salted and slow on purpose. Argon2id and scrypt also need memory What the example shows The two sha256 lines for sunshine are identical, so one cracked hash cracks every user with that password. With a random salt the same password gave two different PBKDF2 strings. The stored string keeps the algorithm, rounds and salt, exactly in the format Django uses (Django's own hasher produced the same string in a check). 600,000 rounds took about 120 ms. The OWASP Password Storage cheat sheet asks for at least 600,000 rounds of PBKDF2-SHA256, and Django 6.1 uses 1,500,000. hmac.compare_digest compares in constant time, so the response time leaks nothing. Tokens, keys and reset codes come from the secrets module. The random module is for games and simulations only. In the exam Keep secret keys out of the code: read them with os.environ[\"SECRET_KEY\"], keep a local .env file in .gitignore, and use a vault (AWS Secrets Manager, HashiCorp Vault) in production. A key that was ever pushed to git is burnt: rotate it, because deleting the commit does not remove copies. Lesson 3 of 12"],[50,"jwt","JSON Web Tokens from scratch","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 jwt_by_hand.py. Part What it holds Example Header The algorithm and the type {\"alg\": \"HS256\", \"typ\": \"JWT\"} Payload (claims) Who, what and until when sub (user), exp (expiry), iat (issued), nbf (not before), aud (audience), iss (issuer), jti (id), scope Signature HMAC-SHA256 of header.payload with the secret key base64url of 32 bytes Encoding base64url without = padding, parts joined with dots eyJhbGci... . eyJzdWIi... . Q46Cf6DE... What the example shows A JWT is three base64url parts. The first two are plain JSON that anyone can decode, so a JWT hides nothing. It only proves who made it. Changing role to admin without the secret made the signature check fail. So did a token signed with another key. A token past its exp was refused even though the signature was fine. The verify function decides the algorithm itself. A token that says \"alg\": \"none\" is refused instead of trusted. PyJWT, the library used next, made the exact same token from the same claims and key, byte for byte. Going deeper: HS256 or RS256 HS256 uses one shared secret to sign and to check, so every service that checks tokens could also make them. RS256 and ES256 sign with a private key and check with a public key, which an auth server publishes as a JWKS (a JSON list of public keys, chosen by the kid in the header). Use them when many services must check tokens but only one may issue them. Lesson 4 of 12"],[50,"tokens","Real tokens with PyJWT: expiry, refresh and key rotation","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 pyjwt_tokens.py. Practice Why Pin algorithms=[\"HS256\"] when decoding Stops alg confusion and alg none tricks Short access tokens (5 to 15 minutes) A stolen token is useful only for minutes Long refresh tokens that work once (rotation) A second use shows theft, so all sessions can be ended Check aud and iss A token for another API or from another issuer is refused kid in the header with a key table Keys can be swapped without logging everyone out jti and a deny list for log out A JWT cannot be deleted, so remember the ids of revoked ones until they expire Keep tokens out of URLs and logs URLs end up in browser history, proxies and log files Remember In a browser, an HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite cookie is the safest place for a token, because JavaScript (and so an XSS bug) cannot read it. localStorage is readable by any script on the page. Lesson 5 of 12"],[50,"oauth","OAuth 2.0 logins, scopes and owner checks in FastAPI","Run this one on your own computer. FastAPI's test client runs the app in a helper thread, and threads do not work in the browser's Python. Install everything with pip install fastapi httpx pyjwt python-multipart. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 oauth2_api.py. OAuth 2.0 flow Who uses it Notes Authorization code with PKCE Web, mobile and single page apps logging users in The standard choice today. PKCE stops a stolen code from being used Client credentials A server calling another server, with no user The client id and secret give a token Device code TVs and command line tools The user types a short code on another device Password (resource owner) Only your own first party app Legacy. The FastAPI tutorial uses it because it is simple Implicit Nobody Deprecated. Tokens in the URL leaked What the example shows A wrong password gave 401 with WWW-Authenticate: Bearer, the header RFC 6750 asks for. The message does not say whether the name or the password was wrong. The login hashes a password even for an unknown name, so the response time does not reveal which names exist. Ben asked for notes:write but only got the scopes he really has. Writing then gave 403, which means logged in but not allowed. Ben's valid token could not read Ann's note: the owner check answered 404. A valid token is not the same as permission for every object. OAuth2PasswordBearer also put the login and the scopes into the OpenAPI docs, so /docs shows an Authorize button. 401 or 403 401 Unauthorized 403 Forbidden Meaning We do not know who you are We know who you are, and the answer is no Typical cause No token, bad signature, expired Missing scope or role Client should Log in or refresh the token Not retry, ask for access Bottom line: For another user's object many APIs answer 404, so that the caller cannot tell which ids exist. Lesson 6 of 12"],[50,"cors","CORS, CSRF and security headers","Run this one on your own computer. FastAPI's test client runs the app in a helper thread, and threads do not work in the browser's Python. Install everything with pip install fastapi httpx. The output above comes from a real run with CPython when this page was built. Download this program (.py)Run it with python3 cors_headers.py. What the example shows For the listed origin the answer carried Access-Control-Allow-Origin with that exact origin, plus Vary: Origin so caches keep one copy per origin. For an unknown origin the header was missing, so a browser hides the answer. The server still ran the request, which is why CORS never replaces a login. The preflight OPTIONS listed the allowed methods and headers. Starlette also lists the safe headers that browsers always allow, such as Accept. A preflight for DELETE was refused with 400. A small middleware added HSTS (always use HTTPS), nosniff, a strict Content-Security-Policy and no referrer to every answer. CORS and CSRF CORS CSRF What it is Rules that let a server allow other sites to READ its answers in a browser An attack where another site makes your browser SEND a request with your cookies Who enforces it The browser, from the server's headers The server, with tokens and cookie settings When it matters Any API called from a page on another origin When the login is a cookie the browser adds by itself The fix or setting An exact list of origins, never * with credentials SameSite=Lax or Strict cookies plus a CSRF token (Django has it built in) Bearer tokens Still needed Not a risk: the browser never adds the Authorization header by itself Bottom line: CORS loosens the same origin policy on purpose. CSRF protection closes a hole that cookies open. Lesson 7 of 12"],[50,"owasp","The OWASP API Security Top 10","Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 owasp_demo.py. OWASP API 2023 What goes wrong Python fix API1 Broken Object Level Authorization Change the id in the URL and read someone else's data Check the owner on every object (filter by user in the query) API2 Broken Authentication Weak passwords, no rate limit on login, tokens that never expire Slow hashes, login limits, short exp, pinned algorithms API3 Broken Object Property Level Authorization Mass assignment (is_admin from the body) or returning secret fields Input models with extra=\"forbid\", response_model for output API4 Unrestricted Resource Consumption Huge pages, huge uploads, no rate limit Cap limit, body size and requests per client API5 Broken Function Level Authorization A normal user can call an admin endpoint Role and scope checks as dependencies API6 Unrestricted Access to Sensitive Business Flows Bots buy every ticket or create many accounts Limits per account, device and payment, CAPTCHA API7 Server Side Request Forgery The API fetches a user URL that points inside the network https only, block internal addresses, an allowlist API8 Security Misconfiguration Debug pages, open CORS, missing headers, default keys DEBUG off, exact CORS list, security headers API9 Improper Inventory Management An old /v1 or a test server nobody patches A list of every API version and host, retire old ones API10 Unsafe Consumption of APIs Trusting data from a partner API without checks Validate it with models, use timeouts, follow no redirects blindly What the example shows The f-string query turned the input into SQL and returned every user. The ? placeholder sent it as a value and returned nothing. Injection is on the main OWASP Top 10 list, and the fix is the same in every database driver and ORM. A model with extra=\"forbid\" refused is_admin instead of saving it. The URL check refused http, 127.0.0.1, the cloud metadata address 169.254.169.254 and a private 10.x address. A client that asked for a million rows got at most 100. Real world scenario: a bug bounty report A tester signs up for a delivery app and opens their own order: GET /api/orders/81234. They change the number to 81233 and see a stranger's name, address and phone. Every endpoint checked that the token was valid, and none checked who owned the order. The fix was to write Order.objects.get(id=order_id, customer=request.user), and a test now calls every id endpoint with a second user and expects 404. Lesson 8 of 12"],[50,"ops","Rate limits, HTTPS, logging and secrets in production","Control How in Python Notes HTTPS everywhere TLS at the proxy (nginx, a cloud load balancer), HSTS header Tokens and passwords travel in every request Rate limits A token bucket per client, often in Redis, or slowapi for FastAPI and DRF throttles Send 429 Too Many Requests with Retry-After Login limits Count failures per account and per IP, slow down or lock for a while Stops password guessing Secrets os.environ, a vault, never in git, rotate on a schedule Scan commits with a secret scanner in CI Logging Log user id, path, status and time, never passwords or tokens Mask Authorization headers in error reports Dependencies pip-audit or the GitHub dependency alerts Most vulnerabilities come from old libraries Least privilege A database user that can only do what the app needs A leak then exposes less A security checklist before an API goes live Every endpoint has an authentication dependency, and public ones are marked public on purpose. Every endpoint that takes an id filters by the caller, and a test with a second user proves it. Input models forbid extra fields, response models hide secret fields, and page sizes are capped. Passwords use a slow salted hash, tokens are short lived and checked with pinned algorithms, aud and iss. CORS lists exact origins, security headers are set, DEBUG is off and keys come from the environment. Login and expensive endpoints have rate limits, and logs never contain tokens. Lesson 9 of 12"],[50,"maths","The maths: guessing, entropy, hashing cost and rate limits","Entropy of a random secretExam favourite bits = length × log2(alphabet size) 12 random lower case letters: 12 × log2 26 ≈ 56.4 bits. Guesses to try every valueExam favourite guesses = 2bits A 6 digit code: 106 = 1,000,000 values. Time to crackExam favourite time = guesses × cost per guess / machines With PBKDF2 at 0.12 s, one million guesses take 120,000 s on one core. Chance to guess with a rate limit P = attempts / values 5 tries on a 6 digit code: 5 / 1,000,000 = 0.0005 %. Token bucket allowed in t seconds ≤ capacity + rate × t Capacity 10, rate 2 per second: at most 10 + 2 × 60 = 130 requests in a minute. Birthday bound for random ids P(any repeat) ≈ n2 / 2bits + 1 1 billion random 128 bit ids: about 1018 / 2129, practically zero. Worked example: is a 6 digit login code safe? There are 106 = 1,000,000 codes, which is about 20 bits. Without a limit a script tries them all in minutes, because each guess costs only one HTTP request. With 5 attempts per code and a new code each time, the chance of a lucky guess is 5 / 1,000,000. The code also expires after 10 minutes, so an attacker cannot collect tries over days. short codes are only safe together with attempt limits and expiry Going deeper: Why HMAC and not sha256(secret + message) SHA-256 is built so that from sha256(secret + message) anyone can compute sha256(secret + message + more) without knowing the secret. This is the length extension attack. HMAC hashes twice with two derived keys, which closes it. That is why JWTs, webhooks and signed cookies all use HMAC. Lesson 10 of 12"],[50,"levels","From beginner to expert","One idea, four depths Never store passwords in plain text, keep secrets out of code, use HTTPS and know 401 from 403. Hash passwords with PBKDF2 or Argon2, issue and check JWTs with PyJWT, protect FastAPI endpoints with OAuth2PasswordBearer and scopes, and set CORS correctly. Add owner checks everywhere, refresh token rotation, key rotation with kid, rate limits and security headers, and test each with a second user. Run an OAuth 2.0 server with PKCE and RS256 keys published as JWKS, threat model each API against the OWASP list, and audit logs and dependencies in CI. Beginner Safe defaults. Intermediate Correct tokens and checks. Expert Defence in depth. Going deeper: OpenID Connect OAuth 2.0 only says what an app may do. OpenID Connect adds who the user is: the auth server also returns an ID token, a JWT with the user's id, name and e-mail, signed with RS256. \"Log in with Google\" is OpenID Connect. Libraries such as Authlib handle the flow in Flask, Django and FastAPI. Lesson 11 of 12"],[50,"leetcode","LeetCode practice","Problems that match the ideas of this chapter: substitution and shift ciphers, a transposition, the space of k bit codes, spotting replayed sequences, a value that differs from every known one, linking accounts that share an e-mail, a full strong password checker, a rolling hash and the KMP prefix table. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 2325 Decode the Message Easy Build a substitution table from first appearances This is a simple substitution cipher. The first time each letter appears in the key it stands for the next letter of the alphabet. A dict holds the table, and spaces stay as they are. Ciphers like this are easy to break, which is why real systems use keys and HMAC instead. 1844 Replace All Digits with Characters Easy Shift a letter by a digit Every digit says how far to move the letter before it, the same idea as a Caesar shift. chr(ord(letter) + digit) does the move. 1528 Shuffle String Easy Put each character at its target index A transposition: indices[i] says where s[i] belongs. Fill a list of the right length, then join it. 1461 Check If a String Contains All Binary Codes of Size K Medium A set of every window of length k There are 2k codes of length k, the same count an attacker must try to guess a k bit token. Put every window of the string in a set and compare its size with 2k. 187 Repeated DNA Sequences Medium Two sets: seen once and seen again Slide a window of 10 letters and remember each one in a set. A window that is already there is a repeat, like a replayed request id that a server must refuse. The answer is sorted here so the tests can compare it. 1980 Find Unique Binary String Medium Cantor diagonal: differ from string i at place i Take bit i of string i and flip it. The new string differs from every given string in at least one place, so it cannot be any of them. The same trick proves that some sets cannot be listed. 721 Accounts Merge Medium Union find over e-mail addresses Two accounts are one person when they share an e-mail, like linking identities in a login system. Union find joins every e-mail with the first e-mail of its account, then each group becomes one sorted account. The list is sorted at the end so the tests can compare it. 420 Strong Password Checker Hard Count runs, then spend deletions where they save a replacement A strong password has 6 to 20 characters, a lower case letter, an upper case letter and a digit, and never three equal characters in a row. Short passwords only need inserts. Long ones need deletes, and a delete in a run whose length is a multiple of 3 saves one replacement, so spend deletes on those runs first, then on runs with remainder 1 (two deletes) and then three at a time. 2156 Find Substring With Given Hash Value Hard Rolling hash, walking from the right The hash gives the first letter the power p0, so it is easy to add a letter on the LEFT: multiply by p and add the new value. Walk from the end of the string, drop the letter that falls out of the window, and remember the leftmost window that matches. Rolling hashes also power rsync and duplicate file checks. 1392 Longest Happy Prefix Hard The KMP failure table A happy prefix is also a suffix. The prefix function of KMP stores, for each position, the longest proper prefix that ends there, and its last value is the answer. It runs in O(n), with no hash collisions to worry about. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 12 of 12"],[50,"recap","Chapter summary","Store passwords with a slow salted hash (PBKDF2, bcrypt, scrypt or Argon2id), compare with hmac.compare_digest and make tokens with the secrets module. A JWT is header.payload.signature in base64url. Anyone can read it, only the key holder can sign it. Pin the algorithm and check exp, aud and iss. Use short access tokens, refresh tokens that work once, and a kid for key rotation. OAuth 2.0 with PKCE is the standard login flow. Authentication is not authorization: check scopes (403) and the owner of every object (404) on every endpoint. CORS lets listed origins read answers in a browser, CSRF needs SameSite cookies and tokens, and the OWASP API Top 10 lists the common holes. Next: Next: the web revision exam. .py Practice files for this chapterThree tasks: store passwords like Django, sign and check JWTs by hand, and write the CORS rules a server applies.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[51,"","Web revision exam: HTTP, Flask, Django, FastAPI and security","Four papers that run in the page, eight bugs, the web traps, a design answer and a mock quiz. · web revision · exam practice · mock quiz · predict output · find the bug · system design · Flask · Django · FastAPI · interview Brainy BytesPython learning path Python›Web and APIs›Step 6: Securing APIs and the web revision exam›Web revision exam Step 6 · Topic 2 of 2 · Advanced Revise the whole Web and APIs part: four predict the output papers that run in the page, a Flask service with eight bugs, the traps in one table, a system design answer, a mock quiz and timed problems. About 100 min11 lessons3 formulas10 worked problems13 quiz questions3 assignmentsThis chapter on the cheatsheet 1How to use this revision chapter 2Paper A: HTTP, URLs and JSON 3Paper B: Flask 4Paper C: Django and Django REST framework 5Paper D: Pydantic, tokens and security 6Find the bugs 7The traps in one table 8A system design answer: a link shortener API 9Exam technique and a study plan 10LeetCode review set 11Chapter summary Lesson 1 of 11"],[51,"map","How to use this revision chapter","Source: Original practice questions written for this course. Every answer was printed by a real run of Python 3.14 with Flask 3, Django 6.1, Django REST framework 3.18, Pydantic 2.14 and PyJWT 2.15. This chapter teaches nothing new. It revises the whole Web and APIs part in the style of a university web programming exam and a backend job interview: four predict the output papers, a find the bug listing, the traps in one table, a system design answer, a mock quiz in the Quiz tab and timed LeetCode problems. Python web frameworks have no single official certificate, so the questions follow what interviews and university courses test most often. Paper Covers Chapters A URLs, query strings, encoding, status codes, idempotency, cookies, JSON and raw HTTP 1.1 HTTP, 1.2 sockets, 2.1 requests, 2.2 API data B Flask routes, converters, 404 and 405, JSON bodies, query arguments, g, sessions, blueprints, url_for 3.1 Flask, 3.2 Flask apps C Django QuerySets, Q and F, annotate, JOINs, URLs, templates and autoescape, DRF serializers 4.1 Django, 4.2 ORM, 5.1 DRF D Pydantic conversion and errors, nested models, model_dump, PyJWT checks, hashes, secrets and IP checks 5.2 FastAPI, 6.1 API security Remember Cover the answers, write down what you think each line prints, then open the answers. Every wrong guess points to the chapter in brackets. All four papers run in this page, so press Run to see the real output too. Lesson 2 of 11"],[51,"paper_a","Paper A: HTTP, URLs and JSON","Paper A: what does each line printpython 1import json2from http import HTTPStatus3from http.cookies import SimpleCookie4from urllib.parse import parse_qs, quote, urlencode, urljoin, urlsplit56u = urlsplit(\"https://api.shop.example:8443/v2/books?q=python&page=2#top\")7print(\"Q1\", u.scheme, u.hostname, u.port, u.path, u.query, u.fragment)89print(\"Q2\", parse_qs(\"tag=a&tag=b&empty=\"), parse_qs(\"tag=a&tag=b&empty=\", keep_blank_values=True)[\"empty\"])1011print(\"Q3\", urlencode({\"q\": \"tea & cake\", \"page\": 1}), quote(\"/a b/\"), quote(\"/a b/\", safe=\"\"))1213print(\"Q4\", urljoin(\"https://x.example/api/v1/\", \"books\"), urljoin(\"https://x.example/api/v1\", \"books\"),14 urljoin(\"https://x.example/api/v1/\", \"/books\"))1516print(\"Q5\", [s.value for s in (HTTPStatus(201), HTTPStatus.NOT_FOUND)], HTTPStatus(429).phrase,17 [code // 100 for code in (204, 301, 422, 503)])1819SAFE = {\"GET\", \"HEAD\", \"OPTIONS\"}20IDEMPOTENT = SAFE | {\"PUT\", \"DELETE\"}21print(\"Q6\", sorted(m for m in [\"GET\", \"POST\", \"PUT\", \"PATCH\", \"DELETE\"] if m not in IDEMPOTENT))2223c = SimpleCookie()24c.load(\"session=abc123; theme=dark\")25print(\"Q7\", c[\"session\"].value, sorted(c))2627body = json.dumps({\"price\": 9.5, \"tags\": (\"a\", \"b\"), \"ok\": None, 1: \"one\"})28print(\"Q8\", body, json.loads(body)[\"1\"])2930raw = \"HTTP/1.1 301 Moved Permanently\\r\\nLocation: /new\\r\\nContent-Length: 0\\r\\n\\r\\n\"31status_line, *header_lines = raw.split(\"\\r\\n\")[:-2]32headers = dict(line.split(\": \", 1) for line in header_lines)33print(\"Q9\", status_line.split(\" \", 2), headers[\"Location\"])3435print(\"Q10\", len(\"café\".encode()), len(\"café\"), \"caf%C3%A9\" == quote(\"café\")) Open the answers for paper A Q Output Why (chapter) 1 https api.shop.example 8443 /v2/books q=python&page=2 top urlsplit gives each part. The port is an int, and the fragment after # is never sent to the server (1.1) 2 {'tag': ['a', 'b']} [''] parse_qs always gives lists and drops empty values unless keep_blank_values=True (2.1) 3 q=tea+%26+cake&page=1 /a%20b/ %2Fa%20b%2F urlencode writes a space as + and & as %26. quote keeps / unless safe=\"\" (2.1) 4 https://x.example/api/v1/books https://x.example/api/books https://x.example/books Without the trailing slash, the last part of the base is replaced. A leading / starts again at the root (2.1) 5 [201, 404] Too Many Requests [2, 3, 4, 5] The class of a status code is code // 100: 2 success, 3 redirect, 4 client error, 5 server error (1.1) 6 ['PATCH', 'POST'] GET, HEAD, OPTIONS, PUT and DELETE are idempotent: repeating them changes nothing more. POST and PATCH are not (1.1) 7 abc123 ['session', 'theme'] A Cookie header holds name=value pairs separated by semicolons, and SimpleCookie reads them (1.1) 8 {\"price\": 9.5, \"tags\": [\"a\", \"b\"], \"ok\": null, \"1\": \"one\"} one JSON turns a tuple into a list, None into null and the int key 1 into the string \"1\" (2.2) 9 ['HTTP/1.1', '301', 'Moved Permanently'] /new A raw response is a status line, headers and a blank line. A 301 sends the new address in Location (1.2) 10 5 4 True é takes 2 bytes in UTF-8, and Content-Length counts bytes. Percent encoding also works on the UTF-8 bytes (1.2) Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 paper_a.py. Lesson 3 of 11"],[51,"paper_b","Paper B: Flask","Paper B: what does each line printpython 1from flask import Blueprint, Flask, abort, g, jsonify, request, session, url_for23app = Flask(__name__)4app.secret_key = \"exam-only\"5BOOKS = {1: \"Emma\", 2: \"Dune\"}678@app.get(\"/books/<int:book_id>\")9def get_book(book_id):10 if book_id not in BOOKS:11 abort(404)12 return {\"id\": book_id, \"title\": BOOKS[book_id]}131415@app.post(\"/books\")16def add_book():17 data = request.get_json(silent=True) or {}18 if \"title\" not in data:19 return jsonify(error=\"title is required\"), 40020 new_id = max(BOOKS) + 121 BOOKS[new_id] = data[\"title\"]22 return {\"id\": new_id}, 201, {\"Location\": url_for(\"get_book\", book_id=new_id)}232425@app.get(\"/search\")26def search():27 page = request.args.get(\"page\", 1, type=int)28 return {\"q\": request.args.get(\"q\"), \"page\": page}293031@app.before_request32def load_user():33 g.user = request.headers.get(\"X-User\", \"guest\")343536@app.get(\"/me\")37def me():38 session[\"visits\"] = session.get(\"visits\", 0) + 139 return {\"user\": g.user, \"visits\": session[\"visits\"]}404142@app.errorhandler(404)43def not_found(err):44 return {\"error\": \"not found\", \"path\": request.path}, 404454647admin = Blueprint(\"admin\", __name__, url_prefix=\"/admin\")484950@admin.get(\"/stats\")51def stats():52 return {\"books\": len(BOOKS)}535455app.register_blueprint(admin)56client = app.test_client()5758r = client.get(\"/books/2\")59print(\"Q1\", r.status_code, r.get_json())60print(\"Q2\", client.get(\"/books/two\").status_code, client.get(\"/books/9\").get_json())61print(\"Q3\", client.delete(\"/books/1\").status_code, client.post(\"/books/1\").status_code)62r = client.post(\"/books\", json={\"title\": \"Ulysses\"})63print(\"Q4\", r.status_code, r.get_json(), r.headers[\"Location\"])64print(\"Q5\", client.post(\"/books\", data=\"not json\").get_json())65print(\"Q6\", client.get(\"/search?q=tea&page=x\").get_json(), client.get(\"/search?page=3\").get_json())66client.get(\"/me\")67print(\"Q7\", client.get(\"/me\", headers={\"X-User\": \"ann\"}).get_json())68print(\"Q8\", app.test_client().get(\"/me\").get_json()[\"visits\"])69print(\"Q9\", client.get(\"/admin/stats\").get_json(), client.get(\"/stats\").status_code)70with app.test_request_context():71 print(\"Q10\", url_for(\"get_book\", book_id=7), url_for(\"admin.stats\"), url_for(\"search\", q=\"a b\", page=2)) Open the answers for paper B Q Output Why (chapter) 1 200 {'id': 2, 'title': 'Dune'} A view that returns a dict is sent as JSON (3.1) 2 404 {'error': 'not found', 'path': '/books/9'} \"two\" does not fit int:, so no route matches and the answer is 404, not 400. The 404 handler formats both cases (3.1) 3 405 405 The path exists but not for DELETE or POST: 405 Method Not Allowed (3.1) 4 201 {'id': 3} /books/3 A view can return (body, status, headers). url_for builds the Location from the view name (3.1) 5 {'error': 'title is required'} get_json(silent=True) returns None for a body that is not JSON instead of failing (3.1) 6 {'page': 1, 'q': 'tea'} {'page': 3, 'q': None} type=int falls back to the default when the value is not a number. A missing argument is None. Flask sorts the keys of JSON answers (3.1) 7 {'user': 'ann', 'visits': 2} before_request fills g for each request. The session cookie from the first call came back with the second (3.2) 8 1 A new test client has no cookie, so it starts a new session (3.2) 9 {'books': 3} 404 The blueprint adds the prefix /admin, so /stats alone does not exist (3.2) 10 /books/7 /admin/stats /search?q=a+b&page=2 Blueprint views are named blueprint.view. Arguments that are not in the rule become the query string (3.1) Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 paper_b.py. Lesson 4 of 11"],[51,"paper_c","Paper C: Django and Django REST framework","Paper C: what does each line printpython 1import logging2import os34import django5from django.apps import AppConfig6from django.conf import settings789class ExamConfig(AppConfig):10 name = \"__main__\"11 label = \"exam\"12 path = os.getcwd()131415settings.configure(16 DEBUG=False, SECRET_KEY=\"exam-only\", ROOT_URLCONF=__name__, ALLOWED_HOSTS=[\"testserver\"],17 INSTALLED_APPS=[\"django.contrib.contenttypes\", \"django.contrib.auth\", \"rest_framework\", \"__main__.ExamConfig\"],18 DATABASES={\"default\": {\"ENGINE\": \"django.db.backends.sqlite3\", \"NAME\": \":memory:\"}},19 DEFAULT_AUTO_FIELD=\"django.db.models.BigAutoField\",20 REST_FRAMEWORK={\"UNAUTHENTICATED_USER\": None},21)22django.setup()23logging.getLogger(\"django.request\").setLevel(logging.ERROR)2425from django.db import connection, models26from django.db.models import Count, F, Q27from django.http import JsonResponse28from django.template import Context, Engine29from django.urls import path, resolve, reverse30from rest_framework import serializers31from rest_framework.test import APIClient323334class Author(models.Model):35 name = models.CharField(max_length=50)363738class Book(models.Model):39 title = models.CharField(max_length=100)40 year = models.IntegerField()41 stock = models.IntegerField(default=0)42 author = models.ForeignKey(Author, on_delete=models.CASCADE, related_name=\"books\")434445with connection.schema_editor() as editor:46 editor.create_model(Author)47 editor.create_model(Book)48ann = Author.objects.create(name=\"Ann\")49bob = Author.objects.create(name=\"Bob\")50for title, year, stock, who in [(\"Emma\", 1815, 3, ann), (\"Dune\", 1965, 0, bob), (\"Ubik\", 1969, 5, bob)]:51 Book.objects.create(title=title, year=year, stock=stock, author=who)525354def book_detail(request, pk):55 return JsonResponse({\"pk\": pk})565758urlpatterns = [path(\"books/<int:pk>/\", book_detail, name=\"book-detail\")]5960qs = Book.objects.filter(year__gt=1900)61print(\"Q1\", type(qs).__name__, qs.count(), list(qs.values_list(\"title\", flat=True)))6263print(\"Q2\", Book.objects.filter(Q(stock=0) | Q(author__name=\"Ann\")).order_by(\"title\").values_list(\"title\", flat=True)[:5])6465Book.objects.filter(stock__gt=0).update(stock=F(\"stock\") - 1)66print(\"Q3\", list(Book.objects.order_by(\"id\").values_list(\"stock\", flat=True)))6768counts = Author.objects.annotate(n=Count(\"books\")).order_by(\"-n\").values_list(\"name\", \"n\")69print(\"Q4\", list(counts))7071print(\"Q5\", \"JOIN\" in str(Book.objects.filter(author__name=\"Bob\").query), str(Book.objects.all().query).count(\"SELECT\"))7273print(\"Q6\", reverse(\"book-detail\", args=[7]), resolve(\"/books/7/\").kwargs, resolve(\"/books/7/\").url_name)7475tpl = Engine().from_string(\"{% for b in books %}{{ b|upper }}{% if not forloop.last %}, {% endif %}{% empty %}none{% endfor %} {{ html }}\")76print(\"Q7\", tpl.render(Context({\"books\": [\"emma\", \"dune\"], \"html\": \"<b>hi</b>\"})))777879class BookSerializer(serializers.Serializer):80 title = serializers.CharField(max_length=10)81 year = serializers.IntegerField(min_value=1450)82 stock = serializers.IntegerField(required=False, default=0)838485s = BookSerializer(data={\"title\": \"A very long title\", \"year\": \"1200\"})86print(\"Q8\", s.is_valid(), sorted(s.errors))87s = BookSerializer(data={\"title\": \"Emma\", \"year\": \"1815\"})88print(\"Q9\", s.is_valid(), dict(s.validated_data))8990r = APIClient().get(\"/books/42/\")91print(\"Q10\", r.status_code, r.json(), APIClient().get(\"/books/x/\").status_code) Open the answers for paper C Q Output Why (chapter) 1 QuerySet 2 ['Dune', 'Ubik'] filter gives a lazy QuerySet. year__gt is a field lookup, and SQL runs only when the values are needed (4.2) 2 <QuerySet ['Dune', 'Emma']> Q objects joined with | make an OR. A slice of a QuerySet is still a QuerySet (4.2) 3 [2, 0, 4] update with F(\"stock\") - 1 runs one SQL UPDATE, only on the rows with stock above 0 (4.2) 4 [('Bob', 2), ('Ann', 1)] annotate(Count(\"books\")) adds a count per author, using the related_name (4.2) 5 True 1 Filtering across the foreign key adds a JOIN. all() is a single SELECT (4.2) 6"],[51,"paper_c","Paper C: Django and Django REST framework","/books/7/ {'pk': 7} book-detail reverse turns a name into a URL and resolve turns a URL back into the view and its arguments (4.1) 7 EMMA, DUNE &lt;b&gt;hi&lt;/b&gt; upper is a filter, forloop.last skips the last comma, and autoescape turns < into &lt; so user text cannot inject HTML (4.1) 8 False ['title', 'year'] Every field is checked and all errors are collected: the title is longer than 10 and 1200 is below 1450 (5.1) 9 True {'title': 'Emma', 'year': 1815, 'stock': 0} \"1815\" becomes the int 1815, and the default fills the missing stock (5.1) 10 200 {'pk': 42} 404 int: converts 42 and refuses x, which gives 404 (4.1) Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 paper_c.py. Lesson 5 of 11"],[51,"paper_d","Paper D: Pydantic, tokens and security","Paper D: what does each line printpython 1import base642import hashlib3import hmac4import ipaddress5import secrets6from datetime import datetime, timedelta, timezone7from typing import Literal89import jwt10from pydantic import BaseModel, Field, ValidationError111213class Item(BaseModel):14 name: str15 qty: int = 116 size: Literal[\"S\", \"M\", \"L\"] = \"M\"17 tags: list[str] = []181920print(\"Q1\", Item(name=\"tea\", qty=\"3\").qty, Item(name=\"tea\").tags is Item(name=\"cake\").tags)21try:22 Item(name=\"tea\", qty=\"3.5\", size=\"XL\")23except ValidationError as err:24 print(\"Q2\", err.error_count(), [e[\"loc\"][0] for e in err.errors()])252627class Address(BaseModel):28 city: str29 zip_code: str = Field(pattern=r\"^\\d{5}$\")303132class Order(BaseModel):33 id: int34 ship_to: Address35 note: str | None = None363738try:39 Order(id=1, ship_to={\"city\": \"Bonn\", \"zip_code\": \"5311\"})40except ValidationError as err:41 print(\"Q3\", err.errors()[0][\"loc\"])42o = Order(id=1, ship_to={\"city\": \"Bonn\", \"zip_code\": \"53111\"})43print(\"Q4\", o.model_dump(exclude_none=True), o.model_dump_json(include={\"id\"}))4445KEY = \"k\" * 3246token = jwt.encode({\"sub\": \"ann\", \"aud\": \"shop\", \"exp\": datetime.now(timezone.utc) + timedelta(minutes=5)}, KEY, algorithm=\"HS256\")47print(\"Q5\", token[:3], token.count(\".\"), base64.urlsafe_b64encode(b'{\"a').decode())48for kwargs in [{}, {\"algorithms\": [\"HS256\"]}, {\"algorithms\": [\"HS256\"], \"audience\": \"shop\"}]:49 try:50 jwt.decode(token, KEY, **kwargs)51 print(\"Q6\", \"ok\")52 except jwt.InvalidTokenError as err:53 print(\"Q6\", type(err).__name__)5455digest = hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac(\"sha256\", b\"pw\", b\"salt\", 1000)56print(\"Q7\", len(digest), len(digest.hex()), len(secrets.token_hex(16)), len(secrets.token_urlsafe(16)))57print(\"Q8\", hmac.compare_digest(\"abc\", \"abc\"), hmac.new(b\"k\", b\"msg\", \"sha256\").hexdigest() == hmac.new(b\"k\", b\"msg\", \"sha256\").hexdigest())58print(\"Q9\", [ipaddress.ip_address(a).is_private for a in [\"10.1.2.3\", \"172.32.0.1\", \"192.168.0.9\", \"8.8.8.8\", \"::1\"]])59print(\"Q10\", hashlib.sha256(b\"a\").hexdigest() == hashlib.sha256(b\"a\").hexdigest(),60 hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac(\"sha256\", b\"a\", b\"s1\", 10) == hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac(\"sha256\", b\"a\", b\"s2\", 10)) Open the answers for paper D Q Output Why (chapter) 1 3 False \"3\" becomes 3. Pydantic copies a mutable default, so each object gets its own list (5.2) 2 2 ['qty', 'size'] \"3.5\" is not a whole number and XL is not one of the Literal values. Pydantic lists every error (5.2) 3 ('ship_to', 'zip_code') Errors in nested models have the full path, which FastAPI puts after body in a 422 answer (5.2) 4 {'id': 1, 'ship_to': {'city': 'Bonn', 'zip_code': '53111'}} {\"id\":1} exclude_none drops note. include keeps only the named fields, and model_dump_json writes compact JSON (5.2) 5 eyJ 2 eyJh Every JWT starts with eyJ because it is base64 of {\" and has three parts, so two dots (6.1) 6 DecodeError, InvalidAudienceError, ok PyJWT 2 refuses to decode without algorithms=. A token with aud needs audience= as well (6.1) 7 32 64 32 22 SHA-256 gives 32 bytes, which is 64 hex digits. token_hex(16) gives 32 characters and token_urlsafe(16) gives 22 (6.1) 8 True True The same key and message always give the same HMAC. compare_digest only changes how long the comparison takes (6.1) 9 [True, False, True, False, True] 172.16.0.0/12 ends at 172.31.255.255, so 172.32.0.1 is public. ::1 is the IPv6 loopback (6.1) 10 True False A hash is the same for the same input. Different salts give different results, which defeats precomputed tables (6.1) Download this program (.py)Save it, open it in any editor and run it with python3 paper_d.py. Lesson 6 of 11"],[51,"bugs","Find the bugs","This Flask service has eight problems. Find them before you read onpython 1import sqlite323import requests4from flask import Flask, jsonify, request56app = Flask(__name__)7app.config[\"SECRET_KEY\"] = \"dev\"8db = sqlite3.connect(\"shop.db\", check_same_thread=False)91011@app.get(\"/orders/<int:order_id>\")12def get_order(order_id):13 row = db.execute(f\"SELECT id, customer, total FROM orders WHERE id = {order_id}\").fetchone()14 return jsonify(row)151617@app.post(\"/users\")18def create_user():19 data = request.get_json()20 db.execute(\"INSERT INTO users (name, is_admin) VALUES (?, ?)\", (data[\"name\"], data.get(\"is_admin\", 0)))21 return jsonify(data), 200222324@app.get(\"/rate\")25def rate():26 answer = requests.get(\"https://rates.example/eur\")27 return answer.json()282930@app.get(\"/orders\")31def list_orders():32 limit = int(request.args.get(\"limit\", 20))33 rows = db.execute(\"SELECT id, total FROM orders LIMIT ?\", (limit,)).fetchall()34 return jsonify(rows)353637if __name__ == \"__main__\":38 app.run(debug=True, host=\"0.0.0.0\") The eight problems A secret in the code. SECRET_KEY = \"dev\" signs the session cookies, so anyone who reads the repository can forge them. Read it from os.environ (6.1). No owner check. Any caller can read any order by changing the number. Filter by the logged in user and answer 404 for other people's orders (6.1, OWASP API1). SQL built with an f-string. Here int: happens to protect it, but the habit leads to injection. Always use ? placeholders (4.2, 6.1). Mass assignment. is_admin comes from the request body, so anyone can make themselves an admin. Accept only the fields you expect (6.1, OWASP API3). No commit and the wrong status. The INSERT is never committed, and a create should answer 201 with the new id, not echo the input with 200 (3.1, 4.2). No timeout and no status check. requests.get without timeout= can hang the worker for ever, and a 500 from the partner is passed on as if it were data. Use timeout=5 and raise_for_status() (2.1). No upper limit. limit=10000000 makes the database send every row. Cap it, for example min(limit, 100), and refuse values that are not numbers with 400 (6.1, OWASP API4). Debug mode on every network interface. debug=True with host 0.0.0.0 offers the interactive debugger, which can run code, to anyone who can reach the port, with only a PIN in the way. Use a real server such as gunicorn with debug off (3.2). Lesson 7 of 11"],[51,"traps","The traps in one table","Trap Looks like Really Chapter URL fragment Sent to the server Stays in the browser 1.1 PUT and PATCH The same PUT replaces the whole resource and is idempotent, PATCH changes part of it 1.1 requests.get(url) Gives up after a while Waits for ever without timeout= 2.1 A 404 or 500 from requests Raises an error Only raise_for_status() raises 2.1 JSON keys Keep their type Always become strings 2.2 Flask int: converter Bad values give 400 They give 404 because no route matches 3.1 A route without methods= Accepts every method Only GET (and HEAD, OPTIONS) 3.1 Flask debug=True Fine on a server The debugger can run code: never in production 3.2 QuerySet Runs SQL when made Runs when looped, sliced with a step, counted or printed 4.2 book.author in a loop One query One query per book (N + 1): use select_related 4.2 |safe in a template Just formatting Turns off autoescape: XSS if the text came from a user 4.1 serializer.data before is_valid() Works Raises an AssertionError for input data 5.1 time.sleep in async def Waits only this request Blocks every request on the event loop 5.2 A JWT payload Secret Readable by anyone, only signed 6.1 CORS Protects the API Only tells browsers which sites may read answers 6.1 A valid token Can read anything Still needs the owner check on every object 6.1 Lesson 8 of 11"],[51,"design","A system design answer: a link shortener API","Interviews for backend roles often end with a design question. Here is a model answer for \"Design an API that shortens links\", using only what this part taught. The answer, step by step Requirements. POST /links with a long URL gives a short code. GET /{code} redirects. Owners can list and delete their links. Expect 100 new links and 10,000 redirects a second. API. POST /links (201, Location header), GET /{code} (301 or 302 with Location), GET /links?cursor=... (cursor pagination), DELETE /links/{code} (204). Pydantic or a DRF serializer checks the URL (5.1, 5.2). Codes. 7 characters from 62 letters and digits give 627 ≈ 3.5 × 1012 codes. Make them from secrets or a counter turned into base 62, and retry on the rare clash (6.1). Storage. A table code (primary key), url, owner, created. Redirects read by primary key, which is one index lookup (4.2). Speed. Reads are 100 times the writes, so put a cache (Redis) in front: most redirects never touch the database. Answer 301 when links never change, so browsers cache them too (1.1). Safety. Only http and https URLs, a block list for known bad sites, a login for POST and DELETE, an owner check on delete, and a rate limit per user (6.1). Async. The redirect does no slow work, so plain workers are fine. Click counting goes to a queue so the redirect never waits for it (5.2). Codes availableExam favourite codes = alphabetlength 627 ≈ 3.52 × 1012. Storage per year rows = writes per second × seconds per year 100 × 31,536,000 ≈ 3.2 × 109 rows. Load on the database with a cache reads to the database = reads × (1 − hit rate) 10,000 × (1 − 0.99) = 100 a second. Lesson 9 of 11"],[51,"plan","Exam technique and a study plan","Two weeks of revision Days 1 to 3: paper A, then chapters 1.1 to 2.2. Write a requests call with a timeout, retries and raise_for_status from memory. Days 4 to 6: paper B, then 3.1 and 3.2. Build a small Flask JSON API with a blueprint and a test with test_client. Days 7 to 10: paper C, then 4.1, 4.2 and 5.1. Write a model, a serializer, a ViewSet and one APITestCase without notes. Days 11 to 13: paper D, then 5.2 and 6.1. Add a JWT login and an owner check to a FastAPI app. Day 14: the bug listing, the traps table, the design answer and the mock quiz in the Quiz tab under a timer. In the exam In a written exam, trace each snippet on paper before you look at the options, and watch for the answers \"404\", \"405\" and \"the code raises\". In an interview, say your assumptions out loud, start with the simplest working API, then add caching, security and limits. One idea, four depths Explain HTTP methods and status codes, call an API with requests, and build a small Flask app. Build a Django site with models and the admin, and JSON APIs with DRF and FastAPI, all with tests. Make it fast and safe: no N + 1 queries, async used correctly, tokens, scopes, owner checks and rate limits. Design whole services: choose the framework, the storage, the cache and the security for given traffic, and defend the choice. Beginner Can build it. Intermediate Can test and secure it. Expert Can design it. Lesson 10 of 11"],[51,"leetcode","LeetCode review set","Ten problems with the shape of backend work: the slowest call in a log, a request queue on one server, migration order, conversion rates in a graph, a connection pool, a stable latency window, a lock with only 10,000 codes, a deployment pipeline, paid batch jobs and deadlines. Try each in under 30 minutes, as in an interview. No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1629 Slowest Key Easy One pass over the gaps between times Like finding the slowest request in an access log: each duration is the gap to the previous time. Keep the longest, and on a tie the larger letter. 1701 Average Waiting Time Medium Simulate one worker with a clock One server handles requests in the order they arrive. The clock is the time the server becomes free. A request starts at max(clock, arrival), and its waiting time is the finish time minus the arrival. 210 Course Schedule II Medium Topological sort with in-degrees (Kahn) The same problem as running database migrations or loading apps in dependency order. Start with every item that depends on nothing, and each time one is done, lower the count of the items waiting for it. A cycle leaves items behind, so the answer is empty. 399 Evaluate Division Medium A weighted graph and a search Like a currency API that only knows some rates: a / b = 2 is an edge from a to b with weight 2 and from b to a with 0.5. A query multiplies the weights along any path, or gives -1.0 when there is none. 1942 The Number of the Smallest Unoccupied Chair Medium Two heaps: free chairs and busy chairs by leaving time This is exactly how a connection pool hands out the lowest free connection number. Go through arrivals in time order, first free every chair whose guest has left, then give the smallest free one. 1438 Longest Continuous Subarray With Absolute Diff Less Than or Equal to Limit Medium Sliding window with a max deque and a min deque Like finding the longest stretch where response times stayed within a band. Two deques keep the window's maximum and minimum in O(1). When max minus min is too big, move the left edge. 752 Open the Lock Medium Breadth first search over 10,000 states A 4 digit lock has only 104 codes, which is why short codes need attempt limits. BFS from 0000 turns one wheel up or down at each step and skips the dead ends, so the first time it reaches the target is the fewest turns. 2050 Parallel Courses III Hard Longest path in a DAG, in topological order Like a deployment pipeline where jobs run in parallel once their needs are done: a job finishes at its own time plus the latest finish among the jobs it waits for. The answer is the latest finish of all. 1235 Maximum Profit in Job Scheduling Hard Sort by end time, DP with binary search Like choosing which paid batch jobs a single worker should run. Sort jobs by end time. The best profit up to a job is either the best without it, or its profit plus the best among jobs that end by its start, found with bisect. 630 Course Schedule III Hard Greedy by deadline with a max heap of durations Take tasks in order of their deadline. When the total time passes a deadline, drop the longest task taken so far, because that frees the most time while keeping the count. A max heap (negative numbers in heapq) finds it. .py Solutions to these LeetCode problemsOne simple solution per problem, with the idea, the time and space cost, and tests you can run.Try each problem on leetcode.com first. Open the file only when you are stuck. Run it with python3 leetcode.py to see every test pass. Download solutions Lesson 11 of 11"],[51,"recap","Chapter summary","HTTP: the fragment stays in the browser, status class is code // 100, POST and PATCH are not idempotent, JSON keys become strings. Flask: a bad int: value gives 404, a wrong method 405, dicts become JSON, blueprints add prefixes, and debug=True never goes to production. Django and DRF: QuerySets are lazy, F updates in SQL, select_related stops N + 1, templates escape by default, serializers list every error. FastAPI and Pydantic: hints convert and check, every error has a location, async def must not block. Security: slow salted hashes, signed short lived tokens with pinned algorithms, owner checks on every object, timeouts and limits everywhere. Next: You have finished the Web and APIs part. The interview set and the cheatsheet cover the whole subject in one place. .py Practice files for this chapterThree exam style tasks: pick the right status code, build a Flask style URL router with converters, and add ETags for caching and safe updates.practice.py has every assignment with its starter code and tests. solutions.py has the model answers. Each program of the lessons also has its own download link under it. Download practice.pyDownload solutions.py"],[52,"","Cheatsheet","Every key idea, formula, table, memory aid and common mistake from all three sections on one page. Core Python runs from print() and types to strings, lists, dictionaries, functions, exceptions, files and classes. Advanced Python runs from magic methods, decorators and metaclasses to generators, testing, complexity, regular expressions and concurrency. Web and APIs runs from HTTP and REST to requests, Flask, Django, DRF, FastAPI and JWT. Search it, filter it, print it or download the PDF. · cheatsheet · cheat sheet · formula sheet · revision · PDF · download · quick reference · print sep end · integer division · operator precedence · slicing · list methods · dict methods · LEGB · *args **kwargs · try except finally · file modes · MRO · magic methods · decorators · generators · big O · regex · GIL · HTTP status codes · Flask routes · Django ORM · FastAPI · JWT"],[52,"","Step 1Core Python · Getting started","1.1"],[52,"py-start","What a program is and your first Python program","Open chapter Programs, compiler and interpreter, CPython, REPL and scripts, print() with sep and end, keywords, indentation, error kinds, PEP 8. Key ideas Program and language A program is a list of exact instructions. Lexis is the vocabulary, syntax the grammar, semantics the meaning. How CPython runs a file Source is checked and compiled to bytecode, then the virtual machine runs it top to bottom. A SyntaxError anywhere stops everything. print() print(*values, sep=\" \", end=\"\\n\"). str() on each value, sep between, end after. N = L + (n − 1)s + e. Keywords and blocks 35 keywords in 3.14, soft keywords match, case, type, _. # starts a comment. Indent blocks with 4 spaces. Three errors Syntax error before running, runtime error at the bad line, logic error never reported. Formulas The full form of print()Exam favourite print(*values, sep=\" \", end=\"\\n\", file=sys.stdout, flush=False) values: any number of values. sep: text between them. end: text after the last one. file: where to write. flush: write at once. Lesson: print(): showing results on the screen Characters written by one print()Exam favourite N = ∑ len(str(vi)) + (n − 1) · len(sep) + len(end) n values v1 to vn. There are n − 1 gaps between n values. Lesson: print(): showing results on the screen Characters from one print()Exam favourite N = L + (n − 1) · s + e L = total length of all values as text, n = number of values, s = len(sep), e = len(end). Lesson: The maths: counting what print() writes Bytes in a plain English text file bytes = characters Each English letter, digit, space and new line is 1 byte in UTF-8. Letters from other scripts can take 2 to 4 bytes (Core 3.1). Lesson: The maths: counting what print() writes Tables Table: word · meaning · in everyday language Word Meaning In everyday language Lexis The words and symbols a language allows (print, if, +, \"text\") The vocabulary of English Syntax The grammar rules: how words may be put together \"I rice eat\" breaks English grammar Semantics What a correct sentence means \"The stone ate the rice\" has good grammar but makes no sense Lesson: What a program is Table: · compiler · interpreter Compiler Interpreter How it works Translates the whole program first into a separate machine code file, then you run that file Reads the program and runs it straight away, statement by statement Like Translating a full book into Hindi, printing it, then reading it A live interpreter at a meeting who translates each sentence as it is spoken When errors show Most mistakes are found before anything runs A mistake is found when Python reaches that line (except grammar mistakes, see below) Speed Usually faster to run Usually slower, but you see results at once Examples C, C++, Go, Rust Python, JavaScript in old browsers, shell scripts Lesson: Compiler, interpreter and how Python runs your file Table: python implementation · written in · where it is used Python implementation Written in Where it is used CPython C The standard one from python.org. This course uses CPython 3.14 PyPy Python (RPython) Has a just in time compiler, often several times faster on long loops MicroPython C Small boards like the Raspberry Pi Pico and ESP32 Pyodide CPython compiled to WebAssembly Runs Python inside your web browser. The Run buttons on this page use it Lesson: Compiler, interpreter and how Python runs your file Table: way to run · what it is · good for Way to run What it is Good for Interactive mode (REPL) Type python3 with no file. The prompt >>> waits for one line, runs it and shows the result at once Quick tests, like a calculator Script mode Save lines in a .py file and run python3 file.py Real programs you want to keep and run again An editor or IDE IDLE (comes with Python), VS Code, PyCharm, Thonny Writing, running and debugging bigger programs A notebook Jupyter or Google Colab: code cells and notes in one page Data science and learning Lesson: Install Python and run your first program Table: you write · the screen shows You write The screen"],[52,"py-start","What a program is and your first Python program","shows print(\"A\", \"B\") A B print(\"A\", \"B\", sep=\"\") AB print(\"A\", end=\"\"), then print(\"B\") AB print(\"A\", \"B\", sep=\"-\", end=\"!\") A-B! print() an empty line print(3 + 4, \"3 + 4\") 7 3 + 4 Lesson: print(): showing results on the screen Table: part · what it is · example Part What it is Example Keyword A reserved word with a fixed job. Python 3.14 has 35 of them if, else, for, while, def, class, return, True, None Soft keyword A word that is a keyword only in one place, and a normal name everywhere else match, case, type, _ Statement One complete instruction price = 120 Comment Text after # that Python ignores. It is a note for people # add the tax Indentation Spaces at the start of a line. They show which lines belong to a block 4 spaces under an if Docstring A text in triple quotes at the top of a file or function that explains it \"\"\"Works out the bill.\"\"\" Lesson: Keywords, statements, comments and indentation Table: kind · when python finds it · example · what you see Kind When Python finds it Example What you see Syntax error Before anything runs, while checking the grammar of the whole file print(\"Hi\" SyntaxError, and not one line has run Runtime error (exception) Only when the bad line runs 10 / 0, a misspelt name A traceback. The lines before it have already run Logic error Never. Python cannot know what you meant price + 2 instead of price * 2 A wrong answer and no message at all Lesson: Three kinds of errors Table: pep 8 rule · good · bad PEP 8 rule Good Bad Indent with 4 spaces four spaces before print a tab, or 2 spaces Variable and function names: lower case with underscores (snake_case) total_price TotalPrice, totalprice Class names: CapWords BankAccount bank_account Constants: all capitals MAX_SIZE = 100 maxsize = 100 One space around = and + in normal code x = a + b x=a+b Lines no longer than 79 characters short lines one very long line Two blank lines between top level functions a gap you can see everything packed together Lesson: Writing code other people can read: PEP 8 and PEP 20 Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 2235 Add Two Integers Easy Arithmetic Return num1 + num2. The point is to get used to a function that returns a value instead of printing it. 2469 Convert the Temperature Easy Formula Kelvin = Celsius + 273.15 and Fahrenheit = Celsius × 1.80 + 32.00. Return both in a list. 2413 Smallest Even Multiple Easy Arithmetic If n is already even, n itself works. If n is odd, the answer is 2 × n. 1108 Defanging an IP Address Easy String replace Replace every dot with [.] using the string method replace(). Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps Remember Python is often called an interpreted language, but CPython does compile your code to bytecode first. Both words are partly true. The Advanced Python step 7 chapter \"How Python runs\" opens every one of these stages with an animation.Lesson: Compiler, interpreter and how Python runs your file Common mistake In the REPL, typing 2 + 3 shows 5 without print(). In a .py file, nothing appears unless you call print(). Beginners often expect the REPL behaviour inside a file.Lesson: Install Python and run your first program In the exam sep and end must be given by name (keyword arguments). print(\"A\", \"B\", \"-\") prints A B - with the dash as a third value, it does not change sep.Lesson: print(): showing results on the screen Common mistake Python uses indentation where many languages use curly brackets. Mixing tabs and spaces, or using 3 spaces on one line and 4 on the next, gives IndentationError. PEP 8 says: always 4 spaces.Lesson: Keywords, statements, comments and indentation In the exam PCEP asks which names follow PEP 8 conventions. snake_case for variables and functions, CapWords for classes, UPPER_CASE for constants.Lesson: Writing code other people can read: PEP 8 and PEP 20 1.2"],[52,"py-values","Variables, values, types and numeral systems","Open chapter Names and objects, the basic types and literals, numeral systems, type conversion, naming rules, multiple assignment. Key ideas Names and objects name = value binds a name to an object. b = a gives a second name to the same object. Objects have id, type, value. Basic types int (unlimited), float (about 15 to 17 digits), complex, bool (subclass of int), str, None. All immutable. Bases 0b, 0o, 0x literals. bin(), oct(), hex() to text. int(text, base) back. N = sum of di bi. Conversions int() cuts towards zero, int(\"3.5\") is ValueError, round() ties to even, bool(\"0\") is True. Naming Letters, digits, _ , no leading digit, no keyword, case sensitive, snake_case, do not shadow built ins. Formulas Scientific notationExam favourite a e b = a × 10b 1.5e3 = 1.5 × 103 = 1500.0. The result is always a float. Lesson: The basic data types and their literals Underscores in numbers 1_000_000 == 1000000 Since Python 3.6 (PEP 515) underscores can group digits. Python ignores them. Lesson: The basic data types and their literals Value of a number written in base bExam favourite value = dkbk + ... + d1b1 + d0b0 Each digit is multiplied by the base to the power of its position, counting from 0 on the right. Lesson: Numeral systems: binary, octal and hexadecimal Digits needed in base 2 bits(n) = ⌊log2 n⌋ + 1 For n > 0. Python gives it as n.bit_length(). 156 needs 8 bits. Lesson: Numeral systems: binary, octal and hexadecimal Base b to decimalExam favourite N = ∑i=0k di · bi di is the digit at position i from the right. Lesson: The maths: place value and conversions Decimal to base bExam favourite d0 = N mod b, N ← N // b, repeat The remainders in reverse order are the digits. Lesson: The maths: place value and conversions Largest number in k bits 2k − 1 8 bits hold 0 to 255. 16 bits hold 0 to 65,535. Lesson: The maths: place value and conversions One hex digit = 4 bits, one octal digit = 3 bits So 0xFF is 1111 1111 and 0o7 is 111. Lesson: The maths: place value and conversions Tables Table: type · what it holds · literals · can it change? Type What it holds Literals Can it change? int Whole numbers of any size 7, -3, 1_000_000, 0b101, 0o17, 0xFF No (immutable) float Numbers with a decimal point, about 15 to 17 correct digits 3.14, -0.5, 2.0, 1.5e3, 1e-9 No complex Numbers with a real and an imaginary part 2 + 3j, 1j No bool True or False True, False No str Text, any number of characters \"hi\", 'hi', \"\"\"many lines\"\"\" No NoneType The single value meaning \"nothing here\" None No Lesson: The basic data types and their literals Table: base · digits · python literal · turn into text Base Digits Python literal Turn into text Example 2 (binary) 0, 1 0b1010 bin(10) 0b1010 == 10 8 (octal) 0 to 7 0o17 oct(15) 0o17 == 15 10 (decimal) 0 to 9 42 str(42) 42 16 (hexadecimal) 0 to 9, A to F 0xFF hex(255) 0xFF == 255 Lesson: Numeral systems: binary, octal and hexadecimal Table: call · result · rule Call Result Rule int(\"42\") 42 Text with only digits (and a sign, spaces around) int(3.99) 3 Cuts towards zero, it does not round int(\"3.5\") ValueError A decimal point in the text is not allowed int(\"ff\", 16) 255 A second argument gives the base float(\"3.5\") 3.5 Also accepts \"1e3\", \"inf\", \"nan\" str(7) \"7\" Anything can become text bool(0), bool(\"\") False Zero and empty things are False bool(\"0\"), bool(\" \") True Any non empty text is True, even \"0\" round(2.5), round(3.5) 2, 4 Ties go to the even number (bankers rounding) Lesson: Changing one type into another Table: rule · valid · not valid Rule Valid Not valid Letters, digits and underscores only total_2 total-2, total 2 Must not start with a digit price2 2price Must not be a keyword klass, class_ class, for, None Case matters Total and total are two different names Letters from any language are allowed año, naam Lesson: Naming rules and good names Table: · good · poor Good Poor Says what it holds total_price, student_count x, tp, data2 Follows PEP 8 snake_case for variables TotalPrice for a variable (that style"],[52,"py-values","Variables, values, types and numeral systems","is for classes) Avoids built in names items, values list, str, sum (they hide the built in functions) Lesson: Naming rules and good names Table: form · meaning Form Meaning a, b = 1, 2 Two names at once. The right side is a tuple that is unpacked a, b = b, a Swap. The right side is worked out fully before any name changes x = y = 0 Both names point to the same object 0 n += 1 Augmented assignment, n = n + 1. Also -=, *=, /=, //=, %=, **= total: int = 0 A type hint. Python does not check it, tools like mypy do (Advanced 3.1) Lesson: More ways to assign Table: · python · c · java Python C Java Type belongs to The object (dynamic typing) The variable, fixed when declared The variable, fixed when declared Declare first? No, the first assignment creates the name Yes: int age; Yes: int age; What a variable holds A reference to an object The value itself, in a box of fixed size A value (primitives) or a reference (objects) Int size Unlimited 32 or 64 bits, can overflow 32 bits (int) or 64 bits (long) Type errors found When the line runs, or earlier with mypy When compiling When compiling Lesson: How Python variables compare with other languages Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1281 Subtract the Product and Sum of Digits of an Integer Easy Digits with % and // Take the last digit with n % 10 and drop it with n // 10 until n is 0. Keep a product and a sum. 191 Number of 1 Bits Easy Binary bin(n) gives the binary text. Count the \"1\" characters in it. 1290 Convert Binary Number in a Linked List to Integer Easy Place value Start with 0. For each bit, value = value * 2 + bit. Here the list is given as a Python list of bits. 405 Convert a Number to Hexadecimal Easy Base conversion For negative numbers add 2 ** 32 first (two's complement). Then repeat: digit = num % 16, num = num // 16. 504 Base 7 Easy Base conversion Same as base 2 but divide by 7. Handle the sign separately. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps Remember In Python a variable has no type of its own. The object has the type. The same name can point to an int now and to a str later. This is called dynamic typing.Lesson: What a variable really is In the exam bool is a kind of int: True == 1 and False == 0, so True + True is 2. This surprises many people in exams.Lesson: The basic data types and their literals Common mistake A leading zero is not allowed on a decimal literal in Python 3: 017 is a SyntaxError. Write 0o17 for octal.Lesson: Numeral systems: binary, octal and hexadecimal In the exam int(-3.99) is -3, not -4. int() cuts towards zero. math.floor(-3.99) is -4 because floor goes down.Lesson: Changing one type into another Common mistake Naming a variable list or str hides the built in. After list = [1, 2], calling list(\"abc\") fails with TypeError: 'list' object is not callable.Lesson: Naming rules and good names 1.3"],[52,"py-operators","Operators, expressions and the order Python works them out","Open chapter Arithmetic, // and %, precedence, comparisons, float precision, logic and short circuit, bitwise operators. Key ideas Division / gives float. // floors (towards minus infinity). % has the sign of the divisor. a == (a // b) * b + a % b. Priority brackets, **, unary, * / // %, + -, shifts, & ^ |, comparisons, not, and, or. ** right to left. -2 ** 2 = -4. Comparisons Chained 1 < x < 10. Floats: use math.isclose(). 0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3 is False. Logic and, or short circuit and return an operand. not > and > or. Bits ~x = -x - 1. x << k = x * 2**k. x & 1 tests odd. x & (x - 1) == 0 tests power of 2. Formulas True division a / b Always gives a float: 6 / 3 = 2.0. Lesson: Arithmetic: the seven number operators Floor divisionExam favourite a // b = ⌊a ÷ b⌋ Divide, then round down towards minus infinity. 7 // 2 = 3, -7 // 2 = -4. Lesson: Arithmetic: the seven number operators Remainder (modulo)Exam favourite a % b = a − b · (a // b) Has the same sign as b. -7 % 2 = 1. Lesson: Arithmetic: the seven number operators The division identityExam favourite a == (a // b) * b + a % b Always true in Python for b ≠ 0. Use it to check your answers. Lesson: Arithmetic: the seven number operators Safe float comparisonExam favourite |a − b| ≤ max(rel · max(|a|, |b|), abs_tol) This is math.isclose(a, b). The default rel is 1e-09 and abs_tol is 0. Lesson: Comparisons and the trouble with floats Machine epsilon ε = 2−52 ≈ 2.22 × 10−16 The gap between 1.0 and the next float. sys.float_info.epsilon. Lesson: Comparisons and the trouble with floats Bitwise notExam favourite ~x = −x − 1 Python ints act like two's complement numbers with endless bits. Lesson: Bitwise operators ShiftsExam favourite x << k = x · 2k, x >> k = x // 2k Shifting is a fast way to multiply or divide by powers of 2. Lesson: Bitwise operators Useful tricks x & 1 (odd?), x & (x − 1) == 0 (power of 2?), x ^ x == 0 Used in LeetCode problems such as Single Number and Power of Two. Lesson: Bitwise operators Euclidean style division in PythonExam favourite a = q · b + r, q = ⌊a/b⌋, 0 ≤ r < b (b > 0) For b < 0, b < r ≤ 0. Lesson: The maths behind the operators Last digit and dropping itExam favourite n % 10, n // 10 Used to loop over the digits of a number. Lesson: The maths behind the operators Sum of digits test for 9 n % 9 == digit_sum(n) % 9 Because 10 % 9 = 1, every place value leaves remainder 1. Lesson: The maths behind the operators Float spacing near x ulp(x) ≈ |x| · 2−52 math.ulp(x). Bigger numbers have bigger gaps between floats. Lesson: The maths behind the operators Tables Table: group · operators · example · result Group Operators Example Result Arithmetic + - * / // % ** 17 // 5 3 Comparison == != < <= > >= 3 < 5 True Logical and or not True and False False Bitwise & | ^ ~ << >> 12 & 10 8 Assignment = += -= *= /= //= %= **= n += 1 n grows by 1 Membership in, not in \"a\" in \"cat\" True Identity is, is not x is None True or False Lesson: Operators: the verbs of a program Table: operand types · + - * // % ** · / Operand types + - * // % ** / int and int int (but int ** negative int is float) float int and float float float bool and int int (True counts as 1) float Lesson: Arithmetic: the seven number operators Table: priority (high to low) · operators · direction Priority (high to low) Operators Direction 1 ( ) brackets innermost first 2 ** right to left 3 +x, -x, ~x (unary) 4 * / // % left to right 5 + - left to right 6 << >> left to right 7 &, then ^, then | left to right 8 comparisons, in, not in, is, is not chained 9 not 10 and 11 or 12 if else (conditional expression), lambda, := Lesson: Which operation goes first Table: · float · decimal.decimal · fractions.fraction float decimal.Decimal fractions.Fraction Stores base 2, 53 bits base 10, as many digits as you set an exact top and bottom number 0.1 + 0.2 == 0.3 False True (with Decimal(\"0.1\")) True Speed Fastest, done by the chip Slower Slowest Use for Science, graphics, machine learning Money and bills Exact maths"],[52,"py-operators","Operators, expressions and the order Python works them out","with ratios Lesson: Comparisons and the trouble with floats Table: a · b · a and b · a or b a b a and b a or b not a True True True True False True False False True False False True False True True False False False False True Lesson: Logical operators and short circuit Table: operator · with text · example Operator With text Example + Joins two texts \"ab\" + \"cd\" is \"abcd\" * Repeats a text a whole number of times \"ha\" * 3 is \"hahaha\" in Is one text inside another? \"py\" in \"python\" is True == < > Compare letter by letter by Unicode number \"Zebra\" < \"apple\" is True Lesson: Text operators and assignment shortcuts Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1009 Complement of Base 10 Integer Easy Bitwise xor with a mask Make a mask of 1s as long as n in binary, (1 << n.bit_length()) - 1, then n ^ mask flips every bit. n = 0 gives 1. 231 Power of Two Easy Bit trick A power of two has exactly one 1 bit, so n & (n - 1) removes it and leaves 0. n must be above 0. 258 Add Digits Easy Maths (digital root) Without a loop: 0 stays 0, otherwise the answer is 1 + (num - 1) % 9. 461 Hamming Distance Easy xor then count bits x ^ y has a 1 exactly where the bits differ. Count the 1s. 29 Divide Two Integers Medium Shifts and truncation Work with absolute values. Subtract the biggest divisor << k that fits, add 1 << k to the answer. LeetCode truncates towards zero and clamps to 32 bits. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps In the exam A unary operator has one operand (-x, not x, ~x). A binary operator has two (a + b). The same symbol - can be either.Lesson: Operators: the verbs of a program In the exam C and Java cut integer division towards zero, so -7 / 2 is -3 there. Python floors, so -7 // 2 is -4. Exams love this difference.Lesson: Arithmetic: the seven number operators Common mistake -2 ** 2 is -4, not 4. The power is done first, then the minus. Write (-2) ** 2 when you mean minus two squared.Lesson: Which operation goes first In the exam not has a higher priority than and, and and is higher than or. So True or False and False is True or (False and False) = True.Lesson: Logical operators and short circuit Common mistake \"5\" + 5 is a TypeError. Python never turns a number into text by itself. Use str(5) or an f-string.Lesson: Text operators and assignment shortcuts 1.4"],[52,"py-io","Input, output and formatting text","Open chapter input() and conversion, split and map, f-strings, the format spec, tables, older formatting, safe input. Key ideas input() Shows a prompt, waits, returns text. Convert with int() or float(). a, b = map(int, input().split()). f-strings f\"{expr}\", {x!r}, {x = }, {x:spec}. {{ and }} for real brackets. Format spec [[fill]align][sign][#][0][width][grouping][.precision][type]. Width is a minimum. .2f rounds half to even. Older formatting \"%s %d\" % (a, b) and \"{} {}\".format(a, b). Prefer f-strings. Safe input while True + try int(text) except ValueError + range check + break. Formulas Format spec grammarExam favourite [[fill]align][sign][#][0][width][grouping][.precision][type] Every part is optional, but the order is fixed. align is < > ^ or =. Lesson: The format spec: width, alignment, decimals and more Common types d (int), f (fixed), e (scientific), % (percent), b o x X (bases), s (text) No type means the same as str() for most values. Lesson: The format spec: width, alignment, decimals and more Padding added by a widthExam favourite pad = max(0, width − len(text)) Width is a minimum. It never cuts the text. Lesson: The maths: widths and rounding Centre alignmentExam favourite left = ⌊pad / 2⌋, right = pad − left With an odd pad the extra character goes on the right. Lesson: The maths: widths and rounding Rounding to p places round half to even on the exact binary value f\"{0.125:.2f}\" is 0.12 because 0.125 is exact and 2 is even. Lesson: The maths: widths and rounding Digits of a number len(str(n)) = ⌊log10 n⌋ + 1 For a whole number n > 0. Lesson: The maths: widths and rounding Tables Table: you want · write · user types · you get You want Write User types You get Text name = input(\"Name: \") Asha \"Asha\" A whole number n = int(input(\"N: \")) 42 42 A decimal number x = float(input(\"X: \")) 3.5 3.5 Several numbers on one line a, b = map(int, input().split()) 4 9 4 and 9 Yes or no ok = input(\"y/n? \").strip().lower() == \"y\" Y True Lesson: input() always returns text Table: inside the brackets · meaning · example · gives Inside the brackets Meaning Example Gives {x} The value as str(x) f\"{3 * 4}\" 12 {x!r} repr(x), shows quotes on text f\"{'hi'!r}\" 'hi' {x = } The expression text, then the value (Python 3.8+) f\"{price = }\" price = 45.5 {x:spec} Formatted with a format spec f\"{3.14159:.2f}\" 3.14 {{ and }} A real curly bracket f\"{{x}}\" {x} Lesson: f-strings: the modern way to build text Table: · % formatting · str.format() · f-string % formatting str.format() f-string Looks like \"%s is %d\" % (name, age) \"{} is {}\".format(name, age) f\"{name} is {age}\" Since Python 1 (copied from C printf) Python 2.6 Python 3.6 Readability Values far from their places Better, numbered or named fields Best, values sit in place Speed Medium Slowest Fastest Still useful for logging messages: log.info(\"%s\", x) templates stored as data, built before the values exist almost everything else Lesson: Older ways: % formatting and str.format() Table: stream · python name · normally · used by Stream Python name Normally Used by Standard input sys.stdin The keyboard input() Standard output sys.stdout The screen print() Standard error sys.stderr The screen, often in red Tracebacks, print(..., file=sys.stderr) Lesson: Where input and output really go Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 412 Fizz Buzz Easy Modulo and building text Check divisible by 15 first, then 3, then 5. Build a list of texts. 709 To Lower Case Easy Character codes Without lower(): for \"A\" to \"Z\" add 32 to the character code with ord() and chr(). 1678 Goal Parser Interpretation Easy Text replace Replace \"()\" with \"o\" and then \"(al)\" with \"al\". 2864 Maximum Odd Binary Number Easy Counting and building text Keep one \"1\" for the end (odd). Put all other 1s at the front, then the 0s. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps Remember The Run button above feeds the typed answers (Asha and 25) for you. On your own computer, Python waits for you to type them.Lesson:"],[52,"py-io","Input, output and formatting text","Input and output: how a program talks to people Common mistake input() returns text even when the user types digits. \"5\" + \"5\" is \"55\". Convert first, then calculate.Lesson: input() always returns text Remember Since Python 3.12 (PEP 701) you may reuse the same quotes inside an f-string expression, for example f\"{\", \".join(names)}\". Older versions need the other kind of quote inside.Lesson: f-strings: the modern way to build text In the exam .2f rounds the stored binary value, and exact halves go to the even digit: f\"{2.5:.0f}\" gives 2 and f\"{2.675:.2f}\" gives 2.67.Lesson: The format spec: width, alignment, decimals and more Remember Loops arrive in Core 2.2 and try/except in Core 5.1. You can use this pattern now and understand every line later.Lesson: Reading input safely"],[52,"step-core-2","Step 2Core Python · Control flow","2.1"],[52,"py-conditions","Making decisions: if, elif, else and match","Open chapter if, elif, else, truthiness, combining conditions, conditional expression, walrus, match and case, De Morgan. Key ideas Chains if, any number of elif, optional else. First truthy condition wins, the rest are skipped. Strictest test first. Truthiness Falsy: False, None, 0, 0.0, \"\", empty collections. \"0\" and [0] are truthy. Combining and, or, not, 13 <= x <= 19, x in (a, b). x == a or b is always truthy. One liners a if cond else b. (n := len(x)) assigns inside an expression. match Literal, capture, _, |, [a, *rest], {\"k\": v}, Class(), guard if. A bare name captures. Formulas Conditional expressionExam favourite value_if_true if condition else value_if_false An expression, so it gives a value: kind = \"even\" if n % 2 == 0 else \"odd\". Lesson: One line choices: the conditional expression and the walrus Assignment expression (walrus) (name := expression) Python 3.8+. Assigns and gives the value in one go, useful inside if and while conditions. Lesson: One line choices: the conditional expression and the walrus De Morgan lawsExam favourite not (a and b) == (not a) or (not b), not (a or b) == (not a) and (not b) Use them to simplify or flip a condition. Lesson: The maths: boolean algebra Number of pathsExam favourite k independent if statements give up to 2k combinations An elif chain with k conditions and an else has exactly k + 1 paths. Lesson: The maths: boolean algebra Distributive law a and (b or c) == (a and b) or (a and c) Like a × (b + c) = ab + ac in school algebra. Lesson: The maths: boolean algebra Tables Table: · if / elif / else · separate if statements · nested if if / elif / else separate if statements nested if How many blocks can run Exactly one (or none without else) Any number Depends on the tree Use when The cases exclude each other (a grade) Several things can be true together (flags) A second question only makes sense after the first Risk Wrong order catches cases too early Several outputs when you wanted one Deep indentation that is hard to read Lesson: if, elif and else chains Table: falsy (counts as false) · truthy (counts as true) Falsy (counts as False) Truthy (counts as True) False, None True 0, 0.0, 0j any other number, even -2 and 0.001 \"\" (empty text) \"0\", \" \", \"False\" (non empty text) [], (), {}, set(), range(0) [0], (None,), {\"a\": 1} (non empty collections) Lesson: Truthy and falsy values Table: need · write · instead of Need Write Instead of Both must hold a and b a nested if inside if Either is enough a or b two ifs with the same block A range 13 <= age <= 19 age >= 13 and age <= 19 One of several values day in (\"Sat\", \"Sun\") day == \"Sat\" or day == \"Sun\" The opposite not done done == False Lesson: Combining conditions Table: pattern · fits · example Pattern Fits Example Literal A value equal to it case 404: Capture Anything, and binds it to the name case code: Wildcard Anything, binds nothing case _: Or Any of the alternatives case 200 | 201: Sequence A list or tuple of that length case [\"go\", direction]: Star Collects the rest of a sequence case [\"drop\", *things]: Mapping A dict with those keys (others allowed) case {\"x\": x, \"y\": y}: Class An instance of the class, with attributes case Point(x=0, y=y): Guard The pattern plus a condition case int(n) if n > 0: Lesson: match and case: pattern matching Table: a · b · not (a and b) · not a or not b a b not (a and b) not a or not b True True False False True False True True False True True True False False True True Lesson: The maths: boolean algebra Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1455 Check If a Word Occurs As a Prefix of Any Word in a Sentence Easy Condition inside a loop Split the sentence. Return the 1 based position of the first word that starts with searchWord, else -1. 2129 Capitalize the Title Easy if else on length Words of length 1 or 2 become all lower case. Longer words get a capital first letter and the rest lower. 1672 Richest Customer Wealth Easy Keep the biggest with if Add up each row."],[52,"py-conditions","Making decisions: if, elif, else and match","Keep the biggest sum seen so far with an if. 2678 Number of Senior Citizens Easy Condition on a slice Characters 11 and 12 of each passenger text are the age. Count ages above 60. 1344 Angle Between Hands of a Clock Medium Formula and min Minute hand at 6 × minutes degrees, hour hand at 30 × (hour % 12) + 0.5 × minutes. Return the smaller of the gap and 360 minus the gap. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps Remember Write if items: instead of if len(items) > 0:, and if not name: instead of if name == \"\":. PEP 8 recommends this short form. But write if x is None: when you really mean None, because 0 and \"\" are falsy too.Lesson: Truthy and falsy values Common mistake if day == \"Sat\" or \"Sun\": is always True. Python reads it as (day == \"Sat\") or \"Sun\", and \"Sun\" is truthy. Write day in (\"Sat\", \"Sun\").Lesson: Combining conditions Remember Do not chain many conditional expressions in one line. a if x else b if y else c is legal but hard to read. Use an if, elif, else statement instead.Lesson: One line choices: the conditional expression and the walrus Common mistake A plain name in a case is a capture, not a comparison. case RED: catches everything and binds it to RED. To compare with a constant, use a dotted name such as case Color.RED:.Lesson: match and case: pattern matching 2.2"],[52,"py-loops","Loops: while, for, range, break and continue","Open chapter while, for, range, break, continue, pass, loop else, loop patterns, nested loops, enumerate and zip, loop maths. Key ideas while and for while repeats while a condition is True. for repeats once per item of an iterable. range range(start, stop, step) excludes stop. len = max(0, ceil((stop - start)/step)). step 0 is an error. Loop control break leaves, continue skips, pass does nothing, loop else runs when no break happened. Patterns Accumulator, counter, running max, search with break, build a list. Nested loops: outer x inner turns. Pythonic loops enumerate(items, start=1), zip(a, b) stops at the shortest, reversed(), sorted(). Formulas range(start, stop, step)Exam favourite start, start + step, start + 2·step, ... while still before stop stop is never included. start defaults to 0, step to 1. step must not be 0. Lesson: for loops and range() How many numbers range givesExam favourite len = max(0, ⌈(stop − start) / step⌉) range(1, 10, 3) has ⌈9/3⌉ = 3 numbers: 1, 4, 7. Lesson: for loops and range() Turns of a nested loopExam favourite outer × inner 3 rows × 5 columns = 15 prints. Three nested loops of n each run n3 times. Lesson: Loops inside loops Triangle loopExam favourite 1 + 2 + ... + n = n(n + 1)/2 When the inner loop runs i times on turn i, the total is a triangular number. Lesson: Loops inside loops Length of a rangeExam favourite len(range(a, b, s)) = max(0, ⌈(b − a)/s⌉) Works for negative steps too: range(10, 0, -3) has ⌈-10/-3⌉ = 4 numbers. Lesson: The maths of loops Last number of a range a + (len − 1) · s range(0, 100, 7)[-1] = 0 + 14 × 7 = 98. Lesson: The maths of loops Sum of an arithmetic sequenceExam favourite S = n(first + last)/2 sum(range(1, 101)) = 100 × 101 / 2 = 5050 (the young Gauss story). Lesson: The maths of loops Turns to halve n down to 1 ⌊log2 n⌋ while n > 1: n //= 2 runs about log2 n times. This is why binary search is fast. Lesson: The maths of loops Digits of n (turns of the digit loop) ⌊log10 n⌋ + 1 The digit loop above ran 5 times for 90731. Lesson: The maths of loops Tables Table: statement · what it does · typical use Statement What it does Typical use break Leaves the innermost loop at once Stop searching once you found what you wanted continue Skips the rest of this turn and starts the next Ignore bad rows and carry on pass Does nothing. A placeholder where a statement is needed An empty block you will write later else (on a loop) Runs once after the loop if it was not stopped by break Say \"not found\" after a search Lesson: break, continue, pass and else Table: pattern · start with · each turn · built in shortcut Pattern Start with Each turn Built in shortcut Accumulator (sum) total = 0 total += x sum(items) Counter count = 0 if test: count += 1 sum(1 for x in items if test) Running maximum best = first item if x > best: best = x max(items) Search found = None if test: found = x and break next((x for x in items if test), None) Build a new list result = [] result.append(f(x)) [f(x) for x in items] (Core 4.3) Sentinel read a value while value != \"stop\": ... read again Lesson: Loop patterns every programmer uses Table: · index loop · enumerate Index loop enumerate Code for i in range(len(names)): print(i, names[i]) for i, name in enumerate(names): print(i, name) Reads Two steps: index, then look up Says what you mean Mistakes Off by one, wrong list in names[i] Fewer When you still need indexes Changing items in place, comparing names[i] with names[i + 1] Lesson: enumerate, zip, reversed and sorted Table: · for · while for while Number of turns Known: one per item Unknown: until a condition changes Stops by itself Yes, when items run out Only if your code makes the condition False Examples Each student, each line of a file, range(10) Digits of a number, asking until the input is valid, a game loop, Collatz Lesson: When to use while Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1480 Running Sum of 1d Array Easy Accumulator Keep a total. Each turn add the"],[52,"py-loops","Loops: while, for, range, break and continue","current number and append the total. 9 Palindrome Number Easy Digit loop with % and // Negative numbers are not palindromes. Build the reversed number digit by digit and compare. 1295 Find Numbers with Even Number of Digits Easy Counter Count the digits of each number with len(str(n)) and count the even ones. 7 Reverse Integer Medium Digit loop and range check Reverse the absolute value with a digit loop, put the sign back, and return 0 if it leaves the 32 bit range. 202 Happy Number Easy while loop with a seen set Replace n by the sum of the squares of its digits. Stop at 1 (happy) or when a number repeats (a cycle). 1137 N-th Tribonacci Number Easy Loop with three running values Keep the last three numbers a, b, c and move them forward n times. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps In the exam range(5) is 0, 1, 2, 3, 4: five numbers, but 5 itself is not included. This \"stop is excluded\" rule matches list indexes, so range(len(items)) gives every valid index.Lesson: for loops and range() In the exam The loop else is not \"else if the loop did not run\". It means \"no break happened\". A for loop over an empty list runs its else block.Lesson: break, continue, pass and else Remember Learn the loops first, then the shortcuts. In an interview you may be asked to write max() yourself, and the running maximum pattern is the answer.Lesson: Loop patterns every programmer uses Common mistake break only leaves the inner loop. To leave two loops at once, put them in a function and use return, or set a flag and check it in the outer loop.Lesson: Loops inside loops Common mistake zip stops at the shortest list. zip([1, 2, 3], \"ab\") gives only two pairs. Python 3.10 added zip(..., strict=True) to raise an error when the lengths differ.Lesson: enumerate, zip, reversed and sorted Remember An infinite loop is not always a bug: servers and games run while True: with a break or return inside. In the REPL or a terminal, press Ctrl+C to stop a runaway loop.Lesson: When to use while"],[52,"py-loops","Step 3Core Python · Data collections","3.1"],[52,"py-strings","Strings: text, slicing, methods and Unicode","Open chapter str, quotes, escapes, indexing, slicing, immutability, methods, split and join, Unicode, UTF-8, string maths. Key ideas Making strings Quotes: single, double or triple. Escapes start with a backslash. r\"...\" is a raw string. len() counts characters. Index and slice s[0] first, s[-1] last. s[a:b:c] includes a, excludes b. s[::-1] reverses. Slices never raise IndexError. Immutable You cannot change a character. Methods return new strings: write s = s.upper(). Key methods strip, split, join, replace, find (-1) vs index (ValueError), count, startswith, isdigit, isalpha, upper, lower. Unicode ord(\"A\") = 65, chr(97) = \"a\". UTF-8 uses 1 to 4 bytes per character. Compare strings by code point. Formulas Index of a characterExam favourite 0, 1, ..., len(s) − 1 from the left, and −1, −2, ..., −len(s) from the right s[-k] is the same as s[len(s) − k]. Lesson: Indexing and slicing SliceExam favourite s[start:stop:step] start is included, stop is not. Leave out start for \"from the beginning\" and stop for \"to the end\". Lesson: Indexing and slicing Length of a simple slice len(s[a:b]) = b − a when 0 ≤ a ≤ b ≤ len(s). \"PYTHON\"[1:4] is \"YTH\", 3 characters. Lesson: Indexing and slicing Number of substrings of a text of length nExam favourite n(n + 1) / 2 Pick a start and an end. \"abc\" has 6: a, b, c, ab, bc, abc. Lesson: The maths of strings Number of subsequences (keep or drop each letter) 2n Including the empty one. \"abc\" has 8. Lesson: The maths of strings Number of different arrangements (anagrams)Exam favourite n! / (c1! · c2! · ...) ci is how often each letter repeats. \"banana\": 6! / (3! · 2! · 1!) = 60. Lesson: The maths of strings Caesar shift of a lower case letter new = (ord(ch) − 97 + k) mod 26 + 97 mod 26 wraps z round to a. Lesson: The maths of strings Polynomial hash of a text (used in Rabin Karp search) h = (c0·bn−1 + c1·bn−2 + ... + cn−1) mod m Lets a program compare a window of text with a pattern in O(1) by sliding the hash one letter at a time. Lesson: The maths of strings Tables Table: you write · you get · name You write You get Name \\n a new line newline \\t a tab tab \\\\ one backslash backslash \\' and \\\" a quote inside quotes of the same kind quote \\u20b9 the character with code point 20B9 (the rupee sign) Unicode escape Lesson: Escape characters and raw strings Table: · str (immutable) · list of characters (mutable) str (immutable) list of characters (mutable) Change one character Not allowed: build a new string chars[0] = \"R\" works Safe to share Yes, nobody can change it behind your back No, every name sees the change Can be a dict key or set item Yes No Best for Storing and passing text around Many small edits, then \"\".join(chars) at the end Lesson: Strings never change Table: operation · example · result Operation Example Result + joins \"ab\" + \"cd\" \"abcd\" * repeats \"ha\" * 3 \"hahaha\" in searches \"cat\" in \"concatenate\" True < > compare \"apple\" < \"banana\" True ord() ord(\"A\") 65 chr() chr(97) \"a\" Lesson: Joining, repeating, searching and comparing Table: group · methods · note Group Methods Note Clean strip(), lstrip(), rstrip(), removeprefix(), removesuffix() strip() with no argument removes spaces, tabs and new lines at both ends Case upper(), lower(), title(), capitalize(), swapcase(), casefold() Use casefold() to compare text without caring about case Search find(), rfind(), index(), rindex(), count(), startswith(), endswith() find() gives −1 when not found, index() raises ValueError Test isdigit(), isalpha(), isalnum(), isspace(), isupper(), islower() Return True or False. An empty string gives False Change replace(old, new, count) Returns a new string Pad zfill(), center(), ljust(), rjust() Make columns of the same width Lesson: The most used string methods Table: · adding in a loop (s += piece) · \"sep\".join(pieces) Adding in a loop (s += piece) \"sep\".join(pieces) Code result = \"\" then result += w + \", \" each turn \", \".join(words) Extra separator at the end Yes, you must remove it No Speed Each += may copy"],[52,"py-strings","Strings: text, slicing, methods and Unicode","the whole text so far: up to O(n2) work Works out the size once and copies each piece once: O(n) Pieces must be Anything you convert with str() All strings already Lesson: split and join Table: code point range · utf-8 bytes · examples Code point range UTF-8 bytes Examples 0 to 127 1 A, z, 5, space 128 to 2,047 2 é, ñ, Greek and Arabic letters 2,048 to 65,535 3 ₹, Hindi and most Indian scripts, Chinese 65,536 to 1,114,111 4 emojis, old scripts Lesson: Unicode, code points and UTF-8 Table: · str · bytes · list of str str bytes list of str Holds Unicode characters Numbers 0 to 255 Any items, here single characters Written as \"café\" b\"caf\\xc3\\xa9\" [\"c\", \"a\", \"f\", \"é\"] Changeable No No (bytearray is) Yes len(\"café\") 4 characters 5 bytes in UTF-8 4 items Used for Text you read and show Files, images, network data Editing text one character at a time Lesson: str, bytes and lists of characters Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 344 Reverse String Easy Two pointers The input is a list of characters. Swap the first and last, then move both ends inward. 125 Valid Palindrome Easy Clean then compare Keep only letters and digits in lower case, then compare the text with its reverse. 58 Length of Last Word Easy split split() with no argument ignores extra spaces. Take the last word. 1768 Merge Strings Alternately Easy Index loop over the longer length Loop i up to the longer length and add word1[i] and word2[i] when they exist. 14 Longest Common Prefix Easy Compare column by column Take the first word. For each position, check that every other word has the same character there. 28 Find the Index of the First Occurrence in a String Easy Sliding window of slices Try every start i and compare haystack[i:i + len(needle)] with needle. str.find does this for you. 13 Roman to Integer Easy Look ahead one character Add each value, but subtract it when the next symbol is bigger (IV, IX, XL). 151 Reverse Words in a String Medium split and join split() removes the extra spaces. Reverse the list of words and join with one space. 6 Zigzag Conversion Medium Simulate rows Keep one string per row. Walk down and up the rows, adding each character to the current row. 5 Longest Palindromic Substring Medium Expand around the centre Every palindrome has a centre: a letter or the gap between two letters. Grow outwards from each of the 2n − 1 centres. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps Remember Single and double quotes make exactly the same string. Pick one style and keep it. Use the other kind when the text itself contains a quote.Lesson: What a string is In the exam A backslash starts an escape sequence: the backslash and the next character together stand for one special character. That is why len of the new line escape is 1. In a raw string (r before the quote) Python keeps every backslash, which is handy for Windows paths and regular expressions (Advanced 6.1).Lesson: Escape characters and raw strings In the exam A single index outside the string raises IndexError. A slice outside the string never fails: it just gives what is there, or an empty string.Lesson: Indexing and slicing Common mistake Strings are compared letter by letter by code point, not by dictionary order. All upper case letters (65 to 90) come before all lower case letters (97 to 122), so \"Zebra\" < \"apple\" is True. \"10\" < \"9\" is True too, because \"1\" comes before \"9\". Turn text into numbers before comparing numbers.Lesson: Joining, repeating, searching and comparing Common mistake Every method returns a new string. s.upper() alone on a line does nothing useful. Write s = s.upper() to keep the result.Lesson: The most used string methods In the exam split() with no argument and split(\" \") are different. \"a b\".split() gives [\"a\", \"b\"], but \"a b\".split(\" \") gives [\"a\", \"\", \"b\"] because there are two spaces.Lesson: split and join Remember Most text problems follow one plan: walk through the characters with for ch in text, test each one (isalpha, isdigit, a"],[52,"py-strings","Strings: text, slicing, methods and Unicode","comparison), and collect or count the ones you want. The Caesar cipher above uses ord() and chr() to do arithmetic on letters.Lesson: Working through text with loops Common mistake Always say the encoding when you open a text file: open(name, encoding=\"utf-8\"). Otherwise Python uses the system default, which is not UTF-8 on every Windows computer (Core 5.3).Lesson: Unicode, code points and UTF-8 3.2"],[52,"py-lists","Lists: storing, changing, sorting and copying","Open chapter list methods, sort vs sorted, aliases and copies, matrices, bubble sort, unpacking, list growth and costs. Key ideas Lists Ordered, changeable, any types. Index from 0, slice like strings. len, min, max, sum, in. Add and remove append (one item), extend (many), insert(i, x). remove(x) first match, pop() last, pop(i), del items[i]. Sorting sort() changes the list and returns None. sorted() returns a new list. key= and reverse=. Stable. Copies b = a is the same list. a[:] or a.copy() is a shallow copy. copy.deepcopy for nested lists. [[0]*n for _ in range(m)]. Costs append, pop(), index access O(1). insert(0), pop(0), in, remove O(n). Bubble sort n(n-1)/2 comparisons. Formulas Item of a matrixExam favourite matrix[row][col] The first index picks the row, the second the column. Lesson: Lists in lists: matrices Transpose T[c][r] = M[r][c] An r × c matrix becomes c × r. Lesson: Lists in lists: matrices Number of items rows × cols A 3D list (a cube) has rows × cols × depth items. Lesson: Lists in lists: matrices Comparisons in the worst caseExam favourite (n − 1) + (n − 2) + ... + 1 = n(n − 1)/2 5 items need at most 10 comparisons, 1,000 items about 500,000. That is O(n2). Lesson: Bubble sort, step by step Best case with the early stop n − 1 comparisons An already sorted list needs one pass. Lesson: Bubble sort, step by step Average cost of an append (amortised)Exam favourite (n + total copies) / n < a small constant With growth by a factor g > 1 the copies form a geometric series n/g + n/g2 + ... < n/(g − 1). Lesson: The maths of lists Bubble sort comparisonsExam favourite n(n − 1)/2 The same sum as the triangle loop of Core 2.2. Lesson: The maths of lists Number of slices items[a:b] with a < b C(n + 1, 2) = n(n + 1)/2 Choose 2 of the n + 1 cut points. Lesson: The maths of lists Mean of a list sum(items) / len(items) Undefined for an empty list: guard against ZeroDivisionError. Lesson: The maths of lists Binary search steps on a sorted list ⌊log2 n⌋ + 1 A sorted list of 1,000,000 items needs at most 20 checks with bisect (Advanced 4.2). Lesson: The maths of lists Tables Table: method · what it does · cost Method What it does Cost append(x) Add x at the end O(1) on average insert(i, x) Add x before index i, the rest move right O(n) extend(items) Add every item of another iterable at the end O(k) for k new items items[i] = x Replace one item O(1) items[a:b] = other Replace a slice with any number of items O(n) Lesson: Adding and changing items Table: way · removes · gives back · if missing Way Removes Gives back If missing remove(x) The first item equal to x None ValueError pop() The last item The item IndexError on an empty list pop(i) The item at index i The item IndexError del items[i] or del items[a:b] By index or slice Nothing (it is a statement) IndexError for a single index clear() Everything None Never fails Lesson: Removing items Table: · items.sort() · sorted(items) items.sort() sorted(items) Changes the original Yes No Returns None A new list Works on Lists only Any iterable: tuples, strings, sets, dict keys Options key=..., reverse=True key=..., reverse=True Lesson: Sorting: sort() and sorted() Table: way · new outer list · new inner lists Way New outer list New inner lists b = a No No a[:], a.copy(), list(a) Yes No (shallow copy) copy.deepcopy(a) Yes Yes, at every level Lesson: Shallow copy, deep copy and lists of lists Table: · list · tuple · set list tuple set dict Order kept Yes Yes No Yes (insertion order) Can change Yes No Yes Yes Repeats allowed Yes Yes No Keys no, values yes Find an item O(n) O(n) O(1) average O(1) average by key Written as [1, 2] (1, 2) {1, 2} {\"a\": 1} Best for A changing sequence A fixed record Unique items and membership Looking up a value by a key Lesson: Lists compared with other collections Table: operation · time Operation Time items[i], items[i] = x, len, append, pop() O(1) insert(0, x), pop(0), remove(x), x in items, index(x) O(n) sort(), sorted() O(n log n) items[a:b] (copy"],[52,"py-lists","Lists: storing, changing, sorting and copying","of k items) O(k) Lesson: Lists compared with other collections Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1929 Concatenation of Array Easy List repetition The answer is the list followed by itself. 66 Plus One Easy Walk from the right with a carry Start at the last digit. A 9 becomes 0 and the carry moves left. If every digit was 9, put a 1 in front. 27 Remove Element Easy Two pointers (read and write) Keep a write position. Copy every item that is not val to the write position. Return how many you kept. 26 Remove Duplicates from Sorted Array Easy Two pointers The list is sorted, so repeats sit next to each other. Write an item only when it differs from the last one written. 283 Move Zeroes Easy Two pointers Move every non zero item forward to a write position, then fill the rest with zeros. 121 Best Time to Buy and Sell Stock Easy Running minimum Keep the lowest price so far. Each day, check the profit if you sold today. 88 Merge Sorted Array Easy Three pointers from the back Fill nums1 from its end. Each time place the bigger of the two last items. 118 Pascal's Triangle Easy Build each row from the one above Each row starts and ends with 1. Every inner number is the sum of the two numbers above it. 189 Rotate Array Medium Three reversals Reverse the whole list, then reverse the first k items and the rest. Use k % n first. 75 Sort Colors Medium Dutch national flag (three pointers) Keep low, mid and high. A 0 swaps to low, a 2 swaps to high, a 1 just moves mid on. 48 Rotate Image Medium Transpose, then reverse each row Swap matrix[r][c] with matrix[c][r] above the diagonal, then reverse every row. 54 Spiral Matrix Medium Shrinking borders Keep top, bottom, left and right borders. Walk right, down, left and up, moving a border in after each side. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps In the exam append adds one item, even if that item is a list. [1, 2].append([3, 4]) gives [1, 2, [3, 4]]. extend adds the items one by one and gives [1, 2, 3, 4].Lesson: Adding and changing items Common mistake Do not remove items from a list while a for loop is walking over it. The loop skips the item after each removal. Loop over a copy (for x in items[:]) or build a new list instead.Lesson: Removing items Remember x in items checks each item from the left until it finds a match, so it takes O(n) time. For many lookups in a big collection use a set or a dict (Core 3.3 and 3.4), which answer in O(1) on average.Lesson: Finding items In the exam [[0] * 3] * 3 makes one inner list and three references to it, so changing one row changes all three. Build a grid with a comprehension: [[0] * 3 for _ in range(3)].Lesson: Shallow copy, deep copy and lists of lists Remember Never use bubble sort in real code. sorted() is far faster. Learn it to understand sorting and to answer exam questions.Lesson: Bubble sort, step by step In the exam The number of names must match the number of items, unless one name has a star. a, b = [1, 2, 3] raises ValueError: too many values to unpack.Lesson: Unpacking, swapping and a first look at comprehensions Remember pop(0) and insert(0, x) move every other item. For a queue that adds at one end and removes at the other, use collections.deque, which does both in O(1) (Advanced 4.2).Lesson: Lists compared with other collections 3.3"],[52,"py-tuples-sets","Tuples and sets: fixed records and unique items","Open chapter tuples, packing and unpacking, hashable keys, namedtuple, sets, set algebra, deduplication, O(1) membership, set maths. Key ideas Tuples Immutable ordered sequence. (5,) is a tuple, (5) is not. count and index only. Unpack with a, b = t. Hashable Dict keys and set items must be hashable: numbers, strings, tuples of hashables, frozensets. Not lists, sets or dicts. Sets Unique items, no order. set() is empty. add, remove (KeyError), discard (safe), pop (any item). Set algebra | union, & intersection, - difference, ^ symmetric difference, <= subset. Methods accept any iterable. Counting |A u B| = |A| + |B| - |A n B|. A set of n items has 2^n subsets. x in set is O(1) on average. Formulas Size of a union (inclusion and exclusion)Exam favourite |A ∪ B| = |A| + |B| − |A ∩ B| The middle part was counted twice, so take it away once. Lesson: The maths of sets Three sets |A ∪ B ∪ C| = |A| + |B| + |C| − |A ∩ B| − |A ∩ C| − |B ∩ C| + |A ∩ B ∩ C| Add singles, take away pairs, add back the triple. Lesson: The maths of sets Symmetric difference |A Δ B| = |A| + |B| − 2|A ∩ B| A ^ B in Python. Lesson: The maths of sets Number of subsets (the power set)Exam favourite 2n Each item is in or out. {1, 2, 3} has 8 subsets. Lesson: The maths of sets De Morgan's laws not (A or B) = (not A) and (not B) For sets inside a universe U: U − (A ∪ B) = (U − A) ∩ (U − B). Lesson: The maths of sets Tables Table: you can · example You can Example Index and slice t[0], t[-1], t[1:] Join and repeat (1, 2) + (3,), (0,) * 4 Search x in t, t.index(x), t.count(x) Loop and unpack for a, b in pairs, x, y = point Not: append, remove, sort, item assignment Use sorted(t) to get a sorted list Lesson: Making and using tuples Table: · tuple · list tuple list Brackets ( ) [ ] Can change No Yes Hashable (dict key, set item) Yes, if every item is hashable No Memory for (1, 2, 3) 72 bytes on CPython 3.14 88 bytes Methods count, index about 11, including append and sort Meaning A fixed record: (name, age), (x, y) Many items of the same kind that grows or shrinks Lesson: Tuple or list Table: method · does · if the item is missing Method Does If the item is missing add(x) Adds x (nothing happens if it is already there) remove(x) Removes x KeyError discard(x) Removes x Nothing happens pop() Removes and returns some item (you cannot choose which) KeyError on an empty set update(items) Adds every item of an iterable clear() Empties the set Lesson: Making sets and changing them Table: operator · method · means Operator Method Means A | B A.union(B) In A or B or both A & B A.intersection(B) In both A − B A.difference(B) In A but not in B A ^ B A.symmetric_difference(B) In exactly one of them A <= B A.issubset(B) Every item of A is in B A < B Subset and not equal A.isdisjoint(B) No item in common Lesson: Union, intersection, difference Table: · x in list · x in set x in list x in set Average time O(n) O(1) Keeps order and repeats Yes No Items must be Anything Hashable Extra memory Less More (the table has empty slots) Lesson: Why sets are fast Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 217 Contains Duplicate Easy Seen set Add each number to a set. If it is already there, you found a duplicate. 349 Intersection of Two Arrays Easy Set intersection Turn both lists into sets and take the intersection. 771 Jewels and Stones Easy Set membership Put the jewel letters in a set, then count the stones that are in it. 1832 Check if the Sentence Is Pangram Easy Set size A pangram uses every letter a to z. The set of its letters has 26 items. 268 Missing Number Easy Gauss sum The numbers 0 to n add up to n(n + 1)/2. The missing one is that total minus the sum of the list. 2215 Find the Difference of Two Arrays Easy Set difference A − B and B − A answer the two parts. 1436 Destination City Easy Set difference The destination is a city that appears as an end but never as a start. 645 Set Mismatch Easy Set and Gauss sum The repeated number is sum(nums) −"],[52,"py-tuples-sets","Tuples and sets: fixed records and unique items","sum(set(nums)). The missing number is n(n + 1)/2 − sum(set(nums)). 3 Longest Substring Without Repeating Characters Medium Sliding window with a set Grow the window on the right. While the new letter is already in the window, drop letters from the left. 128 Longest Consecutive Sequence Medium Set, start only at the beginning of a run Put all numbers in a set. Start counting only from x where x − 1 is not in the set, so each run is walked once: O(n). Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps In the exam The comma makes a tuple, not the brackets. (5) is the number 5. (5,) is a tuple with one item. This is a favourite exam trick.Lesson: Making and using tuples Common mistake A tuple holds references. You cannot change which objects it holds, but if one of them is a list, that list can still change. Such a tuple is not hashable, so it cannot be a dict key.Lesson: What \"cannot change\" means Remember A named tuple gives each position a name, so p.x is clearer than p[0]. For records with many fields or that need to change, use a dataclass (Advanced 1.3).Lesson: Tuples as keys, and named tuples In the exam {} is an empty dict, not an empty set. Write set() for an empty set. Also, a set can only hold hashable items: numbers, strings, tuples of them, but not lists or other sets.Lesson: Making sets and changing them In the exam The operators need two sets. The methods accept any iterable, so A.union([1, 2]) works but A | [1, 2] raises TypeError.Lesson: Union, intersection, difference Remember set(items) is the fastest way to remove repeats, but the order is lost. If order matters, use list(dict.fromkeys(items)), which keeps the first time each item appears.Lesson: Removing duplicates Remember A frozenset is a set that cannot change. Because it never changes it is hashable, so it can be a dict key or an item of another set.Lesson: frozenset and set comprehensions 3.4"],[52,"py-dicts","Dictionaries: keys, values and hash tables","Open chapter dict basics, get and setdefault, keys values items, counting, grouping, nesting and JSON, merging, hash table internals. Key ideas Dicts Key to value mapping. Keys unique and hashable, values anything. Ordered by insertion since 3.7. Read safely d[k] raises KeyError. d.get(k, default) does not. k in d checks keys only. Change d[k] = v adds or replaces. del d[k], d.pop(k, default), d.popitem() (last added), update, |, |=. Loop for k in d (keys), d.values(), d.items(). Do not add or remove keys while looping, use list(d). Patterns Count: d[k] = d.get(k, 0) + 1 or Counter. Group: dict of lists or defaultdict(list). Two Sum: dict of seen values. Formulas Slot of a keyExam favourite slot = hash(key) mod table size Table sizes are powers of 2, so this is just the last few bits of the hash. Lesson: The maths of hashing Load factorExam favourite α = used slots / table size ≤ 2/3 A CPython dict with 8 slots holds at most 5 keys. The 6th key makes it grow. Lesson: The maths of hashing New table size smallest power of 2 ≥ 3 × (number of keys) When the 6th key arrives, 5 keys are stored: 3 × 5 = 15, so the new size is 16. Lesson: The maths of hashing Expected probes for a lookup that hits (uniform hashing) about (1/α) ln(1/(1 − α)) At α = 2/3 this is about 1.6 probes, so lookups stay fast. Lesson: The maths of hashing Chance that two of k keys share a slot of m (birthday problem) 1 − e−k(k − 1)/(2m) With 23 people and 365 days the chance of a shared birthday is above 50 percent. Collisions are normal, so tables must handle them. Lesson: The maths of hashing Tables Table: code · does · if the key is missing Code Does If the key is missing d[k] = v Adds k, or replaces its value Adds it del d[k] Removes k KeyError d.pop(k) Removes k and returns its value KeyError d.pop(k, default) Same, with a default Returns default d.popitem() Removes and returns the last added pair KeyError on an empty dict d.update(other) Adds or replaces every pair of other Adds them d.clear() Empties the dict Lesson: Adding, changing and removing Table: · d[k] · d.get(k, default) · k in d first d[k] d.get(k, default) k in d first Missing key KeyError Returns default (None if not given) You decide in an if Use when The key must be there and a missing key is a bug A missing key is normal You need different code for the two cases Lesson: Safe lookups: get and setdefault Table: way · result Way Result a | b A new dict. For equal keys the value from b wins a |= b or a.update(b) Changes a in place {**a, **b} A new dict, works before Python 3.9 {k: v for ...} A dict comprehension dict.fromkeys(keys, value) Every key gets the same value dict(zip(keys, values)) Pairs keys and values from two lists Lesson: Merging, comprehensions and other ways to build Table: · two lists (names, marks) · list of pairs · dict two lists (names, marks) list of pairs dict Get Ravi's mark Find the index in names, then marks[i]: O(n) Loop until the name matches: O(n) marks[\"Ravi\"]: O(1) Add a student Append to both lists and keep them in step Append a pair marks[\"Zara\"] = 80 Repeated names Allowed (can be a bug) Allowed Not possible, the key is unique Keeps order Yes Yes Yes (insertion order) Lesson: dict compared with list and other mappings Table: tool · use it for Tool Use it for dict Any key to value lookup collections.Counter Counting things collections.defaultdict Grouping, when missing keys need a starting value collections.OrderedDict Moving keys to the end (move_to_end), an LRU cache types.MappingProxyType A read only view of a dict Lesson: dict compared with list and other mappings Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1 Two Sum Easy Dict of seen values For each number x, the partner is target − x. Keep a dict from value to index of the numbers seen so far. 242 Valid Anagram Easy Count letters Two words are anagrams when every letter appears the same number of times. 387 First Unique Character in a String Easy Count, then scan again First count"],[52,"py-dicts","Dictionaries: keys, values and hash tables","every letter. Then walk the text again and return the first index whose letter count is 1. 383 Ransom Note Easy Count and spend Count the magazine letters. Each letter of the note uses one up. 1512 Number of Good Pairs Easy Count as you go Each new copy of a value makes a pair with every earlier copy, so add the count so far before increasing it. 169 Majority Element Easy Count, or Boyer Moore voting Count each value and return the one seen more than n/2 times. The O(1) memory trick is the Boyer Moore vote. 205 Isomorphic Strings Easy Two dicts, one per direction Each letter of s must always map to the same letter of t, and no two letters may map to the same one. 290 Word Pattern Easy Two dicts, one per direction Split the text into words. Then it is Isomorphic Strings with letters on one side and words on the other. 49 Group Anagrams Medium Group by a sorted key Anagrams have the same letters in sorted order. Use that sorted text as the dict key and a list of words as the value. 347 Top K Frequent Elements Medium Count, then bucket by count Count each number. Put numbers into buckets by their count, then read the buckets from the highest count down. 560 Subarray Sum Equals K Medium Prefix sums in a dict A part from i to j sums to k when prefix[j] − prefix[i] = k. Count how often each prefix sum has appeared. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps In the exam for x in d gives the keys, not the values and not the pairs. Use d.values() for values and d.items() for (key, value) pairs. x in d also checks keys only.Lesson: Looping over a dict Common mistake keys(), values() and items() return views: windows onto the dict that update by themselves. Adding or removing keys while you loop over a dict raises RuntimeError. Loop over list(d) instead.Lesson: Views, and changing a dict while looping Remember Counting is the most common use of a dict. Learn the get(word, 0) + 1 line by heart, then use collections.Counter in real code. Counter also adds, subtracts and finds the most common items.Lesson: Counting with a dict Remember A dict of dicts and lists looks exactly like JSON, the text format that web APIs use. json.dumps turns a dict into JSON text and json.loads turns it back. The web part of this subject uses this all the time.Lesson: Nested dicts and JSON Common mistake dict.fromkeys(keys, []) gives every key the same list object. Appending to one changes all of them. Use a comprehension {k: [] for k in keys} instead.Lesson: Merging, comprehensions and other ways to build In the exam Keys are compared with ==. Because 1 == 1.0 == True and they have the same hash, they are the same key: the dict keeps the first key object and the last value. Use one type for your keys.Lesson: What can be a key"],[52,"step-core-4","Step 4Core Python · Functions","4.1"],[52,"py-functions","Functions: def, return and every kind of argument","Open chapter def, return, None, argument kinds, *args, **kwargs, / and *, unpacking, mutable defaults, docstrings, first-class functions. Key ideas def and return def name(params): body. return value leaves the function. No return gives None. print is not return. Argument kinds Positional by order, keyword by name, positional before keyword. Defaults make parameters optional. Star parameters *args tuple of extra positionals, **kwargs dict of extra keywords. Bare * means keyword-only after it, / means positional-only before it. Mutable default Defaults are made once at def time. Use None, then create the list inside. Functions are objects Assign them, store them, pass them: sorted(key=f), map(f, items). Docstring on the first line, type hints are not enforced. Formulas A function maps each input to one outputExam favourite f: X → Y, x ↦ f(x) area(width, height) maps each pair of numbers to one number. Lesson: Functions in maths and in code CompositionExam favourite (f ∘ g)(x) = f(g(x)) apply_twice(f, x) is f(f(x)). len(str(n)) is len ∘ str. Lesson: Functions in maths and in code Idempotent function f(f(x)) = f(x) abs, str.strip and sorted are idempotent: doing them twice changes nothing more. Lesson: Functions in maths and in code How many functions from a set of m inputs to n outputs nm Each of the m inputs can go to any of the n outputs. From {1, 2, 3} to {a, b}: 23 = 8. Lesson: Functions in maths and in code Arguments a call may give with p positional parameters, d of them with defaults and no *args between p − d and p positional arguments describe has p = 3, d = 1, so 2 or 3 positional arguments. Lesson: Functions in maths and in code Tables Table: · print(x) · return x print(x) return x What it does Shows x on the screen Gives x back to the code that called the function Can the caller use the value No, it is gone Yes: store it, add it, pass it on The call evaluates to None x Stops the function No Yes, at once Lesson: return, print and None Table: kind of argument · example call · rule Kind of argument Example call Rule Positional describe(\"Ravi\", 23) Matched by position, left to right Keyword describe(age=30, name=\"Meera\") Matched by name, any order Mixed describe(\"John\", city=\"Goa\", age=40) All positional arguments must come before any keyword argument Default value def describe(..., city=\"Pune\") Used when the caller gives nothing for that parameter Lesson: Positional, keyword and default arguments Table: in the def line · meaning · inside the function it is In the def line Meaning Inside the function it is a Positional or keyword The value a=1 Optional, with a default The value or the default *args Collects extra positional arguments A tuple **kwargs Collects extra keyword arguments A dict * alone Every parameter after it is keyword-only / Every parameter before it is positional-only (Python 3.8+) Lesson: *args, **kwargs, keyword-only and positional-only Table: · copy and paste · function · method (core 6.1) Copy and paste Function Method (Core 6.1) Module (Core 5.2) Defined with Nothing def def inside a class a .py file Fix a bug in Every copy One place One place One place Called as name(args) obj.name(args) module.name(args) Best for Never One job A job tied to an object Sharing many functions between programs Lesson: Kinds of reusable code compared Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1342 Number of Steps to Reduce a Number to Zero Easy Loop inside a function While num > 0: halve it if even, else take away 1. Count the steps and return the count. 1920 Build Array from Permutation Easy Index into a list ans[i] is nums[nums[i]]. Build a new list and return it. 1470 Shuffle the Array Easy Two halves side by side Walk i from 0 to n − 1 and add nums[i] then nums[i + n]. 2011 Final Value of Variable After Performing Operations Easy A helper decides plus or minus Every operation contains either \"++\" or \"--\". Add 1 or take away 1. 1365 How Many Numbers Are Smaller Than the Current Number Easy"],[52,"py-functions","Functions: def, return and every kind of argument","Helper function per item Write a small helper that counts the numbers smaller than x, then call it for every item. 1431 Kids With the Greatest Number of Candies Easy Find the max once Work out max(candies) once. Kid i can be the greatest if candies[i] + extra reaches it. 2652 Sum Multiples Easy Small test function Add every number from 1 to n that divides by 3, 5 or 7. 1323 Maximum 69 Number Easy Change the first 6 Turn the number into text, replace the first 6 with a 9, and turn it back. 2160 Minimum Sum of Four Digit Number After Splitting Digits Easy Sort the digits Sort the 4 digits a ≤ b ≤ c ≤ d. The best split is (10a + c) + (10b + d). 1641 Count Sorted Vowel Strings Medium Maths in a function A sorted string of length n over 5 vowels is a choice of n items from 5 with repeats: C(n + 4, 4). Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps In the exam A parameter is the name in the def line (name, width). An argument is the value you pass in the call (\"Asha\", 4). Exams use both words, so learn the difference.Lesson: What a function is In the exam A function with no return, or with a bare return, gives back None. That is why a = show_double(5) printed 10 but left a as None.Lesson: return, print and None In the exam describe(name=\"Sara\", 25) is a SyntaxError: a positional argument may not follow a keyword argument. Python refuses to even start the program.Lesson: Positional, keyword and default arguments Remember The full order is: positional-only, /, normal, *args (or a bare *), keyword-only, **kwargs. The names args and kwargs are only a habit. What matters is the * and **.Lesson: *args, **kwargs, keyword-only and positional-only In the exam In a def line, * and ** collect arguments. In a call, * and ** spread a list or dict out into separate arguments. Same symbols, opposite jobs.Lesson: Spreading lists and dicts into a call Remember A docstring is a string on the first line of the body. help(bmi) and editors show it. Type hints such as height_m: float and -> float tell readers and tools like mypy what you expect, but Python itself does not check them when the program runs (Advanced 3.1).Lesson: Docstrings and type hints Remember A function name is just a variable that holds a function object. You can give it another name, store it in a list or dict, and pass it to another function. Functions that take or return functions are called higher-order functions. Core 4.3 uses this with sorted, map, filter and closures.Lesson: Functions are objects too 4.2"],[52,"py-scope-recursion","Scope and recursion: LEGB, closures and the call stack","Open chapter scope, LEGB, global, nonlocal, closures, recursion, call stack, recursion limit, memoisation, recurrences. Key ideas Scopes Local (inside a call), Enclosing, Global (module), Built-in: LEGB. Assigning makes a name local in the whole function. global and nonlocal global x assigns the module x. nonlocal x assigns the enclosing function x. Reading needs neither. Closures An inner function that remembers enclosing names (cells). Used for counters, factories and decorators. Recursion Base case + smaller recursive case. Each call is a frame. Limit about 1000, RecursionError. Costs T(n) = T(n-1) + c is O(n). Naive fib 2F(n+1) - 1 calls, lru_cache makes it n + 1. Hanoi 2^n - 1. Fast power O(log n). Formulas Recurrence for a simple recursionExam favourite T(n) = T(n − 1) + c gives T(n) = O(n) factorial and countdown do constant work and then one smaller call. Lesson: The maths of recursion Calls made by naive fib(n)Exam favourite C(n) = C(n − 1) + C(n − 2) + 1 = 2F(n + 1) − 1 fib(5) makes 2 × 8 − 1 = 15 calls, as the animation counts. Lesson: The maths of recursion Golden ratio growth F(n) ≈ φn / √5, φ = (1 + √5)/2 ≈ 1.618 So naive fib is exponential: about 1.6n calls. Lesson: The maths of recursion Tower of Hanoi movesExam favourite M(n) = 2M(n − 1) + 1 = 2n − 1 3 discs need 7 moves, 64 discs need about 1.8 × 1019. Lesson: The maths of recursion Fast power T(n) = T(n/2) + c gives T(n) = O(log n) power(2, 1000) needs only about 10 levels of calls. Lesson: The maths of recursion Master theorem (divide and conquer) T(n) = a·T(n/b) + O(nd) If d > logb a: O(nd). If equal: O(nd log n). If less: O(nlogb a). Merge sort: a = 2, b = 2, d = 1 gives O(n log n). Lesson: The maths of recursion Tables Table: letter · scope · made by Letter Scope Made by L Local Assignments and parameters inside the current function call E Enclosing The local names of any outer function, for a function inside a function G Global Assignments at the top level of the module (the .py file) B Built-in Names Python gives you: print, len, range, int, ValueError ... Lesson: The LEGB rule Table: · global variable · pass in and return global variable pass in and return Code global visits then visits += 1 total = next_count(total) Who can change it Any function, from anywhere Only code that gets the returned value Testing Hard: the result depends on hidden state Easy: same input, same output Good for Settings read in many places, small scripts Almost everything else Lesson: The global statement Table: part · factorial · question to ask Part factorial Question to ask Base case if n <= 1: return 1 What is the smallest input I can answer at once? Recursive case return n * factorial(n - 1) How does the answer use the answer for a smaller input? Progress n − 1 is smaller than n Does every call get closer to the base case? Lesson: Recursion: base case and recursive case Table: · recursion · loop Recursion Loop Fits best Nested data: trees, folders, JSON, divide and conquer Walking a list, counting, simple repeats Memory One frame per pending call: O(depth) Usually O(1) Depth limit in Python About 1,000 None Speed in CPython Slower (each call has a cost) Faster Readability Very clear when the problem is recursive Very clear when it is a sequence Lesson: Recursion or a loop Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 509 Fibonacci Number Easy Recursion with memo or a loop F(0) = 0, F(1) = 1. Use two names a and b and move them forward n times. 70 Climbing Stairs Easy Recurrence ways(n) = ways(n − 1) + ways(n − 2) The last step was 1 or 2 stairs. It is Fibonacci shifted by one. 326 Power of Three Easy Divide while you can Keep dividing by 3 while n % 3 == 0. A power of three ends at 1. 342 Power of Four Easy Recursive halving Base cases 1 (True) and anything not divisible by 4 (False). Otherwise ask the same question for n // 4. 1688 Count of Matches in Tournament Easy Recursion, then a maths shortcut Each round plays n // 2 matches and n − n // 2"],[52,"py-scope-recursion","Scope and recursion: LEGB, closures and the call stack","teams go on. In the end every team but one loses once: n − 1 matches. 50 Pow(x, n) Medium Fast power by halving xn = (xn/2)2, times x when n is odd. For negative n use 1 / x−n. 22 Generate Parentheses Medium Backtracking Build the text one bracket at a time. Add \"(\" while you have some left, and \")\" only when it closes an open one. 46 Permutations Medium Backtracking Pick each unused number for the next place, recurse, then undo the choice. 78 Subsets Medium Take it or leave it For each number, make two recursive calls: one that takes it and one that skips it. 779 K-th Symbol in Grammar Medium Recursion on the parent Symbol k of row n comes from symbol (k + 1) // 2 of row n − 1. An odd k copies the parent, an even k flips it. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps In the exam Assigning to a name inside a function makes a new local name, even if a global with the same name exists. This is called shadowing. Changing an object (items.append) is different from rebinding a name (items = [...]): the first is seen by the caller, the second is not.Lesson: Local names hide global names In the exam Python decides which names are local when it compiles the function, not while it runs. If a name is assigned anywhere in the function, it is local in the whole function.Lesson: The LEGB rule Remember The fix is either to rename the local variable, to pass count in as a parameter, or, if you really mean the global, to add global count at the top of the function.Lesson: UnboundLocalError In the exam global reaches the module scope. nonlocal reaches the nearest enclosing function scope and is a SyntaxError at the top level. Decorators (Advanced 1.2) are built from closures.Lesson: nonlocal and closures Common mistake Each call uses a frame on the call stack. To protect the program, CPython stops at about 1,000 frames deep with RecursionError. sys.setrecursionlimit can raise the limit, but a recursion that deep is usually better written as a loop.Lesson: The recursion limit Remember total(items[1:]) is clear but copies the list each time, which makes it O(n2). Real code uses sum(). Fast power is the opposite: halving the exponent makes it O(log n), which is how pow(a, b, m) handles huge numbers in cryptography.Lesson: Classic recursive functions 4.3"],[52,"py-functional","lambda, map, filter and comprehensions","Open chapter lambda, key functions, map, filter, reduce, list, dict and set comprehensions, generator expressions, higher-order functions. Key ideas lambda lambda args: expression. One expression, no statements. Best as an argument, def otherwise. key functions sorted/min/max(items, key=f). Tuple keys sort by several fields. -x reverses a number key. map, filter, reduce Lazy iterators: list(map(f, a)), list(filter(test, a)). functools.reduce(f, a, start) folds to one value. Comprehensions [expr for x in a if test], [a if t else b for x in s], {k: v for ...}, {x for ...}. Outer for first. Generator expressions (expr for x in a): lazy, one use, tiny memory. sum(x*x for x in a), any(...), all(...). Formulas List comprehensionExam favourite [expression for item in iterable if condition] Read it as: the expression, for each item, but only if the condition holds. Lesson: List comprehensions With if-elseExam favourite [a if test else b for item in iterable] The if-else is part of the expression, so it comes before for. A filter if comes after. Lesson: List comprehensions Set builder notationExam favourite { f(x) | x ∈ S, p(x) } Maths wrote \"the set of f(x) for x in S where p(x)\" long before Python. {f(x) for x in S if p(x)} is the same idea. Lesson: The maths behind comprehensions map is f applied to each item map(f, [a1, ..., an]) = [f(a1), ..., f(an)] The length does not change. Lesson: The maths behind comprehensions reduce is a foldExam favourite reduce(⊕, [a1, a2, a3], s) = ((s ⊕ a1) ⊕ a2) ⊕ a3 With + this is the sum, with max it is the maximum. Lesson: The maths behind comprehensions Size of a comprehension with two for clauses |A| × |B| [(x, y) for x in range(1, 4) for y in \"ab\"] has 3 × 2 = 6 pairs, the Cartesian product A × B. Lesson: The maths behind comprehensions Sum of squares 12 + 22 + ... + n2 = n(n + 1)(2n + 1)/6 sum(n * n for n in range(1, 11)) = 10 × 11 × 21 / 6 = 385. Lesson: The maths behind comprehensions Tables Table: · lambda · def lambda def Body One expression, its value is returned Any statements, return when you want Name Anonymous (shown as <lambda>) Has a real name for errors and tracebacks Docstring and type hints No Yes Best for A tiny throw away function passed to sorted, max, map Anything you name, reuse or test Lesson: lambda: a function in one expression Table: tool · takes · gives · comprehension version Tool Takes Gives Comprehension version map(f, items) A function and one or more iterables A lazy iterator of f(x) [f(x) for x in items] filter(test, items) A test function (or None) and an iterable A lazy iterator of the x where test(x) is true [x for x in items if test(x)] reduce(f, items, start) A function of two values One value A loop with an accumulator, or sum, max, min Lesson: map, filter and reduce Table: brackets · builds · example Brackets Builds Example [ ] list [w.upper() for w in words] { } with key: value dict {w: len(w) for w in words} { } with one value set {w[0] for w in words} ( ) generator (lazy, not a tuple) (len(w) for w in words) Lesson: Dict and set comprehensions Table: · [... for ...] · (... for ...) [... for ...] (... for ...) Makes The whole list now One value at a time, when asked Memory for a million items Megabytes A couple of hundred bytes Use again Yes, as often as you like No, once only Index, len, slicing Yes No Best for Results you keep or reuse Feeding sum, max, any, all, join, or a for loop once Lesson: Generator expressions: comprehensions without the list Table: · for loop · comprehension · map and filter for loop comprehension map and filter Code result = [] then for n in nums: if n % 2 == 0: result.append(n * n) [n * n for n in nums if n % 2 == 0] list(map(lambda n: n * n, filter(lambda n: n % 2 == 0, nums))) Lines 4 1 1 Reads like Instructions A sentence Nested calls, inside out Speed in CPython Slowest Fast Fast with built-in functions, slower with lambdas Good when Several steps, prints, try and except in the body One expression and an optional filter You"],[52,"py-functional","lambda, map, filter and comprehensions","already have a named function: map(str.upper, words) Lesson: Loop, comprehension or map Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 977 Squares of a Sorted Array Easy Comprehension and sorted Square every number with a comprehension, then sort. (A two pointer version does it in O(n).) 867 Transpose Matrix Easy Nested comprehension Row c of the answer is column c of the matrix. 1572 Matrix Diagonal Sum Easy Generator expression with sum Add mat[i][i] and mat[i][n − 1 − i]. When n is odd the middle cell was added twice. 2942 Find Words Containing Character Easy Comprehension with enumerate Keep the index i of every word that contains x. 1748 Sum of Unique Elements Easy Count, then sum with a filter Count each value, then add the values whose count is 1. 1913 Maximum Product Difference Between Two Pairs Easy sorted Biggest two times each other, minus smallest two times each other. 1636 Sort Array by Increasing Frequency Easy sorted with a tuple key Sort by (count, −value): rarer numbers first, and for equal counts the bigger number first. 451 Sort Characters By Frequency Medium Count, sort by count, join Count each letter, sort the letters by count from high to low, and repeat each one count times. 791 Custom Sort String Medium sorted with a dict key Give each letter of order its position. Letters not in order can go anywhere, here at the end. 179 Largest Number Medium Custom comparison with cmp_to_key Put a before b when a + b is bigger than b + a as text. Join, and turn \"000\" into \"0\". Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps In the exam A lambda cannot contain statements such as if blocks, for loops, return or assignment with =. It can contain a conditional expression: lambda n: \"even\" if n % 2 == 0 else \"odd\".Lesson: lambda: a function in one expression Remember key is a function that is called once per item. Python sorts by what it returns. To sort by two things, return a tuple: tuples compare item by item. To reverse just one of two numeric keys, make it negative.Lesson: Sorting and choosing with key Common mistake map and filter return iterators, not lists. They give each value once, only when asked. Wrap them in list() to see all the values, and do not loop over the same map object twice.Lesson: map, filter and reduce In the exam [n for n in nums if n % 2 == 0 else 0] is a SyntaxError. An if after for only filters and has no else.Lesson: List comprehensions Remember In [v for row in matrix for v in row] the for clauses are in the same order as nested loops: outer first. A comprehension inside the expression ([[... for c] for r]) builds a list of lists. If it needs more than two for clauses, a normal loop is easier to read.Lesson: Nested comprehensions Remember A higher-order function takes a function or returns one: sorted, map, filter, make_greeter, compose and partial are all examples. functools.partial is often clearer than a lambda that only fixes some arguments.Lesson: Higher-order functions, compose and partial"],[52,"step-core-5","Step 5Core Python · Errors, modules and files","5.1"],[52,"py-exceptions","Exceptions: try, except, else, finally and raise","Open chapter try, except, else, finally, exception hierarchy, propagation, raise, custom exceptions, assert, EAFP, with. Key ideas try and except try: risky code. except X as err: handles X and its children. First matching handler only. else and finally else runs if no exception. finally always runs (cleanup). Order: try, except..., else, finally. Hierarchy BaseException > Exception > ArithmeticError > ZeroDivisionError. LookupError > KeyError, IndexError. KeyboardInterrupt is not an Exception. raise raise ValueError(\"msg\"). Bare raise re-raises. raise New() from err sets __cause__. Custom: class MyError(Exception). Style Catch specific classes, never bare except. assert is for programmer checks (-O removes it). EAFP. with closes resources. Formulas Handlers checked before a matchExam favourite position of the first matching except clause Python stops at the first match, so order decides the cost and the meaning. Lesson: Counting and reasoning about failures Chance that at least one of n independent steps failsExam favourite 1 − (1 − p)n With p = 1 percent per network call, 50 calls fail at least once with chance 1 − 0.9950 ≈ 39 percent. Errors are normal at scale, so handle them. Lesson: Counting and reasoning about failures Expected tries until success (retry loop) 1 / q If each try succeeds with chance q = 0.8, you need 1.25 tries on average (a geometric distribution). Lesson: Counting and reasoning about failures Exponential back off before retry k wait = base × 2k 1, 2, 4, 8 seconds. Used by web clients so a busy server is not flooded (Web 2.2). Lesson: Counting and reasoning about failures Tables Table: · syntaxerror · exception at run time SyntaxError Exception at run time When Before the program starts, while Python reads the file While the program runs Example print(\"hi\" (a missing bracket) int(\"seven\"), 10 / 0, {}[\"x\"] Can try and except catch it Not in the same file, which never starts Yes Fix Correct the code Handle it, or prevent the cause Lesson: try and except Table: exception · typical cause Exception Typical cause ValueError Right type, wrong value: int(\"seven\") TypeError Wrong type: \"2\" + 3, len(5) ZeroDivisionError Dividing by zero with / // or % IndexError List index out of range: [1, 2][5] KeyError Missing dict key NameError A name that was never defined (UnboundLocalError is its child) AttributeError A missing attribute or method: (5).append(1) FileNotFoundError Opening a file that does not exist (a child of OSError) ImportError and ModuleNotFoundError A module that cannot be found or loaded RecursionError Too deep recursion (a child of RuntimeError) KeyboardInterrupt Ctrl+C. A child of BaseException, not of Exception Lesson: Several handlers, and the exception object Table: clause · runs when Clause Runs when try Always first except X An exception of class X (or a child) was raised in try else try finished with no exception finally Always last: after try, except or else, even after return, break or an unhandled exception Lesson: else and finally Table: form · meaning Form Meaning raise ValueError(\"message\") Raise a new exception with a message raise ValueError Python makes the object for you, with no message raise (alone, inside except) Raise the same exception again, after logging or cleaning up raise NewError(...) from err Raise a new exception and record err as its cause (__cause__) raise NewError(...) from None Hide the original exception from the traceback Lesson: Raising exceptions yourself Table: · assert condition, message · if not condition: raise ... assert condition, message if not condition: raise ... Meant for Things that can only fail if the code itself has a bug Bad input, missing files, anything the outside world causes Turned off by python -O Yes No Exception AssertionError Any class you choose Lesson: assert: checks for programmers Table: · lbyl (look before you leap) · eafp (ask forgiveness) LBYL (look before you leap) EAFP (ask forgiveness) Code if key in d: use d[key] try: use d[key]"],[52,"py-exceptions","Exceptions: try, except, else, finally and raise","then except KeyError Fast when Errors are common Errors are rare (no extra check on the normal path) Race conditions Possible: a file can disappear between the check and the open None: the action itself is the check Python style Fine for simple checks Preferred by most Python code Lesson: Two styles: EAFP and LBYL Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 20 Valid Parentheses Easy Stack Push every opening bracket. A closing bracket must match the top of the stack. At the end the stack must be empty. 1614 Maximum Nesting Depth of the Parentheses Easy Counter Add 1 for \"(\" and take 1 for \")\". The answer is the largest value the counter reaches. 1021 Remove Outermost Parentheses Easy Depth counter Keep a bracket only when it is not at depth 0 before an opening or after a closing. 682 Baseball Game Easy Stack of scores \"+\" adds the last two, \"D\" doubles the last, \"C\" removes the last, anything else is a number. 8 String to Integer (atoi) Medium Careful parsing Skip leading spaces, read one optional sign, read digits until something else, then clamp to the 32 bit range. 150 Evaluate Reverse Polish Notation Medium Stack Numbers go on a stack. An operator pops two numbers, applies itself and pushes the result. Division truncates toward zero. 468 Validate IP Address Medium Validate every part IPv4: 4 parts of digits, 0 to 255, no leading zero. IPv6: 8 parts of 1 to 4 hex digits. Check characters yourself, because int() also accepts signs and spaces. 71 Simplify Path Medium Stack of folder names Split on \"/\". Skip \"\" and \".\", pop for \"..\", push anything else. Join with \"/\". 227 Basic Calculator II Medium Stack with the previous operator Read a number, then apply the operator before it: + and − push, * and / combine with the top of the stack. Sum the stack. 65 Valid Number Hard Scan with a few flags Track whether you saw a digit, a dot and an exponent. A sign is allowed only at the start or right after e. A dot is not allowed after e. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps Remember Read a traceback from the bottom up. The last line names the exception and the reason. The lines above show where it happened, with the newest call last. Python 3.11 and later mark the exact part of the line with ^ and ~ characters.Lesson: When things go wrong: exceptions Common mistake Avoid a bare except: or except BaseException:. They also catch KeyboardInterrupt and SystemExit, so Ctrl+C stops working. Catch Exception at most, and only where you log or report the error.Lesson: The exception hierarchy and the order of handlers In the exam The order is fixed: try, then except clauses, then else, then finally. You need at least one except or a finally. else keeps code out of the try block, so you do not accidentally catch errors from it.Lesson: else and finally Remember A low level function often cannot know what the right reaction is. average should not print \"No marks yet\" because it may be used in a web page or a report. Let it raise, and handle the exception at the level that knows what to tell the user. This is called delegating responsibility.Lesson: Exceptions travel up through function calls Remember Make your own exceptions by subclassing Exception (never BaseException). Give your program one base class, such as BankError, and make specific errors its children. Callers can then catch one exact problem or every problem of your program. Classes are explained in Core 6.1.Lesson: Your own exception classes Remember A with statement uses a context manager that closes the file (or releases a lock, or ends a database transaction) even when an exception happens. It is the short, safe form of try and finally. Advanced 2.2 shows how to write your own.Lesson: Cleaning up: finally and with 5.2"],[52,"py-modules","Modules and packages: import, the standard library and pip","Open chapter import forms, sys.modules and sys.path, math, random, platform, own modules and packages, __all__, main guard, pip and venv. Key ideas import forms import m, import m as a, from m import x, from m import x as y. Avoid import *. Relative: from . import x. How import works Check sys.modules, search sys.path, run the file once, cache it, bind the name. importlib.reload re-runs. math and random ceil, floor, trunc, sqrt, isqrt, hypot, factorial, comb, gcd, log, isclose. randint(a, b) includes b, randrange excludes stop, seed repeats. Packages Folder + __init__.py. _name is private by convention. __all__ controls import *. __pycache__ holds .pyc files. Main guard and pip if __name__ == \"__main__\": main(). pip install inside a venv. pip freeze > requirements.txt. Formulas Combinations and permutationsExam favourite C(n, k) = n! / (k!(n − k)!), P(n, k) = n! / (n − k)! math.comb(5, 2) = 10, math.perm(5, 2) = 20. Lesson: Maths you get from math and random Euclid: greatest common divisorExam favourite gcd(a, b) = gcd(b, a mod b), gcd(a, 0) = a gcd(84, 36) = gcd(36, 12) = gcd(12, 0) = 12. lcm(a, b) = a × b / gcd(a, b). Lesson: Maths you get from math and random Change of base for logarithms logb x = ln x / ln b math.log(8, 2) = 3.0. Floats can make this 2.9999999999999996 for other numbers, so use log2 for base 2. Lesson: Maths you get from math and random Expected value of a fair die (randint(1, 6)) E = (1 + 2 + ... + 6)/6 = 3.5 The mean of many rolls gets close to 3.5 (law of large numbers). Lesson: Maths you get from math and random Monte Carlo estimate of π π ≈ 4 × (points with x2 + y2 ≤ 1) / (all points) Random points in a square, counted inside a quarter circle. The error shrinks like 1/√n. Lesson: Maths you get from math and random Tables Table: form · binds · use as Form Binds Use as import math math math.sqrt(2) import math as m m m.sqrt(2) from math import sqrt, pi sqrt and pi sqrt(2) from math import sqrt as root root root(2) from math import * every public name (or those in __all__) sqrt(2), pi, ... (avoid in real code) import os.path os (and loads os.path) os.path.join(...) from . import circle circle, from the same package circle.area(1) (relative import, inside packages only) Lesson: Every way to write import Table: · import math · from math import sqrt import math from math import sqrt Where a name comes from Clear: math.sqrt You must look at the top of the file Name clashes None: math.log and your own log can live together Possible: a later def log replaces the imported one Typing Longer Shorter Lesson: Every way to write import Table: function · does · example Function Does Example ceil(x), floor(x) Round up, round down to a whole number ceil(4.2) = 5, floor(-4.2) = −5 trunc(x) Cut off the fraction, toward zero trunc(−4.8) = −4 sqrt(x), isqrt(n) Square root as float, exact whole number square root isqrt(20) = 4 hypot(x, y) Length of the hypotenuse, √(x2 + y2) hypot(3, 4) = 5.0 factorial, comb, perm n!, C(n, k), P(n, k) comb(5, 2) = 10 gcd, lcm Greatest common divisor, least common multiple gcd(84, 36) = 12 log, log2, log10, exp Logarithms and ex log(8, 2) = 3.0 isclose(a, b) Compare floats safely isclose(0.1 + 0.2, 0.3) is True Lesson: The math module Table: function · gives Function Gives random() A float with 0.0 ≤ x < 1.0 randint(a, b) A whole number with a ≤ n ≤ b (b included!) randrange(start, stop, step) Like range: stop is not included choice(seq) One item sample(seq, k) k different items, as a new list shuffle(list) Mixes the list in place and returns None seed(n) Restart the sequence, so results repeat (useful for tests) Lesson: The random module Table: name · meaning by convention Name Meaning by convention slug Public: part of what the module offers _clean Internal: may change without warning. from module import * skips it __all__ The list of names that from module import * takes __name__, __file__, __doc__ Set by Python for every module Lesson: Public and private names, and __all__ Table: module"],[52,"py-modules","Modules and packages: import, the standard library and pip","· for · chapter Module For Chapter math, statistics, random, decimal, fractions Numbers Core 1.3 and here datetime, time, calendar Dates and times Here collections, itertools, functools, heapq, bisect Data structures and iteration Core 3.4, 4.3, Advanced 2.1 and 4.2 os, os.path, pathlib, shutil, glob Files and folders Core 5.3 json, csv, sqlite3, xml.etree, configparser, logging Data formats and logs Advanced 6.2 re Regular expressions Advanced 6.1 threading, multiprocessing, asyncio Doing several things at once Advanced 5.1 socket, http, urllib Networks and the web Web 1.1 to 1.2 unittest, doctest Testing Advanced 3.2 tkinter Desktop windows Advanced 6.3 Lesson: A short tour of the standard library Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1979 Find Greatest Common Divisor of Array Easy math.gcd Take the smallest and the largest number and return math.gcd of the two. 1071 Greatest Common Divisor of Strings Easy math.gcd on lengths If str1 + str2 differs from str2 + str1 there is no answer. Otherwise the answer is the first gcd(len1, len2) characters. 69 Sqrt(x) Easy Binary search (or math.isqrt) Find the largest m with m × m ≤ x by halving the search range. 367 Valid Perfect Square Easy math.isqrt math.isqrt(n) is the exact whole square root rounded down. Square it back and compare. 1185 Day of the Week Easy datetime datetime.date(year, month, day).weekday() gives 0 for Monday. Map it to the names. 1360 Number of Days Between Two Dates Easy datetime subtraction Turn both texts into dates with date.fromisoformat and take abs of the difference in days. 1154 Day of the Year Easy datetime Subtract 1 January of the same year and add 1. 172 Factorial Trailing Zeroes Medium Count factors of 5 Each trailing zero needs a 10 = 2 × 5, and 5s are rarer. Add n // 5 + n // 25 + n // 125 + ... 384 Shuffle an Array Medium random and the Fisher Yates shuffle Keep a copy of the original. To shuffle, swap item i with a random item from i to the end. 528 Random Pick with Weight Medium Prefix sums, random and bisect Build running totals. Pick a random number up to the total and find its slot with bisect. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps In the exam floor goes down and trunc goes toward zero. They agree for positive numbers and differ for negative ones: floor(−4.8) is −5, trunc(−4.8) is −4. round() is a built-in, not in math, and rounds halves to the even number.Lesson: The math module Common mistake random gives pseudo-random numbers from the Mersenne Twister algorithm: good for games and simulations, but predictable. For passwords, tokens and anything secret use the secrets module (secrets.token_hex(16)).Lesson: The random module Remember The output above came from the build machine. Press Run on this page and platform.system() says Emscripten, because the Python on this page runs inside your browser (Pyodide). The same code gives Windows or Darwin on other computers.Lesson: platform and sys: where am I running Common mistake Any file name.py that Python can find is a module called name. Use lower case names with underscores, and never name your file after a standard module: a file called random.py in your folder hides the real random module, and import random gives you your own file.Lesson: Writing your own module Remember Since Python 3.3 a folder without __init__.py can still be imported as a namespace package, but an __init__.py makes the intent clear and lets you run setup code and choose what the package exports.Lesson: Packages: folders of modules Remember Run pip as python -m pip install ... when you are not sure which Python a plain pip belongs to. Modern projects describe their dependencies in a pyproject.toml file, and tools such as uv and poetry manage environments for you.Lesson: pip, PyPI and virtual environments 5.3"],[52,"py-files","Files: reading, writing, modes, paths and streams","Open chapter open modes, with, read and write methods, binary files, encodings, errno, pathlib, os and shutil, streams, csv and json. Key ideas open and with with open(path, mode, encoding=\"utf-8\") as f: closes automatically. Modes r, w (empties), a, x (new only), +, b. Reading read(), read(n), readline() (\"\" at end), readlines(), for line in f (best for big files). Lines keep their new line. Writing write(s) and writelines(list) add no new lines. print(x, file=f) does. write returns characters written. Binary and cursor \"rb\"/\"wb\" use bytes. bytearray + readinto. seek(n) moves, tell() reports. Errors and paths OSError family: FileNotFoundError (ENOENT), FileExistsError, PermissionError. pathlib.Path / \"x\", glob, read_text. Formulas File size in bytes of an ASCII textExam favourite sum of len(line) for all lines, new lines included \"milk\\n\" is 5 bytes. On Windows a text file stores each new line as 2 bytes (\\r\\n). Lesson: Sizes, bytes and cursors UTF-8 sizeExam favourite 1 byte for code points 0 to 127, 2 up to 2,047, 3 up to 65,535, 4 above \"नमस्ते ₹100 café\\n\" has 16 characters but 31 bytes. Lesson: Sizes, bytes and cursors Units 1 KB = 1,000 bytes, 1 KiB = 1,024 bytes Disks are sold in KB, MB, GB (powers of 10). Operating systems often show KiB, MiB, GiB (powers of 2). Lesson: Sizes, bytes and cursors Cursor after a read in text mode (ASCII) new position = old position + characters read In binary mode tell() is always a byte offset. In text mode it is an opaque number, so only seek() to values that tell() gave you. Lesson: Sizes, bytes and cursors Lines in memory readlines(): O(file size). for line in f: O(longest line) A 10 GB log can be processed with a for loop on a laptop with 8 GB of memory. Lesson: Sizes, bytes and cursors Tables Table: method · returns · at the end of the file Method Returns At the end of the file read() The rest of the file as one str \"\" read(n) Up to n characters \"\" readline() One line, including its new line character \"\" readlines() A list of all remaining lines [] for line in f One line per turn, without loading the whole file The loop ends Lesson: Reading a text file Table: mode · read · write · file must exist Mode Read Write File must exist Empties the file Cursor starts r (default) Yes No Yes (else FileNotFoundError) No Start w No Yes No, it is created Yes Start a No Yes, always at the end No, it is created No End x No Yes Must NOT exist (else FileExistsError) New file Start r+ Yes Yes Yes No Start w+ Yes Yes No Yes Start a+ Yes Yes, at the end No No End Lesson: Open modes Table: · f = open(...) then f.close() · with open(...) as f: f = open(...) then f.close() with open(...) as f: If an exception happens in between close() is skipped, the file may stay open and data may not be saved The file is closed anyway If you forget close() Data may sit in a buffer and never reach the disk Not possible Lines of code More, plus try and finally to be safe Fewer Lesson: Always use with Table: · text (\"r\", \"w\") · binary (\"rb\", \"wb\") text (\"r\", \"w\") binary (\"rb\", \"wb\") read() returns str bytes write() takes str bytes or bytearray Encoding Decodes and encodes (say encoding=\"utf-8\") None, raw bytes New line characters Changed to and from the system style Never changed Use for Text, CSV, JSON, code Images, audio, zip, PDF, anything not text Lesson: Binary files, bytes and bytearray Table: exception (all are oserror) · errno name · when Exception (all are OSError) errno name When FileNotFoundError ENOENT The file or folder does not exist FileExistsError EEXIST Mode \"x\" or mkdir on something that exists PermissionError EACCES or EPERM Not allowed to read or write it IsADirectoryError EISDIR open() on a folder NotADirectoryError ENOTDIR A path part is a file, not a folder OSError with ENOSPC ENOSPC The disk is full Lesson: File errors and errno Table: · os.path (strings) · pathlib.path (objects) os.path (strings) pathlib.Path (objects) Join os.path.join(\"a\", \"b.txt\") Path(\"a\") / \"b.txt\" Parts os.path.basename,"],[52,"py-files","Files: reading, writing, modes, paths and streams","splitext p.name, p.stem, p.suffix, p.parent Read and write open(...) then read() p.read_text(), p.write_text() Find files glob.glob(\"*.csv\") p.glob(\"*.csv\"), p.rglob(\"*.csv\") Style Older, everywhere in old code Modern, clearer Lesson: Paths with pathlib Table: stream · python name · used by Stream Python name Used by Standard input sys.stdin input() reads from it Standard output sys.stdout print() writes to it Standard error sys.stderr Tracebacks and warnings, print(..., file=sys.stderr) Lesson: stdin, stdout and stderr Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1816 Truncate Sentence Easy split and join Split on spaces, keep the first k words, join them again. 819 Most Common Word Easy Clean text, count words Turn every non letter into a space, lower case everything, count the words that are not banned and take the most common. 884 Uncommon Words from Two Sentences Easy Count words of both texts A word is uncommon if it appears exactly once in the two sentences together. 1592 Rearrange Spaces Between Words Easy Count spaces, divide Count the spaces and words. Put spaces // (words − 1) between words and the rest at the end. 2042 Check if Numbers Are Ascending in a Sentence Easy Parse tokens Split into tokens. Every token made of digits must be bigger than the previous number. 609 Find Duplicate File in System Medium Group paths by content Each entry is \"folder name(content) ...\". Group full paths by content in a dict and keep groups with more than one path. 388 Longest Absolute File Path Medium Depth by tabs, a stack of lengths Split into lines. The number of tabs is the depth. Keep the path length at each depth. A name with a dot is a file. 1233 Remove Sub-Folders from the Filesystem Medium Sort, then compare with the last kept folder After sorting, a sub folder comes right after its parent. Keep a folder unless it starts with the last kept folder plus \"/\". 1047 Remove All Adjacent Duplicates In String Easy Stack Push each character. If it equals the top of the stack, pop instead. 68 Text Justification Hard Greedy lines, spread spaces Fill each line with as many words as fit. Spread the spaces, giving the left gaps one extra when they do not divide evenly. The last line is left aligned. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps Remember All the programs in this chapter first move into a new empty temporary folder with tempfile.mkdtemp(), so they never touch your own files. Drop that line when you work in your project folder.Lesson: Files: data that lasts In the exam Lines keep their new line character. Use line.strip() or line.rstrip(\"\\n\") before you compare or split them. For big files loop with for line in f, which reads one line at a time instead of loading gigabytes into memory.Lesson: Reading a text file Common mistake Add t (text, the default) or b (binary) to any mode: \"rb\", \"wb\", \"ab\", \"r+b\". The most common accident is opening a file with \"w\" when you meant \"a\": the old contents are gone at once.Lesson: Open modes Common mistake Without encoding=..., open() uses a default that depends on the computer. It is UTF-8 on Linux and macOS but can be an old code page on Windows, so a file written on one computer may not read on another. Since Python 3.15 the default becomes UTF-8 everywhere (PEP 686), but writing encoding=\"utf-8\" is still the safe habit.Lesson: Encodings: always say utf-8 Common mistake os.remove, os.rename and shutil.rmtree do not use a recycle bin: deleted files are gone. Print the paths and check them before you delete anything in a loop.Lesson: Folders with os and shutil Remember The page shows only stdout, so the stderr line above is not in the output. In a terminal both appear, but they are separate streams: python3 app.py > out.txt saves only stdout and still shows errors on the screen.Lesson: stdin, stdout and stderr Common mistake Never split CSV lines with line.split(\",\"). A value like \"Ravi, Jr\" contains a comma and is stored in quotes. The csv module handles quotes,"],[52,"py-files","Files: reading, writing, modes, paths and streams","commas and new lines inside values correctly. Advanced 6.2 covers csv, json, xml and sqlite3 in depth.Lesson: CSV and JSON files"],[52,"step-core-6","Step 6Core Python · Object oriented programming","6.1"],[52,"py-classes","Classes and objects: attributes, methods, self and encapsulation","Open chapter classes, objects, __init__, self, instance and class attributes, __str__ and __repr__, introspection, name mangling, is and ==. Key ideas Class and object class C: defines a blueprint. C(...) makes an object and runs __init__(self, ...). self is the object. Two kinds of attribute self.x is per object (in obj.__dict__). x = ... in the class body is shared. Read looks in the object, then the class. Write goes to the object. Shared list trap A list or dict class attribute is shared by all objects. Create it in __init__. Text forms __str__ for print and str, __repr__ for repr, lists and debugging. Write __repr__ at least. Introspection and privacy type, isinstance, hasattr, getattr, setattr, vars, __name__, __module__, __bases__. _x is a convention, __x becomes _Class__x. is = same object. Formulas Length of a vector (Pythagoras)Exam favourite |v| = √(x2 + y2) Vector(3, 4).length() is √(9 + 16) = 5. Lesson: The maths inside a class Dot productExam favourite a · b = axbx + ayby (3, 4) · (1, 2) = 3 + 8 = 11. Lesson: The maths inside a class Angle between two vectors cos θ = (a · b) / (|a| |b|) cos θ = 11 / (5 × 2.236) = 0.984, so θ = 10.3 degrees. Lesson: The maths inside a class Counting objects with a class attribute count after n objects = n One class attribute is shared, so 3 students give Student.count = 3. Lesson: The maths inside a class Name mangling __name inside class C becomes _C__name __balance in Account becomes _Account__balance (17 characters). Lesson: The maths inside a class Memory n objects store n copies of each instance attribute but 1 copy of each class attribute and method 1,000 dogs: 1,000 names, 1 species, 1 bark function. Lesson: The maths inside a class Tables Table: word · meaning · in the program Word Meaning In the program class The blueprint Dog object or instance One thing made from the class rex, moti attribute A value stored on an object or a class name, age, species method A function defined inside the class bark, birthday constructor The code that sets up a new object __init__ self The object a method is working on self.name Lesson: Classes and objects Table: you write · python does You write Python does rex = Dog(\"Rex\") Makes an empty object, then calls Dog.__init__(that object, \"Rex\"), then names it rex rex.bark() Finds bark on the class and calls Dog.bark(rex) Dog.bark A plain function rex.bark A bound method: the function plus the object, ready to call Lesson: Methods, self and __init__ Table: · instance attribute · class attribute instance attribute class attribute Made by self.name = ... (usually in __init__) name = ... in the class body Stored in obj.__dict__ Class.__dict__ How many copies One per object One for the whole class Good for Data that differs between objects: name, age, balance Constants and data every object shares: species, bank name, a counter Lesson: Instance attributes and class attributes Table: · __str__ · __repr__ __str__ __repr__ Used by print(obj), str(obj), f\"{obj}\" repr(obj), f\"{obj!r}\", the REPL, items inside a list Written for Users Developers Good style Short and friendly: (3, 4) Clear and exact, ideally valid code: Point(3, 4) If missing Python uses __repr__ Python shows the class name and memory address Lesson: __str__ and __repr__ Table: tool · answers Tool Answers type(obj), obj.__class__ Which class made this object isinstance(obj, C) Is obj a C (or a child class of C, see 6.2) hasattr(obj, \"x\") Can obj.x be read without an error getattr(obj, \"x\", default) Read an attribute by its name as a string setattr(obj, \"x\", v), delattr(obj, \"x\") Set or delete an attribute by name vars(obj), obj.__dict__ The instance attributes as a dict C.__name__, C.__module__, C.__bases__, C.__doc__ The class name, its module, its parent classes and its docstring dir(obj) Every attribute name, including the inherited ones Lesson: Introspection: looking inside objects Table: name · meaning · enforced Name Meaning Enforced balance Public: anyone may use it Not needed"],[52,"py-classes","Classes and objects: attributes, methods, self and encapsulation","_balance Internal: please use it only inside the class No, a convention only __balance Name mangled to _Account__balance so child classes cannot clash with it by accident Partly: the short name fails outside the class __init__ Special (dunder) name used by Python itself Do not invent your own Lesson: Encapsulation, _private and name mangling Table: · separate variables · a dict · a class separate variables a dict a class Example name1, mark1, name2, mark2 {\"name\": \"Asha\", \"mark\": 88} Student(\"Asha\", 88) Typing mistakes Easy to mix up s[\"nmae\"] fails only when run s.nmae fails, and editors warn early Behaviour Separate functions Separate functions Methods next to the data Rules (no negative marks) Checked everywhere by hand Checked everywhere by hand Checked once, in the class Many of them Impossible A list of dicts A list of objects Best for A few unrelated values Data from JSON, flexible keys Things with data AND behaviour Lesson: Classes compared with other ways to group data Table: · procedural (functions and data apart) · object oriented (classes) procedural (functions and data apart) object oriented (classes) Organised around Steps and functions Things and their behaviour Data protection Any function can change any data Methods guard their own object Reuse Copy or call functions Inheritance and composition (6.2) Good for Short scripts, data pipelines Bigger programs, games, GUIs, web frameworks Lesson: Classes compared with other ways to group data Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 155 Min Stack Medium Keep a second stack of minimums Next to the normal stack, keep a stack whose top is always the smallest value so far. Push to it when the new value is not bigger. 232 Implement Queue using Stacks Easy Two stacks Push onto an inbox stack. To pop, pour the inbox into an outbox stack (which reverses it) only when the outbox is empty. 225 Implement Stack using Queues Easy Rotate one queue After adding x to the back, move every older item from the front to the back, so x is at the front. 705 Design HashSet Easy Buckets of lists Keep 1,000 small lists. A key goes in bucket key % 1000, so each search looks at only one short list. 706 Design HashMap Easy Buckets of key and value pairs Like the hash set, but each bucket holds [key, value] pairs. put updates a pair or adds one. 1603 Design Parking System Easy One counter per car type Store the free spaces in a list indexed by car type. A car parks only if its counter is above zero. 1472 Design Browser History Medium A list and a position Keep the pages in a list and the index of the current page. visit cuts off the forward pages, back and forward move the index within limits. 622 Design Circular Queue Medium Fixed list, head index and count The back of the queue is at (head + count) % k. Moving the head with % k makes the list wrap around. 2043 Simple Bank System Medium Check, then change Every method first checks that the account numbers exist and the money is there, and only then changes the balances. 146 LRU Cache Medium A dict keeps insertion order Python dicts remember the order keys were added. Move a key to the end when it is used, and remove the first key when the cache is full. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps In the exam self is not a keyword. It is only the name every Python programmer uses for the first parameter of a method. Python fills it in for you: rex.bark() becomes Dog.bark(rex). Forget self in the def line and the call fails with \"takes 0 positional arguments but 1 was given\".Lesson: Methods, self and __init__ In the exam Writing obj.attr = value never goes to the class. It always creates or changes the attribute on that one object. That is why a.species = \"wolf\" left b.species and Dog.species alone.Lesson: Instance attributes and class attributes Common mistake A class attribute that holds a list, dict or set is ONE object for all instances. Changing it with append or update through any object changes it for every"],[52,"py-classes","Classes and objects: attributes, methods, self and encapsulation","object. Create such data in __init__ with self.items = [] instead. This is the same trap as a mutable default argument (4.1).Lesson: The shared list trap In the exam Inside a method, change a class attribute through the class name: Student.count += 1. Writing self.count += 1 reads the class value but then stores the result on the object, so the class counter would never change.Lesson: Class attributes as counters Remember getattr with a name in a variable is how frameworks such as Django read a field whose name comes from a settings file or a URL.Lesson: Introspection: looking inside objects In the exam Encapsulation means an object guards its own data and offers methods to work with it, so outside code cannot put it into a bad state (like a negative balance). Python trusts programmers: it has no real private keyword like Java. Use one underscore for internal things, and properties (Advanced 1.3) when you need checks on reading or writing.Lesson: Encapsulation, _private and name mangling In the exam a is b asks \"are these the same object\". a == b asks \"are these equal\". For your own classes == means the same as is until you write __eq__. Writing __eq__ sets __hash__ to None, so the objects can no longer be set items or dict keys unless you also write __hash__ (Advanced 1.1).Lesson: Identity and equality of objects Remember Each method keeps the object valid: deposit refuses amounts that are not positive and withdraw refuses to go below zero. Code outside the class never has to repeat these checks. That is the main gain of object oriented design.Lesson: Putting it together: a bank account 6.2"],[52,"py-inheritance","Inheritance: super(), overriding, polymorphism and the MRO","Open chapter inheritance, overriding, super(), isinstance, polymorphism, duck typing, abstract methods, mixins, MRO, composition. Key ideas Inherit and override class Child(Parent): reuses Parent. Writing a method again overrides it. Lookup: object, class, then parents in MRO order, ending at object. super() super().__init__(...) runs parent setup. super().m() extends a method. super() means the next class in the MRO of the object. Checks isinstance counts children, type(x) is C does not. issubclass(bool, int) is True. __bases__ and __mro__ show the family. Polymorphism One call works on many types. Duck typing needs only the method. Abstract methods: NotImplementedError or abc with @abstractmethod. MRO and composition C3: child before parents, parents in written order, each once. Impossible order is TypeError. Prefer has a over is a. Formulas C3 linearisationExam favourite L[C] = C + merge(L[B1], ..., L[Bn], [B1, ..., Bn]) B1 ... Bn are the parents of C in the order written. Lesson: The MRO and the diamond problem The merge rule take the first head that is not in the tail of any list, remove it everywhere, repeat If every head is in some tail, there is no valid order and Python raises TypeError. Lesson: The MRO and the diamond problem CircleExam favourite A = πr2 Circle(2): 3.14159 × 4 = 12.57. Lesson: The maths: areas, MROs and lookup cost Heron for a triangleExam favourite s = (a + b + c) / 2, A = √(s(s − a)(s − b)(s − c)) Triangle(3, 4, 5): s = 6, A = √(6 × 3 × 2 × 1) = 6. Lesson: The maths: areas, MROs and lookup cost Total area by polymorphism total = sum of shape.area() for every shape 12.57 + 12 + 6 = 30.57, one loop for every kind of shape. Lesson: The maths: areas, MROs and lookup cost Length of an MRO len(C.__mro__) = number of distinct ancestors + 1 (C itself), object included Diamond D(B, C): D, B, C, A, object is 5. Lesson: The maths: areas, MROs and lookup cost Lookup cost worst case one dict check per class in the MRO: O(len(MRO)) CPython caches the result per type, so repeated lookups are close to O(1). Lesson: The maths: areas, MROs and lookup cost Tables Table: · super().method(...) · parent.method(self, ...) super().method(...) Parent.method(self, ...) self Passed for you You pass it by hand If the parent class is renamed Nothing to change Every call must be edited Multiple inheritance Follows the MRO, so every class runs once Can run a class twice or skip one Seen in Modern code Old code Lesson: super(): use the parent version Table: check · true when Check True when isinstance(obj, C) obj was made by C or by any child of C type(obj) is C obj was made by exactly C (children do not count) issubclass(A, B) A is B or a child (at any depth) of B C.__bases__ A tuple of the direct parents of C C.__mro__ The full search order, ending with object Lesson: isinstance, issubclass and __bases__ Table: · through inheritance · duck typing through inheritance duck typing Needs a shared parent Yes No Checked When the code runs When the code runs Error if a method is missing NotImplementedError from the parent, or a TypeError from abc AttributeError at the call Seen in Frameworks: Django models, views Everywhere: len, for loops, files, + Lesson: Polymorphism and duck typing Table: · raise notimplementederror · abc.abc with @abstractmethod raise NotImplementedError abc.ABC with @abstractmethod When the mistake shows Only when the missing method is called As soon as you try to make the object Parent can be made Yes No Import needed No from abc import ABC, abstractmethod Lesson: Abstract methods and a shape family Table: · inheritance (is a) · composition (has a) inheritance (is a) composition (has a) Example class Dog(Animal) self.engine = Engine(...) Link Fixed when the class is written Can be swapped while running Child gets Every parent method, wanted or not Only what you pass on Risk A change in the parent can break children More small methods that pass work on Use when The child really is a kind of the parent The object"],[52,"py-inheritance","Inheritance: super(), overriding, polymorphism and the MRO","uses another object to do a job Lesson: Composition compared with inheritance Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 284 Peeking Iterator Medium Wrap an object and look one step ahead Keep the next value in an attribute. peek returns it, next returns it and fetches a new one. This is composition: the class holds another object. 341 Flatten Nested List Iterator Medium Flatten once with recursion Walk the nested list with a recursive helper and store every integer in a plain list. Then next and hasNext only move an index. 707 Design Linked List Medium Node objects and a dummy head Each node object holds a value and a link to the next node. A dummy node before the first one means adding at index 0 needs no special case. 208 Implement Trie (Prefix Tree) Medium A tree of node objects Each node has a dict of children, one per next letter, and a flag that marks the end of a word. 641 Design Circular Deque Medium Fixed list with a moving front Like the circular queue, but the front can also move back by one: (front − 1) % k. 933 Number of Recent Calls Easy A queue of times Add each new time to the back. Remove times from the front that are older than t − 3000. The length is the answer. 901 Online Stock Span Medium Stack of price and span pairs Keep a stack of [price, span]. While the top price is not bigger than today, pop it and add its span to today. 1352 Product of the Last K Numbers Medium Prefix products, reset at zero Keep running products. The product of the last k is last / products[−k − 1]. A zero clears the list, and asking past it gives 0. 2241 Design an ATM Machine Medium Greedy from the biggest note Store how many of each note there are. To withdraw, take as many big notes as possible, then smaller ones. If money is left over, refuse and change nothing. 1396 Design Underground System Medium Two dicts One dict remembers where and when each card checked in. Another adds up total time and trip count for each pair of stations. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps Remember The child is a special kind of the parent: a Dog is an Animal. If you cannot say \"a Child is a Parent\" in plain words, inheritance is probably the wrong tool (see composition below).Lesson: Inheritance: reuse a class In the exam An overriding method normally keeps the same parameters as the parent one, so code that works with an Animal also works with a Dog. This rule is called the Liskov substitution principle.Lesson: Overriding methods Common mistake A child __init__ replaces the parent one completely. If it does not call super().__init__(...), the attributes the parent sets are never created.Lesson: super(): use the parent version In the exam Prefer isinstance over type(x) == C, because a child object is also a valid parent object. Every class inherits from object in the end, and bool is a child of int, which is why True + True is 2.Lesson: isinstance, issubclass and __bases__ Remember A mixin is a small class that adds one ability (like to_json) and is not meant to be used alone. Django uses mixins a lot, for example LoginRequiredMixin on views. Put mixins first or last consistently and keep them free of __init__ when you can.Lesson: Multiple inheritance and mixins In the exam super() does not mean \"my parent\". It means \"the next class in the MRO of the object I am working on\". Inside B, super().hello() went to C for a D object but to A for a B object. That is what lets every class in a diamond run exactly once.Lesson: The MRO and the diamond problem"],[52,"step-core-7","Step 7Core Python · Exam practice","7.1"],[52,"py-core-exam","Exam practice: PCEP and PCAP style papers","Open chapter Four practice papers, a bug hunt, the traps table, exam technique and a mock quiz. Key ideas Exam facts PCEP-30-02: 30 questions, 40 min, 70 %. PCAP-31-03: 40 questions, 65 min, 70 %. OOP is 34 % of PCAP. Operator traps -2 ** 2 is -4, 2 ** 3 ** 2 is 512, -7 // 2 is -4, -7 % 3 is 2, round(2.5) is 2, 0.1 + 0.2 != 0.3. Collection traps b = a aliases, [[0] * 3] * 3 shares a row, (1,) is a tuple, {} is a dict, sort returns None. Function traps Mutable defaults are shared, assignment makes a name local, lambdas in loops bind late, finally always runs. Object traps obj.x = v never changes the class, __x becomes _Class__x, a child __init__ must call super().__init__(). Formulas Time per questionExam favourite minutes × 60 / questions PCEP: 40 × 60 / 30 = 80 seconds. PCAP: 65 × 60 / 40 = 97.5 seconds. Lesson: Exam technique and a study plan Questions to pass (if every item counts the same)Exam favourite correct needed = ceil(pass mark × questions) PCEP: 0.7 × 30 = 21. PCAP: 0.7 × 40 = 28. Lesson: Exam technique and a study plan Points by block block share of the exam = block weight Object oriented programming is 34 % of PCAP, more than any other block, so it deserves the most practice. Lesson: Exam technique and a study plan Tables Table: · pcep-30-02 (entry) · pcap-31-03 (associate) PCEP-30-02 (entry) PCAP-31-03 (associate) Questions 30 40 Time 40 minutes, plus 5 for the agreement and tutorial 65 minutes, plus 10 for the agreement and tutorial Pass mark 70 % overall 70 % overall Question types Single and multiple choice, drag and drop, gap fill, sort, code fill, code insertion Single and multiple choice, plus interactive and scenario items Blocks and weights Fundamentals 18 %, control flow 29 %, data collections 25 %, functions and exceptions 28 % Modules and packages 12 %, exceptions 14 %, strings 18 %, object oriented programming 34 %, miscellaneous (comprehensions, lambdas, closures, files) 22 % Chapters here 1.1 to 4.3 and 5.1 3.1, 4.1 to 6.2 Lesson: The exams and how to use this chapter Table: paper · covers · lesson Paper Covers Lesson A Operators, numbers, loops, booleans, print 2 B Lists, tuples, slices, dicts, sets, strings 3 C Arguments, defaults, scope, closures, exceptions 4 D Classes, inheritance, strings, modules, generators 5 Lesson: The exams and how to use this chapter Table: q · output · why (chapter) Q Output Why (chapter) 1 -4 512 ** binds tighter than the minus sign, so -2 ** 2 is -(2 ** 2). ** groups from the right: 2 ** 9 (1.3) 2 -4 2 -2 // rounds down (towards minus infinity). The result of % takes the sign of the divisor (1.3) 3 False 2 4 0.1 and 0.2 are not exact in binary. round() goes to the even neighbour on a tie (1.2) 4 True True Chained comparisons mean 3 > 2 and 2 > 1 and 1 == 1 (1.3) 5 done 12 The loop ends normally when x reaches 12, so the else block runs (2.2) 6 i is 1 break skips the else block. The loop variable keeps its last value (2.2) 7 31 15 5 1000 int(text, 16) reads hexadecimal. 0o is octal, 0b is binary, _ is only for reading (1.2) 8 True False True False Any non empty string is true, even \"False\". [0] is a non empty list (1.2) 9 ababab 55 10 * repeats a string, + joins strings, + adds numbers (1.2) 10 a-b! sep goes between the values, end replaces the new line (1.4) Lesson: Paper A: fundamentals and control flow Table: q · output · why (chapter) Q Output Why (chapter) 1 [1, 2, 3, 4] b is the same list as a, and += changes a list in place (3.2) 2 (1, 2, 3) (1, 2, 3, 4) A tuple cannot change, so += builds a new tuple and only u points to it (3.3) 3 [[1, 0, 0], [1, 0, 0], [1, 0, 0]] [row] * 3 repeats the SAME inner list three times. Use a comprehension (3.2) 4 [4, 2] [4, 3, 2] [] A negative step walks backwards. The stop index is never included. Slices never raise IndexError (3.2) 5 ['a', 'b'] 0 2 Changing a value keeps the key in its place. get gives the default for a missing key (3.4) 6 yth hon -1 P Slices stop before the end index. find gives -1 when nothing is found (3.1) 7"],[52,"py-core-exam","Exam practice: PCEP and PCAP style papers","tuple int The comma makes a tuple, not the brackets (3.3) 8 {1, 2, 3, 4} {2} | is union, & is intersection, and a set keeps one copy of each value (3.3) 9 [1, 2, 3] None sort changes the list and returns None. sorted would return a new list (3.2) 10 1 {True: 'c'} True == 1 == 1.0 with equal hashes, so they are one key. The first key stays, the last value wins (3.4) Lesson: Paper B: data collections Table: q · output · why (chapter) Q Output Why (chapter) 1 (1, 3, (4, 5), 6, {'d': 7}) Positional values fill a and b, the rest go to *args. c is keyword only. Unknown keywords go to **kw (4.1) 2 [1, 2] The default list is made once, when def runs, and shared by every call (4.1) 3 UnboundLocalError x = 5 anywhere in g makes x local in all of g, so print(x) reads it too early (4.2) 4 [2, 2, 2] Each lambda looks up i when it is called, and by then i is 2 (4.3) 5 finally runs, then try finally runs before the function really returns (5.1) 6 V, then fin The first matching except wins. else runs only when no error happened. finally always runs (5.1) 7 None A function without return gives None (4.1) 8 KeyError 'k' KeyError is a child of LookupError. str of a KeyError shows the key with quotes (5.1) 9 [2, 6, 10] filter keeps 1, 3 and 5 (n % 2 is 1, which is true), map doubles them (4.3) 10 2 nonlocal lets inner change n in outer. The closure keeps n between calls (4.2) Lesson: Paper C: functions and exceptions Table: q · output · why (chapter) Q Output Why (chapter) 1 1 2 True Assigning through an instance adds an instance attribute and leaves the class alone (6.1) 2 False True __secret is name mangled to _B__secret (6.1) 3 QP True False super() calls the parent version. A child is an instance of its parent, not the other way round (6.2) 4 True True True 65 a Strings compare code point by code point. \"Z\" is 90, \"a\" is 97, and \"1\" comes before \"9\" (3.1) 5 ['a', 'b', '', 'c'] ['x', 'y'] a-b-c split(\",\") keeps empty parts. split() with no argument splits on runs of spaces and drops the ends (3.1) 6 **Hello** Abc Def True True center pads both sides. title capitalises each word (3.1) 7 -3 -2 -2 4.0 floor goes down, ceil goes up, trunc cuts towards zero. sqrt always returns a float (5.2) 8 __main__ A file run directly gets the name __main__. Imported, it would get its module name (5.2) 9 C! C!? C! print, str and f-strings all use __str__ (6.1) 10 0 14 next takes 0. sum uses what is left: 1 + 4 + 9 (4.3) 11 [0, 3, 6, 9] {0: 0, 1: 1, 2: 4} A list comprehension with if, and a dict comprehension (4.3) 12 12 abab make returns a closure that remembers k. * works on strings too (4.2) 13 AttributeError Child.__init__ never calls super().__init__(), so name is never set (6.2) Lesson: Paper D: objects, strings and modules Table: trap · looks like · really · chapter Trap Looks like Really Chapter Power and minus -2 ** 2 is 4 -4 1.3 Floor division -7 // 2 is -3 -4 1.3 Rounding round(2.5) is 3 2 (round half to even) 1.2 Integer from text int(\"3.5\") is 3 ValueError (use int(float(\"3.5\"))) 1.2 Boolean of text bool(\"False\") is False True 1.2 is and == a is b means equal is means the same object 1.3 Copy b = a copies a list b is another name for the same list 3.2 Nested repeat [[0] * 3] * 3 makes 3 rows It makes 3 names for one row 3.2 One item tuple (5) is a tuple It is the int 5, write (5,) 3.3 Empty set {} is an empty set It is an empty dict, write set() 3.3 sort x = x.sort() sorts x becomes None 3.2 Default argument def f(x=[]) gets a new list each call One list for all calls 4.1 Scope Reading a global inside a function that also assigns it works UnboundLocalError 4.2 Lambda in a loop Each lambda keeps its own i They all see the last i 4.3 else on loops Runs when the loop runs Runs when the loop was not stopped by break 2.2 finally return skips finally finally always runs 5.1 Class attribute obj.x = 1 changes the class It adds an instance attribute 6.1 Forgotten super() Parent attributes exist anyway AttributeError 6.2 Lesson: The traps in one table Table:"],[52,"py-core-exam","Exam practice: PCEP and PCAP style papers","no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 11 Container With Most Water Medium Two pointers Start at both ends. The area is width times the shorter wall. Move the shorter wall inwards, because only that can make the area bigger. 12 Integer to Roman Medium Greedy table Walk a table from 1000 (M) down to 1 (I), including 900, 400, 90, 40, 9 and 4. Take each value as many times as it fits. 15 3Sum Medium Sort, then two pointers Sort the list. For each first number, look for two more with two pointers. Skip equal neighbours so no triple repeats. 17 Letter Combinations of a Phone Number Medium Grow the list digit by digit Start with [\"\"] and, for every digit, add each of its letters to every string made so far. 36 Valid Sudoku Medium Sets for rows, columns and boxes Keep a set for each row, column and 3 by 3 box. A digit seen twice in any of them makes the board invalid. The box number is (r // 3) * 3 + c // 3. 38 Count and Say Medium Describe the previous term Read the previous string in runs of equal digits and write the run length followed by the digit. 56 Merge Intervals Medium Sort by start Sort the intervals by start. If the next one starts before the last merged one ends, stretch the end, otherwise start a new interval. 67 Add Binary Easy Add from the right with a carry Walk both strings from the end, add the digits and the carry, write total % 2 and carry total // 2. 238 Product of Array Except Self Medium Products from the left and from the right answer[i] is the product of everything left of i times everything right of i. Two passes, no division. 448 Find All Numbers Disappeared in an Array Easy Set difference The numbers should be 1 to n. Put the list in a set and keep the numbers from 1 to n that are not in it. Lesson: LeetCode review set Tips and traps Common mistake The Python Institute has announced newer versions, PCEP-30-03 (35 questions, 50 minutes, 70 %) and PCAP-31-04 (42 questions, 65 minutes, 72 %), released on 31 October 2026. The skills tested are the same core Python, but check which version your voucher is for before you book.Lesson: The exams and how to use this chapter Remember Cover the answers, write down what you think each line prints, then open the answers. A wrong guess tells you exactly which chapter to reread.Lesson: The exams and how to use this chapter In the exam In the exam: read the whole snippet before the options, trace it line by line on scratch paper, watch for indentation and for a missing comma or bracket that turns the answer into \"the code raises an error\". Mark hard questions and come back, every question is worth the same.Lesson: Exam technique and a study plan"],[52,"py-core-exam","Step 1Advanced Python · Advanced object oriented programming","1.1"],[52,"py-dunder","Special methods and operator overloading","Open chapter special methods, operator overloading, reflected operators, total_ordering, the hash contract, container protocol, __call__, __format__. Key ideas How operators work a + b calls type(a).__add__(a, b). Missing or NotImplemented: try type(b).__radd__(b, a), then TypeError. Lookup is on the class. Text __repr__ (developers, lists, REPL), __str__ (print), __format__ (f-string specs). Compare and hash __eq__ plus __lt__ with total_ordering. Equal objects need equal hashes. __eq__ alone makes a class unhashable. Containers __len__, __getitem__ (ints and slices), __setitem__, __contains__, __iter__. __bool__ falls back to __len__. Other hooks __call__ for callable objects, __iadd__ returns self, __getattr__ only on failed lookup, __setattr__ on every assignment. Formulas Adding fractionsExam favourite a/b + c/d = (ad + cb) / bd 1/2 + 1/3 = (3 + 2) / 6 = 5/6. Lesson: The maths: fractions and vectors with operators Lowest termsExam favourite divide top and bottom by g = gcd(top, bottom) 2/4: gcd(2, 4) = 2, so 1/2. Lesson: The maths: fractions and vectors with operators Euclid gcd(a, b) = gcd(b, a mod b), gcd(a, 0) = a gcd(48, 18) = gcd(18, 12) = gcd(12, 6) = gcd(6, 0) = 6. Lesson: The maths: fractions and vectors with operators Vector length |v| = √(x2 + y2) abs(Vector(3, 4)) = 5.0, using math.hypot. Lesson: The maths: fractions and vectors with operators Scaling k(x, y) = (kx, ky) 2 * Vector(3, 4) = Vector(6, 8), through __rmul__. Lesson: The maths: fractions and vectors with operators Dot product with @ a @ b = axbx + ayby Python 3.5 added @ (__matmul__) for exactly this kind of product. Lesson: The maths: fractions and vectors with operators Tables Table: you write · python calls · group You write Python calls Group repr(x), str(x), format(x, s) __repr__, __str__, __format__ Text x + y, x - y, x * y, x / y, x // y, x % y, x ** y, x @ y __add__, __sub__, __mul__, __truediv__, __floordiv__, __mod__, __pow__, __matmul__ Arithmetic y + x when y does not know x __radd__ and the other r methods on x Reflected x += y __iadd__ (or __add__ then assign) In place -x, +x, abs(x), ~x __neg__, __pos__, __abs__, __invert__ Unary x == y, x != y, x < y, x <= y, x > y, x >= y __eq__, __ne__, __lt__, __le__, __gt__, __ge__ Comparison hash(x), set and dict keys __hash__ Hashing bool(x), if x __bool__, else __len__ Truth len(x), x[i], x[i] = v, del x[i], v in x __len__, __getitem__, __setitem__, __delitem__, __contains__ Container for v in x, iter(x), next(it) __iter__, __next__ Iteration (2.1) x(...) __call__ Callable with x: __enter__, __exit__ Context manager (2.3) x.name when it is missing, x.name = v __getattr__, __setattr__ Attributes int(x), float(x), round(x) __int__, __float__, __round__ Conversion Lesson: The main special methods Table: · all six methods by hand · @total_ordering with __eq__ and __lt__ all six methods by hand @total_ordering with __eq__ and __lt__ Lines of code Many, easy to make inconsistent Two methods Speed Fastest Slightly slower for the generated ones Needed for sorted, min, max __lt__ is enough __lt__ is enough Good for Hot loops Almost everything else Lesson: Comparisons and total_ordering Table: · __getattr__ · __getattribute__ · __setattr__ __getattr__ __getattribute__ __setattr__ Called when Normal lookup failed EVERY attribute read EVERY attribute assignment Typical use Defaults, proxies, lazy loading Rare: logging every access Validation, read only objects Danger Small Infinite recursion if you read self.x inside it Infinite recursion if you write self.x inside it Lesson: Attribute hooks: __getattr__ and __setattr__ Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 592 Fraction Addition and Subtraction Medium Parse, add, reduce with gcd Read each signed fraction, add it to a running a/b with (ad + cb) / bd, then divide by the gcd. 166 Fraction to Recurring Decimal Medium Long division, remember remainders Do long division. When a remainder comes back, the digits since its first"],[52,"py-dunder","Special methods and operator overloading","appearance repeat, so wrap them in brackets. 1656 Design an Ordered Stream Easy A list and a pointer Store each value at its id. After each insert, return the run of filled slots starting at the pointer and move the pointer past them. 1845 Seat Reservation Manager Medium A min heap of free seats Keep the free seats in a heap so the smallest one is always on top. 2336 Smallest Number in Infinite Set Medium A counter plus a set of returned numbers Numbers from next_new upwards are all present. Numbers added back below it go into a heap and a set. 1381 Design a Stack With Increment Operation Medium A list with a size limit push only when there is room. increment adds val to the bottom k items. 1286 Iterator for Combination Medium Make the combinations up front itertools.combinations gives the combinations in order. Store them and walk through them with an index. 900 RLE Iterator Medium Use up counts Walk the pairs (count, value). Take n from the current count, moving to the next pair when one runs out. 1670 Design Front Middle Back Queue Medium A plain list A list with insert and pop at the right index is simple and fast enough for the limits. The middle index is len // 2 for push and (len - 1) // 2 for pop. 2349 Design a Number Container System Medium Dict plus a lazy heap per number Remember the number at each index. For each number keep a heap of indexes and drop stale ones when you look at the top. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps In the exam Special methods are looked up on the class, not on the object. Setting obj.__len__ = ... on one object does nothing for len(obj). Also, never invent your own __names__: they are reserved for Python.Lesson: The main special methods In the exam Return the special value NotImplemented (not raise NotImplementedError) when your method cannot handle the other type. That gives the other object a chance to answer. Raising stops Python from trying.Lesson: Arithmetic, reflected and unary operators Remember If you write __eq__ but not __ne__, Python gives != for free by inverting __eq__.Lesson: Comparisons and total_ordering Remember A class with only __len__ and __getitem__ already works with for, in, reversed and slicing. That is why Python calls this the sequence protocol. Inheriting from collections.abc.Sequence (or MutableSequence) also gives you index, count and more for free.Lesson: Emulating containers In the exam x += y first tries x.__iadd__(y) and then ASSIGNS the result to x. That is why __iadd__ must return self. A tuple has no __iadd__, so += makes a new tuple and other names still see the old one.Lesson: Callable objects, text formats and in place operators Remember 0.1 + 0.2 is 0.30000000000000004 with floats, but Fraction(1, 10) + Fraction(2, 10) is exactly 3/10. Use Fraction or Decimal when exact answers matter, such as money.Lesson: The maths: fractions and vectors with operators 1.2"],[52,"py-decorators","Extended arguments, closures and decorators","Open chapter parameters, closures, decorators, wraps, decorator factories, stacking, class decorators, cache. Key ideas Parameters a, / positional only. *args tuple. after * keyword only. **kwargs dict. f(*seq, **d) unpacks at a call. Closures Functions are objects. An inner function remembers the variables of the outer one (__closure__), even after it returned. Decorator shape @d def f is f = d(f). def d(func): @wraps(func) def wrapper(*args, **kwargs): ... return func(*args, **kwargs). return wrapper. Arguments and stacking @d(x) is f = d(x)(f): three levels. @a @b def f is a(b(f)): applied bottom up, run top down. Built ins @cache, @lru_cache(maxsize), @wraps, @property, @classmethod, @staticmethod, @dataclass, @contextmanager. Cache only pure functions. Formulas Calls of plain recursive FibonacciExam favourite C(n) = 1 + C(n − 1) + C(n − 2), C(0) = C(1) = 1, so C(n) = 2F(n + 1) − 1 fib(25): 2 × 121,393 − 1 = 242,785 calls, as the program counted. Lesson: The maths: memoisation and backoff Calls with @cacheExam favourite each n from 0 to N is computed once: N + 1 misses fib(25): 26 misses and 23 hits. Lesson: The maths: memoisation and backoff Growth F(n) ≈ φn / √5, φ = (1 + √5) / 2 ≈ 1.618 Plain recursion is exponential, O(φn). The cached version is linear, O(n). Lesson: The maths: memoisation and backoff Exponential backoff wait before retry k = base × 2k, total for n retries = base(2n − 1) base 1 s, 4 retries: 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 = 15 s. Lesson: The maths: memoisation and backoff LRU hit rate hit rate = hits / (hits + misses) square with maxsize 2 on 1, 2, 1, 3, 2: 1 hit, 4 misses, 20 %. Lesson: The maths: memoisation and backoff Tables Table: part of def · meaning · example call Part of def Meaning Example call name, / Positional only: cannot be passed by keyword (PEP 570) describe(\"Asha\", ...) age Positional or keyword describe(\"Asha\", 30) or age=30 *hobbies Collects extra positional values into a tuple \"chess\", \"music\" city=\"Pune\" after * Keyword only: must be passed by name (PEP 3102) city=\"Delhi\" **extra Collects extra keyword values into a dict lang=\"hi\" f(*seq, **mapping) At a CALL, * and ** unpack instead of collecting describe(*values, **options) Lesson: Extended argument syntax Table: · attribute on the wrapper function · a class with __call__ · a closure variable with nonlocal attribute on the wrapper function a class with __call__ a closure variable with nonlocal Example wrapper.calls self.calls nonlocal calls Readable from outside Yes: greet.calls Yes: square.calls No Works on methods Yes Needs extra work (__get__) Yes Best for Simple counters and flags Complex state Private state Lesson: Decorators that keep state Table: decorator · module · what it does · chapter Decorator Module What it does Chapter @functools.cache, @lru_cache(maxsize) functools Remembers results for arguments seen before 1.2 @functools.wraps(func) functools Copies name and docstring onto a wrapper 1.2 @functools.total_ordering functools Fills in comparison methods 1.1 @property, @x.setter built in Methods that look like attributes 1.3 @classmethod, @staticmethod built in Methods that get the class, or nothing 1.3 @abc.abstractmethod abc Methods every child must write 1.3 @dataclasses.dataclass dataclasses Writes __init__, __repr__ and __eq__ for you 1.3 @contextlib.contextmanager contextlib Turns a generator into a with statement helper 2.3 @app.route(\"/\") Flask Registers a function as a web page Web 3.1 Lesson: Built in decorators and caching Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 746 Min Cost Climbing Stairs Easy Top down with @cache best(i) is the cheapest way to stand past the top from step i: cost[i] + min(best(i + 1), best(i + 2)). Start from step 0 or 1. 62 Unique Paths Medium Paths from a cell, cached paths(r, c) = paths(r + 1, c) + paths(r, c + 1), and 1 on the last row or column. 64 Minimum Path Sum Medium Cheapest from a cell, cached cost(r, c) is the grid value plus the cheaper of moving down or"],[52,"py-decorators","Extended arguments, closures and decorators","right. 91 Decode Ways Medium Ways from a position, cached From position i, take one digit (if not 0) or two digits (if 10 to 26) and add the ways for the rest. 139 Word Break Medium Can the rest be split, cached From position i, try every dictionary word that matches there and ask the same question for the rest. 198 House Robber Medium Rob or skip, cached best(i) = max(rob house i and jump two, or skip to i + 1). 322 Coin Change Medium Fewest coins for an amount, cached fewest(a) = 1 + the smallest fewest(a - coin). Use infinity for impossible amounts. 343 Integer Break Medium Best split, cached For each first piece k, either keep the rest whole or break it further: max(k * (n - k), k * best(n - k)). 96 Unique Binary Search Trees Medium Catalan numbers, cached Choose each value as the root. The trees on the left and right are independent, so multiply their counts. 494 Target Sum Medium Ways from (index, total), cached At each number add it or subtract it. Count the ways that land exactly on target. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps In the exam The order in a def is always: positional only, /, normal, *args (or a bare *), keyword only, **kwargs. *args, **kwargs in a wrapper accept any call and pass it on unchanged, which is why every general decorator uses them. Notice the fourth line of output: name=\"Asha\" did not fill name. Because describe has **extra, it went into extra, so Python reports name as missing.Lesson: Extended argument syntax Remember A closure is a function that remembers the variables of the function it was made in, even after that function has returned. greet remembers greeting. That memory, stored in __closure__, is what lets a wrapper keep hold of the original function.Lesson: Functions are objects, closures remember Common mistake Without @wraps every decorated function is called \"wrapper\" and loses its docstring, which breaks help(), debuggers, logging and frameworks that look up functions by name (Flask uses the name for its routes). Always put @wraps(func) on the wrapper.Lesson: functools.wraps: keep the name and docstring Common mistake A decorator with arguments has three nested functions: the factory (settings), the decorator (the function) and the wrapper (the call). Forgetting the brackets, @retry instead of @retry(3), passes the function as attempts and fails in a confusing way.Lesson: Decorators with arguments In the exam Decorators are applied from the bottom up (nearest the def first) and run from the top down at call time. @a then @b above def f means f = a(b(f)).Lesson: Stacking decorators Common mistake Only cache pure functions, whose result depends only on the arguments. Arguments must be hashable. @cache never forgets, so for many different arguments use lru_cache with a maxsize.Lesson: Built in decorators and caching 1.3"],[52,"py-class-design","Class design: methods, properties, abstract classes and dataclasses","Open chapter class and static methods, properties, cached_property, ABCs, dataclasses, __slots__, descriptors. Key ideas Method kinds Instance method gets self. @classmethod gets cls (alternative constructors, returns cls(...)). @staticmethod gets nothing. Properties @property getter, @x.setter with checks, no setter means read only. Store the value as self._x. @cached_property computes once. Abstract classes class C(ABC) with @abstractmethod: C() and incomplete children raise TypeError. collections.abc offers ready contracts. Dataclasses @dataclass writes __init__, __repr__, __eq__. field(default_factory=list), frozen, order, __post_init__, kw_only, slots, asdict, replace. Slots and descriptors __slots__ removes __dict__. Descriptors have __get__ and __set__. Data descriptors beat the instance dict. Formulas Celsius to Fahrenheit (a computed property)Exam favourite F = C × 9 / 5 + 32 25 °C is 25 × 1.8 + 32 = 77 °F. Lesson: The maths: conversions, ordering and memory The other way C = (F − 32) × 5 / 9 212 °F is 180 × 5 / 9 = 100 °C. Lesson: The maths: conversions, ordering and memory Absolute zero C ≥ −273.15, so F ≥ −459.67 The setter rejects anything colder. Lesson: The maths: conversions, ordering and memory order=True compares as tuplesExam favourite (a1, a2, ...) < (b1, b2, ...) at the first position where they differ Version(3, 9, 7) < Version(3, 14, 0) because 9 < 14, even though \"9\" > \"14\" as text. Lesson: The maths: conversions, ordering and memory Memory for n objects total ≈ n × bytes per object 1,000,000 objects at 40 bytes less each save about 40 MB. Lesson: The maths: conversions, ordering and memory Age from a birth year (ignoring the birthday) age = current year − birth year 2026 − 1996 = 30. Lesson: The maths: conversions, ordering and memory Tables Table: · instance method · @classmethod · @staticmethod instance method @classmethod @staticmethod First argument self (the object) cls (the class) none Can read or change The object and its class The class only Only its arguments Called as obj.m() Cls.m() or obj.m() Cls.m() or obj.m() Typical use Almost everything Alternative constructors like from_text, dict.fromkeys, datetime.now A helper that belongs to the topic, like is_adult With inheritance self is the child object cls is the child class Nothing changes Lesson: Instance, class and static methods Table: · a public attribute · get_x() and set_x() methods · @property a public attribute get_x() and set_x() methods @property Caller writes t.celsius = 5 t.set_celsius(5) t.celsius = 5 Checks values No Yes Yes Can add checks later without changing callers No No Yes Feels like Python Yes No (Java style) Yes Lesson: Properties: attributes with rules Table: option · what it adds Option What it adds @dataclass __init__, __repr__ and __eq__ from the annotated fields field(default_factory=list) A new default object for every instance (a plain [] default is refused) frozen=True Assignment raises FrozenInstanceError, and __hash__ is generated order=True <, <=, >, >= comparing the fields as a tuple, in order field(init=False) and __post_init__ Computed fields and checks after __init__ kw_only=True Every field must be passed by name slots=True Generates __slots__ for less memory and faster access asdict, astuple, replace Convert to a dict or tuple, or copy with changes Lesson: Dataclasses: classes for data Table: · tuple · namedtuple · dict tuple namedtuple dict @dataclass Fields by name No Yes Yes (keys) Yes Can change No No Yes Yes (or frozen) Methods and properties No Some No Yes Type hints and defaults No Defaults only No Yes Best for Small fixed groups Light records JSON style data Most records in an app Lesson: Dataclasses: classes for data Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1146 Snapshot Array Medium Per index history plus binary search For each index keep a list of (snap id, value) changes. get looks for the last change at or before the snap id with bisect. 981 Time Based Key-Value"],[52,"py-class-design","Class design: methods, properties, abstract classes and dataclasses","Store Medium Lists of times per key, bisect Timestamps arrive in increasing order, so each key keeps a sorted list. get finds the last time not after the asked time. 1797 Design Authentication Manager Medium A dict of expiry times Store when each token expires. renew only works while the token is alive. Count the tokens that expire after the current time. 1500 Design a File Sharing System Medium Reuse the smallest free id with a heap Hand out ids from a heap of freed ids first, then new ids. Each user has a set of chunks. 1244 Design A Leaderboard Medium A dict of scores addScore adds to a player, top sorts the scores and adds the K biggest, reset sets a player back to 0. 2526 Find Consecutive Integers from a Data Stream Medium Count the current run Keep how many values in a row have equalled value. consec returns whether that run has reached k. 2296 Design a Text Editor Hard Two stacks around the cursor Keep the text left of the cursor and the text right of it (reversed) as two lists. Every operation moves characters between them. 1825 Finding MK Average Hard Keep the last m values, sort when asked A deque keeps the last m values. To answer, sort them, drop k from each end and average the middle. Simple, and fine for small m. 2034 Stock Price Fluctuation Medium Dict plus two lazy heaps Store the price at each time. Push into a max heap and a min heap, and skip heap tops whose price was later corrected. 1600 Throne Inheritance Medium A family tree and a preorder walk Keep each person's children in birth order and a set of the dead. The order of succession is a preorder walk that skips the dead. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps In the exam Student.from_text returned a Student because the method calls cls(...), not Person(...). That is why alternative constructors should be class methods.Lesson: Instance, class and static methods Common mistake Store the real value under another name (self._celsius). If the setter wrote self.celsius = value it would call itself forever and raise RecursionError.Lesson: Properties: attributes with rules Remember An abstract base class is a contract: \"every Payment can pay and has a name\". Python refuses to create an object until every abstract method is filled in, so a forgotten method fails at once instead of in front of a customer. collections.abc gives ready made contracts like Sized, Iterable, Sequence and Mapping.Lesson: Abstract base classes Remember __slots__ removes the per object __dict__, so objects are smaller and adding a misspelt attribute fails at once. In Python 3.14 a normal object already keeps its attributes compactly until you touch __dict__, so the saving is smaller than older guides say: about a third in this run. Use slots for classes with very many objects.Lesson: __slots__ and descriptors In the exam A descriptor is an object with __get__, __set__ or __delete__, stored as a class attribute. property, classmethod, staticmethod and every normal method are descriptors. Write your own when the same rule (like \"must be positive\") applies to many attributes in many classes.Lesson: __slots__ and descriptors 1.4"],[52,"py-errors-copy","Exceptions in depth, copying and serialisation","Open chapter exception hierarchies, chaining, tracebacks, notes, exception groups, warnings, shallow and deep copy, pickle and shelve. Key ideas Exception design class AppError(Exception) plus children. super().__init__(message). Catch the child for one problem, the base for all. Chaining raise New() from err sets __cause__. Raising inside except sets __context__. from None hides it. err.__traceback__ has the frames. 3.11 tools err.add_note(text) and err.__notes__. ExceptionGroup(msg, [errors]) caught with except* Type. Copies b = a no copy. a.copy(), a[:], list(a), dict(a), copy.copy: shallow. copy.deepcopy: everything, loops handled by memo. Saving objects pickle.dumps/loads (bytes), dump/load (files in \"wb\"/\"rb\"). Never unpickle untrusted data. shelve: dict on disk, returns copies. Formulas Chance that k independent attempts all failExam favourite P(all fail) = pk, P(success) = 1 − pk A call fails 20 % of the time. With 3 attempts: 1 − 0.23 = 99.2 % success. Lesson: The maths: retries, copies and sizes Objects created by copies of an n by m nested listExam favourite shallow: 1 list. deep: 1 + n lists For [[1, 2], [3, 4]]: shallow makes 1 new list, deep makes 3. The ints are immutable and shared either way. Lesson: The maths: retries, copies and sizes Attempts needed for a target k ≥ log(1 − target) / log(p) p = 0.2, target 99.99 %: k ≥ log(0.0001) / log(0.2) ≈ 5.7, so 6 attempts. Lesson: The maths: retries, copies and sizes Expected attempts until success E = 1 / (1 − p) p = 0.2: 1.25 attempts on average. Lesson: The maths: retries, copies and sizes Tables Table: you write · attribute set on the new error · traceback says You write Attribute set on the new error Traceback says raise New() from old __cause__ = old \"The above exception was the direct cause of the following exception\" raise New() inside an except block __context__ = the error being handled \"During handling of the above exception, another exception occurred\" raise New() from None __cause__ = None, __suppress_context__ = True Only the new error Lesson: Chaining: __cause__ and __context__ Table: · raise one exception · add_note · exceptiongroup and except* raise one exception add_note ExceptionGroup and except* Since Always Python 3.11 Python 3.11 Reports The first problem One problem with extra context Many problems at once Caught with except except (notes in err.__notes__) except*, each matching part separately Typical use Most errors Adding the row, file or user while an error passes up Form checks, many parallel tasks (asyncio TaskGroup) Lesson: Tracebacks, notes and exception groups Table: · exception · warning · assert exception warning assert Stops the program Yes, unless caught No, by default it prints once Yes (AssertionError) For Errors the caller must handle Old or risky use that still works Checks of your own logic while developing Turned off by Never warnings filters python -O removes every assert Example raise ValueError warnings.warn(..., DeprecationWarning) assert total >= 0 Lesson: Warnings and assertions Table: · b = a · shallow copy · deep copy b = a shallow copy deep copy Written as b = a a.copy(), a[:], list(a), dict(a), copy.copy(a) copy.deepcopy(a) New outer object No Yes Yes New inner objects No No, shared Yes, all the way down Cost Free O(length) O(every object inside) Safe to change inner items No No Yes Lesson: Shallow and deep copies Table: · json · pickle · shelve json pickle shelve Format Text, readable by any language Python only bytes A pickle per key in a small database Saves dict, list, str, numbers, bool, None Almost any Python object Almost any object, by string key Safe with untrusted data Yes NO NO Good for APIs, config, data exchange Caches, saving program state, sending objects between Python processes A simple persistent dict Lesson: pickle and shelve: save objects Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 138 Copy List with Random Pointer Medium Deep copy with a memo dict Map every old node to"],[52,"py-errors-copy","Exceptions in depth, copying and serialisation","its new copy in a dict, exactly like copy.deepcopy does with its memo. Then set next and random from the map. 133 Clone Graph Medium DFS with a memo, like deepcopy Copy a node, remember the copy, then copy its neighbours. The memo stops the copy from looping forever around cycles. 297 Serialize and Deserialize Binary Tree Hard Preorder with markers Write the tree in preorder with # for an empty child. Read it back with an iterator, building the left subtree before the right. This is pickling by hand. 449 Serialize and Deserialize BST Medium Preorder values and bounds A BST is fixed by its preorder alone. Rebuild it by giving each value a range (low, high) it must fit in. 535 Encode and Decode TinyURL Medium A counter and two dicts Give each new long URL the next number as its short code, and remember both directions. 606 Construct String from Binary Tree Easy Preorder with brackets Write the value, then the left child in brackets, then the right child in brackets. Keep () for a missing left child only when a right child exists. 331 Verify Preorder Serialization of a Binary Tree Medium Count open slots Start with one slot. Every token fills a slot, and a number opens two more. The string is valid if slots never go below zero and end at zero. 652 Find Duplicate Subtrees Medium Serialise every subtree Turn each subtree into a string (its own little pickle). Count the strings and report a root the second time its string appears. 394 Decode String Medium A stack of unfinished parts Read the text once. A number before [ says how many times to repeat. On [ save the text so far and the count on a stack. On ] repeat the inner text and glue it to the saved text. This is how a loader rebuilds nested data from a flat string. 1106 Parsing A Boolean Expression Hard A stack of values and operators Push every character except commas. On ) pop the values back to the (, take the operator in front of it, and push the single result. Nested expressions are solved from the inside out. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps Remember Give your package one base exception (ShopError) and children for each problem. Callers can then catch one specific error, or everything from your package with one except.Lesson: Exceptions, copies and saved objects In the exam Use raise ... from err when you turn a low level error into a meaningful one, so the original is never lost. Use from None only when the original adds nothing for the reader.Lesson: Chaining: __cause__ and __context__ Common mistake Never unpickle data from a source you do not trust. pickle.loads can run any code the data asks for, so a crafted file can take over your computer. For data from users or the internet use JSON.Lesson: pickle and shelve: save objects In the exam A shelf returns copies. db[\"ravi\"][\"tags\"].append(\"work\") changes a copy that is thrown away. Read the value, change it and assign it back (or open with writeback=True).Lesson: pickle and shelve: save objects 1.5"],[52,"py-metaclasses","Metaprogramming: type, class hooks and metaclasses","Open chapter classes as objects, type(), introspection, __init_subclass__, class decorators, metaclasses, creation order. Key ideas Classes are objects type(x) gives the class. type(Dog) is the metaclass (normally type). type(type) is type. Classes can be stored, passed and returned. type() with three arguments type(name, bases, namespace) builds a class. A class statement does the same. The body runs once and fills the namespace. Introspection getattr(obj, name, default), setattr, hasattr, delattr, callable, vars, dir, __class__, __bases__, __mro__, __subclasses__(), inspect.signature. Hooks __init_subclass__ on the parent runs for every child (registries, checks). Class decorators get the finished class. Metaclasses class Meta(type): __new__ builds the class, __call__ makes objects. Inherited by children. Conflicts raise TypeError. Prefer lighter tools. Formulas Classes made by a factory classes = n1 × n2 × ... × nk make_record for 3 colours and 4 sizes builds 3 × 4 = 12 classes. Lesson: The maths: counting classes, lookups and steps Classes in a full hierarchy below one baseExam favourite b + b2 + ... + bd = b(bd − 1) / (b − 1) With b = 3 children each and d = 2 levels: 3 + 9 = 12 subclasses, each one seen by __init_subclass__. Lesson: The maths: counting classes, lookups and steps Average checks in an if and elif chainExam favourite average = (n + 1) / 2 for n equally likely names A chain of 9 actions needs 5 checks on average. A dict or getattr needs about 1 lookup. Lesson: The maths: counting classes, lookups and steps Worst case attribute lookup dicts checked ≤ 1 + len(C.__mro__) The instance dict, then each class in the MRO. For Kitten, Cat, object: at most 1 + 3 = 4. Lesson: The maths: counting classes, lookups and steps Objects made by a singleton objects = 1 for any n ≥ 1 calls, against n for a normal class 50 calls of Config() make 1 object. Lesson: The maths: counting classes, lookups and steps Steps from an object up to type type(rex) = Dog, type(Dog) = Meta, type(Meta) = type, type(type) = type Three steps reach type, and then the chain repeats itself. Lesson: The maths: counting classes, lookups and steps Tables Table: · class statement · type(name, bases, dict) · collections.namedtuple or dataclasses.make_dataclass class statement type(name, bases, dict) collections.namedtuple or dataclasses.make_dataclass Readable Best Hard for big classes Good Name and fields known only at run time No Yes Yes Methods Written normally Functions in the dict Generated for you, add more by hand Typical use Almost always Factories, plugins, tests Record types built from data Lesson: Building classes with type() Table: tool · what it gives you Tool What it gives you getattr(obj, \"name\", default) The attribute with that name, or the default instead of AttributeError setattr(obj, \"name\", value), delattr(obj, \"name\") Set or remove an attribute when the name is in a string hasattr(obj, \"name\") True if getattr would work callable(x) True if x can be called: functions, methods, classes, objects with __call__ vars(obj) or obj.__dict__ The attributes stored in the object itself dir(obj) Every name you can reach from obj, including inherited ones, sorted obj.__class__, C.__name__, C.__bases__, C.__mro__ The class of an object, its name, its parents and its lookup order C.__subclasses__() The direct children that exist right now inspect.signature(f) The parameters of a function, with defaults Lesson: Introspection: looking inside while the program runs Table: · if and elif chain · getattr(obj, name) · a dict of name to function if and elif chain getattr(obj, name) a dict of name to function Code to add a new action A new branch every time Only the new method One new dict entry Speed Checks branches one by one One lookup One lookup Safety with user input Safe, only listed names Can reach any attribute, so check the name first Safe, only listed names Lesson: Introspection: looking inside while the program runs Table: · class decorator ·"],[52,"py-metaclasses","Metaprogramming: type, class hooks and metaclasses","__init_subclass__ · metaclass class decorator __init_subclass__ metaclass Applies to Only the decorated class Every child (and grandchild) of the parent Every class it builds, and their children When it runs After the class exists While the class is being built Before, during and after building, and on every call of the class Can change the namespace before the class exists No No Yes, in __new__ or __prepare__ Can change how objects are made Only by replacing methods Only by replacing methods Yes, with __call__ Mixes with other metaclasses Always Always Can clash (metaclass conflict) How hard to read Easy Easy Hard Lesson: __init_subclass__ and class decorators Table: · a module level object · a metaclass __call__ · __new__ that returns a saved object a module level object a metaclass __call__ __new__ that returns a saved object Code needed One line: config = Config() A metaclass class A few lines in the class Easy to understand Yes No Medium __init__ runs again on each call Not called again No Yes, a common surprise Easy to test and replace Yes Hard Hard Lesson: Metaclasses Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 211 Design Add and Search Words Data Structure Medium A trie of dicts, search with recursion Store each word letter by letter in nested dicts. A dot must try every child, so search with a small recursive helper. 380 Insert Delete GetRandom O(1) Medium A list plus a dict of positions The list allows a random pick. The dict maps each value to its index. To remove, move the last value into the hole and pop. 2502 Design Memory Allocator Medium Scan a list of owners Keep one owner id per memory unit (0 means free). allocate looks for the first run of free units that is long enough. 2353 Design a Food Rating System Medium A heap per cuisine with lazy deletion Push (-rating, name) into the heap of the cuisine. When asked, pop entries whose rating is out of date before reading the top. 2671 Frequency Tracker Medium Two counters Count each number, and also count how many numbers have each frequency. Then hasFrequency is one dict lookup. 1993 Operations on Tree Medium Parents list, children lists, a lock dict lock and unlock check one node. upgrade checks that no ancestor is locked and at least one descendant is, then unlocks every descendant. 1357 Apply Discount Every n Orders Medium A counter and a price dict Count customers. Every n-th bill is multiplied by (100 − discount) / 100. 1476 Subrectangle Queries Medium Keep a list of updates Instead of painting every cell, remember each update. getValue checks the updates from newest to oldest and falls back to the original grid. 2166 Design Bitset Medium A list of bits plus a flipped flag flip only toggles a flag, so it is O(1). Keep a count of ones and fix it up when flip happens. 2424 Longest Uploaded Prefix Medium A set and a pointer that only moves forward Remember uploaded videos in a set. The prefix pointer moves right while the next video is there. Each video is passed once in total. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps In the exam type has two jobs. With one argument, type(x) tells you the class of x. With three arguments, type(name, bases, namespace) builds a new class. And type(type) is type: type is its own class, which is where the chain ends.Lesson: Classes are objects too Remember A class statement and a type() call give exactly the same kind of class. Python itself turns every class statement into a call like type(\"Cat\", (object,), namespace). You only need type() directly when the name or the fields are known only while the program runs, for example from a config file.Lesson: Building classes with type() Common mistake The class body runs once, at the moment Python reaches the class statement, not each time you make an object. Names it creates (tax, price, total) end up in the namespace dict, which becomes the class __dict__. That is why a class body can hold any normal code, even print.Lesson: Building classes with type() In the"],[52,"py-metaclasses","Metaprogramming: type, class hooks and metaclasses","exam __init_subclass__ (PEP 487, Python 3.6) runs on the PARENT every time a child class is created. It is a class method automatically. Keyword arguments in the class line, like name=\"csv\", are passed to it. Always call super().__init_subclass__(**kwargs) so other parents in a multiple inheritance chain get their turn.Lesson: __init_subclass__ and class decorators Remember A class decorator is a function that takes a finished class and returns a class, usually the same one after changing it. @dataclass, @functools.total_ordering and @enum.unique are class decorators from the standard library. Stacked decorators run from the bottom up, so auto_repr ran before route.Lesson: __init_subclass__ and class decorators In the exam A metaclass is a subclass of type. Its __new__(mcs, name, bases, namespace) gets everything the class statement collected and must return the new class, normally from super().__new__. Children of Job use CheckMeta too, because Python uses the metaclass of the base class when none is given. The check fires when the class is DEFINED, long before anyone uses it.Lesson: Metaclasses Common mistake Calling a class, Config(...), really calls type(Config).__call__. The normal type.__call__ runs __new__ and then __init__. Overriding __call__ in a metaclass changes what calling the class does: here it returns the saved object. Notice that the second call did not even run __init__, so mode stayed \"prod\".Lesson: Metaclasses Remember You already use metaclasses: ABC is built by ABCMeta (that is how abstract methods block objects) and every Enum by EnumType (that is why len(Colour) and for c in Colour work on the class itself). A class whose bases have two unrelated metaclasses cannot be made, because Python cannot pick one metaclass that is a child of both.Lesson: Metaclasses"],[52,"step-advanced-2","Step 2Advanced Python · Iterators, generators and context managers","2.1"],[52,"py-generators","Iterators and generators","Open chapter iterator protocol, iterator classes, yield, pipelines, yield from, send, throw, close, lazy built ins. Key ideas Iterator protocol iter(x) gives an iterator. next(it) gives a value or raises StopIteration. next(it, default) avoids the error. A for loop does this for you. Iterable versus iterator Iterables (list, range, dict) give a fresh iterator each time. Iterators (zip, map, files, generators) are used up after one pass. Generators A function with yield returns a generator. Nothing runs until next(). It pauses at each yield and keeps its locals. Return ends it. Pipelines and laziness Chain generators: values are made only when asked, memory stays constant, and islice or break stops work early. Advanced control yield from delegates and returns the inner return value. send(v), throw(e), close() (GeneratorExit). Prime with next() before send. Formulas Memory of a list against a generatorExam favourite list: about 8n bytes of pointers plus the items, generator: a constant (about 200 bytes) 100,000 squares: the list needs about 800 KB of pointers, the generator stays near 200 bytes. Lesson: The maths: memory, early stopping and counting Work done by a lazy searchExam favourite items read = position of the first match (k), not n Looking for the first even square above 50 in 1, 2, 3, ... reads 8 numbers and stops (8 × 8 = 64). Lesson: The maths: memory, early stopping and counting Running average after k values averagek = (x1 + x2 + ... + xk) / k After 10, 20 and 60: (10 + 20 + 60) / 3 = 30. Lesson: The maths: memory, early stopping and counting Number of next() calls to see StopIteration values + 1 countdown(4) gives 4 values, and the 5th call raises StopIteration. Lesson: The maths: memory, early stopping and counting Subsets and combinations (LeetCode 77 and 90) subsets = 2n, combinations of k from n = n! / (k! (n − k)!) From 5 numbers choose 2: 5! / (2! × 3!) = 10. Lesson: The maths: memory, early stopping and counting A pipeline of s stages over n items time grows with n × s, memory with s only 106 lines through 4 stages: 4 × 106 stage steps, but only 4 paused generators in memory. Lesson: The maths: memory, early stopping and counting Tables Table: · iterable (like shelf, list) · iterator (like countdown) iterable (like Shelf, list) iterator (like Countdown) Has __iter__ Yes, returns a NEW iterator Yes, returns itself Has __next__ No Yes Loop over it twice Works both times Second loop gets nothing Remembers a position No Yes Examples list, str, dict, range, set iter(list), zip, map, enumerate, files, generators Lesson: Writing iterators as classes Table: · return a list · iterator class · generator function return a list iterator class generator function Lines of code Few Many (__init__, __iter__, __next__) Few Memory for n items Grows with n Constant Constant Can be endless No Yes Yes Stops work early No, everything is made first Yes Yes Use twice, index, len Yes No No Lesson: Generator functions and yield Table: · read everything into lists · a generator pipeline read everything into lists a generator pipeline Memory The whole file, maybe gigabytes One line at a time First result appears After the whole file is read Right away Stopping early Too late, the work is done No extra work Code One big loop with many ifs Small reusable stages Lesson: Generator pipelines Table: method · what it does Method What it does next(g) Run to the next yield and return the value it yields g.send(v) Resume, and make the paused yield expression evaluate to v. The first call must be next(g) or g.send(None) g.throw(exc) Raise exc at the paused yield. The generator may catch it and go on g.close() Raise GeneratorExit at the paused yield so finally blocks run, then mark it closed Lesson: yield from, send, throw and close Table: built in · lazy? · use more than once? Built in Lazy? Use more than once? range Yes, works out each value from start, step and index Yes, it is a sequence dict.keys(), values(), items() Yes, live views of the"],[52,"py-generators","Iterators and generators","dict Yes zip, map, filter, enumerate, reversed Yes, iterators No open(...) file objects Yes, one line per next() No, unless you seek(0) iter(function, sentinel) Calls function until it returns sentinel No sorted, list, tuple, sum No, they use up the whole input They return a value Lesson: Lazy tools you already use Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 94 Binary Tree Inorder Traversal Easy A recursive generator with yield from Yield everything from the left subtree, then the node, then everything from the right subtree. 173 Binary Search Tree Iterator Medium Keep one generator inside the class An inorder generator of a BST gives the values in sorted order. next takes the next value. To answer hasNext, look one value ahead and remember it. 1305 All Elements in Two Binary Search Trees Medium Merge two sorted generators Each BST gives a sorted stream with an inorder generator. heapq.merge joins sorted streams lazily, like the merge step of merge sort. 23 Merge k Sorted Lists Hard Turn each list into a generator, then heapq.merge A small generator walks one linked list. heapq.merge takes k sorted streams and always hands out the smallest next value, using a heap of size k. 77 Combinations Medium A backtracking generator Choose the next number from start to n, yield when k numbers are chosen. itertools.combinations does the same in C. 90 Subsets II Medium Sort, then skip equal neighbours Sort first so equal numbers sit together. At each depth, do not start a branch with the same number twice. 216 Combination Sum III Medium Filter a combinations stream Every answer is a set of k different digits from 1 to 9. Generate the combinations lazily and keep the ones whose sum is n. 401 Binary Watch Easy Loop over every time, count the lit bits There are only 12 × 60 times. For each one count the 1 bits of the hour and the minute with bin(x).count(\"1\"). 1415 The k-th Lexicographical String of All Happy Strings of Length n Medium Generate in order, stop at k A generator makes happy strings in alphabetical order. islice jumps to the k-th one without building the rest. 119 Pascal's Triangle II Easy A generator of rows Each row is made from the one before: add neighbours and put 1 at both ends. Yield rows one by one and keep the one you need. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps In the exam iter(nums) gave a NEW iterator each time, so the first answer was False. An iterator returns itself from iter(), so the second was True. That is the whole difference: an iterable can make many iterators, an iterator is one walk that can be used once.Lesson: Iterators and generators: one value at a time Common mistake Raising StopIteration ends the loop. Do not raise it inside a generator body: since Python 3.7 (PEP 479) it is turned into RuntimeError. In a generator, simply return.Lesson: Writing iterators as classes In the exam A function that contains yield anywhere is a generator function. Calling it runs nothing: the first line, print(\"start\"), only ran at the first next(). After the last value the generator is closed, and list() on a used generator gives an empty list.Lesson: Generator functions and yield Remember Building the pipeline read nothing. Asking for 2 errors read only 4 lines and never touched \"WARN slow\" or the last line. Each value travels through all three stages before the next line is read. Unix pipes such as grep | cut | head work the same way.Lesson: Generator pipelines Remember yield from other passes every value of other straight to whoever is asking, and when other finishes, its return value becomes the value of the yield from expression. It is the clean way to split a big generator into smaller ones, and it handles send and throw for you too.Lesson: yield from, send, throw and close Common mistake A common bug: saving map(...) or zip(...) in a variable, looping over it once for a check, and then finding it empty the second time. Wrap it in list(...) if you need it twice.Lesson: Lazy tools you"],[52,"py-generators","Iterators and generators","already use 2.2"],[52,"py-itertools","itertools, functools and operator","Open chapter endless iterators, slicing, joining, groupby, combinatorics with formulas, reduce, partial, singledispatch, operator. Key ideas Endless and slicing count, cycle, repeat never stop: use islice, zip, takewhile. takewhile/dropwhile cut at the first failure. compress applies a mask. Joining chain, chain.from_iterable, zip_longest(fillvalue=), pairwise, batched, starmap, accumulate(func, initial=), tee. groupby Joins neighbours with equal keys only. Sort by the same key first. Use each group before moving on. Combinatorics product n**r, permutations n!/(n-r)!, combinations C(n,r), with replacement C(n+r-1,r). math.comb and math.perm count without listing. functools and operator reduce (left fold), partial, singledispatch, lru_cache and cache_info. itemgetter, attrgetter, methodcaller as key functions. Formulas product(items, repeat=r)Exam favourite nr 3 items, r = 2: 32 = 9 tuples. Lesson: The maths: counting, prefix sums and folds permutations(items, r)Exam favourite P(n, r) = n! / (n − r)! 4 items, r = 2: 24 / 2 = 12. Lesson: The maths: counting, prefix sums and folds combinations(items, r)Exam favourite C(n, r) = n! / (r! (n − r)!) 4 items, r = 2: 24 / (2 × 2) = 6. Lesson: The maths: counting, prefix sums and folds combinations_with_replacement(items, r) C(n + r − 1, r) 3 items, r = 2: C(4, 2) = 6. Lesson: The maths: counting, prefix sums and folds Pascal's rule C(n, r) = C(n − 1, r − 1) + C(n − 1, r) C(4, 2) = C(3, 1) + C(3, 2) = 3 + 3 = 6: tuples with the first item, plus tuples without it. Lesson: The maths: counting, prefix sums and folds Prefix sums with accumulateExam favourite P = accumulate(a, initial=0), sum of a[i] to a[j] = P[j + 1] − P[i] a = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5], P = [0, 3, 4, 8, 9, 14], a[1] + a[2] + a[3] = 9 − 3 = 6. Lesson: The maths: counting, prefix sums and folds reduce is a left fold reduce(f, [a, b, c]) = f(f(a, b), c) reduce(operator.sub, [10, 3, 2]) = (10 − 3) − 2 = 5. Lesson: The maths: counting, prefix sums and folds Tables Table: · filter · takewhile · dropwhile filter takewhile dropwhile compress Looks at Every item Items until the test first fails Items until the test first fails Each item and its mask value Keeps Items that pass The front part that passes Everything from the first failure on Items where the mask is true Works on endless input Yes, but never finishes Yes, it stops by itself Yes Yes Typical use Clean a list Read until a blank line Skip a header Apply a yes or no column Lesson: Taking and dropping items Table: tool · what it gives · instead of Tool What it gives Instead of chain(a, b, c) All items of a, then b, then c a + b + c, which builds new lists chain.from_iterable(lists) Flattens one level A nested loop zip_longest(a, b, fillvalue=x) Pairs until the LONGEST input ends zip, which stops at the shortest pairwise(xs) Each item with the next one zip(xs, xs[1:]) batched(xs, n) Tuples of n items, the last may be shorter Slicing by hand starmap(f, pairs) f(*pair) for each pair A lambda that unpacks accumulate(xs, f, initial=) Running results of f (sum by default) A loop with a total variable tee(it, n) n independent iterators from one list(it), when the data is small Lesson: Joining, pairing and running totals Table: · sorted then groupby · a dict of lists · collections.counter sorted then groupby a dict of lists collections.Counter Needs sorted data Yes No No Memory One group at a time All groups at once One count per key Keeps runs (aaa then b then aa) Yes, on unsorted data No No Best for Run length encoding and data that is already sorted General grouping Counting only Lesson: groupby: runs of equal keys Table: · nested for loops · itertools · math.comb and math.perm nested for loops itertools math.comb and math.perm Number of choices fixed in the code Yes, one loop each No, repeat=r or r is a variable Not needed Gives the actual tuples Yes Yes, lazily No, only the count Speed Python speed C speed Instant Use when Two or three simple loops You need the tuples You only need how"],[52,"py-itertools","itertools, functools and operator","many Lesson: Combinations and permutations Table: · partial(power, exponent=2) · lambda b: power(b, 2) · def square(b) partial(power, exponent=2) lambda b: power(b, 2) def square(b) Code Short Short Longest Shows what it wraps Yes: .func, .args, .keywords No No Can be pickled and sent to another process Yes No Yes Has its own docstring and name No No Yes Lesson: functools: reduce, partial, singledispatch and caches Table: · lambda r: (r[1], r[0]) · itemgetter(1, 0) lambda r: (r[1], r[0]) itemgetter(1, 0) Readable Yes Yes, once you know it Speed Python function call Faster, written in C Works with attributes Yes Use attrgetter(\"pages\") Can be pickled No Yes Lesson: operator: itemgetter, attrgetter, methodcaller Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 303 Range Sum Query - Immutable Easy Prefix sums with accumulate Build P = accumulate(nums, initial=0) once. Then the sum from left to right is P[right + 1] − P[left], one subtraction per query. 1732 Find the Highest Altitude Easy Running totals, then max The altitudes are the running totals of gain starting from 0, which is accumulate(gain, initial=0). 1310 XOR Queries of a Subarray Medium Prefix XOR with accumulate XOR works like a sum that undoes itself: P[r + 1] ^ P[l] is the XOR of arr[l] to arr[r]. 1313 Decompress Run-Length Encoded List Easy batched, repeat and chain Read the list two numbers at a time with batched. Each pair (freq, val) becomes repeat(val, freq). chain joins them. 443 String Compression Medium groupby gives the runs Each run of the same letter becomes the letter plus its count (only if the count is above 1). Write the result back into chars and return its length. 1446 Consecutive Characters Easy Longest run with groupby groupby splits the text into runs of one letter. The answer is the length of the longest run. 830 Positions of Large Groups Easy groupby over enumerate Group (index, letter) pairs by the letter. The first and last index of each group give its interval. Keep groups of 3 or more. 784 Letter Case Permutation Medium product of choices per character A letter has two choices (lower, upper), a digit has one. product of all the choices gives every string. 47 Permutations II Medium permutations, then remove repeats permutations treats equal numbers as different positions, so it repeats some orders. A set removes the repeats. Fine for small inputs, backtracking with a counter is faster. 1588 Sum of All Odd Length Subarrays Easy Prefix sums, then every odd window With prefix sums each subarray sum is one subtraction. Try every start and every odd length. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps Common mistake count, cycle and repeat never end on their own. Always stop them: with islice, zip with something finite, takewhile, or a break.Lesson: The iterator toolbox Remember tee keeps every value that one copy has seen and the other has not, so if one copy runs far ahead it can use as much memory as a list. Use it when both copies move at a similar pace.Lesson: Joining, pairing and running totals Common mistake groupby only joins items that are NEXT to each other. On unsorted data tea appeared twice. Sort by the same key first, or use a dict (collections.defaultdict(list)) when you cannot sort.Lesson: groupby: runs of equal keys In the exam singledispatch chose the int version for 21, the list version for [1, 2] and the base version for 2.5. It looks at the type of the FIRST argument only. For methods use functools.singledispatchmethod.Lesson: functools: reduce, partial, singledispatch and caches Remember lru_cache(maxsize=2) keeps the 2 most recently used answers. Calling 1, 2, 1, 3, 1 gave 2 hits: when 3 arrived, 2 was the least recently used and was thrown away.Lesson: functools: reduce, partial, singledispatch and caches Remember The itertools documentation ends with a long list of recipes like these: sliding_window, unique_everseen, first_true, roundrobin, partition and more. The third party package more-itertools has"],[52,"py-itertools","itertools, functools and operator","all of them ready to install with pip.Lesson: Recipes built from the tools 2.3"],[52,"py-context","Context managers and the with statement","Open chapter with, __enter__ and __exit__, suppressing errors, @contextmanager, standard managers, exit order, ExitStack. Key ideas The with protocol with X as v: v = X.__enter__(), body, then X.__exit__(type, err, tb) always. Methods are looked up on the type. Suppressing __exit__ returning True hides the exception. contextlib.suppress(Error) hides only the listed types. @contextmanager Set up, then yield the as value inside try, then clean up in finally. Re raise errors you do not handle. Works as a decorator. Standard managers open, Lock, TemporaryDirectory, chdir, redirect_stdout, localcontext, closing, nullcontext, sqlite3 (transaction), mock.patch. Several managers Entered left to right, exited right to left. Bracketed form across lines. ExitStack: enter_context and callback for a dynamic number. Formulas Exit order of n nested managersExam favourite the k-th one entered is the (n − k + 1)-th one exited with A, B, C: A is entered 1st and exited 3rd. Lesson: The maths: leaks, failures and exit order Chance that at least one of n steps failsExam favourite 1 − (1 − p)n Each of 10 steps fails with p = 0.01: 1 − 0.9910 ≈ 9.6 %. Errors are common, so clean up must not depend on success. Lesson: The maths: leaks, failures and exit order Expected leaks without with leaks = calls × p (chance a path forgets to close) 1000 calls with p = 0.02 leak about 20 files. With with: 0. Lesson: The maths: leaks, failures and exit order Time until a leaking server runs out of file handles requests = free handles / leaks per request 1024 handles and 2 leaks per request: 512 requests. Lesson: The maths: leaks, failures and exit order Elapsed time with a timer context manager elapsed = texit − tenter Entered at 1.50 s, exited at 2.75 s: 1.25 s. Lesson: The maths: leaks, failures and exit order Tables Table: method · called · gets · returns Method Called Gets Returns __enter__(self) When the with line runs Nothing else The value stored by as (often self, a file, or a connection) __exit__(self, kind, err, tb) When the block ends, for ANY reason None, None, None, or the exception type, object and traceback True to hide the exception, False or None to let it continue Lesson: Writing a context manager class Table: · clean up as the last line · try and finally · with and a context manager clean up as the last line try and finally with and a context manager Runs after an error No Yes Yes Runs after return or break No Yes Yes Code at each use One line, often forgotten Four or more lines One line Set up and clean up kept together No No, they are far apart Yes, in one class or function Reusable No No Yes Lesson: Writing a context manager class Table: · try / except / pass · with suppress(error) try / except / pass with suppress(Error) Lines Four Two Says what it means Not at a glance Yes Can run code on the error Yes No Other errors Pass through Pass through Lesson: Suppressing exceptions Table: · a class with __enter__ and __exit__ · @contextmanager on a generator a class with __enter__ and __exit__ @contextmanager on a generator Code Longer Short Keeps state between uses Yes, in attributes Only for one with Can decide to hide errors Return True from __exit__ Catch the error around yield and do not re raise Can be used as a decorator With ContextDecorator Yes, automatically Best for Reusable objects like connections Quick set up and clean up pairs Lesson: contextmanager: a context manager from a generator Table: context manager · set up · clean up Context manager Set up Clean up open(...) Open a file Close it threading.Lock() Acquire the lock Release it tempfile.TemporaryDirectory() Make a folder Delete it with everything inside contextlib.chdir(path) Change the working folder Change back contextlib.redirect_stdout(f) Send print to f Send it back to the screen decimal.localcontext() A private copy of the decimal settings Restore the old settings contextlib.closing(obj) Nothing Call obj.close() contextlib.nullcontext(v) Nothing, gives v Nothing sqlite3"],[52,"py-context","Context managers and the with statement","connection Start a transaction Commit, or roll back on error (it does not close) unittest.mock.patch(...) Replace an object for a test Put the original back Lesson: Context managers in the standard library Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 636 Exclusive Time of Functions Medium A stack of running functions Logs open and close like nested with blocks. Keep a stack of running ids. When something starts or ends, give the time since the last event to the function on top. 946 Validate Stack Sequences Medium Replay the pushes and pops on a real stack Push each value. After every push, pop while the top is the next value that should come out. It works if the stack ends empty, just as every with block must close in reverse order. 921 Minimum Add to Make Parentheses Valid Medium Count open brackets Track how many \"(\" are still open. A \")\" with nothing open needs an added \"(\". At the end every open one needs a \")\". 1249 Minimum Remove to Make Valid Parentheses Medium A stack of open positions Push the index of each \"(\". A \")\" with an empty stack is removed. Any \"(\" left on the stack at the end is removed too. 856 Score of Parentheses Medium A stack of scores per level Opening starts a new level with score 0. Closing turns the level into 1 if it was empty, or double its score otherwise, and adds it to the level outside. 1190 Reverse Substrings Between Each Pair of Parentheses Medium A stack of unfinished text On \"(\" save the text so far and start fresh. On \")\" reverse the inner text and glue it to the saved text. 32 Longest Valid Parentheses Hard A stack of indexes with a base Keep the index of the last unmatched position at the bottom of the stack. After each match, the length is the current index minus the index on top. 735 Asteroid Collision Medium A stack of survivors Only a left moving asteroid can hit the right moving ones on the stack. Pop the smaller ones, stop if it is destroyed. 844 Backspace String Compare Easy Build each text with a stack Push letters, pop on \"#\". Compare the two results. 2390 Removing Stars From a String Medium A stack again Each star removes the closest letter on its left: push letters and pop on \"*\". Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps Remember A file object is its own context manager: its __exit__ calls close(). Always open files with with. In CPython an unclosed file is usually closed by garbage collection, but other Pythons (PyPy) and long running programs can run out of file handles first.Lesson: Context managers and the with statement Common mistake Only return True from __exit__ on purpose and only for the exact errors you expect. Hiding every error makes bugs invisible. contextlib.suppress(SomeError) is the clear, ready made way to say \"this error is fine here\".Lesson: Suppressing exceptions In the exam Everything before yield is the set up, the yielded value goes into as, and everything after yield is the clean up. The try and finally around the yield is what makes the clean up run after an error: without it, an exception in the body would be raised at the yield and skip the rest.Lesson: contextmanager: a context manager from a generator Remember nullcontext is handy when a with is sometimes needed and sometimes not: lock if threaded else nullcontext().Lesson: Context managers in the standard library In the exam Managers are entered left to right and exited in the reverse order, like a stack: the last one opened is the first one closed. That is what you want: an inner resource that depends on an outer one is cleaned up first.Lesson: Several managers and ExitStack Remember ExitStack is for when the number of managers is only known while the program runs. enter_context adds a manager, callback adds any function to call at the end, and everything is cleaned up in reverse order, even if opening the third file fails.Lesson: Several managers and ExitStack"],[52,"step-advanced-3","Step 3Advanced Python · Professional code: style, testing and logging","3.1"],[52,"py-style","Clean code: PEP 8, docstrings and type hints","Open chapter Zen of Python, PEP 8 layout and names, bug preventing habits, docstrings, doctest, type hints, ruff, black, mypy. Key ideas PEP 8 layout 4 spaces, 79 characters, 2 blank lines around top level defs, imports one per line grouped stdlib, third party, local. PEP 8 names snake_case functions and variables, CapWords classes, UPPER_CASE constants, _private by convention. Bug preventing habits is None, if items:, isinstance, startswith and endswith, None instead of mutable defaults, small try blocks. Docstrings PEP 257: \"\"\"One line summary.\"\"\" then details. __doc__ and help() read it. doctest runs >>> examples. Type hints and tools list[int], int | None, Callable, Protocol, TypedDict, Literal, Final, def f[T]. Not checked at run time: use mypy, ruff and black. Formulas Cyclomatic complexity (McCabe)Exam favourite M = E − N + 2 (edges and nodes of the control flow graph) = decision points + 1 circle_area has one if, so M = 1 + 1 = 2. ruff rule C901 warns when M is above a limit, often 10. Lesson: The maths: complexity, paths and line budgets Paths through k independent if statementsExam favourite paths = 2k 4 separate ifs give 16 paths. Smaller functions keep this testable. Lesson: The maths: complexity, paths and line budgets Minimum tests to cover every independent path tests ≥ M A function with M = 6 needs at least 6 tests for basis path coverage. Lesson: The maths: complexity, paths and line budgets Characters left on a line room = 79 − indent Code nested 2 levels deep (8 spaces) has 71 characters left. Lesson: The maths: complexity, paths and line budgets Spaces of indentation spaces = 4 × depth 3 levels deep: 12 spaces. Deep nesting eats the line budget, a hint to split the function. Lesson: The maths: complexity, paths and line budgets Tables Table: rule · do · do not Rule Do Do not Indentation 4 spaces per level Tabs, 2 spaces, or a mix Line length At most 79 characters (72 for comments and docstrings) Lines that need side scrolling Blank lines 2 around top level functions and classes, 1 between methods None at all, or many Imports One per line, at the top: standard library, then third party, then your own, each group sorted import sys, os in the middle of the file Spaces x = 1, a == b, f(a, b), d[\"k\"] x=1, f( a ,b ), d [\"k\"] Functions and variables snake_case: circle_area, total_price CircleArea, totalPrice, A Classes CapWords: ShoppingCart shopping_cart Constants UPPER_CASE: MAX_USERS maxUsers Private by convention _helper, _cache __everything (name mangling, only when needed) Long expressions Break inside brackets, operators at the start of the new line Backslashes at line ends Lesson: PEP 8: layout and names Table: · x == none · x is none · if not x x == None x is None if not x Asks Does x say it equals None (__eq__)? Is x the None object? Is x empty, zero, False or None? Can a class change the answer Yes No Yes, with __bool__ or __len__ Right for Never for None Checking for a missing value Checking for \"nothing in it\" Lesson: Rules that prevent bugs Table: pep 257 rule · example PEP 257 rule Example Always triple double quotes \"\"\"Return the mean.\"\"\" First line: a short summary that ends with a full stop, written as a command \"\"\"Return the mean of a list.\"\"\", not \"\"\"Returns the mean\"\"\" Longer docstring: summary, blank line, then details Arguments, return value, errors raised, examples Every public module, function, class and method gets one Private helpers may skip it A class docstring says what the class is, __init__ says how to make one See Wallet above Lesson: Docstrings and doctest (PEP 257) Table: hint · means Hint Means int, str, float, bool, None That exact kind of value list[int], dict[str, int], tuple[int, str], set[str] Containers and what is inside int | None (old: Optional[int]) An int or None Callable[[int], str] A function taking an int and returning a str Literal[\"up\", \"down\"] Only these values TypedDict A dict with known keys and value types Protocol Any class that has these methods"],[52,"py-style","Clean code: PEP 8, docstrings and type hints","(structural, \"duck\" typing) Final Must not be changed after it is set def f[T](x: list[T]) -> T and type Alias = ... Generics and aliases with the Python 3.12 syntax Lesson: Type hints Table: · no hints · hints and mypy · checks with isinstance at run time no hints hints and mypy checks with isinstance at run time When it is found When a user hits that line Before the program runs When that line runs Cost while running None None A little on every call Documents the code No Yes Partly Catches every mistake No Most, if hints are complete Only the ones you check Lesson: Type hints Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 520 Detect Capital Easy Three allowed shapes Like a naming rule: all capitals, all small, or only the first letter capital. 482 License Key Formatting Easy Clean, then group from the right Remove dashes, make it upper case, then cut groups of k from the end so only the first group may be shorter. 273 Integer to English Words Hard Groups of three digits with clear helper names Split the number into groups of three from the right (ones, Thousand, Million, Billion). Turn each group under 1000 into words with a small helper, and skip groups that are zero. 824 Goat Latin Easy Follow the rules word by word Vowel start: add \"ma\". Otherwise move the first letter to the end and add \"ma\". Then add one \"a\" per word position. 1859 Sorting the Sentence Easy Sort by the last character Each word ends with its position. Sort by that digit and remove it. 2047 Number of Valid Words in a Sentence Easy Check each rule, like a linter No digits, at most one hyphen with letters on both sides, at most one punctuation mark and only at the end. 929 Unique Email Addresses Easy Normalise, then count with a set In the local part drop everything after \"+\" and remove dots. Keep the domain as it is. 1556 Thousand Separator Easy Format with a separator Python can group digits with format(n, \",\"). LeetCode wants dots, so swap the commas. 1694 Reformat Phone Number Easy Blocks of 3, end with 2 and 2 if 4 are left Keep only digits. Take blocks of 3 while more than 4 remain. Then 4 digits become 2 and 2, otherwise one last block. 2299 Strong Password Checker II Easy One check per rule At least 8 characters, a lower case letter, an upper case letter, a digit, a special character, and no two equal characters side by side. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps Remember The Zen is a set of values, not rules. Lines like \"Readability counts\", \"Explicit is better than implicit\" and \"Errors should never pass silently\" explain WHY PEP 8 and good habits exist.Lesson: Clean code: style, docstrings and type hints In the exam black fixed the layout but not the names, the unused import or == None: a formatter only moves spaces and line breaks, it never changes meaning. That is why teams use a formatter AND a linter. ruff can do both (ruff format and ruff check).Lesson: PEP 8: layout and names Remember PEP 8 also says: use isinstance instead of comparing types, use startswith and endswith instead of slicing, do not compare booleans to True with ==, and keep try blocks small so you only catch the error you expect.Lesson: Rules that prevent bugs Remember doctest finds >>> lines in docstrings, runs them and compares the output with the next line. TestResults(failed=0, attempted=2) means both examples still work. It keeps documentation honest. Real test suites come in 3.2.Lesson: Docstrings and doctest (PEP 257) In the exam Hints are stored in __annotations__ but Python never checks them while running: first(\"hello\") worked although the hint says list. In Python 3.14 annotations are evaluated lazily (PEP 649 and 749), so a hint can even name a class defined later in the file.Lesson: Type hints 3.2"],[52,"py-testing","Testing: unittest, pytest and mocks","Open chapter unittest, fixtures, failures and errors, mocks, pytest, coverage, property based testing, test strategy. Key ideas unittest class T(unittest.TestCase), methods test_..., assertEqual, assertTrue, assertIn, assertRaises (with), assertAlmostEqual, setUp, subTest, skip. Outcomes PASSED, FAILED (assert false), ERROR (other exception). Read the last line. Tests must not depend on each other. Mocks Mock().m.return_value, side_effect (list or exception), assert_called_once_with, call_count, patch and patch.object. Inject dependencies. pytest test_ functions with plain assert, fixtures by parameter name, parametrize, approx, raises(match=), tmp_path, monkeypatch, -v, -k. Strategy Edges and just outside, every fixed bug, coverage is not correctness, property tests with Hypothesis, the test pyramid, TDD. Formulas Line coverageExam favourite coverage = lines run / total lines × 100 % 17 of 19 lines run: 17 / 19 ≈ 89.5 %. Lesson: The maths: coverage, boundaries and binary search Boundary value tests for a range a to bExam favourite test a − 1, a, b, b + 1 (and one value inside) percent from 0 to 100: test −1, 0, 100, 101 and 50, five tests. Lesson: The maths: coverage, boundaries and binary search Combinations of k options with v values each all combinations = vk 3 settings with 4 values each: 64 tests. Pairwise testing covers every pair of values with far fewer, about v2 = 16. Lesson: The maths: coverage, boundaries and binary search Binary search steps (the LeetCode set)Exam favourite steps ≤ ⌈log2(n + 1)⌉ n = 1000 sorted items: at most 10 steps. This is also how git bisect finds the commit that broke a test. Lesson: The maths: coverage, boundaries and binary search Chance a random test hits a bug that needs one value out of N 1 − (1 − 1/N)t after t tests N = 1000 and t = 100: about 9.5 %. Picking edge cases on purpose beats random luck. Lesson: The maths: coverage, boundaries and binary search Tables Table: tool · what it does Tool What it does setUp(self) and tearDown(self) Run before and after EVERY test, so each test starts fresh setUpClass(cls) and tearDownClass(cls) Run once for the whole class (for slow set up like a database) self.subTest(...) Inside a loop, report each case separately and keep going after a failure @unittest.skip(reason), skipIf(cond, reason) Do not run this test, and say why @unittest.expectedFailure A known bug: the test is expected to fail for now python -m unittest discover Find every test_*.py file in the folder and run it Lesson: setUp, subTest and skip Table: · passed · failed · error PASSED FAILED ERROR Means Every assert was true An assert was false The test crashed with another exception Usually points to Nothing to do Wrong result: a bug in the code or in the test A crash: bad input handling, a typo, a missing file First thing to read The two values in the message The last line of the traceback Lesson: Reading failures and errors Table: mock feature · use Mock feature Use Mock().method.return_value = x The fake method returns x side_effect = [a, b] or an exception A different answer on each call, or raise an error to test failure handling assert_called_once_with(...), call_count, call_args Check HOW your code used the dependency patch(\"module.name\") and patch.object(Class, \"attr\") Replace a real object for the length of a with block or a test, then put it back MagicMock A Mock that also supports special methods like len() and with Lesson: Mocks: replacing what you cannot test Table: · call the real service · a mock · a fake local server call the real service a Mock a fake local server Speed Slow Instant Fast Works offline and in CI No Yes Yes Can test timeouts and errors Hard Easy with side_effect Yes Catches changes in the real service Yes No No Lesson: Mocks: replacing what you cannot test Table: · unittest · pytest · doctest unittest pytest doctest Install Built in pip install pytest Built in A test is A method in a TestCase class Any test_ function A >>> example in a docstring Checks"],[52,"py-testing","Testing: unittest, pytest and mocks","with self.assertEqual and friends Plain assert, with detailed messages Printed output Set up setUp and tearDown Fixtures, shared and combined by name None Many inputs subTest parametrize More examples Runs the others No Yes, unittest and doctest tests too No Lesson: pytest: the most used test runner Table: kind of test · checks · speed · how many Kind of test Checks Speed How many Unit test One function or class, dependencies mocked Milliseconds Most of them Integration test Several parts together: code plus a real database or file Seconds Some End to end test The whole program as a user uses it (for web apps: Selenium or Playwright) Slow A few for the main journeys Lesson: What to test and how much Table: always test · example for discount(price, percent) Always test Example for discount(price, percent) A normal case discount(80, 50) is 40 The edges of every range percent 0 and 100, price 0 Just outside the range percent −1 and 101 raise ValueError Awkward values floats like 0.3, very large prices Every bug you fixed A regression test that would have caught it Lesson: What to test and how much Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 704 Binary Search Easy Halve the range each step Keep low and high. Look at the middle. The famous bug is an off by one in the loop condition, so test arrays of length 0, 1 and 2. 35 Search Insert Position Easy Binary search for the first place not smaller When the loop ends, low is where target belongs. Test before the first item and after the last. 278 First Bad Version Easy Binary search on a yes or no question Exactly what git bisect does with a failing test. The is_bad check is passed in, so a test can give a fake one. 374 Guess Number Higher or Lower Easy Binary search against an API The guess API answers -1, 1 or 0. In tests we inject a fake API, like a Mock with a side effect. 34 Find First and Last Position of Element in Sorted Array Medium Two binary searches Find the first index not smaller than target and the first index bigger than target. bisect_left and bisect_right do exactly this. 162 Find Peak Element Medium Walk uphill with binary search If the middle is lower than its right neighbour, a peak lies to the right. Otherwise it lies at the middle or to the left. 153 Find Minimum in Rotated Sorted Array Medium Compare the middle with the right end If the middle is bigger than the last item, the drop (and the minimum) is to the right. 33 Search in Rotated Sorted Array Medium One half is always sorted Check which half is in order, then whether target lies inside it. Many edge cases: test a rotation at every position. 875 Koko Eating Bananas Medium Binary search on the answer Try a speed in the middle and count the hours needed. Too slow: go faster. Fast enough: try slower. 4 Median of Two Sorted Arrays Hard Binary search the cut in the shorter array Cut both arrays so the left parts hold half of all items and every left item is at most every right item. Then the median comes from the four items around the cuts. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps In the exam unittest is built into Python. A test is a method whose name starts with test_ inside a class that inherits from unittest.TestCase. assertEqual, assertTrue, assertIn, assertRaises and assertAlmostEqual (for floats) check results and give a clear message when they fail.Lesson: Why we test, and a first test suite Common mistake Tests must not depend on each other or on their order. test_add_more added a book, but test_total still saw only the pen, because setUp made a new cart for each test.Lesson: setUp, subTest and skip Remember advice() receives the client as an argument (dependency injection), so a test can hand it a fake. Code that creates its own network client deep inside is much harder to test. Designing for testability means passing dependencies in.Lesson: Mocks: replacing what you cannot test Common mistake 100 % coverage means every line ran during the tests, NOT that every case was"],[52,"py-testing","Testing: unittest, pytest and mocks","checked. The next lesson shows a bug that survives full coverage.Lesson: pytest: the most used test runner In the exam All 8 example tests passed with 100 % coverage, yet price − price × 100 / 100 can come out a tiny bit below zero because of float rounding. The fix is price × (100 − percent) / 100, which gives exactly 0 at 100 %. The failing price changes from run to run, because Hypothesis tries random inputs.Lesson: Property based testing Remember This shape, many fast unit tests at the bottom and few slow end to end tests at the top, is called the test pyramid. Run the fast tests on every save and all tests in CI (see the Git and CI/CD subject).Lesson: What to test and how much 3.3"],[52,"py-logging","Logging and debugging","Open chapter log levels, loggers, handlers, formatters, habits, dictConfig, rotation, print, assert and pdb debugging. Key ideas Levels DEBUG 10, INFO 20, WARNING 30 (default), ERROR 40, CRITICAL 50. basicConfig(level=, format=, stream=, force=True). Parts getLogger(__name__), logger and handler levels, Formatter fields (%(asctime)s %(levelname)s %(name)s %(message)s), propagate to parents. Habits logger.info(\"x %s\", x) not f-strings, logger.exception in except, no secrets in logs, libraries never configure logging. Configuration dictConfig once at start, RotatingFileHandler (maxBytes, backupCount), TimedRotatingFileHandler, QueueHandler. Debugging f\"{x=}\", assert (removed by -O, not for input checks), breakpoint() and python -m pdb: b, c, n, s, p, l, w, q. Formulas Log volumeExam favourite bytes = lines per second × bytes per line × seconds 50 lines a second of 200 bytes for a day (86,400 s) is 864,000,000 bytes, about 864 MB. Lesson: The maths: log volume, rotation and bisecting Disk used by a rotating logExam favourite max size = (backupCount + 1) × maxBytes maxBytes 1 MB with backupCount 4: at most 5 MB, for ever. Lesson: The maths: log volume, rotation and bisecting Levels shown for a given level L shown = levels with number ≥ L With WARNING (30): WARNING, ERROR and CRITICAL, 3 of 5. Lesson: The maths: log volume, rotation and bisecting Sampling lines kept = events / N when keeping 1 in N 1 in 100 of 2,000,000 requests: 20,000 lines. Lesson: The maths: log volume, rotation and bisecting Bisecting a bug (halving the suspect code) checks = ⌈log2 n⌉ 64 suspect lines: 6 checks with well placed prints or breakpoints. Lesson: The maths: log volume, rotation and bisecting Error budget allowed failures = requests × (1 − target) 1,000,000 requests at 99.99 % success allow 100 failures. 120 ERROR lines use 120 % of the budget. Lesson: The maths: log volume, rotation and bisecting Tables Table: level · number · use it for Level Number Use it for DEBUG 10 Details for developers: values, steps INFO 20 Normal events: started, saved, user logged in WARNING 30 Something odd that the program can handle (the default level) ERROR 40 An operation failed CRITICAL 50 The program may not be able to go on Lesson: Logging: a diary for your program Table: part · job · example Part Job Example Logger Named entry point, first level check, part of a tree split by dots logging.getLogger(\"shop.cart\") Handler Sends records somewhere, with its own level StreamHandler, FileHandler, RotatingFileHandler, SMTPHandler Formatter Turns a record into text \"%(asctime)s %(levelname)s %(name)s: %(message)s\" Filter Extra yes or no rule for a logger or handler Drop records that contain a password Lesson: Loggers, handlers and formatters Table: format field · gives Format field Gives %(asctime)s Date and time %(levelname)s DEBUG, INFO, ... %(name)s Logger name %(message)s Your message with its arguments filled in %(funcName)s, %(lineno)d, %(filename)s Where the call was made %(process)d, %(threadName)s Which process and thread Lesson: Loggers, handlers and formatters Table: · print · logging print logging Turn off without editing code No Yes, change the level Time, module and line Only if you add them by hand Yes, from the format Where it goes The screen Screen, files, servers, email, all at once Importance All the same Five levels Good for Quick checks while learning and the normal output of a script Everything in programs that run for real Lesson: Good logging habits Table: handler (logging.handlers) · keeps logs under control by Handler (logging.handlers) Keeps logs under control by RotatingFileHandler(path, maxBytes, backupCount) Starting a new file at maxBytes and keeping backupCount old ones TimedRotatingFileHandler(path, when=\"midnight\", backupCount=7) Starting a new file every day (or hour) and keeping a week QueueHandler and QueueListener Writing from a background thread, so slow disks do not slow the program SysLogHandler, HTTPHandler Sending logs to a"],[52,"py-logging","Logging and debugging","central server Lesson: Configuring logging for a whole app Table: pdb command · does pdb command Does b 4 (break) Stop at line 4. b alone lists breakpoints c (continue) Run until the next breakpoint n (next) Run the current line, stepping over calls s (step) Step into a function call p expr, pp expr Print (pretty print) a value l, ll List the code around here, or the whole function w (where), u, d Show the call stack, move up or down in it r (return) Run until the current function returns cl 1 (clear) Remove breakpoint 1 q (quit) Stop debugging Lesson: Debugging: print, assert and pdb Table: · print / f\"{x=}\" · logging · pdb or an ide debugger print / f\"{x=}\" logging pdb or an IDE debugger Set up None Once per project One breakpoint() line See values at any line Only where you printed Only where you logged Yes, and change them Works on a server with nobody watching No Yes No Best for Quick checks Problems in running programs Puzzling bugs you can reproduce Lesson: Debugging: print, assert and pdb Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 937 Reorder Data in Log Files Medium A sort key that splits two kinds of logs Letter logs come first, sorted by content then identifier. Digit logs keep their order: sorted is stable, so give them all the same key. 1598 Crawler Log Folder Easy Track the depth \"../\" goes up one level (never above the top), \"./\" stays, anything else goes one level down. 2933 High-Access Employees Medium Group times per person, then a window of 3 Turn \"hhmm\" into minutes, sort each person's times, and check whether any 3 in a row fit within 59 minutes. 1604 Alert Using Same Key-Card Three or More Times in a One Hour Period Medium The same window idea on access logs Group by name, sort the minutes, and alert if times[i] − times[i − 2] is at most 60. 1817 Finding the Users Active Minutes Medium A set of minutes per user Collect each user's different minutes in a set. The size of the set is that user's active minutes. Count users per size. 2747 Count Zero Request Servers Medium Sort logs and queries, slide a window Sort logs by time. Answer queries in time order with two pointers that keep only logs inside [q − x, q] and a count per server. 1169 Invalid Transactions Medium Parse, then compare every pair A transaction is invalid above 1000, or when the same name has another one in a different city within 60 minutes. 539 Minimum Time Difference Medium Turn log times into minutes and sort Change each \"HH:MM\" into minutes, sort, and compare neighbours. Do not forget the gap that wraps around midnight: first + 1440 − last. 1418 Display Table of Food Orders in a Restaurant Medium Turn order records into a report table Count orders in a dict of table number to a dict of food to count. The header is \"Table\" plus the sorted food names. Rows go in number order of the tables. 165 Compare Version Numbers Medium Compare number by number Split on dots, turn each part into an int (so 01 equals 1) and treat missing parts as 0. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps In the exam Without basicConfig the root logger shows only WARNING and above, on stderr. basicConfig sets the level, the format and the stream in one call, but only the first time, unless you pass force=True.Lesson: Logging: a diary for your program Remember Loggers form a tree by name: shop.cart is a child of shop, which is a child of root. A record goes to the handlers of its logger and then, unless propagate is False, up to the parents. Add handlers in one place (often the root or the top package logger) and just call getLogger(__name__) in every module.Lesson: Loggers, handlers and formatters In the exam Pass values as arguments: logger.debug(\"total %s\", total). The text is only built if the record passes the level checks. An f-string is always built, even when the message is thrown away, as the first slow __str__ line shows.Lesson: Good logging habits Common mistake Inside except, logger.exception(\"...\") logs at ERROR level and adds the"],[52,"py-logging","Logging and debugging","full traceback. Never log passwords, card numbers or personal data: logs are read by many people and kept for a long time.Lesson: Good logging habits Remember Configure logging once, at program start, with dictConfig (or a file read into a dict, often YAML or TOML). Libraries must never call basicConfig: they only call getLogger(__name__), and the application decides what to show. dictConfig switches off loggers that already exist unless you set \"disable_existing_loggers\": False.Lesson: Configuring logging for a whole app Remember On the second stop total is 70, not 70 + 0: the line replaces total instead of adding to it. The fix is total += s.Lesson: Debugging: print, assert and pdb Common mistake assert checks a condition you believe is always true and raises AssertionError if not. Python started with -O skips every assert, so never use assert to check user input. Raise ValueError for that.Lesson: Debugging: print, assert and pdb"],[52,"step-advanced-4","Step 4Advanced Python · Speed: complexity and smart data structures","4.1"],[52,"py-complexity","Complexity, timing and profiling","Open chapter Big O, operation costs, timeit, cProfile, memory, amortized cost and caching. Key ideas Big O Drop constants, keep the biggest term. Loops in a row add, nested loops multiply. O(1) < O(log n) < O(n) < O(n log n) < O(n²) < O(2n). Costs list: index O(1), append O(1) amortized, x in list O(n), insert(0)/pop(0) O(n). dict and set: O(1) average. sort O(n log n). Measure timeit (python -m timeit -s setup stmt, best of several runs), time.perf_counter, realistic n. Profile python -m cProfile -s cumulative file.py, tottime vs cumtime, fix only the hot spot. tracemalloc for memory, getsizeof is shallow. Trade offs A better algorithm first. Sets for lookups, lru_cache for repeated work, generators to save memory. Formulas Definition of Big OExam favourite f(n) = O(g(n)) if there are numbers c > 0 and n0 with f(n) ≤ c · g(n) for every n ≥ n0 3n + 5 = O(n): take c = 4 and n0 = 5, since 3n + 5 ≤ 4n when n ≥ 5. Lesson: The maths behind Big O Big Omega and Big Theta Ω(g) is a lower bound, Θ(g) means both O(g) and Ω(g) Comparison sorting is Ω(n log n): no comparison sort can do better. Lesson: The maths behind Big O Sum of a loop inside a loopExam favourite 1 + 2 + ... + n = n(n + 1) / 2 = O(n²) Comparing each item with the ones after it: 1000 items give 499,500 comparisons. Lesson: The maths behind Big O Geometric sum (doubling)Exam favourite 1 + 2 + 4 + ... + n = 2n − 1 < 2n Copies for 1024 appends with doubling: 1 + 2 + ... + 512 = 1023, about 1 per append. Lesson: The maths behind Big O Halving steps = ⌊log2 n⌋ + 1 Binary search in 1,000,000 sorted items: at most 20 steps. Lesson: The maths behind Big O Master theoremExam favourite T(n) = a·T(n/b) + O(nd) gives O(nd) if d > logb a, O(nd log n) if d = logb a, O(nlogb a) if d < logb a Merge sort: a = 2, b = 2, d = 1. log2 2 = 1 = d, so O(n log n). Lesson: The maths behind Big O Time estimate seconds ≈ steps / (about 107 simple Python steps per second) n = 105 with O(n²) is 1010 steps: about 1000 s. With O(n log n) it is 1.7 × 106: about 0.17 s. Lesson: The maths behind Big O Tables Table: big o · name · n = 1000 needs about · typical code Big O Name n = 1000 needs about Typical code O(1) constant 1 step d[key], x in a set, list[i], append O(log n) logarithmic 10 steps binary search, bisect, heap push O(n) linear 1,000 steps one loop, x in a list, sum, max, copy O(n log n) linearithmic 10,000 steps sorted(), list.sort() O(n²) quadratic 1,000,000 steps a loop inside a loop over the same data O(2n) exponential more than the atoms in the universe trying every subset, naive recursion Lesson: Big O: how the work grows Table: operation · list · dict / set · collections.deque Operation list dict / set collections.deque Get or set by index or key O(1) O(1) on average O(n) in the middle, O(1) at the ends x in container O(n) O(1) on average O(n) Add at the end O(1) amortized (append) O(1) on average (d[k] = v, add) O(1) (append) Add or remove at the front O(n) (insert(0, x), pop(0)) not ordered by position O(1) (appendleft, popleft) Remove a given value O(n) (remove) O(1) on average (del d[k], discard) O(n) Sort O(n log n) sorted(d) O(n log n) not supported in place Copy, len O(n), O(1) O(n), O(1) O(n), O(1) Lesson: Finding the Big O of code Table: · list · set list set x in c O(n): looks at items one by one O(1): jumps to the right place with the hash Memory Small About 3 to 4 times more Keeps order and duplicates Yes No Items must be Anything Hashable (no lists or dicts) Best for Data you go through in order Many \"is it there\" questions Lesson: Finding the Big O of code Table: rule for fair timing · why Rule for fair timing Why Use timeit or time.perf_counter, not time.time perf_counter is the most precise clock, and timeit repeats the code many times Take the minimum of several runs Other programs only ever slow a run down, so the fastest run is closest to the truth Time with realistic sizes With 10 items everything is fast. Differences show up with large n Keep setup out of the timed"],[52,"py-complexity","Complexity, timing and profiling","part Building test data is not what you want to measure Compare on the same computer Times are not comparable across machines, ratios are Lesson: Measuring with timeit Table: · time.time() · time.perf_counter() · timeit time.time() time.perf_counter() timeit Precision Can be coarse, and jumps if the clock is changed The best clock available Uses perf_counter Repeats the code No No Yes, many loops, several rounds Best for Dates and times of day Timing one block in a real program Comparing two small pieces of code Lesson: Measuring with timeit Table: column · means Column Means ncalls How many times the function was called tottime Time spent in the function itself, without the functions it calls cumtime Time in the function including everything it calls percall tottime / ncalls and cumtime / ncalls Lesson: Finding the slow part with cProfile Table: · cprofile (built in) · line_profiler · py-spy, scalene cProfile (built in) line_profiler py-spy, Scalene Shows Time per function Time per line A live sampling of a running program, Scalene also memory Install Nothing pip install line_profiler pip install py-spy or scalene Slows the program Somewhat A lot, only on chosen functions Hardly at all (sampling) Best for Finding the slow function Finding the slow line inside it Programs already running in production Lesson: Finding the slow part with cProfile Table: way to save memory · how Way to save memory How Generators instead of lists sum(x * x for x in data) makes one value at a time __slots__ in classes with many objects No per object __dict__, often half the memory array.array or NumPy for numbers Raw numbers in one block instead of separate int objects Read files line by line for line in f instead of f.read() or f.readlines() del large objects you no longer need The memory can be reused as soon as nothing refers to it Lesson: Memory: getsizeof, tracemalloc and generators Table: · a better algorithm or data structure · caching (lru_cache, a dict) · small tweaks (local variables, fewer function calls) A better algorithm or data structure Caching (lru_cache, a dict) Small tweaks (local variables, fewer function calls) Typical gain 10 to 1,000,000 times Big when the same inputs repeat 10 % to 2 times Cost Thinking Memory, and care with changing data Harder to read code When First When profiling shows repeated work Last, and only in the hot spot Lesson: Amortized cost and trading memory for time Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 219 Contains Duplicate II Easy Remember the last index of each value A dict from value to its last index answers \"where did I see this before\" in O(1), so one pass is enough: O(n) instead of comparing all pairs. 2006 Count Number of Pairs With Absolute Difference K Easy Count what you have seen For each number, the partners seen so far are x − k and x + k. A dict of counts finds them in O(1), so the whole list takes O(n). 1010 Pairs of Songs With Total Durations Divisible by 60 Medium Group by remainder Two songs fit when their remainders mod 60 add up to 0 or 60. Count remainders seen so far: O(n) instead of O(n²). 454 4Sum II Medium Meet in the middle Trying all four lists is O(n4). Count every a + b in a dict (O(n²)), then look up −(c + d) for every pair of the other two lists (O(n²)). 974 Subarray Sums Divisible by K Medium Prefix sums and remainders A subarray sum is prefix[j] − prefix[i]. It is divisible by k when both prefixes have the same remainder. Count remainders: O(n) instead of trying all O(n²) subarrays. 209 Minimum Size Subarray Sum Medium A sliding window Grow the window on the right, shrink it from the left while the sum is big enough. Each index enters and leaves once, so it is O(n). 1004 Max Consecutive Ones III Medium A window with at most k zeros Move the right end one step at a time. When the window holds more than k zeros, move the left end until it does not. O(n) time, O(1) memory. 204 Count Primes Medium Sieve of Eratosthenes Testing each number by"],[52,"py-complexity","Complexity, timing and profiling","division is about O(n √n). The sieve crosses out multiples of each prime starting at p², which is O(n log log n). 287 Find the Duplicate Number Medium Treat the list as a linked list Without changing the list and with O(1) memory: i points to nums[i]. The repeated value is where a cycle starts. Floyd's slow and fast pointers find it in O(n). 41 First Missing Positive Hard Use the list itself as the table O(n) time and O(1) extra memory: swap each value v into place v − 1. Then the first place i that does not hold i + 1 gives the answer. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps Remember Look at the table: for 1000 items linear search needs 1000 steps, binary search only 10, and comparing every pair needs 1,000,000. The same computer, the same data size, and a 100,000 times difference.Lesson: Big O: how the work grows Common mistake Hidden loops count too. x in my_list, my_list.index(x), my_list.remove(x), my_list.insert(0, x), \"\".join and slicing a[i:j] each walk through items. Putting one of them inside a loop is the most common way Python code becomes quadratic by accident.Lesson: Finding the Big O of code In the exam Average case versus worst case: a dict lookup is O(1) on average, but if many keys had the same hash it could take O(n). Python adds random salt to str hashes so attackers cannot cause this on purpose. For interviews and exams, dict and set operations are O(1).Lesson: Finding the Big O of code Remember 60.9 microseconds against 19.4 nanoseconds: the set is about 3,000 times faster for 10,000 numbers. -s is the setup, which runs once and is not timed. timeit chooses how many loops to run and reports the best of 5 rounds.Lesson: Measuring with timeit Remember slow_unique takes 0.136 of the 0.138 seconds, so that is the only function worth fixing. Speeding up anything else could save at most 0.002 seconds. Always profile before you optimise: the slow part is often not where you expect.Lesson: Finding the slow part with cProfile In the exam sys.getsizeof is shallow. For a list it counts the list and its slots, not the objects inside, which is why a list holding a huge number has the same size as one holding a small number. tracemalloc counts every allocation made while it runs, so it shows the real memory a piece of code needed.Lesson: Memory: getsizeof, tracemalloc and generators Remember Most appends cost one step. Now and then the array is full and must copy everything, which costs n steps. But because the space doubles each time, the copies add up to less than n in total, so the average (amortized) cost per append stays about 1. That is why list.append is called O(1) amortized.Lesson: Amortized cost and trading memory for time Remember fib_slow(20) makes 21,891 calls because it works out the same values again and again. With lru_cache each value from 0 to 20 is computed once: 21 calls. We spend a little memory (21 stored answers) to save a huge amount of time, which is the idea behind dynamic programming.Lesson: Amortized cost and trading memory for time 4.2"],[52,"py-collections","collections, heapq and bisect","Open chapter Counter, deque, namedtuple, OrderedDict, ChainMap, heapq priority queues and bisect. Key ideas Counter Counter(items), c[x] is 0 if missing, most_common(k), total(), elements(), + - & | on counts. deque append, appendleft, pop, popleft all O(1), rotate(n), deque(maxlen=n). Queues and BFS use popleft. Records namedtuple(\"P\", [\"x\", \"y\"]) with _replace, _asdict, _fields. OrderedDict.move_to_end, popitem(last=False). ChainMap(first, fallback). defaultdict(list). heapq heappush, heappop O(log n), heapify O(n), heap[0] smallest, nlargest and nsmallest O(n log k), merge, (priority, count, item), heappush_max in 3.14. bisect bisect_left, bisect_right, insort, key= since 3.10. Range count = right(high) − left(low). Only on sorted lists. Formulas Heap indexesExam favourite parent(i) = (i − 1) // 2, children 2i + 1 and 2i + 2 Index 4 has parent 1 and children 9 and 10. Lesson: The maths: heap indexes, heights and costs Height of a heapExam favourite h = ⌊log2 n⌋ 1,000,000 items: 19 levels below the top, so a push makes at most 19 swaps. Lesson: The maths: heap indexes, heights and costs heapify is O(n) work ≤ ∑ (n / 2h+1) · h < n Most items are near the bottom and move only a little, so the total is less than n swaps. Lesson: The maths: heap indexes, heights and costs Top k with a heapExam favourite O(n log k) instead of O(n log n) for a full sort k = 10 of n = 106: about 3.3 × 106 steps against 2 × 107. Lesson: The maths: heap indexes, heights and costs Binary search comparisons ≤ ⌈log2(n + 1)⌉ 1000 sorted items: at most 10 comparisons. Lesson: The maths: heap indexes, heights and costs Front removals from a list shifts = (n − 1) + (n − 2) + ... + 1 = n(n − 1) / 2 Emptying 10,000 items with pop(0): about 5 × 107 shifts. With deque.popleft: 0. Lesson: The maths: heap indexes, heights and costs Tables Table: you need to · use · speed of the key operation You need to Use Speed of the key operation Count things collections.Counter O(1) per item Group things by a key collections.defaultdict(list) O(1) per item Add and remove at both ends (a queue) collections.deque O(1) at each end Keep only the last n items deque(maxlen=n) O(1), old items drop off A small read only record with names namedtuple or a frozen dataclass Same as a tuple Always get the smallest (or largest) item heapq O(log n) push and pop, O(1) peek The k largest or smallest of many heapq.nlargest, heapq.nsmallest O(n log k) Search or insert in a sorted list bisect O(log n) search, O(n) insert Look through several dicts in order collections.ChainMap O(number of dicts) Lesson: The right container for the job Table: counter operation · result Counter operation Result c + d Adds counts, keeps only positive ones c - d Subtracts counts, keeps only positive ones c & d The smaller count of each item (intersection) c | d The bigger count of each item (union) c.update(items), c.subtract(items) Change counts in place (subtract can go below 0) c.elements() Each item repeated count times c.total() Sum of all counts (3.10+) Lesson: Counter: counting made easy Table: · list with pop(0) · collections.deque · queue.queue list with pop(0) collections.deque queue.Queue Remove from the front O(n) O(1) O(1) Index in the middle O(1) O(n) Not supported Safe between threads No append and popleft are thread safe Yes, with blocking get and put Best for Stacks and arrays Queues, BFS, sliding windows, last n items Passing work between threads (step 5) Lesson: deque: a fast queue with two ends Table: · tuple · namedtuple · dataclass tuple namedtuple dataclass dict Fields by name No, only p[0] Yes, p.x Yes, p.x Yes, p[\"x\"] Can change No No (use _replace) Yes, unless frozen=True Yes Memory Smallest Same as a tuple More, unless slots=True Most Methods and defaults No defaults=, few methods Yes, any No Best for Two or three values that go together Read only rows, like CSV lines Objects with behaviour Data whose keys are not known in advance Lesson: namedtuple, OrderedDict, ChainMap and defaultdict"],[52,"py-collections","collections, heapq and bisect","Table: · min(list) each time · keep the list sorted · heapq min(list) each time Keep the list sorted heapq Add an item O(1) O(n) (insort shifts items) O(log n) Take the smallest O(n) O(1) from the end of a reverse sorted list O(log n) Good when You ask only once or twice You rarely add, often read in order You keep adding and taking, as in a scheduler or Dijkstra Lesson: heapq: a priority queue in a list Table: function · returns Function Returns bisect_left(a, x) The first index where x could go: before any equal items bisect_right(a, x) (also bisect) The index after the last item equal to x insort(a, x) Puts x into a, keeping it sorted (search O(log n), insert O(n)) key=func (3.10+) Compare func(item) instead of the item, without building a new list Lesson: bisect: binary search in a sorted list Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1046 Last Stone Weight Easy A max-heap with negative numbers Always smash the two heaviest stones. heapq is a min-heap, so store −weight. (Python 3.14 also has heappop_max, but LeetCode may run an older Python.) 1351 Count Negative Numbers in a Sorted Matrix Easy bisect on each row Each row goes down from left to right. With key=lambda v: −v it goes up, so bisect finds where the negatives start in O(log n) per row. 215 Kth Largest Element in an Array Medium A min-heap of size k Keep the k largest numbers seen so far in a min-heap. Its top is the kth largest. O(n log k) time and O(k) memory. 973 K Closest Points to Origin Medium nsmallest with a key Compare squared distances x² + y² (no square root needed, the order is the same). heapq.nsmallest does it in O(n log k). 692 Top K Frequent Words Medium Counter, then sort by two keys Count with Counter. Sort by count going down, then by the word going up for ties: key=(-count, word). 2300 Successful Pairs of Spells and Potions Medium Sort once, then bisect for each spell A spell s needs a potion of at least ⌈success / s⌉. Sort the potions and bisect_left gives how many are too weak. O((n + m) log m). 300 Longest Increasing Subsequence Medium Patience sorting with bisect tails[i] is the smallest last number of an increasing run of length i + 1. Each number replaces the first tail that is not smaller, found with bisect_left. O(n log n). 621 Task Scheduler Medium Count the most common task The most common task (count m) needs m − 1 gaps of n slots after it. Tasks that share that top count fill the last row. The answer is never less than the number of tasks. 239 Sliding Window Maximum Hard A monotonic deque of indexes Keep indexes whose values go down from front to back. Drop smaller values from the back before adding, and the front once it leaves the window. Each index enters and leaves once: O(n). 295 Find Median from Data Stream Hard Two heaps A max-heap (negative numbers) holds the smaller half, a min-heap the bigger half. Keep their sizes equal or the low half one bigger. The median is at the tops. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps Remember Counter is a dict subclass, so everything a dict can do works too. Unlike a dict, a missing key gives 0 instead of KeyError. most_common(k) returns the k most common items with their counts, and items with the same count keep the order in which they were first seen.Lesson: Counter: counting made easy In the exam A deque (say \"deck\") is a double ended queue. Adding and removing at either end is O(1). A list does the same at the end, but list.insert(0, x) and list.pop(0) must shift every other item, which is O(n).Lesson: deque: a fast queue with two ends Remember Breadth first search (BFS) always takes the oldest path from the front of the queue and adds new paths at the back. That is why it finds the path with the fewest steps first, and why it needs a deque, not a list.Lesson: deque: a fast queue with two ends In the exam Since Python 3.7 every dict keeps insertion order. OrderedDict still has extras: move_to_end(key), popitem(last=False) for the oldest item, and == compares"],[52,"py-collections","collections, heapq and bisect","order too. That makes it the classic tool for an LRU cache.Lesson: namedtuple, OrderedDict, ChainMap and defaultdict Remember ChainMap does not copy anything. It searches the dicts one after another, so a change to user is seen at once. Writes go to the first dict only. It is how Python looks up names: locals, then globals, then builtins.Lesson: namedtuple, OrderedDict, ChainMap and defaultdict In the exam A heap is a list where every item is smaller than or equal to its two children: heap[i] ≤ heap[2i + 1] and heap[i] ≤ heap[2i + 2]. So heap[0] is always the smallest. The rest is NOT sorted. heappush and heappop are O(log n), heapify is O(n), and looking at heap[0] is O(1).Lesson: heapq: a priority queue in a list Remember Put tuples like (priority, count, task) in a heap. Tuples compare item by item, so the priority decides first. The count (from itertools.count()) breaks ties, so two tasks with the same priority never have to be compared, which would fail for objects that cannot be compared.Lesson: heapq: a priority queue in a list Remember bisect_right(a, x) − bisect_left(a, x) is how many times x appears. Counting values in a range or looking up which band a number falls into (grades, tax brackets, prices) are classic uses.Lesson: bisect: binary search in a sorted list"],[52,"step-advanced-5","Step 5Advanced Python · Doing many things at once: threads, processes and asyncio","5.1"],[52,"py-concurrency","Threads and processes","Open chapter threads, races and locks, worker queues, futures, the GIL, processes and free-threaded Python. Key ideas When to use what I/O bound: threads or asyncio. CPU bound: processes (or python3.14t). Measure first. Threads Thread(target, args), start, join, daemon. Shared memory means races: protect with with lock:. Sync tools Lock, RLock, Semaphore(n), Event, Condition, Barrier, queue.Queue. Same lock order everywhere to avoid deadlock. Executors ThreadPoolExecutor and ProcessPoolExecutor: submit, map (keeps order), as_completed (finish order), result() raises errors. Processes if __name__ == \"__main__\" guard, picklable functions and data, start methods fork, spawn, forkserver (Linux default in 3.14). Formulas Speedup and efficiencyExam favourite S = T1 / Tn, E = S / n 1.30 s alone, 0.40 s with 4 processes: S = 3.25, E = 0.8125 (about 81 %). Lesson: The maths: speedup, Amdahl and pool sizes Amdahl's lawExam favourite S(n) = 1 / ((1 − p) + p / n) p = 0.9 of the work can run in parallel, n = 4: S = 1 / (0.1 + 0.225) ≈ 3.08. Lesson: The maths: speedup, Amdahl and pool sizes The limit of Amdahl's law S(∞) = 1 / (1 − p) With p = 0.9, even a million cores give at most 10 times. Lesson: The maths: speedup, Amdahl and pool sizes Gustafson's law S(n) = n − (1 − p)(n − 1) If the problem grows with the cores: n = 4, p = 0.9 gives 3.7. Lesson: The maths: speedup, Amdahl and pool sizes Threads for I/O workExam favourite threads ≈ cores × (1 + wait time / compute time) 4 cores, 90 ms waiting and 10 ms computing per job: 4 × 10 = 40 threads. Lesson: The maths: speedup, Amdahl and pool sizes Little's law L = λ × W 200 requests per second that each take 0.25 s: 50 requests in progress, so about 50 workers. Lesson: The maths: speedup, Amdahl and pool sizes Lost updates final = expected − lost 4 threads × 100 additions should give 400. Every overwrite loses one. Lesson: The maths: speedup, Amdahl and pool sizes Tables Table: kind of work · example · best tool Kind of work Example Best tool I/O bound: mostly waiting Downloading pages, calling APIs, reading many files, database queries Threads (or asyncio, chapter 5.2) CPU bound: mostly calculating Image processing, prime numbers, simulations, parsing huge files Processes (or the free-threaded build, or NumPy which releases the GIL) A mix A web crawler that also parses pages Threads or asyncio for I/O, a process pool for the heavy parts Lesson: Doing many things at once Table: threading · does threading Does Thread(target=f, args=(...), name=...) Makes a thread that will run f(*args) t.start() Starts it. It runs next to the main thread t.join(timeout=None) Waits until t has finished Thread(..., daemon=True) A background thread that does not keep the program alive threading.current_thread(), active_count() Which thread is running this line, how many threads exist Lesson: Threads: start, join and share Table: tool · use it to Tool Use it to Lock Let only one thread at a time run a block (use with lock:) RLock A lock the same thread may take again, for recursive code Semaphore(n) Let at most n threads in at once, for example 5 connections Event Let threads wait until one sets a flag: event.set(), event.wait() Condition Wait until some state is true, with wait_for and notify_all Barrier(n) Wait until n threads have all arrived queue.Queue Pass data between threads safely, with no lock code of your own Lesson: Race conditions and locks Table: · threading.thread · queue.queue + workers · threadpoolexecutor threading.Thread queue.Queue + workers ThreadPoolExecutor Get results back By hand (shared dict or list) A results queue Futures: result(), map, as_completed Errors in the thread Printed and lost You must catch them Raised again by result() Limit how many run at once By hand Number of workers max_workers Best for One long background job Long running pipelines Most everyday jobs Lesson: Worker threads with queue and futures Table: · threads · processes Threads Processes Memory Shared: easy to"],[52,"py-concurrency","Threads and processes","pass data, easy to break it Separate: data is pickled and copied Pure Python CPU work on many cores No, with the GIL (yes in python3.14t) Yes Start up cost Small Bigger (a new interpreter) A crash Can take the whole program down Stays inside one process Best for I/O bound work CPU bound work Lesson: The GIL and processes Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1114 Print in Order Easy Two Events second waits for an Event that first sets, and third waits for one that second sets. The threads may start in any order. 1115 Print FooBar Alternately Medium Two Semaphores take turns foo starts with one permit and bar with none. Each thread waits on its own semaphore and then gives a permit to the other one. 1116 Print Zero Even Odd Medium Three Semaphores zero prints 0 and then wakes odd or even depending on the next number. odd and even print and wake zero again. 1117 Building H2O Medium Semaphores for places, a Barrier for the group A Semaphore(2) lets two hydrogen in, a Semaphore(1) one oxygen. A Barrier(3) makes the three wait for each other before they bond. 1195 Fizz Buzz Multithreaded Medium One Condition and a shared counter Every thread waits until the current number is its kind (or the end is reached), prints, moves the counter on and wakes the others with notify_all. 1226 The Dining Philosophers Medium Always take the lower numbered fork first A deadlock needs a cycle of waiting. If every philosopher takes the lower numbered fork first, no cycle can form, so somebody can always eat. 1834 Single-Threaded CPU Medium A heap of ready tasks Sort tasks by arrival. Push every task that has arrived into a heap of (time needed, index), run the shortest, and jump ahead in time when nothing is ready. 1882 Process Tasks Using Servers Medium A heap of free servers and a heap of busy ones Free servers by (weight, index), busy servers by (free time, weight, index). Before each task, move servers that are free again. If none is free, jump to the time the first one is. 2402 Meeting Rooms III Hard Free rooms and busy rooms in two heaps Take meetings by start time. Free every room whose meeting has ended. Use the lowest free room, or delay the meeting until the room that frees first is free. 1094 Car Pooling Medium Changes at each point, then a running total At each pickup the load goes up and at each drop it goes down. Sort the points and add up the changes. Too many people at any point means False. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps Remember Four downloads of 0.5 seconds take 2 seconds one by one but only 0.5 seconds with four threads, because the waiting happens at the same time. While a thread waits for I/O (sleep, network, files) it lets go of the GIL, so others can run.Lesson: Doing many things at once Common mistake Every thread in a process shares the same global variables and objects. That makes it easy to pass data, and also easy to break data: two threads changing the same object at the same time is a race condition.Lesson: Threads: start, join and share Common mistake counter = counter + 1 is really three steps: read, add, write. If another thread writes between your read and your write, your write wipes out its update. time.sleep(0) here forces a switch at the worst moment, but real programs hit the same bug by chance, often only under heavy load.Lesson: Race conditions and locks In the exam A deadlock happens when thread 1 holds lock A and waits for lock B while thread 2 holds B and waits for A. Neither can go on. Prevent it by always taking locks in the same order, holding them briefly, or using a timeout.Lesson: Race conditions and locks Remember queue.Queue does the locking for you. Producers put jobs in, worker threads get them out. get() waits when the queue is empty. Putting one None per worker is a common way to tell workers to stop.Lesson: Worker threads with queue and futures In the exam With the GIL, 4 threads are no faster than one (1.37 s against 1.30 s). 4 processes are 3.3 times"],[52,"py-concurrency","Threads and processes","faster. The free-threaded build python3.14t (officially supported since 3.14) has no GIL, so there 4 threads are fast too.Lesson: The GIL and processes 5.2"],[52,"py-asyncio","asyncio: async and await","Open chapter coroutines, the event loop, gather and TaskGroup, timeouts, limits, queues and httpx. Key ideas Basics async def, await (only inside async def), asyncio.run(main()) once. Calling a coroutine function does not run it. Together gather (results in order), TaskGroup with except*, create_task (keep a reference), as_completed (finish order). Control asyncio.timeout(s) and wait_for, task.cancel() and CancelledError (raise it again), Semaphore(k) to limit. Pipelines asyncio.Queue(maxsize) with await put and get for back pressure. Async generators, async for, async with. Never block No time.sleep or requests in coroutines. Use asyncio.sleep, httpx.AsyncClient, asyncio.to_thread or run_in_executor. Formulas Time one by oneExam favourite T = n × t 20 requests of 0.2 s: 4 s. Lesson: The maths: waits that overlap Time with all at onceExam favourite T ≈ max(ti) + overhead 20 requests of 0.2 s: about 0.2 s (measured 0.26 s). Lesson: The maths: waits that overlap With a limit of k at onceExam favourite T ≈ ⌈n / k⌉ × t 20 requests, at most 5 at once, 0.2 s each: 4 rounds, 0.8 s. Lesson: The maths: waits that overlap Throughput requests per second = k / t 5 at once, 0.2 s each: 25 requests per second. Lesson: The maths: waits that overlap Little's law for open connections L = λ × W 1000 requests per second with 0.5 s waits: 500 tasks waiting at any moment. Lesson: The maths: waits that overlap Memory for many waiters memory ≈ n × size of one waiter 10,000 tasks of about 2 KB is about 20 MB. 10,000 threads reserve about 80 GB of address space for stacks. Lesson: The maths: waits that overlap Tables Table: word · means Word Means async def f() Defines a coroutine function. Calling it gives a coroutine object await x Pause this coroutine until x is done, and let the loop run others. Only allowed inside async def Task A coroutine scheduled on the loop. It runs on its own, next to the others Event loop The scheduler: runs ready tasks one at a time and wakes waiting ones asyncio.run(main()) Start a loop, run main until it finishes, close the loop. Call it once, at the top Lesson: asyncio: many waits in one thread Table: tool · use it to Tool Use it to asyncio.create_task(coro) Start a coroutine in the background and get a Task you can await later await asyncio.gather(a, b, ...) Run several and get their results as a list, in the order given async with asyncio.TaskGroup() as g Start tasks with g.create_task. If one fails, the others are cancelled and errors come out as an ExceptionGroup asyncio.as_completed(aws) Get results in the order they finish asyncio.wait(aws, return_when=...) Wait for the first one, the first error, or all, and get done and pending sets Lesson: Tasks, TaskGroup and as_completed Table: · gather · taskgroup (3.11+) gather TaskGroup (3.11+) Results A list in order task.result() for each task When one fails The error is raised, the others keep running (unless you cancel them) The others are cancelled and all errors arrive in an ExceptionGroup Catching errors try / except try / except* Best for A fixed list of jobs, return_exceptions=True to collect errors Structured code where no task may be left running Lesson: Tasks, TaskGroup and as_completed Table: · threads · asyncio Threads asyncio Switches between jobs Any time (the OS decides) Only at await Shared data Needs locks Safe between awaits Cost of one job A thread: about 8 MB of address space reserved for its stack A task: a few KB Thousands of connections Heavy Easy Libraries Any normal library Needs async libraries (httpx, aiohttp, asyncpg) CPU heavy work Not with the GIL Never: it blocks the loop Lesson: Real network code, and blocking calls Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 2621 Sleep Easy await asyncio.sleep LeetCode asks this one in JavaScript. In Python asyncio the same idea is a coroutine that awaits asyncio.sleep(millis / 1000). 2723 Add Two Promises Easy Await both at the same time A JavaScript problem. In Python, gather"],[52,"py-asyncio","asyncio: async and await","awaits both coroutines at the same time and returns their values, so the total wait is the longer one, not the sum. 2648 Generate Fibonacci Sequence Easy An endless generator A JavaScript generator problem. In Python, a while True loop with yield gives the numbers one at a time, and itertools.islice takes as many as you need. 2649 Nested Array Generator Medium yield from for the nested lists A JavaScript generator problem. Walk the list, and for a nested list hand over to a recursive generator with yield from. 2637 Promise Time Limit Medium asyncio.wait_for A JavaScript problem. Wrap the coroutine in asyncio.wait_for with the limit in seconds, and turn the TimeoutError into the message the problem asks for. 2721 Execute Asynchronous Functions in Parallel Medium gather keeps the order A JavaScript problem (write Promise.all without Promise.all). In Python, gather starts all of them, keeps the results in the order given and raises the first error. 2622 Cache With Time Limit Medium Store the value with its end time A JavaScript design problem. Keep key: (value, expires_at). A key counts only while the clock is before its end time. The clock is passed in so the tests do not have to wait. 2627 Debounce Medium Cancel the waiting call and schedule a new one A JavaScript problem. With asyncio, loop.call_later schedules the call. A new call cancels the handle of the one still waiting. 2636 Promise Pool Medium n workers share one iterator A JavaScript problem. Start n worker coroutines that all take the next function from the same iterator. At most n functions run at any moment. 2650 Design Cancellable Function Hard Drive a generator by hand, race each wait against cancel A JavaScript problem, and exactly how await works inside: send each result into the generator, throw errors into it, and throw a Cancelled error if cancel() wins the race. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps Common mistake Calling make_tea(...) only creates a coroutine object, it runs nothing. The code runs when it is awaited, or when it is wrapped in a task (gather, create_task, TaskGroup). Forgetting await is the most common asyncio bug: Python warns \"coroutine ... was never awaited\".Lesson: asyncio: many waits in one thread Common mistake Keep a reference to every task you create with create_task (in a list or a set), or use a TaskGroup. The loop holds tasks only weakly, so a task nobody refers to can disappear before it finishes.Lesson: Tasks, TaskGroup and as_completed In the exam Cancelling a task raises CancelledError inside it at its current await. Clean up in finally blocks, and do not swallow CancelledError without raising it again, or the task cannot be stopped.Lesson: Timeouts, cancelling and limits Remember A Semaphore limits how many tasks do something at once. Without it, gathering 10,000 downloads opens 10,000 connections at the same moment, which servers often punish by refusing or blocking you.Lesson: Timeouts, cancelling and limits Remember asyncio.Queue is like queue.Queue, but put and get are awaited. With maxsize=2 the producer waits when two items are waiting. This back pressure stops a fast producer from filling memory while a slow consumer catches up.Lesson: Pipelines with asyncio.Queue Remember 20 × 0.2 s = 4 s one by one. With asyncio all 20 waits overlap, so it takes about one wait: 0.26 s, 16 times faster, in one thread.Lesson: Real network code, and blocking calls Common mistake A normal blocking call (time.sleep, requests.get, a slow calculation) inside a coroutine freezes the whole loop. Use the async version (asyncio.sleep, httpx.AsyncClient, aiofiles), or push the blocking call to a thread with asyncio.to_thread, or to a process pool with loop.run_in_executor.Lesson: Real network code, and blocking calls"],[52,"py-asyncio","Step 6Advanced Python · The standard library toolbox: text, data, databases and GUIs","6.1"],[52,"py-regex","Regular expressions","Open chapter regex syntax, the re functions, groups, sub and split, flags, greedy and lazy, lookarounds. Key ideas Syntax . \\d \\w \\s [abc] [^abc] ^ $ \\b, repeats * + ? {m,n}, lazy *? +?, | and ( ). Always raw strings. Functions search (anywhere), match (start), fullmatch (whole), findall (strings), finditer (match objects), sub, subn, split. Groups m.group(n), m.groups(), (?P<name>...), m.groupdict(), (?:...) non-capturing, \\1 backreference, \\g<name> in sub. Flags IGNORECASE, MULTILINE, DOTALL, VERBOSE. re.compile for named patterns, re.escape for literal text. Care Greedy versus lazy, lookarounds (?=) (?!) (?<=) (? Formulas Choices for a character classExam favourite patterns of length n over a class of size k match kn strings \\d{4} matches 104 = 10,000 strings, 0000 to 9999. Lesson: The maths: languages and automata A range of repeats [ab]{1,3} matches 2 + 4 + 8 = 14 strings ∑ ki for i from min to max. Lesson: The maths: languages and automata Positions tried by search up to n + 1 starting places Text of length 17: starts 0 to 17. Lesson: The maths: languages and automata A regular languageExam favourite built from single characters with union (|), concatenation and star (*) Every such pattern can be matched by a finite automaton with no memory. Lesson: The maths: languages and automata Catastrophic backtrackingExam favourite ways to split n letters among nested repeats grows like 2n (a+)+$ on 30 a's followed by \"!\": about 230 ≈ 109 tries. Lesson: The maths: languages and automata Linear time engines time = O(m × n) for pattern size m and text size n RE2 and Rust's regex guarantee this, Python's re does not. Lesson: The maths: languages and automata Tables Table: pattern · matches · pattern · matches Pattern Matches Pattern Matches . Any character except a newline \\d, \\D A digit, not a digit \\w, \\W A word character (letter, digit, _), not one \\s, \\S Whitespace, not whitespace [abc], [a-z] One of these characters, a range [^0-9] Any character NOT in the set ^, $ Start, end of the text (or line with MULTILINE) \\b A word edge *, +, ? 0 or more, 1 or more, 0 or 1 {3}, {2,5} Exactly 3, from 2 to 5 a|b a or b (...) A group, also captured \\. \\$ \\( A real dot, dollar, bracket *?, +? The lazy versions Lesson: Regular expressions: patterns for text Table: · str methods (in, find, split, replace) · re module str methods (in, find, split, replace) re module Fixed text Best: simple and fast Works, but more than needed Patterns (any digits, optional parts, either or) Many lines of code One pattern Readability Very clear Can be cryptic: use re.VERBOSE and comments Nested structures (HTML, JSON, brackets) No No: use a real parser Lesson: Regular expressions: patterns for text Table: function · looks · returns Function Looks Returns re.search(p, s) Anywhere, first match A match object or None re.match(p, s) Only at the start A match object or None re.fullmatch(p, s) The whole string A match object or None re.findall(p, s) Everywhere A list of strings (or tuples of groups) re.finditer(p, s) Everywhere An iterator of match objects m.group(), m.start(), m.end(), m.span() On a match object The text and where it is Lesson: search, match, fullmatch, findall, finditer Table: syntax · meaning Syntax Meaning (...) Capture this part as group 1, 2, ... (?P<name>...) A named group, read with m[\"name\"] or m.group(\"name\") (?:...) Group without capturing, for | or repeats \\1, (?P=name) The same text that group 1 or name matched m.groups(), m.groupdict() All groups as a tuple, named groups as a dict Lesson: Groups: pulling parts out Table: flag · effect Flag Effect re.IGNORECASE (re.I) Upper and lower case match each other re.MULTILINE (re.M) ^ and $ match at the start and end of every line re.DOTALL (re.S) . matches newlines too re.VERBOSE (re.X) Spaces and # comments in the pattern are ignored re.ASCII (re.A) \\w, \\d and \\s match only ASCII characters Lesson: sub, split, flags and compile Table: syntax · meaning Syntax Meaning .*, .+ Greedy: take as much"],[52,"py-regex","Regular expressions","as possible, then back off .*?, .+? Lazy: take as little as possible, then take more (?=...), (?!...) Lookahead: what follows must (not) match. Uses up no characters (?<=...), (?<!...) Lookbehind: what comes before must (not) match *+, ++, (?>...) Possessive and atomic (3.11+): never give characters back Lesson: Greedy, lazy, anchors and lookarounds Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1805 Number of Different Integers in a String Easy findall the digit runs r\"\\d+\" finds every run of digits. int() removes leading zeros, and a set keeps the different values. 2114 Maximum Number of Words Found in Sentences Easy Count words with split split() with no argument splits on any run of whitespace, which is the same as re.split(r\"\\s+\", s.strip()). 2788 Split Strings by Separator Easy re.split with an escaped separator The separator may be a character that is special in regex, such as \".\" or \"|\", so pass it through re.escape. Drop empty pieces. 1910 Remove All Occurrences of a Substring Medium A stack of characters Removing one copy can create a new one, so a single re.sub is not enough. Build the result character by character and cut the part off whenever it ends with it. 722 Remove Comments Medium Join the lines, then one regex Join the source with newlines. r\"//.*|/\\*(.|\\n)*?\\*/\" matches a line comment or a block comment (lazy, across lines). Remove them, split again and drop empty lines. 1023 Camelcase Matching Medium Build a regex from the pattern Between and around the pattern letters, only lower case letters may be added: \"FB\" becomes ^[a-z]*F[a-z]*B[a-z]*$. 1451 Rearrange Words in a Sentence Medium A stable sort by length Lower case the words, sort by length (sorted is stable, so equal lengths keep their order), then capitalise the first letter of the result. 10 Regular Expression Matching Hard Dynamic programming over the two strings match(i, j) asks whether s[i:] matches p[j:]. With a * after p[j], either skip \"x*\" or use it once if the first characters match. Memoise with lru_cache. 44 Wildcard Matching Hard The same dynamic programming with ? and * ? matches one character, * any run (also empty). A * can either match nothing (move in the pattern) or one more character (move in the text). 591 Tag Validator Hard Regex for the pieces, a stack for the nesting Regex cannot check nesting, so use it only for the pieces: remove CDATA blocks, then find tags with a regex and match open and close tags with a stack. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps In the exam Use fullmatch to check input such as a postcode or a username. search would also accept \"xx12345yy\" because it finds 12345 inside it.Lesson: search, match, fullmatch, findall, finditer Common mistake findall changes what it returns when the pattern has groups: with one group it returns that group's text, with several it returns tuples. Use (?:...) for groups you only need for | or repeats.Lesson: Groups: pulling parts out Remember re.compile(pattern) gives a pattern object with the same methods. re caches recent patterns, so compiling is mostly about clarity: give a long pattern a name and write it once.Lesson: sub, split, flags and compile Common mistake Some patterns make the engine try a huge number of ways before failing, for example (a+)+$ on \"aaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaaa!\". This is catastrophic backtracking, and attackers use it to freeze servers (ReDoS). Avoid nested repeats of the same thing, or use possessive quantifiers.Lesson: Greedy, lazy, anchors and lookarounds Remember A named, verbose pattern reads like documentation. fullmatch makes sure the line has exactly the expected shape, and broken lines are skipped instead of giving wrong data.Lesson: Parsing real text 6.2"],[52,"py-formats","CSV, JSON, XML and settings files","Open chapter CSV, JSON with custom types, XML with ElementTree, INI and TOML settings, pickle safety. Key ideas CSV csv.DictReader, DictWriter(f, fieldnames), writeheader, open(..., newline=\"\"), delimiter, quoting. Values are strings. JSON dumps and loads, dump and load, indent, sort_keys, ensure_ascii, default=, object_hook, JSONDecodeError. Tuples become lists, keys strings. XML ET.fromstring, ET.parse, find, findall, findtext, iter, .//tag[@attr=\"v\"], SubElement, indent, tostring. defusedxml for untrusted XML. Settings configparser: sections, getint, getboolean, fallback=, %(name)s. tomllib.load(binary file) and loads, read only. pickle dumps and loads any object, Python only, NEVER load untrusted data (__reduce__ runs code). Formulas CSV file sizeExam favourite bytes ≈ rows × (sum of value lengths + separators + 2 for \\r\\n) 1000 rows of 3 values of 6 characters: 1000 × (18 + 2 + 2) = 22,000 bytes. Lesson: The maths: sizes and overheads JSON overhead for a list of dicts every row repeats every key with quotes, a colon and a comma Keys \"name\", \"city\", \"age\" add about 25 bytes to each row, often more than the data itself. Lesson: The maths: sizes and overheads Base64 for binary data in JSON or XMLExam favourite size out = 4 × ⌈n / 3⌉ A 300 KB image becomes 400 KB of text. Lesson: The maths: sizes and overheads Unicode escapes ensure_ascii=True writes a non ASCII letter as 6 characters \"Zürich\" grows from 7 bytes (UTF-8) to 11 characters with \\u00fc. Lesson: The maths: sizes and overheads Sniffing a separator pick the character that appears the same number of times on every line \"id;price\" and \"1;2,50\": the ; appears once on each line, the comma does not. Lesson: The maths: sizes and overheads Tables Table: · csv · json · xml CSV JSON XML TOML / INI pickle Shape Flat table Nested lists and dicts Nested tags with attributes Settings in sections Any Python object Types kept None, all strings str, numbers, bool, null, list, dict All strings TOML: numbers, bools, dates, lists All of them Read by people Yes, and spreadsheets Yes Yes, wordy Yes, made for it No (bytes) Other languages Every one Every one Every one Many Python only Safe with untrusted data Yes Yes With care (defusedxml) Yes NO: loading can run code Typical use Exports, reports, data science Web APIs, settings, logs Documents, older APIs, office files pyproject.toml, app config Caches inside one trusted program Lesson: Data formats: saving and sharing data Table: csv tool · does csv tool Does csv.reader(f), csv.writer(f) Rows as lists csv.DictReader(f), csv.DictWriter(f, fieldnames) Rows as dicts keyed by the header delimiter=\";\", quotechar='\"' Other separators and quote characters quoting=csv.QUOTE_ALL, QUOTE_MINIMAL, QUOTE_NONNUMERIC When values get quotes csv.Sniffer().sniff(sample) Guess the separator of an unknown file Lesson: CSV: tables as text Table: python · json · back to python Python JSON Back to Python dict object {...} dict (keys always str) list, tuple array [...] list str string \"...\" str int, float number int or float True, False true, false True, False None null None set, date, Decimal, your classes TypeError unless default= handles them Lesson: JSON: nested data for the web Table: elementtree · does ElementTree Does ET.parse(path), ET.fromstring(text) Read a file or a string into a tree el.tag, el.attrib, el.get(name), el.text The tag name, attributes and text of an element el.find(path), el.findall(path), el.findtext(path) Children by a simple path, such as \"book/title\" el.iter(tag) Every element with that tag, at any depth .//book[@lang=\"de\"] The XPath subset: any depth, attribute tests, positions ET.SubElement, el.set, ET.indent, ET.tostring, tree.write Build, change and write XML Lesson: XML with ElementTree Table: · ini (configparser) · toml (tomllib) · json INI (configparser) TOML (tomllib) JSON Environment variables Types Strings, with getint, getboolean Real numbers, bools, dates, lists, tables Numbers, bools, lists, dicts Strings Comments"],[52,"py-formats","CSV, JSON, XML and settings files","Yes Yes No No Write with the standard library Yes No, read only (tomli-w writes) Yes os.environ Typical use Older tools, setup.cfg pyproject.toml, new apps Web app settings Secrets and per server values (12 factor apps) Lesson: Settings: INI and TOML Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 171 Excel Sheet Column Number Easy Base 26 with letters Spreadsheet columns count A to Z, then AA. Each letter is a digit from 1 to 26: result = result × 26 + value. 168 Excel Sheet Column Title Easy Base 26 the other way, with a shift There is no zero digit, so subtract 1 before each divmod by 26. 1507 Reformat Date Easy Split and look up the month Split \"20th Oct 2052\" into three parts. Strip the two letter ending from the day, look the month up in a list, and pad with zfill(2). 2194 Cells in a Range on an Excel Sheet Easy Two nested loops over letters and rows The range \"K1:L2\" gives column letters K to L and rows 1 to 2. Columns go in the outer loop. 1410 HTML Entity Parser Medium Replace the entities, the ampersand one last Replace each named entity with its character. Do the ampersand entity last, or the text you just made could be read as a new entity. 385 Mini Parser Medium A stack of lists while reading characters LeetCode uses a NestedInteger class. Here plain ints and lists stand for it. Push a new list at [, finish a number at , or ], and pop at ]. 831 Masking Personal Information Medium Two formats, two rules An email keeps the first and last letter of the name and lower cases everything. A phone keeps only digits, shows the last 4, and stars for a country code. 640 Solve the Equation Medium A regex for the terms on each side The pattern [+-]?\\d*x|[+-]?\\d+ finds terms like +2x, -x or 5. Add up x coefficients and numbers on each side, then move everything to one side. 726 Number of Atoms Hard A stack of Counters Read an atom name (capital letter and lower case letters) and its count. At ( push a new Counter, at ) multiply the top one by the number after it and add it to the one below. 1096 Brace Expansion II Hard A recursive parser for union and product A comma means union of sets, writing things next to each other means every combination (product). Parse with a function that reads until } and calls itself at {. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps In the exam Always open CSV files with newline=\"\". The csv module handles line endings itself (rows end with \\r\\n by default), and quotes inside values. Without newline=\"\" you can get blank lines between rows on Windows.Lesson: CSV: tables as text Remember dump and load work with files, dumps and loads (s for string) with strings. indent=2 makes it readable, sort_keys=True makes the output stable for tests and diffs, and ensure_ascii=False keeps letters like ü instead of \\u00fc.Lesson: JSON: nested data for the web Common mistake Never parse XML from strangers with the standard library alone: specially made files can use huge amounts of memory (the \"billion laughs\" attack). Use the defusedxml package for untrusted XML.Lesson: XML with ElementTree Common mistake pickle can save almost any Python object, but pickle.loads can run any code the creator of the data chose, as the Evil class shows with a harmless print. Never unpickle data from the internet, uploads or anyone you do not fully trust. Use JSON for data you exchange.Lesson: pickle: Python objects as bytes 6.3"],[52,"py-sqlite","Databases with sqlite3 and SQL","Open chapter sqlite3, SQL queries and joins, placeholders against injection, transactions, constraints and indexes. Key ideas Basics sqlite3.connect(path or \":memory:\"), execute, executemany, executescript, commit, close, fetchone, fetchall, lastrowid, rowcount. SQL CREATE TABLE, INSERT, SELECT, UPDATE and DELETE with WHERE, ORDER BY, GROUP BY, HAVING, JOIN, LEFT JOIN, COUNT, SUM, COALESCE, LIMIT. Safety Values only through ? or :name. Never f-strings in SQL. Check table and column names against a fixed list. Transactions \"with conn:\" commits or rolls back (does not close). IntegrityError from PRIMARY KEY, UNIQUE, NOT NULL, CHECK, foreign keys (PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON). Speed sqlite3.Row, CREATE INDEX, EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN (SCAN vs SEARCH), one commit for many inserts, B-tree height log base f of N. Formulas Full scan cost ≈ N rows read Finding one user among 1,000,000 without an index reads 1,000,000 rows. Lesson: The maths: indexes, joins and estimates B-tree heightExam favourite h = ⌈logf N⌉, f = keys per page (fan out) N = 1,000,000 and f = 100: h = 3 page reads instead of a million rows. Lesson: The maths: indexes, joins and estimates Binary search in a sorted index comparisons ≈ ⌈log2 N⌉ log2 1,000,000 ≈ 20. Lesson: The maths: indexes, joins and estimates Selectivity of WHERE a = vExam favourite rows ≈ N / V(a), V(a) = number of different values 1,000,000 rows and 100 cities: about 10,000 rows per city. Lesson: The maths: indexes, joins and estimates Equi join size estimate |R ⋈ S| ≈ |R| × |S| / max(V(R, a), V(S, a)) 1000 customers, 5000 orders, joined on 1000 customer ids: 1000 × 5000 / 1000 = 5000 rows. Lesson: The maths: indexes, joins and estimates Cost of commits time ≈ commits × time per disk sync 1000 inserts with 1000 commits at 5 ms each: 5 s. One commit: about 5 ms. Lesson: The maths: indexes, joins and estimates Tables Table: · json or csv file · sqlite (sqlite3) · postgresql or mysql JSON or CSV file SQLite (sqlite3) PostgreSQL or MySQL pandas DataFrame Setup None None, one file A server to install and run pip install pandas Find one record in a million Read everything Milliseconds with an index Milliseconds with an index Fast, but all data in memory Safe when a program crashes No Yes, transactions Yes, transactions No, memory only Many writers at once No One writer at a time Yes, many No Rules on the data None Types, NOT NULL, UNIQUE, CHECK, foreign keys The same and more Only in your code Typical use Small settings and exports Apps, phones, browsers, tests, prototypes Web sites with many users Analysis and reports Lesson: Databases and SQL from Python Table: step · code · meaning Step Code Meaning Open conn = sqlite3.connect(\"shop.db\") Opens the file, or makes it. \":memory:\" gives a database in memory only. Cursor cur = conn.cursor() An object that runs SQL and walks through results. conn.execute() makes one for you. Run SQL cur.execute(sql, values) Runs one statement. Values go in a tuple, matching the ? marks. Save conn.commit() Makes INSERT, UPDATE and DELETE permanent. Without it they are lost on close. Read fetchone(), fetchall(), fetchmany(n), for row in cur Rows come back as tuples. Close conn.close() Frees the file. Uncommitted changes are thrown away. Lesson: Connect, execute, commit, fetch Table: sql · does · runs in step SQL Does Runs in step FROM a JOIN b ON ... Takes rows from tables and pairs them up 1 WHERE condition Keeps rows where the condition is true 2 GROUP BY column Puts rows with the same value in one group 3 HAVING condition Keeps groups (it can use COUNT, SUM, AVG) 4 SELECT columns Picks and names the output columns 5 ORDER BY column DESC Sorts the result 6 LIMIT n OFFSET k Keeps n rows after skipping k 7 Lesson: SQL in one lesson: SELECT, WHERE, JOIN, GROUP BY Table: change data · example Change data Example Make a table CREATE TABLE t (id INTEGER PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT NOT NULL UNIQUE) Add rows INSERT INTO t (name) VALUES (?) Change rows UPDATE t SET name = ? WHERE id = ? Remove"],[52,"py-sqlite","Databases with sqlite3 and SQL","rows DELETE FROM t WHERE id = ? Remove the table DROP TABLE IF EXISTS t Lesson: SQL in one lesson: SELECT, WHERE, JOIN, GROUP BY Table: · inner join (join) · left join · cross join INNER JOIN (JOIN) LEFT JOIN CROSS JOIN Keeps Only pairs that match Every left row, with NULL when nothing matches Every left row with every right row Rows for 4 customers, 3 with orders One per matching order Matching orders plus 1 row for the customer without orders 4 × number of orders Use it for Orders with their customer Customers and their orders, even with none All combinations (rarely) Lesson: SQL in one lesson: SELECT, WHERE, JOIN, GROUP BY Table: style · sql · values Style SQL Values qmark WHERE name = ? AND age > ? a tuple: (\"ann\", 30) named WHERE name = :name AND age > :age a dict: {\"name\": \"ann\", \"age\": 30} Many rows executemany(\"INSERT INTO t VALUES (?, ?)\", rows) a list of tuples A script executescript(\"CREATE ...; INSERT ...\") no values: only for your own fixed SQL Lesson: Placeholders and SQL injection Table: letter · means · in the example Letter Means In the example A, atomic All the changes happen, or none The failed transfer did not give Ann 500 C, consistent Rules such as CHECK and UNIQUE always hold No balance is ever below 0 I, isolated Others do not see half done changes Another connection sees the old balances until commit D, durable After commit the data survives a crash or power cut SQLite writes a journal before it changes the file Lesson: Transactions and rules on the data Table: rule in create table · stops · error Rule in CREATE TABLE Stops Error PRIMARY KEY, UNIQUE Two rows with the same value IntegrityError: UNIQUE constraint failed NOT NULL A missing value IntegrityError: NOT NULL constraint failed CHECK (balance >= 0) Values that break your rule IntegrityError: CHECK constraint failed REFERENCES other(col) A link to a row that does not exist IntegrityError: FOREIGN KEY constraint failed (after PRAGMA foreign_keys = ON) Lesson: Transactions and rules on the data Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 175 Combine Two Tables Easy LEFT JOIN keeps people without an address Every person must appear, even without an address, so use LEFT JOIN. Missing columns come back as None. 182 Duplicate Emails Easy GROUP BY with HAVING COUNT Group the rows by email and keep the groups that have more than one row. 183 Customers Who Never Order Easy NOT IN a subquery, or LEFT JOIN ... IS NULL Find customers whose id is not in the list of customer ids of the orders. 197 Rising Temperature Easy Join the table with itself on yesterday Pair each day with the day before using DATE(day, '-1 day'), then keep pairs where the temperature went up. 196 Delete Duplicate Emails Easy DELETE rows that are not the smallest id Keep MIN(id) of each email and delete every other row. 176 Second Highest Salary Medium DISTINCT, ORDER BY, LIMIT 1 OFFSET 1 inside a subquery The outer SELECT turns \"no row\" into NULL, which is None in Python. 178 Rank Scores Medium The DENSE_RANK window function DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY score DESC) gives equal scores the same rank and leaves no gaps after ties. 184 Department Highest Salary Medium Join with the maximum of each department A subquery finds MAX(salary) per department. Join it back to keep every employee who earns that much, ties included. 185 Department Top Three Salaries Hard DENSE_RANK per department with PARTITION BY PARTITION BY departmentId restarts the ranking in each department. Keep rank 3 or better. Ties share a rank. 262 Trips and Users Hard Join twice to the users table, then average a condition Join Users once for the client and once for the driver and keep trips where neither is banned. AVG(status != 'completed') is the share of cancelled trips, because a true test counts as 1. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps In the exam The most common beginner bug: INSERT works, the program ends, and the data is gone. You forgot conn.commit(). Using \"with conn:\" commits for"],[52,"py-sqlite","Databases with sqlite3 and SQL","you.Lesson: Connect, execute, commit, fetch Remember SQL types in SQLite are flexible: INTEGER, REAL, TEXT, BLOB and NULL. Python int, float, str, bytes and None map to them. Store dates as ISO text such as \"2026-11-07\", because the automatic date converters are deprecated since Python 3.12.Lesson: Connect, execute, commit, fetch Common mistake An UPDATE or DELETE without WHERE changes EVERY row. Write the WHERE first, and try it as a SELECT before you run it.Lesson: SQL in one lesson: SELECT, WHERE, JOIN, GROUP BY Common mistake Never put values into SQL with f-strings, +, % or .format(). Use ? or :name every time. Placeholders stop SQL injection, handle quotes in names like O'Neil, and let the database reuse the prepared statement.Lesson: Placeholders and SQL injection In the exam Placeholders are for VALUES only. A table or column name cannot be a ?. If a user picks the column to sort by, check it against a fixed list such as {\"name\", \"price\"} before you put it into the SQL.Lesson: Placeholders and SQL injection Remember \"with conn:\" commits or rolls back, but it does NOT close the connection. Since Python 3.12 you can also pass autocommit=False to sqlite3.connect for the transaction rules of the database standard (PEP 249).Lesson: Transactions and rules on the data Remember An index is a sorted copy of one column with a pointer to each row, kept in a B-tree. It turns a scan of every row into a few page reads. It costs disk space and makes INSERT and UPDATE a little slower, so index the columns you search, join or sort by often.Lesson: Friendlier rows and fast lookups with indexes 6.4"],[52,"py-tkinter","Desktop windows with tkinter","Open chapter tkinter widgets, layouts, variables, events, Canvas animation with after() and responsive apps. Key ideas First window root = tk.Tk(), title, geometry(\"WxH\"), Label, Button(command=f), pack, root.mainloop(). command=f without brackets. Layout grid(row, column, sticky=\"nsew\", columnspan, padx, pady), columnconfigure(i, weight=1), pack(side, fill, expand), place(x, y). One manager per parent. Input and variables Entry.get, insert, delete. StringVar, IntVar, BooleanVar, textvariable, variable, trace_add(\"write\", f). messagebox, filedialog. Events and Canvas bind(\"\", f) with event.x, event.y, event.keysym. create_line, create_oval, move, coords. after(ms, f) for animation. Responsive apps Callbacks return fast. after() for timers, threads plus queue.Queue for slow work, widgets only from the main thread. Model apart from the view. Formulas Screen coordinates x grows to the right, y grows DOWN, (0, 0) is the top left corner A point 50 pixels from the top has y = 50, not y = height − 50. Lesson: The maths: pixels, frames and how fast people click Centre a windowExam favourite x = (screen width − window width) // 2, y = (screen height − window height) // 2 A 300 × 200 window on 1920 × 1080: x = 810, y = 440, geometry \"300x200+810+440\". Lesson: The maths: pixels, frames and how fast people click Frames and speed delay = 1000 / fps ms, speed = pixels per step × 1000 / delay after(20, ...) gives 50 frames a second. 4 pixels a step is 200 pixels a second. Lesson: The maths: pixels, frames and how fast people click Sharing extra space extra for column i = extra × weighti / sum of weights 300 extra pixels, weights 1 and 2: 100 and 200 pixels. Lesson: The maths: pixels, frames and how fast people click Fitts's lawExam favourite time = a + b × log2(D / W + 1) D = distance to the target, W = its width. Big, close buttons are faster to hit. Lesson: The maths: pixels, frames and how fast people click Hick's law time = b × log2(n + 1) n equal choices. 7 menu items: log2 8 = 3 times b. Lesson: The maths: pixels, frames and how fast people click Tables Table: · tkinter · pyside6 or pyqt6 · kivy tkinter PySide6 or PyQt6 Kivy Web app (Flask, Django) Textual Install Comes with Python pip, about 100 MB pip pip, plus a browser pip Look Simple, native with ttk Modern, very rich Its own touch style Anything HTML and CSS can do Text in the terminal Runs on Desktop Desktop Desktop, Android, iOS Any device with a browser Any terminal, also over SSH Licence Python licence LGPL (PySide6) or GPL / paid (PyQt6) MIT Your choice MIT Best for Small tools, teaching, the PCPP exam Large desktop applications Touch and mobile apps Apps for many users Developer tools Lesson: Windows, buttons and the event loop Table: step · code · what it does Step Code What it does 1 root = tk.Tk() Make the main window 2 w = tk.Label(root, text=...) Make a widget inside a parent 3 w.pack() or w.grid(...) Place it, or it stays invisible 4 command=function, w.bind(event, function) Connect events to your functions 5 w.config(text=...) or w[\"text\"] = ... Change a widget later 6 root.mainloop() Start the event loop Lesson: Your first window Table: · pack · grid · place pack grid place Idea Stack widgets from one side (top, left ...) A table of rows and columns Exact x, y position or relative position Good for Toolbars, simple columns of widgets Forms, dialogs, almost every real layout Overlays, games, special cases Grows with the window fill and expand sticky and weight Only with relx, rely, relwidth Main options side, fill, expand, padx, pady, anchor row, column, sticky, columnspan, rowspan, padx, pady x, y, relx, rely, anchor, width, height Lesson: Placing widgets: pack, grid and place Table: widget · use it for · read or set Widget Use it for Read or set ttk.Label, tk.Message Text or an image config(text=...) or textvariable ttk.Button An action command= ttk.Entry One line of text get(), insert(0, s), delete(0, \"end\") tk.Text Many lines of text get(\"1.0\", \"end\"),"],[52,"py-tkinter","Desktop windows with tkinter","insert(\"end\", s) ttk.Checkbutton, ttk.Radiobutton Yes or no, one of several variable= BooleanVar or StringVar ttk.Combobox, tk.Listbox Pick from a list get(), curselection() ttk.Scale, ttk.Spinbox A number in a range variable= or get() tk.Canvas Drawing and animation create_line, create_oval, move, coords ttk.Frame, ttk.LabelFrame Group other widgets a parent for others tk.Menu, filedialog, messagebox Menus and standard dialogs askopenfilename(), showinfo() Lesson: Widgets and control variables Table: event string · happens when · useful event fields Event string Happens when Useful event fields <Button-1>, <Button-3> Left or right mouse button pressed event.x, event.y <Double-Button-1> Double click event.x, event.y <B1-Motion>, <Motion> Mouse moved with or without the button down event.x, event.y <Key>, <Return>, <Escape>, a Any key, Enter, Esc, the a key event.keysym, event.char <Control-s> Ctrl and S together event.state <Configure> The widget changed size event.width, event.height <FocusIn>, <FocusOut> The widget got or lost the keyboard event.widget Lesson: Events, the Canvas and animation with after() Table: · everything in the callbacks · model and view apart Everything in the callbacks Model and view apart Testing Only by clicking by hand Normal unit tests with pytest Changing the look Touches the rules too Only the view changes Moving to another toolkit or the web Rewrite everything Keep the model, write a new view Lesson: A real app: classes, scrollbars and saving Table: long task · what to use Long task What to use Waiting or a timer root.after(ms, function) Many small steps (a counter, an animation) Do one step, then after(ms, next_step) Slow input and output (download, database) A thread that puts results in a queue.Queue, checked with after() Heavy CPU work A process (concurrent.futures.ProcessPoolExecutor), results checked with after() Never time.sleep() or a long loop inside a callback Lesson: Long tasks without freezing the window Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 657 Robot Return to Origin Easy Count the moves in each direction Like moving an item on a canvas: R adds to x, L takes away. Back at the start means x and y are both 0. 733 Flood Fill Easy The paint bucket tool: breadth first search Start at the clicked pixel and paint every neighbour (up, down, left, right) that has the old colour. Stop at once if the new colour equals the old one. 836 Rectangle Overlap Easy Overlap on both axes Two boxes overlap when the left edge of each is before the right edge of the other, on x AND on y. Touching edges do not count. Games use this for collisions. 1232 Check If It Is a Straight Line Easy Compare slopes with a cross product Points are on one line when (y - y0) × (x1 - x0) equals (y1 - y0) × (x - x0) for every point. Multiplying avoids dividing by zero for vertical lines. 223 Rectangle Area Medium Both areas minus the overlap Add the two areas and take away the overlap. The overlap width is max(0, min of right edges minus max of left edges), the same for the height. 593 Valid Square Medium Six distances between four points A square has 4 equal sides and 2 equal, longer diagonals. Use squared distances to stay with whole numbers, and reject a length of 0. 874 Walking Robot Simulation Medium Directions as a list, obstacles as a set Keep the four directions in a list and turn by moving the index. Store obstacles as a set of tuples so each step is checked in O(1). 1041 Robot Bounded In Circle Medium Run the instructions once and look at the result After one run the robot is either back at the start or facing a new direction. In both cases it stays in a circle. Only \"away and still facing north\" escapes. 391 Perfect Rectangle Hard Areas add up and only four corners stay The small areas must add up to the big box. Toggle every corner in a set: inner corners appear an even number of times and cancel out, so exactly the four outer corners must be left. 850 Rectangle Area II Hard"],[52,"py-tkinter","Desktop windows with tkinter","Coordinate compression on a grid Collect every x and y edge, sort them and mark which small cells between them are covered. Add up the covered cells and take the result modulo 109 + 7. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps In the exam command=say_hello() (with brackets) is the classic beginner bug: it calls the function ONCE while the button is made and stores its result, None, as the command. Then clicks do nothing. To pass arguments, use command=lambda: say(name).Lesson: Your first window Common mistake Never mix pack and grid for widgets with the same parent: Tk raises TclError (\"cannot use geometry manager grid inside . which already has slaves managed by pack\"). Put each group in its own Frame instead, and each Frame can use its own manager.Lesson: Placing widgets: pack, grid and place Remember ttk widgets (from tkinter import ttk) use the look of the operating system and are styled with themes. The older tk widgets use options such as bg and fg. Prefer ttk where it has the widget. Listbox, Text, Canvas and Menu exist only in tk.Lesson: Widgets and control variables Remember bind on a widget catches events in that widget. root.bind catches them anywhere in the window, and bind_all in every window of the program. For the close button use root.protocol(\"WM_DELETE_WINDOW\", function).Lesson: Events, the Canvas and animation with after() Common mistake Tk is not thread safe. Create and change widgets only in the thread that runs mainloop. Other threads hand their results over through a queue, and the main thread picks them up with after().Lesson: Long tasks without freezing the window"],[52,"step-advanced-8","Step 8Advanced Python · Exam practice","8.1"],[52,"py-adv-exam","Exam practice: PCPP1 style papers","Open chapter Four PCPP1 style papers, a bug hunt, the traps table, exam technique and a mock quiz. Key ideas Exam facts PCPP1 (PCPP-32-101) as listed by training providers: 45 questions, 65 minutes, 70 %. Confirm on pythoninstitute.org/pcpp1. Object traps __eq__ needs __hash__, NotImplemented for unknown types, super() follows the MRO, wraps keeps the name, slots remove __dict__. Iteration traps Generators run lazily and once, send resumes a yield, yield from returns the inner value, groupby needs sorted input. Error traps __exit__ True swallows, raise from sets __cause__, except* splits groups, never except Exception: pass. Library traps Raw strings for regex, json makes lists and str keys, csv gives str, commit() in sqlite3, command=f in tkinter. Formulas Safety margin aim = pass mark + 15 points in practice Practise until you score about 85 %, because exam nerves cost a few points. Lesson: Exam technique and a study plan Tables Table: · pcpp1 (pcpp-32-101), as listed by training providers PCPP1 (PCPP-32-101), as listed by training providers Questions 45 Time 65 minutes Pass mark 70 % Before it PCAP (Certified Associate) is recommended Blocks Advanced object oriented programming. Coding conventions, best practices and standards (PEP 8, PEP 257). GUI programming with tkinter. Network programming (sockets, REST). File processing and the program environment (sqlite3, CSV, XML, logging, configparser). Lesson: The PCPP1 exam and how to use this chapter Table: exam block · chapters in this course Exam block Chapters in this course Advanced OOP 1.1 to 1.5 (special methods, decorators, class design, exceptions and copies, metaclasses) Conventions and best practices 3.1 (style, PEP 8, PEP 257, type hints), 3.2 testing GUI programming 6.4 tkinter Network programming Web and APIs part (HTTP, sockets, requests, REST) Files and environment 3.3 logging, 6.2 CSV, JSON, XML and settings, 6.3 sqlite3 Extra (not in the exam, used every day) 2.1 to 2.3 generators, itertools, context managers, 4.1 and 4.2 speed, 5.1 and 5.2 concurrency, 6.1 regex Lesson: The PCPP1 exam and how to use this chapter Table: paper · covers · lesson Paper Covers Lesson A Special methods, MRO, class and static methods, properties, ABCs, decorators, metaclasses, slots, copies 2 B Generators, send, yield from, iterators, itertools, context managers, exception chaining and groups 3 C Regular expressions, json, csv, collections, heapq, sqlite3 4 D functools, dataclasses, enum, parameter kinds, type hints, sorting, the walrus, match 5 Lesson: The PCPP1 exam and how to use this chapter Table: q · output · why (chapter) Q Output Why (chapter) 1 V(3) True False + calls __add__, == calls __eq__, but is compares identity: two different objects (1.1) 2 TypeError A class that defines __eq__ without __hash__ gets __hash__ = None, so its objects cannot go in a set (1.1) 3 BCA ['D', 'B', 'C', 'A', 'object'] super() follows the MRO of the object, not the parent of the class. In B it calls C, not A (1.3) 4 2 hi The classmethod gets the class, the staticmethod gets nothing. Both can be called on the class (1.3) 5 25 too cold The setter refused -300 before changing _c, so the old value stays (1.3) 6 cannot make a Shape A class with an abstract method that is not overridden cannot be instantiated (1.3) 7 10 wrapper The decorator doubles the result. Without functools.wraps the function now reports the name wrapper (1.2) 8 ['x', 'x', 'x'] repeat(3) runs first and returns the real decorator (1.2) 9 thing Meta type The metaclass __new__ added tag. The type of a class is its metaclass, and the type of type is type (1.5) 10 False __slots__ removes the per object __dict__, so new attributes are refused (1.3) 11 [1, 2, 9] [1, 2] False True A shallow copy is a new outer list that shares the inner lists. deepcopy copies them too (1.4) Lesson: Paper A: advanced object oriented programming Table: q · output · why (chapter) Q Output Why (chapter) 1 made, start, 1, middle, [2] Calling a generator"],[52,"py-adv-exam","Exam practice: PCPP1 style papers","function runs nothing. Each next runs to the next yield (2.1) 2 got 10, then 5 20 send(10) resumes the paused yield with the value 10. The print inside runs before the Q2 line can print (2.1) 3 [1, 2, 'done'] yield from passes the inner values on and gives back the inner return value (2.1) 4 True [3] in on an iterator uses it up until it finds 2, so only 3 is left (2.1) 5 [10, 15, 20] [1, 3, 6] count is endless, islice takes 3. accumulate gives running totals (2.2) 6 ['a', 'b', 1] ['a', 'b', 'a'] 10 groupby groups only NEIGHBOURS that are equal, so a appears twice. 5 choose 2 is 10 (2.2) 7 <b>, B, </b> The code before yield is the set up, the finally part is the clean up (2.3) 8 exit saw ZeroDivisionError, after the with block __exit__ returning True swallows the exception (2.3) 9 KeyError True raise ... from err sets __cause__ and hides the automatic context (1.4) 10 V 2, T 1 except* splits the group: each handler gets the matching part (1.4) Lesson: Paper B: generators, context managers and exceptions Table: q · output · why (chapter) Q Output Why (chapter) 1 ['1', '22', '333'] home at ann findall returns strings. In the replacement, \\2 and \\1 are the groups (6.1) 2 None True True match only tries at the start, search anywhere, fullmatch the whole text (6.1) 3 ['<a><b>'] ['<a>', '<b>'] .+ is greedy and takes as much as it can, .+? is lazy (6.1) 4 {\"a\": null, \"b\": [1, 2]} [1, 2.0, True] None becomes null, tuples become lists, true becomes True (6.2) 5 [['x', 'y, z', '3']] str The quoted field keeps its comma. csv gives every value as a string (6.2) 6 [('a', 3), ('n', 2)] {'a': 1} most_common sorts by count. A defaultdict is a dict (4.2) 7 [0, 2, 3] maxlen=3: append 4 pushes out 1, appendleft 0 pushes out 4 from the right end (4.2) 8 1 [1, 2] 1 [2, 5, 4] heapify puts the smallest first. After heappop the list is still a heap, not sorted (4.2) 9 3 Point(x=9, y=2) {'x': 1, 'y': 2} A namedtuple works by name and by index. _replace makes a new one (4.2) 10 (3, 3, 6) Aggregates give one row: COUNT, MAX and SUM (6.3) Lesson: Paper C: the standard library Table: q · output · why (chapter) Q Output Why (chapter) 1 832040 31 With the cache every fib(n) from 0 to 30 is worked out once: 31 misses (1.2, 4.1) 2 25 24 partial fixes exp=2. reduce multiplies 1 × 2 × 3 × 4 (2.2) 3 greet Say hi. functools.wraps copies the name and the docstring to the wrapper (1.2) 4 True Card(rank=2, suit='hearts', tags=[]) True order=True compares the fields in order. The dataclass writes __repr__ and __eq__ (1.3) 5 Color.RED GREEN 1 2 Color(2) finds by value, Color[\"RED\"] by name (1.3) 6 a is positional only, then 6 Parameters before / cannot be passed by name. After * they must be (1.2) 7 6 {'x': <class 'int'>, 'return': <class 'str'>} Type hints are not checked when the code runs. Use mypy for that (3.1) 8 ['C', 'a', 'b'] ['a', 'b', 'C'] 0 Upper case letters sort before lower case ones. key=str.lower ignores case. max needs default= for an empty list 9 True 3 1 2 3 := assigns inside an expression. *x unpacks the list into separate arguments 10 x is 1 The first case needs x == 0. The second captures the value in x0 Lesson: Paper D: functions, data classes and modern syntax Table: trap · looks like · really · chapter Trap Looks like Really Chapter __eq__ only Objects still work in sets They become unhashable 1.1 NotImplemented Return False when the type is wrong Return NotImplemented, so Python can try the other side 1.1 super() Calls the parent class Calls the next class in the MRO of the object 1.3 Decorator without wraps Keeps the name and docstring The function is now called wrapper 1.2 Decorator with arguments @repeat is enough @repeat(3): three levels of functions 1.2 Mutable class attribute Each object has its own list All objects share one list 1.3 Shallow copy copy.copy copies everything Inner objects are shared 1.4 Generator Can be looped over twice It is empty after the first loop 2.1 groupby Groups all equal items Groups only neighbours: sort"],[52,"py-adv-exam","Exam practice: PCPP1 style papers","first 2.2 __exit__ return value Return True to say \"done\" True swallows the exception 2.3 raise from Same as a plain raise Sets __cause__ and shows \"direct cause\" 1.4 Type hints Python checks them Only tools such as mypy check them 3.1 Regex without r \"\\d\" and r\"\\d\" are the same Some escapes, like \\b, change meaning 6.1 pickle Safe like JSON Loading can run code 6.2 sqlite3 Changes are saved at once Call commit() or use with conn 6.3 tkinter callback command=f() is right command=f, without brackets 6.4 pack and grid Can be mixed in one parent TclError: use one per parent 6.4 Lesson: The traps in one table Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 304 Range Sum Query 2D - Immutable Medium A class with a table of prefix sums Build sums[r + 1][c + 1] = the total of the rectangle from (0, 0) to (r, c) once in __init__. Any rectangle is then four lookups. 438 Find All Anagrams in a String Medium A sliding window of Counters Keep a Counter of the last len(p) letters. Add the new letter, remove the oldest, and compare with the Counter of p. 39 Combination Sum Medium A recursive generator A generator yields each combination as soon as it is found. Pass the start index so a number is never used before a smaller one, which avoids repeats. 131 Palindrome Partitioning Medium yield from for every first piece Try every palindrome as the first piece, then partition the rest the same way. A generator with yield from keeps the code short. 1143 Longest Common Subsequence Medium Recursion with lru_cache best(i, j) compares text1[i:] and text2[j:]. Equal letters add 1, otherwise skip one letter on either side. functools.cache stores every answer. 221 Maximal Square Medium Grow squares from three neighbours size[r][c] is the side of the biggest all \"1\" square ending at (r, c): 1 + the smallest of the sizes above, to the left and diagonal. 72 Edit Distance Medium A table of smaller answers dist[i][j] is the cost to turn the first i letters of word1 into the first j letters of word2. Equal letters cost nothing, otherwise 1 + the best of insert, delete or replace. 42 Trapping Rain Water Hard Two pointers with the highest wall on each side Water above a bar is the lower of the highest walls on its left and right, minus the bar. Move the pointer on the lower side, because that side decides the water level. 76 Minimum Window Substring Hard Grow the window, then shrink it Move the right end until the window holds every letter of t (count with a Counter). Then move the left end while it still does, and remember the shortest window. 460 LFU Cache Hard A dict of OrderedDicts, one per use count Keep the value and the use count of each key, and an OrderedDict of keys for every count. Evict the oldest key in the group with the smallest count. Lesson: LeetCode review set Tips and traps Common mistake Check pythoninstitute.org/pcpp1 before you book. Exam versions, prices and weights change, and this page could not confirm them from the official site.Lesson: The PCPP1 exam and how to use this chapter Remember Cover the answers, write down what you think each line prints, then open the answers. A wrong guess tells you exactly which chapter to reread.Lesson: The PCPP1 exam and how to use this chapter In the exam In the exam: read the whole snippet first, then the options. Trace it on paper and watch for the answer \"the code raises an exception\". Mark hard questions and come back, because every question is worth the same.Lesson: Exam technique and a study plan"],[52,"step-web-1","Step 1Web and APIs · How the web works: HTTP, REST and sockets","1.1"],[52,"py-http","HTTP and REST: how the web talks","Open chapter HTTP messages, URLs, methods, status codes, headers, cookies, caching and REST design. Key ideas Messages Request line (method, path, version), headers, blank line, body. Status line, headers, blank line, body. \\r\\n line ends. Methods and codes GET read, POST create (201 + Location), PUT replace, PATCH change, DELETE (204). 2xx ok, 3xx redirect, 4xx client, 5xx server. URLs urlsplit, parse_qs, parse_qsl, urlencode, quote, urljoin. Percent encoding: one byte becomes %XX. Headers Content-Type, Accept, Authorization, Set-Cookie (HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite), Cache-Control, ETag and If-None-Match for 304. Python http.server.BaseHTTPRequestHandler (do_GET, send_response, send_header, end_headers, wfile), urllib.request, HTTPError for 4xx and 5xx. Formulas Time to first byteExam favourite about (TCP 1 + TLS 1 + request 1) round trips × RTT RTT 50 ms: 3 × 50 = 150 ms before the first byte of the answer, however small it is. Lesson: The maths: latency, bandwidth and caching Transfer time time = size × 8 / bandwidth 2 MB at 40 Mbit/s: 16 Mbit / 40 = 0.4 s. Lesson: The maths: latency, bandwidth and caching Average time with a cacheExam favourite average = hit rate × hit time + (1 − hit rate) × miss time 80 % hits at 5 ms, misses at 200 ms: 0.8 × 5 + 0.2 × 200 = 44 ms. Lesson: The maths: latency, bandwidth and caching Little's law requests in progress = arrival rate × time per request 200 requests a second, 50 ms each: 10 requests at the same moment. Lesson: The maths: latency, bandwidth and caching Bandwidth delay product bits in flight = bandwidth × RTT 100 Mbit/s and 80 ms: 8 Mbit (1 MB) on the wire at once. Lesson: The maths: latency, bandwidth and caching Percent encoding size a byte outside the allowed set becomes 3 characters \"é\" is 2 bytes in UTF-8, so it becomes 6 characters: %C3%A9. Lesson: The maths: latency, bandwidth and caching Tables Table: · rest over http · graphql · grpc REST over HTTP GraphQL gRPC WebSocket Idea URLs name resources, methods say what to do One URL, the client writes a query for exactly the fields it wants Call functions on a server, messages in Protocol Buffers One connection that stays open, both sides send at any time Data Usually JSON JSON Binary, typed Text or binary frames Good for Public APIs, most web apps Apps that need flexible queries Fast calls between internal services Chat, live prices, games Caching Built into HTTP Harder Do it yourself Not for messages In Python requests, Flask, Django, FastAPI graphene, strawberry grpcio websockets Lesson: How the web works: one request, one response Table: part · example · meaning Part Example Meaning scheme https How to talk: http, https, ftp, mailto ... host api.example.com Which computer. DNS turns it into an IP address port 8443 Which program on that computer. Default 80 for http, 443 for https path /v1/books/42 Which resource on the server query fields=title,year&lang=en Extra options as name=value pairs joined with & fragment reviews A place inside the page. The browser keeps it and never sends it Lesson: URLs: the address of a resource Table: method · means · safe (changes nothing) · idempotent (same result if repeated) Method Means Safe (changes nothing) Idempotent (same result if repeated) Has a body GET Read a resource Yes Yes No HEAD Like GET, but only the headers Yes Yes No POST Create something, or run an action No No Yes PUT Replace the whole resource No Yes Yes PATCH Change part of a resource No Not always Yes DELETE Remove a resource No Yes Usually no OPTIONS Ask what is allowed (used by CORS) Yes Yes No Lesson: Inside an HTTP message Table: range · meaning · common codes Range Meaning Common codes 1xx Wait, more is coming 101 Switching Protocols (WebSocket) 2xx Success 200 OK, 201 Created, 204 No Content 3xx Look somewhere else 301 Moved Permanently, 302 Found, 304 Not Modified 4xx The client made a mistake 400 Bad Request, 401 Unauthorized (not logged in), 403 Forbidden (not allowed), 404 Not Found, 409 Conflict, 422"],[52,"py-http","HTTP and REST: how the web talks","invalid data, 429 Too Many Requests 5xx The server failed 500 Internal Server Error, 502 Bad Gateway, 503 Service Unavailable, 504 Gateway Timeout Lesson: Inside an HTTP message Table: action · method and path · success · typical errors Action Method and path Success Typical errors List books GET /books 200 with a JSON list Read one book GET /books/2 200 with the book 404 if it does not exist Create a book POST /books with JSON 201 Created and a Location header 400 or 422 for bad data Replace a book PUT /books/2 with JSON 200 or 204 404, 400 Change a book PATCH /books/2 with some fields 200 404, 400 Delete a book DELETE /books/2 204 No Content 404 Lesson: REST: designing an API with resources Table: header · sent by · does Header Sent by Does Host Client Which site on a server with many sites Accept, Content-Type Both Which format is wanted, which format is sent Authorization Client Proves who you are, for example Bearer and a token User-Agent Client Names the program that sends the request Set-Cookie, Cookie Server, then client The server stores a small value in the browser, which sends it back on every request Cache-Control, ETag, If-None-Match Both How long a copy may be kept, its version, and \"is my version still right?\" Location Server Where a new resource is, or where to go for a redirect Access-Control-Allow-Origin Server Which other websites may call this API from a browser (CORS) Lesson: Headers, cookies and caching Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1961 Check If String Is a Prefix of Array Easy Join pieces until the text is long enough Like building a URL path from its parts. Add the words one by one and stop as soon as the joined text is as long as s. 1309 Decrypt String from Alphabet to Integer Mapping Easy Look two places ahead for # This is decoding, like reading %20 in a URL. If the character two places ahead is #, read two digits, otherwise one. 1773 Count Items Matching a Rule Easy Pick the column by its name Like a query string such as ?color=silver filtering records. Map the rule key to a column index with a dict. 2185 Counting Words With a Given Prefix Easy str.startswith Routers match URL paths by prefix in the same way. Count the words that start with the prefix. 811 Subdomain Visit Count Medium Every suffix of a host name A visit to discuss.leetcode.com also counts for leetcode.com and com. Split on dots and add the count to every ending with a Counter. 93 Restore IP Addresses Medium Try every split into four parts Each part is 0 to 255 with no leading zero, like a real IPv4 address. Try lengths 1, 2 and 3 for each of the four parts. 1268 Search Suggestions System Medium Sort once, then filter by the growing prefix This is what a search box API returns while you type. Sort the products, and for each typed prefix keep the first three that start with it. 2284 Sender With Largest Word Count Medium A Counter, then max with a tuple key Count the words each sender wrote. Pick the largest count, and on a tie the name that is largest in alphabetical order: max by (count, name). 140 Word Break II Hard Recursion with a cache of endings All the ways to cut a string into dictionary words. sentences(start) returns every sentence for s[start:], and functools.cache remembers each answer. 301 Remove Invalid Parentheses Hard Breadth first search by number of removals Check all strings with one character removed, then two, and so on. The first level that has valid strings gives the answer with the fewest removals. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps In the exam URLs may only contain a small set of characters. Others, like spaces, é or & inside a value, must be percent encoded: each byte becomes % and two hex digits, so a space is %20 and é (2 bytes in UTF-8) is %C3%A9. Use urlencode or let requests do it with params=, never glue query text together by hand.Lesson: URLs: the address of a resource Remember BaseHTTPRequestHandler answers with HTTP/1.0 unless you set protocol_version ="],[52,"py-http","HTTP and REST: how the web talks","\"HTTP/1.1\" in the class. Every line ends with \\r\\n (carriage return and new line), and an empty line separates the headers from the body.Lesson: Inside an HTTP message In the exam urllib.request.urlopen raises HTTPError for 4xx and 5xx answers. The requests library in the next chapters does not: it returns the response, and you call raise_for_status() when you want an exception.Lesson: REST: designing an API with resources Common mistake HTTP itself has no memory between requests. Logins work because the server gives the browser a session cookie or a token, and the client sends it back every time. Mark session cookies HttpOnly, Secure and SameSite, as in the example.Lesson: Headers, cookies and caching 1.2"],[52,"py-sockets","Sockets, TCP and UDP: under HTTP","Open chapter TCP and UDP sockets, bytes and struct, message framing, many clients with asyncio, IP addresses and subnets. Key ideas Sockets socket(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM) for TCP, SOCK_DGRAM for UDP. An address is (host, port). Ports 0 to 65,535. 127.0.0.1 local only, 0.0.0.0 every network card. TCP calls Server: bind, listen, accept (new socket per client). Client: create_connection(addr, timeout). sendall, recv(n) gives 1 to n bytes, b\"\" means closed. Framing TCP keeps no message borders. Length prefix with struct.pack(\"!I\", n), or an end marker such as a new line. Loop until a message is complete. UDP sendto and recvfrom, no handshake, borders kept, may lose or reorder. Used for DNS, games, video. 8 byte header. Numbers MSS = MTU − 40. throughput ≤ window / RTT. window = bandwidth × RTT. /n network has 232 − n addresses. Formulas Addresses in a networkExam favourite addresses = 232 − prefix, usable = addresses − 2 /24: 256 addresses, 254 usable. /20: 4,096 addresses. Lesson: The maths: windows, round trips and loss Data in one packet MSS = MTU − IP header − TCP header 1,500 − 20 − 20 = 1,460 bytes of your data. Lesson: The maths: windows, round trips and loss Window limit on speedExam favourite throughput ≤ window / RTT A 64 KB window with RTT 100 ms: 65,535 × 8 / 0.1 ≈ 5.2 Mbit/s, however fast the line is. Lesson: The maths: windows, round trips and loss Window needed window = bandwidth × RTT (the bandwidth delay product) 100 Mbit/s and 50 ms: 100,000,000 × 0.05 / 8 = 625,000 bytes. Lesson: The maths: windows, round trips and loss TCP with loss (Mathis formula)Exam favourite throughput ≈ (MSS / RTT) × 1.22 / √p MSS 1,460 B, RTT 100 ms, loss p = 0.0001: about 14.2 Mbit/s. Lesson: The maths: windows, round trips and loss Chance of at least one loss P = 1 − (1 − p)n 100 packets with p = 0.01: 1 − 0.99100 ≈ 0.634. Lesson: The maths: windows, round trips and loss Tables Table: · tcp socket · udp socket · unix domain socket TCP socket UDP socket Unix domain socket QUIC Setup Three way handshake None None, a file path on one computer One round trip, with TLS built in Delivery Every byte, in order, no duplicates May lose, repeat or reorder packets Reliable Reliable, per stream Message borders No: a stream of bytes Yes: one datagram is one message Stream or datagram, you choose Streams inside one connection Good for HTTP/1 and HTTP/2, databases, SSH, email DNS, video calls, games, metrics Programs on the same computer, such as Docker or PostgreSQL HTTP/3 In Python socket.SOCK_STREAM, asyncio streams socket.SOCK_DGRAM socket.AF_UNIX aioquic library Lesson: What a socket is Table: struct code · c type · bytes · range struct code C type Bytes Range B unsigned char 1 0 to 255 H unsigned short 2 0 to 65,535 (a port number fits here) I unsigned int 4 0 to about 4.29 billion (an IPv4 address fits here) Q unsigned long long 8 0 to about 1.8 × 1019 h, i, q signed versions 2, 4, 8 Negative numbers too ! byte order mark Network order: the most important byte first (big endian), no padding Lesson: Bytes: what really travels Table: error · when · what to do Error When What to do ConnectionRefusedError Nothing listens on that port Check the host and port, is the server running TimeoutError (socket.timeout) No answer within the timeout Retry later or give up with a clear message ConnectionResetError The other side crashed or closed hard Reconnect, and send again only if that is safe OSError: Address already in use Another program, or your old one, has the port Pick another port, or wait. SO_REUSEADDR helps after a restart socket.gaierror The host name could not be found (DNS) Check the spelling and the network Lesson: A TCP client and server Table: framing method · how the receiver finds the end · used by Framing method How the receiver finds the end Used by Length prefix Read a fixed size length first, then exactly that many bytes PostgreSQL, Kafka, gRPC, WebSocket frames End marker Read until a special byte sequence, such as a new line SMTP"],[52,"py-sockets","Sockets, TCP and UDP: under HTTP","email, Redis, HTTP headers (end with an empty line) Fixed size Every message has the same size Simple sensor data, some games Close the connection The message ends when the sender closes HTTP/1.0 without Content-Length Lesson: TCP is a stream: framing messages Table: · tcp · udp TCP UDP Connection connect and accept first None, just sendto an address Lost packets Sent again automatically Gone, the program must notice Order Always the order they were sent Can arrive in any order Message borders Not kept Kept: one sendto is one recvfrom Header size 20 to 60 bytes 8 bytes Speed of the first message After one round trip At once Python calls connect, sendall, recv sendto, recvfrom Lesson: UDP: single packets, no promises Table: · one at a time · a thread per client · selectors One at a time A thread per client selectors asyncio Idea accept, serve, then accept the next Each accepted socket gets its own thread One loop asks the OS which sockets are ready Coroutines that wait with await, on one event loop Many slow clients Everyone else waits Fine up to a few thousand Fine, tens of thousands Fine, tens of thousands Code Very simple Simple, but shared data needs locks Hard to write by hand Looks like normal code with async and await In Python socket socketserver.ThreadingTCPServer selectors.DefaultSelector asyncio.start_server Lesson: Serving many clients at once Table: layer · job · example · in python Layer Job Example In Python Application What the program means HTTP, DNS, SMTP, your own protocol requests, http.server, your code Transport Which program, and how reliable TCP, UDP (ports) socket.SOCK_STREAM, socket.SOCK_DGRAM Internet Which computer, and the route there IPv4, IPv6 (IP addresses) socket.AF_INET, socket.AF_INET6, ipaddress Link The next hop on the local network Ethernet, Wi-Fi (MAC addresses) The operating system does it Lesson: IP addresses, ports and layers Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 190 Reverse Bits Easy Take the last bit, push it in from the right Byte order and bit order matter on the wire. Take the lowest bit of n 32 times and add it to the result from the other end. 1971 Find if Path Exists in Graph Easy Breadth first search from the source Can a packet get from one computer to another? Keep a set of visited computers and a queue of computers to visit. 2073 Time Needed to Buy Tickets Easy Count what each person buys before k is done A queue served in turns, like a server that sends one piece to each client in turn. People before k buy at most tickets[k], people after k at most tickets[k] minus 1. 743 Network Delay Time Medium Dijkstra with a heap A signal spreads from computer k over links with delays. Dijkstra finds the fastest time to each computer, and the answer is the slowest of those. 1319 Number of Operations to Make Network Connected Medium Union find counts the groups You need at least n minus 1 cables. If there are enough, the answer is the number of separate groups minus 1. 1514 Path with Maximum Probability Medium Dijkstra on the largest product Each link delivers a packet with some probability. Use a heap of negative probabilities so the most likely path comes out first. 787 Cheapest Flights Within K Stops Medium Bellman Ford for k + 1 rounds Like a packet with a hop limit (TTL). Each round allows one more flight. Copy the prices first so one round uses only one more flight. 1615 Maximal Network Rank Medium Degrees of both cities, minus 1 if they are linked The rank of two cities is the number of roads touching either one. Try every pair and do not count a shared road twice. 1192 Critical Connections in a Network Hard Tarjan: discovery time and lowest reachable time A cable is critical if cutting it splits the network. In a depth first search, the link to a child is critical when the child cannot reach back above itself. 1579 Remove Max Number of Edges to Keep Graph Fully Traversable Hard Two union finds, shared edges first Use the edges both Alice and Bob"],[52,"py-sockets","Sockets, TCP and UDP: under HTTP","can use first, then each person's own edges. Every edge that joins nothing new can be removed. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps In the exam A socket only knows bytes. Turn text into bytes with .encode(\"utf-8\") before sending and back with .decode(\"utf-8\") after receiving. \"é\" is one letter but 2 bytes, so always measure the length of the bytes, never of the text.Lesson: Bytes: what really travels Common mistake Host \"127.0.0.1\" (localhost) only accepts connections from the same computer. \"0.0.0.0\" accepts them from every network card, so other computers can connect. Use 127.0.0.1 while you learn.Lesson: A TCP client and server Common mistake A very common bug: the sender calls sendall twice and the receiver expects two recv calls to return the two messages. On a fast local test it often works, and then it fails on a real network. recv only promises \"some bytes\", so always loop until you have a whole message.Lesson: TCP is a stream: framing messages Remember A UDP datagram larger than the path allows (about 1,472 bytes of data on normal Ethernet) gets split by IP, and if one piece is lost the whole datagram is lost. Keep UDP messages small.Lesson: UDP: single packets, no promises In the exam A /24 network has 232 − 24 = 256 addresses. The first is the network address and the last is the broadcast address, so 254 are left for computers. Ports go from 0 to 65,535, and ports below 1024 usually need admin rights.Lesson: IP addresses, ports and layers"],[52,"step-web-2","Step 2Web and APIs · Calling web APIs","2.1"],[52,"py-requests","Calling web APIs with requests","Open chapter requests: GET and POST, params and json, timeouts, errors, sessions, auth and JSON answers. Key ideas Calls requests.get, post, put, patch, delete(url, params=, json=, data=, headers=, timeout=). Response: status_code, ok, headers, text, content, json(). Errors raise_for_status() for 4xx and 5xx. HTTPError, ConnectionError, Timeout, JSONDecodeError, all under RequestException (catch it last). Timeouts No default timeout. timeout=5 or timeout=(connect, read). Always pass one. Session Reuses connections, keeps cookies and headers. Auth: Authorization: Bearer token, auth=(user, pw), API key header. Secrets come from environment variables. JSON object to dict, array to list, null to None. [] for required keys, .get() for optional. datetime.fromisoformat for dates. Formulas Pages to fetchExam favourite pages = ⌈items / page size⌉ 95 items, 20 per page: ⌈4.75⌉ = 5 requests. Lesson: The maths: round trips, timeouts and limits Time without a Session n × (handshake RTTs + 1) × RTT 10 calls, 2 handshake RTTs, RTT 50 ms: 10 × 3 × 50 = 1,500 ms. Lesson: The maths: round trips, timeouts and limits Time with a SessionExam favourite (handshake RTTs + n) × RTT (2 + 10) × 50 = 600 ms, so 900 ms saved. Lesson: The maths: round trips, timeouts and limits Longest wait with timeout=(c, r) up to c + r for each silent stretch timeout=(3, 2): up to 3 s to connect, then 2 s with no data before ReadTimeout. Lesson: The maths: round trips, timeouts and limits Rate limit minimum time = requests / allowed rate 300 calls at 60 per minute: 5 minutes. Lesson: The maths: round trips, timeouts and limits Chance one of k tries worksExam favourite P = 1 − (1 − p)k p = 0.9, k = 3: 1 − 0.001 = 0.999. Lesson: The maths: round trips, timeouts and limits Tables Table: · urllib.request · requests · httpx urllib.request requests httpx aiohttp Comes with Python Yes No, pip install requests No, pip install httpx No, pip install aiohttp A JSON call Encode, build a Request, read, json.loads requests.get(url).json() httpx.get(url).json() Inside async with blocks 4xx and 5xx Raises HTTPError Returns the response, raise_for_status() raises Returns, raise_for_status() raises Returns, raise_for_status() raises async and HTTP/2 No No Both async only Best for Scripts that must not install anything Most scripts and apps The same as requests, plus async code Many calls at once in async servers Lesson: What a web API is, and why requests Table: on the response · gives · example On the Response Gives Example r.status_code, r.reason The status number and its words 200 and OK r.ok True when the status is below 400 False for 404 r.headers A dict of headers that ignores upper and lower case r.headers[\"content-type\"] r.text The body as a str, decoded with r.encoding '[{\"id\": 3 ...' r.content The body as raw bytes, for images and files b'\\x89PNG...' r.json() The body parsed from JSON into Python a list or a dict r.url, r.request The final URL, and the request that was really sent r.request.headers Lesson: Your first GET request Table: argument · sends · content-type it sets · use it for Argument Sends Content-Type it sets Use it for params=dict The dict in the URL query string None Search words, filters, page numbers json=obj The object as JSON text in the body application/json Almost every modern API data=dict The dict as a web form in the body application/x-www-form-urlencoded HTML forms, OAuth token requests files=dict Files as multipart form data multipart/form-data Uploading pictures and documents headers=dict Extra headers Whatever you set API keys, Accept, a custom User-Agent Lesson: Sending data: POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE Table: exception · means · parent Exception Means Parent requests.HTTPError raise_for_status() found a 4xx or 5xx status RequestException requests.ConnectionError No connection: the server is down, the name is wrong, no network RequestException requests.Timeout (ConnectTimeout, ReadTimeout) The connect or a read took longer than timeout"],[52,"py-requests","Calling web APIs with requests","RequestException requests.TooManyRedirects Redirects went round in a circle RequestException requests.JSONDecodeError r.json() on a body that is not JSON RequestException and ValueError requests.RequestException The parent of all of them OSError Lesson: When a call fails Table: way to log in · how requests sends it · notes Way to log in How requests sends it Notes API key in a header headers={\"X-API-Key\": key} Simple. Keep the key out of your code, read it from an environment variable Bearer token headers={\"Authorization\": f\"Bearer {token}\"} Most modern APIs, often an OAuth 2 access token Basic auth auth=(\"user\", \"password\") Name and password in base64, only safe over HTTPS Session cookie A Session keeps Set-Cookie and sends it back Websites that have a login form OAuth 2 Get a token first, then send it as Bearer Libraries such as requests-oauthlib do the steps for you Lesson: Sessions, cookies and authentication Table: json · python after json.loads · watch out JSON Python after json.loads Watch out object {...} dict Keys are always str array [...] list string str Dates stay strings, parse them with datetime.fromisoformat number int or float 1 becomes int, 1.0 becomes float true, false True, False null None A missing key and a null value are different. dict.get covers both Lesson: Working with JSON answers Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 2418 Sort the People Easy Sort pairs by one field Like sorting API records by a field. Zip names with heights and sort by height, largest first. 1854 Maximum Population Year Easy Count people alive in each year Records with a start and an end, like user sessions in logs. Years are only 1950 to 2050, so count each year directly. 1700 Number of Students Unable to Eat Lunch Easy Only the counts matter A queue of requests waiting for a matching resource. Count how many want each kind, then serve the stack until no one wants the top item. 2456 Most Popular Video Creator Medium Group records by creator Typical API analytics: total views per creator, and each creator's best video (most views, then the smallest id). 1366 Rank Teams by Votes Medium Sort by a tuple of vote counts Count how often each team gets each place. Sort by the counts, most first, and by the letter on a tie. 355 Design Twitter Medium Dicts of lists and sets, heapq.nlargest A small social API. Store each user's tweets with a counter as the time. The feed is the 10 newest tweets of the user and the people they follow. 1348 Tweet Counts Per Frequency Medium Put each time into a bucket API dashboards count events per minute, hour or day. The bucket of a time t is (t − start) // size. 1606 Find Servers That Handled Most Number of Requests Hard Two heaps: busy servers and free servers A load balancer. Request i prefers server i % k, or the next free one after it. Store a free server j as i + (j − i) % k so the smallest number in the heap is the right server. 2102 Sequentially Ordinal Rank Tracker Hard Two heaps split at the i-th best Keep the places already returned in one heap with the worst on top, and the rest in another with the best on top. get() moves one place across. 352 Data Stream as Disjoint Intervals Hard A set of numbers, joined into runs when asked Like tracking which parts of a file have arrived. Keep the numbers in a set, and on getIntervals walk them in order and join neighbours into ranges. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps Common mistake Always pass timeout=. requests has no timeout by default, so a server that never answers keeps your program waiting for ever. A number such as timeout=5 is used for connecting and again for each wait while reading.Lesson: Your first GET request Remember For a big download use requests.get(url, stream=True, timeout=10) and write for chunk in r.iter_content(64_000) into a file opened with \"wb\". Then the whole file never has to fit in memory.Lesson: Sending data: POST, PUT, PATCH and DELETE In the exam A Session keeps cookies and default"],[52,"py-requests","Calling web APIs with requests","headers, and it reuses the TCP (and TLS) connection for every request to the same host. The example shows 3 requests over 1 connection. That saves one or two round trips each time, so use a Session whenever you make more than a couple of calls.Lesson: Sessions, cookies and authentication Common mistake Never put a real token or password in code that you share or commit to git. Read it with os.environ[\"API_TOKEN\"] and keep it in a .env file that git ignores.Lesson: Sessions, cookies and authentication Remember Use data[\"key\"] when the key must be there (a missing key is a bug you want to see), and data.get(\"key\", default) when it is optional. The next chapter shows pydantic, which checks the whole shape of an answer at once.Lesson: Working with JSON answers 2.2"],[52,"py-api-data","Real API work: pages, retries, limits and validation","Open chapter Pagination, retries with backoff and jitter, rate limits, pydantic validation and async calls with httpx. Key ideas Pages Loop with a generator until no next page or cursor. Cursors stay correct when data changes. pages = ⌈items / size⌉. Retries Only timeouts, connection errors, 429, 502, 503, 504, and only idempotent requests. wait = random(0, min(cap, base × 2n)). Stop after k tries. Limits 429 plus Retry-After (seconds or a date). Token bucket: capacity for bursts, rate for the average. X-RateLimit-Remaining. pydantic class M(BaseModel), Field(min_length=, ge=, le=), M.model_validate(dict), model_dump(), TypeAdapter(list[M]).validate_json(text), ValidationError.errors(). httpx httpx.Client like a Session, httpx.AsyncClient with await, asyncio.gather for many, asyncio.Semaphore(n) to limit them. Formulas Backoff waitExam favourite waitn = min(cap, base × 2n) base 0.5 s: 0.5, 1, 2, 4, 8 s for n = 0 to 4. Lesson: The maths: backoff, limits and pages Total backoff time base × (2k − 1) for k waits base 1 s, 4 waits: 1 + 2 + 4 + 8 = 15 s. Lesson: The maths: backoff, limits and pages Full jitter wait = random(0, base × 2n), mean = base × 2n / 2 n = 3, base 1 s: random between 0 and 8 s, 4 s on average. Lesson: The maths: backoff, limits and pages Token bucket in time TExam favourite at most capacity + rate × T calls rate 5/s, capacity 20, 10 s: 20 + 50 = 70 calls. Lesson: The maths: backoff, limits and pages Rows skipped by an offset skipped = (page − 1) × size page 50, size 20: 980 rows read and thrown away. Lesson: The maths: backoff, limits and pages Calls in flight (Little's law)Exam favourite L = rate × time per call 100 calls/s at 0.25 s each: 25 at the same moment. Lesson: The maths: backoff, limits and pages Tables Table: · no retry · fixed wait · exponential backoff No retry Fixed wait Exponential backoff Backoff with jitter Waits None 1, 1, 1 s 1, 2, 4, 8 s random(0, 1), random(0, 2), random(0, 4) s A short outage Fails Recovers Recovers Recovers Many clients at once Fail together Retry together, keep the server down Less load, still together Spread out, the server recovers Use it for Requests that must not repeat Rarely a good choice A single client Almost everything Lesson: What changes when an API is real Table: style · the request · good · weak Style The request Good Weak Page number ?page=3&size=50 Simple, can jump to any page Items shift when data changes, so you can see one twice or miss one Offset and limit ?offset=100&limit=50 The same as pages, in rows Slow for big offsets, the database still skips every row Cursor ?after=item3 or ?cursor=eyJpZCI6... Stable while data changes, fast Cannot jump to page 40 Link header Link: <...?page=4>; rel=\"next\" The server gives the next URL You must read the header (requests: r.links[\"next\"][\"url\"]) Lesson: Pagination: getting every page Table: retry? · status or error · why Retry? Status or error Why Yes ConnectionError, Timeout The network or the server had a short problem Yes 429 Too Many Requests Wait as long as Retry-After says, then try again Yes 502, 503, 504, sometimes 500 A server or a gateway is busy or restarting No 400, 401, 403, 404, 422 The request itself is wrong, it will fail the same way again Careful POST and PATCH Not idempotent: the first try may have worked. Retry only with an idempotency key Lesson: Retries with backoff, jitter and Retry-After Table: header · meaning Header Meaning Retry-After: 30 Wait 30 seconds before the next call (it can also be a date) X-RateLimit-Limit: 5000 How many calls you may make in the current window X-RateLimit-Remaining: 12 How many are left X-RateLimit-Reset: 1793000000 When the window starts again, often as a Unix time RateLimit-Policy, RateLimit Newer standard names for the same ideas (an IETF draft) Lesson: Rate limits: the token bucket Table: · trust it · checks by hand · dataclass Trust it Checks by hand dataclass pydantic Wrong type, such as \"seven\" for an int Crashes later, far away Found if you wrote"],[52,"py-api-data","Real API work: pages, retries, limits and validation","the check Not found Found at once with a clear message \"7\" for an int Stays a str Your choice Stays a str Turned into 7 (lax mode) Missing field KeyError somewhere Found if you wrote the check TypeError when you build it Listed with its path, such as author.name Nested objects and lists Plain dicts Much code By hand Built in Cost None Many lines Few lines One class, a fast core written in Rust Lesson: Checking the data with pydantic Table: need · tool Need Tool A few calls in a script requests or httpx.Client Many calls at once in a script httpx.AsyncClient with asyncio.gather and a Semaphore, or a ThreadPoolExecutor with requests Calls inside an async web app (FastAPI) httpx.AsyncClient, never requests, which would block the event loop HTTP/2 httpx.Client(http2=True) after pip install httpx[http2] Lesson: Many calls at once with httpx Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 21 Merge Two Sorted Lists Easy Walk both lists, always take the smaller head Like merging two sorted pages of results from two APIs. A dummy first node keeps the code short. 350 Intersection of Two Arrays II Easy Counter and the & operator Which ids do two API answers share, counting repeats? Counter(a) & Counter(b) keeps the smaller count of each value. 1450 Number of Students Doing Homework at a Given Time Easy Check each time range Like counting the sessions that were active at a given moment in API logs. A range counts when start <= time <= end. 703 Kth Largest Element in a Stream Easy A min heap of size k Keep only the k largest values seen so far in a heap. Its smallest value is the k-th largest, like the slowest of the top k response times. 57 Insert Interval Medium Before, overlapping, after Add a time range to a sorted list of ranges. Copy the ranges that end before it, merge the ones that overlap, then copy the rest. 729 My Calendar I Medium Two ranges overlap when each starts before the other ends A booking API must refuse a slot that overlaps an existing one. [s1, e1) and [s2, e2) overlap when s1 < e2 and s2 < e1. 731 My Calendar II Medium Keep the bookings and the double booked parts Now two bookings may overlap but never three. Keep a list of the parts that are already double booked. A new booking that touches one of them must be refused. 435 Non-overlapping Intervals Medium Greedy: keep the range that ends first Sort by end time. Keep a range when it starts after the last kept one ends, otherwise remove it. Ending early leaves the most room. 480 Sliding Window Median Hard Keep the window sorted with bisect Monitoring shows the median response time of the last k calls. Keep the window sorted: insort the new value, remove the old one, and read the middle. 732 My Calendar III Hard Sweep line: +1 at a start, −1 at an end How many bookings overlap at the busiest moment? Add +1 at each start and −1 at each end, then walk the times in order and keep a running sum. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps Remember Write the loop as a generator with yield from. The caller sees one long stream of items and can stop early, and pages are only fetched when they are needed.Lesson: Pagination: getting every page In the exam A token bucket holds up to capacity tokens and refills rate tokens every second. Each call takes one token, and with no token the call must wait or gets 429. So it allows short bursts but keeps the average at the rate. Servers use it to protect themselves, and clients can use it to stay polite.Lesson: Rate limits: the token bucket Remember By default pydantic ignores fields it does not know (like \"extra\" above). Set model_config = ConfigDict(extra=\"forbid\") to reject them, or strict=True to refuse \"7\" for an int.Lesson: Checking the data with pydantic"],[52,"step-web-3","Step 3Web and APIs · Flask: small and flexible web apps","3.1"],[52,"py-flask","Flask basics: routes, templates, forms and JSON","Open chapter Flask routes, request and response, Jinja templates, forms and sessions, a JSON API and running the app. Key ideas Routes @app.route(rule, methods=[...]) or @app.get / post. Converters string, int, float, path, uuid. url_for(endpoint, **values). 404 no match, 405 wrong method. Request request.args.get(name, default, type=int), request.form, request.get_json(silent=True), request.files, headers, cookies. Responses str is HTML, dict or list is JSON, (body, status, headers), redirect(url_for(...)), abort(code), make_response, @app.errorhandler(404). Templates and sessions render_template, {{ }} escaped, {% for %}, filters, extends and block. session is a signed cookie, needs secret_key. flash and get_flashed_messages. Run and test app.test_client() for tests. flask --app app run --debug only on your computer. gunicorn -w 4 \"app:app\" in production. Formulas Requests in progress (Little's law)Exam favourite L = λ × W 120 requests/s, 0.2 s each: 24 requests at the same moment, so at least 24 workers or threads. Lesson: The maths: workers, time and limits Gunicorn starting point workers = 2 × CPU cores + 1 4 cores: 9 worker processes. Lesson: The maths: workers, time and limits Throughput of sync workersExam favourite requests/s ≤ workers / time per request 9 workers, 50 ms each: 9 / 0.05 = 180 requests/s. Lesson: The maths: workers, time and limits Python time limit with threads (GIL) requests/s ≤ 1 / CPU time per request, per process 10 ms of Python per request: at most 100 requests/s for one process, however many threads. Lesson: The maths: workers, time and limits Cookie size base64 makes data 4 / 3 as big A 4,096 byte cookie holds about 3,072 bytes of session data. Lesson: The maths: workers, time and limits Error rate rate = errors / requests × 100 % 30 errors out of 600 requests: 5 %. Lesson: The maths: workers, time and limits Tables Table: · http.server · flask · django http.server Flask Django FastAPI Size Standard library, very basic Small core, add what you need Batteries included: ORM, admin, auth, forms Small core focused on APIs Routing Write if statements on self.path @app.route(\"/books/<int:id>\") urls.py with path(\"books/<int:id>/\", view) @app.get(\"/books/{id}\") Data checks By hand By hand or an extension Forms and serializers Automatic from type hints (pydantic) Interface Its own WSGI (sync), async views possible WSGI and ASGI ASGI (async) Good for Learning, tiny tools Small and medium sites, APIs, prototypes Big sites with users, admin and a database Fast JSON APIs with automatic docs Lesson: What Flask is Table: converter · matches · gives the view Converter Matches Gives the view <name> or <string:name> Any text without a slash str <int:id> Whole numbers such as 42 int <float:price> Numbers such as 9.5 float <path:rest> Text that may contain slashes str <uuid:key> A UUID uuid.UUID Lesson: Routes and URLs Table: on request · holds · example On request Holds Example request.args The query string, like a dict request.args.get(\"page\", 1, type=int) request.form Fields of an HTML form request.form[\"user\"] request.get_json() The JSON body silent=True gives None instead of an error request.files Uploaded files request.files[\"photo\"].save(path) request.headers, cookies Headers and cookies request.headers[\"User-Agent\"] request.method, path, url The method and the address \"POST\", \"/login\" Lesson: The request and the response Table: a view returns · flask sends A view returns Flask sends A str 200 with Content-Type text/html A dict or a list 200 with JSON (keys sorted by default) (body, status) or (body, status, headers) Your status and extra headers redirect(url) 302 with a Location header abort(code) Stops at once with that error status make_response(...) A Response object you can change: headers, cookies Lesson: The request and the response Table: jinja · does · example Jinja Does Example {{ value }} Prints a value, HTML escaped {{ user }} {% for ... %} ... {% endfor %} A loop, with loop.index and an else"],[52,"py-flask","Flask basics: routes, templates, forms and JSON","part for empty lists for book in books {% if ... %} A condition if book.year < 1900 value|filter Changes a value upper, length, default(\"none\"), round(2) {% extends %} and {% block %} One base page, other pages fill its blocks extends \"base.html\" {% include %}, macros Reuse a part of a page include \"nav.html\" Lesson: Templates with Jinja Table: request · view · success · errors Request View Success Errors GET /api/books list_books 200 and a JSON list GET /api/books/7 get_book 200 and the book 404 with a JSON error POST /api/books create_book 201, the book and Location 422 for bad data DELETE /api/books/1 delete_book 204 with an empty body 404 Lesson: A JSON API in Flask Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 434 Number of Segments in a String Easy str.split() with no argument Like counting the words a user typed into a search box. split() with no argument skips any amount of spaces, so empty parts never appear. 1662 Check If Two String Arrays are Equivalent Easy Join and compare A page built from template parts must give the same text however it was cut. Join both lists and compare the results. 1544 Make The String Great Easy A stack of letters Cleaning user input with a stack. Push each letter, but pop instead when it is the same letter in the other case as the top. 2696 Minimum String Length After Removing Substrings Easy A stack removes pairs as they appear Removing \"AB\" and \"CD\" again and again is the same as a stack that pops when the new letter completes a pair with the top. 648 Replace Words Medium Check the shortest roots first Like a router that finds the shortest registered prefix of a path. Put the roots in a set and try prefixes from short to long. 1209 Remove All Adjacent Duplicates in String II Medium A stack of [letter, count] Keep each letter with how many times it repeats on top of the stack. When the count reaches k, pop the whole group. 820 Short Encoding of Words Medium Drop words that are endings of other words A word that is the end of another word needs no space of its own. Remove every ending of every word from a set, then add len(word) + 1 for each word that is left. 916 Word Subsets Medium Merge the needs into one Counter A word is universal when it has every letter of every word in words2, as often as needed. Merge words2 into one Counter of the largest counts, then check each word once. 30 Substring with Concatenation of All Words Hard Cut each window into words and compare Counters Find every place where all the words appear joined in any order, like spotting a set of template parts in a page. Each window has a fixed length, so cut it into word sized pieces and compare counts. 224 Basic Calculator Hard A stack of (result, sign) for each bracket Templates and config files evaluate small expressions. Walk the text, build numbers digit by digit, and push the result so far when a bracket opens. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps Remember Build links with url_for(\"hello\", name=\"Bob\") and never by hand. If you rename a URL later, every link still works, and url_for escapes special characters for you. Extra arguments become the query string, as with page=3 above.Lesson: Routes and URLs In the exam A route answers only GET (and HEAD, OPTIONS) unless you pass methods=. A wrong method gives 405 Method Not Allowed, a URL that matches no rule gives 404 Not Found.Lesson: Routes and URLs Remember request looks like a global variable, but each request in each thread sees its own. Flask does this with context variables, so two users at the same moment never see each other's data.Lesson: The request and the response Common mistake Jinja escapes every value by default in .html templates, so a name such as <script> is shown as text and never runs. This stops cross site scripting (XSS). Only mark text as safe with |safe when you made it yourself.Lesson: Templates with Jinja Common mistake The session cookie is signed, NOT encrypted: the example decodes it and reads the user name."],[52,"py-flask","Flask basics: routes, templates, forms and JSON","Never put secrets in it, keep secret_key secret and long, and read it from an environment variable.Lesson: Forms, sessions and flash messages In the exam Real forms also need protection against cross site request forgery (CSRF), so another website cannot post forms as your user. The extension Flask-WTF adds a hidden token to every form and checks it.Lesson: Forms, sessions and flash messages Remember @app.get, @app.post, @app.put, @app.patch and @app.delete are short forms of @app.route with one method. They make an API easier to read.Lesson: A JSON API in Flask Common mistake A global dict like BOOKS is fine for learning, but it is lost when the server restarts, and each Gunicorn worker process has its own copy. Real apps keep data in a database, as the next chapter shows.Lesson: A JSON API in Flask 3.2"],[52,"py-flask-apps","Real Flask apps: structure, database, login and tests","Open chapter Application factory, blueprints, sqlite3, login with hashed passwords, pytest, config and deployment. Key ideas Structure create_app(settings) returns a new app. Blueprint(name, __name__, url_prefix=...), app.register_blueprint(bp). url_for(\"bp.view\"). current_app, not a global app. Database get_db() on g, sqlite3.Row, ? placeholders, commit(), app.teardown_appcontext(close_db), @app.cli.command(\"init-db\"). Auth generate_password_hash / check_password_hash (salted, slow). session.clear() then session[\"user_id\"]. before_app_request loads g.user. login_required with functools.wraps. Tests pytest fixture in conftest.py builds create_app({\"TESTING\": True}) and returns app.test_client(). app.test_cli_runner() for commands. Production Config from environment (from_prefixed_env), app.logger, errorhandler passing HTTPException through, gunicorn \"shop:create_app()\" behind HTTPS. Formulas Guesses per second against a slow hashExam favourite guesses/s = raw hashes per second / iterations 10 million SHA-256 per second, 1,000,000 PBKDF2 iterations: 10 guesses per second. Lesson: The maths: hashing cost, commits and capacity Number of passwords N = (letters in the alphabet)length 8 lower case letters: 268 ≈ 2.09 × 1011. Lesson: The maths: hashing cost, commits and capacity Average time to guessExam favourite time ≈ N / 2 / guesses per second 2.09 × 1011 / 2 / 10 per second ≈ 331 years. Lesson: The maths: hashing cost, commits and capacity Commits many commits cost n × commit time, one transaction costs one 200 inserts with a 5 ms commit each: 1,000 ms. In one transaction: about 15 ms. Lesson: The maths: hashing cost, commits and capacity Salt collisions (birthday bound) n ≈ 1.18 × √(number of salts) for a 50 % chance 32 bit salts: 1.18 × 65,536 ≈ 77,000 users. Werkzeug uses 16 random characters, far more. Lesson: The maths: hashing cost, commits and capacity Test suite time time = tests × time per test 120 tests at 15 ms: 1.8 s, fast enough to run on every save. Lesson: The maths: hashing cost, commits and capacity Tables Table: · one app.py file · factory and blueprints · django project One app.py file Factory and blueprints Django project Where code lives Everything in one file A package: __init__.py, auth.py, books.py, db.py Apps with models.py, views.py, urls.py Tests Share one global app Each test builds its own app A test runner with a test database Settings Often written in the code Defaults, then settings, then environment settings.py, often read from the environment Database and login By hand sqlite3 by hand, or Flask-SQLAlchemy and Flask-Login Built in: ORM, migrations, auth, admin Good for Demos Most Flask apps Big apps that need everything Lesson: From one file to a real app Table: file or folder · holds File or folder Holds shop/__init__.py create_app(): config, database setup, register_blueprint for each part shop/db.py get_db(), close_db(), the init-db command shop/auth.py Blueprint auth: register, login, logout, login_required shop/books.py Blueprint books: the pages of the shop shop/api.py Blueprint api with url_prefix=\"/api\": the JSON API shop/templates/, shop/static/ Jinja templates, CSS, JavaScript and images instance/ Files that belong to one installation: the SQLite file, a local config. Not in git tests/conftest.py, tests/test_*.py pytest fixtures and tests pyproject.toml The package name and its dependencies Lesson: The application factory and blueprints Table: · sqlite3 by hand · flask-sqlalchemy · django orm sqlite3 by hand Flask-SQLAlchemy Django ORM You write SQL text with ? placeholders Python classes, queries as method calls Python classes, queries as method calls Changing tables later Write ALTER TABLE yourself Flask-Migrate (Alembic) makes migration files makemigrations and migrate are built in Other databases Change the code for PostgreSQL Change one URL in the config Change the settings Good for Learning SQL, small apps Most Flask apps that grow Django apps Lesson: A database with sqlite3"],[52,"py-flask-apps","Real Flask apps: structure, database, login and tests","Table: rule · why Rule Why Store generate_password_hash(...), never the password A stolen database must not reveal passwords Use a slow hash with a salt (scrypt or PBKDF2, Werkzeug picks scrypt by default) Slow makes guessing expensive, the salt makes the same password give different hashes Say \"wrong name or password\", not which one Attackers cannot test which names exist session.clear() before storing the user No data from before the login survives (session fixation) Set SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE and SESSION_COOKIE_HTTPONLY in production The cookie only travels over HTTPS and JavaScript cannot read it Limit login attempts, for example 5 a minute per user Stops fast guessing Lesson: Users, passwords and login_required Table: kind of test · checks · tool Kind of test Checks Tool Unit test One function, such as bad_book(data) pytest Route test One URL: status code, JSON, redirects app.test_client() Command test flask init-db and other commands app.test_cli_runner() End to end test A real browser clicking through pages Playwright or Selenium (see those subjects) Coverage Which lines the tests ran coverage run -m pytest, then coverage report Lesson: Testing a Flask app Table: source · example · use it for Source Example Use it for Defaults in create_app app.config.from_mapping(PAGE_SIZE=10) Values that are safe everywhere Settings passed to create_app create_app({\"TESTING\": True}) Tests A file in instance/ app.config.from_pyfile(\"config.py\", silent=True) One installation Environment variables FLASK_SECRET_KEY=... with from_prefixed_env() Secrets and production values Lesson: Configuration, errors and logging Table: way to host · you manage · example Way to host You manage Example A platform (PaaS) Only the code and settings Render, Railway, Fly.io, Google App Engine A container A Dockerfile, the platform runs it Docker image on Cloud Run, AWS ECS, Kubernetes Your own server Everything: Linux, nginx, Gunicorn, updates A virtual machine with systemd Lesson: Deploying a Flask app Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 595 Big Countries Easy WHERE with OR A country is big when its area is at least 3,000,000 or its population at least 25,000,000. One WHERE with OR does it. 1757 Recyclable and Low Fat Products Easy WHERE with AND Both columns must be \"Y\". The kind of filter a shop page runs for every search. 584 Find Customer Referee Easy NULL needs IS NULL referee_id != 2 is not true for NULL, because NULL compared with anything is unknown. Add OR referee_id IS NULL. 1148 Article Views I Easy DISTINCT with a column compared to another Authors who viewed their own article: author_id = viewer_id. DISTINCT removes repeats. 570 Managers with at Least 5 Direct Reports Medium GROUP BY with HAVING, then join back Count the people per managerId, keep the ones with 5 or more, and join with the table again to get the names. 1934 Confirmation Rate Medium LEFT JOIN, AVG of a CASE, IFNULL Every user who signed up must appear, even with no confirmations, so use a LEFT JOIN. The rate is the average of 1 for confirmed and 0 otherwise. 180 Consecutive Numbers Medium Join the table with itself twice Pair each row with the next two ids. If all three have the same number, that number appears at least three times in a row. 550 Game Play Analysis IV Medium First day per player, then look for the next day Find each player's first login with MIN, check whether they came back the day after, and divide by the number of players. 601 Human Traffic of Stadium Hard Window function: id minus row number groups a run Keep the days with 100 or more people. Within a run of consecutive ids, id minus ROW_NUMBER() stays the same, so it names the run. Keep runs of 3 or more. 432 All O`one Data Structure Hard A dict of counts (simple version) Count page views per key and ask for the most and least viewed key. This simple version keeps a dict and uses max and min. The O(1) version keeps a linked list of buckets, one bucket for each count. Lesson:"],[52,"py-flask-apps","Real Flask apps: structure, database, login and tests","LeetCode practice Tips and traps In the exam Inside a blueprint, link with the blueprint name: url_for(\"api.status\") or url_for(\".status\") for the same blueprint. Use current_app instead of a global app variable, because with a factory there is no global app.Lesson: The application factory and blueprints Remember login_required is a normal decorator (Advanced step 2). functools.wraps keeps the view name, which Flask needs, because the endpoint name comes from the function name.Lesson: Users, passwords and login_required In the exam For bigger apps, Flask-Login manages the logged in user and \"remember me\", and Flask-WTF adds CSRF tokens to forms. They do the same jobs as the code here, with more cases covered.Lesson: Users, passwords and login_required Remember With pytest the helper becomes a fixture in tests/conftest.py, and pytest passes it to every test that has a parameter called client: import pytest from notes import create_app @pytest.fixture def client(): app = create_app({\"TESTING\": True}) return app.test_client() Then run python3 -m pytest -q. A real run of exactly this printed \"2 passed in 0.18s\".Lesson: Testing a Flask app Remember Flask turns an exception in a view into a 500 answer. An errorhandler(Exception) can log it and send a friendly message, but must pass HTTPExceptions such as 404 through unchanged, as the example does. Use app.logger, never print, so logs get levels and can go to files or a log service.Lesson: Configuration, errors and logging"],[52,"step-web-4","Step 4Web and APIs · Django: the framework with everything built in","4.1"],[52,"py-django","Django basics: project, URLs, views, templates, models and admin","Open chapter Project and apps, urlpatterns, views, templates, models with QuerySets, and the admin site. Key ideas Project django-admin startproject mysite, python manage.py startapp books, add \"books\" to INSTALLED_APPS. manage.py runserver, makemigrations, migrate, createsuperuser, shell, check --deploy. URLs and views urlpatterns = [path(\"books/<int:book_id>/\", views.book_detail, name=\"book-detail\")], include(\"books.urls\"). A view takes request and returns HttpResponse or JsonResponse. Http404, redirect, reverse. Templates render(request, \"books/list.html\", context). {{ value|filter }}, {% for %}{% empty %}{% endfor %}, {% if %}, {% url \"name\" arg %}, {% extends %} with {% block %}, {% csrf_token %} in POST forms. Values are escaped. Models class Book(models.Model) with CharField, DecimalField, ForeignKey(on_delete=...). Book.objects.create / all / filter(year__gte=1900) / get / update / delete. related_name for the other side. Admin @admin.register(Book) class BookAdmin(admin.ModelAdmin) with list_display, list_filter, search_fields. path(\"admin/\", admin.site.urls). Only staff users can log in. Formulas URL matching checks for a URL ≤ number of patterns Django tries patterns from the top: with 40 patterns the worst case is 40 checks, still well under a millisecond. Lesson: The maths: URL matching, pages, queries and hashing Number of pagesExam favourite pages = ⌈ n / per page ⌉ 1,234 books at 50 a page: ⌈24.68⌉ = 25 pages. Lesson: The maths: URL matching, pages, queries and hashing OFFSET of a pageExam favourite OFFSET = (page − 1) × per page Page 3 at 50 a page: LIMIT 50 OFFSET 100. Lesson: The maths: URL matching, pages, queries and hashing The N + 1 problemExam favourite queries = 1 + n A list of 50 books that reads book.author in the template: 1 + 50 = 51 queries. select_related makes it 1. Lesson: The maths: URL matching, pages, queries and hashing Hashing cost guesses/s = hashes per second / iterations Django 6.1 uses PBKDF2 with 1,500,000 iterations: 109 hashes per second give about 667 guesses per second. Lesson: The maths: URL matching, pages, queries and hashing Workers needed (Little's law) busy workers = requests per second × seconds per request 200 requests per second at 0.05 s each keep 10 workers busy. Lesson: The maths: URL matching, pages, queries and hashing Tables Table: · flask · django · fastapi Flask Django FastAPI Style Small core, add what you need Everything built in Built for JSON APIs, uses type hints Database Your choice (sqlite3, SQLAlchemy) The Django ORM with migrations Your choice (SQLAlchemy, SQLModel) Users and admin Extensions such as Flask-Login Built in: auth and a full admin site Write them or add libraries HTML pages Jinja templates Django templates Possible, but not its focus Good for Small apps, services, learning Shops, news sites, internal tools, big projects Fast APIs with automatic docs Used by Pinterest (early), many services Instagram, Mozilla, Disqus Many new API services Lesson: What Django is and why it has so much built in Table: file · what it is for File What it is for manage.py Runs every command: runserver, startapp, makemigrations, migrate, createsuperuser, shell, test mysite/settings.py All settings: INSTALLED_APPS, MIDDLEWARE, DATABASES, TEMPLATES, DEBUG, SECRET_KEY, ALLOWED_HOSTS mysite/urls.py The first URL list. It sends parts of the site to the apps with include() mysite/wsgi.py, asgi.py The entry point for Gunicorn (WSGI) or Uvicorn (ASGI) in production books/models.py The models of the app, one class for each table books/views.py, books/urls.py The views of the app and their URL patterns (you make urls.py yourself) books/admin.py Which models show up in the admin site, and how books/migrations/ The migration files that makemigrations writes. Keep them in git books/templates/books/ The HTML templates of the app. The second books folder stops name clashes between apps Lesson: A project and its apps Table: path converter · matches · gives the view Path converter"],[52,"py-django","Django basics: project, URLs, views, templates, models and admin","Matches Gives the view <str:name> (the default) Any text without a slash str <int:book_id> 0 or a positive whole number int <slug:slug> Letters, digits, hyphens and underscores, such as dune-messiah str <uuid:token> A UUID such as 075194d3-6885-417e-a8a8-6c931e272f00 uuid.UUID <path:file> Any text, slashes too str re_path(r\"...\") A regular expression, for special cases str for each group Lesson: URLs and views Table: · function based view · class based view Function based view Class based view Looks like def book_list(request): ... class BookList(ListView): model = Book HTTP methods if request.method == \"POST\": ... or @require_http_methods A method for each: get(), post() Reuse Helpers and decorators Inheritance and mixins, many generic views ready made Good for Clear, special logic Standard pages: lists, details, create, update, delete Lesson: URLs and views Table: template code · what it does Template code What it does {{ book.title }} Shows a value. The dot tries a dict key, then an attribute, then a list index {{ name|default:\"guest\"|title }} Filters change a value, and you can chain them {% for book in books %} ... {% empty %} ... {% endfor %} A loop, with a part for an empty list. forloop.counter counts from 1 {% if book.year < 1900 %} ... {% endif %} A condition {% url \"book-detail\" book.id %} Builds a link from the URL name, like reverse() {% extends \"base.html\" %} and {% block content %} Template inheritance: a page fills the blocks of a base layout {% csrf_token %} The hidden token that every POST form needs {# a comment #} A comment that never reaches the browser Lesson: Templates Table: · django template language · jinja (used by flask) Django template language Jinja (used by Flask) Calling functions No arguments allowed, keeps logic out of the page Full expressions such as f(x, y) Speed Fast enough for most sites Faster for heavy templates Escaping On by default On by default in Flask In Django The default Possible, as a second backend in TEMPLATES Lesson: Templates Table: field · column in sqlite · use it for Field Column in SQLite Use it for CharField(max_length=200) varchar(200) Short text such as a title TextField() text Long text such as a description IntegerField(), PositiveIntegerField() integer Whole numbers DecimalField(max_digits=6, decimal_places=2) decimal Money. Never use FloatField for money BooleanField(default=True) bool Yes or no DateField(), DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True) date, datetime Dates. auto_now_add fills in the time of creation ForeignKey(Author, on_delete=models.CASCADE) author_id plus an index Many books for one author EmailField(), URLField(), SlugField() varchar Text with a check in forms Lesson: Models: Python classes that become tables Table: modeladmin option · what it changes ModelAdmin option What it changes list_display = [\"title\", \"year\"] The columns of the list page list_filter = [\"in_stock\"] A filter box at the side search_fields = [\"title\"] A search box. ?q=dune becomes WHERE title LIKE %dune% ordering = [\"-year\"] The order of the list list_per_page = 50 How many rows on each page readonly_fields, fields, fieldsets What the edit form shows and how inlines = [ReviewInline] Edit related rows, such as reviews, on the same page Lesson: The admin site Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 257 Binary Tree Paths Easy Depth first search that carries the path so far Like listing every URL of a project whose urls.py files include each other. Walk down the tree, add each value to the path, and save the path at every leaf. 228 Summary Ranges Easy Walk once and close a range when the next number is not one more The admin and paginators show ranges such as 1-3. Keep where the current range starts and close it when the numbers stop following each other. 599 Minimum Index Sum of Two Lists Easy A dict from name to position Like joining two lists of favourites in the ORM. Store the positions of the first list in a dict, then look up each name of the second"],[52,"py-django","Django basics: project, URLs, views, templates, models and admin","list once. 1441 Build an Array With Stack Operations Medium Write down the operations, like makemigrations does makemigrations writes a list of operations that turn the old state into the new one. Here: read the numbers 1, 2, 3 ... in order, Push each one, and Pop it again when it is not in target. 1003 Check If Word Is Valid After Substitutions Medium A stack, the same way template tags must close A Django template is only valid when every {% block %} has its {% endblock %} in the right order. Here \"abc\" plays that part: push letters and remove \"abc\" whenever it appears on top. 1487 Making File Names Unique Medium A dict with the next free number for each name When two users upload photo.jpg, Django storage saves the second one under a new name. Keep the next number to try for each name, so no name is checked again and again. 1048 Longest String Chain Medium Sort by length, then dynamic programming on a dict Like a migration history where every migration adds one field. Sort the words by length and, for each word, try removing one letter to find the best chain that ends just before it. 1912 Design Movie Rental System Hard Sorted lists play the part of ORDER BY with an index This is a small ORM: one table of copies, a query \"cheapest 5 shops for a movie\" and a report \"cheapest 5 rented copies\". Keep sorted lists of (price, shop) and (price, shop, movie) so each query only reads the first 5. 736 Parse Lisp Expression Hard A recursive parser with a stack of scopes Like the context of a Django template: {% with %} adds names that live only inside its block, and inner names hide outer ones. Parse the tokens recursively and pass a new dict of names into every let. 472 Concatenated Words Hard Word break with a set and memory Like checking that a URL slug such as \"redbookshop\" is made only of known words. For each word, test every cut point and remember which endings were already solved. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps In the exam Django calls its pattern MTV: the Model holds the data, the Template shows it, and the View decides what to show. It is the same idea as MVC, only the names differ: the Django view does the job of an MVC controller.Lesson: What Django is and why it has so much built in Remember A project is the whole website with its settings. An app is one feature, such as books, accounts or payments. A good app does one thing, so you could even reuse it in another project.Lesson: A project and its apps In the exam The test client does not check CSRF tokens unless you ask it to, so the POST in the example works without one. In a real project CsrfViewMiddleware is on, and every POST form needs {% csrf_token %}. The animation above shows what happens without it.Lesson: URLs and views Remember Every value is escaped, so the script tag in the note shows up as harmless text. Only mark text as safe (the safe filter or mark_safe) when you made it yourself, never for text a user typed.Lesson: Templates In the exam In a project you never call schema_editor yourself. You change models.py, run makemigrations to write a migration file, and migrate to run it. Chapter 4.2 goes much deeper into the ORM and migrations.Lesson: Models: Python classes that become tables Remember The admin is for trusted staff, not for customers. Use a long random password for the superuser, keep DEBUG off in production, and consider a less obvious address than /admin/.Lesson: The admin site 4.2"],[52,"py-django-orm","Django in depth: ORM, migrations, forms, users and tests","Open chapter Q, F, aggregate, annotate, the N + 1 problem, migrations, forms, users and permissions, transactions and TestCase. Key ideas QuerySets Lazy until read. filter, exclude, order_by, get, first, exists, count. Lookups: __gte, __icontains, __in, author__name. Q(a) | Q(b), ~Q(c). F(\"col\") inside filter and update. Aggregation and speed aggregate(Avg(\"price\")) gives a dict, annotate(n=Count(\"books\")) adds n per row. select_related for ForeignKey, prefetch_related for ManyToMany. only, values_list, bulk_create, indexes. Migrations makemigrations then migrate. Commit the files. New fields need a default or null=True. Data migrations use RunPython with apps.get_model. migrate app 0001 goes back. sqlmigrate shows the SQL. Forms form = BookForm(request.POST), form.is_valid(), cleaned_data, form.save(). clean_<field> for one field, clean() with add_error for several. ModelForm with Meta.model and Meta.fields. Users, transactions, tests create_user, check_password, authenticate, login_required, permission_required, Group. transaction.atomic() all or nothing. TestCase rolls back each test, setUpTestData, self.client, assertNumQueries. Formulas Queries in a loopExam favourite N + 1 without help, 1 with select_related, 2 with prefetch_related 20 books: 21, 1 and 2 queries. Lesson: The maths: query counts, indexes and transactions Time of a pageExam favourite time ≈ queries × round trip + work 21 queries at 1.5 ms: 31.5 ms. 1 query: 1.5 ms plus a bigger row. Lesson: The maths: query counts, indexes and transactions Search without an index rows read = n 1,000,000 books: the database reads all of them. Lesson: The maths: query counts, indexes and transactions Search with a B-tree indexExam favourite steps ≈ logb(n) With about 100 keys per page: log100(1,000,000) = 3 page reads. Lesson: The maths: query counts, indexes and transactions Rows from a join table (many to many) pairs = books × average tags 20 books with 2 tags each: 40 rows in book_tags. Lesson: The maths: query counts, indexes and transactions Weighted average (LeetCode 1251) average = Σ(price × units) / Σunits (5 × 100 + 20 × 15) / 115 = 800 / 115 ≈ 6.96. Lesson: The maths: query counts, indexes and transactions Tables Table: · raw sql (sqlite3) · django orm · sqlalchemy Raw SQL (sqlite3) Django ORM SQLAlchemy You write SQL text with ? placeholders Book.objects.filter(...) select(Book).where(...) or the ORM Changes to tables ALTER TABLE by hand makemigrations and migrate Alembic Joins Written by hand author__name follows the ForeignKey join() or relationships Hard reports Full SQL power annotate, Subquery, Window, or raw() when needed Very close to SQL Used with Small scripts Django Flask, FastAPI, scripts Lesson: The ORM: asking the database in Python Table: tool · example · sql it becomes Tool Example SQL it becomes filter and exclude filter(year__gte=1900).exclude(stock=0) WHERE year >= 1900 AND NOT stock = 0 Lookups title__icontains=\"dune\", year__in=[1965, 1969], author__country=\"UK\" LIKE, IN, a JOIN to author Q objects Q(a) | Q(b), ~Q(c) OR and NOT F expressions filter(price__gt=F(\"stock\") * 2), update(stock=F(\"stock\") + 10) Compare or change columns inside the database aggregate aggregate(Avg(\"price\")) One answer for the whole QuerySet: a dict annotate annotate(n=Count(\"books\")) One extra value per row, with GROUP BY values, values_list values(\"title\", \"year\"), values_list(\"title\", flat=True) Only these columns, as dicts or tuples Relations ForeignKey, ManyToManyField, OneToOneField author_id column, a join table, a unique author_id Lesson: QuerySets: filter, Q, F, aggregate and annotate Table: · select_related(\"author\") · prefetch_related(\"tags\") select_related(\"author\") prefetch_related(\"tags\") Works for ForeignKey and OneToOne (one related row) ManyToMany and the reverse of a ForeignKey (many rows) How One query with a JOIN One extra query with WHERE id IN (...), joined in Python Queries for 20 books 1 2 Watch out Joining many tables makes wide rows Filtering"],[52,"py-django-orm","Django in depth: ORM, migrations, forms, users and tests","inside needs a Prefetch object Lesson: Fast queries: the N + 1 problem Table: more ways to make queries cheaper · why More ways to make queries cheaper Why only(\"title\") or defer(\"description\") Read fewer columns values_list(\"title\", flat=True) No model objects at all, only the values exists() instead of len(qs) > 0 The database stops at the first row count() instead of len(qs) SELECT COUNT(*), not every row bulk_create, bulk_update Many rows in one query db_index=True or Meta.indexes An index makes filters and order_by on that column fast iterator() Streams a huge QuerySet without keeping it all in memory Lesson: Fast queries: the N + 1 problem Table: command · what it does Command What it does makemigrations Compares the models with the migration files and writes a new file with the changes makemigrations --empty --name fill_slugs An empty migration for your own code, such as a data migration migrate Runs every migration that is not applied yet, in dependency order migrate books 0001 Goes back: undoes every later migration of books sqlmigrate books 0002 Shows the SQL of a migration without running it showmigrations Lists the migrations with [X] for applied makemigrations --check Fails when a model change has no migration yet, useful in CI Lesson: Migrations: changing tables safely Table: step · what runs · where your code goes Step What runs Where your code goes 1. Bind form = BookForm(request.POST) In the view 2. Field checks to_python, required, max_length, min_value, unique (ModelForm) Field options 3. One field clean_isbn() after the field checks pass def clean_<field>(self): return the value 4. Many fields clean() with all cleaned values def clean(self): use self.add_error 5. Result is_valid() is True: cleaned_data, or save() for a ModelForm Redirect after saving 6. Errors form.errors, shown next to each field in the template Render the form again Lesson: Forms: checking what users send Table: · forms.form · forms.modelform forms.Form forms.ModelForm Fields come from You list them The model (Meta.model, Meta.fields) Saves to the database No, you do it form.save() makes or updates the row Model rules Not used max_length, unique and choices are checked for you Good for Search, contact, login Create and edit pages for a model Lesson: Forms: checking what users send Table: part · what django gives you Part What Django gives you User model username, email, password (a hash), is_active, is_staff, is_superuser, groups, user_permissions Passwords create_user and set_password store PBKDF2 with 1,500,000 iterations and a random salt, check_password compares Login authenticate(username=..., password=...) then login(request, user), or the ready LoginView In views @login_required, @permission_required, LoginRequiredMixin, PermissionRequiredMixin In templates user.is_authenticated, user.username, perms.books.change_book Permissions add, change, delete and view for every model, grouped with Group Your own user AUTH_USER_MODEL = \"accounts.User\", set before the first migrate Lesson: Users, logins and permissions Table: tool · use it for Tool Use it for transaction.atomic() Changes that must all happen or none: a with block or a decorator F(\"balance\") + amount Change a value inside the database, safe when two requests run at the same time select_for_update() Lock rows until the transaction ends (PostgreSQL, MySQL) TestCase Every test runs in a transaction that is rolled back, so tests never see each other's data setUpTestData Rows made once for the whole class, which is fast self.client The test client inside a TestCase: get, post, login, force_login assertContains, assertRedirects, assertNumQueries Check a page, a redirect, or the number of queries python manage.py test Finds tests.py and test_*.py, makes a test database, runs everything Lesson: Transactions and tests Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1683 Invalid Tweets Easy annotate(Length(...)) then filter A computed column: annotate each tweet"],[52,"py-django-orm","Django in depth: ORM, migrations, forms, users and tests","with the length of its text, then keep the ones longer than 15. 1378 Replace Employee ID With The Unique Identifier Easy Subquery with OuterRef, a LEFT JOIN without a ForeignKey The two tables share an id but have no ForeignKey. A Subquery that looks up the unique id for each employee works like a LEFT JOIN, and gives None when there is no match. 1068 Product Sales Analysis I Easy Follow a ForeignKey inside values() With a ForeignKey, product__product_name in values() makes Django write the JOIN for you. 1661 Average Time of Process per Machine Easy Conditional aggregation with Sum(Case(When(...))) Group by machine. Add the end times and subtract the start times with Case and When, then divide by the number of processes. 1280 Students and Examinations Easy Count per group, then fill in the zeros Every student must appear with every subject, even with 0 exams. Count the exams per (student, subject) with values() and annotate(), then loop over all pairs. 1251 Average Selling Price Easy Subquery for the price on the sale date, then a weighted average Each sale needs the price that was valid on its date. A Subquery with OuterRef and two date lookups finds it, then Sum(price times units) / Sum(units) gives the weighted average. 1211 Queries Quality and Percentage Easy Avg of a computed value and Count with a filter Group by query name. Avg(rating / position) gives the quality, and Count with filter=Q(rating__lt=3) counts the poor results. 607 Sales Person Easy exclude with a subquery of ids Find the sales people with an order for the company RED, as a QuerySet of ids, and exclude them. Django puts the inner query into one SQL statement with NOT IN. 1193 Monthly Transactions I Medium Group by a computed month, Count and Sum with filters Annotate each row with its month (the first 7 characters of the date), group by month and country, and use filter=Q(state=\"approved\") inside Count and Sum. 1174 Immediate Food Delivery II Medium Subquery for the first order of each customer Find each customer's first order date with a Subquery that sorts by date and takes one row, keep only those first orders, then count how many were delivered on the day they were ordered. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps Remember The QuerySet keeps the rows it read. Looping twice over the same QuerySet runs one query, but calling filter() again builds a new QuerySet and runs new SQL. Save a QuerySet in a variable when you need it twice.Lesson: QuerySets: filter, Q, F, aggregate and annotate In the exam In tests, assertNumQueries(n) fails when a change adds queries, so an N + 1 problem cannot sneak back in. In development, django-debug-toolbar shows every query of a page.Lesson: Fast queries: the N + 1 problem Remember SQLite cannot change most column types in place, so the SQL above makes a new table, copies the rows and renames it. PostgreSQL runs a short ALTER TABLE instead. Django picks the right way for each database.Lesson: Migrations: changing tables safely In the exam The usual view: if request.method == \"POST\", bind and check the form, save and redirect when it is valid. Otherwise make an empty form. Either way render the template, which shows the errors. In the template put {% csrf_token %} inside the form tag.Lesson: Forms: checking what users send Remember Start every new project with a custom user model (class User(AbstractUser): pass) and AUTH_USER_MODEL. Changing it later, when tables already point to auth.User, is a painful migration.Lesson: Users, logins and permissions In the exam login_required sends a person who is not logged in to LOGIN_URL with ?next=, as the 302 above shows. permission_required with raise_exception=True gives 403 instead, which is right for a logged in user who simply may not do this.Lesson: Users, logins and permissions In the exam Look at the query count: inside a test the atomic() of transfer becomes SAVEPOINT and RELEASE, because TestCase already opened a transaction around the test. Outside tests it is BEGIN and"],[52,"py-django-orm","Django in depth: ORM, migrations, forms, users and tests","COMMIT.Lesson: Transactions and tests"],[52,"step-web-5","Step 5Web and APIs · Building APIs: Django REST framework and FastAPI","5.1"],[52,"py-drf","Django REST framework: JSON APIs on top of Django","Open chapter Serializers, ViewSets and routers, pagination and filters, authentication, permissions, throttles and API tests. Key ideas Serializers class BookSerializer(serializers.ModelSerializer) with Meta.model, Meta.fields. serializer.data, Serializer(data=...).is_valid(raise_exception=True), validated_data, save(). validate_<field>, validate, SerializerMethodField, read_only. Views and routers @api_view([\"GET\", \"POST\"]) with Response(data, status=201). class BookViewSet(viewsets.ModelViewSet) with queryset and serializer_class. router.register(\"books\", BookViewSet). @action. get_queryset, perform_create. Security DEFAULT_AUTHENTICATION_CLASSES (Token, Session, JWT). permission_classes: IsAuthenticated, IsAuthenticatedOrReadOnly, has_object_permission. 401 no credentials, 403 not allowed. Throttles answer 429 with Retry-After. Lists DEFAULT_PAGINATION_CLASS PageNumberPagination with PAGE_SIZE: count, next, previous, results. SearchFilter ?search=, OrderingFilter ?ordering=-year. Always order the queryset. CursorPagination for feeds. Tests and design APITestCase, self.client.post(url, data, format=\"json\"), force_authenticate, assertNumQueries. Nouns in URLs, right status codes, one error shape, versioning, idempotent retries. Formulas Number of pagesExam favourite pages = ⌈ count / page size ⌉ 5 books at 2 a page: ⌈2.5⌉ = 3 pages. Lesson: The maths: pages, limits and payload size Requests allowedExam favourite rate = requests / window 3/minute: the 4th request in 60 s gets 429. Lesson: The maths: pages, limits and payload size Wait time in a sliding windowExam favourite Retry-After = oldest + window − now Requests at 0, 10 and 20 s, a 4th at 30 s: 0 + 60 − 30 = 30 s. Lesson: The maths: pages, limits and payload size Requests per day at a limit per day = rate × windows per day 100/hour: 100 × 24 = 2,400 a day. Lesson: The maths: pages, limits and payload size Payload size bytes ≈ rows × bytes per row 20 books of 120 bytes: 2,400 bytes. All 50,000: 6 MB. Lesson: The maths: pages, limits and payload size Requests to read everything requests = ⌈ count / page size ⌉ 50,000 rows at 100 a page: 500 requests. Lesson: The maths: pages, limits and payload size Tables Table: · plain django views · django rest framework · fastapi Plain Django views Django REST framework FastAPI Input checks By hand or with forms Serializers Pydantic models from type hints CRUD endpoints Write each view ModelViewSet and a router Write each function Logins, permissions, limits Django sessions only Token, session, JWT (add on), permissions, throttles Dependencies you write or add Docs None Browsable API, OpenAPI with an add on OpenAPI and Swagger UI built in Good for One or two JSON endpoints APIs on top of a Django project and its models New API only services, async work Lesson: What Django REST framework adds Table: serializer part · what it does Serializer part What it does ModelSerializer with Meta.model and Meta.fields Makes the fields from the model, with its rules serializer.data Object to dict, ready to become JSON Serializer(data=...).is_valid() Checks incoming data. errors holds the messages, validated_data the clean values validate_<field>(self, value) A rule for one field validate(self, data) A rule for several fields. Errors go to non_field_errors SerializerMethodField A read only value from a get_<name> method read_only=True, write_only=True Shown but never written, or accepted but never shown (passwords) save() Calls create() or update(), which you can override Lesson: Serializers and API views Table: http request · viewset action · answer HTTP request ViewSet action Answer GET /api/books/ list 200, a page of books POST /api/books/ create 201, the new book GET /api/books/7/ retrieve 200 or 404 PUT /api/books/7/ update 200, every field replaced PATCH /api/books/7/ partial_update 200, only the fields sent DELETE /api/books/7/ destroy 204, empty body GET /api/books/oldest/ @action(detail=False) Your own extra endpoint Lesson: ViewSets,"],[52,"py-drf","Django REST framework: JSON APIs on top of Django","routers, filters and pages Table: · apiview or @api_view · generic views · viewsets APIView or @api_view Generic views ViewSets You write Each method yourself queryset and serializer_class queryset and serializer_class Example def get(self, request) ListCreateAPIView ModelViewSet URLs path() for each path() for each One router.register Good for Special endpoints A few endpoints Full CRUD of a model Lesson: ViewSets, routers, filters and pages Table: setting or class · job Setting or class Job TokenAuthentication Reads \"Authorization: Token ...\" and finds the user SessionAuthentication Uses the Django login cookie, for a front end on the same site (needs CSRF) JWT (djangorestframework-simplejwt) Signed tokens that expire, no database lookup per request IsAuthenticated, IsAuthenticatedOrReadOnly, IsAdminUser Who may use the view has_object_permission Rules for one object, such as \"only the owner may change it\" AnonRateThrottle, UserRateThrottle Limits such as 100/hour per IP or per user, answered with 429 perform_create(serializer) Adds server side values such as owner=request.user Lesson: Authentication, permissions and throttling Table: · 401 unauthorized · 403 forbidden 401 Unauthorized 403 Forbidden Means We do not know who you are We know who you are, and you may not Example No token, or a wrong token Bob deletes ann's note The client should Log in and try again Not try again Lesson: Authentication, permissions and throttling Table: way to show a relation · gives · good for Way to show a relation Gives Good for PrimaryKeyRelatedField (the default) \"author\": 2 Small answers, the client looks up more if needed StringRelatedField or source=\"author.name\" \"author\": \"Jane Austen\" Showing a name, read only A nested serializer \"author\": {\"id\": 2, \"name\": ...} Fewer requests for the client HyperlinkedRelatedField \"author\": \"http://.../authors/2/\" APIs that link resources together Lesson: Nested data and API tests Table: rule · good · avoid Rule Good Avoid Nouns in URLs, HTTP methods as verbs POST /api/orders/ POST /api/createOrder Plural names, ids in the path /api/books/7/ /api/book?id=7 Filters, search and order in the query string /api/books/?author=2&ordering=-year A different URL for each filter Always paginate lists count, next, previous, results Returning 100,000 rows Use the right status code 201 with the new object, 204 after delete 200 with {\"error\": ...} Same error shape everywhere {\"field\": [\"message\"]} or {\"detail\": \"...\"} Plain text errors Version when you break things /api/v2/ or an Accept header Changing fields under existing clients Make retries safe PUT and DELETE are idempotent, POST can take an Idempotency-Key Charging a card twice on retry Lesson: Designing a good API Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1408 String Matching in an Array Easy Check each word against the others with in Like the SearchFilter of an API: a word is a match when it appears inside another word. Two loops and the in operator are enough for small lists. 1207 Unique Number of Occurrences Easy Counter, then compare the counts with a set A validation rule in a serializer often counts things. Count each value with Counter, then the counts are unique when a set of them has the same size. 1640 Check Array Formation Through Concatenation Easy A dict from first number to piece Like joining pages of API results back into one list. Each piece starts with a different number, so a dict from first number to piece finds the next piece at once. 911 Online Election Medium Precompute the leader after each vote, then binary search the time Like an API that answers \"what was true at time t\". Work out the leader after every vote once, then each question is a binary search on the times. 2080 Range Frequency Queries Medium Positions per value, then two binary searches Like an API filter ?value=4&from=1&to=2. Keep the sorted positions of each value, then count the positions inside the range with bisect. 2707 Extra Characters in"],[52,"py-drf","Django REST framework: JSON APIs on top of Django","a String Medium Dynamic programming from the end of the string Like splitting a URL into known resource names. best[i] is the fewest extra letters from position i. Either skip the letter at i, or jump over a dictionary word that starts at i. 381 Insert Delete GetRandom O(1) - Duplicates allowed Hard A list plus a dict of index sets, swap with the last item to delete Like a load balancer that picks a random server where some servers count twice. The list gives random choice in O(1), the dict of positions finds an item to remove, and swapping with the last item keeps removal O(1). 715 Range Module Hard A sorted list of disjoint intervals Like a cache that remembers which ranges of ids it already fetched from an API. Keep the ranges sorted and merged, and use bisect to find the ones that touch a new range. 2276 Count Integers in Intervals Hard Merge intervals as they arrive and keep a running total Like counting how many ids a set of API keys may read. Keep disjoint intervals sorted by start, merge every new one with the ones it touches, and update the total on the way. 1622 Fancy Sequence Hard Store one lazy formula a × x + b for every value Like update(price=F(\"price\") * 2 + 1) for every row at once: never touch each value. Keep one global formula and store each new value \"undone\" with the modular inverse, so the formula applies to it correctly later. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps Remember Response(data) is not JSON yet. The renderer turns it into JSON for programs and into the browsable HTML page for a person with a browser. That page lets you try the API by hand.Lesson: Serializers and API views In the exam SearchFilter and OrderingFilter come with DRF. For filters such as ?year=1965&author=2, the django-filter add on reads them from a filterset_fields list. Always order the queryset, otherwise pages can show a row twice.Lesson: ViewSets, routers, filters and pages Remember Never trust ids or owners from the request body. The example ignores \"owner\": \"bob\" in the body and sets the owner from the token in perform_create. Mark such fields read only in the serializer.Lesson: Authentication, permissions and throttling 5.2"],[52,"py-fastapi","FastAPI: fast, typed APIs with automatic docs","Open chapter Pydantic models, typed parameters, response_model, Depends, async def and the event loop, docs and pytest. Key ideas Endpoints @app.get(\"/books/{book_id}\") def get_book(book_id: int). Query parameters are plain parameters, Query(10, le=100) adds limits. A Pydantic model parameter is the body. response_model, status_code, HTTPException(404, \"...\"). Pydantic class BookIn(BaseModel) with Field(gt=0), Field(min_length=...), Literal, nested models, @field_validator, @model_validator. model_dump(), model_dump_json(), model_json_schema(). Bad input is a 422 with locations. Dependencies Depends(get_db) and Annotated[T, Depends(f)]. yield for clean up. Header() for tokens, OAuth2PasswordBearer for logins. app.dependency_overrides in tests. Async async def only with awaitable libraries (httpx.AsyncClient, asyncpg). Plain def for blocking code, which runs in a thread pool. Never time.sleep or requests.get in async def. BackgroundTasks, lifespan. Run and test fastapi dev main.py, fastapi run or uvicorn main:app --workers 4. /openapi.json, /docs, /redoc. TestClient(app) with pytest fixtures and parametrize. Formulas Time with awaitExam favourite total ≈ the longest single wait 10 requests that each wait 0.2 s: about 0.2 s. Lesson: The maths: concurrency, workers and timeouts Time with a blocked loopExam favourite total ≈ n × wait 10 × 0.2 = 2.0 s. Lesson: The maths: concurrency, workers and timeouts Requests one process can hold (Little's law)Exam favourite in flight = rate × time per request 500 requests a second at 0.2 s each: 100 in flight at once. Lesson: The maths: concurrency, workers and timeouts Thread pool limit requests per second ≤ threads / time per request 40 threads at 0.2 s: at most 200 plain def requests a second per process. Lesson: The maths: concurrency, workers and timeouts Workers workers ≈ 2 × CPU cores + 1 (a common start for sync servers) 4 cores: 9 workers. Async servers often use one worker per core. Lesson: The maths: concurrency, workers and timeouts Timeout budget total ≤ sum of the calls in a row Three calls of 0.3 s in a row: 0.9 s. In parallel with gather: about 0.3 s. Lesson: The maths: concurrency, workers and timeouts Tables Table: · fastapi · flask · django rest framework FastAPI Flask Django REST framework Input checks Pydantic from type hints By hand or with an extension Serializers Docs OpenAPI, Swagger UI and ReDoc built in Extensions Browsable API, OpenAPI with an add on Async Built on ASGI, async def everywhere Mainly sync (WSGI) Mainly sync, async views possible Database and admin Your choice (SQLAlchemy, SQLModel) Your choice Django ORM and admin Good for New API services, many waiting calls Small apps and services APIs next to a Django site Lesson: What FastAPI is and why type hints matter Table: tool · example · effect Tool Example Effect Type hint quantity: int \"3\" becomes 3, \"abc\" is an error Field limits Field(gt=0, le=10), Field(min_length=2), Field(pattern=...) Ranges, lengths, patterns Literal size: Literal[\"S\", \"M\", \"L\"] = \"M\" Only these values, with a default Nested model address: Address A dict is checked as an Address field_validator @field_validator(\"customer\") Your own rule or clean up for one field model_validator @model_validator(mode=\"after\") A rule across several fields Output model_dump(), model_dump_json() A dict or JSON text Schema model_json_schema() The JSON Schema that FastAPI puts in the docs Lesson: Pydantic: data that checks itself Table: where the value comes from · how you write it · example Where the value comes from How you write it Example Path A name in {} in the route and a parameter @app.get(\"/books/{book_id}\") def get_book(book_id: int) Query string A simple parameter that is not in the path min_year: int = 0, limit: int = Query(10, le=100) JSON body A parameter with a Pydantic model type def add_book(book: BookIn) Header or cookie Header() or Cookie() authorization: Annotated[str, Header()] Answer shape response_model and status_code"],[52,"py-fastapi","FastAPI: fast, typed APIs with automatic docs","@app.post(..., response_model=BookOut, status_code=201) Errors raise HTTPException HTTPException(404, \"Book not found\") Lesson: Your first API: parameters, bodies and errors Table: use of depends · example Use of Depends Example A database session per request, closed after def get_db(): db = ... yield db ... close The current user from a token def current_user(authorization: str = Header()) ... Shared query parameters def paging(skip: int = 0, limit: int = 20) A permission check def admin_only(user = Depends(current_user)): if not user.is_admin: raise HTTPException(403) Settings loaded once def get_settings(): return Settings() with lru_cache Fakes in tests app.dependency_overrides[get_db] = fake_db Lesson: Dependencies Table: · async def with await · def (plain) · async def with a blocking call async def with await def (plain) async def with a blocking call Example await asyncio.sleep(0.2), await client.get(...) time.sleep(0.2), requests.get(...) time.sleep(0.2) inside async def Runs on The event loop A thread pool The event loop, which it blocks 10 requests at once 0.2 s 0.2 s 2.0 s Verdict Best with async libraries Fine with normal libraries Never do this Lesson: async def, background tasks and start up Table: tool · use it for Tool Use it for BackgroundTasks Small work after the answer, such as an email A task queue (Celery, RQ, arq) Heavy or important work that must survive a restart lifespan Open pools and load models once at start up, close them at shut down httpx.AsyncClient with ASGITransport Calling the app directly from async tests WebSocket routes (@app.websocket) Two way messages such as chat or live prices Lesson: async def, background tasks and start up Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1122 Relative Sort Array Easy Sort with a key: (position in the order, value) Like an API that lists fields in a fixed order and the rest A to Z after them. A dict gives each wanted value its position, and the sort key puts the others at the end. 1200 Minimum Absolute Difference Easy Sort, then only neighbours matter After sorting, the closest pair is always next to each other, so one pass finds the smallest gap and the pairs that have it. 1287 Element Appearing More Than 25% In Sorted Array Easy Compare with the item a quarter further on In a sorted list a value that fills more than a quarter must still be there a quarter of the length later. One loop, no counting. 1013 Partition Array Into Three Parts With Equal Sum Easy Walk once and count parts that reach a third The total must split into three equal parts. Add numbers from the left and count each time the running sum reaches one third, then start again. 1237 Find Positive Integer Solution for a Given Equation Medium Two pointers on a function you can only call You can only call the hidden function f(x, y), like a remote API that you want to call as few times as possible. f grows with x and with y, so start at x = 1 and y = 1000 and move one of them each call. 2069 Walking Robot Simulation II Medium Moves wrap around the border, so use the remainder The robot only walks around the edge of the grid. One full lap is a fixed number of steps, so a huge step count shrinks to steps % lap, like a cursor that wraps around. 1801 Number of Orders in the Backlog Medium Two heaps: highest buy and lowest sell An order book API: a buy matches the cheapest sell, a sell matches the highest buy. A min heap of sells and a max heap of buys (negated prices) give the best match at once. 1032 Stream of Characters Hard A trie of reversed words, checked from the newest letter Like a WebSocket that gets one letter at a time and must say at once if the latest letters end a banned word. Store the words backwards in a trie and walk it from the newest letter back. 2642 Design Graph With Shortest Path Calculator Hard Dijkstra with a heap on every query Like a gateway that finds the cheapest chain of services. Keep the edges in a dict of lists, let addEdge append, and"],[52,"py-fastapi","FastAPI: fast, typed APIs with automatic docs","run Dijkstra with a heap for each shortestPath question. 2286 Booking Concert Tickets in Groups Hard A segment tree with the max and the sum of free seats per range A booking API must answer \"first row with k free seats together\" and \"book k scattered seats\" fast. A segment tree keeps the most free seats in a row and the total free seats for every range of rows. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps Remember Pydantic converts when it is safe: \"3\" becomes 3, but \"3.5\" for an int is an error, and so is the 13th month. Every problem is listed with its location, such as address.zip_code, which is exactly what FastAPI sends back in a 422 answer.Lesson: Pydantic: data that checks itself In the exam Run the real server with fastapi dev main.py while you develop (it reloads on every save) and fastapi run main.py or uvicorn main:app --workers 4 in production. Then open http://127.0.0.1:8000/docs.Lesson: Your first API: parameters, bodies and errors Remember For real logins FastAPI has OAuth2PasswordBearer, which reads \"Authorization: Bearer ...\" and also adds an Authorize button to /docs. The token is usually a JWT signed with a secret key (chapter 6.1).Lesson: Dependencies"],[52,"step-web-6","Step 6Web and APIs · Securing APIs and the web revision exam","6.1"],[52,"py-api-security","API security: passwords, JWT, OAuth 2.0, CORS and OWASP","Open chapter Password hashing, JWTs by hand and with PyJWT, OAuth 2.0 scopes in FastAPI, CORS and CSRF, and the OWASP API Top 10. Key ideas Passwords hashlib.pbkdf2_hmac(\"sha256\", pw, salt, 600_000) or Argon2id. Random salt per user, stored as algorithm$rounds$salt$hash. hmac.compare_digest. secrets.token_urlsafe(32), never random. Keys from os.environ, never in git. JWT header.payload.signature in base64url, HS256 = HMAC-SHA256. Readable by anyone. jwt.encode(claims, key, algorithm=\"HS256\"). jwt.decode(token, key, algorithms=[\"HS256\"], audience=..., issuer=...). Claims sub, exp, iat, nbf, aud, iss, jti. Short access, rotated refresh, kid for keys. FastAPI auth OAuth2PasswordBearer(tokenUrl=\"token\", scopes=...), OAuth2PasswordRequestForm, Security(current_user, scopes=[...]). 401 with WWW-Authenticate: Bearer, 403 for missing scope, 404 for another user's object. Browser rules CORSMiddleware(allow_origins=[exact list], allow_credentials=True, allow_methods, allow_headers). Preflight OPTIONS. Never * with credentials. CSRF: SameSite cookies and CSRF tokens. Headers: HSTS, nosniff, Content-Security-Policy. OWASP API Top 10 API1 object level checks, API2 authentication, API3 property level (mass assignment, extra=\"forbid\"), API4 resource limits, API5 function level, API7 SSRF. ? placeholders against injection. Rate limits with 429 and Retry-After. Formulas Entropy of a random secretExam favourite bits = length × log2(alphabet size) 12 random lower case letters: 12 × log2 26 ≈ 56.4 bits. Lesson: The maths: guessing, entropy, hashing cost and rate limits Guesses to try every valueExam favourite guesses = 2bits A 6 digit code: 106 = 1,000,000 values. Lesson: The maths: guessing, entropy, hashing cost and rate limits Time to crackExam favourite time = guesses × cost per guess / machines With PBKDF2 at 0.12 s, one million guesses take 120,000 s on one core. Lesson: The maths: guessing, entropy, hashing cost and rate limits Chance to guess with a rate limit P = attempts / values 5 tries on a 6 digit code: 5 / 1,000,000 = 0.0005 %. Lesson: The maths: guessing, entropy, hashing cost and rate limits Token bucket allowed in t seconds ≤ capacity + rate × t Capacity 10, rate 2 per second: at most 10 + 2 × 60 = 130 requests in a minute. Lesson: The maths: guessing, entropy, hashing cost and rate limits Birthday bound for random ids P(any repeat) ≈ n2 / 2bits + 1 1 billion random 128 bit ids: about 1018 / 2129, practically zero. Lesson: The maths: guessing, entropy, hashing cost and rate limits Tables Table: · session cookie · jwt bearer token · api key Session cookie JWT bearer token API key OAuth 2.0 What the client holds A random session id in a cookie A signed token with claims A long random string An access token from an authorization server What the server keeps A session table Only the secret key A table of hashed keys Depends on the token type Log out at once Easy: delete the session Hard: wait for exp or keep a deny list Delete the key Revoke at the authorization server Watch out for CSRF, so use SameSite and CSRF tokens Long lifetimes, alg confusion, storing in localStorage Keys leaked in code or logs Using the wrong flow Good for Websites with server pages (Django, Flask) APIs and mobile apps Server to server calls Letting another app act for a user Lesson: What API security is and why every endpoint needs it Table: way to store a password · verdict · why Way to store a password Verdict Why Plain text Never One leak shows every password, and people reuse them on other sites Encrypted Never Whoever has the key can read them all sha256(password) Never Billions of guesses a second on a graphics card, and the same password gives the same hash sha256(salt + password) Weak The salt stops shared tables, but the hash is still fast PBKDF2, bcrypt, scrypt, Argon2id Yes Salted and slow on purpose. Argon2id and scrypt also need memory Lesson: Storing passwords and secrets Table: part · what it holds · example Part What it holds Example Header"],[52,"py-api-security","API security: passwords, JWT, OAuth 2.0, CORS and OWASP","The algorithm and the type {\"alg\": \"HS256\", \"typ\": \"JWT\"} Payload (claims) Who, what and until when sub (user), exp (expiry), iat (issued), nbf (not before), aud (audience), iss (issuer), jti (id), scope Signature HMAC-SHA256 of header.payload with the secret key base64url of 32 bytes Encoding base64url without = padding, parts joined with dots eyJhbGci... . eyJzdWIi... . Q46Cf6DE... Lesson: JSON Web Tokens from scratch Table: practice · why Practice Why Pin algorithms=[\"HS256\"] when decoding Stops alg confusion and alg none tricks Short access tokens (5 to 15 minutes) A stolen token is useful only for minutes Long refresh tokens that work once (rotation) A second use shows theft, so all sessions can be ended Check aud and iss A token for another API or from another issuer is refused kid in the header with a key table Keys can be swapped without logging everyone out jti and a deny list for log out A JWT cannot be deleted, so remember the ids of revoked ones until they expire Keep tokens out of URLs and logs URLs end up in browser history, proxies and log files Lesson: Real tokens with PyJWT: expiry, refresh and key rotation Table: oauth 2.0 flow · who uses it · notes OAuth 2.0 flow Who uses it Notes Authorization code with PKCE Web, mobile and single page apps logging users in The standard choice today. PKCE stops a stolen code from being used Client credentials A server calling another server, with no user The client id and secret give a token Device code TVs and command line tools The user types a short code on another device Password (resource owner) Only your own first party app Legacy. The FastAPI tutorial uses it because it is simple Implicit Nobody Deprecated. Tokens in the URL leaked Lesson: OAuth 2.0 logins, scopes and owner checks in FastAPI Table: · 401 unauthorized · 403 forbidden 401 Unauthorized 403 Forbidden Meaning We do not know who you are We know who you are, and the answer is no Typical cause No token, bad signature, expired Missing scope or role Client should Log in or refresh the token Not retry, ask for access Lesson: OAuth 2.0 logins, scopes and owner checks in FastAPI Table: · cors · csrf CORS CSRF What it is Rules that let a server allow other sites to READ its answers in a browser An attack where another site makes your browser SEND a request with your cookies Who enforces it The browser, from the server's headers The server, with tokens and cookie settings When it matters Any API called from a page on another origin When the login is a cookie the browser adds by itself The fix or setting An exact list of origins, never * with credentials SameSite=Lax or Strict cookies plus a CSRF token (Django has it built in) Bearer tokens Still needed Not a risk: the browser never adds the Authorization header by itself Lesson: CORS, CSRF and security headers Table: owasp api 2023 · what goes wrong · python fix OWASP API 2023 What goes wrong Python fix API1 Broken Object Level Authorization Change the id in the URL and read someone else's data Check the owner on every object (filter by user in the query) API2 Broken Authentication Weak passwords, no rate limit on login, tokens that never expire Slow hashes, login limits, short exp, pinned algorithms API3 Broken Object Property Level Authorization Mass assignment (is_admin from the body) or returning secret fields Input models with extra=\"forbid\", response_model for output API4 Unrestricted Resource Consumption Huge pages, huge uploads, no rate limit Cap limit, body size and requests per client API5 Broken Function Level Authorization A normal user can call an admin endpoint Role and scope checks as dependencies API6 Unrestricted Access to Sensitive Business Flows Bots buy every ticket or create many accounts Limits per account, device and payment, CAPTCHA API7 Server Side Request Forgery The API fetches a user URL that points inside the network https only, block internal addresses, an allowlist API8 Security Misconfiguration Debug pages, open CORS, missing"],[52,"py-api-security","API security: passwords, JWT, OAuth 2.0, CORS and OWASP","headers, default keys DEBUG off, exact CORS list, security headers API9 Improper Inventory Management An old /v1 or a test server nobody patches A list of every API version and host, retire old ones API10 Unsafe Consumption of APIs Trusting data from a partner API without checks Validate it with models, use timeouts, follow no redirects blindly Lesson: The OWASP API Security Top 10 Table: control · how in python · notes Control How in Python Notes HTTPS everywhere TLS at the proxy (nginx, a cloud load balancer), HSTS header Tokens and passwords travel in every request Rate limits A token bucket per client, often in Redis, or slowapi for FastAPI and DRF throttles Send 429 Too Many Requests with Retry-After Login limits Count failures per account and per IP, slow down or lock for a while Stops password guessing Secrets os.environ, a vault, never in git, rotate on a schedule Scan commits with a secret scanner in CI Logging Log user id, path, status and time, never passwords or tokens Mask Authorization headers in error reports Dependencies pip-audit or the GitHub dependency alerts Most vulnerabilities come from old libraries Least privilege A database user that can only do what the app needs A leak then exposes less Lesson: Rate limits, HTTPS, logging and secrets in production Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 2325 Decode the Message Easy Build a substitution table from first appearances This is a simple substitution cipher. The first time each letter appears in the key it stands for the next letter of the alphabet. A dict holds the table, and spaces stay as they are. Ciphers like this are easy to break, which is why real systems use keys and HMAC instead. 1844 Replace All Digits with Characters Easy Shift a letter by a digit Every digit says how far to move the letter before it, the same idea as a Caesar shift. chr(ord(letter) + digit) does the move. 1528 Shuffle String Easy Put each character at its target index A transposition: indices[i] says where s[i] belongs. Fill a list of the right length, then join it. 1461 Check If a String Contains All Binary Codes of Size K Medium A set of every window of length k There are 2k codes of length k, the same count an attacker must try to guess a k bit token. Put every window of the string in a set and compare its size with 2k. 187 Repeated DNA Sequences Medium Two sets: seen once and seen again Slide a window of 10 letters and remember each one in a set. A window that is already there is a repeat, like a replayed request id that a server must refuse. The answer is sorted here so the tests can compare it. 1980 Find Unique Binary String Medium Cantor diagonal: differ from string i at place i Take bit i of string i and flip it. The new string differs from every given string in at least one place, so it cannot be any of them. The same trick proves that some sets cannot be listed. 721 Accounts Merge Medium Union find over e-mail addresses Two accounts are one person when they share an e-mail, like linking identities in a login system. Union find joins every e-mail with the first e-mail of its account, then each group becomes one sorted account. The list is sorted at the end so the tests can compare it. 420 Strong Password Checker Hard Count runs, then spend deletions where they save a replacement A strong password has 6 to 20 characters, a lower case letter, an upper case letter and a digit, and never three equal characters in a row. Short passwords only need inserts. Long ones need deletes, and a delete in a run whose length is a multiple of 3 saves one replacement, so spend deletes on those runs first, then on runs with remainder 1 (two deletes) and then three at a time. 2156 Find Substring With Given Hash Value Hard Rolling hash, walking from the right The hash gives the first letter the power p0, so it is easy to add a letter on the LEFT: multiply by p and add the new value. Walk from the end of the string, drop the letter that falls out of the window,"],[52,"py-api-security","API security: passwords, JWT, OAuth 2.0, CORS and OWASP","and remember the leftmost window that matches. Rolling hashes also power rsync and duplicate file checks. 1392 Longest Happy Prefix Hard The KMP failure table A happy prefix is also a suffix. The prefix function of KMP stores, for each position, the longest proper prefix that ends there, and its last value is the answer. It runs in O(n), with no hash collisions to worry about. Lesson: LeetCode practice Tips and traps In the exam Keep secret keys out of the code: read them with os.environ[\"SECRET_KEY\"], keep a local .env file in .gitignore, and use a vault (AWS Secrets Manager, HashiCorp Vault) in production. A key that was ever pushed to git is burnt: rotate it, because deleting the commit does not remove copies.Lesson: Storing passwords and secrets Remember In a browser, an HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite cookie is the safest place for a token, because JavaScript (and so an XSS bug) cannot read it. localStorage is readable by any script on the page.Lesson: Real tokens with PyJWT: expiry, refresh and key rotation 6.2"],[52,"py-web-exam","Web revision exam: HTTP, Flask, Django, FastAPI and security","Open chapter Four papers that run in the page, eight bugs, the web traps, a design answer and a mock quiz. Key ideas HTTP Status classes 2xx, 3xx, 4xx, 5xx. 201 with Location, 204, 301, 304, 400, 401, 403, 404, 405, 412, 422, 429. Idempotent: GET, PUT, DELETE. requests.get(url, timeout=5) and raise_for_status(). Flask @app.get(\"/books/<int:book_id>\"), request.args.get(\"page\", 1, type=int), request.get_json(), return dict, status, headers. g, session, Blueprint(url_prefix=...), url_for, errorhandler, test_client(). Django and DRF Lazy QuerySets, Q, F, annotate, select_related and prefetch_related, makemigrations and migrate, reverse and resolve, autoescape. Serializer.is_valid() then validated_data, ModelViewSet with a router. FastAPI Type hints decide path, query and body. Pydantic gives 422 with locations. response_model, Depends with yield, async def only with await, TestClient and dependency_overrides. Security Slow salted hashes, JWT with pinned algorithms and short exp, scopes give 403, owner checks give 404, CORS for browsers, ? placeholders, caps on page size, secrets in the environment. Formulas Codes availableExam favourite codes = alphabetlength 627 ≈ 3.52 × 1012. Lesson: A system design answer: a link shortener API Storage per year rows = writes per second × seconds per year 100 × 31,536,000 ≈ 3.2 × 109 rows. Lesson: A system design answer: a link shortener API Load on the database with a cache reads to the database = reads × (1 − hit rate) 10,000 × (1 − 0.99) = 100 a second. Lesson: A system design answer: a link shortener API Tables Table: paper · covers · chapters Paper Covers Chapters A URLs, query strings, encoding, status codes, idempotency, cookies, JSON and raw HTTP 1.1 HTTP, 1.2 sockets, 2.1 requests, 2.2 API data B Flask routes, converters, 404 and 405, JSON bodies, query arguments, g, sessions, blueprints, url_for 3.1 Flask, 3.2 Flask apps C Django QuerySets, Q and F, annotate, JOINs, URLs, templates and autoescape, DRF serializers 4.1 Django, 4.2 ORM, 5.1 DRF D Pydantic conversion and errors, nested models, model_dump, PyJWT checks, hashes, secrets and IP checks 5.2 FastAPI, 6.1 API security Lesson: How to use this revision chapter Table: q · output · why (chapter) Q Output Why (chapter) 1 https api.shop.example 8443 /v2/books q=python&page=2 top urlsplit gives each part. The port is an int, and the fragment after # is never sent to the server (1.1) 2 {'tag': ['a', 'b']} [''] parse_qs always gives lists and drops empty values unless keep_blank_values=True (2.1) 3 q=tea+%26+cake&page=1 /a%20b/ %2Fa%20b%2F urlencode writes a space as + and & as %26. quote keeps / unless safe=\"\" (2.1) 4 https://x.example/api/v1/books https://x.example/api/books https://x.example/books Without the trailing slash, the last part of the base is replaced. A leading / starts again at the root (2.1) 5 [201, 404] Too Many Requests [2, 3, 4, 5] The class of a status code is code // 100: 2 success, 3 redirect, 4 client error, 5 server error (1.1) 6 ['PATCH', 'POST'] GET, HEAD, OPTIONS, PUT and DELETE are idempotent: repeating them changes nothing more. POST and PATCH are not (1.1) 7 abc123 ['session', 'theme'] A Cookie header holds name=value pairs separated by semicolons, and SimpleCookie reads them (1.1) 8 {\"price\": 9.5, \"tags\": [\"a\", \"b\"], \"ok\": null, \"1\": \"one\"} one JSON turns a tuple into a list, None into null and the int key 1 into the string \"1\" (2.2) 9 ['HTTP/1.1', '301', 'Moved Permanently'] /new A raw response is a status line, headers and a blank line. A 301 sends the new address in Location (1.2) 10 5 4 True é takes 2 bytes in UTF-8, and Content-Length counts bytes. Percent encoding also works on the UTF-8 bytes (1.2) Lesson: Paper A: HTTP, URLs and JSON Table: q · output · why (chapter) Q Output Why (chapter) 1 200 {'id': 2, 'title': 'Dune'} A view that returns a dict is sent as JSON (3.1) 2 404 {'error': 'not found', 'path': '/books/9'} \"two\" does not fit int:, so no route matches and the answer is 404, not 400. The"],[52,"py-web-exam","Web revision exam: HTTP, Flask, Django, FastAPI and security","404 handler formats both cases (3.1) 3 405 405 The path exists but not for DELETE or POST: 405 Method Not Allowed (3.1) 4 201 {'id': 3} /books/3 A view can return (body, status, headers). url_for builds the Location from the view name (3.1) 5 {'error': 'title is required'} get_json(silent=True) returns None for a body that is not JSON instead of failing (3.1) 6 {'page': 1, 'q': 'tea'} {'page': 3, 'q': None} type=int falls back to the default when the value is not a number. A missing argument is None. Flask sorts the keys of JSON answers (3.1) 7 {'user': 'ann', 'visits': 2} before_request fills g for each request. The session cookie from the first call came back with the second (3.2) 8 1 A new test client has no cookie, so it starts a new session (3.2) 9 {'books': 3} 404 The blueprint adds the prefix /admin, so /stats alone does not exist (3.2) 10 /books/7 /admin/stats /search?q=a+b&page=2 Blueprint views are named blueprint.view. Arguments that are not in the rule become the query string (3.1) Lesson: Paper B: Flask Table: q · output · why (chapter) Q Output Why (chapter) 1 QuerySet 2 ['Dune', 'Ubik'] filter gives a lazy QuerySet. year__gt is a field lookup, and SQL runs only when the values are needed (4.2) 2 <QuerySet ['Dune', 'Emma']> Q objects joined with | make an OR. A slice of a QuerySet is still a QuerySet (4.2) 3 [2, 0, 4] update with F(\"stock\") - 1 runs one SQL UPDATE, only on the rows with stock above 0 (4.2) 4 [('Bob', 2), ('Ann', 1)] annotate(Count(\"books\")) adds a count per author, using the related_name (4.2) 5 True 1 Filtering across the foreign key adds a JOIN. all() is a single SELECT (4.2) 6 /books/7/ {'pk': 7} book-detail reverse turns a name into a URL and resolve turns a URL back into the view and its arguments (4.1) 7 EMMA, DUNE &lt;b&gt;hi&lt;/b&gt; upper is a filter, forloop.last skips the last comma, and autoescape turns < into &lt; so user text cannot inject HTML (4.1) 8 False ['title', 'year'] Every field is checked and all errors are collected: the title is longer than 10 and 1200 is below 1450 (5.1) 9 True {'title': 'Emma', 'year': 1815, 'stock': 0} \"1815\" becomes the int 1815, and the default fills the missing stock (5.1) 10 200 {'pk': 42} 404 int: converts 42 and refuses x, which gives 404 (4.1) Lesson: Paper C: Django and Django REST framework Table: q · output · why (chapter) Q Output Why (chapter) 1 3 False \"3\" becomes 3. Pydantic copies a mutable default, so each object gets its own list (5.2) 2 2 ['qty', 'size'] \"3.5\" is not a whole number and XL is not one of the Literal values. Pydantic lists every error (5.2) 3 ('ship_to', 'zip_code') Errors in nested models have the full path, which FastAPI puts after body in a 422 answer (5.2) 4 {'id': 1, 'ship_to': {'city': 'Bonn', 'zip_code': '53111'}} {\"id\":1} exclude_none drops note. include keeps only the named fields, and model_dump_json writes compact JSON (5.2) 5 eyJ 2 eyJh Every JWT starts with eyJ because it is base64 of {\" and has three parts, so two dots (6.1) 6 DecodeError, InvalidAudienceError, ok PyJWT 2 refuses to decode without algorithms=. A token with aud needs audience= as well (6.1) 7 32 64 32 22 SHA-256 gives 32 bytes, which is 64 hex digits. token_hex(16) gives 32 characters and token_urlsafe(16) gives 22 (6.1) 8 True True The same key and message always give the same HMAC. compare_digest only changes how long the comparison takes (6.1) 9 [True, False, True, False, True] 172.16.0.0/12 ends at 172.31.255.255, so 172.32.0.1 is public. ::1 is the IPv6 loopback (6.1) 10 True False A hash is the same for the same input. Different salts give different results, which defeats precomputed tables (6.1) Lesson: Paper D: Pydantic, tokens and security Table: trap · looks like · really · chapter Trap Looks like Really Chapter URL fragment Sent to the server Stays in the browser 1.1 PUT and PATCH The same PUT replaces the whole resource and is idempotent, PATCH changes part of it 1.1 requests.get(url) Gives up after a while Waits for ever"],[52,"py-web-exam","Web revision exam: HTTP, Flask, Django, FastAPI and security","without timeout= 2.1 A 404 or 500 from requests Raises an error Only raise_for_status() raises 2.1 JSON keys Keep their type Always become strings 2.2 Flask int: converter Bad values give 400 They give 404 because no route matches 3.1 A route without methods= Accepts every method Only GET (and HEAD, OPTIONS) 3.1 Flask debug=True Fine on a server The debugger can run code: never in production 3.2 QuerySet Runs SQL when made Runs when looped, sliced with a step, counted or printed 4.2 book.author in a loop One query One query per book (N + 1): use select_related 4.2 |safe in a template Just formatting Turns off autoescape: XSS if the text came from a user 4.1 serializer.data before is_valid() Works Raises an AssertionError for input data 5.1 time.sleep in async def Waits only this request Blocks every request on the event loop 5.2 A JWT payload Secret Readable by anyone, only signed 6.1 CORS Protects the API Only tells browsers which sites may read answers 6.1 A valid token Can read anything Still needs the owner check on every object 6.1 Lesson: The traps in one table Table: no. · problem · level · pattern No. Problem Level Pattern Hint 1629 Slowest Key Easy One pass over the gaps between times Like finding the slowest request in an access log: each duration is the gap to the previous time. Keep the longest, and on a tie the larger letter. 1701 Average Waiting Time Medium Simulate one worker with a clock One server handles requests in the order they arrive. The clock is the time the server becomes free. A request starts at max(clock, arrival), and its waiting time is the finish time minus the arrival. 210 Course Schedule II Medium Topological sort with in-degrees (Kahn) The same problem as running database migrations or loading apps in dependency order. Start with every item that depends on nothing, and each time one is done, lower the count of the items waiting for it. A cycle leaves items behind, so the answer is empty. 399 Evaluate Division Medium A weighted graph and a search Like a currency API that only knows some rates: a / b = 2 is an edge from a to b with weight 2 and from b to a with 0.5. A query multiplies the weights along any path, or gives -1.0 when there is none. 1942 The Number of the Smallest Unoccupied Chair Medium Two heaps: free chairs and busy chairs by leaving time This is exactly how a connection pool hands out the lowest free connection number. Go through arrivals in time order, first free every chair whose guest has left, then give the smallest free one. 1438 Longest Continuous Subarray With Absolute Diff Less Than or Equal to Limit Medium Sliding window with a max deque and a min deque Like finding the longest stretch where response times stayed within a band. Two deques keep the window's maximum and minimum in O(1). When max minus min is too big, move the left edge. 752 Open the Lock Medium Breadth first search over 10,000 states A 4 digit lock has only 104 codes, which is why short codes need attempt limits. BFS from 0000 turns one wheel up or down at each step and skips the dead ends, so the first time it reaches the target is the fewest turns. 2050 Parallel Courses III Hard Longest path in a DAG, in topological order Like a deployment pipeline where jobs run in parallel once their needs are done: a job finishes at its own time plus the latest finish among the jobs it waits for. The answer is the latest finish of all. 1235 Maximum Profit in Job Scheduling Hard Sort by end time, DP with binary search Like choosing which paid batch jobs a single worker should run. Sort jobs by end time. The best profit up to a job is either the best without it, or its profit plus the best among jobs that end by its start, found with bisect. 630 Course Schedule III Hard Greedy by deadline with a max heap of durations Take tasks in order of their deadline. When the total time passes a deadline, drop the longest task taken so far, because that frees the most time while keeping the count. A max heap (negative numbers in"],[52,"py-web-exam","Web revision exam: HTTP, Flask, Django, FastAPI and security","heapq) finds it. Lesson: LeetCode review set Tips and traps Remember Cover the answers, write down what you think each line prints, then open the answers. Every wrong guess points to the chapter in brackets. All four papers run in this page, so press Run to see the real output too.Lesson: How to use this revision chapter In the exam In a written exam, trace each snippet on paper before you look at the options, and watch for the answers \"404\", \"405\" and \"the code raises\". In an interview, say your assumptions out loud, start with the simplest working API, then add caching, security and limits.Lesson: Exam technique and a study plan"],[53,"","Interview Questions","Python interview questions for developers, data and QA engineers: mutable and immutable types, is against ==, copying, list and dict internals, comprehensions, *args and **kwargs, closures and decorators, generators, the GIL and concurrency, exceptions, OOP, MRO and magic methods, testing, and Flask, Django, REST and FastAPI questions, with output questions run on CPython. · interview · interview questions · viva · FAQ · job preparation · mutable vs immutable · is vs == · shallow vs deep copy · list vs tuple · dict internals · mutable default argument · args kwargs · closure · decorator · generator · GIL · multiprocessing · asyncio · MRO · dunder methods · pytest fixtures · Flask vs Django · REST · FastAPI · JWT Python · Interview Questions 🎤"],[53,"","Python Interview Questions","The Python questions asked of developers, data engineers and QA automation engineers, from mutable types, copying and the data model to decorators, generators, the GIL, testing, and Flask, Django, REST, FastAPI and JWT. Every Output question was run on CPython 3.14 to confirm what it prints. ✍️ Read the question and write your own answer in the box, as you would say it in the interview. 💡 Stuck? Open the hint. It points you in the right direction without giving the answer away. ✅ Open the answer to compare. You get the model answer, key points and a detailed explanation. 🎯 Rate yourself. Your notes and ratings stay in this browser, so you can filter to the ones to review. 🎤"],[53,"interview","Python Interview Questions","Say your answer out loud first, open the hint if you are stuck, then compare with the model answer and the follow up questions. 0/53known ⭐ Most important topics for interviews 🧱 Objects and types Mutable against immutable, is against ==, shallow and deep copies, and why a list cannot be a dict key. 📦 Built in containers List, tuple, set and dict: what each costs, how dicts and sets hash, and comprehensions. 🧰 Functions Mutable default arguments, *args and **kwargs, LEGB scope, closures, lambdas and decorators. 🔁 Iteration Iterators, generators, yield and yield from, and why generators save memory. 🏛️ OOP and the data model Classes, inheritance, MRO and super(), dunder methods, properties, dataclasses and ABCs. ⚡ Concurrency The GIL, threads against processes against asyncio, and when each one helps. 🧪 Testing and quality pytest fixtures and parametrize, mocking, type hints and virtual environments. 🌐 Web and APIs Flask against Django against FastAPI, REST and status codes, the ORM N + 1 problem, JWT and CORS. 0 of 53 answers opened, 0 known, 0 to review Objects and types Q1 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptObjects and types Which built in types are mutable and which are immutable, and why does it matter? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Ask whether the object itself can change after it is made. Your answer Model answer Immutable: int, float, bool, str, tuple, frozenset, bytes and None. Mutable: list, dict, set, bytearray and most user classes. It matters because a mutable object shared by two names changes for both, and because only hashable (normally immutable) objects can be dict keys or set members. Immutable: int, float, str, tuple, frozenset Mutable: list, dict, set Only hashable objects can be keys Detailed explanation \"Changing\" an immutable value really makes a new object: s += \"x\" binds s to a new string. A tuple is immutable, but a tuple that holds a list can still see that list change, and such a tuple is not hashable. How did you do? Q2 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptObjects and types What is the difference between is and ==? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first One compares values, the other compares identity. Your answer Model answer == calls __eq__ and compares values. is checks that both names point to the same object, which is the same as comparing id(). Use is only for singletons such as None, True and False. Detailed explanation CPython caches small ints from -5 to 256 and some strings, so is can look like it works for them by accident. Never rely on that. Follow up questions the interviewer may ask Why does PEP 8 say to write \"if x is None\" and not \"if x == None\"? How did you do? Q3 🌿 Medium🖨️ OutputObjects and types What does this print? a = [1, 2, 3] b = [1, 2, 3] c = a print(a == b, a is b) print(c is a) c.append(4) print(a, c) ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Two lists with the same values are still two objects. Your answer Model answer It prints True False, then True, then [1, 2, 3, 4] [1, 2, 3, 4]. Detailed explanation a and b hold equal values, so == is True, but they are different objects, so is is False. c = a makes a second name for the same list, so changing c also changes what a shows. TrueFalseTrue [1, 2, 3, 4] [1, 2, 3, 4] How did you do? Q4 🌿 Medium🖨️ OutputObjects and types What does this print? Explain shallow and deep copies. import copy grid = [[1, 2], [3]] shallow = copy.copy(grid) deep = copy.deepcopy(grid) grid[0].append(9) print(grid, shallow, deep) ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first copy.copy makes a new outer list. What about the lists inside it? Your answer Model answer It prints [[1, 2, 9], [3]] [[1, 2, 9], [3]] [[1, 2], [3]]. A shallow copy makes a new outer object that shares the inner objects. A deep copy copies every level. Detailed explanation list(x), x[:], x.copy() and copy.copy(x) are all shallow. copy.deepcopy(x) follows every reference and handles cycles with a memo dict. Deep copies are slower and can copy things you did not mean to, such as a shared cache. [[1, 2, 9], [3]] [[1, 2, 9],"],[53,"interview","Python Interview Questions","[3]] [[1, 2], [3]] How did you do? Q5 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptObjects and types Why can a tuple be a dict key but a list cannot? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Keys must keep the same hash for their whole life. Your answer Model answer A dict finds keys by their hash. A list can change, so its hash would change and the dict would lose it, which is why list sets __hash__ = None. A tuple of hashable items never changes, so its hash is stable. Detailed explanation The rule for your own classes: objects that compare equal must have equal hashes. If you define __eq__ without __hash__, Python makes the class unhashable. A frozen dataclass writes both for you. How did you do? Built in containers Q6 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptBuilt in containers When would you use a tuple instead of a list? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Think about change, hashing and meaning. Your answer Model answer Use a tuple for a fixed group of values whose position has meaning, such as a point (x, y) or a database row, and when you need it as a dict key. Use a list for a collection of similar items that grows or changes. Detailed explanation Tuples are a little smaller and faster to create, and a namedtuple adds field names. Returning several values from a function really returns a tuple. How did you do? Q7 🌱 Easy⌨️ CodingBuilt in containers Count how often each word appears in a sentence. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first The collections module has a class made for this. Your answer Model answer Use collections.Counter(text.split()). It is a dict subclass, and most_common(n) gives the n most frequent words. Detailed explanation Without Counter, use counts[w] = counts.get(w, 0) + 1 or a defaultdict(int). Mention the cost: O(n) time and O(k) space for k different words. from collections importCounterdef word_counts(text): returnCounter(text.lower().split()).most_common() How did you do? Q8 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptBuilt in containers What is the cost of x in a_list, x in a_set, a_list.insert(0, x) and a_list.append(x)? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Lists are arrays, sets are hash tables. Your answer Model answer x in list is O(n), x in set is O(1) on average, insert(0, x) is O(n) because every item moves, and append is amortised O(1). Detailed explanation For a queue use collections.deque, where appendleft and popleft are O(1). Turning a list into a set once and then testing many values is a common interview speed up. How did you do? Q9 🌿 Medium🖨️ OutputBuilt in containers What does this print? print([n * n for n inrange(5) if n % 2 == 0]) print({n: n * n for n inrange(3)}) print([(a, b) for a in (1, 2) for b in\"ab\"]) print({len(w) for w in [\"a\", \"bb\", \"c\"]}) ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Read each comprehension from left to right, like nested for loops. Your answer Model answer It prints [0, 4, 16], {0: 0, 1: 1, 2: 4}, [(1, 'a'), (1, 'b'), (2, 'a'), (2, 'b')] and {1, 2}. Detailed explanation The if filters, the expression comes first, and two for parts nest in the order they are written. A set comprehension drops the repeated values. [0, 4, 16] {0: 0, 1: 1, 2: 4} [(1, 'a'), (1, 'b'), (2, 'a'), (2, 'b')] {1, 2} How did you do? Q10 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptBuilt in containers How does a Python dict work inside, and what are the costs of its operations? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Hash table with open addressing. Your answer Model answer A dict is a hash table. The hash of the key picks a slot, and on a clash it probes other slots (open addressing). Lookup, insert and delete are O(1) on average and O(n) in the worst case. Since Python 3.7 dicts keep insertion order, because entries live in a compact array and the table only stores indexes into it. Hash picks a slot, probing on clashes O(1) average Insertion order kept since 3.7 Detailed explanation The table grows when it is about two thirds full, so an insert is amortised O(1). Sets use the same idea without values. Because of hash randomisation, the hash of a str changes between runs unless PYTHONHASHSEED is"],[53,"interview","Python Interview Questions","set. How did you do? Functions Q11 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptFunctions What are *args and **kwargs? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first One collects extra positional arguments, the other extra named ones. Your answer Model answer *args collects extra positional arguments into a tuple and **kwargs collects extra keyword arguments into a dict. In a call, * and ** do the opposite and unpack a sequence or a dict into arguments. Detailed explanation They are how decorators pass any arguments through: def wrapper(*args, **kwargs): return fn(*args, **kwargs). A bare * in a signature makes the following parameters keyword only, and / makes the earlier ones positional only. How did you do? Q12 🌿 Medium🖨️ OutputFunctions What does this print, and how do you fix the function? def add(x, items=[]): items.append(x) return items print(add(1)) print(add(2)) ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first When is the default value created? Your answer Model answer It prints [1], then [1, 2]. The default list is created once, when def runs, and every call that uses the default shares it. Use None as the default and make a new list inside. Detailed explanation The fix: def add(x, items=None): then if items is None: items = []. The same trap applies to dicts, sets and any object made in the default. Linters such as ruff flag it as B006. [1] [1, 2] How did you do? Q13 🌿 Medium🖨️ OutputFunctions What does this print? x = 0def outer(): x = 1def inner(): nonlocal x x += 1print(\"inner\", x) inner() print(\"outer\", x) outer() print(\"global\", x) ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Local, Enclosing, Global, Built in. And what do nonlocal and global change? Your answer Model answer It prints inner 2, outer 2 and global 0. Detailed explanation Python looks names up in the order Local, Enclosing, Global, Built in. nonlocal lets inner rebind the x of outer instead of making a new local. Without nonlocal, the assignment would make a local x and outer would still print 1. inner 2 outer 2global0 How did you do? Q14 🌿 Medium⌨️ CodingFunctions Write a decorator that prints how long a function takes, and keep the function's name. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first A function that takes a function and returns a wrapper. functools has a helper for the name. Your answer Model answer The decorator defines a wrapper that records the time, calls the function with *args and **kwargs, prints the time and returns the result. @functools.wraps(fn) copies the name, docstring and other attributes to the wrapper. Detailed explanation Common mistakes: forgetting to return the result, and forgetting wraps, which makes every decorated function report the name wrapper. A decorator with arguments, such as @retry(times=3), needs one more level: a function that returns the decorator. import functools import time def timed(fn): @functools.wraps(fn) def wrapper(*args, **kwargs): start = time.perf_counter() try: return fn(*args, **kwargs) finally: print(f\"{fn.__name__} took {time.perf_counter() - start:.3f} s\") return wrapper How did you do? Q15 🔥 Hard🖨️ OutputFunctions What does this print, and why? late = [lambda: i for i inrange(3)] early = [lambda i=i: i for i inrange(3)] print([f() for f in late]) print([f() for f in early]) ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first When does each lambda read i? Your answer Model answer It prints [2, 2, 2] and then [0, 1, 2]. Closures look up i when they are called, not when they are made, and by then the loop has finished with i = 2. A default argument captures the value at creation time. Detailed explanation This is called late binding. The fix lambda i=i: i works because defaults are evaluated when the lambda is created. functools.partial is another clean fix. [2, 2, 2] [0, 1, 2] How did you do? Q16 🔥 Hard🖨️ OutputFunctions What does this print? def bold(fn): returnlambda: \"<b>\" + fn() + \"</b>\"def italic(fn): returnlambda: \"<i>\" + fn() + \"</i>\"@bold@italicdef greet(): return\"hi\"print(greet()) ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Decorators are applied from the bottom up,"],[53,"interview","Python Interview Questions","but the wrappers run from the outside in. Your answer Model answer It prints <b><i>hi</i></b>. Detailed explanation @bold above @italic means greet = bold(italic(greet)). italic wraps the text first, then bold wraps the result, so the bold tags are outside. <b><i>hi</i></b> How did you do? Iteration Q17 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptIteration Why would you use a generator to read a 10 GB log file? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first How much of the file is in memory at once? Your answer Model answer A generator yields one line at a time, so memory stays small no matter how big the file is. A list would load every line into memory first. Detailed explanation Example: errors = (line for line in open(path) if \"ERROR\" in line). Generators also compose into pipelines, each step pulling one item from the step before. sum(x for x in ...) needs no brackets because a generator expression is the only argument. How did you do? Q18 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptIteration What is the difference between an iterable and an iterator? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first One can be looped over many times, the other only once. Your answer Model answer An iterable has __iter__ and gives a new iterator each time, like a list. An iterator has __next__ (and __iter__ returning itself), remembers its position and is used up after one pass. A for loop calls iter() once and then next() until StopIteration. Detailed explanation Files, generators, zip, map and enumerate return iterators, which is why looping over the same generator twice gives nothing the second time. How did you do? Q19 🌿 Medium🖨️ OutputIteration What does this print? def gen(): print(\"start\") yield1yield2print(\"end\") g = gen() print(\"made\") print(next(g)) print(next(g)) rest = list(g) print(list(g)) ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Calling a generator function runs none of its body. Your answer Model answer It prints made, start, 1, 2, end and []. Detailed explanation gen() only builds the generator object, so \"made\" comes first. Each next runs to the next yield. list(g) runs the rest, which prints end, and the generator then has nothing left, so the second list is empty. made start 12 end [] How did you do? Q20 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptIteration What does yield from do that a for loop with yield does not? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Think about send() and the return value of the inner generator. Your answer Model answer yield from inner passes every value of inner through, forwards send() and throw() to it, and evaluates to the return value of inner. A for loop with yield only passes values out. Detailed explanation It is the natural way to write recursive generators, such as walking a tree: yield from walk(child). Before async and await existed, coroutines were built on yield from. How did you do? OOP and the data model Q21 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptOOP and the data model What is the difference between an instance method, a classmethod and a staticmethod? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Look at the first parameter each one receives. Your answer Model answer An instance method receives the object (self). A classmethod receives the class (cls) and is often an alternative constructor such as Date.from_string(\"2026-11-08\"). A staticmethod receives nothing extra and is a plain function kept in the class for grouping. Detailed explanation A classmethod constructor also works for subclasses, because cls is the subclass when called on it. How did you do? Q22 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptOOP and the data model Which dunder methods would you add to a Money class, and what does each give you? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Printing, comparing, adding, hashing and truth. Your answer Model answer __repr__ for a clear debug text, __eq__ and __hash__ together for comparisons and use as keys, __lt__ (with functools.total_ordering) for sorting, __add__ for +, and __bool__ so zero money is falsy. Detailed explanation Return NotImplemented (not False) from __eq__ or __add__ for types you do not handle, so Python can try the other side. A"],[53,"interview","Python Interview Questions","frozen dataclass writes __init__, __repr__, __eq__ and __hash__ for you. How did you do? Q23 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptOOP and the data model Why use @property instead of get and set methods? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Start with a plain attribute, add rules later. Your answer Model answer A property lets code keep writing obj.price while the class adds a check or a computed value behind it. You can start with a plain attribute and change it to a property later without breaking any caller. Detailed explanation The setter can validate (raise ValueError for a negative price). A read only property has no setter. functools.cached_property computes a value once and stores it. How did you do? Q24 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptOOP and the data model Compare a dataclass, a namedtuple, a TypedDict and a Pydantic model. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Which ones check types when the program runs? Your answer Model answer A dataclass writes __init__, __repr__ and __eq__ for a normal mutable class. A namedtuple is an immutable tuple with field names. A TypedDict only describes the shape of a dict for type checkers. A Pydantic model checks and converts data when the object is created, which makes it the choice for input from outside, such as JSON in an API. Detailed explanation None of the first three check types at runtime. Choose a dataclass for internal objects, Pydantic at the edges of the system. How did you do? Q25 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptOOP and the data model What is an abstract base class and when would you use one? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first from abc import ABC, abstractmethod. Your answer Model answer An ABC defines methods that subclasses must implement. A class that still has an abstract method cannot be instantiated, so mistakes show up at once. Use one when several classes must share an interface, such as different payment providers or storage backends. Detailed explanation typing.Protocol is the alternative based on duck typing: a class matches if it has the methods, with no inheritance. Use it when you cannot or do not want to change the classes. How did you do? Q26 🔥 Hard🖨️ OutputOOP and the data model What does this print? classA: def who(self): return\"A\"classB(A): def who(self): return\"B\" + super().who() classC(A): def who(self): return\"C\" + super().who() classD(B, C): def who(self): return\"D\" + super().who() print(D().who()) print([k.__name__ for k inD.__mro__]) ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first super() follows the MRO of the object, not the parent of the class. Your answer Model answer It prints DBCA and ['D', 'B', 'C', 'A', 'object']. Detailed explanation The MRO of D is D, B, C, A, object (C3 linearisation). super() in B calls the next class in that order, which is C, not A. This is why every class in a cooperative hierarchy should call super(). DBCA ['D', 'B', 'C', 'A', 'object'] How did you do? Errors and resources Q27 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptErrors and resources Why define your own exception classes, and how should you catch exceptions? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Callers want to catch your errors and only yours. Your answer Model answer Your own classes, such as class PaymentError(Exception), let callers catch exactly the errors of your code. Catch the most specific exception you can handle, keep try blocks short, and never write a bare except: or except Exception: pass, which hides bugs. Detailed explanation Use raise NewError(...) from err to keep the original cause in the traceback. Log with logger.exception inside an except block to get the traceback in the log. How did you do? Q28 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptErrors and resources What does a with statement do, and how do you write your own context manager? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first __enter__ and __exit__, or contextlib. Your answer Model answer with calls __enter__ at the start and __exit__ at the end, even when an exception happens, so files, locks and connections are always released. Write one as a class with those two methods, or as a generator with"],[53,"interview","Python Interview Questions","@contextlib.contextmanager, putting the clean up after yield in a finally. Detailed explanation __exit__ returning True swallows the exception, which is rarely what you want. Examples in real code: with open(...), with lock:, with transaction.atomic(): in Django and with conn: in sqlite3. How did you do? Q29 🔥 Hard🖨️ OutputErrors and resources What does this print? def f(): try: print(\"try\") return\"try wins\"finally: print(\"finally\") return\"finally wins\"print(f()) ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first finally always runs, even after return. Your answer Model answer It prints try, finally and finally wins. Detailed explanation The return in try is about to happen, then finally runs. A return inside finally replaces the first one. Never return from finally, because it also swallows exceptions. Python 3.14 warns about it (PEP 765). tryfinallyfinally wins How did you do? Concurrency Q30 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptConcurrency What is the GIL, and what does it mean for threads? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first One lock, one thread running bytecode at a time. Your answer Model answer The Global Interpreter Lock in CPython lets only one thread run Python bytecode at a time. Threads still help for I/O bound work, because the GIL is released while waiting for the network or disk. For CPU bound work use multiprocessing (or a C library that releases the GIL). One thread runs bytecode at a time Threads help with I/O Processes help with CPU work Detailed explanation Python 3.13 added an optional free threaded build (PEP 703), and in 3.14 it is officially supported, but the default build still has the GIL. Most interviewers want the I/O against CPU distinction. How did you do? Q31 🔥 Hard🌍 ScenarioConcurrency Threads, processes or asyncio: which do you pick for (a) downloading 1,000 web pages, (b) resizing 1,000 images, (c) a chat server with 10,000 open connections? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Is the work waiting or computing, and how many tasks wait at once? Your answer Model answer (a) Threads or asyncio, because the work is waiting on the network. (b) Processes (ProcessPoolExecutor), because resizing is CPU work that the GIL would serialise. (c) asyncio, because 10,000 waiting coroutines are cheap while 10,000 threads would each need their own stack. Detailed explanation concurrent.futures gives the same interface for thread and process pools. asyncio needs async libraries all the way down (httpx, asyncpg), and one blocking call stalls everything. How did you do? Q32 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptConcurrency Two threads each run counter += 1 a million times. Why can the result be below two million, and how do you fix it? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first How many bytecode steps is +=? Your answer Model answer counter += 1 is a read, an add and a write. A thread switch between the read and the write loses an update. Protect it with a threading.Lock (with lock: counter += 1), or avoid shared state with a queue or by giving each thread its own counter. Detailed explanation The GIL does not make your code thread safe. It only protects the interpreter itself. Single operations such as list.append happen to be atomic in CPython, but compound updates are not. How did you do? Testing and quality Q33 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptTesting and quality Why use a virtual environment, and how do you pin dependencies? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Each project has its own packages. Your answer Model answer A virtual environment gives each project its own Python packages, so two projects can need different versions. Create it with python -m venv .venv or uv venv, and pin versions in requirements.txt or a lock file (uv.lock, poetry.lock) so every machine installs the same set. Detailed explanation pyproject.toml is the modern place for project metadata and dependencies. In CI install from the lock file, and scan dependencies with pip-audit. How did you do? Q34 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptTesting and quality Does Python check type hints when the program runs? ✍️ Step 1. Write your"],[53,"interview","Python Interview Questions","answer first Who reads the hints? Your answer Model answer No. Type hints are stored in __annotations__ and ignored by the interpreter. Tools such as mypy and pyright check them before the code runs, editors use them for completion, and libraries such as Pydantic and FastAPI read them on purpose to validate data. Detailed explanation Hints catch whole classes of bugs, such as passing None where a str is expected, and they document the code. Add them first to public functions. How did you do? Q35 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptTesting and quality What is a pytest fixture, and what do the scopes mean? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Set up and clean up that tests ask for by name. Your answer Model answer A fixture is a function decorated with @pytest.fixture that prepares something a test needs, such as a database or a logged in client. A test gets it by naming it as a parameter. Code after yield is the clean up. The scope (function, class, module, package, session) says how often it is created. Detailed explanation conftest.py shares fixtures between files. Built in fixtures include tmp_path, monkeypatch and capsys. @pytest.mark.parametrize runs one test with many inputs. import pytest @pytest.fixture def client(): app = create_app(testing=True) with app.test_client() as c: yield c def test_home(client): assert client.get(\"/\").status_code == 200 How did you do? Q36 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptTesting and quality How do you test code that calls an external API without calling the real API? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first unittest.mock, and where to patch. Your answer Model answer Replace the call with a mock: @patch(\"shop.payments.requests.post\") and set its return_value. Patch the name where it is looked up (the module that uses it), not where it is defined. Then assert on the call with assert_called_once_with. Detailed explanation Alternatives: dependency injection (pass the client in), the responses or respx libraries for HTTP, and dependency_overrides in FastAPI. Too many mocks test the mocks, so keep a few real integration tests. How did you do? Coding round Q37 🌱 Easy⌨️ CodingCoding round Reverse the order of words in a sentence, and also check whether a string is a palindrome ignoring case and spaces. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first split, slicing and join. Your answer Model answer Words: \" \".join(s.split()[::-1]). Palindrome: keep only letters and digits in lower case and compare the text with its reverse. Detailed explanation split() without arguments also removes extra spaces. For the palindrome, two pointers from both ends use O(1) extra memory, which is a common follow up. def reverse_words(s): return\" \".join(s.split()[::-1]) def is_palindrome(s): t = [c.lower() for c in s if c.isalnum()] return t == t[::-1] How did you do? Q38 🌿 Medium⌨️ CodingCoding round Find the first character that does not repeat in a string. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Count first, then walk the string again. Your answer Model answer Count every character with a Counter, then return the first character whose count is 1. Two passes, O(n) time. Detailed explanation Because dicts keep insertion order, you could also walk the Counter itself, but walking the string makes the intent clear. from collections importCounterdef first_unique(s): counts = Counter(s) return next((c for c in s if counts[c] == 1), None) How did you do? Q39 🌿 Medium⌨️ CodingCoding round Flatten a nested list such as [1, [2, [3, 4]], 5] to any depth. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Recursion with a generator. Your answer Model answer Walk the items. If an item is a list, yield from the flattened item, otherwise yield it. This handles any depth. Detailed explanation For very deep nesting the recursion limit (about 1,000) can be hit, so an explicit stack is the safe version. Strings are iterable too, so check for list, not for \"iterable\". def flatten(items): for x in items: if isinstance(x, list): yieldfrom flatten(x) else: yield x How did you do? Web and APIs Q40 🌱 Easy📘"],[53,"interview","Python Interview Questions","ConceptWeb and APIs Flask, Django or FastAPI: how do you choose? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Batteries included, small core, or typed APIs. Your answer Model answer Django comes with an ORM, admin, auth, forms and migrations, so it suits full websites and data heavy apps. Flask is a small core where you pick the parts, good for small services and learning. FastAPI builds JSON APIs from type hints, with validation, docs and async built in, good for new API services. Detailed explanation For an API next to a Django site, Django REST framework is the usual choice. A good answer names the team, the data and the traffic, not only the framework. How did you do? Q41 🌱 Easy📘 ConceptWeb and APIs What makes an API RESTful, and which status codes should it use? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Resources, methods and codes. Your answer Model answer Resources have URLs (/books/7), HTTP methods say what to do (GET reads, POST creates, PUT replaces, PATCH changes, DELETE removes), and status codes report the result: 200, 201 Created, 204 No Content, 400, 401, 403, 404, 409, 422, 429 and 500. Detailed explanation Requests are stateless, so every request carries its own authentication. GET, PUT and DELETE are idempotent and POST is not, which matters for retries. Use plural nouns, not verbs, in URLs. How did you do? Q42 🌿 Medium🌍 ScenarioWeb and APIs A Django page that lists 100 books with their authors makes 101 queries. Why, and how do you fix it? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first One query for the books, then one per author. Your answer Model answer This is the N + 1 problem. book.author is loaded lazily, one query per book. Use Book.objects.select_related(\"author\") for foreign keys (one JOIN) and prefetch_related for many to many or reverse relations (one extra query with IN). Detailed explanation Find it with django-debug-toolbar or assertNumQueries in tests. The same problem exists in SQLAlchemy (joinedload and selectinload). How did you do? Q43 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptWeb and APIs What are WSGI and ASGI? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first The interface between a web server and a Python app. Your answer Model answer WSGI is the standard sync interface: the server calls the app once per request and waits (Flask and classic Django, with gunicorn). ASGI is the async successor that also supports WebSockets and long connections (FastAPI and Starlette, Django async views, with uvicorn). Detailed explanation Both let you swap servers without changing the framework. ASGI only helps if the app really awaits I/O instead of blocking. How did you do? Q44 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptWeb and APIs How does FastAPI validate a request, and what happens when the data is wrong? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Type hints and Pydantic. Your answer Model answer FastAPI reads the type hints of the endpoint. Path and query parameters are converted to their types, and a parameter typed with a Pydantic model is read from the JSON body. Pydantic checks everything, and on failure FastAPI answers 422 with a list of errors and their locations, before your function runs. Detailed explanation response_model filters the answer the same way, so fields such as password hashes never leak. The same hints produce the OpenAPI docs at /docs. How did you do? Q45 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptWeb and APIs What is a JWT, and what are its risks? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Three base64url parts. Signed, not encrypted. Your answer Model answer A JSON Web Token is header.payload.signature. The payload holds claims such as sub and exp, and the signature (for example HMAC-SHA256 with a secret) proves the server issued it unchanged. Anyone can read the payload, so it must not hold secrets. Detailed explanation Risks: tokens that live too long (use short access tokens and refresh tokens), not pinning the algorithm when decoding (the \"alg\": \"none\" trick), storing tokens where JavaScript can read them, and no way to log out at once without a deny list. Follow up questions the interviewer"],[53,"interview","Python Interview Questions","may ask How would you revoke a JWT before it expires? How did you do? Q46 🌿 Medium📘 ConceptWeb and APIs How should an application store passwords? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Slow, salted, and compared safely. Your answer Model answer With a slow salted hash: Argon2id, bcrypt, scrypt or PBKDF2 with many rounds. Each user gets a random salt, the stored string keeps the algorithm, rounds and salt, and the check uses a constant time comparison such as hmac.compare_digest. Detailed explanation Never plain text, never encryption and never a fast hash such as sha256, which a graphics card guesses billions of times a second. Django and passlib do this for you. How did you do? Q47 🌿 Medium🖨️ OutputWeb and APIs What does this print? from urllib.parse import parse_qs, urljoin print(parse_qs(\"tag=a&tag=b&page=2\")) print(urljoin(\"https://x.example/api/v1\", \"books\")) print(urljoin(\"https://x.example/api/v1/\", \"books\")) ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Query values always come back as lists, and the trailing slash matters for urljoin. Your answer Model answer It prints {'tag': ['a', 'b'], 'page': ['2']}, then https://x.example/api/books and https://x.example/api/v1/books. Detailed explanation parse_qs gives a list for every key, because a key can repeat. urljoin replaces the last part of the base unless the base ends with a slash. {'tag': ['a', 'b'], 'page': ['2']} https://x.example/api/books https://x.example/api/v1/books How did you do? Q48 🌿 Medium🖨️ OutputWeb and APIs What does this print? import json text = json.dumps({1: (1, 2), \"ok\": None}) print(text) print(\"1\"in json.loads(text)) ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first JSON has no tuples, and its keys are always text. Your answer Model answer It prints {\"1\": [1, 2], \"ok\": null} and then True. Detailed explanation json.dumps turns the tuple into a list, None into null and the int key 1 into the string \"1\". After loads, the key is \"1\", not 1, which surprises people who round trip dicts through JSON. {\"1\": [1, 2], \"ok\": null} True How did you do? Q49 🔥 Hard🌍 ScenarioWeb and APIs A FastAPI endpoint written as async def calls requests.get and the whole API becomes slow under load. Why? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first What does a blocking call do to the event loop? Your answer Model answer requests.get blocks, and inside async def it blocks the single event loop, so no other request can run until it returns. Either use an async client (httpx.AsyncClient with await) or write the endpoint as a plain def, which FastAPI runs in a thread pool. Detailed explanation Ten requests that each wait 0.2 s finish in about 0.2 s with await, but take 2 s when the wait blocks the loop. How did you do? Q50 🔥 Hard🌍 ScenarioWeb and APIs A user changes /api/orders/81234 to /api/orders/81233 and sees another customer's order. What went wrong and how do you prevent it? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Authentication passed. What check was missing? Your answer Model answer Broken Object Level Authorization, number 1 on the OWASP API Top 10. The endpoint checked that the token was valid but not who owns the order. Filter by the caller, for example Order.objects.get(id=order_id, customer=request.user), and answer 404 for other people's objects. Detailed explanation Add a test that calls every endpoint taking an id with a second user. Random ids such as UUIDs make guessing harder but never replace the check. How did you do? Q51 🔥 Hard📘 ConceptWeb and APIs What is the difference between CORS and CSRF? ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first One loosens a browser rule, the other is an attack. Your answer Model answer CORS is a set of response headers that let listed origins read your API's answers in a browser. CSRF is an attack where another site makes the browser send a request that carries your cookies. CORS is configured with an exact origin list, and CSRF is stopped with SameSite cookies and CSRF tokens. Detailed explanation CORS never protects the server, because curl ignores it. APIs that use"],[53,"interview","Python Interview Questions","Bearer tokens in the Authorization header are not exposed to CSRF, because browsers do not add that header by themselves. How did you do? Q52 🧠 Expert🏗️ DesignWeb and APIs Design rate limiting for a public API that runs on 10 servers behind a load balancer. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first Where does the counter live, and which algorithm? Your answer Model answer Keep the counters in a shared store such as Redis, keyed by API key or user (IP only for anonymous traffic). Use a token bucket (bursts allowed, steady rate) or a sliding window, updated atomically with a Lua script or INCR with EXPIRE. Answer 429 Too Many Requests with Retry-After and rate limit headers. Detailed explanation Follow ups: fail open or closed when Redis is down, different limits per plan, a stricter limit on login, and limits at the gateway (nginx, a cloud API gateway) before traffic reaches Python. A token bucket of capacity C and rate r allows at most C + r × t requests in t seconds. How did you do? Q53 🧠 Expert🏗️ DesignWeb and APIs Design a link shortener API in Python. ✍️ Step 1. Write your answer first API, code generation, storage, cache, safety. Your answer Model answer POST /links returns a short code (201 with Location), GET /{code} redirects with 301 or 302. Codes are 7 characters of base 62 (about 3.5 trillion), made from a counter or random bytes. Store code, url and owner with the code as primary key, put Redis in front for the read heavy redirects, and add login, owner checks on delete, URL validation and rate limits. Detailed explanation FastAPI or DRF both fit. Count clicks through a queue so redirects never wait. Discuss what happens when two random codes clash (retry) and how long links are kept. How did you do? No questions match these filters."]]};
